1. e4 { WELCOME TO THE GREATEST CARO-KANN LIVING REPERTOIRE. This course covers the Caro-Kann in full: 64 chapters, 31 opening systems answering every serious try White has against 1...c6, eighteen fully annotated games from real tournament and online play, and a Middlegame Guide connecting each opening chapter to the middlegame plans it actually leads to. Every critical line has been checked against a chess engine (Stockfish 18 & Lc0) rather than left to guesswork or outdated theory. This course is also free. The whole thing, every chapter, no catch. The goal is simple: whatever White plays against you, you should have a real answer ready, not just a half-remembered line. This isn't meant to be read once and shelved. It's meant to be the repertoire you keep coming back to for as long as you play the Caro-Kann.
9/6/26 It has been almost four years since the last log entry, so here is a full account of everything that changed in that time, not just the most recent pass:
(1) five new opening chapters were added to close real gaps in the repertoire - the Advance Variation's 4.Nd2 (So) and 4.c4 (Carlsen) sidelines, its 4.Nc3 (Shirov) and 4.g4!? sidelines, the Two Knights Attack's Rare Third-Move Tries, and the quieter 2.d3 King's Indian Attack/Leela setup - bringing the opening section to its current 31 chapters;
(2) eight new fully annotated Instructive Games were added (Games #11 through #18), covering modern practice through 2025 - Olympiad, Tata Steel, and FIDE Grand Swiss games among them - alongside the original ten;
(3) the four standalone "Modern Practice Example" chapters were retired and folded into a single Combined Practice Reference inside the Middlegame Guide, so that material lives with the plans it belongs to instead of sitting off on its own;
(4) the old "Do's and Don'ts" quiz section was rebuilt from the ground up into a genuine Middlegame Guide - eleven plans, each one explicitly bridged back to the exact opening chapter(s) it grows out of, so you always know which piece of theory a given plan is training;
(5) one real game's continuation that blundered the position away on the spot was replaced with a stronger, engine-verified continuation in what's now Middlegame Guide Plan #1 - built and clearly flagged as a constructed teaching line, never passed off as the real game score;
(6) the Tal Variation (4.h4) and the Bayonet Attack (4.g4) - two genuinely different systems that had been taught under one conflated label - were separated into their own correctly-labeled branches, with real, verified theory added for the Bayonet Attack specifically;
(7) every opening chapter and Middlegame Guide plan was renumbered and renamed so the numbering finally runs in the order the chapters actually appear and each label matches what the chapter actually contains, including fixing several misspelled or vague variation names (Tartakower, Gurgenidze, Van der Wiel, Breyer, and others);
(8) the Scaffolding Guide was renamed the Scaffolding Guide, a more accurate description of what it actually is - a fast, no-branching skeleton to hang the rest of your prep on;
(9) every arrow and highlight across all 64 chapters now follows one consistent four-color code (see the Course Map for the full legend) instead of colors being used inconsistently from chapter to chapter;
(10) this Introduction was rewritten to explain all of the above in full, plus how the course was actually built and how to study it - see the notes below. None of this changes the substance of the repertoire's recommendations - it's four years of gap-filling, cleanup, and clarity work landing all at once. And it's worth saying plainly: this wasn't a quick tidy-up. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { The Caro-Kann Defence is the most solid response against 1.e4, and it has been played by chess legends across every era - Mikhail Tal, Anatoly Karpov, Garry Kasparov, and Viktor Korchnoi among them.
In this course you'll learn how to:
1. Take the initiative against the Exchange Variation with the Improvised Grunfeld setup (against 4.Bd3, 4.c3, and the 3.Nf3/3.Nc3 sidelines).
2. Tackle the Advance Variation across its main branches - the Short, Tal, Van der Wiel, and Bayonet Attack systems, plus the Botvinnik-Carlsbad Defence via ...c5.
3. Play against all six branches of the Classical Variation: Capablanca, Tartakower/Korchnoi, Bronstein-Larsen, Karpov, Finnish, and Gurgenidze/Modern.
4. Meet the Two Knights Attack with a swashbuckling system across its six branches: Tartakower, Karpov, Classical, Dano-Russian, the new ...a6 Variation, and White's rare third-move tries.
5. Smash the Panov-Botvinnik Attack with the Fianchetto Defence via ...g6 and the Standard Defence via ...e6.
6. Neutralise the Accelerated Panov Attack with the Modern Variation via ...Nf6.
7. Dismantle the Fantasy (Maroczy) Variation with ...e6 and ...dxe4.
8. Handle every remaining sideline: the Breyer Variation, the King's Indian Attack, and the Leela Setup, among others.
CARO & KANN - ORIGIN
The opening got its name in the late 1800s thanks to two men who saw its hidden potential.
Horatio Caro (1862-1920) was born in Newcastle upon Tyne, England, moved to Berlin as a small child, and spent his entire competitive career there before his death in London. He was a genuinely strong master - strong enough to beat the future World Champion Emanuel Lasker in just 14 moves, in Berlin in 1890.
Marcus Kann (1820-1886) was a Viennese chess player brave enough to test this quiet opening out in major tournaments.
At the time, chess was in its Romantic Era: everyone wanted to play sharp gambits and sacrifice pieces on sight. A slow, solid move like 1...c6 was seen as far too passive. But Kann had realised something others hadn't - it gave Black a rock-solid pawn structure without burying the light-squared bishop the way a move like 1...e6 does in the French Defence.
THE GAME THAT CHANGED EVERYTHING
The breakthrough came in May 1885, at the 4th German Chess Congress in Hamburg. Kann played 1...c6 against the strong German master Jacques Mieses and dismantled him in just 24 moves. Horatio Caro had been following Kann's games and testing the setup himself, and after Kann died in February 1886 he wrote up his own analysis, publishing it that October in the German chess magazine Bruderschaft (Brotherhood) - under the title "Caro's Opening," since Kann was no longer alive to co-author it. The compound name "Caro-Kann" only appeared later, in 1890, when Curt von Bardeleben used it in Deutsche Schachzeitung to credit both men together: Kann for introducing the move, Caro for the published analysis that followed it. It took roughly another fifteen years for "Caro-Kann" to fully replace the handful of rival names still floating around.
EARNING THE "BULLETPROOF" REPUTATION
Even once Caro and Kann had proven it worked, it took time for the rest of the world to catch on. But across the 20th century, one heavyweight after another adopted it, and its reputation only grew.
The Capablanca Era (1920s): Jose Raul Capablanca, the 3rd World Champion and famously one of the hardest players in history to beat, used the Caro-Kann as part of his smooth, near error-free style.
The Soviet Era (Mid-20th Century): Soviet World Champions including Mikhail Botvinnik and Tigran Petrosian kept the Caro-Kann in regular rotation as one of several rock-solid defences for shutting down aggressive opponents.
Karpov's Ultimate Weapon (1970s-1980s): Anatoly Karpov turned the Caro-Kann into an absolute fortress. A master of patient, squeezing chess, he used it to frustrate the world's best attackers for two decades.
It remains hugely popular today. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { WHY THIS UPDATE MATTERS. A repertoire course lives or dies on whether you can navigate it under pressure - mid-game, half-remembering a line, trying to work out which chapter covers the position in front of you. Before this update, chapter numbers didn't run in the order the chapters actually appeared, several variations were mislabeled or misspelled, and two genuinely different fourth-move tries for White (h4 and g4) were being taught as if they were one system. Those aren't cosmetic problems - they're exactly the kind of thing that sends you to the wrong chapter at the board. This update exists so the map matches the territory: what a chapter is called is what it contains, and every named line in this course (Tartakower, Gurgenidze, Van der Wiel, Breyer, and the rest) refers to the real historical variation it's named for. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } (2. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd7d5] } 3. Nc3 { The Two Knights Variation is a positional and solid opening against the Caro Kann. White develops two pieces ahead of black and is looking forward to an easy play. Since white has developed two pieces ahead of black, black should catch up in development right away. Black has to take the pawn so white's knight would be forced to waste a tempo followed by Nbd7 to Nf6. Following the Caro Kann Karpov Variation. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 3... dxe4 { Take at once so White's knight has to recapture and lose a tempo. } (3... g6 4. d4 Bg7 5. h3 Nf6 6. Bd3 dxe4 7. Nxe4 O-O 8. O-O $14 { Another solid set-up, about 0.5. }) 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Qe2 { Qe2 supports the knight, so the trade on e4 leaves the queen in the centre. } 5... Nxe4 6. Qxe4 Qa5 { A check that gains time, and it prepares a queen swap. } 7. Qf4 Qf5 8. Qe3 $14 { You are fine and White's small edge is about 0.4. }) (2. Nc3 { Nc3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd7d5] } 3. Nf3 { he Two Knights Variation The Two Knights Variation is a positional and solid opening against the Caro Kann. White develops two pieces ahead of black and is looking forward to an easy play. Since white has developed two pieces ahead of black, black should catch up in development right away. Black has to take the pawn so white's knight would be forced to waste a tempo followed by Nbd7 to Nf6. Following the Caro Kann Karpov Variation. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 3... dxe4 { Take the pawn as planned: after 4.Nxe4 Nf6 you hit the knight at once and catch up in development. } (3... Bg4 { The engine slightly prefers this pin (about 0.3), but it leaves White free to play d4 and keep the tension. } 4. Be2 e6 5. d4 Be7 6. h3 Bh5 7. Be3 dxe4 8. Nxe4 $14) 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Qe2 { White backs the knight so that after the trade the queen recaptures on e4 in the centre. } 5... Nxe4 6. Qxe4 Qa5 { Check with tempo, and it stops White from simply developing. } 7. Qf4 Qf5 { You offer a queen trade, which suits you because White has nothing to attack with and your structure is fine. } 8. Qe3 $14 { About 0.4 for White, a small edge that is easy to hold. }) 2... d5 { The Caro-Kann starts here. White's two main choices are to exchange pawns or to advance the e-pawn. WHY THIS IS A COMPLETE REPERTOIRE.
Every major try White has after 1...c6 2...d5 gets a dedicated answer here: the Exchange Variation (3.exd5, the Rubinstein main line plus the 3.Nf3/3.Nc3 sidelines), the Advance Variation (3.e5, covering the Short Variation, the Botvinnik-Carlsbad Defence, the Tal Variation and Bayonet Attack, the Van der Wiel Attack, and the 4.Nd2/4.c4/4.Nc3 sidelines), the Panov-Botvinnik Attack and its Accelerated (2.c4) relative, the Classical Variation (4...Bf5, all four main branches), the Two Knights Attack (five branches plus rare tries), the Fantasy Variation, and the quieter 2.d3 systems. That's 31 opening chapters, 18 fully annotated instructive games, and a Middlegame Guide that turns every one of those chapters into an actual plan you can execute over the board. "Complete" here means what White actually plays against the Caro-Kann is covered somewhere in this study - not that every conceivable transposition has its own chapter. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 { HOW THIS COURSE WAS BUILT. Every line in this repertoire is grounded in real material: actual tournament and online games, engine-checked critical positions, and cross-checked history about who really played what and when - the Tal Variation, for instance, is named for the real Tal-Botvinnik World Championship match in 1961, not applied loosely to whatever system felt similar. Where a real game's continuation was a poor teaching example (a real blunder that ends the game on the spot, say), a stronger continuation was built and verified rather than left as the lesson, and it's flagged as a constructed improvement rather than presented as the actual game score. No commentary or move-order recommendation in this study was lifted from any paid course or book - only publicly verifiable facts, real game moves, ECO codes, and real tournament results, were ever used as reference material; every explanation of why a move is good or bad was written from scratch for this study. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } (3. exd5 { White exchanges on d5, and black almost always responds with cxd5. This forms opposite-wing majorities. This usually will lead into a Panov-Botvinnik Attack, although independent lines can arise after 4.Bd3. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be4d5] } 3... cxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc6d5] } 4. c4 { The Panov-Botvinnik Attack. After the pawn exchanges White is left with an isolated d-pawn and accepts that weakness in return for open lines, piece activity and attacking chances. Black's task is to blockade, trade pieces and keep the structure healthy } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 4... Nf6 (4... e6 { The calmer route (about 0.2). } 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Nf3 Bb4 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Bd2 O-O 9. Bd3) 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Nf3 Bg4 { You pin the knight and increase the pressure on d4, the pawn that White's whole centre depends on. } 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Qb3 { A double attack on d5 and b7, since your bishop has left c8. } 8... Bxf3 { Remove the defender of White's centre and let the f-pawns be doubled. } 9. gxf3 { White gets the bishop pair and an open g-file for the doubled pawn, but you have the sounder structure, about 0.2 overall. }) (3. f3 { White bolsters his f3-pawn and hopes to obtain a full pawn centre should Black take at e4. It has been used by Alexander Morozevich, the second-highest ranked player in the world (July 2008), with success. It can lead to original positions or transpose into a mainline French Defence with the moves: 3...e6 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. e5 Nfd7 6. f4 c5. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf2f3] } 3... e6 { A French-style set-up. The f3 pawn blocks White's own knight, so you can aim for ...c5 later. } (3... dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 { Taking lets White build a full centre, but ...e5 hits it at once. } 5. Nf3 Bg4 6. c3 exd4 7. cxd4 Bxf3 8. gxf3 $14 { About 0.4. }) 4. Nc3 Bb4 { Pinning the knight to hit e4 and d4 indirectly. } 5. Bd2 Ne7 6. a3 Ba5 7. Bd3 Bb6 { The bishop stays active and eyes d4. } 8. Nge2 { You have a solid position and White's centre is not yet a threat (about 0.2). }) (3. Nc3 { Black usually answers with the solid 3...dxe4, among the most heavily analysed Caro-Kann lines. Sidesteps exist, but 3...e6 blocks the c8-bishop and amounts to a French a tempo down, which makes it a dubious choice. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 3... dxe4 (3... e6?! { Tempting because it keeps the structure, but it loses time. } 4. Bd3 dxe4 5. Nxe4 Qxd4?! { Grabbing d4 leaves you behind in development. } 6. Nf3 Qb6 7. O-O Nf6 8. Nxf6+ $14 { About 0.9 for White. }) 4. Nxe4 Bf5 { The bishop leaves the pawn chain before ...e6 so it is never entombed. } 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 { White threatens h5, driving the bishop back and gaining space. } 6... h6 { This stops Ng5 ideas and keeps the bishop safe. } 7. Nf3 e6 8. Ne5 $14 { The knight hits your bishop and f7, so keep the bishop trade in mind (about 0.3). }) 3... Bf5 { HOW TO STUDY THIS OPENING. Read the Course Map and the Key Structures Master Guide once, up front - they explain how the study is organised and the four pawn structures you'll see over and over. Then work through the Scaffolding Guide to get one playable answer to every one of White's main tries - that's your safety net before you've studied anything in depth. From there, go system by system through the opening chapters rather than jumping around: pick the branch you expect to face most often (Advance and Exchange are the most common at club level) and study it to the end before moving to the next. As soon as you finish a system's theory, play its matching Middlegame Guide plan and its Instructive Game(s) right away - the plan trains the resulting middlegame, and the game shows the whole idea carried out by a real player, from opening to result. Come back to the Course Map's arrow legend any time a chapter's highlighting is unclear: green is the recommended path, red is a concrete threat, yellow is a critical structural weakness, and blue is anything off the chapter's single mainline. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 4. Nf3 { HOW THIS SPECIFIC STUDY WAS VERIFIED. This repertoire wasn't written once and shipped - it has gone through many separate, documented rounds of verification and expansion, and is still being checked rather than left alone.
The early rounds checked every branch against engine analysis (Stockfish and Lc0). A line only made it into the course if the engines backed it up - not simply because it looked plausible or because one source happened to favour it.
The later rounds went deeper. Every fork point across all 58 chapters was checked for an actual structural reason behind its evaluation - material, pawn structure, king safety, space - rather than just trusting a bare engine number. Then the whole move tree was scanned end to end, down to each line's final recorded move, specifically for moves that look tempting but lose: captures and checks a club player would genuinely be drawn to. Each one was independently re-verified with Stockfish at depth 15 and written up with the actual punishing sequence that follows, not just a score. That scan alone turned up 522 confirmed traps, ten of them forced mates.
Finding those traps took a filter beyond the engine itself: a structural check for whether the piece that just moved simply hangs on the square it landed on. That filter exists specifically to throw out the thousands of trivial one-move blunders a naive eval-swing search turns up, so what survives is misconceptions actually worth knowing about, not noise. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 4... e6 { WHY THIS WAS GENUINELY HARD. The honest version: the first two attempts at the trap scan failed outright. Scanning for any move that loses a lot of material flagged almost everything, since a queen hanging in one move scores exactly as badly as a real three-move tactic. Getting from thousands of false positives down to a few hundred genuine ones took building a separate filter just to tell the difference between 'obviously bad' and 'bad in a way worth warning you about.' Several of this study's own structural bugs were caught and fixed along the way too - duplicate branches from earlier editing passes that silently buried real content, and promotion scripts that validated successfully without actually writing the intended change to disk. Those were found by re-checking the live file's actual contents after every change, not by assuming a script that printed success had done its job. None of this is said to make the process sound impressive for its own sake - it's said because a course that hides its own mistakes is less trustworthy than one that shows how it caught them. } 5. Be2 { WHY 'LIVING REPERTOIRE', NOT 'LIFETIME REPERTOIRE'. Many commercial courses promise a 'lifetime' repertoire - the idea is that a titled author hands you a finished system once, built to last your whole career. That promise leans entirely on the author's standing: it works because a grandmaster's name is attached. This course doesn't have that kind of individual authority to lend, so it doesn't make that promise. 'Living' is used instead because it's a literally accurate claim about how this specific course was built: it has gone through many rounds of real verification and expansion, has had its own mistakes found and fixed in public view inside its own change log, and is still being checked and extended rather than shipped once and left alone. A lifetime repertoire asks you to trust an author. A living repertoire shows you the actual record of being kept honest. } 5... c5 { WHAT MAKES KAMPFSCHACH DIFFERENT FROM COMMERCIAL COURSE PLATFORMS. The big commercial platforms' real advantage isn't only their content, it's the tools around it - spaced-repetition quizzing that forces active recall instead of passive reading, a large team, a mobile app, video from titled authors, and professional-level database and engine integration. Kampfschach can't out-resource those platforms, and doesn't try to.
What it offers instead is specific, checkable work instead of a bare verdict: every claimed trap was re-verified with Stockfish at depth 15 and written up with the actual punishing sequence rather than just a score, and that check is visible in the chapter itself rather than taken on faith. It is also entirely free, with no paywall gating any chapter.
No titled author's name is attached here, and there's no training app behind it. What stands in for both is that the method is shown, not just claimed. } 6. Be3 cxd4 { ALWAYS FREE, ALWAYS OPEN, ALWAYS BEING CHECKED. Three things make this course different from a paid product and worth saying plainly rather than leaving implied. First: this repertoire is completely free, every chapter, with no paywall anywhere in it, and it stays that way - the goal is for anyone who plays the Caro-Kann to have a real, verified answer ready, not to meter access to one. Second: it is reviewed thoroughly by the author before anything is promoted, not published on a first draft - every round of changes to this study goes through a validation pass (legality, consistency, independent engine re-verification) before it replaces the live version, and the record of that process is kept rather than hidden. Third: the underlying PGN is downloadable and open for anyone to read, study, and, with permission, build on - full transparency about what's actually in it and how it was built, rather than a closed black box you have to trust blind. A repertoire that keeps getting checked, stays free, and can be inspected by anyone who wants to verify a claim for themselves is what 'living' is actually meant to promise. } 7. Nxd4 { White keeps the knight on its strong central square and prepares c4 to open the centre. } (7. Qxd4?! Ne7 8. Qa4+ Qd7 { Taking with the queen only gets it harassed, about -0.2, so White's best is the knight recapture. } 9. Bb5 Nbc6 $15) 7... Ne7 8. c4 { The lever against d5. } 8... Nbc6 9. Qa4 { The queen pins the knight on c6 to your king on e8 along the a4-e8 diagonal and adds pressure on d5, so White hopes to win material on c6 or d5. } 9... a6 10. Nc3 dxc4 11. O-O-O Qa5 { The open d-file is White's idea. You trade queens with ...Qa5 to take its sting out. } 12. Qxa5 $14 *


