1. e4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { HOW TO USE THIS STUDY.
This course is organised in four blocks rather than a single line of numbered parts.
Block 1, Getting Started: the introduction, this course map, the master guide to the four Caro-Kann pawn structures, and a 20-minute scaffolding guide giving one main line per system for fast preparation.
Block 2, the 31 opening chapters (Chapter #1 through Chapter #31): Exchange Variation (#1-#3), Panov-Botvinnik and Accelerated Panov Attacks (#4-#8), Classical Variation (#9-#14), Advance Variation (#15-#21), Two Knights Attack (#22-#27), Fantasy Variation (#28-#29), and 2.d3 Systems (#30-#31). Sidelines and rarer tries are not split into a separate section of their own - each one gets its own numbered chapter inside its parent system (for example Chapter #3 for Exchange sidelines, Chapters #18-#19 for Advance Variation sidelines, Chapter #27 for Two Knights Attack's rare third-move tries), so you never have to jump elsewhere to find a system's minor branches.
Block 3, the Middlegame Guide: eleven plans (Plan #1 through Plan #11) covering technique, decision-making, and critical structural weaknesses, each one grown out of a specific opening chapter or group of chapters.
Block 4, eighteen Instructive Games: complete annotated games from real tournament and online play showing the repertoire in practice. SUGGESTED STUDY ORDER. First pass: read the introduction, this map, the pawn-structures guide, and the scaffolding guide, so you have a playable answer to each of White's main tries. Second pass: take one opening system at a time from Chapter #1 through Chapter #31 and study it to the end, including its sideline chapters, before moving to the next system, rather than reading breadth-first. Third pass: the Middlegame Guide and the Instructive Games together, since the middlegame plans and the annotated games reinforce each other directly.
WHAT THE ARROWS AND CIRCLES MEAN. Green marks a good square, a constructive plan, or the move being recommended. Red marks a concrete threat - a capture, an attacking idea, or something the opponent is or will be threatening. Yellow marks a critical weakness - a structural or king-safety flaw that the surrounding notes are built around. Blue marks a subvariation: any arrow or circle attached to a move that branches off this chapter's main line (rather than sitting on the single mainline path from the chapter's first move) is shown in blue, regardless of what that side branch itself is about, purely so you can tell at a glance whether you are looking at the main story of the chapter or a side note.
MIDDLEGAME GUIDE COVERAGE MAP.
The Middlegame Guide is built to connect back to the opening systems taught in Chapters #1-#31, not just illustrate isolated technique.
Plan #1: Two Knights Attack, Mindeno Variation (Chapters #22-#26, plus Chapter #27, Rare Third-Move Tries).
Plan #2: Classical Variation (Chapters #9-#14: Capablanca, Tartakower/Korchnoi, Bronstein-Larsen, Karpov/Smyslov-Petrosian, Finnish, Gurgenidze/Modern).
Plan #3: Advance Variation, Botvinnik-Carlsbad Defence (Chapters #17-#18).
Plan #4: Advance Variation, Tal Variation (Chapter #20). Plan #7: Advance Variation, Bayonet Attack (Chapter #20).
Plan #5: Fantasy Variation (Chapters #28-#29).
Plans #6, #7, and #8: Advance Variation, Short Variation (Chapter #15 and Chapter #19, 4.Nd2 (So)/4.c4 (Carlsen) Sidelines).
Plan #9: Classical Variation, Gurgenidze/Modern (Chapter #14).
Plan #10: Classical Variation, Bronstein-Larsen (Chapter #11).
Plan #11 (Combined Practice Reference): Exchange Variation (Chapters #1-#3), Panov-Botvinnik Attack Fianchetto and Standard Defences (Chapters #4-#5), Accelerated Panov Attack (Chapters #7-#8), Two Knights Attack Tartakower treatment (Chapter #22), Fantasy Variation (Chapters #28-#29), and 2.d3 Systems (Chapters #30-#31).
Three specialist chapters are not tied to a dedicated plan of their own: Chapter #6 (Panov-Botvinnik Attack, QGD Structure) shares its technique. 
CHOOSING YOUR REPLY - SAFE, BALANCED OR SHARP? Every chapter now carries extra sidelines and short notes starting with CHOICE GUIDE that say which branch suits which player. SAFE / POSITIONAL means low risk, little theory and long manoeuvring games. BALANCED means normal risk and normal theory. SHARP / RISKY means more memorisation, more ways to go wrong and more chances to win. If you want a quick answer: the safest full repertoire is Exchange with 4...Nc6, Advance with 3...Bf5, Classical with 4...Bf5 or 4...Nd7, Two Knights with 5.Nxf6+ exf6, Fantasy with 3...dxe4 and 2.d3 with ...e5. The sharpest options are Advance 3...c5, Classical 5...gxf6 and Fantasy 3...Qb6.
ADVANCE (3.e5, Chapters #15-#21). Choose 3...Bf5 for a SAFE / POSITIONAL game: the bishop gets out before the chain locks, the structures resemble a French with a good bishop and the theory is manageable. The Short Variation 4.Nf3 e6 5.Be2 (Chapter #15, Plans #6 and #8) is the calmest, the Van der Wiel plan 4.Nc3 e6 5.g4 (Chapter #16) and the h4/g4 pawn storms (Chapter #20, Plans #4 and #7) are BALANCED to SHARP because you must know the first eight moves. Choose 3...c5 for a SHARP / RISKY game that unbalances the position at once. Against 4.dxc5 you can pick 4...e6 (BALANCED, easy plan) or 4...Nc6 (more tension, heavier theory); against 4.Nf3 cxd4 5.Qxd4 Nc6 6.Qf4 pick 6...g6 for a solid fianchetto or 6...f6 for a sharp counter; against 5.Nxd4 the quiet ...e6 and ...Nc6 set-up is SAFE (Chapters #17-#18).
CLASSICAL (3.Nc3/3.Nd2 dxe4 4.Nxe4, Chapters #9-#14). 4...Bf5 (Chapter #9, Plan #2) and 4...Nd7 (Chapter #12) are SAFE / POSITIONAL. 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ exf6 (Chapter #10) gives a rock-solid pawn structure at the cost of a small White edge: SAFE. 5...gxf6 (Chapter #11, Plan #10) is the SHARP / RISKY choice with attacking chances and a weaker structure. The Alien Gambit 5.Ng5 c5 is SHARP but good for Black (Chapter #10).
EXCHANGE (3.exd5 cxd5, Chapters #1-#3). The structure is symmetrical, so play is decided by pieces. 4.Bd3 Nc6 is the SAFE / POSITIONAL Rubinstein-style set-up; 4.Bd3 Nf6 5.c3 Bg4 with an early ...e5 is BALANCED and more dynamic (Chapter #2); against 4.Nf3 simple development with ...Nc6 and ...Bf5 is SAFE (Chapter #3).
PANOV-BOTVINNIK (4.c4, Chapters #4-#6). 5...e6 gives a QGD-like structure with little theory: SAFE / POSITIONAL. 5...g6 (Chapter #4) is BALANCED. 5...Nc6 (Chapter #6) is BALANCED to SHARP: 6.Nf3 a6 is the flexible choice, 6.Bg5 a6 and 6.Bf4 g6 are the more demanding lines. The Accelerated Panov (Chapter #7) is answered SAFELY with ...Nf6, ...Nbd7, ...g6 and ...Bg7.
TWO KNIGHTS (2.Nf3, Chapters #22-#27). 5.Nxf6+ exf6 (Chapter #22) is SAFE / POSITIONAL. 5.Qe2 Na6 is a trappy BALANCED surprise (the e7-pawn is pinned, so ...Nbd7?? loses to Nd6#). 5...gxf6 (Chapter #25) is SHARP / RISKY and gives White a clear edge, so choose it only if you accept real risk. Avoid 4...Bf5 against 2.Nf3, since the Ne5 and Qh5 tactics are annoying.
FANTASY (3.f3, Chapters #28-#29). 3...dxe4 4.fxe4 e5 is SAFE with active pieces. 3...e6 is BALANCED and French-like. 3...Qb6 (Chapter #29) is the SHARP / RISKY, slightly unusual option.
2.d3, KING'S INDIAN ATTACK AND RARE MOVES (Chapters #27, #30-#32). 2...d5 3.Nd2 e5 4.Ngf3 Bd6 (Chapters #30-#31) is SAFE / POSITIONAL and easy to learn; against 2.Nf3 d5 3.d3 simple development with ...Qc7 is SAFE. For the rare second moves in Chapter #32 (2.Ne2, 2.f4, 2.b3, 2.Bc4, 2.Nc3 d5 3.Qf3) fight for the centre with ...d5 and avoid the traps; against the Hillbilly 2.Bc4 d5 3.Bb3 dxe4 4.Qh5 g6 Black is simply better. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. Nc3 { The most testing is 2.Nc3, aiming for a Classical-style game with d4 next. } (2. c4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Nf3 Bg4 { This is the Panov structure, about 0.2. }) 2... d5 3. d4 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 { White's h-pawn pushes to harass the bishop, and ...h6 is your standard answer so Ng5 and h5 ideas lose their bite. } 7. Nf3 $14 *


