[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/P2Jl3jAg"]

1. e4 $14 { FOUR STRUCTURES, ONE OPENING. Every serious try White has against the Caro-Kann funnels into one of four pawn structures below, and it is White's third move that decides which one you get. 3.exd5 heads for the Carlsbad structure (Structure One) unless White follows up with c4, which instead produces an isolated-pawn structure (Structure Four). 3.e5 locks the centre into a French-style pawn chain (Structure Two). 3.Nc3 heads for a solid, weakness-free structure (Structure Three). Each one gets its own full breakdown, tied to the exact move that creates it, at the STRUCTURE heading below. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 $14 { 2...d5 is the move that defines this whole repertoire: rather than waiting, Black challenges the centre on move two. Everything forks from here on White's third move - see the STRUCTURE notes ahead for exactly where each of the four structures begins and what it looks like once it is reached. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. exd5 $10 { STRUCTURE ONE OF FOUR: THE CARLSBAD STRUCTURE. 3.exd5 trades off the central pawns and leads, after ...cxd5, to the Carlsbad structure: White pawns on d4 (and usually c3) and a queenside majority (a, b and c against a and b), Black pawns on d5 and e6 and a kingside majority (e, f, g and h against f, g and h). It is the Carlsbad structure with the colours reversed from the Queen's Gambit: Black has the queenside minority, which is why ...b5-b4 against c3 is Black's thematic plan. The pawn count is level and the centre is fixed, which means absolutely nothing will be decided by force here - everything depends on which side gets the better minor pieces and which pawn break arrives first. The full reference position is reached a few moves further down this line. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Re4d5] } (3. e5 $14 { STRUCTURE TWO OF FOUR: THE LOCKED CHAIN. 3.e5 commits White to a locked pawn chain - White pawns on d4 and e5 pointing at Black's kingside, Black pawns on c6, d5 and e6 pointing back the other way. This is a French Defence structure with one enormous difference in Black's favour, shown on the very next move. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } 3... Bf5 $14 { There it is. The bishop leaves the chain BEFORE ...e6 is ever played. In the French this piece is the source of all of Black's problems; in the Caro-Kann it is out and active before the door closes. Every single thing that makes this opening reliable comes back to this one move. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 4. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 4... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 5. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 5... c5 $14 { The thematic break. In a locked chain you attack the base, and d4 is the base of White's chain. Black almost always plays ...c5 at some point in the Advance Variation; the only real question is the timing and how it is supported. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 6. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 6... Nd7 $14 { The reference position for the whole Advance section. BLACK'S PLANS: hit d4 with ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6; reroute the king's knight via e7 to f5 where it attacks both d4 and the e3 bishop; trade off White's space advantage by exchanging on d4 at the right moment. WHITE'S PLANS: kingside space, an f4-f5 advance, and piece pressure against the backward e6 pawn. WHAT TO AVOID AS BLACK: releasing the tension on d4 too early. Trading on d4 is not a mistake in itself (the engine rates 6...cxd4 only slightly behind 6...Nd7), but doing it with no follow-up plan simply hands White the centre and leaves Black cramped for free. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 7. Nbd2 (7. c4 cxd4 8. Bxd4 Bb4+ 9. Nc3 Ne7 { If White hurries with c4, ...Bb4+ trades off the dark bishop and ...dxc4 follows. } 10. Qb3 Bxc3+ 11. Bxc3 dxc4) 7... Ne7 { The knight heads to c6 and f5 to attack d4. } 8. c4 { A lever against d5. } 8... Nc6 9. O-O Be7 10. Rc1 cxd4 11. Nxd4 Ndxe5 { You win back the e5 pawn that d4 protected. White still has some play on the c-file, so stay alert. } 12. Nxc6 $14) (3. Nc3 $14 { STRUCTURE THREE OF FOUR: THE SOLID, WEAKNESS-FREE STRUCTURE. 3.Nc3 invites ...dxe4, trading down into a structure where Black keeps the c6 and e6 pawns against White's c2 and d4 - the pawn count is level and White has slightly more space in the centre, but Black has a completely sound structure with no weaknesses whatsoever, and a bishop that gets out before the chain ever closes. The full reference position, reached around move 10, has both sides' plans spelled out further down this line. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 3... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 4... Bf5 $14 { Again the bishop comes out first. The pattern repeats across every structure in this repertoire and it is the thread that ties the whole opening together. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 5. Ng3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Be4g3] } 5... Bg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf5g6] } 6. h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 6... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 7. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 7... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 8. h5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh4h5] } 8... Bh7 { WATCH OUT: instead of 8...Bh7, the check 8...Qa5+ looks natural and tempting, but it leaves Black close to lost (about +5.0 for White, i.e. roughly -5.0 from Black's side of the board). After 9.Bd2 the queen is chased away and grabbing 9...Bxc2 only loses more: 10.Qxc2 Qb6 11.Ne5 e6 12.Nxd7 Kxd7 13.O-O-O } { [%csl Bh7][%cal Bg6h7] } 9. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 9... Bxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bh7d3] } 10. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd1d3] } 10... e6 $14 { The reference Classical position. Black has c6 and e6 against White's c2 and d4 - level on pawns, a little less space, but not one weakness on the board. THE DEFINING QUESTION IS WHERE THE KINGS GO. White very often castles queenside and throws the kingside pawns forward; the h4-h5 pawn already on the board is part of that plan. BLACK'S PLANS: complete development with ...Ngf6 and ...Bd6 or ...Be7, then generate queenside counterplay with ...c5 or ...b5 depending on where White's king lands. Speed matters more than material here. WHAT TO AVOID AS BLACK: castling kingside into an oncoming pawn storm without having started counterplay first, and trading off the defenders of the dark squares around the king. If White gets a free hand on the kingside the position becomes very unpleasant very quickly. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 11. Bd2 (11. Be3 Qa5+ 12. Bd2 Bb4 13. c3 Be7 { If White develops the bishop to e3, Qa5+ gains a tempo and you are fine (about 0.1). } 14. c4 Qc7) 11... Ngf6 12. O-O-O { White castles long and throws its h- and g-pawns at your king, but the pawn on h5 is already fixed. } 12... Be7 13. Ne4 O-O { Kingside castling is safe here because the h5 pawn has no more to attack with. } 14. Kb1 Nxe4 15. Qxe4 Nf6 { You hit the queen and trade pieces, making White's attack harder. } 16. Qe2 $14) 3... cxd5 { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc6d5] } 4. Bd3 $14 { Bd3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } (4. c4 $10 { STRUCTURE FOUR OF FOUR: THE ISOLATED QUEEN'S PAWN. 4.c4 is the Panov move: unlike the other three structures in this guide, this one is not weakness-free - White accepts an isolated pawn on d4 in exchange for open lines, the e5 and c5 outposts, and faster piece play. It is the single most double-edged structure in the whole repertoire, decided by whether Black gets a piece blockading d5 before White gets the initiative moving. The full reference position and both sides' rules of thumb are spelled out a few moves further down this line, at move 7. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 4... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 5. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 5... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 6. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 6... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 7. cxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc4d5] } 7... Nxd5 $14 { The reference IQP position. Everything about playing against an isolated pawn compresses into two rules. RULE ONE: blockade the square in front of it. The d5 square is not just a good square for a black knight, it is the square that makes White's extra central pawn permanently harmless. RULE TWO: trade minor pieces, not major ones. Every minor piece that leaves the board reduces White's attacking potential while the weak pawn stays exactly where it is; in a pure endgame the isolated pawn is simply a liability. WHITE'S SIDE OF THE BARGAIN: the isolated pawn buys open lines, the e5 and c5 outposts, and fast piece play. WHAT TO AVOID AS BLACK: drifting into passive defence and allowing d4-d5. If White ever achieves the d4-d5 break under good conditions, the isolated pawn stops being a weakness and becomes a battering ram. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 8. Bd3 { White develops and aims at the kingside. } (8. Nxd5 Qxd5 9. Bd3 Bd7 10. O-O Bb5 11. Bxb5+ Qxb5 12. d5 { White tries to rid itself of the isolated pawn by pushing it. The engine gives about 0.0. } 12... Qxd5) 8... O-O 9. O-O Nc6 10. Re1 Nf6 { The knight steps back to f6 so it cannot be hit by Nxd5 or Bc4 ideas, and you keep d5 under control from f6 and later b7. } 11. a3 b6 12. Bf4 Bb7 $14 { Now both your knight and bishop control d5, so the blockade holds. About 0.4 for White. }) 4... Nc6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 5. c3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 5... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 6. Bf4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gc1f4] } 6... Bg4 $14 { The single most important move of the structure. Black brings the light-squared bishop out beyond the pawn chain first, and only afterwards commits to ...e6. This is the whole justification for meeting 1.e4 with 1...c6 rather than 1...e6: in the French Defence this bishop spends the game buried behind its own pawns, and here it is already an active piece. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gc8g4] } 7. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 7... e6 $10 { Only now does the pawn go to e6, once the bishop is safely outside. The resulting position is the reference picture for this entire section. BLACK'S PLANS: pressure d4 with ...Qb6 or ...Bd6 and ...Qc7; prepare the freeing ...e5 break once the pieces support it; consider a queenside minority advance with ...b5-b4 to create a target on c3. WHITE'S PLANS: advance the queenside majority (a, b and c against a and b), a knight route to e5, and pressure down the half-open e-file. WHAT TO AVOID AS BLACK: passive shuffling. If neither ...e5 nor queenside expansion ever happens, Black simply gets squeezed on the kingside with no counterplay at all. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 8. h3 { White asks the bishop to decide. } (8. Qb3 Bd6 9. Bxd6 Qxd6 10. Nbd2 O-O 11. O-O Bf5 12. Ne1 Ne7 $10 { White's queen move is not a threat (about 0.0). }) 8... Bh5 { Keep the pin on the knight to hold White's pieces back. } 9. Nbd2 Bd6 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 { The queen recaptures, so the dark-squared bishop trade costs you nothing and removes White's active bishop on f4. } 11. O-O O-O 12. Re1 Qc7 $14 { You get a very solid position, though White has a small edge (about 0.2). } *


