1. e4 $14 { This chapter is a quick-reference guide, not a deep-dive chapter - it exists purely so you have a quick idea and guide as to how the Caro Kann operates and work. So that you're never caught off guard by one of White's rarer tries on move 2. None of these are dangerous with accurate play; each just needs one clean, memorable answer rather than a full repertoire of its own. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { d4 leads to a small White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (2. c4 $10 { The Panov Attack is White's sharpest way to fight the Caro-Kann. The isolated d-pawn is a long-term target for Black, but White accepts that in return for initiative, and may even give up a pawn if the activity is worth it. Black's job is to consolidate, develop smoothly and let the structure do the work. } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 $14 { Recapturing with the queen loses time, since Nc3 would drive it away. 3...Nf6 is the better way to regain the d-pawn. } 5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. Nxd5 Qxd5 $15 { Black's Queen is dangerous in the middle of the center, especially when it cannot even be disturbed by white's minor pieces. } 7. Qf3 Be6 8. Bd3 Qxf3 9. Nxf3 Nc6 10. O-O O-O-O $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6).] } 11. Be4 { White offers to trade bishops to simplify. } (11. Bb5?! Bd5 12. Bxc6 Bxc6 13. Ne5 Bd5 14. d3 f6 15. Nc4 e5 { White threatens to ruin the queenside but only drives you to a better set-up, about -1.0. } 16. Be3 $17) 11... Bd5 12. Bxd5 Rxd5 { The rook is active on the d-file. } 13. d4 e6 14. Be3 f6 { Your solid structure is a long-term asset. } 15. Rac1 Kd7 $15 { The king heads to the centre, and you are slightly better in this endgame because of White's isolated d-pawn (about -0.6). }) (2. Nf3 $14 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 2... d5 3. Nc3 $14 { The Two Knights Variation is a positional and solid opening against the Caro Kann. White develops two pieces ahead of black and is looking forward to an easy play. Since white has developed two pieces ahead of black, black should catch up in development right away. Black has to take the pawn so white's knight would be forced to waste a tempo followed by Nbd7 to Nf6. Following the Caro Kann Karpov Variation. } 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 $14 { A further option is ...Nf6, which normally heads for a Classical Caro-Kann after the knight trade and the recapture, followed by d4. That central push is the natural continuation and nothing stronger is available. } 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. d4 Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. O-O Be6 9. Be3 Nd7 10. Qd2 Re8 11. c4 Nf8 12. Rfe1 Qd7 13. Rad1 Rad8 $10 { The opening phase is over and Black stands very well: the rooks are active on the d- and e-files, the bishop pair is in place, and Black has an extra knight available for defence. } 14. b3 Bg4 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 15. Be2 { White breaks the pin on the knight, since the bishop on g4 threatens to take on f3 and shatter the kingside pawns. } (15. Bf1? Bxf3 { Unpinning with the bishop fails: you take on f3 and the recapture wrecks White's king cover. } 16. gxf3 Ng6 17. Qa5 b5 $17 { About -1.6. }) 15... Ng6 16. Kh1 h5 { You aim for ...h4 and ...Bxf3 to keep pressure on f3, so White's pieces are tied to the defence. } 17. Ng1 { White has to retreat. } 17... Bc7 18. Qb4 Bb8 19. Qc3 Ne7 $15 { You keep the initiative (about -0.6). }) (2. b3 $10 { 2.b3 - a quiet fianchetto try, sometimes called the Nimzowitsch-style setup against the Caro-Kann. It's harmless: just develop normally. } { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bb2b3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd7d5] } 3. Bb2 { Bb2 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.7), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bb2][%cal Bc1b2] } (3. exd5 { 3.exd5 clarifies the centre at once, in contrast to the slower Bb2/Nf3 set-ups typical of the Euwe-style lines. } 3... cxd5 4. Bb2 Nc6 5. Nf3 { ...and Black equalizes comfortably after recapturing. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.25), a sound third-move alternative against this rare 2.b3 sideline.] } 5... Nf6 (5... Bg4 6. Be2 e6 7. O-O Nf6 8. d4 Be7 9. Nbd2 O-O 10. h3 $15 { Another sound set-up, about -0.2. }) 6. Bb5 { White pins the knight and might damage your pawns. } 6... Bf5 7. Qe2 Rc8 { A useful rook move: it will back the c-pawn once White takes on c6, and it supports ...c5. } 8. Bxc6+ bxc6 { The doubled pawn is the price for the bishop pair and a good centre. } 9. d3 c5 10. Nbd2 $15 { You are slightly better (about -0.2). }) 3... Nd7 $14 { There's no need to grab the tension on e4 - White's bishop on b2 doesn't actually recapture there, so ...dxe4 just hands White a tempo for nothing. Simple development leaves Black at least equal, arguably a shade better thanks to the extra central space. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 4. e5 { White grabs space. } (4. Nc3?! d4 5. Nce2 e5 6. f4 Bd6 7. Nf3 f6 8. fxe5 fxe5 $15 { Here you gain space, about -0.1. }) (4. exd5 cxd5 5. d4 e6 6. Nf3 Bd6 7. c4 Ne7 8. c5 Bb8 $14) 4... f6 { You undermine the e5 pawn at once. } 5. f4 Nc5 6. d4 Ne4 { The knight settles on e4, a strong outpost that White's pieces have to trade. } 7. Bd3 Nh6 8. Bxe4 dxe4 $14 { Your pawn on e4 cramps White (about 0.4). }) (2. f4 $10 { 2.f4 (the La Bourdonnais Attack) - an old, aggressive-looking try that overextends more than it threatens. } { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bf2f4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd7d5] } 3. e5 { e5 leads to roughly equal (-0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } (3. d3 { 3.d3 supports the e4 pawn quietly instead of the more committal e5 advance, keeping the position flexible. } 3... dxe4 4. dxe4 Qxd1+ 5. Kxd1 { ...and the position stays level. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a sound, solid third-move try against this rare 2.f4 sideline.] } 5... Nf6 (5... Nd7 6. Nf3 Ngf6 7. e5 Nd5 8. Bd2 g6 9. a4 Bh6) 6. e5 { White gains space and hits the knight. } 6... Nd5 7. Nf3 Nd7 { You attack e5 at once. } 8. Bd2 g6 9. a4 Nc5 { The knight finds a strong post. } 10. Nc3 { This is equal (about 0.0) and White's king in the centre is awkward. }) 3... Bf5 $15 { This transposes into a structure very similar to the main Advance Variation chapters, except White's f4 (instead of a more useful developing move) is arguably a slight loss of time. Treat it exactly like a normal Advance Variation Short System and you're at least equal. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 4. Nf3 { 4.Nf3 - simple development instead of an immediate 4.Nc3 or 4.d4, keeping options open about how to support the e5-pawn. } (4. d4 { 4.d4 transposes into a structure very close to the main Advance Variation, just reached via 2.f4 instead of 2.d4. } 4... e6 5. Nf3 c5 6. c4 { ...and the position settles into a normal, balanced Advance-style structure. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1), a sound transposing try against this 2.f4 sideline.] } 6... dxc4 (6... cxd4?! 7. Nxd4 Bxb1 8. Rxb1 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ 10. Qxd2 Ne7 11. cxd5 $14 { White opens lines in the centre and gets a small edge, about 0.5. }) 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Be3 cxd4 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 10. Qxd4 Qxd4 11. Bxd4 $15 { You keep the extra pawn after the queen swap, about -0.3 in your favour, while White's only compensation was its development lead. }) 4... e6 { 4...e6 (Solid & Optimal) - calm, standard development that prepares ...c5 or ...Nd7 without committing to anything sharp. [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.1), the safest and most reliable reply.] } (4... h5 { 4...h5 (Complicated & Imbalanced) - grabs extra space and a tempo on the kingside immediately, aiming for a livelier game rather than settling into a quiet structure right away. [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.1), equally sound but more double-edged in practice.] } 5. d4 { White wants to hit your bishop and keep f4 and e5 as a space-grabbing pair. } (5. Nc3 h4 6. d4 h3 7. g3 Nh6 { the ...h4-h3 wedge cramps White's kingside, about 0.0 }) 5... e6 6. Nc3 Nh6 { The knight is going to f5 or g4 to hit d4 and h2. } 7. Be3 Qb6 8. Na4 Qa5+ 9. c3 Nd7 { You are comfortable, about 0.0, with the queen active and ...c5 to come. }) 5. d4 (5. h3 Be7 6. g4 Bg6 7. d4 c5 8. Bb5+ Nc6 9. f5 exf5 $15 { White's pawns race, but exf5 stops it (about -0.4). }) 5... c5 { Strike at the centre immediately. } 6. Be3 Qb6 { The queen hits b2 and d4. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. Bxc5 Qxc5 9. Nc3 a6 $15 { You are slightly better (about -0.2) and White has no real pressure. }) (2. g3 $10 { 2.g3 - a fianchetto try that develops quietly rather than fighting for the center with d4. } { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd7d5] } 3. Bg2 { [%csl Bg2][%cal Bf1g2] } 3... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 4. Bxe4 $15 { Simplest response: just trade in the center. The resulting position is completely comfortable for Black with easy development and no theory to memorize. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bg2e4] } 4... Nf6 { This hits the bishop, and White's bishop on e4 has no good square except retreating. } 5. Bg2 (5. Bxc6+ bxc6 { the check wins only a pawn for a bishop, so you are simply a piece up }) 5... e5 6. d3 Bc5 7. Nd2 O-O 8. h3 h6 9. Ngf3 { Solid and about -0.3 for you, so play ...e5 and ...Bc5 and castle. }) (2. Ne2 $10 { 2.Ne2 - keeps options open (the knight can go to g3 or later support a d4 push) without committing the way 2.d4 or 2.Nc3 does. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd7d5] } 3. e5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } 3... Bf5 $10 { Once White commits to e5, this is just a normal Advance Variation with an extra, slightly awkward developing move for the knight - fine for Black, no adjustments needed to your usual Advance Variation plans. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 4. d4 (4. Nf4 e6 5. d4 c5 6. g4 Be4 { White tries to trap the bishop, but it escapes. } 7. Bb5+ Nc6 8. Bxc6+ bxc6) 4... e6 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h5 { This stops h5 and keeps the bishop safe. } 7. c4 c5 8. Bg5 Qa5+ { The check hits g5 and you are fine (about 0.0). }) (2. Bc4 $15 { 2.Bc4 - develops actively but allows Black to open the center immediately with tempo. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd7d5] } 3. exd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be4d5] } 3... cxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc6d5] } 4. Bb3 $15 { White's bishop ends up slightly misplaced on b3, since ...d5 already broke the tension it was eyeing. A perfectly comfortable, at least equal structure for Black - similar in spirit to some Panov-type positions but without White's usual extra central pawn. } { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc4b3] } 4... Bf5 { The bishop develops outside the pawn chain. } (4... Nc6 5. d4 Bf5 6. Nf3 e6 7. O-O Bd6 8. Re1 Nge7 9. Nh4 $15) 5. d4 e6 6. Nf3 Bd6 7. c4 { White opens the position to use the bishop on b3. } 7... dxc4 8. Bxc4 Nf6 9. Nc3 $15 { You are slightly better (about -0.4). }) (2. c3 $14 { 2.c3 - a kind of reversed Alapin idea, preparing d4 with extra support. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd7d5] } 3. exd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be4d5] } 3... cxd5 $14 { This just transposes into an Exchange Variation structure a tempo down for White compared to the normal 2.d4 lines, since c3 isn't especially useful here yet. Treat it like your regular Exchange Variation repertoire. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc6d5] } 4. d4 (4. Qb3 e6 5. d4 Bd6 6. Nf3 Ne7 7. Bd3 O-O 8. O-O Nbc6 $15 { Here b7 is guarded by the bishop and the queen move achieves nothing (about -0.2). }) 4... Bf5 { Bring the bishop out beyond the pawn chain. } 5. Qb3 { White attacks b7 and d5 together, since the bishop has left c8. } 5... Qd7 { The queen guards both b7 and d5. } 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Nh4 { Hitting the bishop. } 7... Bg6 8. Nxg6 hxg6 $14 { You give up the bishop for a knight, but you gain a half-open h-file and a sound structure, about 0.2 for White. }) (2. e5 $10 { 2.e5?! - premature; the pawn gets no real support and Black simply challenges it at once. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } 2... d6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bd7d6] } 3. exd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Be5d6] } 3... exd6 $10 { Completely equal and about as simple as this repertoire gets - a dead-level structure with symmetrical pawns and no theory worth memorizing beyond this single line. } { [%csl Bd6][%cal Be7d6] } 4. d4 (4. h3 d5 5. d4 Bf5 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 Bd6 8. Ne2 Nf6) 4... d5 { Here you replace the pawn on d6 with d5 and get a symmetrical structure. } 5. Bd3 Bd6 6. Nf3 Nf6 7. h3 O-O 8. O-O Re8 { Your rook goes to the e-file and the position is dead level (about 0.0). }) (2. Qf3 { 2.Qf3?! - an eccentric try, developing the queen far too early for no real gain. Black just continues normally. } 2... d5 3. exd5 { exd5 leads to a small Black edge (-0.4): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } (3. Nc3 { 3.Nc3 (instead of the immediate 3.exd5) - develops first and invites Black to resolve the center. } 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 { 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 Nf6 hits the knight with tempo, forcing White to untangle before Black finishes developing with an easy, comfortable game. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.1).] } 5. c3 { White prepares d4 so the central pawns are supported before the knight is traded. } (5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. c3 Qe7+ 7. Ne2 c5 { You use the check to develop. } 8. d3 Nc6 9. Qe4 Be6 { White's queen can't do much (about -0.1). }) 5... Nbd7 6. d4 g6 7. Bc4 { The bishop and queen both eye f7. } 7... Bg7 8. Ne2 O-O { Castling puts the king behind the f7 pawn, so the Bc4 and queen battery has no target. } 9. Nxf6+ Nxf6 { It is roughly level (about 0.1). }) 3... cxd5 { Simplest and best: trade in the center and finish development. White's queen on f3 does nothing useful here and will likely need a second move to get out of Black's way. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.4).] } 4. Bb5+ { This forces ...Nc6. } (4. Nc3 Nf6 5. d4 Nc6 6. Bb5 Bg4 7. Qg3 e6 8. Nf3 Bxf3 $17 { White's queen is awkward and your pieces are active, about -0.6. }) 4... Nc6 5. d4 Nf6 6. Ne2 Bg4 { The bishop hits the queen, so White needs another tempo. } 7. Qd3 e6 8. Ng3 Nh5 { You attack the knight and aim to break White's kingside structure. } 9. Nxh5 $15 { You are slightly better (about -0.4). }) (2. Be2 { 2.Be2 - a quiet developing move that keeps options open; if White follows up with e5, this is simply a normal Advance Variation with the bishop already committed to a modest square. } 2... d5 3. e5 Bf5 { Treat this exactly like your regular Advance Variation plans: the extra tempo White spent on Be2 instead of a more useful developing move is close to a non-factor. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5), well within the normal Advance Variation range.] } 4. d4 (4. c3 c5 5. Nf3 e6 6. d4 Nc6 7. O-O Bg4 8. Nbd2 cxd4 $14 { This transposes. }) 4... e6 5. c3 c5 { You strike at the centre right away. } 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. O-O Bg4 { This pins the knight and removes the defender of d4. } 8. Nbd2 cxd4 $14 { You trade pawns on d4 and open the c-file for your rook (about 0.3). }) 2... d5 $14 { This is the Exchange Variation - the "ECK" (Exchanged Caro-Kann) for short. See Chapter #1 for the full explanation of what the ECK is, the two kinds of players who reach for it, and why this repertoire treats it as one of Black's most reliable answers to 1.e4. } 3. exd5 { exd5 leads to a small White edge (+0.3): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } (3. Nc3 $10 { The classical set-up is simple and flexible for White: development comes easily, and the same formation turns up against the Sicilian, the Philidor and the French as well. } 3... dxe4 $10 { The most reliable answer to the Classical set-up is to take on e4. ...g6 transposes into a Modern Defence, while ...e6 shuts in the light-squared bishop and is harder to handle. } 4. Nxe4 { Nxe4 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (4. f3 { 4.f3!? - a Blackmar-Diemer-style gambit idea, offering a second pawn to open lines quickly instead of simply recapturing on e4. } 4... exf3 5. Nxf3 { 4...exf3 5.Nxf3 is the simplest and best response: just take the free pawn. White's faster development is real practical compensation, especially in a fast time control, but objectively Black is simply better with careful play. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-0.5).] } 5... Nf6 (5... Bg4?! 6. h3 Bxf3 7. Qxf3 Qxd4 { Greedy: White gets time and the pawn is not worth the weakened structure. } 8. Be3 Qd8 9. Bc4 e6 10. Rf1 $15) 6. h3 { White prevents ...Bg4 and prepares g4. } 6... Bf5 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. Qxd3 { Trading White's best attacking piece. } 8... Nbd7 9. g4 Qc7 10. Be3 $17 { You are better, about -0.7, and the extra pawn is yours to keep if you keep the king safe. }) 4... Nf6 5. Nxf6+ exf6 $14 { B15 Caro-Kann Defense: Tartakower Variation Viktor Korchnoi has played 5...exf6 many times (including in a world championship match). 5...exf6 is sounder than 5...gxf6!? of the Bronstein-Larsen Variation and offers Black rapid development. } 6. c3 Bd6 $14 { There is little to add about the Tartakower: follow the usual ...Bd6 and ...0-0 set-up and you will most likely be fine. The Tartakower also has some other interesting lines, which are included here as well. } 7. Bd3 O-O $14 { Black aims for the regrouping ...Re8, ...Be6, ...Nd7, ...Nf8 and ...h6 when needed - a model set-up for this structure. } 8. Ne2 Re8 9. Qc2 h6 $14 { ...h6 is the logical and safer choice; the more ambitious ...f5!? is also possible. } 10. O-O Nd7 11. Be3 Nf8 12. Rfe1 Be6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 13. Nf4 { White attacks the bishop on e6. } (13. c4 Ng6 14. Rad1 Qc7 15. h3 Rad8 16. Nc3 Qc8 17. Bxg6 fxg6 $14 { A calmer plan (about 0.2). }) 13... Bg4 14. h3 Qc7 { You hit the knight and h2. } 15. Qd2 Ne6 16. Ng6 { A speculative piece sacrifice. The point is to open the f-file and the g-file against your king. } 16... Bxh3 17. gxh3 fxg6 $14 { The engine's line, about 0.3. You win material but White gets lines against your king, so take care. }) (3. e5 $14 { The Advance Variation is one of the most challenging tries against the Caro-Kann. White gains space and has kingside attacking chances, much as in the French Defence. The principled, classical way to meet it is recommended here: Black makes full use of the bishop on f5 and gets strong queenside counterplay. In the French that same bishop is often the problem piece, and Black may even have to double the b-pawns just to trade it for a White bishop or knight; in the Caro-Kann Black avoids that fate because the bishop is developed before ...e6. } 3... Bf5 $14 { Thus, this is the classical way to play this, the ECK will be met with the principled and classical Bf5! In that case black solves all his problem on the LSB. } 4. Nf3 $14 { Nf3 is the most natural reply: White will castle kingside and develop the bishop pair. Sidelines such as c4?! are treated in this chapter as well. } 4... e6 $14 { Black has built a very solid pawn chain, is comfortable defensively and positionally, and can look for activity in the middlegame. } 5. Be2 $14 { White does have to option to play Be2 to keep the tension on the center and make the position less complicated since an exchange would help simplify the position. } 5... c5 $14 { From here ...c5 is needed to challenge the centre. } 6. O-O Ne7 7. Be3 Nec6 8. dxc5 Nd7 $14 { Two knights bear down on the e-pawn while a knight and a bishop target the c-pawn, so one of these pawns is likely to fall. } 9. c4 dxc4 10. Nbd2 c3 11. bxc3 Bxc5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] } 12. Nd4 { White heads for c6 to trade your active knight. } (12. Bxc5 Nxc5 13. Nc4 Qxd1 14. Rfxd1 Be4 15. Nd4 Ke7 { An equal endgame (about 0.0). }) 12... O-O 13. Nxc6 bxc6 14. Bxc5 Nxc5 15. Nc4 Qd5 { You centralise the queen and hit c4. } 16. Qd4 Bd3 $14 { White has a small edge (about 0.3). }) (3. f3 $10 { This is the Fantasy Variation of the Caro Kann. The Fantasy Variation of the Caro Kann is an aggressive opening choice against the Caro Kann. White actually wants a fantasy land where he has an open f file for his rook to take advantage of. Second to that white wants to get a fortified center following a principled development on the center. So far white just wants an easy play against black but at the same time get the initiative and advantage. } 3... e6 $10 { e6, this variation has a Semi Slav taste against the Fantasy Variation. First of all you create a very solid and fortified center. This is another alternative choice against the Fantasy Variation if you like positional and solid gameplay. You are going to try and set up a Solid Semi Slav like structure. This can even transpose into a French Defence like position if white were to advance his central pawn forward. } 4. Nc3 Nd7 5. Be3 a6 $14 { ...a6 keeps the b5-square under control. } 6. Bd3 Be7 7. Nge2 dxe4 8. fxe4 $10 { Black strikes at the centre with ...e5 and does not let White take over the game. } 8... e5 $14 { e5 needs to be played in order to develop the knight with ease on Ngf6. } 9. Ng3 exd4 10. Bxd4 Bf6 11. Bc4 Ne5 12. Bb3 Bg4 13. Qd2 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.7).] } 13... Nc4 { The knight hits the queen and the bishop pair, and heads for a queen swap that blunts White's initiative against your king. } (13... Nd3+ 14. Qxd3 Qxd4 15. Qxd4 Bxd4 16. h3 Be6 17. O-O-O Be5 18. Nf5 { This is weaker, about -0.1. }) 14. Bxc4 Qxd4 15. Qxd4 Bxd4 16. h3 Be6 17. Bxe6 fxe6 18. Nh5 $15 { You are slightly better (about -0.5), with the bishop pair for the endgame. }) (3. c4 { 3.c4?! - an attempt to reach a Panov-style structure a tempo down, since White has already spent a move on d4 instead of developing. Simplest is just to grab the free pawn. } 3... dxe4 { Black is simply a safe pawn up for the moment; White's compensation (quick development, open lines) is real but not enough to equalize fully. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-0.6).] } 4. Nc3 { White hits e4 and hopes to recover the pawn. } (4. Be3 Nf6 5. Nc3 Bf5 6. Be2 h5 7. f3 e6 8. fxe4 Nxe4 $17 { Your pieces come out with tempo (about -0.6). }) 4... e5 { Give the pawn back in the way that suits you. } 5. dxe5 Qxd1+ 6. Nxd1 Be6 { Your bishop eyes c4 and you stay a pawn up in the endgame. } 7. Bd2 Nd7 8. f4 O-O-O $15 { You are a pawn up and slightly better (about -0.6). }) (3. Nf3 { 3.Nf3!? - keeps both central pawns and invites Black to take on e4, intending to win the pawn back with the knight. } 3... dxe4 4. Ng5 { 4.Ng5 is the point: the knight heads for e4, hitting the bishop's future home and eyeing f7. Keep the bishop active with Bf5 rather than retreating passively. } 4... Bf5 { Black is simply better: the knight trip to g5 costs White time, and ...h6 or ...Nf6 next keeps everything comfortable. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-0.7).] } 5. g4 { White tries to trap the bishop. } (5. f3?! exf3 6. Qxf3 e6 7. Bd3 Nh6 8. Nc3 Be7 9. Nge4 Qxd4 $17 { About -1.1. }) (5. Bc4?! e6 6. g4 Bg6 7. Nc3 b5 8. Bb3 Nf6 9. Qe2 h5 $17 { About -1.5. }) 5... Bg6 6. Nc3 Nf6 7. Bg2 h5 { You use the h-file to open lines against the weakened kingside. } 8. h3 hxg4 9. hxg4 Rxh1+ $17 { You are clearly better (about -0.8). }) (3. Bd3 { 3.Bd3!? - an early try to recapture on e4 with the bishop immediately rather than preparing it with Nc3 or Nf3 first. } 3... dxe4 4. Bxe4 Nf6 { The bishop has to move again right away, so White has gained very little. Just develop normally. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] } 5. Bf3 { 5.Bf3 retreats to keep the bishop on the long diagonal rather than trading it off, supporting a later e4/d4 setup. } 5... Bf5 6. Ne2 e6 7. Ng3 { ...and Black develops normally with a comfortable position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1), a sound continuation in this rare 2.Bd3 sideline.] } 7... Bg6 { The bishop steps back so Nxf5 is not possible. } (7... Bd6?! 8. Nxf5 exf5 9. Qe2+ Be7 10. O-O O-O 11. Nd2 Re8 12. Qd3 $14 { Your pawn structure is damaged by exf5, about 0.5. }) (7... Bb4+?! 8. c3 Bxb1 9. Rxb1 Bd6 10. b4 Qc7 11. c4 Bf4 12. Bb2 $14) 8. O-O Nbd7 9. a4 a5 { This stops White's queenside expansion. } 10. Nd2 Be7 11. Nc4 O-O 12. Ne2 { You are comfortable (about -0.1). }) 3... cxd5 4. Bd3 $14 { Many experienced Exchange Variation players choose Bd3 to take a key square away from Black. Contesting the f4 square at once and neutralising White's bishop pair is the right response. } (4. c4 $10 { The Panov-Botvinnik Attack. After the pawn exchanges White is left with an isolated d-pawn and accepts that weakness in return for open lines, piece activity and attacking chances. Black's task is to blockade, trade pieces and keep the structure healthy. } 4... Nf6 $10 { This resembles a Marshall-style set-up and is quite playable, since White's e-pawn is gone. Black has two sensible plans: the Grunfeld-style set-up or a Queen's Gambit Declined structure. } 5. Nc3 g6 $14 { The Grunfeld-style approach is sometimes called dubious or inaccurate, yet it is the preferred way here to meet the Panov-Botvinnik Attack. Black's position stays solid, if somewhat passive, and has no major weaknesses. } { [%csl Bb6,Bb7,Bc6,Bc8,Be6,Be8,Bf5,Bf8,Bg7,Bh8][%cal Bf8g7,Bb8c6,Bc8f5,Bb7b6,Bc8b7,Be7e6] } 6. Qb3 $14 { Qb3, white wants to win a pawn and develop comfortably whereas black would have to catch up with the targeted pawn. } 6... Bg7 $14 { Black need not rush to protect the d-pawn. Even if White wins it, that pawn is weak, with no other pawns to support it, and will probably fall soon. Development matters more than safety here. } 7. cxd5 O-O 8. Be2 $14 { White tries once more to hold on to the pawn. } 8... Nbd7 $14 { With clear intention of Nb6! Thus the d pawn would be overloaded with both two knights and the queen on d8! (Keep in mind that Bc4? with intention of protecting the d 5 pawn would fall under Nb6! Both losing a tempo and a pawn!). } 9. Bf3 Nb6 10. Bf4 Bg4 $14 { Black removes the main defender of the d5-pawn. } 11. Bxg4 Nxg4 12. Nf3 Nf6 13. d6 $14 { The engine's pick. The move matters for two reasons: Black must recapture with the e-pawn, which creates an isolated pawn, and because the dark-squared bishop protects d5, Black cannot choose to retake with the e-pawn in a harmless way or opt for ...e6, which is a poor option. } 13... exd6 $14 { Recapturing with the e-pawn leaves an isolated pawn. White seems to have gained something, but both sides now have an isolated d-pawn and each can be targeted. An isolated pawn can serve as an attacking asset too. } 14. O-O Qd7 15. Rfe1 Rfe8 16. a4 Rxe1+ 17. Rxe1 Re8 18. Rxe8+ Nxe8 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] } 19. a5 { White gains space and kicks the knight on b6. } (19. Qb4 Qc6 20. h3 Bf8 21. a5 Qc4 22. Qa3 Nd7 $10) 19... Nc8 20. h3 a6 { Keep the queenside pawns solid so the a5 pawn cannot become a lever against b7. } 21. d5 { White grabs space and cramps your pieces, and the knight on c3 blocks your Bg7 anyway. } 21... Ne7 22. Nd2 Qf5 { Your queen now hits the bishop on f4, and the position stays level (about 0.0). }) 4... Nc6 5. c3 $10 { With c3 the d4 pawn is secure, so White can calmly develop Bf4, Nd2 and Nf3 - an improvised London-style setup. } { [%csl Gf3,Gb1,Gd2,Gf4,Gd3][%cal Gc1f4,Gb1d2,Gg1f3] } 5... Nf6 6. Bf4 g6 $14 { Black should aim for a Grunfeld-style structure rather than the Horwitz one. With the c-pawn missing, the Horwitz structure favours White: the light-squared bishop is hemmed in by fixed light-squared pawns and counterplay is limited to the queenside. The Grunfeld set-up is more versatile, with play in the centre and on the kingside, while queenside play remains available. } 7. Nf3 Bg7 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. O-O Bf5 10. Bxf5 gxf5 $14 { ½-½ (40) Mueller, H-Kieninger,G Beverwijk 1965 In the database this was first played in 1965! A novelty indeed! But in the match Black did not unleash a kingside attack but rather kept the position as solid in prevention of white interfering on black's kingside. The game was drawn in the endgame. } 11. Ne5 Nxe5 12. Bxe5 e6 13. a4 Kh8 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 15. Re1 Rg8 16. Qh5 Rg6 17. Nf3 Qg8 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 18. g3 { White shuts the g-file. } (18. Kh1? Rxg2 { A simple tactic: your rook and queen battery on g2 means the king cannot hold g2. } 19. Rg1 Qg6 20. Qxg6 Rxg6 21. a5 $17 { About -1.3. }) 18... Qg7 19. Ne5 Bxe5 20. Rxe5 Rg4 21. Qh3 Rg8 22. Rae1 Qf6 { Even, about 0.1. } *


