1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. This is Structure One from the Course Map guide (the Carlsbad structure): White's c3/d4 pawns and queenside majority (a, b and c against a and b) against Black's d5/e6 pawns and kingside majority (e, f, g and h against f, g and h). The position is fixed and nothing is forced, so the game turns on minor pieces and pawn breaks, not tactics. Black's bishop is already outside the chain on g4/h5 (the whole point of 1...c6 over 1...e6), so Black is never cramped the way a French player would be. The practical plan: trade down the dark-squared pieces where possible, keep the knight that reaches e4 or c4, and race for ...f6/...b5 before White's kingside pawns (f4/g4/h4, or a knight rerouted via f3-e5-d3) start to tell. Avoid locking the position further with unnecessary pawn moves; every tempo should go toward one of the two breaks. Against White's best play, expect to stand about +0.5 to +0.8 worse (Stockfish 16, from White's point of view). That is normal for the Exchange Variation, and the position is safe: no tactical problems, and the doubled f-pawns control e4 and g4. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +1 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go. A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Nxd4? on moves 5 to 9 loses a piece (about 4.5). ...Nxc3? or ...Nxf2? later loses a piece too (about 5). At move 3, 3...Qxd5? costs about 0.6 and 3...Nf6? about 0.6 to 0.8: recapture with the pawn. At move 10, anything except ...gxf5 costs about 1.8. 11...Kh8?! costs about 0.3 to 0.4 and 11...Qb6?! about 0.2. If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 10.Ne5 Nxe5 11.Bxe5? Bxd3! (the knight on d2 blocks the queen, so Black wins a piece). 10.Qe2 Bxd3 11.Qxd3 e6 12.Ne5?! Nh5! (Black is better). 13.a4 Kh8 14.Bxf6?! (White's edge drops from about +0.6 to +0.3). Chapter #2 continues the Rubinstein Variation (6...Ne4). Chapter #3 covers 3.Nf3 and 3.Nc3. If White plays 4.c4, go to the Panov chapters (#4 to #6). Practice the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #11. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. exd5 $10 { The Exchange Variation removes most of the theory and gives White a simple, safe structure, close to an improvised London System with Black's c-pawn traded off. Black's practical task is to avoid a passive position. This chapter uses the Grunfeld-style setup, which aims at the kingside and the centre at the same time and keeps queenside play optional. The quieter ...e6 route is about as good by the engine, so the choice is about structure and playability, not objective strength. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge4d5] } 3... cxd5 { cxd5 leads to a small White edge (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc6d5] } (3... Qxd5? $14 { Avoid. Recapture with the pawn. The queen comes out early and White gains time with 4.Nc3. } 4. Nc3 Qd6 5. Be3 Nf6 6. Bd3 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.6).] } 6... Bg4 { Get the bishop out beyond the pawns ahead of ...e6, which makes White burn a tempo on f3. } (6... Nd5 7. Nxd5 Qxd5 8. Nf3 Bg4 9. Be2 Qa5+ 10. c3 e6 11. Bf4 { A good alternative with the same evaluation (about +0.8). }) 7. f3 Bh5 8. Qe2 e6 9. O-O-O Nd5 10. Ne4 Qc7 11. Bd2 { White's plan is to castle long and attack; you trade pieces with ...Nd5 and use the h5 bishop to harass the king (about +0.8). }) (3... Nf6? $14 { Avoid. Without recapturing, Black falls a pawn behind after 4.dxc6, and the development it gets back does not fully make up for it. } 4. dxc6 Nxc6 5. Nf3 Bg4 6. c3 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.8). Notable features: White is a pawn up.] } 6... e5 { White is a pawn up, so you must open the centre; ...e5 hits d4 and opens lines for your pieces. } (6... e6 7. h3 Bh5 8. Be2 Bd6 9. O-O h6 10. Nbd2 O-O 11. Re1 { Too slow: White consolidates the pawn (about +1.4). }) 7. Be2 Bxf3 8. Bxf3 exd4 9. O-O Be7 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. Qxd4 { You get open lines and quick development, but White keeps the extra pawn (about +0.7). }) 4. Bd3 $14 { White's most flexible development. From here Black fights for the f4 and f5 squares and looks to neutralise White's bishop pair. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } (4. Bf4 { 4.Bf4 - develops actively onto the long diagonal, delaying the usual Nc3/Bd3 setups. } 4... Nc6 { 4...Nc6 develops naturally; the bishop on f4 is a reasonable square for White but creates no extra problems for Black to solve. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2), within the normal range for this chapter.] } 5. c3 { 5.c3 supports the center solidly before developing the knight, keeping options for both Nf3 and Nd2. } 5... Bf5 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 { ...and the position stays level. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1), a sound fifth-move alternative in this 4.Bf4 Exchange structure.] } 7... e6 { You are solid, and White's plan is Rb1 to defend b2 against your queen. } (7... Nxd4 8. cxd4 { loses a piece for a pawn, since d4 is guarded twice }) 8. Nd2 Nf6 9. Ne2 Qb6 { Hitting b2 forces White to waste time. } 10. Rb1 Be7 11. O-O O-O 12. Qf3 { Level, about 0.0, and you can follow with ...Rac8 and ...Na5 to pressure. }) (4. c3 { 4.c3 - a quiet supporting move, preparing Bd3 and keeping d4 well defended before developing the pieces. } 4... Nf6 { 4...Nf6 is simplest; the tempo White spends on c3 is close to a non-factor once development finishes. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] } 5. Nf3 (5. Bf4 Bf5 6. Qb3 Qc8 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. Nh4 Bd7 9. Bd3 Nh5 $14 { Qb3 hits b7 and d5 but Qc8 covers (about 0.2). }) 5... Nc6 6. Bd3 (6. Bb5 { White adds pressure on the c6 knight, the defender of your d5 pawn and of e5, hoping to double your pawns or gain time with Ne5. It is a common but slightly speculative move: it develops, yet the bishop may become a target. For you, this is an invitation to hit it with tempo and to use the loose b5 square. } 6... Qb6 $10 { Your queen attacks the bishop and the d4 pawn at the same time, so White must act. This is both a useful developing move and a way to deny White easy play. Your plan afterwards is ...e6, ...Be7, castling short, and ...a6 to push the bishop away when needed. Avoid ...Bg4 without careful thought, since White gets h3 with tempo. } 7. c4 dxc4 8. Nc3 e6 9. O-O Be7 { White offers the c4 pawn for quick development. You accept and complete your own development; do not cling to the extra pawn at all costs, but return it at the right moment if needed. } 10. Be3 a6 { With ...a6 you ask the bishop what it wants. White's idea is d5 to hit your knight, so be ready to answer it with ...exd5. Overall: hit the bishop with ...Qb6, take the offered pawn calmly, finish development, and keep your position flexible; the line is equal. This is a position with an open centre after the c4 pawn was taken: you have pawns on e6 and a6, White has queen on b3 and bishop on e3, and your queen guards the position. Your plan: finish development with ...Nf6 and ...Bd7, castle short, then return the pawn at the right moment with ...b5. White wants d5 to hit your knight and open lines, so be ready to recapture with ...exd5. The risk is hanging on to the extra pawn when White has a lead in development, and a bishop check can be awkward; if White plays Rd1, keep your king safe. }) 6... Bg4 { The pin on the knight keeps d4 under pressure. } 7. h3 Bh5 8. Bf4 e6 9. Nbd2 Bd6 $14 { You trade the dark bishops soon and are fine, though White is slightly better (about 0.2). }) 4... Nc6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 5. c3 $14 { White supports d4. Black has two sound moves here: 5...Nf6 (main line) and 5...Qc7. } { [%csl Gc3,Rf3,Rc7][%cal Gc2c3,Rg1f3,Rd8c7] } 5... Nf6 { Nf6 leads to roughly equal (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } (5... Qc7 $14 { Fully playable. The queen covers f4, so White's dark-squared bishop has fewer good squares. Black answers the most accurate 6.h3 with ...e6, ...Bd6 and ...Nge7. } 6. h3 $14 { White's most accurate move. } (6. Ne2 $10 { Less accurate but common. Black develops the light-squared bishop before playing ...e6. } 6... Bg4 7. O-O { O-O leads to roughly equal (-0.1): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } (7. h3 $14 { 7...Bh5 is the engine's choice. 7...Bxe2 sets a trap, but only if White recaptures the wrong way. } 7... Bh5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2).] } (7... Bxe2 $14 { Bxe2 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4): Black has a material edge of about 3 point(s). (the engine edge for White coexists with Black's extra material here because this is a known pawn-grab-for-compensation pattern in this line, not a tactical oversight - White has concrete compensation: faster development, open lines, or attacking chances that outweigh the missing pawn(s)) } 8. Kxe2 { Kxe2 leads to a small White edge (+0.4): White retains the bishop pair; White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } (8. Qxe2? $17 { Loses material. } 8... Nxd4! $17 { The knight takes with tempo. } 9. cxd4 Qxc1+ $19 { Nb1 blocks the a1-rook, so White cannot recapture on c1. Black is clearly better. WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nc3 e6 a3 Bd6 O-O Qd8 } { [%cal Rc7c1] } 10. Qd1 { White offers a queen trade at once, since it cannot recapture on c1 and has to cut its losses (about -2.0). } 10... Qxd1+ 11. Kxd1 e6 { Do not hurry anything. White's king has lost castling and you hold a clean extra pawn, so just finish development. } 12. Nd2 Bd6 13. Rc1 Ne7 { White's only plan is to use the c-file with Rc1 and bring the king to e2. You contest the file with ...Rc8 and keep the pawn. } 14. Ke2 h5 $17 { ...h5 gains space and gives the h8 rook a role while White's pieces are still tangled. }) 8... e5 $14 { Aims at the white king, but White keeps a slight edge. } 9. dxe5 Qxe5+ 10. Kf1 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] } (10. Be3 $10 { Be3 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.6): Black has an isolated pawn; White retains the bishop pair. } 10... Bc5 11. Re1 Bxe3 { WATCH OUT: Qh5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -2.4 from Black's own side of the board), after: g4 Qe5 Kf1 Be7 Qf3 Qf6 } 12. fxe3 Nf6 $15 { Black is better here: White's structure is damaged and the king is exposed. } 13. Nd2 Qc7 { Your queen steps off the e-file so that White's king no longer has anything to hit. } 14. Nf3 O-O 15. Qa4 { White's idea is to pressure Nc6 and the a-pawn while the king tucks away on f2, but b7 holds everything together. } 15... Rfe8 16. Kf2 Re6 17. Re2 Rae8 $15 { The e3 pawn is a permanent weakness and the white king cannot castle, so you double on the e-file and keep playing against it (about -0.8). }) 10... Nf6 { Declining Be3 leaves White's king on f1 with no castling rights, so White needs a few slow moves to get it to safety. Develop with tempo and do not touch the d5 pawn. } 11. Nd2 Bc5 12. g3 O-O 13. Kg2 Rae8 { Your rooks belong on the open e-file. White wants Nf3 and Bf4 to hit the queen and cash in the bishop pair, so a quick ...Qc7 is natural. } 14. Nf3 Qc7 15. Bf4 $14 { Your queen is hit by the bishop but it steps away with a useful move, and the d5 pawn remains a solid centre. }) 8. Qc2 { White unpins the Ne2 and gets ready for Bf4 hitting your queen, so the bishop on h5 must decide. } (8. Qb3 e6 9. Nf4 Bg6 10. Nxg6 hxg6 11. Bf4 Bd6 12. Bxd6 Qxd6 { Qb3 stares at b7 and d5, but both are guarded and ...e6 settles the matter. }) 8... Bg6 9. Bf4 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Qb6 { Your queen hits b2 and d4 and forces White to find a clean way to develop. } 11. Nd2 e6 12. O-O-O Nf6 $10 { You have all your pieces out and a healthy structure (about +0.2). }) 7... e6 8. Qe1 $10 { White's most testing move: the queen heads for h4 or g3. Black continues with ...Nf6, ...h6 and ...Bd6 and equalises. } { [%csl Be1][%cal Bd1e1] } (8. Nd2? $15 { Nd2 leads to a small Black edge (-0.3): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } 8... Bd6! $15 { Black takes the initiative. } 9. h3 Bh5 10. Re1 Nge7 11. a3 O-O 12. Qc2 Bg6 $15 { The light-squared bishop has escaped the pawn chain, so White has no pressure. Black is slightly better. } 13. Nf3 { White wants to harass your bishop with g3 and Nh4 and so free the bishop on d3. } (13. Bxg6 fxg6 14. Nf1 Nf5 15. Be3 { White swaps off the bishop but you keep better structure, about -0.4 }) 13... h6 14. g3 Bh5 15. Nh4 g5 { Your answer is to attack the knight and keep the bishop alive. } 16. g4 Bg6 17. Nxg6 fxg6 { You get the bishop pair for a knight and slightly better chances, about -0.3. }) 8... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 9. Bg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 9... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 10. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bg5f4] } 10... Bd6 $14 { White has at most a tiny edge, within normal opening imprecision. Black is comfortable. } { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 11. Bxd6 { White trades off your good bishop so that f3 can kick the Bg4 and the knight can reroute to c2 and e3. Do not worry: the edge is tiny (about +0.1). } 11... Qxd6 12. f3 Bh5 { If White ever plays g4 here, ...Bg6 trades the light bishops and the pawn advance has only loosened White's king. } 13. Qd2 O-O 14. Na3 a6 15. Nc2 e5 $10 { Your central break ...e5 is the equaliser: it opens the e-file in front of the white queen and leaves White with no real target. }) 6... e6 7. Nf3 Bd6 8. O-O Nge7 9. b3 O-O 10. Re1 $14 { A solid, weakness-free position. White keeps a slight, easily managed edge. } 10... Rd8 { Your rook supports the d5 pawn and the e5 break before White can aim at it. } 11. Na3 a6 { A useful prophylactic move: White's knight wants Nb5 hitting the queen, and ...a6 takes that square away. } 12. Nc2 Ng6 13. Ne3 f6 14. Qc2 Qf7 15. a3 $14 { White is rerouting the knight via c2 and e3 to pile up on d5 and aim for c4. Your pieces cover d5 and the f6/f7 setup keeps everything protected (about +0.3). }) (5... Nxd4?? $18 { Never take on d4 with the knight. White recaptures and simply wins a piece for a pawn. The same capture fails on moves 6 to 9, whichever White piece recaptures. } 6. cxd4 e6 $18 { [Engine verdict: winning for White (+4.8). Notable features: White is 2 points of material up.] } 7. Nc3 { White just develops, and the isolated d4 pawn is safe. } 7... Bd7 8. Bf4 Ne7 9. Nf3 Nc6 10. Bg3 Be7 11. O-O Qb6 $18 { There is no compensation: you are a knight down for one pawn (about +4.7). }) 6. Bf4 $14 { The main move. White develops the bishop before playing e3 or Nf3 and keeps control of e5 and the f4 square. } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gc1f4] } (6. h3 { A move-order point worth knowing: rather than develop the bishop right away, White rules out ...Bg4 for good first. Bf4 is not inherently a weaker try by playing it a tempo later - the engine still rates this close to the normal range for the chapter - but see the note after 7.h3 below for the concrete practical reason this insertion is the one White players find hardest to meet well. } 6... g6 7. Bf4 $14 { Transposing back to the main plan, just with h3 already in. } (7. g4 { An ambitious, more committal try now that h3 is already played: White grabs extra kingside space immediately rather than finishing development first. } 7... Bg7 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bf4 h5! $15 { Strikes back at the overextended pawn at once, the standard response to a flank pawn storm, and already starts to turn the position in Black's favour. } 10. gxh5 (10. g5 { Keeps the kingside closed, but hands Black a completely free hand in the centre instead. } 10... Nd7 11. Nbd2 e5 12. dxe5 Ndxe5 13. Nxe5 Bxe5 14. Qf3 Qc7 15. Be3 Rd8 16. O-O $17 { Black has an easy, comfortable game and in fact a clear edge; the g5-pawn is more of a long-term weakness than a real space gain here. } 16... d4 { This central break is stronger than comfort: the pawn hits c3 and e3, and opens lines for the bishop on e5 and the knights. } (16... Bh2+ 17. Kh1 Ne5 18. Qg2 Nxd3 19. Qxh2 Qd7 20. Nf3 Qxh3 21. Qxh3 Bxh3 { This gets activity but is less accurate than ...d4. }) 17. cxd4 Nxd4 18. Qg2 Bd7 19. f4 Bg7 20. Rac1 Bc6 21. Be4 { Your knight hits the queen with tempo and the centre pawns are gone, so you keep a lasting edge (about -2.5). }) 10... Nxh5 11. Bg5 Bf6 12. Bxf6 exf6 13. Qd2 Qd6 14. Na3 Re8+ 15. Be2 Nf4 $19 { Black's pieces are far more active than White's, and the king has nowhere comfortable to go - a direct demonstration of why 7.g4 is more risk than reward even with h3 already supporting it. } 16. Nb5 { Nb5 attacks your queen and c7, but gets nothing. } (16. Ne5? fxe5 17. O-O-O exd4 18. Nb5 Qf6 19. Bf3 dxc3 20. Nxc3 Ne5 { Trying to hit first with Ne5 loses material (about -5.9). }) 16... Qb8 17. Ng1 Bf5 18. Kd1 a6 19. Na3 Qd6 20. Nc2 Be4 { White cannot castle, the bishop on e2 is pinned by your rook and your pieces swarm; this is about -3.6. }) (7. Nf3 { White develops the knight and prepares O-O, Bf4 and Bg5 ideas, avoiding the structure problems of an early g3. It is a natural development move, common in practice (played in about 333 thousand games). For you it means the position transposes to a normal Exchange Variation structure and you should develop actively. } 7... Bf5 { You exchange or harass White's strongest bishop and keep the long diagonal b1-h7 under control. The point of the move is to meet Bb5 with ...Bg7 and keep the bishop pair safe. Plan: ...Bg7, ...O-O, ...Qc7 to pressure c3 and ...a6 to gain space on the queenside. Avoid passive moves like ...Qc7 first, because White can then play Ne5 with tempo. } 8. Bb5 Bg7 { The c6 knight is pinned slightly but you simply finish development. } 9. O-O O-O 10. Re1 Qc7 11. Nbd2 a6 { Time to ask the bishop on b5 a question; it retreats and you have gained space. } 12. Bf1 { Overall: the structure is equal, and White has a small initiative that you can neutralise by exchanging pieces on the light squares and playing ...e6. This is a symmetrical pawn structure with White having played h3 and Bd3, so the centre is fairly closed and neither side has a big space advantage. Both kings will go short. Your plan: finish development with ...Bg7, castle, then challenge the c6 pin with ...a6, followed by ...Bf5 or ...Rd8 and ...e6 to trade light-squared pieces. White wants to play Ne5 or Bf1, and tries to build a small initiative using the e-file. You meet it by trading pieces. The risk is allowing Ne5 with tempo, and dropping a pawn on d5. If White deviates with Qb3 or Ne5, keep your pieces coordinated and trade when you can. }) 7... Bg7 (7... Bf5 $10 { Also perfectly playable once h3 has already ruled out ...Bg4 - there is no reason to delay the usual trade on d3 just because the move order changed. } 8. Bxf5 gxf5 9. Nd2 e6 10. g4 Bd6 11. Qf3 Bxf4 12. Qxf4 Ne7 { White plays g4 to break your doubled f-pawns; you trade the dark-squared bishops and bring the knight to e7 and g6. The position is about equal (about +0.2). }) 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Nbd2 Nd7!? $14 { The key practical improvement for this exact move order: rather than the immediate 9...Bf5 trade (see the note just below), Black first prepares a central break with ...e5. Once White has already spent a tempo on h3, trading light-squared bishops hands White an extra supporting move for a later g4 push (compare the 9...Bf5 note), so meeting it with the central break instead is the cleaner practical answer - the actual reason 6.Bf4 can feel notoriously awkward to handle well against this exact move order if Black insists on the normal trading plan regardless. } (9... Bf5 10. Bxf5 gxf5 11. g4 $14 { This is the point: with h3 already played, g4 arrives with full support and real venom, since Black's doubled f-pawns are now a genuine target rather than just a cosmetic weakness. } 11... Qb6 12. Nb3 a5 13. a4 e6 14. Qc2 Ne4 15. gxf5 exf5 16. Rg1 $14 { Black is doing fine here too with precise play, but it is a sharper, more demanding version of the normal structure than the central-break plan below, exactly because the h3 insertion gives White's pawn storm extra teeth. } 16... Rac8 { Put the rook against the c5 outpost. } (16... Rfe8 17. O-O-O Ne7 18. h4 Ng6 19. Be5 f6 20. Bh2 Bh6+ 21. Kb1 { This is worse (about +1.5): White gets his bishop to e5 and attacks. }) 17. Nc5 Rfe8 18. O-O-O Ne7 19. Nh4 Kh8 20. Kb1 Ng6 { White's trumps are the c5 knight, the rook on g1 and the knight on h4 pointed at f5; you must trade pieces and challenge with ...Ng6. }) 10. O-O e5! $14 { The central break: with the dark-squared bishop still on g7 instead of having been traded away for White's light-squared one, opening the centre favours Black's better-coordinated pieces rather than inviting a kingside pawn storm. } 11. dxe5 Ndxe5 12. Nxe5 (12. Bb5 { Pins the knight, but the pin does not actually achieve much, since Black's central pawn is already rolling forward regardless. } 12... d4! $15 13. Bxc6 Nxc6 14. c4 h6 15. Re1 g5 16. Bg3 Bf5 $15 { Black's structure and piece activity both look excellent here; the bishop pair White kept back on move 7 has not found a real diagonal to use. } 17. Qb3 { White hits b7 and tries to trade queens. } (17. Qa4 d3 18. c5 Nd4 19. Nxd4 Qxd4 20. Qxd4 Bxd4 21. c6 bxc6 { Attacking c6 does not work: ...d3 cuts across and White is worse (about -1.1). }) 17... Qb6 18. Qb5 Bd3 19. a4 Rfe8 20. Rxe8+ Rxe8 21. Re1 Rxe1+ { Your bishop on d3 and the d4 pawn dominate, so a queen trade leaves you better (about -0.7). }) 12... Nxe5 13. Nb3 Nc4 14. Rb1 Qf6 15. Bh2 Re8 $14 { A fully comfortable, equal-feeling position for Black. [Engine verdict: close to level (around +0.3 to +0.5), well within the normal range for this chapter - the central e5 break neutralises the extra tempo White spent on h3 just as effectively as the normal trading plan does elsewhere in this chapter.] } 16. Nd4 { White aims at your strong c4 knight and wants to remove it with Bxc4. } 16... Bd7 17. Bxc4 dxc4 18. Qd2 Re4 19. Rbe1 Rae8 20. Rxe4 Rxe4 { The trade doubles your pawns, but you get the e-file and queenside play, so it is level (about 0.0). }) (6. Nf3 { A third move order: develop the knight first and meet ...Bg4 with h3 only once Black actually plays it, rather than inserting the prophylactic pawn move unprompted. } 6... Bg4 7. h3 (7. O-O { White simply finishes development and puts the king away before the pin on f3 starts to matter. It is a very common move in practice (played in about 1240 thousand games), so you will meet it often. It carries no direct threat; White wants a calm position with the bishop on d3 aimed at your kingside and the possibility of Re1 and Bf4 later. } 7... Qc7 $10 { The queen steps to c7 so that it looks at h2 once the f3 knight moves, and it frees d8 for a rook. It also keeps your d5 pawn well supported without committing the e-pawn yet. Your plan is to answer h3 with ...Bh5, bring the rook to d8 or c8, play ...e6 and then ...Bd6 to swap off White's good bishop, and castle short. Avoid ...e5 too early: after dxe5 your pieces lose their coordination and White's bishops come alive. } 8. h3 Bh5 9. g3 { White gives the bishop on h5 nothing to attack but weakens the dark squares around h3 and f3, so do not hurry to take on f3. Keep the pin and look for activity. } 9... Ne4 { The knight uses the e4 outpost, eyes g3 and c3, and prepares to challenge the bishop on d3. } 10. Bf4 Qd7 { The queen leaves the diagonal of the bishop on f4 and keeps an eye on the kingside, while the knight stays strong on e4. } 11. c4 Nf6 { After White breaks in the centre, you retreat and keep everything protected. Overall: you are fine and equal here, and your simple plan of ...e6, ...Bd6 and short castling gives a solid, comfortable game. } 12. Nc3 { This is a symmetrical Exchange structure with a closed-ish centre: White's pawn on d4 faces your pawn on d5, and neither side has a clear space edge. Both kings will most likely castle short. Your plan: first play ...e6 to give the queen's bishop a home and free the f8 bishop, then ...Bd6 to trade off the active white bishop on f4, then castle and put a rook on e8. White wants to break with c4 or push the e-pawn for a kingside attack, and you meet c4 by recapturing calmly with the knight on f6. The risk is a hasty ...Bxf3 or ...Nxd4 that loses time. If White deviates, simply keep developing. }) 7... Bh5 8. Bf4 e6 $10 { Transposes back toward the normal Exchange Variation structures covered throughout this chapter; nothing about this particular move order changes Black's basic plan of trading down the dark-squared pieces and racing for the standard breaks. } 9. O-O Bd6 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. Re1 O-O 12. Nbd2 Qc7 13. Qb1 Bg6 { You swap the dark bishops and then offer the light-bishop trade to avoid a cramped game. White's Qb1 is lined up behind the d3 bishop, but your bishop on g6 trades it off (about +0.2). }) 6... g6 $14 { Black aims for a Grunfeld-style structure. The bishop on g7 pressures d4, and Black can play on the kingside and in the centre, not only on the queenside. By engine standards this scores about the same as the quieter 6...e6, and 6...Bg4 is slightly better still, so 6...g6 is chosen for its structure and playability. } { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gg7g6] } 7. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } (7. h3! $14 { This is the move order that actually gives Black the hardest practical choice in the whole Bf4 plan - and it is the real answer to why some players find Bf4 so awkward to meet, rather than Bf4 itself being a poor idea. With ...Bg4 ruled out for good, White is simply a free tempo ahead of the main line below for the same eventual kingside expansion, and the question becomes which of Black's two main replies absorbs that extra tempo best. } 7... Bg7 (7... Bf5?! { Trading immediately looks natural, but with h3 already in, White's attacking chances on the kingside are simply better than in the normal main line (compare 9...Bf5 below), so this needs to be handled very precisely. } 8. Bxf5 gxf5 9. g4! $15 { The point of h3: this pawn storm now comes with full support and real venom, since Black's doubled f-pawns are a long-term target rather than just a structural blemish. } (9. Nf3 { Quieter, and lets Black untangle comfortably. } 9... e6 10. Nbd2 Bd6 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. Qe2 O-O-O $10 { Fully equal; Black's king is perfectly safe on the queenside. } 13. Ne5 { White's idea is the g4 break: with the knight on e5 supporting it, g4 hits f5 and opens the g-file. } (13. O-O-O Ne4 14. Kb1 h5 15. h4 Qf4 16. Nxe4 fxe4 17. Ng5 Rde8 { Castling long gives the same balance (about -0.1). }) 13... Qc7 14. g4 Nxe5 15. Qxe5 Ne4 16. gxf5 Qxe5 17. dxe5 exf5 { You centralise with ...Ne4, trade queens, and the attack disappears; the ending is level (about 0.0). }) (9. Nd2 { This is also a fine response. } 9... Bg7 10. Ne2 O-O 11. g4 fxg4 12. hxg4 Nxg4 13. Nf3 Qd7 14. Qd3 f5 15. Nh2 Nxh2 16. Rxh2 $14 { Black has won a pawn, but White's development gives more than enough compensation here. } 16... Rf7 { Your pawn is real, so guard it: the rook on f7 covers h7 and the seventh rank against the rook lift on h2 and the open g- and h-files (about +1.1 by the numbers, because White is active). } (16... Rf6?! 17. O-O-O Re8 18. Qh3 Rg6 19. Rg1 Kf7 20. Rhg2 Rxg2 21. Qxg2 { Too passive: White takes the g-file (about +2.1). }) 17. O-O-O e5 18. dxe5 Nxe5 19. Qh3 Bf6 20. Rg2+ Kh8 21. Be3 { ...e5 trades off White's central pawn and attacks the bishop; you stay a pawn up and White has no clear break. }) 9... Rg8 { The critical defence: before anything else, Black attacks the g4-pawn down the open file rather than trying to grab material right away. } (9... Qb6?! { Tempting, since b2 is hanging, but it lets White's attack run ahead unchecked. } 10. Nd2 Qxb2 11. Ne2 O-O-O 12. a4! $14 { White's initiative on the kingside and in the centre is now faster than Black's extra pawn is worth. } 12... Qb6 13. gxf5 Nh5 14. Rb1 Qa6 15. Bh2 Rg8 $14 { Black is a pawn up but passively placed and still has real defensive work to do - this is a harder version of the position than simply developing normally. } 16. Rb5 { The rook hits b7 and d5, trying to win the pawn back. } (16. Nf3 e6 17. Qb3 Na5 18. Qb5 Qxb5 19. Rxb5 Nc4 20. fxe6 fxe6 { White offers a queen trade, which suits the side with the extra pawn (about -0.3). }) 16... e6 17. Kf1 Bd6 18. Bxd6 Rxd6 19. Rg1 Rxg1+ 20. Kxg1 Rd8 { Trading the active pieces helps you: the extra pawn is easy to hold once White's attackers are gone, and White has lost the right to castle (about -0.3). }) (9... fxg4?! 10. hxg4 Qb6 11. Nd2 Qxb2 12. g5! $14 { Gains a key tempo by hitting the f6-knight, and White's attack again outruns Black's extra pawn. } 12... Ne4 13. Nxe4 dxe4 14. Ne2 e5 15. dxe5 Bg7 16. Qb1 Qxb1+ 17. Rxb1 b6 $14 { A playable but somewhat uncomfortable ending for Black, a pawn up but with the worse structure and king safety. } 18. Ng3 { White wants to win the pawn on e4 at once. } (18. e6 fxe6 19. Be3 Rc8 20. Rh4 Ne7 21. Rxe4 Kf7 22. Rd1 Rhd8 { Breaking up your structure with e6 is another try, but your king reaches f7 in time (about 0.0). }) 18... O-O 19. Nxe4 Nxe5 20. Bxe5 Bxe5 21. f4 f5 22. gxf6 Bxf6 { Castling gets the king safe. Both sides regain a pawn, so your extra pawn disappears and the position is level (about +0.1). }) 10. gxf5 Qd7 { Black simply recaptures next and keeps development going; the rook on g8 has already done its defensive job. } 11. Qc2 Ne4 12. Nd2 Qxf5 13. Ne2 e6 14. Nxe4 dxe4 (14... Qxe4?! { Centralising the queen looks natural, but it walks into a trade that favours White's structure. } 15. Qxe4 dxe4 16. O-O-O Ne7 17. Rdg1 Rxg1+ 18. Rxg1 $14 { White has the safer king and the better minor piece for the ending. } 18... f5 { This supports the e4 pawn, which cramps White. White's trump is the better minor piece, so fix the pawn on e4 and bring your king to f7. } (18... Nd5 19. Rg4 f5 20. Rh4 h6 21. Bxh6 Be7 22. Rh5 Kf7 23. Be3 { Also level (about 0.0). }) 19. Bd2 Kf7 20. Kc2 Bg7 21. Rf1 Rg8 22. f3 exf3 23. Rxf3 { White's only lever is f3, and after the trade the position is close to equal (about -0.2). }) 15. Be3 $14 { Objectively Black is fine here with precise play (the engine puts this only a little worse than equal, well inside the normal range for the chapter), but this whole 7...Bf5 approach against h3 is clearly the sharper, more demanding path - which is exactly why the central-development plan below is the recommended answer instead. } 15... Qf3 { The rook on h1 and the knight on e2 are attacked together. White must castle long so that the rook on d1 covers h1, and Black can castle long as well. } 16. O-O-O O-O-O 17. Kb1 Bd6 18. c4 Nb4 19. Qd2 Kb8 20. d5 { White's c4 and d5 advance tries to open lines against your king; with the king on b8 and the bishop on d6 covering e5, you are fine (about +0.4). }) 8. Nf3 { Transposing back to the normal developing scheme, just with h3 already banked. } 8... O-O 9. Nbd2 Nd7! $14 { The cleaner practical answer to the h3 move order: rather than trade light-squared bishops into White's now-supported g4 push, Black keeps the bishop on c8 for now and heads straight for the standard central break with ...e5, matching the plan already given above in the pure 6.h3 line. } (9... Bf5 10. Bxf5 gxf5 11. g4 $14 { The same idea as above: with h3 already played, this pawn storm has real teeth against the doubled f-pawns. It's still playable for Black, just unnecessarily sharp when the calmer plan is sitting right there. } 11... Qb6 { Hit b2 and force the knight to b3: with h3 already played the pawn storm has teeth, so counter-attack rather than wait. } (11... e6 12. Rg1 Ne7 13. gxf5 exf5 14. Bh6 Ng6 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 16. Ne5 { Too slow: White gets the g-file and the e5 knight (about +0.9). }) 12. Nb3 a5 13. Qc2 Ne4 14. gxf5 a4 15. Nbd2 a3 16. Rb1 { Your queenside pawns and the e4 knight give you your own initiative (about +0.7). }) 10. O-O e5 11. dxe5 Ndxe5 12. Bb5 { The most testing try, pinning the newly-arrived knight before it settles on e5. } (12. Nxe5 Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Bxe5 14. Nf3 Bf6 15. Qb3 Be6 $10 { A completely level position; the trade of dark-squared pieces has simplified matters nicely for Black. } 16. Rad1 { White puts the rook on the d-file against your d5 pawn and prepares Nd4 and Qxb7. } (16. Qxb7 Qd6 17. Qb3 Rab8 18. Qc2 Qf4 19. Nh2 Rb6 20. Rab1 a5 { An immediate pawn grab gives you the b-file and a small plus (about -0.2). }) 16... Qc7 17. Nd4 Bxd4 18. cxd4 Qf4 19. Qxb7 Rfb8 20. Qc6 a5 { White wins a pawn, but your rooks and queen are active and White's king is exposed, so the position is level (about +0.1). }) 12... Nxf3+ (12... a6!? 13. Bxc6 Nxc6 14. Nb3 h6 15. Re1 Qf6 16. Bg3 Bf5 $14 (16... Be6 $14 17. Nc5 { White's knight attacks the bishop on e6 and the pawn on b7, aiming to double your pawns. } 17... Rfe8 18. Nxe6 fxe6 19. Re2 Qf7 20. Ne5 Qf5 21. Nxc6 bxc6 { White's knights are traded off for your bishop and knight, and your structure comes out the worse. That is why White keeps a clear edge (about +1.2). }) 17. Nbd4 { White wants to trade your active bishop or win the d5 pawn. } (17. Qxd5 Rad8 18. Qc5 Bxh3 19. Nfd2 Bf5 20. Qb6 h5 21. Qxb7 Na5 { White grabs a pawn, but you get activity and a rook on the d-file (about +0.5). }) 17... Be4 18. Nxc6 bxc6 19. Nd2 Qf5 20. Nxe4 dxe4 21. Qe2 Qb5 { You have an isolated c6 pawn, but the e4 pawn and active queen keep the balance (about +0.5). }) (12... d4!? { The most ambitious try, offering a pawn to open lines before White's pieces settle. } 13. cxd4 (13. Bxe5 Nxe5 14. Nxd4 a6 15. Be2 Rb8 16. Nc4 Nxc4 17. Bxc4 b5 18. Bb3 Qf6 $14 { Roughly balanced; Black's bishop pair compensates for the slightly loose queenside pawns. } 19. Re1 Bb7 20. Qe2 b4 21. Rad1 Rfd8 22. Rd3 Bd5 23. Bxd5 Rxd5 { Black plays ...b4 to open lines against c3 and trades a bishop to relieve the cramped queenside; the knight on d4 and the e-file give White a slight initiative (about +0.3). }) (13. Bxc6 Nxc6 14. cxd4 Nxd4 15. Nxd4 Qxd4 $10 16. Qf3 { White protects the bishop and hits b7 at the same time. } 16... Qxb2 17. Ne4 Bf5 18. Nd6 Be6 19. a3 b6 20. Rad1 Rad8 { You are a pawn up with the bishop pair; White's knight on d6 is annoying but your pieces cover the key squares (about -0.8). }) 13... Nxf3+ 14. Nxf3 Nxd4 15. Nxd4 Qxd4 16. Qxd4 Bxd4 $10 { Trades down to a dead-equal position. } 17. Be2 Be6 18. Bf3 Rad8 19. Bxb7 Bxb2 20. Rab1 Bc3 21. a3 Bf5 { The queens are off and both sides have bishops. White takes b7, you take b2, and the position stays level (about 0.0). }) 13. Nxf3 a6 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Re1 Be6 16. Qd2 c5 $14 { A fully typical, comfortable position for Black - the engine has this around +0.3 to +0.5, squarely in the normal range for this chapter, showing that the ...Nd7-and-...e5 plan neutralises the extra tempo White invests in h3 just as reliably as the main line below does without it. } 17. Bh6 { White wants to remove your g7 bishop, your main king defender. } (17. Be5 a5 18. Re2 Re8 19. Rae1 Bf5 20. Bxg7 Rxe2 21. Qxe2 Kxg7 { Trading dark bishops instead is also fine for you (about +0.4). }) 17... Re8 18. Re2 Qd6 19. Rae1 Bd7 20. Bf4 Rxe2 21. Rxe2 Qc6 { Rather than avoiding the bishop trade you simply meet it, then trade rooks on the e-file (about +0.5). }) 7... Bg7 { [%csl Gg7][%cal Gf8g7] } 8. Nbd2 { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } 8... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 9. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 9... Bf5 $14 { Black provokes the trade on f5. It costs a little compared with the engine's first choices 9...Bg4, 9...Qb6 and 9...Nh5, but it leads to clear, well-understood positions. } { [%csl Rf5,Rb5,Re2][%cal Gc8f5,Rd3f5,Rd3b5,Rd3e2,Rd1e2] } (9... Nh5 $14 { One of the engine's first choices. It attacks the bishop on f4. } 10. Be3! $14 { Be3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (10. Bg3? $10 { Bg3 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 10... Nxg3 11. hxg3 Bg4 $10 { White gives up the bishop pair for nothing. Black is fine. } 12. Re1 { Rook to the e-file, eyeing e7 and the centre. } (12. Be2 h5 13. Re1 e6 14. Nh2 Bf5 15. g4 hxg4 16. Nxg4 Bxg4 { Retreating the bishop lets you keep playing for ...h5-h4 (about -0.2). }) 12... h5 { With your bishop on g4, ...h5-h4 will prise open the h-file against White's king. } 13. Bb5 Qd6 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Nb3 Bxf3 16. gxf3 e5 { White spends his bishop to double your c-pawns, but his own kingside is wrecked and your centre pawns roll (about -0.2). }) (10. Be5? $17 { Be5 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 10... f6! $17 { Black wins the initiative and gets a clear advantage. } 11. Bg3 e5 $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.2).] } 12. dxe5 { If White does not take, ...e4 hits the knight and gains space. } (12. Bh4 e4 13. Nxe4 dxe4 14. Bxe4 Ne7 15. Re1 Nf5 16. Qb3+ Kh8 { Giving a knight for two pawns and lines against c6 and your king is tempting but unsound; you keep the extra piece (about -1.6). }) 12... fxe5 13. Ne1 Kh8 14. c4 Nb4 15. Be2 Nxg3 16. hxg3 e4 { Your knight on h5 hits g3, so White cannot keep the bishop, and after the trade your e4 pawn and the g7 bishop dominate (about -1.2). }) 10... Qc7 11. a4 { [%csl Rf4][%cal Rc7f4,Rh5f4] } 11... b6 12. Re1 Bb7 13. h3 e6 14. Nb3 a5 15. Qd2 Ba6 $14 { A fully typical position for this whole chapter: White's edge is the bishop pair and a touch more space, nothing concrete. [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.56), the same normal range given at the top of this chapter.] Black's plan from here is simple and reliable: ...Rfc8, ...Nb6xc4 trading off White's good bishop, or ...f5 to gain central space once the knight on h5 has done its job of provoking Be3. There is nothing to fear in this line; it just needs a touch more patience than the main 9...Bf5 trade. } 16. Nc1 { White offers to trade off the active bishop: after Bxd3 the knight sits well on d3, and g4 will drive away the knight on h5. } (16. Bxa6 Rxa6 17. Nc1 Raa8 18. Nd3 Rfe8 19. Nh2 Nf6 20. Bf4 Qd8 { Taking on a6 gives the same small edge (about +0.6). }) 16... Bxd3 17. Nxd3 Rac8 18. g4 Nf6 19. Bf4 Qb7 20. Qe3 Rfe8 { Your knight simply returns to f6 and White has no concrete target (about +0.6). }) (9... Qb6 $14 { Qb6 leads to a clear White edge (+0.8), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 10. Nb3 $14 { White's best. } 10... a5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7).] } 11. Nc5 { The knight lands on an outpost and hits b7 and e6; White also invites ...Qxb2, a pawn grab that costs you time while White develops. } 11... Bg4 12. h3 Bxf3 13. Qxf3 Qxb2 14. Rfc1 e5 { ...e5 is your freeing break: open the position before White can use the loose queen (about +0.7). } 15. dxe5 Nh5 { The knight hits the bishop on f4. }) 10. Bxf5 $14 { White's best and the critical test. Black recaptures with the g-pawn. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gd3f5] } (10. Be2 $14 { White keeps the bishop pair but loses time. Black gets easy development. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd3e2] } 10... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 11. h3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 11... g5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bg6g5] } 12. Bg3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bf4g3] } 12... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 13. Ne5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 13... Rc8 $14 { Black's kingside is weakened, but in return Black has active pieces. White keeps a modest edge, so this is playable rather than a refutation. } { [%csl Bc8][%cal Ba8c8] } 14. Nxc6 { White trades your active knight and then fixes the queenside with a4-a5. } (14. a4 Nxe5 15. Bxe5 Nd7 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. a5 Qc7 { If White waits you trade a pair of minor pieces and your position relaxes. }) 14... Rxc6 15. a4 a6 16. a5 Re8 17. Bh2 Ne4 18. Re1 Bf8 $14 { Your kingside pawns are looser (g5 and h6), so White will aim at them with h4 and the bishop on h2. You answer with the strong Ne4 and ...Bf8-d6 to trade off the bishops. }) (10. Bb5?! $10 { This pin merely loses a tempo, since White must either trade off the bishop pair or pull the bishop back. It is the line recommended by WGM Sopiko Guramishvili. } { [%csl Bb5,Bb6][%cal Bd3b5,Bd8b6] } 10... Qb6 11. Qa4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Bd1a4] } 11... a6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Ba7a6] } 12. Bxc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb5c6] } 12... bxc6 $10 { Black has the bishop pair and is at least equal. } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb7c6] } 13. Qb3 { White offers the queen trade to cash in the doubled pawns, since your c-pawns are your only weakness. } 13... Nd7 14. Rfe1 Rfe8 15. h3 h5 16. Re3 Qxb3 17. axb3 f6 $10 { After the trade you have the bishop pair and a strong centre. ...f6 and then ...e5 free your pieces, so your bishops soon take over. }) (10. Ne5 $10 { White's knight jump. Black simply takes it. } 10... Nxe5 11. Bxf5 { Bxf5 leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White retains the bishop pair. } (11. Bxe5? $19 { Bxe5 leads to a clear Black edge (-5.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 11... Bxd3! $19 { The knight on d2 blocks the queen, so White cannot recapture on d3. Black wins a piece. } { [%cal Rd1d3] } 12. Re1 { White has no way to win the piece back, so he looks for activity on the e-file. } (12. Bxf6 Bxf6 13. Re1 a5 14. a4 Bf5 15. Nf1 Bc8 16. Ne3 Be6 { Trading bishops first changes nothing (about -5.4). }) 12... a5 13. a4 Bf5 14. h3 Ne8 15. Bh2 Bc8 16. Qe2 Bf6 { Retreat the bishop, drive away the pressure with ...Ne8 and ...Bf6, and keep the extra piece (about -5.1). }) 11... Nc4! $14 { The knight escapes with tempo. } (11... gxf5? $14 { Recapturing on f5 is the natural move, but it costs about 0.6 to 0.7 compared with 11...Nc4!, which comes first. } 12. dxe5 $16 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+1.0). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 12... Nd7 { Hit the wedge on e5 straight away: White wants to keep that pawn as a spearhead and use Qh5 and the bishop on f4 against your doubled f-pawns (about +0.9). } (12... Ng4? 13. Nf3 Kh8 14. Qd2 f6 15. e6 Ne5 16. Nd4 Qb6 17. Rfe1 { Active-looking, but the knight is offside and White's e6 thrust breaks up your centre (about +2.7). }) 13. Re1 e6 14. Qh5 f6 15. Nf3 Rc8 16. Rad1 Qb6 17. exf6 { ...f6 is your lever against the e5 wedge; White has to resolve the tension. }) 12. Nxc4 dxc4 13. Bc2 b5 $14 { Black is fine. } 14. Qe2 { Bc2 already eyes g6, so White plans Qe2 or Qf3 and then h4-h5 to open the h-file. } 14... Rc8 15. a3 e6 16. h4 h5 { ...h5 shuts the h-pawn down. Your own trumps are the c4 pawn, which cramps White, and the b5 chain; follow with ...a5 and ...b4. } 17. Rfe1 Qb6 18. Qf3 a5 { You are comfortable (about +0.3). }) (10. Nb3 $14 { It avoids the trade on f5, but Black gets an easy game. } 10... Nh5 11. Be3 Bxd3 12. Qxd3 b6 $14 { Black has no problems. } 13. Qe2 Re8 14. Rad1 e6 15. Rfe1 a5 { White has parked the knight on b3 and keeps developing slowly. You play ...a5-a4 to push it back and complete the plan with ...e6 and ...Nf6. } 16. Nc1 Nf6 17. Bg5 Ne7 $10 { Your knight comes back to the centre. White's knight heads for d3 and the dark bishops come off. The position is balanced (about +0.3). }) (10. Qe2 $10 { The queen gets in the way of the rook. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 10... Bxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf5d3] } 11. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Be2d3] } 11... e6 { [%csl Be6,Bd5][%cal Be7e6,Bf7d5] } 12. h3 $14 { White's best. White is slightly better, but Black is fine. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } (12. Ne5?! $15 { This looks tempting, but it does not win a piece. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 12... Nh5! $15 { The knight attacks the bishop on f4 and gains time. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bf6h5] } 13. Nxc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Be5c6] } 13... Nxf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bh5f4] } 14. Qf3 { [%csl Bf3,Bg5,Bc7][%cal Bd3f3,Bd8g5,Bd8c7] } 14... Qg5! { Qg5 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bg5g2,Bg4g2,Bf4g2] } (14... Qc7 $15 { Also playable, but 14...Qg5! is stronger. } { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 15. Nb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bc6b4] } 15... a5 { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 16. Nd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bb4d3] } 16... Nxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf4d3] } 17. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf3d3] } 17... b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 18. Nb3 { Nb3 leads to a clear Black edge (-0.8), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd2b3] } (18. a4 $15 { a4 leads to a small Black edge (-0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba2a4] } 18... b4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb5b4] } 19. cxb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bc3b4] } 19... Qb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bc7b6] } 20. b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb4b5] } 20... Qxd4 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.2). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the b-file.] [%csl Bd4][%cal Bb6d4] } 21. Qxd4 { Other queen moves, such as Qe3, leave you clearly better (about -1.2), so White has to trade. } 21... Bxd4 22. Rac1 Bxb2 23. Rc2 Ba3 24. Nb3 Bb4 25. Nd4 Rfb8 $10 { You pick up b2 but the bishop gets harassed by Rc2 and the knight settles on d4. Now hit the b5 pawn with ...Rfb8: the position is balanced (about 0.0), so play for a draw safely or look for small chances. }) 18... b4 $15 { Black has enough queenside counterplay. } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb5b4] } 19. Nc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bb3c5] } 19... Rfc8 { [%csl Bc8][%cal Bg8c8] } 20. f4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bf2f4] } 20... bxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb4c3] } 21. bxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb2c3] } 21... Bf8 { [%csl Bf8][%cal Bg7f8] } 22. Na4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Bc5a4] } 22... Qd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bc7d6] } 23. Rab1 { [%csl Bb1][%cal Ba1b1] } 23... Qa3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Bd6a3] } 24. Qd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bd3d1] } 24... Ra7 { [%csl Ba7][%cal Ba8a7] } 25. Qb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd1b3] } 25... Rc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc8c4] } 26. Qb8 { [%csl Bb8][%cal Bb3b8] } 26... Rac7 $15 { The loose queenside pawns and the hanging knight give Black the upper hand. (Kiryushkin-Feofanov, Berdsk 2010, 0-1.) } { [%csl Bc7][%cal Ba7c7] } 27. Nc5 { The Na4 is attacked twice (by Rc4 and Qa3), so White jumps to c5, a square protected by the d4 pawn. } (27. f5? Qxa4 28. fxe6 Rxc3 { An f4-f5 attack looks tempting but the knight simply falls: about -3.2. }) 27... R4xc5 28. dxc5 Qxc5+ 29. Kh1 Rc8 30. Qb5 Qxc3 31. Qb2 Qe3 $17 { You gave a rook for a knight, but you collect two pawns and White's king is exposed, with the queen tied to its defence (about -0.7). }) 15. h4 { h4 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (15. Nb4 $19 { Nb4 leads to a clear Black edge (-2.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 15... Nxg2 16. Qxg2 Qxd2 $19 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.9).] } 17. Rab1 { The knight on b4 is attacked by your queen, so White first protects b2 and keeps its pieces together. } (17. Qg3? Qxb2 18. Rab1 Qa3 { Looking for a queen trade while leaving b2 hanging just loses the pawn and lets your queen into White's camp (about -3.7). }) 17... a5 18. Rfd1 Qf4 19. Nd3 Qf5 20. h3 Rfc8 21. Qg4 Qf6 $19 { The kick ...a5 sends the knight back, and your queen plus the c-file give you all the play. You are a clean pawn up (about -2.0). }) 15... Qxh4 { WATCH OUT: bxc6 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: hxg5 Rfb8 Qxf4 Rxb2 Rfc1 Rc8 } 16. Nb4 a5 $17 { Black has a clear advantage. } 17. Nd3 { White retreats the knight to d3 to trade off your strong Nf4. } (17. Na6 Rxa6 18. g3 Nh3+ 19. Kg2 Qh6 { A desperate knight sacrifice to open the b-file and lure your rook away. It fails because your queen and knight attack the king (about -4.0). }) 17... Nxd3 18. Qxd3 b5 19. Nf3 Qe4 { Offering the queen trade is the clean way to cash in the extra pawn. } 20. Qxe4 dxe4 21. Ng5 e3 $17 { The e3 pawn cramps White and the knight on g5 soon has few good squares (about -1.3). }) 12... Re8 { [%csl Be8][%cal Bf8e8] } 13. Rfe1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 13... Qb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 14. a4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba2a4] } 14... Nd7 $14 { A quiet improvement. White has a slightly more pleasant position, and nothing here changes the overall assessment. WATCH OUT: Qxb2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.6 from Black's own side of the board), after: Reb1 Qxa1 Rxa1 Rac8 Qb1 Ne4 } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bf6d7] } 15. Nf1 { White reroutes the knight via f1 and e3 to g4 to trade your Nf6, and a4-a5 may also chase the queen. } (15. b4 Qd8 16. Qb5 Qc8 17. a5 a6 18. Qd3 e5 { White expands with b4 and a5, so you retreat the queen and break in the centre with ...e5. }) 15... Rac8 16. Ne3 Nf6 17. Ng4 Ne4 { You meet the plan with ...Rac8 and ...Ne4, putting your knight on a strong blockade square. } 18. Re2 Re7 19. Rae1 Qa6 $14 { A normal middlegame where White is a little freer (about +0.3). }) 10... gxf5 $14 { Black gives up the light-squared bishop and doubles the f-pawns, but the pawns control e4 and g4 and the open g-file gives Black kingside play. Mueller-Kieninger, Beverwijk 1965, was drawn: Black kept the position solid and White could not make progress. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gg6f5] } 11. Ne5 $14 { White's best. The knight takes the e5 square and prepares to trade on c6. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gf3e5] } 11... e6 $14 { The main line and the engine's top choice right here: +0.69, a touch tighter than 11...Nxe5's +0.93, and clearly ahead of 11...Qb6 and 11...Kh8 below. Black keeps the knight on c6 for the moment and meets 12.Nxc6 with ...bxc6, taking doubled c-pawns in return for the open b-file and the bishop pair. White has a wide menu of tries here: 12.Nxc6, 12.Ndf3, 12.Re1, 12.Nd3, 12.Bg5 and 12.Qe2 are all close together, plus the weaker 12.Qf3!? and 12.f3?!, but none of them change the picture much. White keeps a steady, comfortable +0.6 to +0.8 here, never more, the normal range for this whole chapter. } (11... Nxe5 $14 { Playable and close (+0.93, just a touch more than 11...e6's +0.69), so treat 11...e6 above as the tighter recommendation and this as the sound backup. (If you see an older note here claiming Black has a material edge, ignore it: that was just the snapshot mid-recapture, White retakes immediately below and material is level again.) } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gc6e5] } 12. Bxe5 { [%csl Ge5,Re6,Rh8][%cal Gf4e5,Re7e6,Rg8h8] } (12. dxe5 $16 { Also strong (+0.99, essentially matching 12.Bxe5), but it trades the solid d4 point for a pawn on e5 that needs long-term support instead of keeping the bishop actively posted there. 12.Bxe5 stays the simpler recommendation. } 12... Nd7 { The engine's only real try, keeping the structure together. } 13. Re1 e6 14. Qh5 f6 15. Nf3 Qb6 16. Re2 fxe5 17. Bxe5 Bxe5 18. Nxe5 Nxe5 19. Rxe5 Rf6 20. Qe2 Rg6 21. Rd1 Rc8 22. g3 Rc4 23. Re1 Re4 $16 { White keeps a steady +0.8 through this whole sequence, the same modest, nagging edge as everywhere else in this chapter and never more concrete than that. } 24. Qd2 { White protects b2 and prepares the simplifying trade on e4, after which Qh6 pesters the king. } (24. Qh5?! Qxb2 25. R5xe4 fxe4 26. Qe5 Kf7 27. Qc7+ Kg8 28. Qd8+ Kf7 { Hitting the rook with Qh5 looks attractive, but you take b2 and White only has perpetual check (about 0.0). }) 24... Rgg4 25. R5xe4 Rxe4 26. Rxe4 dxe4 27. b3 Qc7 28. Qh6 Qd6 $14 { You keep the position safe by trading one pair of rooks and holding the king. White's edge is only small (about +0.9). }) 12... e6 $14 { Black's structure is solid. Typical Black plans: ...Kh8 and ...Rg8 to use the g-file, ...Ne4 and ...f6 to challenge the bishop on e5, and ...a5 to gain queenside space. Typical White plans: keep the bishop on e5 or f4, bring the knight to f3 and expand with a4-a5. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } (12... Kh8 $14 { A useful waiting move that prepares ...Rg8 and a direct kingside plan. It is as good as 12...e6. } 13. Re1 { Re1 leads to a modest White edge (+0.6): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } (13. Nb3 $14 { Nb3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.6): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } 13... e6 14. Bf4 $14 { White's most testing move here. } (14. Nc1 $14 { Nc1 leads to a modest White edge (+0.6): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } 14... Rg8 15. Nd3 h6 16. g3 Kh7 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 17. Kh1 { White steps off the g-file so that your Rg8 has nothing to hit, and now f3 and g4 are possible. } 17... Bf8 18. Qb3 b6 19. f3 Bd6 { You trade off the dark bishop, which is the best defender of White's centre. } 20. Qc2 Ne8 21. g4 Bxe5 $14 { White's plan is f3 and g4 to break your f5 pawn and open the position. A modest White edge remains (about +0.7), but your structure holds. }) 14... Nd7 { Nd7 clears f6 for the queen and keeps e5 under watch, ready for Qh5 and the Nc5 jump. } 15. Qh5 Qf6 { The queen protects f5 while the rooks wait. } 16. Rae1 Rg8 17. Na5 b6 18. Nc6 Qg6 19. Qh3 $14 { The knight lands on c6 for a moment, but you answer by offering the queens. You are slightly worse (about +0.7) but safe, with your doubled f-pawns firmly protected. }) 13... e6 14. Bf4 $14 { Bf4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.7): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } (14. Qb3 $14 { This attacks b7 but is not White's best. } 14... b6! $14 { Simple and good. It defends b7 and leaves White with little. } (14... Qe7 $14 { Qe7 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } 15. Rad1 { Rad1 leads to a small White edge (+0.3): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } (15. g3 $14 { g3 leads to a small White edge (+0.4): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } 15... Rg8 16. a4 Ne4 17. Bf4 { Bf4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } (17. Bxg7+ $10 { Bxg7+ leads to roughly equal (-0.1): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s); Black has doubled pawns. } 17... Rxg7 18. Nxe4 dxe4 19. Rad1 Rag8 $16 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+1.0). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the e/f-file.] } 20. d5 b6 { A compact move that stops ideas of Qc4-c7. } 21. Qb5 h5 22. dxe6 Qxe6 23. Rd5 a6 24. Qd7 Rg6 $14 { White uses the d5 break to open lines and activate the rooks. Your e- and f-pawns are doubled, so keep ...Rg6 and ...h5 going to harass the king. About +0.9. }) 17... Bf6 $14 { Black stacks the rooks, trades bishops and then attacks the white king. } { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bg8g7,Ba8g8,Bf6g5] } 18. Nxe4 dxe4 19. d5 e5 20. Bd2 Rg6 21. c4 h5 22. Qd1 Kg7 $14 { White trades knights and pushes d5, which blocks your bishop. You answer with ...e5 and bring the rook to g6 and play ...h5, aiming at the white king. Meanwhile White's pawn majority on the queenside gives him the long-term edge (about +0.5). }) 15... Rg8 16. c4 Rad8 17. c5 Ne4 18. Bf4 Qh4 19. Qe3 Bf6 $14 { White closes the position, but the kingside attack stays on the board. } 20. Nf3 Qh3 { White hits your queen, so it goes to h3 where it keeps an eye on g2. } 21. g3 Bg7 22. Ng5 Nxg5 23. Bxg5 Rde8 24. Bf4 h5 $14 { White closed the centre with c5 to reduce your play, then trades the knights. You still have ...h5-h4 for the attack, but White's pieces are well placed for defence (about +1.1). }) (14... Nh5 $14 { A direct try, but it gives White a slightly bigger edge than 14...b6. } 15. Bxg7+ Kxg7 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 16. Nf3 b6 17. g3 Nf6 18. Qd1 Rh8 19. Qe2 h5 20. Nh4 Rc8 $14 { After the bishops come off White aims the knight at f5 via h4, so you bring the h5 knight back with ...Nf6 and protect f5 with the rooks. The edge is small (about +0.6). }) 15. g3 Rg8 16. Rac1 Qf8 $14 { Black is solid and White has little to show for the queen move. } 17. c4 Rd8 { Your rook supports d5 before it is attacked. } 18. Qa4 Ne4 19. Bxg7+ Qxg7 20. Nxe4 dxe4 21. Qxa7 Rxd4 $14 { White grabs a7 but has to allow ...Rxd4 with active rooks. The dark bishops have disappeared and you have good counterplay (about +0.4). }) (14. a4 $14 { Seen in Rasmussen-Sapis, Leningrad 1983. } 14... Rg8 15. Qb3 Nh5 { Nh5 leads to roughly equal (+0.1): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } (15... Ne4 $10 { Ne4 leads to roughly equal (+0.2): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } 16. Nxe4 { White trades knights first so that your central pawn mass is the target. } (16. Bxg7+ Rxg7 17. Nxe4 fxe4 18. Re3 f5 19. Rg3 Rc7 20. f4 Qd6 { Taking on g7 gives you the g-file and the easier game (about -0.4). }) 16... Bxe5 17. dxe5 fxe4 18. c4 Qg5 19. g3 Qxe5 20. cxd5 exd5 $10 { White's queen on b3 watches b7 and d5, so he opens the c-file, but you win the e5 pawn and your central pawns are mobile. Everything is level (about 0.0). }) 16. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } (16. Bxg7+ $15 { Bxg7+ leads to a modest Black edge (-0.7): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s); Black has doubled pawns. (the engine edge for Black coexists with White's extra material here because this is a known pawn-grab-for-compensation pattern in this line, not a tactical oversight - Black has concrete compensation: faster development, open lines, or attacking chances that outweigh the missing pawn(s)) } 16... Rxg7 17. Qxb7 Rb8 18. Qxa7 Rxb2 $16 { Black's rooks and queen invade quickly. } { [%csl Bb2,Bd8,Bf2,Bg2,Bg5,Bg7,Bh2,Bh4,Bh5][%cal Bb2h2,Bg7g1,Bd8h4,Bd8g5] } 19. Reb1 { This is the only move that keeps White clearly ahead (about +1.9): it hits the rook on b2 at once, while the queen on a7 is safe as long as your f7 pawn blocks the seventh rank. } (19. Rab1? Qg5 20. Qa8+ Rg8 21. Qxg8+ Qxg8 22. Rxb2 Nf4 { Qg5 hits d2 and g2 together, and White ends up giving the queen for two rooks (about -0.5). }) 19... Nf4 20. g3 Nh3+ 21. Kg2 Nf4+ 22. Kf1 f6 { Once the f-pawn moves, your Rg7 attacks the queen. } 23. Qxg7+ Kxg7 $18 { White gives the queen but wins the rook on b2 next, and two rooks and a knight against queen and knight leave White clearly better. }) 16... Bxe5 17. Nxe5 Rg7 18. g3 f4 19. Kh1?! $15 { A mistake: Black is already slightly better. } (19. c4 $10 { c4 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc3c4] } 19... fxg3 $15 { About equal. 19.c4 is a better try than 19.Kh1, but Black is fine after 19...fxg3 or 19...f6. } 20. hxg3 { White simply takes the pawn, and the Nh5 and Rg7 now press on g3. } (20. Nxf7+? Rxf7 21. fxg3 dxc4 22. Qxc4 Ng7 23. Rad1 Rf5 24. d5 exd5 { The knight sacrifice looks like a trick, but you simply take, finish the pawn grab, and stay clearly better (about -3.8). }) 20... Qg8 21. Kf1 Nxg3+ 22. fxg3 Rxg3 23. Qxb7 Rb8 24. Nxf7+ Kg7 $10 { Both sides have chances. Your rooks and queen swarm on g3, so White grabs b7 and uses the knight to hit f7 (about 0.0). }) 19... Qg5 $15 { Black is ready to unleash a strong attack on White's kingside. } 20. Qd1 fxg3 21. fxg3 f6 22. Nf3 Qh6 23. Kg2 Nxg3 24. hxg3 Rag8 25. Kf2 Rxg3! $19 { Black converted. (Rasmussen-Sapis, Leningrad 1983, 0-1.) } 26. Rg1 { White trades off the rook on g3 to relieve the pressure, but it does not help. } (26. Nh4 Qxh4 27. Qf3 Rg2+ 28. Ke3 Qh6+ 29. Kd3 Rd2# { Tempting, but it walks into a mating net. }) 26... Rxg1 27. Nxg1 Qh4+ 28. Kf1 Qh1 29. Ke2 Qg2+ 30. Ke3 Qe4+ $19 { Your queen and rook have the exposed king in a net and White cannot hold the knight on g1 (about -4.9). }) 14... Rg8 15. Nf3 Qf8 $14 { Black is solid and White keeps a small edge. } 16. Ne5 Ne4 17. Re2 Rc8 18. f3 Nf6 19. Qd2 Nh5 20. Be3 Qe7 $14 { White's knight on e5 is supported by the bishop on f4 and the d4 pawn. When f3 kicks your Ne4, you swing the knight to h5 to hit the bishop and then trade queens or regroup. White has only a small edge (about +0.7). }) 13. Nf3 $14 { White's best. 13.Nb3, 13.Bf4 and 13.Qe2 are about as good. Black is solid and White's edge is the better structure, not an attack. } (13. a4 $14 { Natural, but not White's most accurate move. } { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga2a4] } 13... Kh8 { [%csl Gh8][%cal Gg8h8] } 14. Bxf6?! $14 { This trades White's good bishop for a knight and gives up part of the edge. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge5f6] } 14... Bxf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg7f6] } 15. Re1 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gf1e1] } 15... Rg8 { [%csl Gg8][%cal Gf8g8] } 16. Qh5?! $10 { Loses most of the remaining edge. } { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gd1h5] } 16... Rg6 { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gg8g6] } 17. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gd2f3] } 17... Qg8 $10 { White now has only two good moves, 18.g3 and 18.Qh3. The natural 18.Kh1? is a mistake: Black is clearly better. } { [%csl Rg8,Rg2][%cal Gd8g8,Rg8g2] } 18. g3 { The point of g3 is to shut the g-file battery of your Qg8 and Rg6 against g2 and to leave the king safe. } (18. Qh3 Qg7 19. Ne5 Bxe5 20. Rxe5 Rg8 21. g3 Rh6 22. Qg2 Qg4 { Qh3 is the other correct move, attacking your pawn on f5. }) 18... Qg7 19. Ne5 Bxe5 20. Rxe5 Rg4 21. Qh3 Rg8 22. Rae1 Qf6 $10 { You keep the g-file and the f5 pawn together, and White's knight has gone. The position is close to equal (about +0.1). }) 13... Ne4 $14 { Black offers the trade of White's strong bishop. } 14. Bf4 $14 { White keeps the bishop. } 14... a5 $14 { Black gains queenside space and prepares ...f6. } (14... Nxc3?? $18 { Do not grab material on c3 or f2. White recaptures and Black has lost a piece for a pawn. The knight on e4 is a strength, not a target. } 15. bxc3 Qd7 16. Bd2 Kh8 $18 { [Engine verdict: winning for White (+5.0). Notable features: White is 2 points of material up; Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 17. Re1 Rac8 18. g3 Rfe8 { You are a piece down for a pawn, since the knight on c3 was lost. White simply consolidates with Re1 and g3 and keeps trading, because your doubled f-pawns and king are not worth the piece. } 19. Ne5 Bxe5 20. Rxe5 Rg8 21. Re3 Rg4 $18 { You can pile up on the g-file, but White covers everything (about +4.8). }) 15. a4 f6 16. g3 Qd7 $14 { A typical setup. Black has no weaknesses, and White keeps a small edge. The bishop on f4 has to guard e5, and the doubled f-pawns cover e4 and g4. } 17. Ne1 { White steers the knight to d3 so that e5 and f4 are covered. } 17... e5 { Do not wait: ...e5 hits the Bf4 and the d4 pawn and activates your bishop. } 18. Be3 Kh8 19. Qh5 Rg8 20. f4 exd4 21. Bxd4 Rgb8 $14 { White opens lines against your king with Qh5 and f4, but ...exd4 and then ...Rb8 give you counterplay on the b-file. White keeps a small initiative (about +0.6). }) (11... Qb6?! $14 { Slightly inaccurate. White keeps about +0.9, a little more than after 11...Nxe5 or 11...e6. } 12. Nb3 $16 { Strong and simple (+0.80). 12.Nd3 is a touch more precise by the engine (+0.87) and 12.Rb1 is also fine; the choice between them barely matters in practice. } (12. Qf3?! $14 { Not White's best, but Black goes wrong later. } 12... Rac8 13. Rab1 Rfd8 14. Bg5 Nxe5 15. dxe5 Ne4 16. Qxf5 Nxg5 17. Qxg5 Qe6 18. Nf3 Rc4 19. h3 Re4 $16 { The kingside attack arrives too late for a side that is already a pawn down. (Commons-Denker, Lone Pine 1976, 1-0.) } 20. Rfe1 Rc8 21. Qd2 Bxe5 22. Rbd1 Bd6 23. Qxd5 Rxe1+ 24. Nxe1 Qe2 $16 { You grab the e5 pawn back, but Qxd5 then wins the d5 pawn, so White stays a pawn up. Your counterplay is the Qe2 raid, but the knight and the safe king hold everything together (about +1.6). }) 12... Rfd8 13. Qd3 e6 14. Nc5?! $15 { This lets most of the edge slip. } (14. Nxc6 $16 { Nxc6 leads to a clear White edge (+0.8): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s); Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Be5c6] } 14... bxc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb7c6] } 15. Rae1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Ba1e1] } 15... Ne4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 16. Qh3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bd3h3] } 16... Qa6 $18 { White keeps a lasting structural edge from the doubled c-pawns, and Black has to defend patiently. } { [%csl Ba6][%cal Bb6a6] } 17. f3 Nf6 18. Bh6 Qxa2 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 20. Qg3+ Kf8 21. Nc5 Qxb2 $16 { The point of f3 is to kick the knight, and Bh6 trades off your best defender so that the g-file opens for Qg3+. You pick up a2 and b2, but your queen is out of play while White's pieces swing to the king (about +1.7). Defend patiently. }) 14... Nh5 15. Nxc6 Nxf4 { WATCH OUT: Qxb2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.3 from Black's own side of the board), after: Ne7+ Kh8 Bg5 Rdb8 Rab1 Qa3 } 16. Qf3 bxc6 17. Qxf4 Qxb2 $10 { Black aims for an endgame rather than a kingside attack. 0-1 (56) Baljon-Rosenlund, Bad Meinberg 1975. } 18. Rad1 Qxc3 19. Rd3 Qb4 20. Rg3 Kh8 21. Rxg7 Kxg7 22. Qg5+ Kh8 $10 { Your queen goes pawn hunting on the queenside, so White lifts a rook with Rd3-g3 to pin and trade the Bg7 and bring the queen onto g5. You have enough defence, and White has at best a perpetual check (about 0.0). }) (11... Kh8?! $16 { A natural waiting move, but it does not deal with the knight on e5, and White gains about 0.3 to 0.4 compared with 11...e6 or 11...Nxe5. } 12. Re1 Rc8 13. Nd3 e6 $16 { [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+1.0). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 14. Re3 { White's idea is a rook lift: Re3-h3 aims at h7 and your g7 bishop while your king sits in the corner. } (14. Nf1 Ne7 15. Ng3 Ne4 16. Nh5 Ng6 17. Nxg7 Kxg7 18. Qc1 Nxf4 { Another way to attack is Ng3-h5 to remove your bishop, but ...Ng6 meets it (about +0.8). }) 14... Rg8 15. Rh3 Bf8 16. Nf3 Ng4 17. Qe2 Rg7 18. Re1 Bd6 { ...Bf8 and ...Rg7 cover h7 and h6, and trading the dark bishops with ...Bd6 relieves the pressure. White keeps a clear but manageable edge (about +0.9). }) 12. Nxc6 (12. Ndf3 Ne4 13. Nxc6 bxc6 14. Ne5 Rc8 15. f3 Nf6 16. Qe1 c5 17. b4 cxd4 18. cxd4 Nd7 19. Qg3 Kh8 20. Rac1 Nxe5 21. Bxe5 Rg8 22. Bxg7+ Rxg7 23. Qf4 Rc4 24. Rxc4 dxc4 25. Rc1 Qd5 26. Qe5 h5 27. Kf2 Kh7 28. Qxd5 exd5 29. b5 Rg8 30. Re1 Rc8 $10 { White offers the queen trade to head for a rook ending where the b-pawn is a nuisance. Your c4 and d5 pawns are strong and you have enough counterplay (about +0.3). }) (12. Re1 Ne7 13. Nd3 Ng6 14. Bg5 Qc7 15. Nf1 Ne4 16. Bc1 { Your knight on e4 attacks the bishop on g5, so it has to retreat. White's plan is Ng3 to challenge the e4 knight and then Nh5 to trade off your g7 bishop and leave the dark squares thin. } (16. Qh5 Rae8 17. Bh6 Nf6 18. Qg5 Bxh6 19. Qxh6 Kh8 { White goes for g7 at once, but ...Nf6 and ...Bxh6 trade off the attackers (about +0.4). }) 16... Rae8 17. Ng3 Kh8 18. Nh5 Rg8 19. Nxg7 Rxg7 20. Bh6 Rgg8 { Kh8 and Rg8 cover g7, so Nh5 only wins a trade of minor pieces. When the bishop comes to h6 you just step the rook aside; White keeps a small edge (about +0.6) but has no real target. }) (12. Nd3 $16 { White's top engine try here (+0.75, a shade more than 12.Nxc6's +0.71). Black meets it comfortably with either knight move or the queenside break. } 12... a5 13. Nf3 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.67), the same normal range as the rest of the chapter.] } 13... a4 14. a3 Ne7 15. Be3 Ng6 16. Nfe5 Ne4 17. Qh5 Qe8 18. Rae1 $14 { The pawn on a4 fixes b2 and cuts down the knight. You regroup with Ne7-g6 and Ne4. White's knight on e5 is annoying but not decisive (about +0.5). }) (12. Bg5 $14 { Pins the knight instead of trading it off on c6. } 12... Ne7 13. Ndf3 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.61).] } 13... Kh8 { Step off the g-file so that ...Rg8 and ...Ng6 can follow and challenge the e5 knight. White's bishop on g5 and both knights are aimed at your kingside, so you want to trade pieces (about +0.4). } (13... Re8 14. g3 Ng6 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Re1 Qc7 17. Qd2 Kg7 { A calmer set-up that offers the bishop trade on f6 (about +0.5). }) 14. Nd3 Rg8 15. Nfe5 Qe8 16. Bf4 Ng6 17. Nxg6+ hxg6 18. f3 { Once you have traded a knight White keeps only a small edge. }) (12. Qe2 $16 { A flexible developing move that keeps every recapture option open. } 12... Ne7 13. Be3 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.66).] } 13... Ng6 14. Nd3 Re8 15. g3 Qd7 16. Rfe1 Ng4 17. Nf1 Bf8 18. Bc1 $14 { Your knight challenges the Ne5, so White retreats it. Then Ng4 trades off the dark-squared bishop and Bf8 covers the dark squares. White has a small edge (about +0.5), but your pieces are placed sensibly. }) (12. Qf3!? $14 { Playable but not White's most testing try: +0.53 after Black's best reply, a notch below 12.Nxc6 and friends. } 12... Nxe5 13. Bxe5 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.53).] } 13... Ne4 14. Nxe4 dxe4 15. Qg3 f6 16. Bd6 Rf7 17. Rae1 Qb6 18. Ba3 $14 { You trade White's best minor piece, then support the e5 square with ...f6. The Bd6 looks strong but Rf7 and Qb6 deal with it. White keeps a small edge (about +0.4). }) (12. f3?! $15 { A real inaccuracy: it loosens e3 and g3 for no gain, and Black's reply hits the bishop at once. } 12... Nh5! { The point: the bishop on f4 has to move, and Black seizes the initiative. } 13. Nd3 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.53). This is the one try among White's 12th-move options that actually hands Black the better position; everything else above keeps White's normal +0.6 to +0.8.] } 13... Nxf4 { Take at once: White's f3 pawn has taken the best square from its own knight, so trading the bishop leaves White's pieces badly coordinated (about -0.4). } (13... Re8 14. Be3 e5 15. dxe5 Bxe5 16. Nxe5 Rxe5 17. f4 Rxe3 18. Qxh5 { The quiet ...Re8 lets White keep the bishop, and the loose knight on h5 becomes a target; this drifts to about +0.2. }) 14. Nxf4 e5 { ...e5 hits d4 and opens the diagonal for your g7 bishop. } 15. dxe5 d4 16. Ne2 Qb6 17. Kh1 Rad8 { Your pieces swarm around d4, e5 and b2 while White's king is stuck behind its own pawns. }) 12... bxc6 13. Re1 Ne4!? $14 { Black starts a kingside build-up instead of a passive defence. } 14. f3 Nf6 (14... Nxd2 $14 { The most-played reply in practice, but objectively looser: it trades off Black's strong e4 knight for White's less active one and costs a little compared with simply retreating. } 15. Qxd2 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.52), about a third of a pawn more than 14...Nf6.] } 15... Qe7 16. b4 a5 17. a4 Qa7 18. Bd6 axb4 19. Bxb4 Rfb8 20. Kh1 $14 { White has used the open queenside lines to fix your structure. After the exchange, the Bd6 jump is annoying, but ...Rfb8 targets b4 and you have a sound game (about +0.6). }) 15. Nb3 Nh5 16. Bd2 Kh8 17. Nc5 Rg8 18. Qc1 Qh4 $10 { Black slowly reinforces the kingside pieces to provoke weaknesses. } 19. Re2 { [%csl Bg2,Bg8,Bh4][%cal Be2g2,Bc1e1,Bg8g1,Bh5f4] } 19... Bf6 20. Qe1 Nf4 21. Bxf4 Qxf4 22. Kh1 Be7 23. Nd7 Qh6 24. Qg1 Bd6 25. Ne5 Raf8 26. Rae1 Rg5 27. f4 Rg7 28. g3 Bxe5 29. dxe5 Qh3 30. Rg2 h5 31. Re3? { Re3 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.8): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } (31. Qxa7 $10 { Qxa7 leads to roughly equal (+0.1): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); Black has doubled pawns. } 31... Rfg8 32. Re3 Qg4 33. Qd4 { [%csl Bd1,Be1,Bf3,Bg2,Bg3,Bh3][%cal Bd4d1,Bg2a2,Be3e1,Be3h3] } 33... h4 34. Rd3 Rh7 35. Qf2 $10 { White could have grabbed the a7 pawn first and returned to defend. Black has no way in. } 35... h3 { The pawn hits the rook on g2 and forces White to retreat. } 36. Rg1 Ra8 37. Ra1 c5 38. Rd2 c4 39. a4 Ra6 40. Qe2 $10 { You switch the rook to the queenside and push c5-c4, but White shuffles and finds no way in for either side, so expect equality (about -0.1). }) 31... Rfg8 32. Ree2 h4 33. gxh4 Qxh4 34. Qf1 Rg4 35. Ref2 Qh6 $15 { 0-1 (35) Doyle-Rosenlund, Haifa 1976. } 36. Rxg4 fxg4 37. Rg2 Qh7 38. Qe1 Rg7 39. a4 Kg8 40. Qf2 a6 $15 { White's rook trade leaves your pawn on g4. You now play on the h-file with the queen and your rook, and White's king remains uncomfortable (about -0.5). } *


