[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Exchange Variation, Rubinstein Variation"]
[StudyName "Greatest Caro Kann: Living Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Chapter #2: Exchange Variation, Rubinstein Variation (II)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/wdNgXE8n"]

1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. Same Carlsbad structure as Chapter #1 (White c3/d4, Black d5/e6), but Black's knight has already landed on e4 rather than heading for g4-type trades, and the position stays more piece-heavy for longer. Because nothing is forced, whoever executes their break first gets the practical edge: Black wants ...b5-b4 to open the queenside before White's minority attack (b4-b5 himself, or a timely Nxe4) resolves the center. Keep the knight on e4 defended by the d5-pawn as long as possible; it is Black's best piece, cramping White's whole position. If it gets traded off for nothing, the Carlsbad position quietly becomes pleasant for White instead of balanced.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.1 to +0.7, a small White edge. Note: along the chapter's line the engine value falls from +0.7 (move 7) to +0.2 at the target, because White does not play the engine's first choice at every step. Against best play, expect the higher figure. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +1 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Nxd4? on moves 5 to 8 loses material (about 4.6); ...Nfxd4? on moves 10 to 14 loses material (about 4.4); 10...e6? (about 0.8; prefer ...Qd7); 7...g5? (about 0.7; prefer ...Nxd2); 10...b6?! (about 0.6; prefer ...Qd7); 7...f5?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Nxd2).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 7.Bxe4? dxe4 (costs White about 1.1).
Chapter #3 continues the Exchange Variation. Other Exchange Variation chapters: #1. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #11. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 $14 { This is the Exchange Variation - the "ECK" (Exchanged Caro-Kann) for short. See Chapter #1 for the full explanation of what the ECK is, the two kinds of players who reach for it, and why this repertoire treats it as one of Black's most reliable answers to 1.e4. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. exd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge4d5] } 3... cxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc6d5] } 4. Bd3 $14 { Many experienced Exchange Variation players choose Bd3 to take a key square away from Black. Contesting the f4 square at once and neutralising White's bishop pair is the right response. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } (4. Bf4 { 4.Bf4 - develops actively onto the long diagonal, delaying the usual Nc3/Bd3 setups. } 4... Nc6 { 4...Nc6 develops naturally; the bishop on f4 is a reasonable square for White but creates no extra problems for Black to solve. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2), within the normal range for this chapter.] } 5. c3 { 5.c3 supports the center solidly before developing the knight, keeping options for both Nf3 and Nd2. } 5... Bf5 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 { ...and the position stays level. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1), a sound fifth-move alternative in this 4.Bf4 Exchange structure.] } 7... e6 { Compact development. } (7... Nf6 8. Nd2 e6 9. Qe2 Qb6 10. Rb1 a6 11. a4 Rc8 12. h3 { Another route to the same position. }) 8. Nd2 Nf6 9. Ne2 Qb6 10. Rb1 Be7 11. O-O O-O 12. Qf3 { Your queen hits b2 and forces White's rook to b1. Your pieces are comfortable and the structure is symmetrical, so it is level (about 0.0). }) (4. c3 { 4.c3 - a quiet supporting move, preparing Bd3 and keeping d4 well defended before developing the pieces. } 4... Nf6 { 4...Nf6 is simplest; the tempo White spends on c3 is close to a non-factor once development finishes. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] } 5. Nf3 { The simplest development: Nf3, then Bd3 and Bf4. } (5. Bf4 Bf5 6. Qb3 Qc8 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. Nh4 Bd7 9. Bd3 Nh5 { White hits b7 with the queen and the f5 bishop with Nh4, but ...Qc8 and ...Bd7 meet both threats (about +0.2). }) 5... Nc6 6. Bd3 Bg4 7. h3 Bh5 8. Bf4 e6 9. Nbd2 Bd6 { Pin the f3 knight and then trade White's active dark bishop with ...Bd6; the extra c3 tempo is not a real factor (about +0.2). }) 4... Nc6 { [%csl Gc6,Rc3,Rf3][%cal Gb8c6,Rc2c3,Rg1f3] } (4... Nf6 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE THIS IF you want to meet 4.Bd3 with 4...Nf6 and keep your structure flexible with an early ...e5 break (more active and more varied than the quiet Rubinstein-style play elsewhere in these chapters). Risk level: MEDIUM. For a calm, structure-based game stay with Chapters #1-#2. After 4.Bd3 Nf6 White has 5.h3, 5.Bf4 and 5.c3 as the main tries. } 5. h3 { 5.h3 stops ...Bg4 for good and prepares a quiet development. Black replies ...Nc6 and ...e5. [Engine verdict: about +0.3.] } (5. Bf4 { 5.Bf4 develops actively and keeps h3 in reserve. ...Nc6 answers it naturally. [Engine verdict: roughly equal.] } 5... Nc6 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.3).] }) (5. c3 Bg4 6. Qb3 { The most testing line: Black's bishop pins the knight and White's queen hits b7, which forces Black to react. ...Qc7 solves the problem. [Engine verdict: about +0.3.] } (6. f3 { 6.f3 tries to chase the bishop but weakens the e3-square. Black retreats with ...Bh5 and ...Bg6 and equalises. [Engine verdict: roughly equal.] } 6... Bh5 7. Ne2 Bg6 8. Nf4 Bxd3 9. Nxd3 e6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.01).] }) 6... Qc7 7. h3 (7. Ne2 { 7.Ne2 offers a trade on e2; after ...Bxe2 Bxe2 e6 Black reaches a solid, equal position. [Engine verdict: about +0.1.] } 7... Bxe2 8. Bxe2 e6 9. O-O Bd6 10. g3 (10. h3 O-O 11. Nd2 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.10).] }) 10... Nc6 11. Nd2 O-O 12. Nf3 Ne4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.08).] }) 7... Bd7 8. Ne2 Nc6 { Black unpins and heads for ...e5. The position is balanced and Black's pieces are well placed. [Engine verdict: about -0.15.] } 9. Bf4 e5 10. dxe5 Nxe5 11. Bxe5 (11. Bb5 Bd6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.08).] }) 11... Qxe5 12. Nd2 Bc5 13. Nf3 Qd6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.2).] }) (5. Nf3 { White brings out the knight, keeps the bishop on d3 and prepares c3 and Bf4 or O-O. It is very common (played in about 743 thousand games). White hopes for a slight pull from the pin ideas and a calm, solid structure. } 5... Bg4 { Pin the knight at once, before h3. The bishop would like to trade itself for the knight, which relieves your cramped position. After h3 retreat to h5, then ...Nc6, ...e6 and ...Bd6 to challenge the strongest white bishop. Your plan is to exchange pieces, keep the d5 pawn solid and castle short. Avoid ...g6 here, which lets White play Bf4 and c3 comfortably with extra space. The position is about level, with a very small plus for White. } (5... g6 { Less ideal. After c3 Bg7 h3 Bf5 White can swap bishops and then Bf4 gives a lasting initiative, so you stay a little passive. } 6. c3 Bg7 7. h3 Bf5 8. Bxf5 gxf5 9. Bf4 e6 10. Nbd2) 6. h3 Bh5 7. Bf4 Nc6 { The knight guards d4 and pressures the centre. Your next moves are ...e6 and ...Bd6. } 8. c3 e6 9. Nbd2 Bd6 { Now you challenge White's good bishop, making the position simple and solid. } 10. Bxd6 { Overall: after the exchange you have a healthy position with no weaknesses and only a tiny deficit, which is a good outcome in this line. This is a balanced position with symmetrical pawns and a pin from your bishop on h5, where White has recaptured on d6 with the bishop. Your plan: recapture with ...Qxd6, then play ...Bg6 to trade White's bishop on d3, castle short, and bring a rook to e8. White wants g4 to trap or bother the bishop on h5, then Qe2 and O-O-O. If g4 comes, retreat with ...Bg6 and recapture on g6 with the h-pawn. The mistake is ignoring the pin on f3, which you can exploit with ...Bxf3 at the right moment. If White deviates with Qe2, continue ...Bg6. }) (5. Ne2 { White develops the knight so that he can keep his structure flexible, and the f1 bishop is on d3 so the knight cannot go to f3 without blocking. The knight on e2 also prepares to go to f4 and keeps the f-pawn free. It is very common in practice (played in about 250 thousand games), so you will meet it often. For you it means White is aiming for a quiet game without early pawn tension. } 5... e6 $10 { You support d5 and prepare ...Bd6 and castling. Your plan is simple: develop ...Nc6, ...Bd7 and ...Bd6 and then castle short, putting pressure on the white queenside with ...Rc8. If White plays Bf4 you must challenge it with ...Bd6 when possible, since trading the dark squared bishops helps you. Avoid ...Bg4 too early unless you check White's reply. } (5... Bg4 6. h3 Bh5 7. c3 e6 8. Qb3 Bxe2 9. Bxe2 Qc7 { This is another fine reply. }) 6. Bf4 Nc6 7. O-O Bd7 { The bishop goes to d7 to prepare ...Nh5 which hits the bishop on f4. } 8. c3 Nh5 { You gain the bishop pair or force the bishop to retreat. } 9. Be3 Bd6 { Overall: you have solid development and equal chances. } 10. Nd2 { This is a closed centre with the symmetrical Exchange Variation structure: d4 against d5, with White's pieces more active but your bishops trading in your favour. Both kings castle short. Your plan: castle short, play ...Rc8 and ...Qe7, retreat the knight from h5 to f6 when needed, and keep the bishop on d6 well placed against the dark squares. White wants Nf4 to hit your knight and the d5 pawn, and a later Bd3 plan against h7. Meet Nf4 with ...Nxf4 and Bxf4, then ...Bxf4. The risk is leaving the knight stranded on h5 without a retreat, where a pinned or trapped piece loses time. }) 5... Nc6 6. c3 e5 { Black hits the centre immediately. After 7.dxe5 Nxe5 8.Qe2 Qe7 the pin-and-trade ideas are balanced. [Engine verdict: about +0.2.] } (6... g6 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.3).] }) 7. dxe5 Nxe5 8. Qe2 Qe7 (8... Ne4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.23).] }) 9. Be3 (9. Bb5+ { White's check forces simplification but gives Black the easier game. [Engine verdict: about -0.2.] } 9... Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Kxd7 11. Qb5+ Kc8 12. Kf1 a6 13. Qe2 Qc7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.06).] }) (9. Bc2 b6 (9... Bd7 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.4).] }) 10. Na3 (10. Bf4 Nc4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.00).] }) 10... Ba6 11. Nb5 O-O-O 12. a4 Re8 13. Kf1 Ne4 14. Bxe4 dxe4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.03).] }) 9... Bd7 (9... Nxd3+ 10. Qxd3 Qe4 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.2).] }) 10. Nf3 (10. Bb5 Bxb5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.12).] }) 10... Nxd3+ 11. Qxd3 Qe4 12. Qe2 Bd6 13. Nbd2 Qe6 14. O-O O-O { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.19).] }) 5. c3 { c3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } (5. Nf3 $15 { Nf3 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 5... Nf6 $14 { A fresh way to face the Exchange Variation with Nf3 is the plan ...Nf6 followed by ...Ne4. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 6. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } (6. O-O { White castles and wants to finish development before deciding on a plan: Re1, c3 or Bg5, and possibly h3 followed by Bf4 and Qb3 against d5. The move is extremely common in practice (played in about 1379 thousand games). For you it means White keeps tension in the centre and you must choose where your bishop goes. } 6... Bg4 $10 { You pin the knight and take the c8 bishop out before it is shut in by ...e6. Your plan is ...e6, ...Bd6, ...O-O, with ...Qd7 and a quiet game. The pin on f3 matters because it stops White from playing e5-style jumps and makes h3 a necessity. After 7.c3 e6 8.h3 Bh5 9.Bf4 you can keep the bishop alive with ...Ne4. } (6... g6 7. Re1 Bg7 8. c3 Qc7 9. Bg5 O-O 10. Na3 { A natural fianchetto, but it is a little passive: White gets Re1 and Bg5 with a comfortable edge and your light squares are loose. Avoid this if you want a dynamic game. }) 7. c3 e6 8. h3 Bh5 9. Bf4 Ne4 { The knight is excellent here: it is supported by d5 and forces White to decide the fate of the bishop on f4. If White plays 10.Qb3 you reply ...Bd6, offering a trade of the dark-squared bishops. After 11.Bxd6 you recapture with the queen and your d5 pawn is well defended. Overall: the game is level with a very solid position for Black. } 10. Qb3 Bd6 11. Bxd6 { This is a fairly open, symmetrical centre: the d5 pawn is solid, White has traded his dark-squared bishop and you have a safe king still to castle. Your plan: castle short at once, play ...Qxd6-style recapture structure with the queen on d6 or d7, then bring the rooks to c8 and e8 and use the knight on e4 as your best piece. White wants the open c-file and the queen on b3 against b7, so keep b7 guarded and watch d5. The risk is hurrying ...O-O into Qxb7 tactics without checking; if White avoids the trade on d6, retreat calmly. }) 6... Ne4!? $14 { The idea behind ...Ne4 is to bring the knight to d6; ...Bg4 and further development can follow. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 7. Bxe4?! $15 { Bishop takes knight would only elude to black having a superior position. First and foremost the bishop pair is gone and also black has a pawn on e4 which evicts white's knight from f3. It would take a couple of tempo ahead before white can resolve that.  [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores -0.41; O-O scores +0.69, so O-O was stronger] [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } (7. Nbd2 $14 { Against Nd2, ...Nd6 is the answer. Black should not trade knights for White's modest Nd2 and should keep the position dynamic. } 7... Nd6 8. Ne5 Bf5 $14 { Exchanging bishops would be beneficial since you could see the light squares on white's position from b1-h7 slowly weaken. } 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Nb3 Bxd3 11. Qxd3 e6 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] } 12. O-O { Castling keeps things simple. } (12. Nc5 a5 13. O-O Nf5 14. a4 Bd6 15. b4 Qh4 16. f4 O-O { A Nc5 jump is met by ...Nf5 and ...Bd6 (about 0.0). }) 12... a5 { ...a5 gains queenside space and takes b4 away. } 13. a4 Ne4 14. Bf4 Qb6 15. Nd2 Bd6 16. Nxe4 Bxf4 { Centralise ...Ne4 and trade dark bishops with ...Bd6; your c6 pawns are doubled but your pieces are active and the position is equal (about 0.0). }) (7. Bf4 $10 { Bf4 comes before Nd2 because otherwise the dark-squared bishop would be shut in. The drawback is ...Bf5, which stabilises the knight on e4. } 7... Bf5 8. Qb3 Qd7 9. O-O { O-O leads to roughly equal (-0.0): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } (9. Ne5 $10 { Ne5 leads to roughly equal (-0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 9... Nxe5 10. dxe5 a6 $10 { This stops the idea of Bb5 pinning the queen; otherwise Black would be in serious trouble. } 11. Qc2 g6 12. Nd2 Nxd2 13. Qxd2 Bxd3 14. Qxd3 Bg7 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1).] } 15. O-O Rc8 { The position is level, so you only need a sensible plan. White's pawn on e5 cramps you, but it is also a target: your Bg7 and the open c-file hit it and c3 at once. } 16. g3 e6 17. h4 h6 { White's h4 grabs space and hints at h5, and ...h6 stops that cheaply. } 18. Rfe1 O-O 19. Rad1 Qa4 { You are castled, the queen is active and the game is balanced (about 0.0). }) 9... e6 10. Ne5 $15 { White cannot answer with a similar ...Ne4 idea: Nxd5 dxe5 a6 and so on would make the position simpler, with Black's knight on e4 still intact. } 10... Nxe5 11. Bxe5 Nc5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5).] } 12. dxc5 { Your Nc5 forks the queen and the bishop on d3, so White must take the knight. } (12. Bxf5 Nxb3 13. axb3 exf5 14. Re1 Kd8 15. c4 f6 16. Bg3 g5 { The bishop trade does not stop the fork; you win the queen for a knight (an evaluation over -5). }) 12... Bxd3 13. Rd1 Bb5 14. a4 Bc6 15. Qc2 f6 16. Bd4 e5 { You recapture the bishop and get the bishop pair and the e5 break (about -0.6). }) 7... dxe4 { [%csl Be4,Bf3][%cal Bd5e4,Be4f3] } 8. Ng5 { [%csl Be4,Bg5][%cal Bf3g5,Bg5e4] } 8... Qd5 $15 { White supports the e4-pawn. Black plans ...Bf5 followed by ...e5, a strong central break. } { [%csl Bd5,Be4][%cal Bd8d5,Bd5e4] } 9. Qe2 { [%csl Be2,Be4,Bg5][%cal Bd1e2,Be2e4,Bg5e4] } 9... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5,Be4][%cal Bc8f5,Bf5e4] } 10. f3 { [%csl Bf3,Bf8,Bg1][%cal Bf2f3,Bf1f8,Be1g1] } 10... exf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Be4f3] } 11. Nxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg5f3] } 11... e5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Be7e5] } 12. Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 12... Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bc6e5] } 13. Qxe5+ { [%csl Be5][%cal Be2e5] } 13... Qxe5+ { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd5e5] } 14. dxe5 $10 { White is left with an ugly isolated pawn, which is a poor outlook. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd4e5] } 14... O-O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1). Notable features: White is a pawn up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side; Black has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bc8,Bd1][%cal Be8c8,Bd8d1] } 15. Be3 (15. Rf1?! Bd3 16. Rxf7 Re8 17. Be3 Rxe5 { Grabbing f7 looks greedy, but the Bd3 fork forces White's hand and you regain e5 with active pieces (about -0.3). }) 15... Rd5 { You attack the e5 pawn, the one thing keeping White a pawn up. } 16. O-O Bd3 { The bishop hits the rook and gains a tempo. } 17. Rd1 Rxe5 { The pawn is back. } 18. Bxa7 Bc2 { Another tempo: the bishop forks Rd1 and Nb1. } 19. Rf1 Bd3 $10 { You have the bishop pair and White's extra pawn has gone. If White repeats, so can you. }) 5... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 6. Bf4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gc1f4] } 6... Ne4! $14 { A surprising choice against a White player who has settled for the London-style setup. The knight heads to e4 to use that square, centralise itself and block the d3 bishop's diagonal. From there Nd6 and ...Bf5 become possible, so Black can offer to trade off White's strong light-squared bishop. } { [%csl Rd2,Ge4][%cal Gf6e4,Rb1d2,Rd3e4] } 7. Nd2 { Nd2 leads to a modest White edge (+0.6), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } (7. Bxe4?! $14 { White's participation in taking the knight would not do him any good since now black's center pawns would bring a great distaste on white's position. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } 7... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 8. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2,Be4][%cal Bb1d2,Bd2e4] } 8... Qd5 { [%csl Bd5,Be4][%cal Bd8d5,Bd5e4] } 9. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 9... e5! $14 { Black's spontaneous attempt in trying to breakthrough white's one and only center pawn would bring black sufficient counterplay on the center. } { [%csl Be5,Bf4,Bd4][%cal Be7e5,Be5f4,Be5d4] } 10. dxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd4e5] } 10... Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bc6e5] } 11. Bxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf4e5] } 11... Qxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd5e5] } 12. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 12... f5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bf7f5] } 13. Qb3 { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bd1b3,Bb3b7] } 13... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7,Bb7][%cal Be5c7,Bc7b7] } 14. Nd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be2d4] } 14... Bd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bc8d7] } 15. Rad1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bb1d1] } 15... O-O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bc8][%cal Be8c8] } 16. Ne6 { The knight on e6 forks your queen and rook, so you take it. } (16. Rfe1 Kb8 17. Ne6 Bxe6 18. Qxe6 Bc5 19. Nc4 Rde8 20. Qd5 g6 { If White delays, ...Kb8 sidesteps the fork and you are a bit better (about -0.4). }) 16... Bxe6 17. Qxe6+ Qd7 18. Qb3 Qc7 { White's queen takes with check and the bishop pair is gone, leaving an equal position (about 0.0). }) 7... Nd6 8. Ngf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 8... Bf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 9. Bxf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gd3f5] } 9... Nxf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gd6f5] } 10. Qb3 { [%csl Gb3,Gb7][%cal Gd1b3,Gb3b7] } 10... Qd7 { [%csl Gd7,Gb7][%cal Gd8d7,Gd7b7] } 11. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 11... e6 $10 { Tango move order of the Exchange Caro Kann - Complete } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 12. Rfe1 $10 { Engine continuation (Stockfish 16, depth 18), not in the original chapter: best play for both sides, added so that the line reaches the target position. Review before relying on it. } 12... Be7 13. Qd1 O-O 14. Nb3 Rac8 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] } 15. h3 h6 16. a4 Rfe8 17. Re2 b6 18. Nc1 Na5 19. Nd3 Nc4 { White makes luft and uses a4 for queenside space; you counter with ...b6 and ...Na5-c4, planting a knight on the outpost. The position is equal (about +0.1). } *


