1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. A looser version of the same Carlsbad idea, reached via 3.Nf3 or 3.Nc3 instead of the main 4.Bd3/4.c4 tries. Because White hasn't committed the bishop or the c-pawn yet, Black has more freedom in move order: develop naturally (...Nf6, ...Bf5 or ...Bg4, ...e6), castle, and only then decide between the queenside minority push and central pressure on d4 depending on how White sets up. Since White has spent an extra tempo on move-order flexibility rather than central commitment, Black's chief resource here is simply sound, harmonious development, there is rarely a single sharp line to memorize, which is exactly why this chapter is a sideline reference rather than a main line.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives -0.3 to +0.1, roughly equal. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Nxd4? on moves 7 to 14 loses material (about 4.2); 12...Qb4? (about 1.2; prefer ...Bxe2); 5...Nc6? (about 0.8; prefer ...a6); 5...g6?! (about 0.6; prefer ...a6); 7...e6?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Bg4).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 8.gxf3? Qd7 (costs White about 1.3); 6.h3? Bxf3 (costs White about 1.1); 6.Bd3? Bxf3 (costs White about 1.1).
Other Exchange Variation chapters: #1, #2. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #11. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 $14 { This is the Exchange Variation - the "ECK" (Exchanged Caro-Kann) for short. See Chapter #1 for the full explanation of what the ECK is, the two kinds of players who reach for it, and why this repertoire treats it as one of Black's most reliable answers to 1.e4. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. exd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge4d5] } 3... cxd5 { [%csl Gd5,Rb1,Rc3,Rf3][%cal Gc6d5,Rb1c3,Rg1f3] } 4. Nc3 $10 { An odd move against the London structure, but White is still going for the e4-e5 idea. } { [%csl Gc3,Rc6,Rf6][%cal Gb1c3,Rb8c6,Rg8f6] } (4. Nf3 $10 { Thus, many players would play something like Nf3 sticking to their e4 e5 principle. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 4... Nc6 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE THIS IF you want to meet the quiet 4.Nf3 with simple development: ...Nc6, ...Bf5 and ...e6. It is positional and low-risk. The chapter also shows how to answer 5.Bb5 (the pin) and 5.Bf4. 4.Nf3 Nc6 is a natural developing reply. White can play 5.c3 to build a Carlsbad structure, or 5.Bb5/5.Bf4 to test Black's set-up. } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 5. Bf4 { Bf4 leads to roughly equal (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bf4,Bb6,Bg4][%cal Bc1f4,Bd8b6,Bc8g4] } (5. Bb5 $10 { White can force the Horwitz move order unless Black plays ...g6, which is the best reply (...e6 would allow it). } 5... g6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bg7g6] } (5... Qa5+ { 5.Bb5 pins the knight, but ...Qa5+ and ...Bg4 give Black comfortable development. [Engine verdict: about +0.2.] } 6. Nc3 Bg4 7. Bd2 (7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 e6 9. O-O Nf6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.07).] }) 7... Rc8 { Black keeps the pressure with ...Rc8 and then breaks the pin with ...Bxf3. Equal. } 8. a4 (8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 e6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.05).] }) 8... Qd8 9. h3 (9. O-O e6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.01).] }) 9... Bxf3 10. Qxf3 e6 11. Ne2 Nf6 12. c3 a6 13. Bd3 g6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.05).] }) 6. Ne5 { [%csl Be5,Bc6][%cal Bf3e5,Be5c6,Bb5c6] } 6... Bd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bc8d7] } 7. Nc3 { Nc3 leads to roughly equal (-0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } (7. Nxd7 $14 { Rather than developing with Nc3 immediately, White should insert the trade Nxd7 first, since it comes with tempo and slightly improves White's structure before continuing normal development. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Be5d7] } 7... Qxd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bd8d7] } 8. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 8... a6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Ba7a6] } 9. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bb5e2] } 9... Qc7 $14 { Fully manageable for Black after ...Qxd7 followed by solid development. This is a minor move-order refinement for White, not a real threat to the soundness of the line. } { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd7c7] } 10. Nd2 Bh6 { Your bishop trades off White's dark-square bishop, often his best minor piece. } 11. O-O Nf6 12. g3 O-O 13. Ne4 Bxc1 14. Nxf6+ exf6 15. Qxc1 { The doubled f-pawns give you control of e5 and shield your king, so this is a fair structure. White keeps only a small edge. }) 7... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 8. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... Bg7 { [%csl Bg7][%cal Bf8g7] } 9. Bg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 9... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. a4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba2a4] } 10... a6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Ba7a6] } 11. Bxc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb5c6] } 11... Bxc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bd7c6] } 12. Nxc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Be5c6] } 12... bxc6 $15 { Black is comfortable here. } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb7c6] } 13. a5 Re8 { White wants to fix your a6 pawn and win it with Qd3. } 14. h3 Rb8 { Your rook goes to the open b-file to hit b2. } 15. Qd3 Qc7 16. Rfe1 Rxb2 17. Qxa6 Rxc2 $15 { Counterplay pays off and Black is happy (about -0.2). }) (5. Bd3 $15 { Bd3 leads to roughly equal (-0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 5... Bg4 6. c3 $10 { White is still heading for the London-style setup. Black should answer with ...g6; otherwise the game drifts into a Horwitz-type structure but without the c-pawn on its usual square. } 6... g6 7. Bf4 { Bf4 leads to roughly equal (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (7. Qb3 $14 { Once the white queen leaves the pin, Black should capture the knight at once. } 7... Bxf3 8. gxf3 Qd7 9. Bf4 Bh6 10. Bxh6 Nxh6 { WATCH OUT: Qe6+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.8 from Black's own side of the board), after: Be3 Nf6 h4 Nh5 Na3 Ng3 } 11. Nd2 Qe6+ 12. Kd1 O-O-O $15 { Castling queenside is dangerous because it hands White a target for an attack. } 13. a4 Rd6 { The rook guards the c6 knight and the sixth rank. White's pawn storm a4-a5-b4-b5 aims at your queenside king. } 14. a5 a6 15. Qa2 Qh3 { You counter on the kingside, where White's pawns are broken. } 16. Re1 Nf5 17. b4 Na7 $15 { Your knight blunts the b5 push, and you are better (about -0.6). }) 7... Bh6 $14 { Black aims to trade bishops and simplify. } (7... Bg7 $14 { Bg7 leads to a modest White edge (+0.7), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 8. O-O Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Nf6 10. Nd2 O-O $14 { A Grunfeld-type set-up has arisen on the board. } 11. Rae1 Nh5 { The knight attacks Bf4 and White must choose. } 12. Bg5 Bf6 13. Bh6 Bg7 14. Qe3 Nf6 15. Bf4 Nh5 { White's bishop dance tries to provoke weaknesses or force your Bg7 away. Each time you answer it correctly nothing happens, and a repetition is acceptable for you (about +0.5). }) 8. Bxh6 { Bxh6 leads to a small White edge (+0.4): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s); White retains the bishop pair. } (8. Bd2 $15 { White does have the option to retreat back his bishop. } 8... Qb6 9. b3 e5 10. dxe5 { WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -5.8 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Kxd1 Nxe5 dxe5 Bxd2 Nxd2 } 10... Nxe5 11. Qe2 { WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.6 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Kxd1 Bxd2 Nxd2 Nf6 Re1 } 11... Bg7 12. Bb5+ { WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.0 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxe2 Bxe2 Bxe5 Be3 Qc7 f4 } 12... Kd8 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1).] } 13. Be3 (13. Bd3?! Bxf3 14. gxf3 Qe6 15. O-O Qh3 16. Bg5+ Ne7 17. Nd2 Rc8 { The natural Bd3 lets you take on f3 and attack the broken kingside (about -0.6). }) 13... Qe6 14. Ng5 Bxe2 15. Nxe6+ fxe6 16. Bxe2 Nf7 17. a4 Ne7 $10 { Queens are off, the Ng5 fork idea is neutralised, and the position is level (about 0.0). }) 8... Nxh6 9. Nbd2 Bf5 $14 { An interesting idea: the knight goes to the rim deliberately so as to support the bishop exchange. } 10. Be2 { Be2 leads to a small White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (10. Bxf5 $14 { The exchange of bishop would only help black. } 10... Nxf5 11. O-O O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] } 12. Re1 { White heads for Nb3 and then Nc5 or Na5 to pressure b7 and make use of the open e-file. } (12. Nb3 b6 13. Re1 f6 14. Qe2 Qd6 15. Rad1 { transposes in spirit, about +0.3 }) 12... e6 13. Nb3 Qd6 14. Qd2 Rae8 { You prepare ...f6 and ...e5 to free your game. } 15. h3 Re7 16. Rad1 f6 { White keeps a small edge, about +0.3, but your position is firm and has no weaknesses. }) 10... Qc7 11. O-O O-O 12. Re1 Kh8 $14 { Black will soon bring the knight back into play. } 13. Rc1 Rad8 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.6).] } 14. Bb5 Bg4 { Your bishop goes to g4 to trade White's knight, the defender of d4 and e5. } 15. h3 Bxf3 16. Nxf3 e6 17. c4 Kg7 18. Bxc6 bxc6 $14 { White opens the centre with c4 and keeps a small edge (about +0.7), but your king is safe. }) (5. Ne5 { 5.Ne5 jumps into the center immediately, eyeing c6/f7 and daring Black to challenge it. } 5... a6 6. c4 e6 7. cxd5 { ...and Black neutralizes it comfortably with normal development. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.15), a real try but objectively one of White's softer options against this Exchange setup.] } 7... exd5 { White's knight on e5 aims at c6 and f7, so you open the e-file and recapture. } (7... Nxe5 8. dxe5 Qxd5 9. Qxd5 exd5 10. Nc3 Be6 11. Be3 Ne7 { Also equal, about 0.0. }) 8. Nxc6 bxc6 9. Bd3 Bd6 10. Qc2 Bd7 { Your bishop pair gives you a healthy position. } 11. O-O Qh4 { Hitting h2 provokes White to weaken the kingside. } 12. g3 { You are fine, about 0.0, and your queen can retreat to e7. }) (5. c3 { 5.c3 reinforces the center immediately rather than developing with Nc3 or Bb5 first. } 5... Bg4 (5... Bf5 6. Qb3 Qd7 { After 5.c3 Bf5 White can hit b7 with 6.Qb3, and ...Qd7 holds everything. Careful: 6...Bd3? loses at once, because 7.Bxd3 e6 leaves Black simply a piece down. [Engine verdict: about +0.25 for White after 6...Qd7.] } (6... Bd3 7. Bxd3 e6 { [Engine check: a large advantage for White (about +6.7).] }) (6... Bd7 7. Bd3 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.6).] }) 7. Bb5 (7. Nh4 Bg4 8. h3 (8. Bd3 f6 9. h3 Bh5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.23).] }) 8... Be6 { 7.Nh4 tries to win the bishop pair, but Black keeps everything defended. [Engine verdict: about +0.3.] } (8... Bh5 9. g4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.19).] }) 9. Bd3 (9. Be2 f6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.10).] }) (9. Be3 f6 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.2).] }) 9... f6 10. f4 Bf7 11. O-O e6 12. Re1 Nh6 13. Be3 Be7 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.5).] }) (7. Ne5 Nxe5 8. dxe5 a6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.16).] }) 7... f6 { 7.Bb5 attacks the knight, and ...f6 answers by defending e5 and freeing the king. Black is fine. [Engine verdict: about -0.2.] } 8. O-O e6 9. c4 dxc4 10. Qxc4 a6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 6. h3 Bh5 7. Qb3 { ...reaching a comfortable, level position. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.25), a sound, solid fifth-move try.] } 7... Qd7 { Your queen covers b7 and d5 at once. } (7... Qc8?! 8. g4 Bg6 9. Qxd5 e6 10. Qc4 h5 11. g5 Bd6 { Qc8 gives up the guard of d5, and after g4 White wins a pawn (about +1.0). }) (7... Bxf3 8. Qxb7 Qc8 9. Qxc8+ Rxc8 10. gxf3 Nxd4 11. Kd1 Nf5 12. Ba6 { A sharper try: White grabs b7 and you grab d4 (about +0.3). }) 8. Ne5 Nxe5 9. dxe5 a6 { ...a6 stops Bb5 ideas. } 10. c4 dxc4 11. Bxc4 e6 12. Be3 $10 { White has space but your position is solid. Level (about +0.2). }) (5. Nc3 { 5.Nc3 develops naturally, preparing Bb5 or Bg5 pressure against the newly-developed knight. } 5... Nf6 6. Bb5 (6. Bg5 { White pins the knight on f6 against the queen, intending to exchange it or take control of d4 and e5 with Ne5. This is a common developing move (played in about 1162 thousand games), and the main idea is to create pressure that forces you to make a choice about the bishop. } 6... h6 $15 { Ask the bishop immediately. After 7.Be3 you have gained a useful tempo because ...h6 is a move you would often want to play anyway, and the bishop on e3 now blocks White's own plans. Your plan is ...Bf5, ...e6, ...Bd6 and castle short. If 7.Bxf6 Qxf6 gives you the bishop pair and an open game. Avoid answering the pin with ...Bg4 and then rushing ...e6 without ...h6: White can keep the pin with Be2 and Ne5. } 7. Be3 Bf5 { Now the bishop is on its best diagonal and eyes b1. White usually tries 8.Ne5, trading knights. } 8. Ne5 e6 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Nxd3 Bd6 { After the trade you develop the bishop to d6 with a rock-solid position. Your remaining plans are ...O-O, ...Ne4 or ...Ne7 and the ...Qd7 setup to finish development. Overall: the middlegame is comfortable and you have the slightly easier game. } 11. Ne2 { This is an open, balanced position with the centre mostly fixed: your pawns stand on d5 and e6, White has the d4 pawn, and the light-squared bishops have been traded. Your plan: castle short, bring the rook to e8 or c8, then use the knight on e4 or e7 to challenge d4 and c3. White wants Qd2 and a small kingside initiative or the minority attack with b4-b5, so you answer with ...a6 and keep the queenside pawns flexible. The risk is passively waiting while White plays Nf4 and Ng5; if White deviates on move 11, simply castle and keep solid. }) 6... Bg4 7. h3 { ...and Black holds the balance comfortably. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.25), a real but slightly softer try than the solid c3 alternative.] } 7... Bxf3 (7... Bd7?! 8. O-O e6 9. Bf4 Qb6 10. Re1 { Retreating gives White time to finish development and a small edge (about +0.2). }) 8. Qxf3 e6 9. O-O Qb6 10. Qd3 Bd6 11. Ba4 O-O 12. Ne2 { Taking on f3 removes the defender of d4 and relieves the pin on c6. Your structure is healthy and ...Qb6 with ...Bd6 keeps the d4 pawn under pressure. }) 5... Bg4 $14 { Black must pin the knight at once. This prevents white from moving his bishop to d3 for free: after 6.Bd3?! Bxf3 7.Qxf3 Nxd4, Black doesn't just win the d4 pawn, the knight also forks the queen on f3 (from d4 it covers f3), so White loses a full tempo retreating it too (engine verdict: a clear Black edge, roughly -0.9). White has to play h3 first instead. } { [%csl Bg4,Bc3,Bd3,Bh3,Bb5][%cal Bc8g4,Bc2c3,Bf1d3,Bf1e2,Bh2h3,Bf1b5] } (5... Qb6 $14 { Moves like Qb6 does not work in this particular opening. } { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 6. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 6... Qxb2?? { Qxb2 leads to a clear White edge (+3.1): Black has a material 