 is simplest; the tempo White spends on c3 is close to a non-factor once development finishes. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] } 5. Nf3 (5. Bf4 Bf5 6. Qb3 Qc8 { Qb3 hits b7 and d5 together, but ...Qc8 covers b7 and the Nf6 guards d5 (about +0.2). }) 5... Nc6 6. Bd3 Bg4 7. h3 Bh5 { Retreating to h5 keeps the pin on Nf3 and avoids an early trade. } 8. Bf4 e6 9. Nbd2 Bd6 $10 { You trade off White's good dark bishop and reach a solid, level game. }) 4... Nf6 { Nf6 leads to roughly equal (+0.0), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } (4... Nc6 $10 { Another move order is ...Nc6, which is preferable to ...Nf6. } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 5. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bc1f4] } 5... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 6. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to roughly equal (-0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } (6. Bd3 $10 { A bishop swap is possible here, steering the game into a Horwitz-like structure. } 6... Bxd3 7. Qxd3 Nf6 8. Nf3 e6 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] } 9. Ne2 Be7 10. O-O O-O 11. c3 Qb6 { You pressure b2 and d4, so White cannot expand calmly. } 12. Rab1 a5 { White wants b4 and a minority attack against your queenside pawns, so ...a5 stops that plan before it begins. } 13. a4 Nh5 $10 { Your knight challenges the active Bf4, aiming to trade off White's best-placed piece. Level. }) 6... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3,Bf5][%cal Bf1d3,Bd3f5] } 7... Nge7 { [%csl Be7,Bf5][%cal Bg8e7,Be7f5] } 8. Bxf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bd3f5] } 8... Nxf5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1).] [%csl Bf5][%cal Be7f5] } 9. Ne2 (9. O-O?! Be7 10. Re1 g5 11. Bc1 h5 { Castling at once lets the same pawn advance arrive with tempo (about -0.6). }) 9... h6 { The idea is to chase the Bf4, White's only piece with an easy dark-square job. } 10. c3 g5 11. Bd2 Nd6 { The knight heads for e4 and c4 and shields your position. } 12. Be3 Be7 13. Qd3 Qd7 $15 { You have space and easy development. The advanced g5 pawn leaves holes on the dark squares, so keep an eye on them. }) 5. Bf4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gc1f4] } (5. Nf3 { White finally brings out the last knight to eye e5 and d4. The motive is plain development: Bd3, castling and perhaps Ne5 follow. It is very common in practice. The key point for you is that your c8 bishop is still free to jump to g4 before you play ...e6, so use it. } 5... Bg4 $10 { Pin the knight and force White to decide. After h3 you trade on f3 and remove a key defender of d4. Plan: ...Nc6, ...e6, then ...Be7 or ...Bb4, castle short, and aim for ...Nb4 or a break with ...e5 at the right moment. Avoid playing ...e6 first, which locks your bishop behind its own pawn. } (5... Bd7 { A passive try that gives up the active jump to g4 and is a little less good. } 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. Ne2 Nb4 8. O-O e6) 6. h3 Bxf3 7. Qxf3 Nc6 { Now your knight pressures d4 and heads for b4, and White's queen on f3 has few good squares. } 8. Be3 e6 9. Bd3 Nb4 { The knight jump hits d3 and c2, so White has to react at once, and if the bishop is traded you are happy with the knight on b4 and the structure. Next you play ...Be7 or ...Bd6 and castle short, with ...Qd7 and ...Rc8 supporting the central break. Overall: you have a solid, equal game with a clear plan. This is a Caro-Kann Exchange position where the pawn centres are symmetrical but you have the half-open c-file against White's c2 pawn. White has the bishop pair and the move. Your plan: use the knight on b4 to hit d3 and c2, trade on d3 if the bishop stays, then play ...Be7 or ...Bd6, castle short, and finish with ...Qd7 and ...Rc8. White wants a3 to kick your knight, then Qd1, Kf1 or a minority attack with b4. If a3 comes, take ...Nxd3+ and recapture. The mistake is leaving the knight on b4 loose without a plan. If White plays Bb5+, block with ...Nc6. }) (5. Bg5 { White pins the knight on f6 to the queen's side and wants to trade it, doubling or weakening your pawns. This is very common (played in about 791 thousand games). For you it means every capture on f6 has to be recaptured sensibly, so decide early how you want to meet it. } 5... Nc6 $10 { A natural developing move that adds to your control of d4 and e5 and keeps ...e6 in reserve. After 6.Nf3 play ...h6, and meet 7.Bh4 with ...Bg4, building the pin on the other side. Your plan is ...e6, ...Be7 and castling, followed by a pawn break at ...Ne4 or ...Qb6 later. If White plays Bxf6 you may recapture with the e-pawn or the g-pawn, choosing the one that keeps your centre healthy. Avoid grabbing material early or hurrying ...Ne4, which can leave the d5 pawn hanging. The numbers show equality, with a tiny edge for you. } (5... Bg4 { Also acceptable. The trade of bishops after Be2 is simple, and Bxf6 gxf6 leaves you with doubled pawns but the bishop pair is gone. Choose it if you like simple play. } 6. Be2 Bxe2 7. Qxe2 Nc6 8. Bxf6 gxf6 9. O-O-O e6 10. a3) 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 Bg4 8. Be2 e6 { You have developed with tempo and the pin on f3 is annoying for White. Your king goes short once Be7 is played. } 9. Ne5 Bxe2 10. Nxe2 { Overall: the position is equal; your piece play is clear, with no weaknesses, and you can aim for ...Nxe5 or ...Be7 with a solid, comfortable game. This is an open game where White's bishop on h4 pins your knight on f6 and his knight on e5 is trying to take over the centre. Your plan: trade on e5 with ...Nxe5, and after dxe5 play ...Qxd1+ to remove the pin and reach a safe ending. White wants dxe5 and then Bxf6 or e5xf6 to damage your pawns. The typical mistake is retreating the knight with ...Nd7 while the queen is still on d8, because Bxd8 wins it. If White recaptures on d1 with the rook or king, follow with ...Nd7 and attack e5. Do not hurry ...g5 before the queens are traded. }) 5... a6 { [%csl Ga6,Gb5][%cal Ga7a6,Gc3b5] } 6. Bd3 { [%csl Gd3,Gh7,Gb8][%cal Gf1d3,Gd3h7,Gf4b8] } 6... Nc6 { [%csl Gc6,Re2,Rf3][%cal Gb8c6,Rg1e2,Rg1f3] } 7. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gf3,Gd4][%cal Gg1f3,Gf3d4] } (7. Nge2 $10 { White may play this to prevent a ...Ng4 pin. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 7... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 8. h3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 8... Bh5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bg4h5] } 9. f3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf2f3] } 9... Bg6 $15 { Always remember this bishop manoeuvre. } { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bh5g6] } 10. h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh3h4] } 10... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 11. g4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bg2g4] } 11... Rc8 { [%csl Bc8][%cal Ba8c8,Bh4h5] } 12. h5 { [%csl Bh5] } 12... Bxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bg6d3,Bd1d3] } 13. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3] } 13... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 14. O-O-O { [%csl Bc1][%cal Be1c1] } 14... b5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1).] [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 15. Kb1 Qb6 { Your king is still in the centre, so you tie White down first: the queen hits b2 and d4. } 16. Ng3 Be7 17. g5 hxg5 18. Bxg5 b4 { White's plan is g5 to open the h-file for the rooks. You take the pawn and kick the Nc3, which guards the centre, with ...b4. } 19. Nce2 Kd7 $10 { The king steps off the dangerous files and sits behind your pawns. Level (about -0.1). }) 7... Bg4 { [%csl Gg4,Gd1,Gf3,Gd4][%cal Gc8g4,Gg4d1,Gf3d4] } 8. Be2 { Be2 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gd3e2] } (8. O-O $17 { O-O leads to a clear Black edge (-0.9): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 9. Qxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd1f3] } 9... Nxd4 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.8). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc6d4] } 10. Qh3 (10. Qd1?! Nc6 11. Qf3 e6 { Retreating wastes time (about -1.4). }) 10... e6 { The queen had to leave f3 because the Nd4 attacks it. White has the bishop pair and development, hoping activity pays for the pawn. You just finish development: ...e6, ...Nc6, ...Bd6. } 11. Rad1 Nc6 12. Ne2 Bd6 13. c4 Bxf4 14. Nxf4 d4 $17 { Trading the dark bishop removes White's compensation, and the d4 pawn cramps the knights. }) 8... e6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 9. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 9... Bd6 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf8d6] } 10. Bxd6 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf4d6] } 10... Qxd6 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gd8d6] } 11. Re1 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gf1e1] } 11... O-O 12. Nh4 $15 { Engine continuation (Stockfish 16, depth 18), not in the original chapter: best play for both sides, added so that the line reaches the target position. Review before relying on it. } 12... Bxe2 13. Nxe2 h6 14. Nf3 b5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3).] } 15. Rc1 Rfc8 { White has no real targets, so you simply improve your pieces. } 16. c3 a5 17. a3 Qb8 18. Ng3 Ra7 19. h3 b4 $15 { The ...b4 break comes when your pieces are well placed: you gain space and open lines on the side where you are better (about -0.3). } *


