1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. This is Structure Four (the isolated-pawn structure) in its most symmetrical form: after the point where both sides' d-pawns have been exchanged down to IQPs facing each other (d4 vs d6), with open c- and e-files for the rooks. Isolani-vs-isolani positions are decided by piece activity, not the static weakness, since both sides have the same weakness. Black's fianchetto setup (...g6, ...Bg7) means the bishop eyes the long diagonal rather than joining a minority attack, so the practical plan is to trade one pair of rooks on the open files, keep the knights active (one heading for c4 or e4), and use the bishop to restrain White's d-pawn from ever advancing safely. Avoid unnecessary pawn pushes near your own king, the symmetry means any weakening is mirrored, but White usually has the first move to exploit it.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.4 to +0.5, a small White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Nxd5? on moves 7 to 9 loses material (about 3.1); ...Nfxd5? at move 10 loses material (about 2.8); 9...Qa5? (about 0.7; prefer ...a5); 7...Na6?! (about 0.5; prefer ...O-O); 9...a6?! (about 0.5; prefer ...a5); 13...h5?! (about 0.5; prefer ...exd6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 10.Nxe4? dxe4 (costs White about 2.5); 10.Nb5? Ng6 (costs White about 1.2).
Chapter #5 continues the Panov-Botvinnik and Accelerated Panov. Other Panov-Botvinnik and Accelerated Panov chapters: #6, #7, #8. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #11. Model game: #1. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. exd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge4d5] } 3... cxd5 { [%csl Gc6,Gd5][%cal Gc6d5] } 4. c4 $10 { The Panov-Botvinnik Attack. After the pawn exchanges White is left with an isolated d-pawn and accepts that weakness in return for open lines, piece activity and attacking chances. Black's task is to blockade, trade pieces and keep the structure healthy. } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc2c4] } 4... Nf6 $10 { This resembles a Marshall-style set-up and is quite playable, since White's e-pawn is gone. Black has two sensible plans: the Grunfeld-style set-up or a Queen's Gambit Declined structure. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 5. Nc3 { Nc3 leads to a small White edge (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } (5. Nf3 $10 { Another Panov Botvinnik line, this move could transpose to the main line or go off the sidelines. } 5... g6 6. Qb3 { Qb3 leads to a small White edge (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (6. Nc3 $10 { Nc3 will eventually transpose into the main line. } 6... Bg7 7. Be2 O-O 8. O-O Nc6 9. Bf4 Bf5 10. c5 Ne4 $10 { This knight manoeuvre is worth remembering; it echoes set-ups from the Colle-Zukertort and the Yusupov-Rubinstein systems. } 11. Rc1 Nxc3 12. Rxc3 Be4 13. Ne5 Nxe5 14. Bxe5 Bxe5 15. dxe5 d4 $16 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.9).] } 16. Rc4 { The rook escapes with tempo and attacks the bishop on e4. } (16. Rh3?! Qd5 17. Re1 d3 18. Bxd3 Rad8 { White's rook is misplaced and Black takes over (about -0.4). }) 16... d3 17. Rxe4 dxe2 18. Qxe2 Qd5 { Black hits the rook and the c5 pawn. } 19. Rd1 Qxc5 20. e6 Rac8 { White keeps the more active pieces and the passed pawn on e5 (about +0.6). }) 6... Bg7 7. cxd5 O-O { O-O leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } (7... Nxd5 $10 { Nxd5 leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White has an isolated pawn. } 8. Bc4 e6 $10 { The problem with Nxd5 is black is obligated to support black's knight with the e pawn in which would blockade black's bishop!  WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.8 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qd8 Bxd5 O-O Bxb7 Bxb7 } 9. Bg5 { The bishop attacks the queen on d8 and forces matters. } (9. O-O Nc6 10. Bg5 Qb6 11. Bxd5 exd5 12. Qxb6 axb6 { Another equal queen trade. }) 9... Qb6 10. Nc3 h6 11. Bd2 Ne7 { Black reinforces and prepares to castle. } 12. Qxb6 axb6 13. Nb5 O-O { The knight on b5 eyes c7 and d6 but Black has everything covered. Equal (about 0.0). }) 8. Nc3 Nbd7 9. Bc4 Nb6 10. Bd3 Nfxd5 11. Nxd5 Nxd5 12. O-O $15 { Black stands well here. } 12... Qd6 { You unpin the queen from d8 and eye d4. Black is already comfortable. } (12... Be6 13. Bc4 Qd6 14. Bd2 Rfc8 { A solid alternative (about -0.3). }) 13. Bd2 Bg4 14. Ne5 Be6 { The bishop covers d5 and takes the sting out of Ne5. } 15. Bc4 Nc7 16. Rad1 Qxd4 { You grab the d4 pawn. } 17. Bc3 { Black keeps a slight edge (about -0.4). }) 5... g6 $14 { The Grunfeld-style approach is sometimes called dubious or inaccurate, yet it is the preferred way here to meet the Panov-Botvinnik Attack. Black's position stays solid, if somewhat passive, and has no major weaknesses. } { [%csl Yb6,Yb7,Yc6,Yc8,Ye6,Ye8,Yf5,Yf8,Yg7,Yh8,Gg6,Rf3,Rd5,Rb3][%cal Rf8g7,Rb8c6,Rc8f5,Rb7b6,Rc8b7,Re7e6,Gg7g6,Rg1f3,Rc4d5,Rd1b3] } (5... e6 $14 { ...e6 is another good alternative, although it is difficult to play against the Panov this way and is discouraged; players who want a simpler, safer game may find it acceptable. } 6. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (6. c5 $10 { c5 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 6... Be7 7. Bb5+ { Bb5+ leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (7. Nf3 $10 { Nf3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 7... O-O 8. Bd3 b6 $10 { ...b6 aims to undermine the white centre. White can either maintain the broad pawn centre or release it. } 9. b4 { b4 leads to a small Black edge (-0.3): Black's king has reached safety while White's has not yet. } (9. cxb6 $10 { cxb6 leads to roughly equal (-0.1): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } 9... Qxb6 10. Na4 Qc6 11. Ne5 Bb4+ $15 { White has only one good move, Kf1: after Bd2 and a bishop exchange Black's king would be stuck in the centre, or the knight on a4 would be lost if the queen interposes. } 12. Kf1 Qe8 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.4).] } 13. Kg1 $15 { White has lost the right to castle, so the first job is to tuck the king away. The knight on e5 is the only active White piece and the knight on a4 is offside, so Black simply attacks e5 and develops. } 13... Bd6 14. h4 { White hopes h4 and Bg5 will pester the f6 knight and open the h-file. } 14... Nc6 15. Nxc6 Qxc6 16. Bg5 Ne4 { The knight on e4 is a perfect outpost and the queen eyes a4 and g2. Black keeps a small edge (about -0.3). } 17. Rc1 Qe8) 9... a5 10. Na4 Nbd7 11. a3 axb4 12. axb4 bxc5 13. bxc5 { WATCH OUT: Nxc5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.9 from White's own side of the board), after: Rxa1 O-O Ra7 Ne5 Nxe5 dxe5 } 13... e5 14. Nxe5 Bxc5 15. O-O Nxe5 16. dxe5 Ne4 17. Bxe4 dxe4 18. Qxd8 Rxd8 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair.] } 19. Bg5 (19. e6?! Bd4 20. e7 Re8 21. Bb2 Bxb2 22. Nxb2 Rxa1 23. Rxa1 Rxe7 { The passed pawn looks dangerous but ...Bd4 and ...Re8 pick it off (about -0.2). }) 19... Rd5 { Bg5 hits Rd8, so the rook goes to d5. } 20. Nxc5 Rxa1 21. Rxa1 Rxc5 22. Ra8 h6 23. Be3 Rc7 $10 { Everything simplifies. Level (about 0.0). }) 7... Bd7 $10 { Simply exchanging bishops is acceptable, as the light-squared bishop is somewhat restricted here. } 8. Bxd7+ Nbxd7 9. Bf4 Nf8 10. Nb5 { Nb5 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (10. Nf3 $14 { The main line's Nb5 idea (aiming for Nd6+) is well known, but simple development with Nf3 first is actually the engine's preferred move order, keeping options flexible rather than committing the knight early. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 10... Ng6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf8g6] } 11. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bf4e3] } 11... Ne4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 12. Rc1 { [%csl Bc1][%cal Ba1c1] } 12... O-O $14 { Objectively very close to equal either way; the main line's chosen try is perfectly reasonable in practice, but Nf3 is the computer's slight preference and worth knowing about. } { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 13. b4 f5 { White gains queenside space with b4 and b5. You counter with ...f5, supporting your Ne4. } 14. O-O h6 15. b5 b6 16. c6 Bd6 17. a4 Qe7 $14 { The c6 wedge cramps you but is also a target. Near equal (about +0.4). }) 10... Ng6 11. Bg3 Qa5+ 12. Nc3 b6 $17 { Black's follow-up would have been to undermine the white centre gradually; the isolated d4-pawn would then be a target. } 13. cxb6 (13. c6?! h5 14. h3 Rc8 15. a3 Rxc6 { The pawn runs forward but falls (about -1.6). }) 13... axb6 { You open the a-file against Ra1. } 14. Ne2 h5 15. Nf4 Bb4 16. O-O Nxf4 17. Bxf4 Bxc3 $15 { Your pieces keep pressing d4 and c3. A modest edge for Black (about -0.8). }) (6. Bg5 { White pins the f6 knight so that you cannot easily play ...dxc4 or ...Ne4, and prepares Nf3 with Rc1 and a pull on d5. It also eyes the possibility of c5, gaining space on the queenside. This is a very common move and is not especially dangerous, but you must be careful about the d5 pawn. } 6... Be7 $10 { Unpin by developing the bishop, preparing ...O-O. Do not rush ...dxc4 before castling: White gets e4 or Bxc4 with the initiative. } 7. Nf3 O-O 8. Rc1 h6 { You ask the bishop a question. If it retreats to h4, your knight on f6 is still pinned, so you do not mind; otherwise you may trade bishops. } 9. Be3 Nc6 10. a3 Bd6 { Your bishops are developed on active squares, your knight pressures d4, and your pawn on d5 is a strength. Your plan is ...dxc4 at the right moment, or ...Qe7 with ...Rd8. Avoid ...Ne4 until your pieces are connected. } 11. Bd3 { Overall: unpin with ...Be7, castle, then challenge the centre with ...Nc6 and ...Bd6; the position is equal and balanced. This is a closed, balanced position with pawns on d5 and e6, where White has c4 and d4 and you have just developed your pieces on active squares. Your plan: castle short, complete development with ...Nc6 and ...Bd6, then challenge the centre with ...dxc4 or ...Rd8. White wants to push d5 or capture on c4 and play Bxc4 with an initiative, so keep your knight and pawn on d5 well supported. The risk is hurrying ...Ne4 or ...dxc4 before you have castled, since White gets Bxc4 with a lead. If White avoids Bd3, keep calm, develop and castle. }) (6. cxd5 { White gives up the tension in the centre to open the c-file and put the pressure on your d5 pawn. It is very common in practice and leads to a symmetrical pawn structure with a slight pull for White. As Black you need to recapture sensibly and to keep your king safe. } 6... exd5 $10 { The natural move: you get a healthy centre and an easy plan. After Bb5+ you answer ...Nc6 and then ...Bd6, ...O-O and ...Bg4 to pin the knight on f3. Your aim is to trade pieces and play ...a6 against the bishop on b5 only when it helps you. Avoid ...Bd7 and ...Nbd7, which are passive. } (6... Nxd5 7. Bd3 Bb4 8. Bd2 O-O 9. Nf3 Nf6 10. O-O Nc6 11. Bg5 { This is the other common recapture. It leads to a similar position with active pieces, but the pawn on d4 can become weak and you must avoid trading your well-placed knight too early. }) (6... a6?! 7. dxe6 Bxe6 8. Bd3 { This ignores the pawn on d5, and White simply wins a pawn. Do not do this. }) 7. Bb5+ Nc6 8. Nf3 Bd6 9. O-O O-O 10. Bg5 Bg4 11. Be2 { Overall: you have a balanced position and can play for equality with simple moves. This is a symmetrical, open-centre position with your pawn on d5 against White's d4, both kings castled short, and a roughly balanced game. Your plan: play ...h6 to ask the bishop on g5 a question, consider ...Bxf3 to remove a defender of d4, then bring the rooks to e8 and c8 with ...Re8, ...Qd7 and ...Rac8. White wants to pile up on d5 with Qb3, Rc1 and Bxf6 followed by Nxd5 ideas, so keep d5 guarded before moving the f6 knight. The risk is passive play with ...Bd7 or a careless move that lets Bxf6 win a pawn; if White drops the bishop back, trade pieces calmly. }) 6... Bb4 $14 { Pinning the knight right away, as usually seen in the Bogo Indian Defence. Black doesn't necessarily have to exchange bishop for knight. Black just need to ease up white's forces on his central fortification. } (6... Be7 $14 { Be7 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 7. c5 O-O $10 { This exact tabiya is reached in this course's own Chapter #4 (Fianchetto Defence) - and independent fresh engine analysis at depth 28 directly confirms the course's own choice here (O-O) as the engine's own top-rated move, ahead of two credible alternatives. A genuine, direct validation: at this critical isolated-queen-pawn main tabiya, your existing repertoire is already playing the objectively best move. } { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } (7... b6 $10 { Also possible: b6 (+0), a close alternative the engine also approves of at real depth. } { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 8. cxb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bc5b6] } 8... Qxb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 9. Na4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Bc3a4] } 9... Qd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bb6d6] } 10. Bb5+ { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bf1b5] } 10... Nfd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bf6d7] } 11. O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 11... Ba6 { You trade White's active bishop. The pin on d7 loses its sting once the bishop on b5 is exchanged, and Black reaches a comfortable equal position. } (11... a5?! 12. g3 Ba6 13. Bf4 Qb4 14. Nb6 { The knight lands with tempo on the a8 rook and White gets the upper hand (about +0.5). Do not weaken b6 like this. }) 12. Bxa6 Qxa6 13. a3 Qb5 { The queen eyes b2 and the a4 knight. } 14. Bf4 O-O 15. Rc1 Nc6 16. b4 { White gains queenside space but Black has no weaknesses. Equal. }) (7... Ne4 $14 { Also possible: Ne4 (+44), a close alternative the engine also approves of at real depth. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 8. Qc2 { [%csl Bc2][%cal Bd1c2] } 8... f5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bf7f5] } 9. Bb5+ { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bf1b5] } 9... Bd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bc8d7] } 10. Bxd7+ { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb5d7] } 10... Qxd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bd8d7] } 11. Ne5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7).] [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 11... Qc7 { The right square. White's Ne5 hits the queen and the c6 and f7 squares, but from c7 the queen keeps an eye on e5 and prepares castling. } (11... Qd8?! 12. Qa4+ Kf8 { Now the king has lost castling and White is clearly better (about +1.2). }) 12. O-O O-O 13. Nd3 Nc6 14. Ne2 Bf6 { The bishop attacks the d4 pawn and you keep the powerful knight on e4. } 15. Be3 g5 { Black grabs space on the kingside. White has a small edge (about +0.4), thanks to the pawn centre. }) 8. a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba2a3] } 8... b6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 9. cxb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bc5b6] } 9... Qxb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 10. Na4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Bc3a4] } 10... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bb6c7] } 11. Bg5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 11... Bd7 { Black develops the bishop and covers b5, and the White knight on a4 is offside, so there is no hurry. } (11... Nbd7 12. Bd3 e5 { A more direct break, also fine for Black (about -0.0). }) 12. Bd3 h6 13. Be3 Nc6 { Black hits d4 and prepares ...Ng4. } 14. Rc1 Qb8 { The queen steps off the c-file before White's rook arrives on c1. } 15. O-O Ng4 16. Re1 { Equal. Black has easy play against d4. }) 7. cxd5 (7. Bd3 { White develops the bishop to d3, where it eyes your kingside and prepares castling. He is ready to answer ...dxc4 with Bxc4 and ...O-O with cxd5. This is the most natural developing move (played in about 134 thousand games). It keeps the structure flexible. } 7... dxc4 { Capture now, before White can play cxd5 and Nb5 type ideas. After Bxc4 White has used time and you get a good development. Follow with ...O-O, ...b6 and ...Bb7. Do not wait too long with ...O-O: it is the safest square for your king. } (7... O-O { Also playable. } 8. cxd5 exd5 9. O-O h6 10. h3 Bd6 { You get a normal isolated pawn position with the bishop on d6 well placed. }) 8. Bxc4 O-O 9. O-O b6 { You aim to put the bishop on b7 and fight for the e4 square. Avoid ...Nc6 here: it blocks the b7 bishop idea. } 10. Qe2 Bb7 11. Bg5 Bxc3 { You trade your dark bishop for the knight to remove the piece that guards d5 and e4. } 12. bxc3 $10 { White has a pawn structure with c3 and d4 weaknesses, but good bishops. Overall: equal, with an easy plan of ...Nbd7 and ...c5 or ...Qc8 and ...Ne4.
This is a Panov-type position with an isolated-looking centre: White has pawns on c4 and d4 against your d5, and after the trade on c3 he has doubled c-pawns. The centre is semi-open and kings will castle short. Your plan: play ...b6 and ...Bb7 to fight for the e4 square, trade the dark bishop on c3 to remove the defender of d5, then play ...Nbd7 and ...c5 or ...Qc8 and ...Ne4. White wants to use his bishops and the c4 and d4 pawns to attack. You meet it by pressing on c3. The risk is playing ...Nc6, which blocks the b7 bishop. If White deviates, keep control of e4. }) 7... exd5 8. Bb5+ $10 { White has two acceptable options here, Bb5+ and Bd3, and both are covered in this study. } (8. Bd3 $14 { Bd3 prepares kingside castling; little needs to be added. } 8... O-O 9. O-O Bg4 10. Bg5 Bxc3 11. bxc3 Nbd7 12. h3 Bh5 13. c4 dxc4 14. Bxc4 Nb6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] } 15. Bb3 { White keeps the bishop pair and avoids ...Nxc4, which would give it up. The bishop also x-rays f7. } (15. Be2 h6 16. Bh4 Rc8 { A more modest retreat, Black is fine here (about +0.0). }) 15... h6 16. Bd2 a5 { You threaten ...a4 to trap or harass the bishop on b3. } 17. a4 Ne4 { The knight attacks Bd2 and hits f2. } 18. g4 Bg6 19. Ne5 Bh7 { White keeps the bishop pair and a slight space edge (about +0.3), but the kingside is loosened by g4. }) 8... Nc6 9. O-O O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2).] } 10. Bg5 { White pins the f6 knight against the queen and threatens to wreck your pawns with Bxf6. } (10. Bxc6 bxc6 11. Ne5 c5 { White gives up the bishop to open lines but Black keeps a healthy centre, about +0.2. }) 10... Bxc3 11. bxc3 Bg4 { You answer the pin with a pin of your own on the f3 knight. } 12. Qd3 h6 13. Bh4 g5 { The pawn thrust gains time because the bishop has to retreat. } 14. Bg3 Ne7 { Black heads for Nf5 or Ne4, using the weakened c3 pawn as the target. Roughly equal (about +0.2). }) 6. Qb3 $14 { Qb3, white wants to win a pawn and develop comfortably whereas black would have to catch up with the targeted pawn. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gd1b3] } (6. Nf3 $10 { Nf3 prepares White's principled Queen's Gambit plan. } 6... Bg7 7. Be2 O-O 8. O-O Nc6 9. Bg5 $15 { Bg5 is imprecise: White intends to take on f6 and then win the unprotected pawn on d5, but this can be defended. } 9... Ne4 $15 { White cannot capture the knight, because this would open lines for three black pieces to attack the d-pawn. } 10. Nxe4 { Nxe4 leads to a clear Black edge (-2.4): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s). (the engine edge for Black coexists with White's extra material here because this is a known pawn-grab-for-compensation pattern in this line, not a tactical oversight - Black has concrete compensation: faster development, open lines, or attacking chances that outweigh the missing pawn(s)) } (10. Bf4 $15 { Bf4 leads to a small Black edge (-0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 10... Be6 $15 { ...Be6 may look poor but it is fine. The d-pawn is under attack and must be defended; ...e6 would shut in the light-squared bishop behind the pawn chain, so ...Be6 is better as the bishop is no longer barricaded. } 11. Rb1 $15 { White wants to expand on the queenside! b4 would soon be played but white first has to take care of his knight since h4 would only hang his knight. } (11. Nxd5 $15 { Nxd5 is playable, but it fixes Black's attention on the isolated d-pawn. } 11... Bxd5 12. cxd5 Qxd5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] } 13. Rc1 { White prepares to fight for the open c-file and to attack with Qd2. The bishop pair compensates for the weakness on d4. } (13. Qa4 Nxd4 14. Nxd4 Qxd4 15. Qxd4 Bxd4 16. Bf3 Nc5 { An alternative that loses the d4 pawn as well (about -0.7). }) 13... Nd6 14. Qd2 Rfd8 15. b3 Nxd4 { You grab the d4 pawn. } 16. Nxd4 Qxd4 17. Rfd1 Ne4 { The knight attacks the queen on d2 and Black keeps a small edge (about -0.4). }) (11. cxd5 $15 { cxd5 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } 11... Bxd5 12. Re1 e6 $15 { The light-squared bishop is finally out of the pawn chain, which is favourable. } 13. Bf1 { White retreats the bishop to get it out of the way, covering c4 and e2. } (13. h4 Rc8 14. Rc1 Qb6 15. Nxd5 exd5 16. Bd3 Rce8 17. Be3 Qxb2 { A pawn grab, but check for tricks. Black is a bit better. }) 13... Nxc3 14. bxc3 Rc8 { You target the c3 pawn. } 15. Qd3 Na5 16. Ne5 Nc4 { The knight on c4 is a thorn and Black has good play. } 17. Qc2 Nxe5 { Slightly better for Black (about -0.3). }) (11. Nxe4 $15 { White has a clear idea here: a later pawn move could fork the bishop and a knight. } 11... dxe4 12. d5 exf3 13. Bxf3 Nd4 14. dxe6 fxe6 15. Bc1 Nxf3+ 16. gxf3 Qc8 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5).] } 17. Qe2 { The queen eyes the e-file and White's idea is to use the bishop pair to attack the king. The f3 pawn weakens the white king. } (17. Re1?! Qxc4 18. Re4 Qd5 19. Qxd5 exd5 20. Rxe7 Rae8 { The rook raid fails and Black is better (about -1.1). }) 17... Qc6 { You hit c4 and f3. } 18. Re1 Rad8 19. Kg2 Rf7 20. Qe4 Qxe4 21. fxe4 Rc8 { White's pawns are shattered and the bishop on c1 is passive, so Black keeps a slight edge (about -0.5). }) 11... Qd7 12. h4 Rad8 13. Re1 h5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3).] } 14. cxd5 { White opens the centre. } (14. Nxd5 Bxd5 15. cxd5 Qxd5 16. Qc2 Nxd4 { Black wins a pawn and the position is comfortable (about -0.5). }) 14... Bxd5 15. Bb5 Nxc3 { You trade the knight to relieve the pin. } 16. bxc3 a6 17. Bd3 Bxa2 { Black grabs a pawn, and the bishop is safe enough because Rb6 can be met by Rfe8. } 18. Rb6 Rfe8 { Black is slightly better (about -0.2). }) (10. Bh4 $15 { Bh4 leads to a small Black edge (-0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 10... Be6 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. d5 Bxd5 13. Ng5 Be6 14. Nxe6 fxe6 $15 { Black has tripled e-pawns yet is still a pawn up and can give back material for the initiative. Pushing ...e3 is even possible: if White captures, the e-file is shut so the rook cannot go after the doubled pawns, and White's own pawn structure stays damaged. (Gusev-Muratov, Novgorod 1961, 0-1.) } 15. Bg4 { White pins down your king with the bishop on g4 which eyes e6. } (15. Qe1 Nd4 16. Bd1 Qc7 { Another tough defence, Black has the initiative (about -0.6). }) 15... Kf7 16. Qc1 Qd4 { The queen attacks b2 and the bishop on g4. } 17. Rd1 Qxb2 18. Qf4+ Bf6 { The bishop blocks the check and White's attack runs out of steam, so Black keeps a slight edge (about -0.5). }) 10... dxe4 11. Ne1 Qxd4 $19 { [Engine verdict: winning for Black (-2.7). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the e-file.] } 12. Rb1 { Black is a pawn up with a monster on d4. White tries to keep the b2 pawn and the queen. } (12. Qxd4 Nxd4 13. Bd1 Be6 14. Rc1 b5 { White cannot regain the pawn and Black is winning (about -3.0). }) 12... h6 { You kick the bishop first. } 13. Be3 Qxd1 14. Bxd1 f5 { Black has the extra pawn and a solid central majority. } 15. Nc2 g5 16. b3 f4 { Black just rolls the kingside pawns, winning (about -2.7). }) (6. cxd5 $10 { This variation tends to help Black because the pressure on the centre has disappeared. } 6... Nxd5 7. Nxd5 { Nxd5 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.5): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s); White has an isolated pawn. (the engine edge for Black coexists with White's extra material here because this is a known pawn-grab-for-compensation pattern in this line, not a tactical oversight - Black has concrete compensation: faster development, open lines, or attacking chances that outweigh the missing pawn(s)) } (7. Bc4 $14 { Another move order for white } 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 Bg7 9. Nf3 O-O 10. Bf4 Nc6 11. O-O b6 12. Rc1 Bb7 13. Re1 e6 $14 { This set-up is worth remembering whenever White plays Bc4. } 14. Ng5 { White sets a trap. The knight eyes e6 and f7, and the bishop on c4 supports both. If you ignore it, Nxe6 wins a pawn. } (14. h3 Ne7 15. Ne5 Nd5 { A quieter alternative, equal. }) 14... Ne7 { The knight guards e6. } 15. Nxf7 { White tries a combination to break through. } 15... Rxf7 16. Bxe6 Bd5 17. Bxf7+ Bxf7 18. c4 Nf5 { White has given two minor pieces for a rook and two pawns, so material is about level and the position is balanced (about +0.2). It is critical to keep the bishop b7 on the long diagonal. }) 7... Qxd5 $15 { A queen on an exposed central square can be harassed, so it is risky to leave it there. } 8. Qf3 { The queen on d5 is exposed, so White develops with tempo, attacking it and hoping to follow with Nb5 or Be3. } (8. Be3 Bg7 9. Ne2 Nc6 10. Nc3 Qa5 11. Bb5 O-O 12. Bxc6 bxc6 { Another way to harass the queen, but Black is comfortable (about -0.5). }) 8... Be6 { Offer the trade. Black's pieces are more active than White's. } 9. Qxd5 Bxd5 10. Ne2 Bg7 11. Nf4 Be4 12. f3 Bc6 { The d4 pawn is isolated and the bishop is safe on c6. You keep a small edge (about -0.4). }) 6... Bg7 $14 { Black need not rush to protect the d-pawn. Even if White wins it, that pawn is weak, with no other pawns to support it, and will probably fall soon. Development matters more than safety here. } { [%csl Gg7][%cal Gf8g7] } 7. cxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc4d5] } 7... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 8. Be2 $14 { White tries once more to hold on to the pawn. } { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gf1e2] } (8. Nf3 $10 { White can also let the d-pawn go and develop freely. White keeps full flexibility while Black also develops easily, so both sides get what they want and the outcome is more drawish. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 8... Nbd7 { [%cal Bb8d7] } 9. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 9... Nb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd7b6] } 10. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 10... Nfxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 11. Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc3d5] } 11... Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bb6d5] } 12. Bd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } 12... Bg4 $10 { Black is also doing well here. } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 13. Ne5 { White attacks the bishop on g4 and wants to gain tempi to grab b7 with the queen. } (13. Be4 Bxf3 14. Bxf3 e6 { A simple line that gives Black a full game (about -0.0). }) 13... Bc8 14. Bc4 Be6 { The bishop returns and defends d5. } 15. Qxb7 Bxe5 16. dxe5 Nb6 { The knight attacks the bishop on c4, and the d2 bishop is loose after Bxe6. } 17. Bxe6 Qxd2 { White has taken a pawn but Black has activity, so the game is balanced (about +0.3). }) (8. Bg5 $10 { Another move that white can actually do is to try and sabotage black's plan by trying his best to get what he wants to get anyways. Black is aiming for the isolated d pawn and white is trying to delay black's operation by cutting off one of his attacking piece. } { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 8... Nbd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 9. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 9... Nb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd7b6] } 10. Bxf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg5f6] } 10... exf6 $10 { Black is happy to get the e file open for his rook to take advantage of. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be7f6] } 11. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 11... a5 { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 12. a4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba2a4] } 12... Qd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bd8d6] } 13. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 13... Qb4 $10 { A queen exchange would favour Black: White has doubled, isolated pawns that are likely to fall, and Black has enough pieces to attack them. The initiative and the advantage lie with Black. } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bd6b4] } 14. Qxb4 { Qxb4 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2): White has a material edge of about 10 point(s); White has an isolated pawn. } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb3b4] } (14. Ra3 $15 { White declines the queen trade and hopes for rapid development, unwilling to give up the initiative and wanting Black to defend. } { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba1a3] } 14... Rd8 { [%cal Bf8d8] } 15. Rd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bf1d1] } 15... Bf5 { [%cal Bc8f5] } 16. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bf3d2] } 16... Rac8 { [%csl Bc8][%cal Ba8c8] } 17. Nf1 { [%csl Bf1][%cal Bd2f1] } 17... Bd7 $10 { The a4 pawn is a target for Black. White's position is slowly crumbling: it is defensible, yet White is under constant pressure and may not enjoy defending it. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bf5d7] } 18. Ne3 { White regroups the f1 knight to cover c4 and d5, because the a4 pawn is under pressure. } (18. g3 Nxa4 19. Qxb4 axb4 20. Rxa4 bxc3 21. Rb4 c2 22. Rc1 Bh6 { If White lets a4 fall, the passed c2 pawn is a monster (about -0.5). }) 18... f5 19. Qa2 f4 { You kick the knight and gain space. } 20. Nc2 Qd6 21. Bd3 h5 22. Qb3 Bg4 { Black keeps pressing on the kingside with ...h4 ideas. Black stays slightly better (about -0.4). }) 14... axb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Ba5b4] } 15. Ne4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 15... Nxd5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair; Black has doubled pawns on the b/f-file.] } 16. Nd6 { White places the knight on d6 to hit b7 and the e8 and f7 squares. Black has the bishop pair but a ragged structure. } (16. a5 Rd8 17. Nc5 Nf4 { A pawn push that hits b6, but Black is fine and a touch better (about -0.3). }) 16... Bd7 17. Bb5 Bxb5 18. axb5 b6 { You cement the knight outpost, but White hopes for Ra6. } 19. Ra6 Rad8 20. Nc4 Rb8 { Equal (about -0.1), and the doubled f6 and b4 pawns are not a real worry. }) 8... Nbd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 9. Bf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Ge2f3] } 9... Nb6 { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gd7b6] } 10. Bf4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gc1f4] } 10... Bg4 $14 { Black removes the main support of the d5-pawn. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gc8g4] } 11. Bxg4 { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gf3g4] } 11... Nxg4 { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gf6g4] } 12. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 12... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg4f6] } 13. d6 $14 { The engine's choice. It matters for two reasons: Black has to retake with the e-pawn, producing an isolated pawn, and since the dark-squared bishop protects d5, ...e6 is not a sensible alternative. } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gd5d6] } 13... exd6 $14 { Recapturing with the e-pawn leaves an isolated pawn. White seems to have gained something, but both sides now have an isolated d-pawn and each can be targeted. An isolated pawn can serve as an attacking asset too. } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Ge7d6] } 14. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 14... Qd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gd8d7] } 15. Rfe1 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gf1e1] } 15... Rfe8 { [%csl Ge8][%cal Gf8e8] } 16. a4 { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga2a4] } 16... Rxe1+ { [%csl Ge1][%cal Ge8e1] } 17. Rxe1 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Ga1e1] } 17... Re8 { [%csl Ge8][%cal Ga8e8] } 18. Rxe8+ { [%csl Ge8][%cal Ge1e8] } 18... Nxe8 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] [%csl Ge8][%cal Gf6e8] } 19. a5 { White kicks the b6 knight so the queenside pawn majority starts to move. } (19. h3 Qc6 20. a5 Qc4 21. Qa3 Nc8 { Black is a touch more active (about -0.0). }) 19... Nc8 20. h3 a6 { You secure b5 and keep the queenside shut. } 21. d5 Ne7 { The central break opens the position but Black's knight has a good square. } 22. Nd2 Qf5 { Both sides now hit loose pieces, the Bf4 and the pawn on b7, so the position is balanced (about 0.0). } *


