1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. Here White has pushed a3-b4-c5 to grab queenside space rather than simplifying, while Black has answered in the center with ...e5, so this is a more dynamic version of the Panov structure rather than a quiet isolani fight. Black's plan is to make the ...e5 break count: open the position for the bishop pair and the better-placed knights before White's queenside pawns (b4-b5, or c5-c6) start cramping the position permanently. Trading off White's advanced c5-pawn at the right moment (often with ...b6 or a well-timed piece trade that lets ...dxc5 or ...bxc5 happen safely) is usually more important than any kingside ambition. If Black just sits still, White's space advantage on the queenside slowly becomes decisive.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.7 to +0.8, a modest White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +1 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Bxh2+? on moves 9 to 11 loses material (about 3.9); ...Bxc5? at move 7 loses material (about 2.8); 7...Bf8? (about 0.9; prefer ...Bc7); 11...Qe7?! (about 0.7; prefer ...Nc6); 10...Nbd7?! (about 0.6; prefer ...a6); 9...a6?! (about 0.6; prefer ...h6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 7.Be2?! Nc6 (costs White about 0.5); 15.Nxe5? Nxe5 (costs White about 1.7; engine-found, not in the chapter tree).
Chapter #6 continues the Panov-Botvinnik and Accelerated Panov. Other Panov-Botvinnik and Accelerated Panov chapters: #4, #7, #8. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #11. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. exd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge4d5] } 3... cxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc6d5] } 4. c4 $10 { The Panov-Botvinnik Attack. After the pawn exchanges White is left with an isolated d-pawn and accepts that weakness in return for open lines, piece activity and attacking chances. Black's task is to blockade, trade pieces and keep the structure healthy. } { [%cal Gc2c4] } 4... e6 $14 { This is the Horwitz-style answer to the Panov-Botvinnik Attack, an improvised Queen's Gambit. It blocks the light-squared bishop but lets Black steer towards a Queen's Gambit Declined. } { [%csl Gc4,Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 5. Nc3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 5... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 6. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 6... Bd6 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores +0.67, while Bb4 scores +0.11, so it costs about 0.6. It is playable, but it is a concession] [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf8d6] } 7. c5 $14 { Instead of the calm developing move Be2 shown in the main line, White has the more ambitious c5, gaining space and kicking the bishop on d6 before Black can react. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc4c5] } (7. Be2 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 18, White's point of view): this is the chapter's original move; it scores +0.12, while c5 scores +0.64, so it costs White about 0.5. The chapter's original line continues here as a variation.] [%csl Ge2][%cal Gf1e2] } 7... Nc6 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores +0.42, while dxc4 scores +0.05, so it costs about 0.4. It is playable, but it is a concession] [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 8. O-O { O-O leads to a small White edge (+0.3): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } (8. c5 $14 { Engine-suggested line (Stockfish 16, depth 20), not in the original chapter. This is White's strongest try here (about +0.45); the chapter's own O-O scores about +0.10. Review before relying on it. } 8... Bc7 9. Bg5 O-O 10. O-O h6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5).] } 11. Bh4 { White keeps the pin, planning Qc1 to hit h6 so that Bxf6 and Qxh6 become a threat. } (11. Be3 Ne4 12. Nb5 Bb8 { A passive choice, Black is fine (about -0.2). }) 11... b6 { You undermine the c5 pawn. } 12. Qc1 bxc5 13. dxc5 a5 14. Bb5 Ne5 { Black trades knights to ease the pressure. } 15. Nxe5 Bxe5 { White keeps a slight edge (about +0.5). }) 8... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 9. c5 $14 { This line is rather a bit complicated since white has successfully seizes the center. White does have a spacious advantage whereas black is going to attack from the rear. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc4c5] } (9. Bg5 $10 { White pins the knight: if the black queen moves, White can take the knight, wreck Black's kingside pawns and expose the king. } 9... dxc4 10. Bxc4 Be7 11. Re1 b6 12. Rc1 { Rc1 leads to roughly equal (+0.1): White has an isolated pawn. } (12. d5 $10 { White wants to open up Black's position. } 12... Nxd5 13. Bxd5 exd5 14. Qxd5 Qxd5 15. Nxd5 Bxg5 16. Nxg5 Bf5 $10 { Black stands well here. } 17. Rac1 { White takes the c-file. } (17. Red1 Rfd8 18. Rac1 Rac8 { Black is fine here. }) 17... Rad8 18. Ne3 Nd4 { A knight on d4 is untouchable. } 19. Nxf5 Nxf5 20. Nf3 Nd4 21. Nxd4 Rxd4 { The simplified position is balanced (about 0.0) and the rook ending is drawish. }) 12... Bb7 13. Bd3 h6 $10 { Black is doing fine and should have good play. } 14. Bxf6 (14. Bh4 Nb4 15. Bb1 Bxf3 16. Qxf3 Qxd4 { Here you win a pawn. }) 14... Bxf6 15. Be4 { White targets b7 and eyes the d5 break. } 15... Rc8 16. d5 exd5 17. Nxd5 Bxb2 18. Rc2 Ba3 { Black grabs a pawn but White gets activity, so it is equal (about 0.0). }) 9... Bc7 { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gd6c7] } 10. Be3 { Be3 leads to roughly equal (+0.0), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gc1e3] } (10. Bg5 $14 { Engine-suggested line (Stockfish 16, depth 20), not in the original chapter. This is White's strongest try here (about +0.61); the chapter's own Be3 scores about -0.08. Review before relying on it. } 10... b6 11. Bb5 Ne7 12. b4 h6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7).] } 13. Bxf6 { White wrecks your kingside pawns and hopes to attack with Qe2, Bd3 and Nh4. } (13. Bh4 Nf5 14. Bg3 Nxg3 15. hxg3 Ng4 { Black is okay here. }) 13... gxf6 14. Qe2 Ng6 15. Bd3 Kg7 { The king covers h6 and f6 well. } 16. Rfe1 bxc5 17. bxc5 a6 { White keeps a modest edge (about +0.7) thanks to your damaged structure, but there are no direct threats. }) 10... Ne4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf6e4] } 11. Nxe4 { Nxe4 leads to roughly equal (+0.0): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s). } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } (11. Qc2 $15 { Qc2 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bc2][%cal Bd1c2] } 11... f5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bf7f5] } 12. Rfd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bf1d1] } 12... Qf6 $15 { Black's plan is to attack the white king. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd8f6] } 13. Nb5 { White attacks the bishop on c7 and tries to get something going. } (13. h3 Bd7 14. Rab1 Be8 15. b4 a6 { White's plan on the queenside is too slow, Black attacks. }) 13... Bb8 14. Nc3 a5 15. Bb5 h6 16. Re1 f4 { Black drives the bishop and starts a kingside attack. } 17. Bxc6 bxc6 { Black has a pleasant attacking position (about -0.7). }) 11... dxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 12. Ng5 { [%cal Gf3g5] } 12... f5 { [%csl Gg5,Gf5][%cal Gf7f5] } 13. Bc4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Ge2c4] } 13... Qf6 $10 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores -0.18, while Qe8 scores -0.66, so it costs about 0.5. It is playable, but it is a concession] [%csl Gf6][%cal Gd8f6] } 14. f3 { The main try is to undermine the e4 pawn and open the f-file against your king. } (14. d5 Na5 15. Be2 exd5 16. Qxd5+ Kh8 17. Bh5 h6 18. Nf7+ Kh7 { A tempting gambit try but it just fails, Black is better (about -0.7). }) 14... Na5 { You hit the Bc4. } 15. Be2 h6 16. Nh3 exf3 17. Bxf3 g5 18. Nf2 Nc6 { Black is a bit better (about -0.2) and the king is safe. }) 7... Bc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd6c7] } 8. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 8... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 9. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 9... h6 $14 { This gives White a genuine, if modest, space-based edge that the passive Be2 does not. Black is still solid after retreating the bishop, but should be aware c5 is the sharper and more testing option for White here. } { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 10. b4 $14 { Engine continuation (Stockfish 16, depth 18), not in the original chapter: best play for both sides, added so that the line reaches the target position. Review before relying on it. } 10... a6 11. Re1 Nc6 12. a3 Re8 13. h3 Bd7 14. Be3 e5 $16 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.9).] } 15. dxe5 { White captures the centre pawn and gets a small edge. } (15. Bc2 e4 16. Nd2 b6 { A less ambitious choice, Black is fine (about -0.3). }) 15... Bxe5 16. Nxe5 Rxe5 17. Bf4 { The bishop hits the rook and Black has to trade. } 17... Rxe1+ 18. Qxe1 d4 19. Na4 Nd5 { Black has a passed pawn on d4 but it is also a weak spot. White keeps a modest edge (about +0.8). } *


