1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. A genuine Queen's Gambit Declined structure transplanted into the Caro-Kann via the Panov move order: White's pawn has gone to d5 (not d4), giving a clamped, QGD-Carlsbad-style position where Black's king often stays in the center a little longer. The half-open a- and e-files for White and the d-file for Black tell you where the rooks belong. Black's practical resource is the knight route to c4 or e4 and pressure against the isolated/advanced White pawns on the queenside; avoid committing to ...e5 too early, since it can hand White's bishop a strong outpost. This structure rewards patience, it is rarely resolved by force, only by which side improves its worst-placed piece first.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.0 to +0.8, a small White edge. Note: along the chapter's line the engine value falls from +0.8 (move 7) to +0.0 at the target, because White does not play the engine's first choice at every step. Against best play, expect the higher figure. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +1 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Nxd5? on moves 7 to 12 loses material (about 3.4); 7...Nb4? (about 2.7; prefer ...Na5); 12...Qd6? (about 1.4; prefer ...Qc7); 12...Qd8? (about 1.1; prefer ...Qc7); 13...Be7? (about 1.0; prefer ...a6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 13.d6? Qxd6 (costs White about 2.0; engine-found, not in the chapter tree); 13.Qd3? Qxe5 (costs White about 2.0; engine-found, not in the chapter tree).
Chapter #7 continues the Panov-Botvinnik and Accelerated Panov. Other Panov-Botvinnik and Accelerated Panov chapters: #4, #5, #8. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. exd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge4d5] } 3... cxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc6d5] } 4. c4 $10 { The Panov-Botvinnik Attack. After the pawn exchanges White is left with an isolated d-pawn and accepts that weakness in return for open lines, piece activity and attacking chances. Black's task is to blockade, trade pieces and keep the structure healthy. } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc2c4] } 4... Nf6 { [%cal Gg8f6] } 5. Nc3 { [%csl Gf6,Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 5... Nc6 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE 5...Nc6 IF you want a flexible, active reply that leads to isolated-pawn positions with plenty of piece activity (medium risk, medium theory). The ...a6 idea covers the d5 square cheaply. For a quieter, structural game see Chapters #4-#5. After 5...Nc6 White's main moves are 6.Nf3, 6.Bg5 and 6.Bf4. Black replies ...a6 or ...g6 depending on the system. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 6. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } (6. Bg5 { 6.Bg5 - pins the knight to the queen and keeps the central tension a little longer before White commits to how to recapture. } 6... e6 { 6...e6 simply finishes development; the pin is not dangerous since ...Be7 or meeting Bxf6 with gxf6 are both comfortable for Black. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2).] } (6... a6 { 6.Bg5 pins the knight and prepares to take on f6 or play c5. 6...a6 prevents Bb5 and prepares ...Be6. [Engine verdict: about +0.05.] } 7. Bxf6 (7. Nf3 Be6 8. c5 g6 { 7.Nf3 Be6 8.c5 g6 leads to a complicated fight, and White's Bxf6 gives up the bishop pair. [Engine verdict: about +0.25.] } 9. Bxf6 exf6 10. g3 (10. Be2 Bg7 11. O-O h5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.00).] }) 10... f5 11. Bg2 f4 12. O-O fxg3 13. hxg3 Bg7 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.3).] }) 7... exf6 8. cxd5 Ne7 9. Qa4+ Bd7 10. Qb3 Nf5 11. Nf3 (11. Qxb7 { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -0.8).] }) 11... Nd6 12. Bd3 Be7 13. O-O O-O { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.13).] }) 7. Nf3 (7. cxd5 exd5 8. Nf3 Bb4 9. Bb5 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 Qa5 11. Bxc6+ bxc6 { A simplification, equal. }) 7... Bb4 8. Rc1 h6 9. Bh4 O-O 10. a3 Be7 { You retreat so that Bxf6 can be met by Bxf6. } 11. c5 g5 { The thrust kicks the bishop and Black is fine. Roughly equal (about +0.2). }) (6. c5 { 6.c5 - grabs queenside space instead of resolving the center, hoping to restrict Black's bishop's diagonal long-term. } 6... e6 { 6...e6 just continues developing normally; the pawn on c5 is static and can become a long-term weakness once Black has castled. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5), within the normal range for this chapter.] } 7. Nf3 (7. Bb5 Be7 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bxc6 bxc6 10. Ne5 Bb7 11. Qb3 Qc8 { White targets the c6 pawn but Black is solid. }) 7... Be7 8. Bb5 O-O 9. O-O Ne4 { You challenge c3 and ease the pin. } 10. Qc2 Bd7 11. a3 Nxc3 { Black plans to undermine c5 with ...b6 and ...e5. White keeps a slight edge (about +0.5). }) (6. cxd5 { 6.cxd5 - resolves the central tension at once rather than adding pressure with Bg5 or c5 first. } 6... Nxd5 { 6...Nxd5 recaptures naturally, reaching a standard isolated-queen's-pawn structure where Black's pieces are already comfortably placed. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2).] } 7. Bc4 { 7.Bc4 develops actively to hit the knight on d5 and eye f7, instead of the quieter Nf3 setups. } 7... Be6 8. Bb3 g6 9. Nf3 { ...and Black holds with accurate development. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.2), a real try but slightly softer than White's main continuations here.] } 9... Bg7 (9... Qd7?! 10. Ng5 Nxc3 11. bxc3 Bd5 12. c4 Bxg2 13. Rg1 h6 14. Nxf7 { The knight strikes at e6 and f7 and White is slightly better (about +0.1). }) 10. O-O O-O 11. Re1 Qd7 { You defend the bishop on e6 and connect the rooks. } 12. Bg5 Rfd8 13. Qd2 Nc7 { The knight steps away from the pin and from Bxd5. } 14. Ba4 { Black is slightly better (about -0.1). }) (6. Bf4 { 6.Bf4 develops the bishop actively to a square where it eyes c7/b8 and supports a later e4 break, instead of the quieter Nf3 setups. } 6... g6 7. Nb5 (7. c5 Bg7 { 6.Bf4 aims at e5 and c7. Black fianchettoes and attacks the c5 pawn. After Bb5 O-O and Nge2 Ng4 the game becomes sharp. [Engine verdict: about -0.1 to -0.6, favouring Black.] } 8. Bb5 O-O 9. Nge2 (9. Nf3 Bg4 { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -0.7).] }) 9... Ng4 10. h3 e5 11. dxe5 (11. hxg4 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.5).] }) 11... Ngxe5 12. Qxd5 a6 13. Bxc6 Nd3+ 14. Kf1 bxc6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.6).] }) 7... e5 8. dxe5 { ...and the center opens up to Black's favor once play is accurate. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.55), a real try but objectively the softer of White's options in this Panov QGD structure.] } 8... Bb4+ { The check forces matters. } (8... Ne4 9. Qxd5 Qa5+ 10. Nc3 Nc5 { A different approach that is equal (about 0.0). }) 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ 10. Qxd2 Nxe5 11. Qb4 Qe7 12. Qxe7+ Kxe7 { Black is slightly better (about -0.5). Watch for Nc7 hitting the rook on a8. }) 6... dxc4 $14 { This is an interesting move order which black gives white space advantage and flexible development but in exchange the d pawn would have been isolated thus giving black some counterplay by pressurizing it. } { [%csl Rd5,Gc4][%cal Gd5c4,Rd4d5,Rf1c4] } (6... a6 { 6...a6 prevents Bb5 and prepares to meet the c4-c5 push. White usually takes on d5 or plays h3 or c5. [Engine verdict: about +0.1.] } 7. cxd5 (7. h3 { 7.h3 is a flexible waiting move that stops ...Bg4. Black takes on c4 and plays ...e6, reaching an equal position. [Engine verdict: about +0.3.] } 7... dxc4 (7... g6 8. c5 Ne4 9. Bd3 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.4).] }) 8