ds to a clear White edge (+1.2): Black has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has an isolated pawn.   (the engine edge for White coexists with Black's extra material here because this is a known pawn-grab-for-compensation pattern in this line, not a tactical oversight - White has concrete compensation: faster development, open lines, or attacking chances that outweigh the missing pawn(s)) } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd4d5] } (7. Bxc4 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 18, White's point of view): this is the chapter's original move; it scores +0.51, while d5 scores +0.97, so it costs White about 0.5. The chapter's original line continues here as a variation.] [%csl Gc4][%cal Gf1c4] } 7... e6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 8. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 8... Be7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gf8e7] } 9. a3 { [%csl Ga3][%cal Ga2a3] } 9... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 10. Re1 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gf1e1] } 10... b6 $14 { Black usually aim for the improvised "Queen's Indian Defence" or QID for short. aiming for QID structure black would usually find play on the queenside. } { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gb7b6] } 11. Bg5 { Bg5 leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White has an isolated pawn. } (11. Bf4 $14 { Feel the wrath of white's domination! But no! Black would play Bd6 and things would have been stabilized by then. } { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bc1f4] } 11... Bb7 { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bc8b7] } 12. Ba2 { [%csl Ba2][%cal Bc4a2] } 12... h6 { [%cal Bh7h6] } 13. Rc1 { [%csl Bc1][%cal Ba1c1] } 13... Rc8 { [%csl Bc8][%cal Ba8c8] } 14. Qd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bd1d2] } 14... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Be7d6] } 15. Bxd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf4d6] } 15... Qxd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bd8d6] } 16. d5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd4d5] } 16... exd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be6d5] } 17. Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc3d5] } 17... Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 18. Qxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd2d5] } 18... Qxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd6d5] } 19. Bxd5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1).] [%csl Bd5][%cal Ba2d5] } 19... Rfd8 { White's bishop on d5 eyes b7 and 