1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Accelerated Panov reaches an isolani structure early and simplifies hard, often trading down to a position where Black's queen and bishop are already very active (Qd5, Be4) while material is light. This is less about long-term structure and more about Black's temporary initiative: use the active pieces to provoke further simplification or a favorable endgame before White's extra development (an open e-file, the bishop pair) starts to matter. Because the position opens up quickly, concrete calculation matters more here than in the slower Panov lines, memorize the forcing sequence rather than relying on general principles, and double-check with the engine line in the PLAN before every further decision point.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.1 to +0.2, roughly equal. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Nxc3? at move 9 loses material (about 2.2); ...Qxd7? at move 14 loses material (about 5.7); 12...Be4? (about 1.7; prefer ...Qxd5); 12...Rc8? (about 1.1; prefer ...Qxd5); 8...Bg4?! (about 0.6; prefer ...e6); 5...g6?! (about 0.4; prefer ...Nxd5).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 19.Ne5? Bxe5 (costs White about 4.6); 10.Nf3? b5 (costs White about 2.1); 11.Nxb5? Nb6 (costs White about 1.1).
Chapter #8 continues the Panov-Botvinnik and Accelerated Panov. Other Panov-Botvinnik and Accelerated Panov chapters: #4, #5, #6. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #11. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. c4 $10 { The Panov Attack is an aggressive opening choice against the Caro Kann. First of all white voluntarily creates an isolated pawn which would be a clear target for black to take on. Second of all White is willing to create weaknesses in exchange for initiative and attack. Even a pawn sacrifice would not matter if white has enough counterplay. Black's goal is to further fortify the position. Develop the pieces with ease and is looking forward to a great game. } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc2c4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. exd5 { exd5 leads to a small White edge (+0.3): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge4d5] } (3. e5 { 3.e5!? - an immediate advance instead of resolving the center with exd5. The pawn on e5 has no support yet, so Black should strike at once. } 3... d4 { 3...d4! is the simplest answer: the pawn grabs space and takes the c3-square away from White's knight, so White has to spend time on an awkward plan like f4/d3 instead of normal, comfortable development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0), comfortable for Black with no memorization burden.] } 4. f4 { The pawn on e5 needs support, so White builds a chain with f4, Nf3 and d3 and hopes his extra space tells. } (4. Nf3 Bg4 5. Qb3 Bxf3 6. e6 Bg4 7. Qxb7 Nd7 8. exd7+ Bxd7 { White grabs b7 but the queen is far from home and you are slightly better (about -0.3). }) 4... g6 5. Nf3 Nh6 { The knight heads for f5, where it eyes d4 and e3. } 6. d3 Bg7 7. Nbd2 O-O 8. Be2 f6 $10 { Your plan is to undermine the e5 pawn with ...f6. The structure is level (about -0.1) and you have a clear way to open the centre. }) (3. cxd5 { 3.cxd5!? (resolving with the c-pawn first, keeping the e4 pawn on the board for now) cxd5 4.e5!? - White pushes immediately instead of developing normally. This just hands Black a tempo, since the pawn isn't doing anything there yet. } 3... cxd5 4. e5 (4. exd5 Nf6 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE THIS IF you want to meet the Accelerated Panov (2.c4 d5 3.cxd5 cxd5 4.exd5 Nf6) with calm development: ...Nbd7, ...g6, ...Bg7 and ...O-O. It is solid and low-risk. After 4...Nf6 White can keep the pawn with 5.Qa4+, or play 5.Nc3 and regain material. } 5. Qa4+ (5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. Bc4 { 5.Nc3 Nxd5 6.Bc4 Nb6 7.Bb3 is the typical set-up. Black develops with ...Nc6 and ...Bf5 and reaches equality. [Engine verdict: about +0.2.] } 6... Nb6 7. Bb3 Nc6 8. Nf3 Bf5 9. d4 e6 10. O-O Be7 11. d5 Nxd5 12. Nxd5 exd5 13. Bxd5 O-O { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.04).] }) 5... Nbd7 6. Nc3 g6 7. Nf3 Bg7 8. Bc4 O-O 9. d3 a6 10. Qa3 { Black has a fully equal position. White's queen on a3 keeps an eye on e7. [Engine verdict: about 0.0.] } (10. O-O { Careless: castling allows ...b5 with a decisive tactic. [Engine verdict: about -2.9 for White.] } 10... b5 11. Bxb5 Nb6 { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -4.0).] }) 10... b6 11. O-O Bb7 12. Be3 (12. Re1 Re8 13. Bf4 (13. Be3 b5 14. Bb3 Nb6 15. Bxb6 Qxb6 16. Qb4 Qd6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.17).] }) 13... b5 14. Bb3 Bf8 15. d6 Bxf3 16. gxf3 exd6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 12... b5 13. Bb3 Nb6 14. Bxb6 Qxb6 15. Qxe7 a5 16. Qe3 Qxe3 { Both sides play natural moves; White stays a pawn up for a moment but ...Qxe3 equalises material. [Engine verdict: about -0.6 for White.] [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.6).] }) 4... Qa5 { 4...Qa5+ is simplest: the check forces White to block with a piece (usually Nc3 or Bd2) rather than gaining any time, and Black develops naturally from there with an easy, fully equal position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.0).] } 5. Nf3 { 5.Nf3 develops simply and defends the e5 pawn, rather than the sharper d4 push some sources prefer immediately. } 5... Nc6 6. Nc3 Bg4 7. d4 { ...and Black's active queen and central pressure give a real, lasting edge. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-0.45), worth recognizing but objectively softer than the immediate d4 try.] } 7... e6 { You solidify the d5 pawn and develop. The queen on a5 and Bg4 are active. } (7... Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nxd4 9. Qxd5 Qxd5 10. Nxd5 O-O-O 11. Ne3 e6 { A simplifying alternative that is comfortable (about -0.1). }) 8. Be2 Bb4 9. Qd3 Nge7 10. O-O O-O 11. Bd2 Qd8 12. h3 { Black has a pleasant, harmonious position (about -0.2). }) 3... cxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc6d5] } 4. cxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc4d5] } 4... Nf6 $14 { Taking back with the queen is too slow, since the knight would drive it away after Nc3. Recapturing the d-pawn with ...Nf6 is better. } { [%csl Gf6,Rf3,Rc3,Ra4][%cal Gg8f6,Rg1f3,Rb1c3,Rd1a4] } 5. Nc3 { Nc3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } (5. Nf3 $10 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 5... Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 6. Nc3 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bb1c3] } 6... Nf6 $14 { Rather than capture the knight (which fixes White's structure, since the isolated d-pawn is repaired by recapturing), Black can simply develop with ...Nf6. } 7. Bc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 7... Nc6 $14 { The f-pawn stays a target for White, so Black should watch for threats there. } { [%csl Bf7,Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 8. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 8... e6 $14 { ...e6 must be played to stop White advancing the d-pawn, even though it blocks the light-squared bishop. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 9. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 9... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6,Bd5,Bg5][%cal Bf8d6,Bd4d5,Bc1g5] } 10. d5 { d5 leads to a modest White edge (+0.6): White has an isolated pawn; White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } (10. Bg5 $14 { Bg5 leads to a modest White edge (+0.7): White has an isolated pawn; White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 10... O-O $14 { Black stands fine here. } { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 11. Qe2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 11... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 12. Bh4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bg5h4] } 12... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bd6e7] } 13. Rad1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Ba1d1] } 13... Nd5 { [%csl Bd5,Bh4][%cal Bf6d5,Be7h4,Bh4e7] } 14. Bg3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bh4g3] } 14... Bb4 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5).] [%csl Bb4][%cal Be7b4] } 15. Rc1 { White adds pressure on the c-file and prepares Bd3 aiming at your kingside. } (15. Nxd5 exd5 16. Bb3 Be6 17. Ne5 Qb6 { This gives you an isolated d5 pawn to look after, but White gets little (about 0.3). }) 15... Nce7 { Guards d5 and gives the knight a better job. } 16. Bd3 a6 17. Nxd5 Nxd5 18. a3 Be7 19. h3 Bd7 $14 { Your knight on d5 is a rock and the bishop retreats with a solid camp. White keeps a small edge (about 0.5) but there are no targets, so just complete development with ...Rc8. }) 10... exd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be6d5] } 11. Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc3d5] } 11... Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 12. Qxd5 { [%csl Bd5,Bf7][%cal Bd1d5,Bc4f7] } 12... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 13. Bg5 { White keeps the queen on d5 and lines up the pin on f6, hoping for Bxf6 and Qxb7. } 13... Be7 14. Bxe7 Qxd5 15. Bxd5 Nxe7 { The point: you recapture on e7 with the knight, which also hits the bishop on d5. } 16. Bc4 Nc6 17. Rfe1 Bg4 $14 { The queens are off and your pieces are well placed. White keeps a small edge (about 0.2) but you have no weaknesses and can play for ...Rfd8. }) (5. Qa4+ $10 { White eventually wants to deliver a check and try to keep hold of the isolated d-pawn. } { [%csl Ba4][%cal Bd1a4] } 5... Nbd7 $10 { White would try to hold unto the pawn in case black ever tries to pressurise it. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 6. Bc4 { Bc4 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } (6. Nc3 { 6.Nc3 develops naturally after the check, heading for a kingside fianchetto setup with g3 and Bg2. } 6... g6 7. g3 Bg7 8. Bg2 { ...reaching a level position where Black's development lead compensates for the slightly exposed king. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.25), a sound continuation after the Qa4+ check.] } 8... O-O 9. Nge2 b5 { The standard answer to a queen on a4: the pawn hits it with tempo. } 10. Nxb5 Nb6 { Hits the queen again and attacks the d5 pawn, which has lost its support. } 11. Qd1 Bf5 12. Nbd4 Bd3 $10 { The bishop on d3 attacks Ne2 and cramps White. The position is level (about -0.1); keep the pressure on d5 and the long diagonal. }) 6... g6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bg7g6] } 7. d3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd2d3] } 7... Bg7 { [%csl Bg7][%cal Bf8g7] } 8. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 8... O-O { [%csl Bg8,Bf3,Bg5][%cal Be8g8,Bg1f3,Bc1g5] } 9. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to roughly equal (+0.0): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } (9. Bg5 $17 { White may try to eliminate on of black's major piece in the target of the d pawn. Problem is black gets to have a bishop pair and a comfortable domination on the board. } { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 9... a6 { [%csl Ba6,Bf3,Bd1][%cal Ba7a6,Bg1f3,Ba4d1] } 10. Qd1 $17 { The biggest problem for White is the e-pawn: the isolated pawn cannot be held, so a piece ends up being lost. } { [%csl Bd5,Bd1][%cal Ba4d1] } (10. Nf3? $19 { ...b5 forks the bishop and the queen. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 10... b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 11. Nxb5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bc3b5] } 11... Nb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd7b6] } 12. Qb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Ba4b3] } 12... axb5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Ba6b5] } 13. Bxb5 $19 { [Engine verdict: winning for Black (-4.4). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the d-file.] [%csl Bb5][%cal Bc4b5] } 13... Qxd5 { Takes the pawn back with the queen, which hits both the bishop on b5 and the queen on b3 and also releases the pin on f6, so White has no time for Bxf6 tricks. The plan after that is simple: trade pieces and convert. } 14. O-O Bd7 { Offers to swap White's last active piece. } 15. Bxd7 Nbxd7 16. d4 Rfb8 17. Qxd5 Nxd5 18. a4 $19 { The queen swap kills White's last chance of an attack, and with the better structure you are close to winning (about -4.2). Finish by doubling rooks on the b-file. }) 10... b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 11. Bb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc4b3] } 11... Nc5 { [%csl Bc5,Bb3][%cal Bd7c5,Bc5b3] } 12. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 12... Bb7 { [%csl Bb7,Bd5][%cal Bc8b7,Bb7d5,Bf6d5] } 13. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 13... Nxb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc5b3] } 14. axb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Ba2b3] } 14... Nxd5 $17 { Effectively winning a piece. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 15. Nxd5 { White trades one of your active pieces and hopes to blunt the bishop pair. } (15. Bd2 Nc7 16. Re1 Ne6 { You keep both bishops and a lasting pull (about -1.8). }) 15... Qxd5 16. d4 { Challenges the centre at once, because the queen on d5 and the bishops on b7 and g7 would otherwise dominate. } 16... Rfe8 17. Re1 e6 18. Be3 Red8 19. h3 h6 $17 { You keep both bishops and the more active pieces while White defends (about -1.5). Follow up with ...Qd7 and ...Rac8, leaning on the long diagonals. }) 9... a6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Ba7a6] } 10. Qa3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba4a3] } 10... b6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 11. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 11... Bb7 { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bc8b7] } 12. Re1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 12... Re8 $10 { Black should protect the e-pawn first. } { [%csl Be8][%cal Bf8e8] } 13. Bg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 13... b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb6b5] } 14. Bb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc4b3] } 14... Nb6 $10 { The d-pawn will eventually fall. } { [%csl Bb6,Be4,Bf6][%cal Bd7b6,Bc3e4,Bg5f6] } 15. Ne4 { Ne4 leads to a small Black edge (-0.3): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } (15. Bxf6 $15 { Whether or not White takes the knight, the d-pawn will hang anyway. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg5f6] } 15... Bxf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg7f6] } 16. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd3d4] } 16... Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bb6d5] } 17. Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc3d5] } 17... Bxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bb7d5] } 18. Bxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bb3d5] } 18... Qxd5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.8).] [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd8d5] } 19. Rac1 { White uses the open c-file and the threat of Rc7 as compensation for the isolated d4 pawn. } 19... Bxd4 20. Nxd4 Qxd4 21. g3 Qf6 22. Rc7 e5 23. Qc5 e4 $15 { You have won the isolated pawn, but the rook on the seventh and the queen on c5 give White real activity, so keep your back rank guarded with ...Re8 and ...Rac8. The edge is modest (about -0.7). }) 15... Nfxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 16. Nc5 { [%csl Bc5,Bb7][%cal Be4c5,Bc5b7] } 16... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 17. Bh4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bg5h4] } 17... a5 { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba6a5] } 18. Bg3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bh4g3] } 18... Qc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bc7c6] } 19. Ne5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 19... Bxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bg7e5] } 20. Bxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bg3e5] } 20... a4?! $19 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores -2.21; b4 scores -5.85, so b4 was stronger] [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba5a4] } 21. Bxd5 { White gets rid of the strong knight before it can reach e3, but the hole on e3 is still there. } (21. Nxb7? b4 22. Bxa4 Nxa4 23. Qb3 Qxb7 { The queen on a3 and the bishop on b3 are both hit and White runs out of good moves (about -4.2). }) 21... Nxd5 22. d4 Ne3 { The point: ...Nc2 forks the queen on a3 and the rook on a1. } 23. f3 Nc2 24. Qc3 Nxa1 25. Rxa1 Rac8 $17 { You have won the exchange for nothing and the bishop on b7 and rooks dominate (about -2.3). }) (5. Bb5+ { 5.Bb5+ inserts the check immediately rather than developing with Nc3 or Qa4+ first, hoping to provoke a weakening ...Nbd7/...Bd7. } 5... Nbd7 6. Nc3 a6 7. Qa4 { ...and Black untangles comfortably. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1), a third sound try alongside Nc3 and Qa4+ in this Accelerated Panov structure.] } 7... g6 { Do not play on the queenside with the knight still pinned: Bb5 and Qa4 already pile up on d7. Fianchetto and castle so that Bxd7 can be answered by Nxd7. } (7... Qc7 8. d4 g6 9. Nge2 Bg7 10. Bf4 Qd8 { The queen has lost two tempi to Bf4 and White keeps a comfortable edge (about 0.6). }) 8. Nf3 Bg7 9. O-O O-O 10. Bxd7 Nxd7 11. d3 b5 $10 { The pin is gone and ...b5 gains space with a threat to the queen. Level (about -0.1); White's Qh4 achieves little. } 12. Qh4) 5... Nxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gf6d5] } 6. Nf3 $14 { Engine line (Stockfish 16, depth 18), not in the original chapter: White's strongest continuation with Black's best replies. Review before relying on it. } (6. Nxd5 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 18, White's point of view): this is the chapter's original move; it scores -0.61, while Nf3 scores +0.21, so it costs White about 0.8. The chapter's original line continues here as a variation.] [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc3d5] } 6... Qxd5 $15 { Black's Queen is dangerous in the middle of the center, especially when it cannot even be disturbed by white's minor pieces. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd8d5] } 7. Qf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gd1f3] } 7... Be6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Gc8e6] } 8. Bd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 8... Qxf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gd5f3] } 9. Nxf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 9... Nc6 { [%csl Gf3,Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 10. O-O { O-O leads to a modest Black edge (-0.7): White has an isolated pawn; White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } (10. Be4 $15 { Engine-suggested line (Stockfish 16, depth 20), not in the original chapter. This is White's strongest try here (about -0.36); the chapter's own O-O scores about -0.82. Review before relying on it. } 10... Rd8 11. Bxc6+ bxc6 12. O-O f6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.4). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair.] } 13. d3 { White offers a pawn for activity. } (13. Re1 Bd5 14. d3 e5 { Black is comfortable too. }) 13... Rxd3 14. Be3 Kd7 15. Rfc1 g6 16. Ne1 Rd6 17. Rc3 Bg7 { Black holds the pawn and the bishop pair, a slight edge (about -0.3). }) 10... O-O-O $15 { The Accelerated Panov Attack. } { [%csl Gc8][%cal Ge8c8] } 11. Be4 { White offers a trade of bishops to blunt the d5 bishop. } (11. Be2 f6 12. d4 Bd5 13. Be3 e6 { Another way for White, similar to the main line. }) 11... Bd5 12. Bxd5 Rxd5 13. d4 e6 14. Be3 f6 15. Rac1 Kd7 { Black has a slightly more comfortable game (about -0.6). }) (6. d4 $10 { Before trading on d5, White's more accurate move order is d4 first, building the center immediately rather than allowing Black any extra tempo. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 6... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 7. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White has an isolated pawn. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } (7. Bc4 { 7.Bc4 develops actively to hit the knight on d5 and eye f7, instead of the quieter Nf3 setups. } 7... Be6 8. Bb3 g6 9. Nf3 { ...and Black holds with accurate development. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.2), a real try but slightly softer than White's main continuations here.] } 9... Bg7 (9... h6 10. O-O Bg7 11. Re1 O-O 12. Rxe6 fxe6 13. Qd3 Rf6 { White cannot justify the exchange sacrifice, so Black is fine. }) 10. O-O O-O 11. Re1 Qd7 12. Bg5 Rfd8 13. Qd2 Nc7 14. Ba4 { Black is fine with the bishop pair gone and a solid position (about -0.1). }) 7... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 8. Qb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd1b3] } 8... Bxf3 $10 { Only a small improvement, and the position remains close to balanced - useful to know, but not a serious gap in the existing repertoire coverage. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 9. gxf3 { White must retake with the pawn: the f-pawns are doubled, but the g-file opens and the queen on b3 eyes both d5 and b7. } (9. Bc4?! Bxg2 10. Bxd5 Bxd5 11. Nxd5 e6 12. Qxb7 Rc8 { Looks active, but you simply win the g2 pawn with a hit on the rook and stay well ahead (about -2.2). }) 9... e6 { Guards the knight so Qxd5 is not a threat; the b7 pawn is the price. } 10. Qxb7 Nxd4 { Now ...Nc2+ would fork king and rook, so White needs the next check. } 11. Bb5+ Nxb5 12. Qc6+ Ke7 13. Qxb5 Qd7 $10 { You offer a queen trade into an equal position (about 0.1). Your king is a little awkward on e7 but nothing is aimed at it. }) 6... Nc6 7. d4 { d4 leads to roughly equal (+0.2): White has an isolated pawn. } (7. Bb5 { 7.Bb5 pins the newly-developed knight to the king immediately instead of castling first. } 7... g6 8. Qb3 Be6 9. Ng5 { ...and Black untangles with normal development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.3), sound but slightly more comfortable for Black than White's other main tries here.] } 9... Nc7 { The point of 9.Ng5 is Nxe6 plus Bxc6+ to wreck your pawns. This knight hits the bishop on b5 and guards e6, so Nxe6 wins nothing. } 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. Qa4 Bd5 { Blocks the long diagonal and covers c6. } 12. O-O Bg7 13. d4 O-O 14. Rd1 $10 { The doubled c-pawns are ugly, but you have the bishop pair and no weaknesses to attack, so the position is level (about -0.3). Aim for ...Qd6 and ...Rfd8. }) 7... Bf5 8. Bb5 e6 9. Qa4 Be7 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. Qxc6+ Kf8 12. Nxd5 Qxd5 13. Ne5 Be4 14. Nd7+ Kg8 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: White is a pawn up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side; Black has the bishop pair.] } 15. Qxd5 { The knight on d7 is deep in your camp and would be trapped after ...Rd8 or ...Bf6, so White trades queens to rescue it and keep the extra pawn. } (15. Qa4? Bd3 16. Ne5 Qxg2 { Your bishop and queen swarm round the uncastled king (about -4.2). }) 15... Bxd5 16. O-O f6 { Takes e5 and g5 away from the knight and gives the king f7. } 17. Be3 Kf7 18. h4 Rhc8 19. a3 Rd8 $10 { Now the knight is attacked and White has to spend a tempo on it. Your bishop pair balances the pawn and the position is level (about 0.1). } *


