1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. This sideline keeps the center fully closed and symmetrical rather than opening into the usual Panov isolani; both sides have doubled/advanced central pawns (Black e5/f6, White e4/f4) and the position resembles a locked Sicilian-type structure more than a typical Caro-Kann. Piece placement outweighs pawn structure entirely here: get the king safe, find a good square for the bishop (often b7 or still on c8 heading to a more active diagonal), and look for a moment to strike with ...f5 or ...d4-type pawn breaks once development is complete. Because the position is unusual for both sides, neither side's 'book' intuition applies cleanly, treat this chapter as a concrete-line memorization task rather than a structure to generalize from.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives -0.6 to -0.5, a small Black edge. Black is already comfortable here if White follows the chapter's line. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: 7...Ba5?! (about 0.5; prefer ...a5); 9...a6?! (about 0.4; prefer ...Nh6); 7...Qb6?! (about 0.4; prefer ...a5).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 4.c5? e5 (costs White about 1.3); 3.Nc3? d4 (costs White about 0.9).
Other Panov-Botvinnik and Accelerated Panov chapters: #4, #5, #6, #7. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #11. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. c4 $10 { The Panov Attack is an aggressive opening choice against the Caro Kann. First of all white voluntarily creates an isolated pawn which would be a clear target for black to take on. Second of all White is willing to create weaknesses in exchange for initiative and attack. Even a pawn sacrifice would not matter if white has enough counterplay. Black's goal is to further fortify the position. Develop the pieces with ease and is looking forward to a great game. } { [%csl Gc6,Gc4][%cal Gc2c4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nc3 $15 { White may even play Nc3 to experiment and put black out of his comfort zone. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } (3. exd5 $10 { exd5 leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be4d5] } 3... cxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc6d5] } 4. c5 $15 { White may even try moves like c5, which is not bad, but Black is clearly better because of the initiative. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc4c5] } 4... e5 { [%csl Be5,Bd5,Bf4,Be4,Bd4,Bc4][%cal Be7e5,Bd7d5,Bd5c4,Be5f4,Bd5e4,Be5d4] } 5. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 5... exd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be5d4] } 6. Qxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd1d4] } 6... a6 $17 { This prevents Bb5. } { [%csl Ba6,Bb5][%cal Ba7a6,Bf1b5] } 7. Nc3 Be6 8. Na4 { WATCH OUT: Nxd5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.8 from White's own side of the board), after: Nc6 Qc3 Qxd5 Be3 Nf6 Be2 } { [%csl Bb6][%cal Ba4b6] } 8... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 9. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 9... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 10. Qd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bd4d1] } 10... Ne4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 11. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3,Ba5][%cal Bf1d3,Bd8a5] } 11... Qa5+ $17 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-1.0).] [%csl Ba5][%cal Bd8a5] } 12. Bd2 { The only reasonable answer to the check: it keeps the knight and offers a trade of the dark bishop. } (12. Ke2? Nb4 13. Bd2 Nxd2 14. Kxd2 O-O-O { The king is stuck in the middle and you are much better (about -3.1). }) 12... Nxd2 13. Nxd2 Bxc5 { Picks up the pawn White grabbed with c5. } 14. Nxc5 Qxc5 15. O-O O-O 16. Rc1 Qd6 $17 { You have won a clean pawn. The d5 pawn is isolated, but your queen holds the centre and the position is clearly better for you (about -1.0). }) (3. e5 { 3.e5!? - an immediate advance instead of resolving the center with exd5. The pawn on e5 has no support yet, so Black should strike at once. } 3... d4 { 3...d4! is the simplest answer: the pawn grabs space and takes the c3-square away from White's knight, so White has to spend time on an awkward plan like f4/d3 instead of normal, comfortable development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0), comfortable for Black with no memorization burden.] } 4. Nf3 { White hopes to defend the pawn and develop, but the knight blocks the f-pawn and e5 is hard to hold. } (4. h3 Bf5 5. Nf3 e6 6. d3 Ne7 7. Be2 c5 8. Nh4 Nd7 { A calmer try that leaves you comfortable (about -0.4). }) 4... Bg4 5. Qb3 Bxf3 6. e6 { The tempting trick: the pawn threatens to take on d7 or f7 and the queen hits b7. } 6... Bg4 7. Qxb7 Nd7 { Your knight takes on e6 or attacks the pawn. } 8. exd7+ Bxd7 $15 { White's queen is far from home and has nothing to show for it, so you are a bit better (about -0.3). }) (3. cxd5 { 3.cxd5!? (resolving with the c-pawn first, keeping the e4 pawn on the board for now) cxd5 4.e5!? - White pushes immediately instead of developing normally. This just hands Black a tempo, since the pawn isn't doing anything there yet. } 3... cxd5 4. e5 Qa5 { 4...Qa5+ is simplest: the check forces White to block with a piece (usually Nc3 or Bd2) rather than gaining any time, and Black develops naturally from there with an easy, fully equal position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.0).] } 5. Nf3 { 5.Nf3 develops simply and defends the e5 pawn, rather than the sharper d4 push some sources prefer immediately. } 5... Nc6 6. Nc3 Bg4 7. d4 { ...and Black's active queen and central pressure give a real, lasting edge. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-0.45), worth recognizing but objectively softer than the immediate d4 try.] } 7... e6 { Solid: it supports d5 and gives the bishop on f8 access to b4. } (7... Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nxd4 9. Qxd5 Qxd5 10. Nxd5 O-O-O { A tactical simplification: the knight on d5 is attacked and you are about equal (about -0.1). }) 8. Be2 Bb4 { Pins the knight on c3 against the king, since the queen on a5 already eyes it. } 9. Qd3 Nge7 10. O-O O-O 11. Bd2 Qd8 12. h3 $15 { The pins are resolved and you are a shade more comfortable (about -0.2). You can aim for ...Bf5 and ...Rc8. }) 3... d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd5d4] } 4. Nce2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gc3e2] } 4... e5 { [%csl Ge4,Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] } 5. d3 { d3 leads to a clear Black edge (-0.9), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd2d3] } (5. f4 $17 { f4 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.7), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bf2f4] } 5... d3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd4d3] } 6. Nc3 $17 { White has too many weaknesses and will crumble before long. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Be2c3] } 6... Na6 $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.4).] [%csl Bb4,Bc2,Bd8,Bf2,Ba6][%cal Bf8c5,Bc5f2,Ba6b4,Bb4c2,Bb8a6] } 7. a3 { White stops ...Nb4 and prepares to challenge the thorn on d3. } (7. Qf3 Nb4 8. Kd1 h5 9. f5 g6 10. Rb1 gxf5 11. a3 Nc2 { Your knight settles on b4 or c2 and White's king is stuck (about -1.8). }) 7... Nf6 8. Qf3 Nc5 9. fxe5 Ng4 10. b4 Nxe5 { This knight hits the queen and the other knight is attacked by b4, but the queen has to move first. } 11. Qg3 Ncd7 $17 { Your d3 pawn still cramps White and your pieces are better placed (about -1.7). Play ...Ng6 and ...Be7 and keep the d3 pawn alive. }) 5... Nd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 6. f4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gf2f4] } 6... Bb4+ { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gf8b4] } 7. Bd2 { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gc1d2] } 7... Bxd2+ { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb4d2] } 8. Qxd2 { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gd1d2] } 8... f6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gf7f6] } 9. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 9... Nh6 { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gg8h6] } 10. Ng3 $15 { Black stands fine here. } { [%csl Gg3][%cal Ge2g3] } 10... O-O $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.6).] [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 11. Be2 { White finishes development first, knowing fxe5 is always available. } (11. fxe5 Nxe5 12. Be2 Neg4 { Opening the f-file looks logical, but your knights hop to g4 and e5 with tempo (about -0.9). }) 11... exf4 12. Qxf4 Ne5 { Takes control of e5 and makes the queen on f4 uncomfortable. } 13. Nxe5 Qa5+ { A zwischenzug: the check first forces the queens off. } 14. Qd2 Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2 fxe5 $15 { Your centre pawns on e5 and d4 give a comfortable endgame and White's king is in the middle (about -0.4). } *


