1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Classical Variation's Capablanca treatment trades down quickly into a simplified position with the queens already exchanged in some lines and open central files (d and e). With reduced material, the side with the better minor piece (Black's bishop, freed early via ...Bf5/...Bg6, against White's sometimes-awkward knight) has the practical edge in the resulting ending or near-ending. Black's plan is simple: trade off anything that doesn't help, keep the good bishop, and use the open central files to get a rook active before White's extra space counts for anything. This is one of the lowest-maintenance chapters in the repertoire precisely because so much simplification has already happened by the target position.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.0 to +0.3, roughly equal. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Bxc2? on moves 5 to 7 loses material (about 4.5); ...Qxd4? at move 4 loses material (about 7.2); 8...Qe7? (about 1.3; prefer ...Qa5+); 5...Nf6?! (about 0.6; prefer ...Bg6); 5...Be6?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Bg6); 6...Qb6?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Nf6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 9.Ke2? Qxe5+ (costs White about 2.4; engine-found, not in the chapter tree); 8.Nfh5? Nxh5 (costs White about 2.3; engine-found, not in the chapter tree).
Chapter #10 continues the Classical Variation. Other Classical Variation chapters: #11, #12, #13, #14. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #2. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 $14 { The core of the Caro-Kann argument lies here. In this semi-open game, minor piece play tends to dominate certain lines. White may handle the resulting tension. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nc3 $10 { The classical set-up is simple and flexible for White: development comes easily, and the same formation turns up against the Sicilian, the Philidor and the French as well. Black usually answers with the solid 3...dxe4, among the most heavily analysed Caro-Kann lines. Sidesteps exist, but 3...e6 blocks the c8-bishop and amounts to a French a tempo down, which makes it a dubious choice. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 3... dxe4 $10 { Against the Classical Caro-Kann, capturing the piece on e4 is the best move. The move g6 and e6, which appears in other lines, is similarly challenging to play. As for the LSB, g6 transposes into a Modern Defense, while e6 prevents it. 3...dxe4 is black's only good move. Other moves are not in the spirit of this opening. This move also denies white his e4/d4 pawn center. White almost invariably responds with 4. Nxe4, but a few gambit continuations do exist. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 4... Bf5 $14 { Known as the Capablanca Variation, this is a deviation from the standard classical move order. Black wants to play e6, but in order to avoid his bishop from being stranded on the chain of light square pawns, he must instead play f5. White may, and almost always does, keep to the standard line, which consists of Ng3 and forces the bishop to retire; nevertheless, the risky Nc5 and aggressive Burris Gambit have given white with surprising victory. } { [%csl Gf5,Rg3,Rc5,Rd3][%cal Gc8f5,Re4g3,Re4c5,Rf1d3] } 5. Ng3 { Ng3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gg3][%cal Ge4g3] } (5. Nc5 $10 { Rarely seen, but the knight's poor positioning on c5 must be punished. White wants to win a pawn since black's b pawn is hanging. However after e5! White is pretty much force to take the b pawn or retreat the knight and lose material. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Be4c5] } 5... e5! { [%csl Be5][%cal Be7e5] } 6. Nxb7 { WATCH OUT: dxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.5 from White's own side of the board), after: Qxd1+ Kxd1 Bxc5 f4 Na6 Bxa6 } { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bc5b7] } 6... Qb6 { WATCH OUT: exd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -7.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxd8 Kxd8 Nf3 Ke8 Qe2+ Be7 } { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 7. Nc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bb7c5] } 7... Bxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 8. dxc5 { WATCH OUT: dxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -9.6 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxf2+ Ke2 Bxg1 Rxg1 Bg4+ Ke1 } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd4c5] } 8... Qxc5 $10 { Black eventually wins the pawn back, but White is behind in development and a little cramped. Black dominates. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bb6c5] } 9. Bd3 { White uses the bishop to take control of e4 and hopes for Bxh7. } (9. Nf3 Nf6 10. c3 Nbd7 11. Be2 O-O 12. O-O h6 13. Re1 Ng4 { The solid alternative, level (about 0.0). }) 9... e4 { Kicks the bishop and gains space. } 10. Bf1 Nf6 11. Ne2 O-O 12. Be3 Qb4+ 13. Qd2 Qxb2 $10 { Grabbing b2 is possible, but your queen is far from home and White's lead in development gives him chances, so the position is level (about 0.1). }) (5. Bd3 $15 { Bd3 leads to a small Black edge (-0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 5... Qxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd8d4] } 6. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 6... Qd8 { WATCH OUT: Bxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxd4 Bxd3 Qxd3 e5 Nf5 g6 } { [%csl Bd8][%cal Bd4d8] } 7. Qe2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 7... Bxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf5e4] } 8. Bxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } 8... Nf6 $15 { Safe or risk-free? Not quite: White gets big attacking chances for the pawn, similar to the Smith-Morra Gambit in the Sicilian. White leads in development, but if Black defends accurately Black ends up a pawn ahead after a short attack. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 9. O-O { [%csl Bd7,Bf6,Bg1][%cal Bd7f6,Be1g1] } 9... Nbd7 $15 { Trying to solidify the position. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 10. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bc1f4] } 10... e6 $10 { The light-squared bishop is off the board, which removes a burdensome piece and can change the outcome of the game. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 11. Rfd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bf1d1] } 11... Qb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 12. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2,Bc4][%cal Bf3d2,Bd2c4] } 12... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 13. Nc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd2c4] } 13... Qa6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Bb6a6] } 14. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Be4d3] } 14... O-O $10 { Black need not fear the bishop eyeing the queen; there is no hidden tactic behind it. } { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 15. Ne5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bc4e5] } 15... Qa4 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: Black is a pawn up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side; White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba6a4] } 16. Nxd7 { White trades off the strong knight and goes for a sharp sequence against h7. } (16. c4 Nxe5 17. Bxe5 Rfd8 { White just loses time and you are better (about -0.4). }) 16... Nxd7 17. Bxh7+ Kxh7 18. Rxd7 Qxf4 19. Rxe7 Rad8 20. Rxb7 Rd2 $10 { A wild sequence that ends level (about 0.0): White regains his pawn and takes on b7 with the rook, but ...Rd2 gives you counterplay against the queen and the second rank. }) (5. Ng5 { White jumps with the knight to threaten f7 and Qf3 ideas, hitting your bishop on f5. It is very common (played in about 507 thousand games). The point is to gain time if you respond carelessly, but it is one of the less dangerous moves for you. } 5... e6 { Solid and good. It opens the way for the f8 bishop and takes away the threat on e6 by the knight. Your plan is to develop with ...Nf6 and ...Be7, trade the light-squared bishops with ...Bxd3, and challenge the centre with ...c5. Castle short. Avoid ...Nf6 too early without taking care of Qf3, since the queen can bother f5 and f7 together. The position is balanced with a tiny edge for you. } (5... Nf6 { Also fine. After Bd3 Bxd3 Qxd3 h6 you drive the knight back, then play ...e6 and ...Be7 with a clear plan. } 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 h6 8. N5f3 e6 9. Ne2 Be7 10. Bf4) 6. N5f3 Nf6 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Be7 { You trade the pair of bishops and develop with a solid structure. Next is castling. } 9. Ne5 c5 10. dxc5 { Overall: you strike in the centre at the right moment and your position is solid, with a small advantage in the numbers and easy development. This is an open position where White is a pawn up but his pieces are undeveloped, with the knight still on g1 and the king on e1, and the queens are facing each other on the d-file. Your plan: trade queens with ...Qxd3 Nxd3, then play ...Nbd7 and ...Nxc5 or ...Bxc5 to win the pawn back, and castle short. White wants to hold c5 with b4 and develop with Nc3 and Bd2. The mistake is ...Qxd3 Nxd3 Bxc5? because Nxc5 wins a piece, so always bring the knight first. If White plays b4, remain patient and target c5 with ...a5. }) (5. f3 $10 { White protects the knight on e4 so that your bishop cannot simply harass it, and prepares to bring the knight to g3 or the queen to d2 and castle long. It is very common, played in about 440 thousand games, but f3 weakens the e3 and g3 squares and blocks the f-pawn, so the position is level. } 5... Nd7 { Develop the knight to d7 so that ...Ngf6 trades off the strong e4 knight. Your plan is ...e5, which challenges d4 and opens lines for your bishops; the c8 bishop is already outside, so ...e5 is your natural break. Avoid ...e6 too early, which shuts in your f5 bishop. } 6. Bd3 e5 { Strike at once. The f5 bishop is shielded by the knight on e4, so you can afford the break. } 7. Ne2 Ngf6 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { Recapture with the queen so f5 stays protected after the discovered attack from d3. } 9. dxe5 Nxe5 { You retake the pawn with a knight that jumps to the centre; the queen on f6 keeps defending f5. Do not recapture with the queen on e5, which would allow Bxf5. } 10. Bxf5 Qxf5 { Overall: after the trades you have a comfortable, active position with the queen on f5 and a good knight on e5. This is an open, active position where your queen on f5 and knight on e5 are well placed, and the position is level. It is White's move, so he may play f4 or Nd4 to hit your queen. Your plan is, first, to develop ...Bd6 and castle long, bringing the rooks to the d-file. Second, keep the knight on e5 strong. Third, use ...Rhe8 and ...Nc4 for pressure. If White plays f4, retreat ...Ng6 or ...Nc6, and if he plays Nd4, move the queen to g6. The risk is losing a tempo to attacks on the queen or on the knight. }) 5... Bg6 { [%csl Gg6,Re2,Rh3,Rh4][%cal Gf5g6,Rg1e2,Rg1h3,Rh2h4] } 6. Nh3 { Nh3 leads to roughly equal (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gg6,Gh3][%cal Gg1h3] } (6. N1e2 $10 { N1e2 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Be2,Bg6,Bf4][%cal Bg1e2,Be2f4,Bf4g6] } 6... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 7. Nf4 $14 { This returns to the Nh3 manoeuvre; review that move order if it is unfamiliar. } { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be2f4] } 7... e5 { You must challenge d4 at once. If you delay, h4-h5 traps the bishop on g6. } (7... Qc7 8. h4 e5 9. dxe5 Qxe5+ 10. Be2 Nbd7 11. h5 Be4 12. O-O { The reason to hurry: h4-h5 forces your bishop to e4 (about 0.4). }) 8. Nxg6 hxg6 9. dxe5 Qa5+ { Regains the pawn with check. } 10. Bd2 Qxe5+ 11. Qe2 Nbd7 12. Qxe5+ Nxe5 $10 { Queens are off. The structure is level (about 0.2) and you can aim for ...Bc5 and ...Nd5 with the open h-file in reserve. }) (6. h4 $14 { h4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 6... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 7. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } (7. f4 { 8.f4 follows up the h4 advance with more space rather than developing with Nf3 first. } 7... e6 8. h5 Bh7 { WATCH OUT: Bb4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: c3 Bh7 cxb4 Nf6 Nf3 a5 WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.3 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Bxc2 Bxa5 Bxd1 Kxd1 Nf6 } 9. Nf3 { ...and the position remains level with chances for both sides. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.25), a legitimate alternative to the Nf3 main line at this point.] } 9... c5 { The thematic freeing move: it challenges d4 and opens the way for your pieces. } 10. Bd3 Bxd3 11. Qxd3 cxd4 12. Nxd4 Nf6 13. Ne4 Nbd7 14. Be3 $10 { The position is level (about -0.1). White's knight on d4 is strong, but you have easy development and a healthy structure. }) (7. Nh3 { 8.Nh3 reroutes the knight toward f4 instead of the more natural Nf3, aiming to hit the bishop on g6 directly. } 7... Nf6 8. Nf4 Bh7 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 Nxg6 fxg6 Bd3 Kf7 } 9. Bc4 { ...reaching a position that's actually a touch pleasant for White without being dangerous. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.1), a sound sideline worth knowing since it avoids the main theoretical paths entirely.] } 9... e6 { Solid: it blocks the diagonal of the bishop on c4 and opens the f8-a3 diagonal for your bishop. } 10. Nfh5 { White jumps in to hit f6 and g7. } 10... Nbd7 11. c3 Rg8 { Defends g7 so that Nxf6+ and Nxg7+ do not win a pawn. } 12. Qf3 Bd6 13. Bf4 Bxf4 14. Nxf4 $10 { You trade the dark-squared bishops and the position is level (about 0.0). }) (7. h5 { White attacks your bishop at once with a pawn push and tries to win time or even trap it. It is common in practice (played in about 850 thousand games). For you this means one thing: the bishop must go to a safe square immediately, and only one square really works. } 7... Bh7 $14 { The bishop retreats to h7, where it still watches the long diagonal b1-h7 and cannot be harassed again. This is the only sensible reply, and your plan afterwards is simple: develop with ...Nf6, trade the bishop pair by ...Bxd3 when White commits the bishop to d3, play ...e6 and ...Be7, then castle short and use ...c5 to open lines. Avoid any retreat to f5 or any capture on c2. } 8. Nf3 Nf6 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 { Trading White's strong bishop removes the danger of Bxh7 ideas and costs you nothing. The e6 pawn fixes the dark squares and opens the way for the kingside bishop. } 11. Bd2 Be7 12. O-O-O { Overall: you have a fully developed, flexible position, only slightly behind in space, and ...c5 or ...Nbd7 with ...c5 is your plan. This is a semi-open position with White's pawn on h5 next to an advanced h-pawn structure; the centre is open and White has a small space edge on the kingside. Both sides may castle on opposite wings, White long and you probably short or even queenside. Your plan: trade the light-squared bishops with ...Bxd3, develop with ...e6 and ...Be7, then strike in the centre with ...c5 or ...Nbd7 followed by ...c5. White wants a quick attack with Bd2, O-O-O and the h-file, using the pawn on h5. You meet it by keeping your pieces flexible and your king safe. The risk is leaving the bishop on g6 loose, because White can hit it, and an early ...h5 weakens you. If White deviates, avoid weakening moves and trade the strong bishop. }) 7... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 8. h5 { h5 leads to a small White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh4h5] } (8. Bd3 { 10.Bd3 offers the trade of light-squared bishops immediately, a move earlier than most main lines which delay it with h5 first. } 8... Bxd3 9. Qxd3 e6 10. Bf4 { ...reaching a very level position with simplified piece count. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), the most deflationary try at this node but fully sound.] } 10... Ngf6 { Develops and keeps ...c5 in reserve. } (10... Be7 11. Ne4 Qa5+ 12. Bd2 Qf5 13. O-O-O O-O-O 14. Qe2 Ngf6 15. Ng3 { Also fine for you (about 0.3). }) 11. O-O-O Be7 12. Kb1 O-O 13. Nf1 c5 { The counter in the centre. } 14. g4 cxd4 15. g5 $10 { White has a pawn storm against your king, so the position is sharp but balanced (about 0.2). Open lines with ...hxg5 and bring pieces back to defend. }) 8... Bh7 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Bxc2 Qxc2 Qc7 Ne4 e6 } { [%csl Bh7][%cal Bg6h7] } 9. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 9... Bxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bh7d3] } 10. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd1d3] } 10... e6 { e