] } (15. O-O-O { 15.O-O-O castles into the center rather than the more modest Kb1 some sources prefer, accepting a sharper middlegame. } 15... Ngf6 16. Kb1 O-O 17. Ne4 { ...reaching a double-edged but balanced position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.35), sound but riskier for White than the quieter alternatives.] } 17... Rad8 { Puts the rook behind the d-file and prepares ...c5. } (17... c5 18. d5 exd5 19. cxd5 Rfe8 20. g4 Nxe4 21. Qxe4 Nf6 { An alternative break, level (about -0.1). }) 18. Nxf6+ Nxf6 19. Qe2 c5 { Strikes in the centre before White's pawn storm starts. } 20. g4 cxd4 21. g5 d3 { The pawn hits the queen. } 22. Qe1 $10 { Level and sharp (about -0.2). Open the h-file with ...hxg5 and bring pieces back to defend. }) 15... cxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc6d5] } 16. cxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc4d5] } 16... Qd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bc7d6] } 17. Qb5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bd3b5] } 17... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6,Bd5][%cal Bg8f6,Bf6d5] } 18. dxe6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Bd5e6] } 18... Qxe6+ { [%csl Be6][%cal Bd6e6] } 19. Kf1 { [%csl Bf1][%cal Be1f1] } 19... O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 20. Re1 { White puts the rook on the open e-file to harass the queen and bishop. } 20... Qd5 { Centralises and offers to trade. } 21. Qxd5 Nxd5 22. Nf5 Rfe8 23. Nxe7+ Rxe7 24. Rxe7 Nxe7 $10 { The trades leave an equal endgame (about 0.1). White's king on f1 is slightly awkward, so you can play for ...Rd8. }) 12. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 12... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bb4e7] } 13. O-O-O { O-O-O leads to a small White edge (+0.3): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bc1][%cal Be1c1] } (13. O-O!? $10 { Whats the point of launching a pawn storm on the kingside only for white to castle? This would effectively weaken white's assault on the kingside and it's pretty much postponed for now. } { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 13... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 14. c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc3c4] } 14... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] [%csl Bg8,Ba5][%cal Be8g8,Ba7a5] } 15. Rad1 { White puts a rook on the d-file and prepares Be5. } 15... b5 { You offer a pawn to open the queenside and free your pieces. } 16. cxb5 cxb5 17. Qxb5 Nd5 { Your knight hits the bishop on f4. } 18. Be5 Nxe5 19. Nxe5 Bd6 $14 { White has grabbed a pawn, but your pieces are very active and the position is close to level (about 0.3). Look for ...Bd6 and ...Rac8 to regain activity. }) 13... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 14. Kb1 { [%csl Bb1][%cal Bc1b1] } 14... a5 $14 { The a-pawn advances to break open White's queenside, with an all-out attack. } { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 15. Ne4 { Ne4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bg3e4] } (15. c4 $10 { c4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } 15... a4 16. Ne4 Nxe4 17. Qxe4 a3 18. b3 Nf6 19. Qc2 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] } 19... Rc8 { Develops the rook to the c-file while keeping your a3 pawn in place. } (19... O-O?! 20. g4 Nxg4 21. Rhg1 f5 22. Ne5 { White's rooks aim at g7 and your king comes under pressure (about 0.4). }) 20. Rh3 b5 21. c5 O-O 22. Bc1 Nd5 23. g4 b4 24. Bd2 $10 { Level (about 0.0). Your a3 and b4 pawns cramp White and keep him from attacking freely. }) 15... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 16. Qxe4 a4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba5a4] } 17. Ne5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 17... a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba4a3] } 18. b3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bb2b3] } 18... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd7f6] } 19. Qf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Be4f3] } 19... Qd5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.6).] [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd7d5] } 20. Rd3 { White lifts the rook to join the attack along the third rank and the g-file. } 20... O-O 21. Bc1 { Attacks the pawn on a3, but your Be7 guards it. } 21... Rad8 22. Rg1 Nd7 23. Qxd5 cxd5 24. f4 Nf6 $14 { Small White edge (about 0.6). You have the better pawn structure but White has more active rooks; keep ...Nf6 and ...Rc8 handy. }) (6. Bc4 { 6.Bc4 develops toward the long diagonal rather than the more common Nf3/f4 setups, eyeing f7 and e6. } 6... e6 7. N1e2 Nf6 8. O-O { ...and Black completes development without difficulty. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1), a sound sixth try alongside the chapter's main continuations.] } 8... Bd6 { Guards e5 and prepares ...Qc7 and ...Nbd7. White's plan is f5 to hit both the bishop on g6 and the e6 pawn. } (8... a5 9. Bg5 Bd6 10. f4 Nbd7 11. f5 exf5 12. Nxf5 Bxh2+ 13. Kh1 { White gets f4 and f5 in quickly (about 0.3). }) 9. Nf4 Qc7 10. Qf3 Nbd7 11. Nxg6 hxg6 { The h-file opens for your rook. } 12. Rd1 O-O 13. Bb3 $10 { White's pieces watch e6 and f7 but your structure holds, so the position is level (about 0.0). Aim for ...c5 or ...b5. }) (6. f4 { 6.f4 grabs extra kingside space immediately, intending f5 to hit the bishop on g6 before Black can secure it. } 6... e6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. Bc4 { ...and the position stays balanced once Black meets the pawn storm with normal development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.15), sound but double-edged.] } 8... Bd6 { Guards e5 and prepares ...Qc7 and ...Ne7. White's idea is f5, which attacks both the bishop and e6. } 9. Qe2 Qc7 10. Ne5 Ne7 11. Ne4 Bxe4 12. Qxe4 Bxe5 13. dxe5 $10 { You have traded both bishops for knights. White has the bishop pair but you get d5 and f5 for the knights and the structure is stable, so the position is level (about -0.1). }) 6... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 7. Nf4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gh3f4] } 7... e5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] } 8. dxe5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gd4e5] } 8... Qa5+ { WATCH OUT: Bb4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -2.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: c3 Qxd1+ Kxd1 Ng4 cxb4 Nxf2+ } { [%csl Ga5][%cal Gd8a5] } 9. c3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 9... Qxe5+ $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] [%csl Ge5][%cal Ga5e5] } 10. Qe2 { The queen blocks the check and offers a trade instead of blocking with a knight. } (10. Nge2? Bc5 11. Be3 Bxe3 12. fxe3 Nbd7 { The knight on e2 is pinned against the king and you are much better (about -2.1). }) 10... Nbd7 11. Qxe5+ Nxe5 12. Be2 Bc5 { Hits f2 and develops. } 13. Nxg6 hxg6 14. Bf4 Ned7 $10 { The queens are off, the bishops are active and the position is level (about 0.1). } *


