1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. Classical Variation, Tartakower/Korchnoi treatment: Black accepts doubled f-pawns (from ...Nxe4/...gxf6-type exchanges or a similar point) in exchange for the bishop pair and the open e-file for a rook. Doubled pawns that control key squares (e4 and g4, exactly as in Chapter #1's Exchange structure) are not really a weakness here, they're a defensive asset. The practical plan: post a rook on the open e-file, keep both bishops (they compensate fully for the structural blemish), and avoid ever creating a second weakness (e.g., a second pawn island) that would make the doubled f-pawns matter. White's half-open d-file is the only real lever White has; watch for pressure down it.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.1 to +0.5, a small White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd4? on moves 4 to 6 loses material (about 6.6); ...Bxh2? on moves 7 to 8 loses material (about 4.5); 9...Kf8? (about 0.8; prefer ...h6); 9...c5?! (about 0.6; prefer ...h6); 4...g6?! (about 0.4; prefer ...Nf6); 4...h6?! (about 0.4; prefer ...Nf6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 6.Nxf7? Kxf7 (costs White about 1.6); 6.N5h3?! Bf5 (costs White about 0.6).
Chapter #11 continues the Classical Variation. Other Classical Variation chapters: #9, #12, #13, #14. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #2. Model game: #3. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nc3 $10 { The classical set-up is simple and flexible for White: development comes easily, and the same formation turns up against the Sicilian, the Philidor and the French as well. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 3... dxe4 $10 { The most reliable answer to the Classical set-up is to take on e4. ...g6 transposes into a Modern Defence, while ...e6 shuts in the light-squared bishop and is harder to handle. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE 5.Nxf6+ exf6 6.Bc4 Bd6 7.Qe2+ Be7 IF you like a calm, solid structure and expect White to play for a small edge (low risk). CHOOSE 5.Ng3 h5 (the Bronstein-style reply) OR the 5.Ng5 c5 trap lines ONLY IF you are comfortable with sharper play (higher risk). Each branch is marked below. After 4...Nf6 White can trade on f6 (the normal choice) or play the sharp 5.Ng5 and 5.Ng3. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 4... Nf6 { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gg8g6] } 5. Nxf6+ { Nxf6+ leads to roughly equal (+0.1): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s). } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge4f6] } (5. Ng3!? $10 { Ng3!? White wants to preserve his knight hoping to find some more sophisticated game and to keep things imbalanced. However after the move h5! Black gets a strong initiative on the kingside. } { [%csl Bg3][%cal Be4g3] } 5... h5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 6. Bc4 { Bc4 leads to roughly equal (-0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } (6. h4 { 10.h4 - grabs the h5-pawn's support square immediately instead of the main 10.Bc4, trying to provoke a weakness before Black finishes developing. } 6... c5 { 10...c5 (Complicated & Imbalanced) - grabs central space immediately instead of pinning first, heading for a sharper position with chances for both sides rather than a quiet equalizing line. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.1), close enough to be a matter of taste rather than soundness.] } (6... Bg4 { 10...Bg4 (Solid & Optimal) - the standard, well-tested developing move; the h-pawn advance costs White a tempo that Black uses to pin the knight and complete development comfortably. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), the calmer and most reliable choice.] } 7. Be2 (7. f3 Bd7 8. N1e2 g6 9. a4 Bg7 10. Nf4 Qc7 11. Bc4 b5 { If White kicks the bishop, retreat and fianchetto: White's f3 weakens the e3 square. Equal. }) 7... c5 { Strike the centre: your bishop and queen face the d1 queen, so after dxc5 the queens come off. } 8. dxc5 Qxd1+ 9. Kxd1 Bxe2+ 10. Kxe2 e6 11. Be3 Nbd7 { The pawn on c5 falls back, and White's king is stuck on e2 (about 0.0). }) 7. Nf3 { 13.Nf3 develops naturally rather than taking on c5 immediately, keeping central tension. } (7. dxc5 { 13.dxc5 resolves the tension immediately, trading into an endgame-leaning structure rather than the sharper Nf3 try. } 7... Qxd1+ 8. Kxd1 e6 9. b4 { ...reaching a position where Black's extra central space gives a real, if modest, edge. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.5), a sound simplifying alternative but less ambitious than Nf3 here.] } 9... a5 { Undermine the b4 pawn that is propping up c5. } 10. c3 axb4 11. cxb4 Nc6 12. Bd2 Nd5 13. Rb1 Be7 14. Nf3 { You have the more active knights and White's king on d1 is slightly awkward, though the edge is tiny (about -0.1). }) 7... Bg4 { ...reaching a double-edged but sound position for White. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3), a real alternative to the immediate dxc5 try.] } (7... Nc6 8. Be3 (8. Bb5 { 8.Bb5 would be a mistake: ...Qa5+ wins material. [Engine verdict: about -3.5.] } 8... Qa5+ { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -3.6).] }) (8. c3 cxd4 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 10. cxd4 g6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.5).] }) 8... cxd4 9. Nxd4 Qa5+ (9... Bg4 10. Qd2 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.09).] }) 10. c3 (10. Bd2 Qe5+ 11. Be3 Bd7 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 10... Bd7 11. Be2 g6 12. O-O Bg7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.07).] }) 8. c3 { Supports d4 and gives the queen a safe route after ...cxd4. } (8. Bf4 Nc6 9. dxc5 Qxd1+ 10. Rxd1 e5 11. Be3 e4 12. Ng5 Bxd1 { Trading queens with dxc5 allows ...e5-e4, which hits the knight and keeps you comfortable (about -0.1). }) 8... cxd4 9. Qxd4 Nc6 10. Bb5 Bxf3 { Trade before the knight pins you. } 11. Qxd8+ Rxd8 12. gxf3 e6 { Level (about 0.0): White's pawns are doubled and the bishop pair is the only compensation. }) (6. Nf3 { White develops the knight to a natural square and protects d4. It also keeps the knight on g3 safe from ...h4 only indirectly, because the knight can later step to e2. It is a very common choice. } 6... h4 { Push the pawn and force the knight back. This gains space on the kingside and takes away the g3 square. Your plan after that is to trade your c8 bishop for the knight with ...Bg4, develop ...Nbd7 and later play ...Qa5+ or ...e6. } (6... c5?! { A tempting break, but White gets slightly the better game because your king's bishop and queen are not ready. } 7. Bc4 h4 8. Ne2 a6 9. Ng5 e6 10. c3 b5 11. Bb3 { White has a more active position. }) 7. Ne2 Bg4 8. h3 Bxf3 { Take the knight, because White's pawns on f2 and g2 will be wrecked. } 9. gxf3 Nbd7 10. Bg2 Qa5+ { With check, you gain time. } 11. Bd2 { Overall: this is equal. White has the bishop pair and a doubled pawn, and you have easy development. This is a closed-centre structure where you have grabbed kingside space with ...h4 against White's d4 and doubled f-pawn setup. You have pinned White's bishop on d2 with ...Qa5, and White still has to castle or play c3. Your plan: add pressure with ...Bb4, challenge the centre with ...e5, and then castle short once your pieces are out. White wants c3 to break the pin, castle and use the bishop pair. Do not allow your queen to be trapped on a5; if White plays c3, retreat with ...Qc7. The risk is overextending on the kingside, so only push ...h3 if it wins something clear. }) 6... h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh5h4] } 7. N3e2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg3e2] } 7... a6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Ba7a6] } 8. a4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba2a4] } 8... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 9. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 9... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 10. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 10... h3! $15 { This move is risky: the h-pawn may fall in a couple of sequences, but once the h-file opens Black gets a strong kingside attack. } { [%csl Bh3,Bg3][%cal Bh4h3,Bg2g3] } 11. g3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 11... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 12. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 12... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 13. Bg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 13... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bf5g4] } 14. Qd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd1d3] } 14... Qd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bd8d7] } 15. Rfe1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 15... O-O-O $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.2).] [%csl Bc8][%cal Be8c8] } 16. Nf4 (16. Nh4 Rh5 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. f3 Bf5 19. Nxf5 Rxf5 20. Nf4 Rxf4 { Nh4 meets ...Rh5, and the pressure on f3 and g4 keeps White in trouble (about -1.3). }) 16... cxd4 { Open the position while White's kingside is frozen by your h3 pawn. } 17. Bb5 dxc3 18. Qxc3 Bxf3 19. Qxf3 Kb8 { Bb5 pins the c6 knight to your queen, so the king steps off the c-file. White's pieces have no targets while your h3 pawn cramps g3 and h2. } 20. Bxc6 Qxc6 { A clear Black edge (about -1.2). }) (5. Ng5 $10 { Ng5 trying to provoke black's kingside. At first impression this might seem like a waste of tempo. However after the move h6 black's kingside is a bit weakened and therefore white gets a bit of initiative in attacking black's kingside.  [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores -0.19; Nxf6+ scores +0.33, so Nxf6+ was stronger] [%csl Bg5][%cal Be4g5] } 5... h6 { [%