e. }) 6... Kxf7 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qd5 Nxh8 Bf5 Nf3 g5 } { [%csl Bf7][%cal Be8f7] } 7. Nf3 { [%csl Bc5,Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 7... c5 { [%csl Bd1,Bc5][%cal Bd8d1,Bc7c5] } 8. Bc4+ { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 8... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 9. Qe2 { [%csl Be2,Be6,Bc6][%cal Bd1e2,Be2e6] } 9... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 10. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 10... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 11. O-O-O { [%csl Bc1,Bd8][%cal Be1c1,Bd1d8,Bf8c5] } 11... Bc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 12. g4 $19 { Trying to compromise black's kingside. } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bg2g4] } 12... Qe7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bd8e7] } 13. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be3f4] } 13... Bd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bc8d7] } 14. h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 14... Nd5 $19 { Black is doing fine. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 15. Bg3 (15. Ne5+?! Nxe5 16. Bxe5 Bc6 17. Rhe1 Rad8 18. Qd3 Qxh4 19. f4 Be7 { Trading pieces on e5 does not help White: you can even take the h4 pawn (about -2.4). }) 15... Rhf8 { Bring the other rook into play. White is making a last attempt at an attack with g5, but your king can retreat. } 16. g5 Ke8 17. Qe4 Rf5 18. Rhe1 Kd8 19. gxh6 gxh6 { Black is clearly better (about -2.4): the king is safe on d8 and White's sacrificed knight is still missing. }) (5. Qd3 { 5.Qd3 brings the queen out early, offering a trade of knights on e4 to simplify toward an endgame-leaning structure. } 5... Nxe4 6. Qxe4 Qd5 7. Qxd5 { ...and the position is dead level after the natural sequence of trades. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), a sound, simplifying fifth-move try.] } 7... cxd5 $10 { The recapture is forced and good (about +0.1): you get a healthy symmetrical centre and the c8 bishop is free. White has no real initiative here. His plan is Bd3 plus Ne2-f4 to lean on d5, and yours is to trade the light bishops and develop c