the natural, essentially forced response, using the open e-file with tempo before White's king finds safety. CONTRASTING CASE: compare this to the 8.Ne2 move-order elsewhere in this chapter, where the identical-looking move ...Re8 is NOT check. The difference has nothing to do with the rook move itself: here White has not yet moved the e1-knight, so the e8-e1 file is completely open and ...Re8+ is a real check; in the 8.Ne2 line, that same knight already sits on e2, blocking the file, so ...Re8 there is just a normal developing move with no check. Whether a rook lift on an open file comes with tempo depends entirely on what already occupies the file, not on the rook move itself. CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE THIS IF you want a very solid pawn structure with easy plans (doubled f-pawns that guard e5 and g5) and the bishop pair in some lines (low risk, medium theory). The queen check on e8 forces White to decide how to block it: 9.Ne2 and 9.Be3 are the main choices. } 9. Ne2 { Ne2 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): Black has doubled pawns; Black's king has reached safety while White's has not yet. } (9. Be3 { 9.Be3 develops instead of the knight, keeping the knight's own route (Ne2 or Ng3) open for later and eyeing c5/d4 support. } 9... g6 { 9...g6 prepares ...Bf5 or ...Nd7 while keeping the long diagonal closed to future Bc4/Bb5 tries. } (9... h5 10. Nf3 Be6 (10... Nd7 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.3).] }) 11. O-O-O (11. O-O { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.14).] }) 11... Na6 12. g4 { White opens the kingside at the cost of safety; ...hxg4 and ...Qa5 give Black counterplay. [Engine verdict: about -0.3.] } (12. Nh4 Bf8 13. g4 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.6).] }) 12... hxg4 13. Nh4 Qa5 14. Nf5 Bxf5 15. Bxf5 Nc7 16. Rhg1 Nd5 17. Rxg4 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 10. Ne2 { 10.Ne2 finally routes the knight to safety, intending Ng3 or O-O-O next. } 10... Nd7 { 10...Nd7 completes development naturally, heading for b6 or f8. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1 to +0.3) throughout, confirming this is a genuine second main try alongside 9.Ne2 - this branch was documented in an earlier round but never actually made it into the promoted file, so this fixes that omission.] } 11. O-O (11. h4 f5 12. h5 Nf6 13. hxg6 fxg6 14. Bg5 Be7 15. Bc4+ Nd5 { White tries to rip open the g6 pawn with h4-h5. Meet it with ...f5 and ...Nf6 and keep the structure together (about +0.1). }) 11... f5 { A small but important point: ...f5 takes e4 away from White, blocks the d3 bishop and gives your rook on e8 and the queen more scope. } 12. Rfe1 Qc7 { Bishop and queen both point at h2. } 13. g3 { White blunts that battery. } 13... c5 { The thematic strike. Your pieces are developed, so open the centre. } 14. Bb5 a6 15. dxc5 Bxc5 { Equal (about +0.2). }) 9... h6 { 9...h6 stops any future Ng5 ideas and gives the king some luft before continuing with ...Nd7/...Bf5/...c5 depending on White's setup. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.4), a genuine second main try alongside the existing 8.Ne2 line.] } (9... h5 10. Be3 (10. Bd2 { 10.Bd2 prepares O-O-O. ...c5 hits the centre and Black gets counterplay. [Engine verdict: about +0.2.] } 10... c5 (10... Nd7 11. O-O-O b5 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.5).] }) 11. O-O-O (11. d5 Qc7 12. c4 Nd7 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.3).] }) (11. dxc5 Bxc5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.00).] }) (11. O-O Nc6 12. Rae1 Bd7 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.5).] }) 11... Nc6 12. Be3 (12. dxc5 Bxc5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.00).] }) 12... Qa5 13. dxc5 (13. Kb1 cxd4 14. Nxd4 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 13... Bxc5 14. Bxc5 Qxc5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.14).] }) 10... Nd7 11. c4 (11. Ng3 Nb6 12. O-O Nd5 13. Bd2 (13. Nxh5 Bg4 14. Ng3 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.11).] }) 13... h4 14. Nf5 (14. Ne4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.00).] }) 14... Bxf5 15. Bxf5 Qc7 16. h3