es a timely e-file pressure or a later Nf4. } 12... Be6 { 12...Be6 develops the last minor piece to a safe, useful square. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2/+0.4), a third sound main try.] } 13. Ng3 (13. b3 Qd7 14. f4 f5 15. c4 Ng6 16. Bc3 Rad8 17. g4 Nh4 { White can also try the f4 and g4 lever to open the kingside. ...f5 and the knight jump to h4 keep it blocked (about +0.3). }) 13... c5 { Strike before White's knight reaches f5 or e4. } 14. dxc5 Bxc5 15. h3 Bd6 16. Bb5 { The bishop now attacks your rook on e8 through the open c6 square. } 16... Re7 17. Nf5 Bxf5 { You trade your good bishop to remove the dangerous knight and stay level (about +0.3). White's plan was to play Ng3-f5 or Ne4 to hit your f6 pawn. }) (11. Be3 { 11.Be3 is the most natural square for the bishop, eyeing c5 and supporting a future d4-d5 break. } 11... Nf8 { 11...Nf8 keeps flexibility, just as against Bf4/Bd2. } 12. Rfe1 { 12.Rfe1 contests the open file Black opened back on move 8. } 12... Bg4 { 12...Bg4 develops actively, hitting f3/e2 and preparing ...Qd7 or ...Rad8. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.3/+0.35), the most frequently seen of the four tries in the source material and arguably White's most natural try at move 11.] } 13. Ng3 (13. h3 Bh5 14. c4 Bc7 15. Ng3 Bg6 16. Rad1 Bxg3 17. fxg3 Qc7 { If White kicks the bishop, retreat to g6 and give up the bishop for the knight so that his pawns become the weak ones. }) 13... Qc7 14. Kh1 Bf4 { White steps off the g1-a7 line and you trade off the dark bishops: your doubled f-pawns are healthier without the pieces that could attack them. } 15. Qd2 Bg5 16. a4 Rad8 17. h3 Bxe3 { Roughly equal (about +0.2). White hopes to push a4-a5 and clamp the queenside, but your rooks cover d-file and e-file. }) 11... Nb6 { 11...Nb6 hits d5/c4 and prepares ...Be6 or ...Bf5 next. } 12. Bd2 { 12.Bd2 finally commits the dark-squared bishop, often followed by Rae1 and a slow central squeeze. } 12... Be6 { 12...Be6 completes development comfortably. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.3/+0.4 through this sequence), a sound fourth main try at move 11 alongside Bf4/Bd2/Be3 below.] } 13. Rfe1 (13. Rad1 Nd5 14. Bh7+ Kh8 15. Be4 Qd7 16. Rfe1 b5 17. b3 Bc7 { The Bh7+ check is harmless: the king steps to h8 and the bishop must return, so White wins nothing and you are level. }) 13... Qd7 14. Rad1 Nc4 { The knight hits the d2 bishop and heads for the weak e3 and b2 squares. } 15. Bc1 Bxg3 { Trade your bishop for the knight so that hxg3 ruins White's pawn structure. } 16. hxg3 Nd6 17. c4 Bg4 { The bishop attacks the rook on d1. Equal (about +0.3). }) 8... Re8 { CONTRASTING CASE: compare this to the 8.Qc2 move-order, where the same ...Re8 IS check. Here White played 8.Ne2 first, and that knight now physically sits on the e-file between the rook and the king, so ...Re8 is just a normal developing/centralizing move, not a check. The lesson: whether a rook lift on the open e-file comes with tempo depends entirely on whether White's knight has already occupied e2, not on the rook move itself. } { [%csl Ge8][%cal Gf8e8] } 9. Qc2 { Qc2 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4): Black has doubled pawns; Black's king has reached safety while White's has not yet. } { [%csl Gc2,Rh5,Rh6][%cal Gd1c2,Rh7h5,Rh7h6] } (9. O-O { 9.O-O completes development immediately instead of delaying with Qc2 first. } 9... Nd7 10. Bf4 Nf8 11. Bxd6 { ...reaching a calm position similar to the chapter's other Ne2 tries. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2), a sound transposition that just castles a move earlier.] } 11... Qxd6 { Recapture at once (about +0.1). } (11... Bf5? 12. Bxf8 Bxd3 13. Qxd3 Rxf8 { You must never skip the recapture on d6: White's bishop would then take the knight on f8 and you would simply be a piece down (about +5.3). }) 12. Qd2 g6 13. Rfe1 Kg7 14. h4 h5 { Stops h5 and keeps your kingside closed. } 15. Qf4 Rd8 16. Qxd6 { Then recapture with the rook and the game is level. }) 9... h6 $14 { ...h6 is the logical and safer choice; the more ambitious ...f5!? is also possible. } { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gh7h6] } (9... h5!? $14 { ...h5 aims at White's kingside. With the help of the bishop pair, White's king will be in real danger once the attack gets going. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 10. O-O { O-O leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } (10. Be3 { 9.Be3 develops the bishop before committing the king, an alternative to the immediate O-O or h3 tries at the same node. } 10... Nd7 11. h3 Nf8 12. g4 { ...and the position stays close to level. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a sound ninth-move alternative in this Qc2/Re8+ tabiya.] } 12... h4 { Keep the h-file shut. The pawn on g4 becomes a target and your knight can later use g6. } (12... hxg4?! 13. hxg4 Bxg4 14. O-O-O Bf3 15. Rh3 Bg2 16. Rh6 f5 17. Rg1 { Grabbing the pawn opens the h-file for White's rook and your bishop is out of play (about +0.5). }) 13. O-O-O Be6 14. c4 b5 { Counterplay. } 15. c5 Bc7 16. Nc3 Bd5 17. Nxd5 { Equal (about 0.0). }) (10. Bd2 { 9.Bd2 develops the bishop flexibly, keeping the option of O-O-O alongside the more common O-O. } 10... Nd7 11. O-O-O Nf8 12. Kb1 { ...and the position stays close to level. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2), a sound alternative alongside Be3 and O-O at this node.] } 12... Be6 { Develop the last minor piece (about +0.2). White has castled long, so he will start a pawn storm with f4 and g4, but your h5 pawn already stands in the way. } (12... b5?! 13. d5 cxd5 14. Bxb5 Bd7 15. Bxd7 Nxd7 16. Be3 Nb6 17. Bxb6 { The pawn thrust looks natural, but it opens lines against your own king and gives White a small edge (about +0.4). }) 13. f4 f5 14. h3 b5 { Counterplay against the king with your own pawn storm. } 15. g4 Bd5 16. Rhg1 fxg4 17. hxg4 { The position is dynamically balanced. }) 10... h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh5h4] } 11. h3 { h3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } (11. Bf4 { 11.Bf4 offers the trade of dark-squared bishops right after castling, instead of developing the queenside pieces first. } 11... Na6 12. h3 Nc7 13. Qd2 { ...and the position remains balanced. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2), a sound alternative try at this point.] } 13... Nd5 { A blockade on d5 trades the bishops and hits the c3 pawn. } (13... Bxf4 14. Qxf4 f5 15. Rfe1 Nd5 16. Qd2 Be6 17. c4 Nf6 18. Rad1 { Also playable. }) 14. Bxd6 Qxd6 15. c4 Ne7 16. Nc3 Bf5 17. d5 Bxd3 18. Qxd3 { Roughly equal (about +0.2). White's c4 and d5 try to gain space, but you trade the light bishops and the structure is fine. }) 11... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 12. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 12... Nf8 { [%csl Bf8][%cal Bd7f8] } 13. Rad1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Ba1d1] } 13... Bd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bc8d7] } 14. Rfe1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 14... Qc8 { [%csl Bc8][%cal Bd8c8] } 15. Nf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be2f4] } 15... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7,Bh2][%cal Bc8c7,Bc7h2] } 16. Qc1 { [%csl Bc1][%cal Bc2c1] } 16... Rad8 { [%csl Bd8][%cal Ba8d8] } 17. Bd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Be3d2] } 17... Qc8 { [%csl Bc8][%cal Bc7c8] } 18. b3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bb2b3] } 18... Bf5 $10 { The opening phase is over and Black is fine. Pieces should be exchanged to avoid complications on the kingside. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bd7f5] } 19. Rxe8 (19. Bc2 Rxe1+ 20. Rxe1 g6 21. Bxf5 Qxf5 22. Qb1 Qxb1 23. Rxb1 f5 { White's tries to keep the bishops lead to a queen trade and a level ending (about -0.1). }) 19... Rxe8 20. Qc2 Qc7 21. Bxf5 Bxf4 { This is the idea: trade every piece that could attack your kingside. After the knight on f4 disappears there is nothing to your king. } 22. c4 g6 23. Bg4 Nh7 { Equal (about 0.0). }) 10. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 10... Nd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 11. Be3 { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gc1e3] } 11... Nf8 { [%csl Gf8][%cal Gd7f8] } 12. Rfe1 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gf1e1] } 12... Be6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Gc8e6] } 13. Rad1 { [%csl Gd1][%cal Ga1d1] } 13... Qc7 { WATCH OUT: Bxa2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a ca