1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Bronstein-Larsen treatment of the Classical Variation leads to opposite-side action: Black's king often stays on c8/queenside while the queen swings out to h5, and Black accepts a slightly loose structure (doubled f-pawns, an isolated h-pawn) for active piece play and attacking chances against White's king. This is one of the sharper chapters in the repertoire, the 'minimum acceptable' benchmark is correspondingly higher because the structure alone doesn't guarantee safety, the activity has to be real. Keep the queen and dark bishop coordinated against White's kingside, and treat the half-open g-file as your primary attacking lever rather than the more common d-file.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.5 to +1.2, a modest White edge. That is more than the usual edge, so treat this line as a practical choice rather than an objective equaliser. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +1 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd4? on moves 4 to 6 loses material (about 6.5); ...Qxf3? at move 7 loses material (about 5.3); 8...Nd7? (about 0.8; prefer ...Bg4); 9...Na6?! (about 0.7; prefer ...Qe6+); 9...Qc4?! (about 0.6; prefer ...Qe6+); 8...Be6?! (about 0.6; prefer ...Bg4).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 10.g3?! Qb4+ (costs White about 0.6); 12.h4? e5 (costs White about 1.0; engine-found, not in the chapter tree).
Chapter #12 continues the Classical Variation. Other Classical Variation chapters: #9, #10, #13, #14. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #2 and #10. Model game: #6. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nc3 $10 { The classical set-up is simple and flexible for White: development comes easily, and the same formation turns up against the Sicilian, the Philidor and the French as well. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 3... dxe4 $10 { The most reliable answer to the Classical set-up is to take on e4. ...g6 transposes into a Modern Defence, while ...e6 shuts in the light-squared bishop and is harder to handle. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 4... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 5. Nxf6+ { Nxf6+ leads to a small White edge (+0.3): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s). } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge4f6] } (5. Qd3 { 5.Qd3 brings the queen out early, offering a trade of knights on e4 to simplify toward an endgame-leaning structure. } 5... Nxe4 6. Qxe4 Qd5 7. Qxd5 { ...and the position is dead level after the natural sequence of trades. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), a sound, simplifying fifth-move try.] } 7... cxd5 $10 { Always recapture with the c-pawn here (about +0.1): the structure is symmetrical, your c8 bishop is liberated and White has no way to exploit the early queen trade. } (7... Nd7? 8. Qb3 { Any other move leaves you a queen down (about +7.1). }) 8. Bd3 g6 9. Ne2 Bf5 10. Bxf5 gxf5 11. c3 e6 12. Nf4 { White's knight heads for d5 and e6 targets. Your solid pawn mass and the quick ...Bd6 bring a level game. }) (5. Bd3 { 5.Bd3 develops immediately, offering Black the chance to grab the d4 pawn with ...Qxd4 for a quick, sharp fight. } 5... Qxd4 6. Nf3 Qd5 { WATCH OUT: Nxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.8 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxd4 Nc5 Bc4 Nbd7 O-O Nb6 } 7. Qe2 { ...and the position is level after the natural queen trade. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.3), a sound fifth-move alternative that leads to simplified, balanced play.] } 7... Nxe4 { Trade the knights first, then the queen steps to h5 (about 0.0). } (7... Bg4 8. O-O Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Nbd7 10. Rd1 g6 11. a4 Qh5 12. Qxh5 { Pinning the knight is slightly more accurate (about -0.2). }) 8. Bxe4 Qh5 9. Be3 Nd7 10. O-O-O Nf6 11. Bf4 Be6 12. Rhe1 { The queen on h5 is a bit exposed, but White has no easy way to chase it. }) 5... gxf6 $16 { The Bronstein-Larsen Variation is the most aggressive way to play against the Classical Caro Kann. First and foremost black weakens his kingside and voluntarily makes a double pawn but in exchange of a dangerous attack on the kingside. The g file is open and further play would be met on the kingside or on the center.  [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores +0.92, while exf6 scores +0.45, so it costs about 0.5. It is playable, but it is a concession] [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg7f6] } 6. c3 $14 { The main line is c3, which supports the d-pawn. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 6... Qd5 $14 { ...Qd5 is the usual main move; its value depends on how White plays, but the queen can swing to h5 or g5 if White attacks on the kingside. After c4!? Black has ...Qa5+. } { [%csl Gd5,Rc4,Rf3][%cal Gd8d5,Rc3c4,Rg1f3] } 7. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a clear White edge (+0.9): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } (7. c4 $14 { c4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.8): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } 7... Qd6 8. Be3 Bf5 9. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.2): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } (9. Bd3 $16 { Developing the bishop to d3 first, rather than Nf3 as in the main line, is a small improvement, since it eyes the h7-b1 diagonal immediately and keeps the knight's best square flexible. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 9... Bg6 { WATCH OUT: Qb4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.9 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qxb2 Bxf5 e6 Qb1 Qxd4 } { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf5g6] } 10. Qc2 { [%csl Bc2][%cal Bd1c2] } 10... b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 11. Rc1 { [%csl Bc1][%cal Ba1c1] } 11... bxc4 $14 { This keeps a modest, normal opening edge for White (doubled f-pawns for Black are a long-term structural concession), consistent with the rest of this variation - not a new problem, just a more accurate move order. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bb5c4] } 12. Qxc4 (12. Bxg6 hxg6 13. Nf3 Bh6 14. Qxc4 Kf8 15. O-O Kg7 16. h3 a5 { If White hurries with Bxg6 you are only a little worse (about +0.2). }) 12... Nd7 { Develop the knight, which can later go to b6 to hit the queen. } 13. Bxg6 hxg6 14. Qxc6 Qxc6 15. Rxc6 Nb6 16. Ke2 Kd7 { White wins the c6 pawn, so he keeps a real edge (about +0.7), but your knight and king chase the rook and White's king is not entirely safe either. }) 9... Rg8 10. g3 { g3 leads to a small White edge (+0.4): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } (10. Qb3 $16 { Rather than g3, White's sharper and more testing try is Qb3, hitting both b7 and d5 at once and forcing Black to make an immediate decision. } { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd1b3] } 10... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd6c7] } 11. g3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 11... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 12. Qc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb3c3] } 12... a5 $16 { This is the most critical try in the whole chapter and keeps a real, lasting edge for White. It's worth having in the course precisely because it's the position's genuine test, not a cosmetic improvement. } { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 13. a3 { White spends a tempo on a3 to slow your queenside play and keep b4 in reserve. } (13. c5 Be4 14. Be2 Na6 15. a3 Qd7 16. O-O Nc7 17. Nd2 f5 { Gaining space with c5 is slightly less accurate (about +0.9). }) 13... a4 14. Bf4 Bd6 15. Bxd6 Qxd6 16. Bg2 Kf8 17. O-O Kg7 { White keeps a lasting edge (about +1.3): his bishop on g2 hits c6 and your doubled f-pawns remain a weakness. }) 10... Qb4+ 11. Qd2 Na6 12. Be2 O-O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 13. a3 { The pawn hits your queen, so you trade queens on d2. } (13. Qc3 e6 14. Nh4 Bh3 15. O-O-O Qxc3+ 16. bxc3 c5 17. Rhg1 cxd4 { Trading queens on c3 ruins White's pawns (about +0.4). }) 13... Qxd2+ 14. Nxd2 Nc7 15. O-O-O Bg4 16. f3 Bd7 17. b4 h5 { White keeps a small edge (about +0.5) thanks to the bishop pair and your doubled f-pawns, but you are solid. }) 7... Rg8 $14 { ...Rg8 is the main idea of the Bronstein-Larsen Variation: it creates chaos and attacking chances on the kingside, and the queen can go to h5 or g5. ...h5 is also possible. } { [%csl Gf3,Gg8,Re3,Rg3][%cal Gh8g8,Rc1e3,Rg2g3] } 8. g3 $14 { g3 answers the attack on g2 by the rook, but Black keeps attacking White's kingside. } { [%csl Gg8,Gg3][%cal Gg2g3] } (8. Be3 $14 { Be3 with intention of simply developing pieces and then casting forward on the kingside. Black should continue with the assault on white's kingside even if he hasn't castled! (Remember to develop your pieces first before committing to an attack. } { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 8... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 9. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 9... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 10. h3 { h3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } (10. Qc2 $14 { Qc2 leads to a small White edge (+0.2): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } 10... Bf5 11. Qb3 Qxb3 12. axb3 Be6 $14 { Endgame is roughly alright for black } 13. O-O Bxb3 { Your bishop grabs the pawn, but then White tries to trap it. } 14. c4 Bc2 15. Rfc1 Be4 16. g3 e5 17. Nd2 exd4 { You have won a pawn, but White gets time and open files for it, so the position stays roughly balanced (about +0.2). }) 10... Bh5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bg4h5] } 11. g4 $14 { White's intention is clear: not to castle kingside but queenside, where the king is safer. If every option runs out, the king can simply stay in the centre or tuck away on f1. } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bg2g4] } 11... Bg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bh5g6] } 12. Rg1 { [%csl Bg1][%cal Bh1g1] } 12... O-O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] [%csl Bc8][%cal Be8c8] } 13. h4 { White wants h5 to harass the bishop on g6, which has few squares. } (13. Qb3 Rh8 14. Qxd5 cxd5 15. h4 h5 16. g5 fxg5 17. Nxg5 Bh6 { White can also trade queens and push on the kingside (about +0.7). }) 13... h6 { Gives the bishop the h7 square. } 14. Qa4 Nb6 15. Qxa7 Kc7 16. Qa3 Ra8 17. c4 Qxc4 { White has won a pawn (about +0.8) but the queen is far offside, so you have counterplay. }) 8... Bg4 { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gc8g4] } 9. Bg2 { [%csl Gg2][%cal Gf1g2] } 9... Nd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 10. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 10... Qh5 $16 { Note how easily the queen swings across to support the attack on the kingside. } { [%csl Gh5,Gh2][%cal Gd5h5,Gh5h2] } 11. Qd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd1d3] } 11... O-O-O $14 { A key attraction of the Bronstein-Larsen Variation is that it resembles the Stafford Gambit but is better, since Black is not committed to a pawn sacrifice and develops flexibly.  [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this mainline ends at +0.85, so White keeps a real edge here] [%csl Gc8][%cal Ge8c8] } 12. Re1 (12. Bd2?! e5 13. Nh4 Be6 14. Rfe1 f5 15. Qf3 Rg4 16. h3 Rxh4 { The natural Bd2 allows ...e5 in time and the lead drops to about +0.2. }) 12... Qf5 { Offers a queen trade on the diagonal and keeps the queen active. } 13. Qe2 e5 14. b4 Nb6 15. a4 Nd5 16. b5 Nxc3 { White's plan is a pawn storm against your long-castled king with b4-b5, which keeps a real edge (about +1.1). Your ...e5 and knight on d5 are the counterweight. } *


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Chapter #12: Classical Variation, Karpov Variation (Smyslov-Petrosian)"]
