[ChapterName "Chapter #12: Classical Variation, Karpov Variation (Smyslov-Petrosian)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/MMXC7zUe"]

1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Karpov/Smyslov-Petrosian treatment is the calm counterpart to Chapter #11: same family of structures, but Black castles short and aims for a slow, safe middlegame rather than a race. The bishop on h7 (tucked away after the standard ...Bg6-h7 retreat) looks passive but is perfectly safe and re-enters the game via f5 or e4 later; don't rush it back into play before the position calls for it. Black's practical plan is to use the half-open d-file for pressure against d4 and only commit to a pawn break once the pieces (especially that bishop) are ready to support it. This chapter rewards patience over the sharper Bronstein-Larsen approach.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.1 to +0.4, a small White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd4? on moves 4 to 7 loses material (about 7.1); ...Bxc2? on moves 8 to 9 loses material (about 5.0); 14...g5? (about 0.8; prefer ...Bf5); 9...Bg6?! (about 0.7; prefer ...Bg4); 9...Be7?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Bg4); 7...Qd6?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Bf5).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 11.Qxb7? Rb8 (costs White about 2.5); 11.c4? Bxc4 (costs White about 2.1); 16.Qxd4? Ne2+ (costs White about 2.1).
Chapter #13 continues the Classical Variation. Other Classical Variation chapters: #9, #10, #11, #14. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #2. Model game: #2. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nc3 $10 { The classical set-up is simple and flexible for White: development comes easily, and the same formation turns up against the Sicilian, the Philidor and the French as well. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 3... dxe4 $10 { The most reliable answer to the Classical set-up is to take on e4. ...g6 transposes into a Modern Defence, while ...e6 shuts in the light-squared bishop and is harder to handle. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 4... Nd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 5. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } (5. Ng5 $14 { This is an interesting move, with chances to blow Black off the Board. Even Kasparov got blown with a crushing knight sacrifice. CHOICE GUIDE - after 5.Ng5 the safe and most popular reply is 5...Ngf6 (about two games in three), which is SAFE / POSITIONAL and leads to a small White edge. 5...e6 and 5...Ndf6 are BALANCED alternatives that score about the same. 5...h6 is the one to avoid: it scores worst for Black because of 6.Ne6!. The new sidelines below cover every sixth move White tries after 5...Ngf6: 6.Bd3 (main line), 6.Bc4, 6.N1f3 and the knight sacrifice 6.Nxf7. } { [%csl Bh6,Bf6,Bg5][%cal Bh7h6,Bg8f6,Be4g5] } 5... Ngf6 { Ngf6 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. WHAT WHITE PLAYS NEXT: 6.Bd3 is the main move and the line given above. 6.Bc4 and 6.N1f3 are the two other common moves, and 6.Nxf7!? is the speculative knight sacrifice that is seen a lot in online play. Each is covered below, with the one reply Black should remember. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } (5... h6!? $14 { This move seems logical, as Nxf7 doesn't work. However, White can get a slight edge with Ne6! and can win quickly if Black makes a blunder. A grandmaster lost to this trap. The e8-h5 diagonal is slightly weakened, and so is f7. The main line is Ne6! Qb6 (Wisely avoiding fxe6?? Qh5+ g6 Qxg6#) Nxf8 Nxf8 with two Bishops and a slight edge. } { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 6. Ne6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Bg5e6] } 6... Qb6 $14 { The only good move which actually holds the position onwards.   WATCH OUT: fxe6 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it's not just worse, it's losing outright for Black: after Qh5+ g6 Qxg6#, White forces mate. } { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 7. Nxf8 { [%csl Bf8][%cal Be6f8] } 7... Nxf8 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.3 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qa4 Nxd7 Qxd4 Bc3 Qe4+ WATCH OUT: Qb4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.9 from Black's own side of the board), after: c3 Qd6 Nxd7 Bxd7 Nf3 O-O-O } { [%csl Bf8][%cal Bd7f8] } 8. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 8... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 9. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 9... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 10. Ne5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 10... N8d7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bf8d7] } 11. Qf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd1f3] } 11... Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd7e5] } 12. Qxf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bf3f5] } 12... Ned7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Be5d7] } 13. Bc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 13... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 14. Qf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf5f3] } 14... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 15. O-O (15. a4?! c5 16. a5 Qc7 17. Bf4 e5 18. dxe5 Nxe5 19. Qg3 Nh5 { An early a4 push loses time and you equalise (about +0.3). }) 15... Rac8 16. Re1 Rfd8 17. Bf1 c5 { Break open the centre. } 18. dxc5 Nxc5 19. h3 Qc6 { White's bishop pair gives a small edge (about +0.7), but your knights are active and you can chip at d4 with ...c5. }) 6. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } (6. Nxf7?! { The knight sacrifice. White gives up a whole piece to drag the king into the open. In online games it scores a little over half, because Black has to find a few accurate moves, but objectively it is simply unsound. [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.4).] CHOICE GUIDE: this is White's gamble, not yours - just take the knight and then follow the rules in the notes below. } 6... Kxf7 { The only move - Black takes the knight with the king. Any other reply (for example ...e6 or ...h6) allows Nxd8 and White simply wins the queen. After the king capture White has two serious tries: 7.Nf3 (developing first) and 7.Bc4+ (check at once). } (6... e6 { WATCH OUT: not recapturing is a blunder because the knight on f7 takes the queen. } 7. Nxd8 { [Engine check: winning for White (+7.6) - a queen for a knight.] } 7... Kxd8 { [Engine check: winning for White (+7.6).] }) 7. Nf3 { The most common move: bring the knight out and keep the Bc4+ idea in reserve. Black's king is exposed, but every White piece is still on its starting square, so Black has time to consolidate. } (7. Bc4+ { The immediate check. Black has three sensible answers: ...e6 (safest), ...Nd5 (most active) and ...Ke8 (playable but scores worse in practice). } 7... e6 { The solid reply, and the one most players choose. The pawn blocks the check and Black plans ...Nb6, ...Be7 and ...Kg8. [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.5).] } (7... Nd5 { The most active reply, blocking with the knight and keeping the pawn structure intact. It is not the engine's top choice but it scores best for Black in practice. [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.2).] } 8. Nf3 e6 9. O-O Bd6 10. Bb3 h6 { [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.6).] }) (7... Ke8 { Also good objectively, and the engine even likes it, but in practice White scores better here because the king is back in the centre and Black has to find ...Nb6 and ...c5 to free the position. Choose this only if you know the line. [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.9).] } 8. Nf3 Nb6 9. Bb3 c5 10. dxc5 Qxd1+ { [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.9).] }) 8. Nf3 Nb6 9. Bd3 Be7 10. Ng5+ Kg8 { [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.6).] }) 7... e6 { The simplest setup: the e6 pawn shuts the c4-g8 diagonal for good, so Bc4 is no longer check, and the bishop can later come out to d6. [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.4 to -1.6).] } 8. Bc4 { White attacks the e6 pawn and aims at the king. Black has three equally good replies - ...Nd5, ...h6 and ...Nb6, all around -1.5. The one move to avoid is 8...Qa5+?!, which lets White trade queens and lose the least (about -0.8). } 8... Nd5 { Black centralises the knight and takes the sting out of Bb3. After ...Bd6 and ...h6 the king is safe on f7 and Black keeps the extra piece. [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.4).] } (8... h6 { A useful waiting move that stops Ng5+ forever and gives the king the h7 square. [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.6).] } 9. Qe2 Nd5 10. O-O Bd6 11. Bd2 Re8 { [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.6).] }) (8... Nb6 { Kick the bishop right away. After Bb3 Bd6 Black should watch the Ng5+ check but remains clearly better. [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.5).] } 9. Bb3 Bd6 10. Ng5+ Ke7 11. c4 h6 { [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.9).] }) 9. O-O Bd6 10. Bb3 h6 11. Re1 Bc7 { [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.4).] }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop eyes f7 at once. It is the second most common move after 6.Bd3. The structure is normal and White keeps a small plus, but there is one big trap: ...h6? loses on the spot to Bxf7#. CHOICE GUIDE: the safe and standard reply is 6...e6; 6...Nd5 is a good, slightly sharper alternative. } 6... e6 { The reply played in nearly every game - the pawn guards f7 and prepares ...Nb6 to kick the bishop. [Engine check: a small White edge (+0.3).] } (6... Nd5 { A good alternative to 6...e6: the knight blocks the bishop's path to f7 and Black keeps the e-pawn flexible. White keeps a modest edge. [Engine check: a modest White edge (+0.6).] } 7. Bb3 a5 8. c4 N5f6 9. Ne2 h6 { [Engine check: a modest White edge (+0.7).] }) (6... h6 { WATCH OUT: the natural-looking ...h6?? loses at once to Bxf7# - the knight on g5 guards the bishop and the king has no square. } 7. Bxf7# { [Engine check: winning for White (+100.0).] }) (6... e5 { WATCH OUT: this one loses a pawn and more - Bxf7+ wins material because the knight on g5 supports the capture. } 7. Bxf7+ Ke7 8. Bb3 Qa5+ 9. Bd2 Qa6 { [Engine check: winning for White (+4.8).] }) 7. Qe2 { White adds pressure to e6 and prepares to develop. Black answers with ...Nb6 and ...h6, getting a normal Caro-Kann position. } 7... Nb6 8. Bb3 h6 9. N5f3 a5 10. a3 a4 11. Ba2 { Black has gained queenside space and the game is balanced. [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.2).] }) (6. N1f3 { A flexible developing move: the other knight comes out so that the g5-knight is supported. White usually follows with Bd3 and Qe2. CHOICE GUIDE: 6...e6 is the safe and positional reply and transposes to the main line; 6...h6 is the more ambitious reply that actually scores slightly better for Black and is the engine's preferred move. } 6... h6 { The ambitious reply - kick the knight at once. White has no good retreat: 7.Bd3? hxg5 wins a piece and 7.Nxf7 Kxf7 just gives the knight away (about -1.2). The best White can do is 7.Nh3, after which Black takes space on the kingside. This is a SHARP / BALANCED choice that scores better for Black in practice than the main-line ...e6. } (6... e6 { The calm reply: after Bd3 Bd6 and Qe2 h6 the game transposes to the main line of the chapter. [Engine check: a small White edge (+0.4).] } 7. Bd3 Bd6 8. Qe2 h6 9. Ne4 { [Engine check: a small White edge (+0.4).] }) 7. Nh3 { The knight steps back to the edge, the awkward but best move. [Engine check: a small Black edge (-0.4).] } 7... Qc7 8. Bd3 g5 9. Nhg1 c5 10. c3 b6 11. Ne2 { Black has expanded on both wings and White has spent a lot of moves with the knight. [Engine check: a modest Black edge (-0.5).] }) 6... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } (6... h6 { WATCH OUT: this is the move that scores worst for Black here (White wins two-thirds of the games). The point is 7.Ne6! and the knight cannot be taken profitably. } 7. Ne6 { [Engine check: a modest White edge (+0.8).] } 7... Qb6 8. Qe2 c5 9. Nxc5 Nxc5 10. dxc5 { The queen is attacked and White keeps a clear edge. [Engine check: a modest White edge (+0.8).] }) 7. N1f3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } (7. Qe2 { A second main move: White develops the queen first and keeps the choice of knight development for later. After ...Bd6 N1f3 h6 Ne4 Nxe4 Qxe4 Qc7 the position transposes to the main line. White plays 7.Qe2 about once in five games here. } 7... Bd6 8. N1f3 h6 9. Ne4 { [Engine check: a modest White edge (+0.5).] }) 7... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 8. Qe2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 8... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 9. Ne4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bg5e4] } (9. Nxe6 { The sacrificial try: the knight takes on e6, offering a piece. Black simply recaptures - a piece for two pawns is not enough. Played in about one game in five here and it scores a little over half for White in practice, but it is not sound. CHOICE GUIDE: the safe and correct answer is 9...fxe6. } 9... fxe6 { [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.0 to -1.2).] } (9... Bb4+ { WATCH OUT: checking first is a mistake. After c3 fxe6 Bg6+ Black loses the bishop on b4 and the king is exposed. } 10. c3 fxe6 11. Bg6+ Ke7 12. cxb4 { [Engine check: a big White advantage (+3.8).] }) 10. O-O Qe7 { [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.0).] }) (9. Nxf7 { Another sacrifice that does not work. Black takes the knight and then simply develops. [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.2).] } 9... Kxf7 10. Bd2 c5 11. Ne5+ Kg8 12. Nc6 Qe8 { [Engine check: a clear Black edge (-1.5).] }) (9. Nh3 { The knight retreats to the rim and White loses the edge. Black castles or plays ...Qc7 and is slightly better. [Engine check: a modest Black edge (-0.7).] }) 9... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 10. Bxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } 10... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 11. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 11... e5 $10 { The problem with the Ng5 knight manoeuvre, black if black knows what he is doing black tendencies might even get an edge since this would allow black to play for this e5! Breakthrough. Effectively dislodging white's grasp on the center. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be7e5] } 12. dxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd4e5] } 12... Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd7e5] } 13. Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 13... Bxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd6e5] } 14. Rd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bf1d1] } 14... Qf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd8f6] } 15. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 15... Re8 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.2).] [%csl Be8][%cal Bf8e8] } 16. g3 (16. Qf3? Bxh2+ 17. Kxh2 Qh4+ 18. Kg1 Rxe4 { The point: Bxh2+ drags the king out and the pin on the e-file gives you the advantage (about -1.0). }) 16... Bh3 { The bishop hits the rook on f1. } 17. Qh5 Qe6 18. f3 Bc7 19. Be3 Bg4 { Level (about -0.3). Take care with White's other moves: your Be5 and queen aim at h2. }) (5. Bc4 { White develops the bishop at once to aim at f7 and prepares Ng5 or Qe2 so that the bishop and the e4 knight work together against your kingside. It is very common in practice (played in about 296 thousand games). For you it means f7 is the sensitive square, so finish developing before you start pawn breaks. } 5... Ngf6 { You challenge the e4 knight and develop at the same time. The other knight stays on d7 in reserve, because ...Nb6 can later hit the bishop with tempo. Your plan is ...e6, ...Be7, castle short and then free your game with ...c5. If White trades on f6, recapture with the knight and keep your healthy structure. White keeps only a small edge here. } (5... Ndf6?! { This looks natural but is less accurate: after Ng3 White keeps his strong knight and your ...h5 and ...h4 pawn advances cost time while the kingside gets loose. } 6. Ng3 h5 7. h3 h4 8. Nf1) 6. Ng5 { White doubles up on f7 and eyes h7. Stay calm and keep the pieces covered. } 6... e6 { This blocks the bishop's diagonal and prepares ...Be7. } 7. Qe2 Nb6 { Now your knight hits the bishop with tempo and heads towards d5 or c4, so White has to spend time retreating. } 8. Bd3 h6 { Kick the knight away so that your kingside stays safe. } 9. N5f3 c5 { This is the freeing break. You complete development with ...Be7 and ...O-O next. } 10. Ne5 { White gets an advanced knight, but you can challenge it with ...Nbd7 or ...Bd6 ideas. Overall: your pieces are safe and ...c5 gives you real counterplay. This is an open-centre game with a pawn on d4 for White and your c5 pawn already challenging it, and both bishops still to develop. White's knight on e5 is advanced but needs support from d4. Your plan: challenge it with ...Nbd7, finish development with ...Be7 and ...Bd7 or ...O-O, and take on d4 when it wins time. White wants to keep the knight on e5, add Be3 and pressure f7 and the h-file. The risk is f7, where Nxf7 and Qh5 tricks appear if you neglect it, and opening the d-file with ...cxd4 before you castle. If White deviates by playing Bxe6, recapture with the f-pawn or bishop calmly. }) (5. Qe2 { White puts the queen on the e-file behind the e4 knight, setting a famous trap and preparing Nf3 and long castling. It is very common in practice (played in about 287 thousand games), so you must know the trap. For you it means one knight move is forbidden. } 5... Ndf6 { This is the safe reply: the d7 knight steps to f6 and the g8 knight stays home, so you are ready to trade on e4. The point is that you never leave the e7 pawn pinned in front of your king. Your plan is ...Nxe4, ...Nf6, then ...g6 and ...Bg7 or ...Bf5 ideas, with ...Qa5+ as a useful resource against the d2 square. Keep the structure flexible and trade pieces. White keeps only a small edge here. } (5... Ngf6?? { This is the trap. The queen pins the e7 pawn against your king, so the knight jumps in with mate: } 6. Nd6#) 6. Nf3 Nxe4 { Trade off the strong knight. } 7. Qxe4 Nf6 { With tempo you hit the queen. } 8. Qd3 g6 { Your bishop will go to g7 and the queen can use a5. } 9. Be2 Qa5+ { This check forces White to put a piece on d2, which hampers his own development. } 10. Bd2 { Overall: a calm, solid position where you complete development with ...Bg7 and ...O-O and aim for ...c5. This is a quiet, semi-open structure with White's pawn on d4 and your solid c6, e7 and g6 pawns. You will castle short with your bishop on g7. After the check, your queen on a5 is attacked, so retreat it with ...Qd5 or ...Qb6, then play ...Bg7, ...O-O and ...Bf5 to hit the queen on d3. Aim for the freeing break ...c5 once you are castled. White wants to castle long or short, play c4 and use his space and the d4 pawn. The big risk is at the fifth move, where ...Ngf6?? loses to Nd6 mate, so always play ...Ndf6. Never grab the a2 pawn with the queen. }) 5... Ngf6 { [%csl Gf6,Rg3,Rg5][%cal Gg8f6,Re4f6,Re4g3,Re4g5] } 6. Nxf6+ { Nxf6+ leads to roughly equal (+0.1): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s). } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge4f6] } (6. Ng3 $14 { The Kasparov Attack keeps the knight and builds tension in the centre. White does not want to trade knights, since that would favour Black, who is happy to simplify and head for an endgame. } { [%csl Bg3][%cal Be4g3] } 6... e6 $14 { Although the bishop is blocked by the e6-pawn, it can be fianchettoed via the queenside and become a good bishop. On b7 in the Karpov Variation it eyes g2 and the Nf3 knight, unlike in the Queen's Indian, where it sees less counterplay; here it gets real play along the b7-h1 diagonal, since White's kingside is exposed in the endgame. } { [%csl Be6,Bc4,Bd3,Bh4][%cal Be7e6,Bf1c4,Bc2c4,Bf1d3,Bh2h4] } (6... Nb6 $14 { Nb6 leads to a modest White edge (+0.8), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 7. Bd3 { Bd3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.6), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (7. c4 $14 { White gains space, which is not necessarily bad but has drawbacks: the d-pawn may become isolated, which is a liability, and Black is encouraged to attack the space advantage. Black's aim is to fight against it and create complications for both sides. } 7... Bg4 8. Be2 $10 { Keep this trap in mind: after 8...Bxf3, recapturing 9.Bxf3? loses a pawn to 9...Nxc4. } 8... Bxf3 9. Bxf3 { Bxf3 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.4): White retains the bishop pair. } (9. gxf3 $14 { White has no other choice other than to take back with the g pawn since other options would not be necessary since the pawn will hang. } 9... e6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: White has the bishop pair; White has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 10. Be3 $14 { White has the bishop pair and the g-file; Be3 and Qd2 prepare Qa5 against your queenside. } 10... Qc7 11. Qd2 c5 { Strike at the centre before White consolidates. } 12. Rd1 O-O-O 13. Qa5 Nbd7 14. Qxc7+ Kxc7 { You reach an endgame with sound pawns and White keeps only a tiny edge (about +0.1). }) 9... Nxc4 $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.4). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] } 10. Qc2 $17 { White hits the c4 knight to regain the pawn, but your knight has a safe retreat. } (10. Qb3? Qa5+ 11. Qc3 e6 12. Kd1 Qxc3 13. bxc3 Ba3 14. Kc2 Bxc1 { White's king is dragged out and White falls further behind (about -1.6). }) 10... Nb6 11. Ne2 e6 12. Bd2 g6 { Fianchetto the bishop to guard the long diagonal. } 13. O-O-O h5 14. Kb1 Bg7 { You are a clean pawn up (about -1.2) and White has little to show for it. }) 7... Bg4 8. c3 e6 9. Bf4 Bd6 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. O-O O-O-O $14 { Beware of castling queenside, sometimes it is alright to castle queenside if white does not have any major attack on the queenside. } 12. h3 Bxf3 13. Qxf3 Nbd5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] } 14. Rae1 $14 { White puts a rook on the e-file to prepare c4 and Ne4. } 14... Qf4 { Offer a queen trade so you can untangle. } 15. Qd1 Kb8 16. c4 Ne7 17. Ne2 Qc7 { White's c4 kicks the knight but you gain a solid setup. } 18. b4 g5 { You expand on the kingside (about +0.2), and the position stays balanced. }) 7. Bd3 { Bd3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } (7. Bc4 $10 { Bc4 is quite a famous move in the Karpov variation of the Caro Kann Defence. White is sticking to his principled opening fundamental. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 7... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 8. Bg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 8... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 9. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 9... Re8 $14 { In the Karpov Variation, keep the rooks on open files or in the centre: if the position opens up, Black gains the advantage because the rooks dominate the open d- and e-files. } { [%csl Be8][%cal Bf8e8] } 10. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 10... b6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 11. Re1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 11... Bb7 { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bc8b7] } 12. Qe2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 12... Nd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 13. Bxe7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg5e7] } 13... Qxe7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bd8e7] } 14. Rad1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Ba1d1] } 14... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 15. dxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd4c5] } 15... Qxc5 $14 { The light-squared bishop is finally free. Black aims for queenside play while keeping the king safe; there are enough pieces to build a kingside fortress with ...Nd7-f8 if White attacks quickly, and putting a rook on the d-file is another plan. Black is happy to head for an endgame. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Be7c5] } 16. Rd4 $14 { The rook swings to d4 where the knight on f3 and the bishop on c4 back it up; all three aim at your d5 knight, the piece that shields your centre and king. } 16... a6 { Take b5 away and prepare ...b5 to kick the bishop. } 17. a4 Red8 { Put your rook on the d-file so Rd4 cannot dominate it. } 18. Red1 Qc7 19. R1d2 h6 { Stops Ng5 before it lands. } 20. Nh5 { Nh5 hits g7 and f6 but your pieces guard the king, so White keeps only a mild initiative (about +0.4). }) (7. h4 $15 { h4 is a rather inaccurate move: White wants kingside space and will castle queenside while attacking on the kingside. Black should not let that develop, and instead strike in the centre and launch a queenside pawn storm before White's attack gets going. } { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 7... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 8. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 8... c5 { [%csl Bc5,Bc3,Be3][%cal Bc6c5,Bc2c3,Bc1e3] } 9. Be3 { Be3 leads to roughly equal (+0.0), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } (9. c3 $10 { White is building up for a kingside attack, and Black should not let White develop easily. Disturbing White's kingside operation is enough to keep the attack at bay. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 9... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 10. cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc3d4] } 10... b6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 11. Ne4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bg3e4] } 11... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bd6e7] } 12. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bc1f4] } 12... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 13. Qc2 { Qc2 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.7): White has an isolated pawn; Black's king has reached safety while White's has not yet. } { [%csl Bc2][%cal Bd1c2] } (13. Nc3 $15 { A minor move-order point: Nc3 first (rather than Qc2) is very slightly more accurate, keeping the knight flexible before committing the queen. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Be4c3] } 13... Bb7 { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bc8b7] } 14. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 14... Nd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 15. Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc3d5] } 15... Bxd5 $10 { Essentially equal either way; included for completeness rather than because it changes anything practical.   WATCH OUT: Bxh4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.0 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bc7 Qc8 Nxh4 Bxd5 Bg3 Bb7 } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bb7d5] } 16. Rc1 { White wants to take the c-file and then bring Ng5 and the h-pawn to bear on h7, but you have an easy answer. } (16. h5 h6 17. Rc1 Rc8 18. Rxc8 Qxc8 19. Ne5 Ba8 20. Ng4 Nf6 { White's pawn thrust gains nothing, about -0.4 }) 16... Rc8 17. Rxc8 Qxc8 18. Ng5 Nf6 { Your knight covers h7 and challenges the bishop. As the note above says, stay clear of the h4 pawn. } 19. Be5 Rd8 20. Bxf6 Bxf6 { Equal, about 0.0. }) 13... Nd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 14. Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf4d6] } 14... f5 { [%csl Bf5,Be4][%cal Bf7f5,Bf5e4] } 15. Bxe7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bd6e7] } 15... Qxe7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bd8e7] } 16. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Be4c3] } 16... Bb7 $15 { White's reinforcement for a kingside attack has failed. Black now has the advantage: White has an isolated d-pawn, and Black's position is rock solid. } { [%csl Bb7,Bd4][%cal Bc8b7] } 17. Nxd5 $17 { White has to take the knight, since otherwise ...Nxc3 would damage the pawn structure. } (17. Kf1 Nf4 18. Qd2 Qd6 19. Nb5 Qb8 20. Bc4 Nf6 21. Re1 Ne4 { Avoiding the trade leaves your pieces dominating (about -0.8). }) 17... Bxd5 18. Qe2 Qb4+ { This check knocks the king off the e1-h4 diagonal. } 19. Kf1 Rac8 { You put pressure on the isolated d4 pawn. } 20. Rh3 Qd6 21. a3 Qf4 { Your queen and rook dominate and the h-file rook has no targets, so White's attack failed and you are better (about -0.6). }) 9... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 10. Bxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be3d4] } 10... Bxg3 { [%csl Bg3,Be4][%cal Bd6g3,Bg3e4] } 11. fxg3 { [%csl Bg3,Bg2,Bh4][%cal Bf2g3] } 11... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 12. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 12... O-O! $14 { White has the bishop pair, but the kingside pawn chain has weaknesses and White is far from reaching his goals. Black has no time to grab pawns and must focus on development, or else suffer from White's piece activity. } { [%csl Bf1,Bg8,Bg3,Bc7][%cal Be8g8,Bc7g3] } 13. Bxf6 Nxf6 14. Ng5 Rd8 15. Qf3 h6 16. Ne4 Nxe4 17. Bxe4 Rb8 $10 { With ...b6 and ...Bb7 coming, Black is roughly fine. } { [%csl Bb2,Bd1,Bb6,Bb7,Bh1][%cal Bd8d1,Bb7b6,Bc8b7,Bb7h1] } 18. Rad1 $10 { White's bishop on e4 is strong and aims at b7 and h7, so White contests the d-file. } 18... Rxd1 19. Rxd1 Bd7 { Step the bishop off c8 so it can challenge the Be4 from c6. } 20. Kh2 b6 21. Rd4 Rc8 22. c3 Be8 { Your bishop finds a safe post and you have no weaknesses, so the game is level (about +0.1). }) (7. c4!? $10 { White plays c4 to gain spacious advantage but black would fight back towards the center anyways. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 7... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 8. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 8... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 9. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 9... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 10... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7,Bc5][%cal Bd8c7,Bc7c5,Be7c5] } 11. Qd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bd1d2] } 11... Rd8 { [%csl Bd8][%cal Bf8d8] } 12. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be3f4] } 12... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Be7d6] } 13. Bxd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf4d6] } 13... Qxd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bc7d6] } 14. Rfd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bf1d1] } 14... b6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 15. dxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd4c5] } 15... Qxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd6c5] } 16. Nd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bf3d4] } 16... Bb7 $10 { Black stands well. } { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bc8b7] } 17. b4 $14 { The pawn hits your queen with tempo and starts queenside expansion, while the Nd4 outpost eyes e6 and b5. } 17... Qe7 { A safe square that keeps e6 protected. } 18. Rac1 a5 { Undermine b4 so White cannot build a queenside clamp. } 19. a3 axb4 20. axb4 Ne5 { Your knight lands on a strong central post and eyes c4. } 21. Qe3 Neg4 { You hit the queen and the f2 and h2 squ