y devolves into an immediate sacrifice on e6. } { [%csl Bg5][%cal Be4g5] } 6... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 7... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 8. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 9. Ne4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bg5e4] } 9... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 10. Bxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } 10... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd7f6] } 11. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Be4d3] } 11... O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 12. Re1 $14 { White supports the Ne5 jump and the e-file. } 12... c5 { Freeing break that opens the bishop. } 13. dxc5 Bxc5 14. Ne5 Qc7 15. Bf4 Bd6 { You offer the bishop trade to kill the pressure on the e5 knight. } 16. Qe2 b6 { You then develop the Bb7; White's edge is tiny (about +0.4) with no concrete threats. }) (6. Bd3 { White develops quickly and eyes the h7 square, hoping you will recapture badly on e4 so that Neg5 becomes dangerous. In practice it is very common and fairly harmless if you simply take on e4, because the knight trade leaves White with a mere slight initiative. For you the key point is to trade knights at once and not allow the white knights to jump to g5. } 6... Nxe4 $10 { Take the knight, and after Bxe4 return with ...Nf6, kicking the bishop. This removes the dangerous white knight and keeps your position sound. } (6... Qc7?! { This looks natural but runs into 7.Neg5, with threats on h7 and on e6, and White keeps a clear initiative. } 7. Neg5 h6 8. Ne6 Qd6 9. Nxf8 Nxf8) 7. Bxe4 Nf6 8. Bd3 Bg4 { Now your light-squared bishop leaves the c8-h3 diagonal to pin the f3 knight before you play ...e6. Your plan is ...e6, ...Be7 or ...Bd6, ...Qc7 and short castling, with the break ...c5 as the long-term idea. If White plays h3 at once, drop back to h5 and keep the pin; trade on f3 only when it helps your structure. } 9. c3 e6 10. h3 Bh5 11. Bd2 { Overall: trade the e4 knight, pin on f3 and develop calmly; White has a small lead in space but no real attack. You should aim to castle and play ...c5 when ready. This is a semi-open position with White holding a small space edge in the centre. White has pawns on c3 and d4, you have c6 and e6, and you have pinned the f3 knight and just played ...Bh5. Your plan: complete development with ...Be7 or ...Bd6, castle short, then break with ...c5 when ready. White wants to castle and use Bd2 and Qe2 to build a kingside attack, so keep your bishop safe on h5 or g6. The risk is trading too early on f3 and losing time; if White plays g4, retreat the bishop to g6 and keep calm. }) 6... Nxf6 { [%csl Gf6,Rd3,Rc4,Rf4][%cal Gd7f6,Rf1c4,Rf1d3,Rc2c4,Rc1f4] } 7. Bc4 $10 { This is one of the most common moves in the database. White develops a bishop against f7 and can soon add a second attacker in the form of the queen. } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gf1c4] } (7. Bd3 $14 { Bd3 leads to roughly equal (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 7... Bg4 $14 { As soon as the knight lands on f3, pin it. } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 8. h3 $15 { h3 is not possible unless white wants to lose a pawn. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } (8. c3 $10 { White has to defend the pawn or otherwise gain a disadvantageous position. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 8... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 9. h3 { [%csl Bh3,Bh5,Bf3][%cal Bh2h3,Bg4h5,Bg4f3] } 9... Bh5 { Bh5 leads to roughly equal (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bg4h5] } (9... Bxf3 $14 { Even Tigran Petrosian liked this idea, since it prevents White from playing Ne5. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 10. Qxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd1f3] } 10... Qd5 $14 { Aiming for quick simplification. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd8d5] } 11. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd3e2] } 11... a5 { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 12. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 12... Qxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd5f3] } 13. Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Be2f3] } 13... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6]