ats it to b3 where it still aims at f7. } 15... b5 { Gain space on the queenside and prepare ...Nd5 or ...c5. } 16. Rc1 Nd5 17. Bd2 Qb7 { Aim at the long diagonal and the b3 bishop. } 18. Bc2 Bxc2 19. Rxc2 Rc8 { You trade your good bishop for the one that eyes your kingside and take the c-file, so the game is balanced (about 0.0). }) 9... Be4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf5e4] } 10. c3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 10... Bd6 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf8d6] } 11. g3 { g3 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Gg3][%cal Gg2g3] } (11. f3 $15 { White desperately tries to eliminate the light-squared bishop and may succeed, but Black gets a very comfortable position in return. } 11... Bd5 12. Bd3 c5 13. c4 Bc6 14. Qe2 O-O $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.4).] } 15. f4 $17 { White wants to give the h4 knight a stable base and grab space, but f3 and f4 have left e3 and the e1-h4 diagonal weak. } (15. dxc5 Bxc5+ 16. Kh1 Qd4 17. Bg5 h6 18. Bxf6 Qxf6 19. g3 Bd4 { Opening the centre does not help either: your queen lands on d4 and White's king is short of pieces nearby (about -1.3). }) 15... cxd4 { You win a pawn. } 16. Nf3 Qb6 { Defend the pawn, hit b2 and pin White to the long diagonal. } 17. Kh1 Nd7 18. b3 Rae8 19. Bb2 e5 { Your central pawns roll forward and you keep the extra pawn with a clear edge (about -1.3). }) 11... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 12. Bg5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gd2g5] } 12... h6 { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gh7h6] } 13. Bxf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg5f6] } 13... Qxf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gd8f6] } 14. Qe2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gd1e2] } 14... Bh7! $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair.] [%csl Gh7][%cal Ge4h7] } 15. Rad1 $10 { White has traded off a pair of minor pieces and now wants to use the d-file and bring the h4 knight back into play before your bishops dominate. } 15... Rad8 16. Rfe1 Qe7 17. Nf3 Bf5 { Your light bishop eyes the kingside and the d3 square. } 18. Kg2 Bg4 19. h3 Bxf3+ 20. Qxf3 { Trading the bishop for the knight cuts out White's best minor piece for use on e5; the position is balanced (about +0.1) and neither king is under pressure. } *


