1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Finnish Variation leaves White with doubled c-pawns after an early structural concession, which is a genuine long-term target rather than a cosmetic flaw: Black's plan is to fix the weakness (avoid trades that let White repair it with c3-c4 or similar), centralize pieces on the open d-file, and head toward an endgame where the extra pawn island decides. There's no sharp tactical content once the structure is reached, this is a technical, 'nurse the small advantage' chapter, and the main practical skill is not rushing: trade pieces that help you reach the endgame, keep pieces that restrain the weak pawns, and avoid any pawn move that would let White's doubled pawns untangle.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.5 to +0.6, a small White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd4? on moves 4 to 7 loses material (about 7.1); ...Qxh2+? on moves 10 to 11 loses material (about 6.7); 11...Qc8? (about 1.1; prefer ...O-O); 6...Bc8? (about 1.0; prefer ...e6); 11...Qd7? (about 0.9; prefer ...O-O); 8...Na6? (about 0.9; prefer ...e6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: no White mistake costing 0.5 or more was found along this line.
Chapter #14 continues the Classical Variation. Other Classical Variation chapters: #9, #10, #11, #12. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #2. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nc3 $10 { Black usually answers with the solid 3...dxe4, among the most heavily analysed Caro-Kann lines. Sidesteps exist, but 3...e6 blocks the c8-bishop and amounts to a French a tempo down, which makes it a dubious choice. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 3... dxe4 $10 { 3...dxe4 is black's only good move. Other moves are not in the spirit of this opening. This move also denies white his e4/d4 pawn center. White almost invariably responds with 4. Nxe4, but a few gambit continuations do exist. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 $14 { 4. Nxe4 is an obvious recapture. Black has 3 main alternatives. The Classical Variation, 4...Bf5, usually sees the bishop controlling the light diagonal from g6. The Smyslov/Karpov variation delays the development of the light bishop, with Nd7, while Nf6 risks doubled pawns for an open file. None of the other alternatives have seen significant usage. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 4... h6 $14 { With move 4...h6 Black creates escape square for light squared bishop's later use. It is usually considered as an inaccuracy and it is therefore rarely seen in higher levels. White's most usual respond is 5. Nf3. Natural development move 5. Bc4 is also played often and usually with an aim to sacrifice bishop in f7. } { [%csl Gh6,Gf5,Gh7,Re2,Rd3][%cal Gh7h6,Gc8f5,Gf5h7,Rg1e2,Rf1d3] } 5. Bd3 $14 { Simply a developing move, black does not have to worry getting his light square bishop harassed because of the Bh7 hiding spot. However many may find this unnecessary since black would typically exchange light square bishops. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } (5. Ne2 $14 { An interesting sideline that provokes ...Nf6, since ...Bf5 would only lead to something like Ng3. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 5... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 6. Nxf6+ { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be4f6] } 6... exf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be7f6] } 7. Nf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be2f4] } 7... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 8. Qe2+ { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 8... Qe7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bd8e7] } 9. Bd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } 9... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.6). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. O-O-O $14 { White castles long and offers a queen trade; the doubled f-pawns are your structural problem. } 10... Qxe2 11. Bxe2 Bf5 12. Bd3 Bxd3 13. Nxd3 Na6 { The knight goes to c7 and e6. } 14. Rhe1 Nc7 { You trade pieces and the doubled pawns are solid; White keeps a small edge (about +0.6). }) (5. Nf3 { Simple developing move, yet it easily transposes to the Capablanca variation with h6 on the board. } 5... Bf5 6. Ng3 Bh7 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Nd7 9. Bd2 e6 10. O-O-O $14 { White castles long and prepares g4 against your h6 and kingside. } 10... Ngf6 11. Rhe1 Be7 12. Ne4 O-O 13. g4 Nxg4 { You take the pawn. } 14. Rg1 f5 { f5 supports the g4 knight and kicks the e4 knight, so you hold on; White keeps a small edge (about +0.6). }) (5. Bc4 { Another move variation that is mostly played. Yet easily transposes to the Capablanca variation with Bf5 then e6. } 5... Bf5 6. Ng3 Bh7 7. N1e2 Nf6 8. O-O e6 9. Nf4 $10 { The knight attacks e6, which is also attacked by Bc4 and defended by f7 and the queen. } (9. f4 Nbd7 10. f5 e5 11. c3 Bd6 12. Qb3 O-O 13. Qxb7 c5 { f5 shuts in the h7 bishop for a moment, but ...e5 and ...c5 free it (about 0.0). }) 9... Be7 { Develop and prepare O-O. } 10. Nfh5 Nxh5 11. Nxh5 O-O 12. Re1 Nd7 13. c3 Bg5 { You trade the f6 knight and get your bishop active. The game is level (about +0.2). }) 5... Bf5 { [%csl Gf5,Rc5,Re2][%cal Gc8f5,Re4c5,Rg1e2] } 6. Nc5 { Nc5 leads to a modest White edge (+0.6), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Ge4c5] } (6. Ne2 $14 { White wants to play Ng3 and kick off the light square bishop forcibly evicting it. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 6... Bxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf5e4] } 7. Bxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } 7... Nf6 $14 { Exchanging the bishop for knight is beneficial, black now does not have to shuffle his bishop thus losing a tempo. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 8. Bf3 { [%csl Be4,Bf3][%cal Be4f3] } 8... Nbd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 9. O-O { [%csl Bg1,Be6,Be5][%cal Be1g1,Be7e6,Be7e5] } 9... e5 $14 { Black plans ...Qc7 followed by ...0-0-0. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be7e5] } (9... e6 $14 { e6 is also possible for those who want to avoid complications on the e file. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 10. c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 10... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 11. Qc2 { [%csl Bc2][%cal Bd1c2] } 11... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.6). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 12. Rb1 $14 { Rb1 prepares b4 and c5 to gain queenside space. } 12... e5 { Strike at d4 first. } 13. b4 Re8 14. Rd1 exd4 15. Bf4 d3 { A pawn sac to gain a tempo on the queen. } 16. Rxd3 Qc8 { You rebuild and play ...Qc8 with a solid position; White keeps the bishop pair and a slight edge (about +0.6). }) 10. Ng3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Be2g3] } 10... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 11. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 11... O-O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bc8][%cal Be8c8] } 12. b4 $14 { White offers a pawn to open lines toward your king on c8, with Bf3 already aiming at c6. } 12... exd4 13. cxd4 Bxb4 { The pawn grab opens the queenside and you must keep the king covered. } 14. Rb1 Bf8 15. Qa4 Kb8 16. Qxc6 Qxc6 17. Bxc6 { White regains the pawn with the pin on c6 and keeps a clear edge (about +0.9). }) 6... Bxd3 $14 { White forces black to exchange bishops possibly showing that the move h6 is a waste of tempo.   WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.0 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 Bxf5 e6 Bd3 Bxc5 } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf5d3] } 7. Qxd3 { WATCH OUT: Nxb7 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -3.6 from White's own side of the board), after: Qb6 Qxd3 Qxb7 Nf3 e6 Ne5 } (7. Nxb7 Qb6 8. Qxd3 Qxb7 9. Nf3 e6 10. Ne5 Nf6 $19 { Develop and cover d5 and h5; you are a knight for a pawn up, so just avoid tricks. } 11. f4 Be7 12. f5 { White tries to rip your uncastled king open. } 12... exf5 13. O-O Nbd7 { You give a pawn back to complete development. } 14. Qxf5 c5 15. Bf4 { You stay a piece up with a big edge (about -3.4). }) 7... Qc7 { [%csl Gc7,Rb3,Rf3,Rd3][%cal Gd8c7,Rd3b3,Rg1f3] } 8. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } (8. Qb3 $14 { Qb3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd3b3] } 8... b6 $14 { ...b6 is forced: there is no other way to continue, and otherwise Black loses a pawn and the game. } { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 9. Nd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bc5d3] } 9... e6 { [%cal Be7e6] } 10. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bc1f4] } 10... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 11. Bxd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf4d6] } 11... Qxd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bc7d6] } 12. O-O-O { [%csl Bc1][%cal Be1c1] } 12... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 13. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 13... Rc8 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] [%csl Bc8][%cal Bg8e7,Be8g8,Ba7a5,Bb6b5,Ba8c8] } 14. Rhe1 { White has castled long and wants Qb4 to hit b6 and e7 before you can untangle. } (14. Nfe5 Ngf6 15. Nc4 Qe7 16. Qa3 Qxa3 17. Nxa3 Nd5 18. Kb1 O-O { the knight jump is no more dangerous, about +0.1 }) 14... Ne7 15. Qb4 Qc7 16. Kb1 c5 { The ...c5 break is your equaliser, since it hits the queen and opens lines against the White king. } 17. dxc5 O-O { You castle with a healthy structure and good play on the c-file. }) 8... e6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 9. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 9... Bxc5 { [%csl Gf8,Gc5][%cal Gf8c5] } 10. dxc5 $14 { Exchanging the bishop for knight is beneficial since now white does not have an outpost on c5 with a knight which is affecting black's development. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gd4c5] } 10... Ne7 { [%csl Gg8,Ge7][%cal Gg8e7] } 11. Rd1 { [%csl Gd1][%cal Gf1d1] } 11... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.6). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the c-file.] [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 12. Qc4 $14 { White's doubled c-pawns give the Bd6 a firm outpost, and the queen eyes your c8 square. } 12... Nd7 13. Bf4 Qc8 14. a4 b6 { Challenge the c5 pawn. } 15. Bd6 Re8 16. a5 Nf5 { You hit the d6 bishop and your pieces get active; White keeps a small edge (about +0.6). } *


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Chapter #14: Classical Variation, Gurgenidze Variation"]
