[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Kampfschach_Official"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Modern Variation"]
[StudyName "Greatest Caro Kann: Living Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Chapter #14: Classical Variation, Gurgenidze Variation"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/BKDKGrt0"]

1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Gurgenidze treatment accepts Black's own doubled f-pawns (mirroring the Tartakower idea from Chapter #10) in a position where White has already committed to a4-b4 queenside expansion. Black's king-side fianchetto (...g6, ...Bg7) means the doubled pawns are actually well-placed, controlling e5/g5 and supporting the king. The practical plan is to meet White's queenside pawn storm with timely counterplay in the center or against the slightly loosened white queenside itself (the a4/b4 pawns can become targets later), rather than passive defense. The half-open e-file for White and d-file for Black are, as usual, where the rooks want to be.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.1 to +0.4, a small White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd4? on moves 9 to 13 loses material (about 6.7); ...Bxd4? at move 6 loses material (about 4.9); 4...Qc7?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Bg7); 6...Qd5?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Nf6); 4...a6?! (about 0.4; prefer ...Bg7).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 22.f5? Bb7 (costs White about 2.9); 18.Qf3? Rxd4 (costs White about 1.1); 9.O-O?! O-O (costs White about 0.6).
Other Classical Variation chapters: #9, #10, #11, #12, #13. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #2 and #9. Model game: #5. The Gurgenidze Variation is named for Bukhuti Gurgenidze (1933-2008), the Georgian grandmaster (GM title, 1970) whose analysis of 3...g6 against 3.Nc3 - together with the sharper 3...b5 "Gurgenidze Counterattack" covered later in this chapter, the same named idea rather than an unrelated one - is credited with pushing 3.Nc3 out of fashion as White's main try during the 1970s. Against the Modern Variation move order used in this course (2.Nd2 instead of 2.Nc3), White's whole point is to sidestep this complex: with the knight not yet committed to c3, White keeps c2-c3 in reserve to support d4 even after Black fianchettoes, denying the Gurgenidze bishop its usual target on the long diagonal. This chapter covers the resulting positions after 2...g6 - both the modern c3 setup and the transposition back into a Two Knights Attack structure via an early Nc3 - plus White's alternative tries against an early ...Bg4. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5,Rc3,Rd2][%cal Gd7d5,Rb1d2,Rb1c3] } 3. Nd2 $14 { The Modern Variation is played for exactly this one reason: to avoid the Gurgenidze system. Now White has still the option to play c3, and then a bishop on g7 wouldn't be very effective. With the Nd2 move order. Black is free to take the pawn and play something like Bf5 or Nf6, Nd7 or even h6! Which brings us back to the Classical Caro Kann. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } (3. Nc3 $10 { Nc3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 3... g6 $14 { Black prepares to fianchetto the bishop on g7, in order to attack White's d4 pawn. There's also the usual pin Nf3 Bg4 incoming, so White can play h3 now to prevent it or one move later after Bg7. The c6-c5 break is not supported by the dark-squared bishop, so the knight has to do this job by going to d7. } { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf8g7,Bg7d4,Bc6c5,Bc8g4,Bb8d7,Bg7g6] } (3... b5 $16 { The real "Gurgenidze Counterattack": Bukhuti Gurgenidze's own sharper answer to White's knight development, alongside the quieter ...g6 fianchetto covered in the rest of this chapter - the same named idea, not an unrelated one. Black skips the fianchetto entirely and strikes on the queenside immediately. } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 4. a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba2a3] } 4... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 5. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 5... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 6. Nxf6+ { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be4f6] } 6... exf6 $14 { After 4.a3 (preventing ...b4) the position usually resolves in the center along fairly standard lines; Stockfish rates the position here as only a modest, ordinary edge for White (about +0.7 at depth 22) - a real try, not a refutation of Black's idea. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be7f6] } 7. Bd3 Be7 8. Ne2 O-O 9. c3 Re8 $14 { A Stockfish-verified illustrative continuation (not a cited game): White develops naturally and keeps that small, ordinary edge (about +0.7 at depth 22) thanks to Black's slightly loose kingside light squares after ...exf6 - nothing dramatic, and certainly not a near-refutation of Black's counterattack. } 10. Qc2 { White lines queen and bishop up on h7, so the point is to attack your king, not to develop. } (10. O-O g6 11. Bh6 Bd6 12. Re1 Na6 13. Qd2 Nc7 14. a4 a6 { Bh6 only provokes ...Bd6 and is not dangerous, about +0.7 }) 10... g6 { This shuts the diagonal at once. } 11. h4 f5 { Stops Bxg6 ideas and gives the king room. } 12. h5 Be6 13. a4 bxa4 14. Qxa4 Bd5 { White has a small edge, about +0.7, but you are solid and the f6 weakness is covered. }) 4. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } (4. h3 $14 { Prevent the pin immediately. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 4... Bg7 { [%csl Bg7][%cal Bf8g7] } 5. Nf3 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4).] [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 5... dxe4 $14 { The pawn on h3 stops ...Bg4, so you take in the centre and recapture with the pawn on f6. That gives you the e-file and the pawn controls e5. } (5... Nf6 6. e5 Ne4 7. a4 O-O 8. Bd3 Nxc3 9. bxc3 c5 10. O-O { A playable alternative (about +0.5): White has doubled c-pawns but a solid centre. }) 6. Nxe4 Nf6 7. Nxf6+ exf6 8. Be2 O-O 9. O-O Re8 { Your rook takes the open e-file at once. } 10. Re1 { White has the slightly better structure (about +0.3) but you have a sound king and active pieces. }) (4. e5 $14 { e5 leads to a clear White edge (+0.8), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } 4... Bg7 { [%csl Bg7][%cal Bf8g7] } 5. f4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bf2f4] } 5... Nh6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bg8h6] } 6. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 6... f6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bf7f6] } 7. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 7... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 8. h3 $16 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.9).] [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 8... Qb6 $14 { You hit b2 and lean on d4, so White must answer with Qc1 or similar instead of finishing development. } (8... fxe5 9. dxe5 Nf5 10. Bf2 d4 11. Bc4+ Kh8 12. Ne4 Nd7 13. c3 { Taking at once is a shade less accurate (about +1.2): the ...d4 push runs into Bc4+ and a strong knight on e4. }) 9. Qc1 { Qc1 covers b2 and keeps the bishop protected. } 9... Nf5 { The knight attacks Be3 and d4 together, so White spends a tempo on the bishop. } 10. Bf2 Bh6 { Your bishop hits the f4 pawn, the head of White's chain, so ...fxe5 can open the f-file later. } 11. Bd3 Ng7 12. Ne2 Ne6 13. O-O { The knight on e6 presses f4 and d4. White has more space but no direct threat, so you wait with ...fxe5 in reserve (about +1.0). }) 4... Bg7 $14 { This is essentially a Two Knights Attack, but Black has fianchettoed the bishop instead of playing ...Nf6 or ...Bg4. That takes two moves, giving White an extra tempo for d4. White will play c3 to support d4, so the c3 knight must move, which returns the tempo to Black. The result is a slow positional fight for d4. Black will castle kingside and then attack on the queenside. } { [%csl Bd4,Bg6,Bc3,Bg7][%cal Bc6c5,Bb8d7,Bd7c5,Bf8g7] } 5. h3 $14 { Most played move here. It prevents Bg4 for the moment. But it also weakens the king-side pawns, and if White castles queen-side later, c3 isn't so attractive anymore. Also, it doesn't develop a piece. } { [%csl Bh3,Bg4][%cal Bh2h3,Bc8g4] } (5. Be2 $14 { An alternative to prevent the pin. Also develops the bishop, and enables king-side castling. Note that White has already developed three pieces, and they can castle. Black has only one piece developed. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 5... Bg4 $14 { anyway. There is no other good square for Black's light-squared bishop. } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } (5... dxe4 $14 { is not recommended, because it improves White's knight. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 6. Nxe4 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] [%csl Be4][%cal Be4f6,Be4c5,Be4g5,Bc3e4] } 6... Nf6 $14 { You invite the exchange. The pawn recaptures on f6 and gives you the e-file and an easy development. } (6... Bf5 7. Ng3 Bg4 8. c3 Nf6 9. O-O O-O 10. a4 Qc7 11. a5 { ...Bf5 loses time here, since Ng3 comes with a gain of a tempo; this is a little less accurate (about +0.4). }) 7. Nxf6+ exf6 8. O-O O-O 9. Bf4 a5 { A useful waiting move that stops White from gaining space with a2-a4 and a later a5 clamp. } 10. Re1 Re8 11. Qd2 { White has a slight lead in development (about +0.3) but your structure is under no real pressure. Aim for ...Nd7 and ...Nb6. }) 6. Be3 { Be3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Be3,Be6,Be4][%cal Bc1e3,Be7e6,Bd5e4] } (6. O-O $14 { is probably the most accurate here. Now it's time to get Black's knight to f6 to enable castling. However, e5 by White would be very uncomfortable, so Black has to take e4 now. } { [%cal Bg8f6,Be8g8] } 6... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 7. Nxe4 { [%csl Bf6,Bf3][%cal Bg8f6,Bg4f3] } 7... Nf6 { Nf6 leads to a modest White edge (+0.8): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } (7... Bxf3?! $14 { Black can win a pawn here temporarily, at the cost of development. This is not recommended. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 8. Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Be2f3] } 8... Qxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd8d4] } 9. Qxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd1d4] } 9... Bxd4 $16 { But White has counterplay. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bg7d4] } 10. Re1! $16 { with the threat of Nd6. It's not so easy to deal with that. } { [%csl Be1][%cal Be4d6,Bd6b7,Be1e7,Bf1e1] } 10... Na6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Bb8a6] } 11. Nd6+ { [%csl Bd6][%cal Be4d6] } 11... Kf8 { [%csl Bf8][%cal Be8f8] } 12. Nxb7 $16 { White has regained the pawn and stands clearly better here (Stockfish: roughly +1.7 at depth 22) - Black's queenside structure is wrecked and White's bishop pair plus the safer king add up to a real, lasting edge, not just material equality. } { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bd6b7] } 12... Rb8 { [%csl Bb8][%cal Ba8b8] } 13. Rd1 $16 { And this rook will enter the 7th rank now. Also, c6 is hanging. } { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bd1d7,Bf3c6,Be1d1] } 13... Bb6 14. Bxc6 Nb4 15. Be4 $16 { A Stockfish-verified illustrative line (not a cited game): 13.Rd1! keeps hammering the loose bishop on d4, and after 13...Bb6 14.Bxc6 White collects a second pawn while Black's pieces stay passive. } 15... Nxc2 $14 { The knight cannot stay on b4 for long, since a2-a3 would drive it away, so you cash it in for the c2 pawn and force the bishop to recapture. } (15... f5 16. Bf4 Rc8 17. c3 fxe4 18. cxb4 Rc2 19. Rd2 Rc4 20. a4 { Hitting the bishop is too slow: Bf4 and c3 trap the knight and White comes out well ahead (about +2.1). }) 16. Bxc2 Rxb7 { You take back the knight on b7, so material is level again, but White keeps the bishop pair and the more active pieces. } 17. Bf4 Ba5 { The bishop covers d8 against Rd8+ ideas and stays active. } 18. Rd5 Bb6 19. Ba4 e6 20. Bc6 { White plants a bishop on c6 and holds the d-file (about +1.4). You must activate the king with ...Ke7 and ...Rd7 to trade the rooks. }) 8. Nxf6+ { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be4f6] } 8... exf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be7f6] } 9. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 9... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. h3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 10... Bf5 $14 $146 { is the more precise move order here. Black wants to trade this bishop for White's good light-squared bishop rather than for a knight. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bg4f5] } 11. Re1 $14 { White puts the rook on the e-file against your doubled f-pawns and prepares Bf4 and a4. } (11. Bf4 Re8 12. Re1 Nd7 13. Qb3 Nb6 14. c4 a5 15. Bf1 Rxe1 { Bf4 and Qb3 aim at b7 and d6, but ...Nb6 covers both and you trade a pair of rooks (about +0.5). }) 11... Qc7 { You step off the d-file and eye the bishop on f4 if it arrives. } 12. a4 Re8 13. a5 Na6 { White gains space and stops ...b5, so your knight heads for c5 or b4. } 14. Be3 Bf8 { The bishop guards the dark squares and prepares ...Rad8 and a ...e5 break. } 15. Nd2 Rad8 { White keeps a small edge (about +0.6) but your position is sound, with ...Bg6 and a central break as your plan. }) 6... dxe4 { dxe4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): Black has a material edge of about 1 point(s); Black has doubled pawns.   (Black's extra pawn doesn't cancel White's edge here - White gets the usual compensation for this kind of early pawn-grab: faster development and open lines, worth more than one pawn this soon.) } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } (6... e6 $14 { Worth considering, transposing into a Grunfeld-Slav Defence. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7. Ne5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 7... Bxe2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg4e2] } 8. Qxe2 { WATCH OUT: exd5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.5 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 dxc6 Nxc6 Nxc6 bxc6 Rxd1 } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 8... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 9. O-O { O-O leads to a modest Black edge (-0.5): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } (9. f3 $14 { Before castling, White does slightly better inserting f3 first, solidifying the center and taking the e4 and g4 squares away from Black's minor pieces. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf2f3] } 9... Nbd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 10. Nd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Be5d3] } 10... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 11. fxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf3e4] } 11... Qa5 $14 { A modest improvement, keeping a small normal-opening edge for White (Stockfish: roughly +0.55 at depth 22) - nothing that requires a new plan from Black, just an incrementally more precise move order to be aware of. } { [%csl Ba5][%cal Bd8a5] } 12. Bd2 $14 { Your queen pins the knight on c3 to the king, so White's first job is to unpin, and Bd2 also prepares castling long. } (12. e5 Ne4 13. b4 Nxc3 14. bxa5 Nxe2 15. Kxe2 O-O 16. Rab1 b6 { The point of e5 is that b4 would trap the queen on a5, but you take on c3 first and the trades come out even (about +0.5). }) 12... e5 { You challenge the centre before White is ready. } 13. d5 cxd5 14. Nxd5 Qd8 { The knight is centralised, but your queen simply steps away and gives White nothing to hit. } 15. Nc3 O-O 16. O-O-O Nb6 { White has the freer game (about +0.7), but you are solid and can target the open d-file with ...Rfd8. }) 9... O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. f3 { White supports e4 so that the knight on e5 has a stable post, and the centre stays intact (about +0.1). } (10. Bg5 Qb6 11. Qd3 Nbd7 12. Nxd7 Nxd7 13. Be3 f5 { Pinning the knight with Bg5 does not impress: ...Qb6 hits b2 and d4 and you get the slightly better chances (about -0.1). }) 10... Nfd7 { You challenge the Ne5, the piece that makes White's setup work. } 11. Nd3 c5 { You open the position for your Bg7 and strike at d4. } 12. Nxc5 Nxc5 13. dxc5 d4 { White wins a pawn, but this push hits both Be3 and Nc3 with tempo and your bishop on g7 comes to life. } 14. Rad1 b6 { You win the pawn back and the position is level. }) 7. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4,Bh6,Bf6,Bd7][%cal Bc3e4,Bg8h6,Bg8f6,Bb8d7] } 7... Nh6 { Nh6 leads to a modest White edge (+0.6), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh6f5,Bf5e3,Bf5d4,Bg8h6] } (7... Nf6 $14 { Nf6 leads to a modest White edge (+0.7), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Be4,Bf6][%cal Be4f6] } 8. h3 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7).] [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } (8. Nxf6+ $14 { Nxf6+ leads to a modest White edge (+0.7): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s). } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be4f6] } 8... exf6 9. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 9... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. c4 $14 { White grabs space and prepares d5 and Qb3 against your doubled f-pawns, and the pawn on c4 takes d5 away from your pieces. } (10. h3 Bf5 11. c4 Re8 12. d5 cxd5 13. Nd4 Be4 14. f3 Bf5 { White's d5 break opens the centre, but your bishop keeps its diagonal (about +0.7). }) 10... Re8 { The rook takes the open file and presses the bishop on e3. } 11. h3 Bf5 { The bishop on g4 is asked a question and retreats rather than being given up for a knight. } 12. Re1 Bf8 { You cover e7 and the dark squares and make room for ...Na6-c7. } 13. Nd2 Na6 14. a3 Nc7 { The knight reaches e6 or d5 and the position is solid. White's small edge (about +0.7) is the better structure, with no direct threat. }) 8... Bxf3 $14 { You trade before h3 can chase the bishop, and the knight on e4 will take on f6 next. } (8... Nxe4 9. hxg4 Nd7 10. Qd3 Qa5+ 11. c3 Nd6 12. Kf1 Rd8 13. g3 { Tempting because it trades a knight for a bishop, but after hxg4 White has the bishop pair and the open h-file (about +0.9). }) 9. Nxf6+ Bxf6 10. Bxf3 Nd7 11. O-O Nb6 { The knight hits c4 and d5, so White cannot comfortably take over the centre with c4. } 12. Qe2 O-O 13. Rfd1 { White has the bishop pair and the d-file, but your position has no weaknesses and ...c5 is your counter (about +0.5). }) 8. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 8... Nf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bf5e3,Bh6f5] } 9. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be3f4] } 9... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 10. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 10... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 11. Re1 $14 { Brown, Martin - Arkell, Keith C, 0-1, GBR-ch 99th, 2012 } { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 11... Bxf3 $14 { You trade the bishop for the knight so that ...e5 comes with a tempo on the bishop and the d4 pawn loses the Nf3 as a defender. } (11... Nf6 12. Nxf6+ Bxf6 13. a4 Bxf3 14. Bxf3 e6 15. g4 Ne7 16. Bg3 { A solid alternative (about +0.7), but White gets g4 in with extra space. }) 12. Bxf3 e5 { Your central break hits both d4 and the bishop on f4. } 13. Bg5 f6 { The pawn kicks the bishop, which was eyeing your queen. } 14. Qb3+ Kh8 { The king steps off the diagonal and the check gains nothing for White. } 15. Bd2 exd4 16. Bg4 { White's bishops are active and Qb3 hits b7, so you must keep your knights protected and your b-pawn watched (about +0.6). }) (5. e5 $14 { e5 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } 5... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 6. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 6... e6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5).] [%csl Be6][%cal Bb8d7,Bc6c5,Bd7c5,Bg4f3,Bg8e7,Be7e6] } 7. Nd2 $14 { White does not want the Bg4 pin to bite on f3, so Nd2 keeps the e5 wedge, frees f3 for the knight and invites the bishop trade (about +0.4). } (7. O-O Ne7 8. Nh4 Bxe2 9. Nxe2 c5 10. c3 cxd4 11. cxd4 O-O { After Nh4 you trade first and play the same plan, hitting d4. }) 7... Bxe2 8. Nxe2 Ne7 { The knight supports c6 and the ...c5 break, and with the light-squared bishop gone the e6 pawn is no longer a weakness. } 9. Nf3 c5 { Your freeing blow: e5 is guarded only by the knight and d4 is attacked by c5 and soon ...Nbc6. } 10. c3 Nbc6 11. O-O O-O { You are fully developed and keep pressure on d4, so White's space is more a target than a trump. }) 5... Nf6 { Nf6 leads to a modest White edge (+0.7), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } (5... dxe4 $14 { The exchange here leads to a very open game with lots of material trades and a high chance for a draw. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 6. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 6... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 7. Nxf6+ { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be4f6] } 7... exf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be7f6] } 8. Bc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 8... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 9. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 9... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 10. Re1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 10... Nb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd7b6] } 11. Bb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc4b3] } 11... a5 { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 12. a4 { a4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.6): Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Ba4,Bd5,Bf5,Be8][%cal Ba2a4,Bb6d5,Bf6f5,Bf8e8] } (12. a3 $14 { a3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4): Black has doubled pawns. } 12... a4 13. Ba2 Re8 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 14. Rxe8+ $14 { White trades the rooks before you can double on the e-file or use ...Bf5 and ...Qd7. } (14. c3 Be6 15. Bxe6 fxe6 16. Bf4 Nc4 17. Nd2 Qd5 18. Qc2 Nxd2 { If White lets you trade on e6, the doubled pawns are repaired and you equalise (about +0.1). }) 14... Qxe8 15. c4 { White gains space and takes d5 away from your pieces. } 15... Be6 { You offer the trade of the light-squared bishops at once, so that the c4 pawn cannot be backed by a d5 push. } 16. Qe2 Bf8 17. Bd2 Bf5 18. Be3 c5 { You have freed the position with ...c5, and the only thing left is White's small edge from the extra space (about +0.4). }) 12... Nd5 { Nd5 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bb6d5] } (12... f5?! $16 { Too early, it gives White a chance to push the rook to the 7th rank. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bf6f5] } 13. Bg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 13... Qc7 { WATCH OUT: Bxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bxd8 Rxd8 Nxd4 Nd5 Bxd5 cxd5 } { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 14. Re7 $16 { [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+1.4). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] [%csl Be7][%cal Be1e7] } 14... Qd6 $16 { Your queen is attacked by the rook on e7, and because the bishop on g5 guards that rook you cannot simply take it. Qd6 moves with a counter-threat: it hits the rook again and takes the d-file. } (14... Nd7 15. Ne5 f4 16. Nxf7 Qb6 17. Nd8+ Qxb3 18. cxb3 Nb6 19. Nxb7 { The knight on d7 is attacked twice and Ne5 brings tricks on f7 and d8 (about +5.8). }) (14... Bd7 15. Ne5 h6 16. Bh4 Rac8 17. Qd2 g5 18. Bxg5 hxg5 19. Qxg5 { Blocking with the bishop runs into Ne5 and a weakened kingside (about +4.2). }) 15. c4 c5 { You hit d4 so the rook must retreat or the centre dissolves. } 16. Re2 Bf6 { Trading bishops removes the piece that was shielding White's rook. } 17. dxc5 Qxd1+ 18. Rxd1 Nd7 19. Bxf6 { White keeps a clear edge (about +1.2) with better pieces, but you are not lost: trade queens and fight to win back c5. }) (12... Re8 $14 { Black should try to exchange the rooks, so White can't invade on the 7th rank once Black plays ...f5. } { [%csl Be8][%cal Bf6f5,Bc1g5,Be1e7,Bf8e8] } 13. Bd2 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } 13... Bf5 $14 { The bishop leaves c8 and fights for the e4 square. Your rook on e8 keeps the e-file contested, so White cannot simply invade with rooks. } (13... Re7 14. Rxe7 Qxe7 15. c4 Be6 16. Qe2 Qc7 17. Rc1 Bd7 18. Bc3 { Offering the rook trade is also playable (about +0.7), but it lets White play c4 with extra space. }) 14. c4 Be4 { The bishop is centralised and hits the knight on f3 and the c2 square. } 15. Bc3 c5 { A central counterstrike: it hits d4 and takes the sting out of the bishop on c3. } 16. dxc5 Nd7 17. Qd4 Bc6 18. Bc2 { You pressure the c5 pawn with ...Nd7 and ...Bc6, and the pieces are active on both sides. White's edge is small (about +0.5). }) 13. c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 13... Nb4 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.6). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] [%csl Bb4][%cal Bd5b4] } 14. d5 $14 { White opens the centre before your knight can settle on b4 for good, hitting the c6 pawn and preparing Bf4 and Rd2 on the open d-file. } (14. Bd2 c5 15. Bc3 b6 16. Qd2 Qc7 17. Re2 Bf5 18. g4 Bd7 { The calmer way: Bd2 hits the knight on b4 and Bc3 keeps the pressure on it. }) 14... b6 15. Bf4 Bb7 { The bishop covers c6 and the long diagonal. } 16. Re2 cxd5 17. Rd2 dxc4 { You take two pawns, but the d-file is open and the rook on d2 and the queen on d1 point at your queen. } 18. Bxc4 Qc8 { The bishop recaptures with tempo on the queen. You are a pawn up, and White has open lines and activity for it (about +0.6). Keep the king covered and aim for ...Nd5. }) 6. e5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } 6... Ne4 $14 { Similar to the Two Knights attack, but here White can take that knight. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 7. Nxe4 { Nxe4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s). } (7. Bd3 $14 { Bd3 leads to a small White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 7... Nxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Be4c3] } 8. bxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb2c3] } 8... c5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the c-file.] [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc7c5] } 9. a4 $14 { White stops ...b5 and gets ready to answer ...Qa5 with Bd2, since the doubled pawn on c3 is the weakness of the structure. } (9. Be2 Qa5 10. Bd2 Nc6 11. a4 c4 12. O-O Bd7 13. Qc1 O-O-O { A move-order alternative that reaches the same structure. }) 9... Qa5 { The queen hits c3 and a4 together, so White must keep the pawn on c3 protected. } 10. O-O c4 { You gain space, kick the bishop and fix White's pawn structure. } 11. Be2 Nc6 12. Bd2 Bd7 { Bd2 guards c3. } 13. Qc1 O-O-O { Your king goes to the queenside; Bd2 and Qc1 aim at h6 but your bishop on g7 covers it (about +0.3). The doubled c-pawns remain White's long-term liability. }) 7... dxe4 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 Nc3 Qxb2 Rb1 Qa3 } 8. Ng5 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bg5e4] } 8... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 9. Bc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 9... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 10... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 11. cxd4 { [%csl Bd4,Bb5,Bc6][%cal Bc3d4,Bb7b5,Bb8c6] } 11... Nc6 $14 { Note that this knight cannot be pinned, in contrast to many other CK variations. } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } (11... b5 $14 { b5 leads to a clear White edge (+0.8): Black has doubled pawns; Black's king has reached safety while White's has not yet. } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 12. Bb3 { WATCH OUT: the two obvious tries here, 12.Nxe4 and 12.Bxb5, both backfire for White (-4.4 and -4.1). Reference lines: 12.Nxe4 bxc4 13.Be3 Bf5 14.Nc3 Bd3 15.Qa4 (-4.4); 12.Bxb5 Qa5+ 13.Bd2 Qxb5 14.Rc1 Na6 15.Qe2 (-4.1) } { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc4b3] } 12... Bb7 $16 { [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+1.7). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the e-file.] [%csl Bb7][%cal Bc8b7] } 13. h4 $16 { White supports the knight on g5. The Ng5 and the Bb3 both aim at f7, so ...h6 now runs into h5 and an open h-file against your king. } (13. e6 f5 14. d5 a5 15. Nf7 Rxf7 16. exf7+ Kf8 17. d6 Nc6 { Opening lines with e6 looks natural, but it is a lot less effective than h4 (about +0.7). }) 13... Nc6 { You hit d4 and e5 at once and prepare to trade off White's strong bishop on b3, the second attacker of f7. } 14. Be3 Na5 15. h5 Nxb3 16. axb3 h6 17. hxg6 hxg5 { The h-file opens and White's rook on h1 looks at your king. You must find defensive resources; White has a clear edge (about +1.4). }) 12. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 12... Qa5+ { [%csl Ba5][%cal Bd8a5] } 13. Kf1 { [%csl Bf1][%cal Be1f1] } 13... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 14. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bg5e4] } 14... Rd8 $10 { Pin the pawn for an attack on e4. } { [%csl Bd8][%cal Bc6e5,Bg7e5,Bf8d8] } 15. f4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bf2f4] } 15... b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 16. Bb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc4b3] } 16... Nxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc6d4] } 17. Bxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be3d4] } 17... Qb4 { [%csl Bb4,Bd4][%cal Bd8d4,Ba5b4,Bb4d4] } 18. Qf3 { Qf3 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.2): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s).   (White's three extra pawns aren't enough compensation here - Black's activity and the exposed White king are worth more, the same pattern as the Qxa8 lines later in this chapter.) } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf3a8,Bd1f3] } (18. Kf2 $10 { Rather than Qf3, the calm king move Kf2 is actually White's best try here, sidestepping tactics on the long diagonal before continuing. } { [%csl Bf2][%cal Bf1f2] } 18... Rxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd8d4] } 19. a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba2a3] } 19... Rxd1 { WATCH OUT: Rxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: axb4 Rxf4+ Ke3 Rf5 Rf1 Rxe5+ } { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bd4d1] } 20. axb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Ba3b4] } 20... Rxh1 $10 { Black's rook grabs the rook on h1, but it's simply hanging back to White's rook on a1 - the point of the whole sequence is that material ends up level, not that Black nets an extra exchange here. } { [%csl Bh1][%cal Bd1h1] } 21. Rxh1 $10 { With material level again, Stockfish calls this position essentially balanced (about +0.04 at depth 22) - a real, forced way to defuse Black's complications rather than a route to any concrete White advantage; treat it as a safe haven, not a winning try. } 21... g5 22. g3 Bf5 23. Ke3 Rd8 $10 { Stockfish's own suggested follow-up, included to show the position stays balanced rather than tipping either way. } 24. Ra1 { White wants the rook on the seventh rank hitting a7 and b7, and the knight on e4 is protected only by the king, so White cannot dawdle. } (24. Nc5 e6 25. g4 Bg6 26. Rd1 Rxd1 27. Bxd1 gxf4+ 28. Kxf4 f6 { A quieter route: the knights come off and you hold the same balance. }) 24... Bf8 { The bishop covers the dark squares and, once ...e6 is played, also hits the pawn on b4. } 25. Rxa7 e6 26. Rb7 gxf4+ 27. gxf4 Bxe4 { gxf4+ forces the recapture, and ...Bxe4 removes White's strong knight. } 28. Kxe4 Rd2 { Your rook hits b2 and is the more active piece, so the position is balanced (about 0.0). }) 18... Rxd4 { [%csl Bf4,Bd4][%cal Bd4f4,Bd8d4] } 19. a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba2a3] } 19... Qa5 { WATCH OUT: Rxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.4 from Black's own side of the board), after: axb4 Bb7 Bc2 Re1+ Kxe1 Bxf3 } { [%csl Ba5][%cal Bb4a5] } 20. Nf6+ { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be4f6] } 20... Bxf6 { Bxf6 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.5): Black has a material edge of about 3 point(s); Black retains the bishop pair. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg7f6] } (20... exf6 $15 { exf6 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.0): Black has a material edge of about 3 point(s); Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be7f6] } 21. Qxa8 { [%csl Ba8][%cal Bf3a8] } 21... Qc7 { WATCH OUT: fxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.0 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qxc8+ Kh7 Kg1 exf4 Bxf7 Rd8 } { [%csl Bc7][%cal Ba5c7] } 22. Kg1 { [%csl Bg1][%cal Bf1g1] } 22... Bf8 $10 { Ibragimov, Ildar - Stangl, Markus, 1-0, Dortmund op-A, 1992 } { [%csl Bf8][%cal Bg7f8] } 23. Kh2 $14 { White is up the exchange, but the king on g1 is exposed to ...Rd1+ and ...Bc5+ tricks, so Kh2 steps out of them first. } (23. Bd5 Rd2 24. Kh2 Qd7 25. Rhc1 Qxh3+ 26. Kg1 Bc5+ 27. Rxc5 Qe3+ { Bd5 looks natural because it blocks the d-file, but ...Rd2 opens an attack on the king and g2 (about -0.9). }) 23... Rxf4 { You win the f4 pawn, and the rook stays active along the fourth rank. } 24. Rhc1 Qxe5 25. Qd5 Qxd5 26. Bxd5 Bf5 27. g4 Bd6 { You have won two pawns for the exchange and your bishops are active. White is slightly better (about +0.4) but the position is dynamically balanced. }) 21. Qxa8 { Qxa8 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.0): White has a material edge of about 2 point(s); Black retains the bishop pair.   (Two extra pawns isn't enough against the bishop pair and Black's open lines here - the same pattern shows up again a few moves later.) } { [%csl Ba8][%cal Bf3a8] } (21. exf6 $19 { exf6 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.1): White has doubled pawns. } 21... Qb6 22. f5 Bb7 $19 { Xiong, Jeffery - Shabalov, A., 0-1, ch-USA 2017  CORRECTION TO AN EARLIER NOTE: an older pass through this position flagged 22...Bxf5, 22...Qxf6 and 22...exf6 as all "clearly worse" for Black, with a placeholder value that never actually got computed. Our own check shows that was wrong - none of the three are mistakes. 22...Bxf5 (after 23.Qxa8+ Kh7 24.Qf3 Bd3+ 25.Ke1 Qa5+) is dead equal (0.00); 22...Qxf6 (after 23.Qxa8 Qxf5+ 24.Ke2 Qe5+ 25.Kf1) keeps a modest Black edge (around -0.7); and 22...exf6 (after 23.fxg6 b4 24.gxf7+ Kf8 25.Qxa8 Rf4+) keeps a somewhat bigger one (around -1.2). They're simply less crushing than the text's actual recommendation, 22...Bb7, which our own check confirms really is close to winning outright (-4.6). WATCH OUT: gxf5 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.0 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qg3+ Kf8 Qg7+ Ke8 Qxf7+ Kd7 } 23. Qe3 $19 { White offers the queens and eyes h6 and f7, hoping the pawn on f6 and the bishop on b3 produce threats. It does not work. } (23. Bxf7+ Kxf7 24. fxg6+ Kxg6 25. Qg3+ Kxf6 26. Re1 Qd6 27. Qxd6+ Rxd6 { Bxf7+ tries to drag the king to g6 where Qg3+ can chase it, but ...Kxf6 simply walks away with the extra material (about -5.3). }) 23... Qxf6 { Taking the pawn removes White's attacking pawn on f6. } 24. Qxh6 Rd3 { The rook hits the bishop on b3 and keeps your pieces together. } 25. Bxf7+ Qxf7 26. Qxg6+ Qxg6 27. fxg6 Rf8+ { The checks run out and you stay clearly ahead (about -4.5). }) 21... Rxf4+ { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bd4f4] } 22. Ke2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 22... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Ba5c7] } 23. exf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be5f6] } 23... Qe5+ { WATCH OUT: Rxf6 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: Raf1 Rc6 Rxf7 Qe5+ Kf3 Qxb2 } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bc7e5] } 24. Kd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Be2d1] } 24... Kh7 { Kh7 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.4): White has a material edge of about 4 point(s).   (Even four pawns up, White's king is too exposed and Black's pieces too active for the material to count for much yet.) WATCH OUT: Rxf6 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qxc8+ Kh7 Qc3 Qg5 Re1 a5 } { [%csl Bh7][%cal Bg8h7] } (24... Qxb2 $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.1).] WATCH OUT: several other checks and captures look tempting here too, and all of them throw away most of that edge: 24...Qxf6, 24...Qd6+, 24...Qh5+ and 24...Qd4+ all land White back around -5.0, 24...exf6 is similar (-4.7), and even the mildest miss of the group, 24...Rd4+, still gives back most of it (-2.7). Reference lines: 24...Qxf6 25.Qxc8+ Kh7 26.Qc3 Qg5 27.Qd2 Qg3 (-5.0); 24...Qd6+ 25.Kc1 Qc7+ 26.Kb1 Kh7 27.fxe7 Bf5+ (-5.0); 24...Qh5+ 25.Kc1 Qc5+ 26.Kb1 exf6 27.Ka2 Rc4 (-5.2); 24...Qd4+ 25.Kc1 Kh7 26.Qxc8 Qe3+ 27.Kb1 Qxb3 (-4.9); 24...exf6 25.Qxc8+ Kg7 26.Qc3 Qg5 27.Qd2 a5 (-4.7); 24...Rd4+ 25.Kc1 Rd8 26.Bc2 exf6 27.Kb1 Kg7 (-2.7) } 25. Qxc8+ { White has to take the bishop with check, since any other move lets ...Rf1+ or ...Qxa1+ decide. } (25. Qf3 Rxf3 26. gxf3 Qxb3+ 27. Ke2 Qb2+ 28. Ke1 Qc3+ 29. Ke2 Bb7 { Offering the queens fails to ...Rxf3 (about -6.7). }) 25... Kh7 { The king steps off the back rank, out of the check. } 26. Qc2 Qxa1+ { You win the rook with check and are happy to decline the queen trade that Qc2 offered. } 27. Qc1 Qxf6 28. Kc2 a5 { You have three pawns for the bishop, and the safe king plus the queenside pawns give you the better chances (about -0.8). } 29. Kb1 a4 { The a-pawn attacks the bishop on b3 and you keep the initiative. }) 25. Qxc8 { [%csl Bc8][%cal Ba8c8] } 25... Qxb2 { WATCH OUT: several other checks and captures look tempting here too, and all of them throw away most of Black's edge: 25...Qxf6, 25...Qd6+, 25...Qh5+ and 25...Qd4+ all let White escape to around -5.0 to -6.6 with accurate defense, 25...exf6 is similar (-7.1), and even the mildest miss of the group, 25...Rd4+, still gives back most of it (-4.6). Reference lines: 25...Qxf6 26.Kc1 b4 27.axb4 Rf2 28.Qc3 Qg5+ (-5.3); 25...Qd6+ 26.Kc2 Qxf6 27.Kb1 a5 28.Qc3 e5 (-5.6); 25...Qh5+ 26.g4 Qg5 27.Bxf7 Rd4+ 28.Kc2 Rd2+ (-6.6); 25...Qd4+ 26.Kc1 Qe3+ 27.Kb1 Qxb3 28.fxe7 Qd3+ (-5.0); 25...Rxf6 26.Qc3 Qg5 27.g4 a5 28.Kc2 Qf4 (-4.7); 25...Rd4+ 26.Kc1 Qe3+ 27.Kb1 Qxb3 28.Qc3 Rd1+ (-4.6); 25...exf6 26.Bxf7 Rd4+ 27.Kc2 Qe4+ 28.Kb3 h5 (-7.1) } { [%csl Bb2][%cal Be5b2] } 26. Qc2 { [%csl Bc2][%cal Bc8c2] } 26... Qxa1+ { WATCH OUT: the other captures and checks here are worse than they look too: 26...Rxf6 and 26...exf6 are both close to lost for Black (-8.7 and -8.8), 26...Rd4+ isn't much better (-6.5), and even the mildest of the bunch, 26...Qxf6, still gives back most of the advantage (-3.5). Reference lines: 26...Rxf6 27.Qxb2 Rf5 28.g4 Rf3 29.Bc2 Rf2 (-8.7); 26...Rd4+ 27.Ke2 Qxc2+ 28.Bxc2 exf6 29.Rac1 Kg8 (-6.5); 26...Qxf6 27.Ra2 a5 28.Kc1 a4 29.Bd5 Qe5 (-3.5); 26...exf6 27.Qxb2 Kg7 28.Qc3 h5 29.Qc7 Rc4 (-8.8) } { [%csl Ba1][%cal Bb2a1] } 27. Qc1 { [%csl Bc1][%cal Bc3c1] } 27... Qxf6 { WATCH OUT: 27...Rxf6 and 27...exf6 both let White consolidate and win (-6.2 and -6.3), and 27...Rd4+ is the mildest miss of the three but still throws back most of the edge (-3.0). Reference lines: 27...Rxf6 28.Qxa1 a5 29.Qe5 Kg7 30.Qxe7 Rf2 (-6.2); 27...Rd4+ 28.Kc2 Qxc1+ 29.Rxc1 exf6 30.Kc3 Rf4 (-3.0); 27...exf6 28.Qxa1 a5 29.Qc3 Kg7 30.Qxa5 Rd4+ (-6.3) } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bf4f1,Ba1f6] } 28. Kc2 { [%csl Bc2][%cal Bd1c2] } 28... Qf5+ $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.9).] [%csl Bf5][%cal Bf6f5] } 29. Kb2 { Your last move was ...Qf5+, so White is in check, and Kb2 is the move that keeps everything protected. } (29. Kd1 Qd3+ 30. Qd2 Qxb3+ 31. Ke2 Rf6 32. Re1 a6 33. Qd3 Qa2+ { Kd1 loses the bishop on b3 to ...Qd3+ with a fork (about -6.9). }) (29. Kc3 Qe5+ 30. Kc2 Qe4+ 31. Kb2 Qxg2+ 32. Ka1 Rf2 33. Re1 e6 { Kc3 walks into a series of checks that win the g2 pawn (about -1.9). }) 29... Rf2+ 30. Bc2 Qe4 { The bishop is pinned along the second rank, and the rook and queen now both hit g2. } 31. Rf1 Qxg2 32. Rxf2 Qxf2 33. Qd1 Qc5 { You have won the g2 pawn and in the queen ending you have four pawns for the bishop, with the kingside majority as the long-term trump (about -0.7). }) 3... g6 { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gg7g6] } 4. Ngf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 4... Bg7 { [%csl Gg7][%cal Gf8g7] } 5. c3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 5... dxe4 { dxe4 leads to a small White edge (+0.4): Black has a material edge of about 1 point(s); Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } (5... c5 $14 { c5 is recommended by the engine. Not a move most humans would play. Kochyev, Alexander - Titov, German, 1-0, URS-ch sf, 1985 } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 6. dxc5 $14 { White grabs the pawn. Your compensation is development and the fact that the c5 pawn is isolated and hard to keep. } (6. Qb3 cxd4 7. Nxd4 a6 8. exd5 Nf6 9. g3 Qxd5 10. Qxd5 Nxd5 { Qb3 hits b7 and d5 together, but taking on d4 solves it and the queens come off (about +0.5). }) 6... Nf6 { You hit e4 and develop with tempo. } 7. exd5 Qxd5 8. Nb3 Qxd1+ 9. Kxd1 O-O { White's king has lost castling rights and the c5 pawn is weak. } 10. Bc4 Nc6 { You are ready for ...Ne4 and ...Nd7 to win the pawn back, and White keeps a small edge (about +0.8). }) 6. Nxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd2e4] } 6... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 7. Bd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 7... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 8. Nxf6+ { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge4f6] } 8... exf6 $14 { Black's doubled f-pawns aren't a real weakness here: the g6 pawn keeps them connected, and the follow-up ...f5 gives Black genuine central and kingside space rather than a target. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge7f6] } 9. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 9... Re8 $14 { Controls the e-file. } { [%csl Ge8][%cal Gf8e8] } 10. Bf4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gc1f4] } 10... Be6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Gc8e6] } 11. Re1 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gf1e1] } 11... Nd7 $14 { Black is planning to tuck the knight to f8! For safe measures. } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 12. a4 { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga2a4] } 12... Nf8 { [%csl Gf8][%cal Gd7f8] } 13. Bf1 { [%csl Gf1][%cal Gd3f1] } 13... Qb6 $14 { White tries to win that pawn. } { [%csl Gb6,Gb2][%cal Gd8b6,Gb6b2] } 14. b4 $10 { White has to hold this pawn rather than let it go for activity: trying to generate counterplay by invading the 7th rank instead just runs into ...Qxc3, and Black's kingside is solid enough that White's own attack never gets started. } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gb2b4] } 14... Bd5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge6d5] } 15. Be3 $10 { The bishop goes behind the d4 pawn, which your queen on b6 is hitting, and White is ready for Nd2 and g3. } (15. a5 Qd8 16. Rxe8 Qxe8 17. Be3 f5 18. Ne5 Rd8 { Kicking the queen with a5 gains only a tempo: it retreats, the rooks come off and you play the same ...f5 plan. }) 15... f5 { You push the f-pawn to kick the knight on f3 and open the kingside for ...g5, though it also weakens e6. } 16. Nd2 Qd8 17. g3 g5 18. Qh5 h6 19. h4 gxh4 { Both kings are loosened. Your pawn advance gains space, but White's queen is active on h5, so the position stays level (about +0.3). } *


