1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The main Advance Variation structure: White's pawn chain reaches d4-e5, locking the center French-style, but, exactly as the Course Map's Structure Two explains, Black's bishop already got out to f5/g6/h4 before the chain closed, which is the whole structural justification for 1...c6 over 1...e6. In the Short Variation specifically, Black often gets in ...f6 to challenge e5 directly, as reflected in the half-open f-file at the target position. The practical plan: use that f-file pressure and the ...c5 break (hitting d4) as the two main levers, keep the good bishop active rather than letting it get shut in again, and remember that trading off White's e5-pawn (rather than just nibbling at it) is usually what actually equalizes the position.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives -0.1 to +0.3, roughly equal. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Nxe5? on moves 7 to 12 loses material (about 4.0); ...Nxd4? on moves 9 to 10 loses material (about 3.0); 14...Rf7? (about 2.0; prefer ...Bxf6); 11...g5? (about 1.4; prefer ...Bxh4); 10...Nh6? (about 0.9; prefer ...Nh4); 5...g6? (about 0.9; prefer ...Bxd3).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 13.Qxb7? Qxa4 (costs White about 3.3); 11.Na3? axb4 (costs White about 2.6); 11.Ba3? Ndxe5 (costs White about 1.2).
Chapter #16 continues the Advance Variation. Other Advance Variation chapters: #17, #18, #19, #20, #21. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #6 and #8. Model games: #4, #9. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 $14 { The Advance Variation is one of the most challenging tries against the Caro-Kann. White gains space and has kingside attacking chances, much as in the French Defence. The principled, classical way to meet it is recommended here: Black makes full use of the bishop on f5 and gets strong queenside counterplay. In the French that same bishop is often the problem piece, and Black may even have to double the b-pawns just to trade it for a White bishop or knight; in the Caro-Kann Black avoids that fate because the bishop is developed before ...e6. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 3... Bf5 $14 { Thus, this is the classical way to play this, the ECK will be met with the principled and classical Bf5! In that case black solves all his problem on the LSB. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 4. Nf3 $14 { Nf3 is the most natural reply: White will castle kingside and develop the bishop pair. Sidelines such as c4?! are treated in this chapter as well. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } (4. Bd3 { 4.Bd3 offers the immediate trade of light-squared bishops before developing anything else, a distinct system from the Nf3-first approach the chapter otherwise builds around. } 4... Bxd3 5. Qxd3 e6 6. Nf3 { ...reaching a simplified, very level position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), a genuine independent fourth-move system alongside Nf3, Nc3, Nd2, h4 and g4.] } 6... c5 $10 { With the light-squared bishops traded, you strike at d4 at once: the e5 pawn depends on it, and your pawn chain on d5 and e6 stays solid. } (6... Qa5+ 7. Nbd2 Qa6 8. c4 Bb4 9. O-O Ne7 10. Qc2 Bxd2 11. Nxd2 { Checking first and offering the queens is fine but a little less precise (about +0.3). }) 7. Be3 (7. c3 { White supports the d4 pawn with a pawn so that the c5 break does not undermine the centre, and it frees c2 for the queen or a knight. It is a natural, solid move that is common in practice (played in about 872 thousand games). For you it means the tension on d4 is going to be resolved soon, so choose your piece placement with that in mind. } 7... Nd7 $10 { The knight heads for the best square at once: from d7 it adds another attacker to d4, keeps e5 under watch and leaves c6 free for your other knight, which will come via e7. You plan ...cxd4 followed by ...Bb4+ to force White to use a knight on d2, then ...Ne7 and ...Ba5 to keep the bishop active on the a5-e1 diagonal. Aim for the knight to land on c6 or f5 and for the ...f6 break to challenge e5. } 8. Qe2 cxd4 9. cxd4 Bb4+ { The check is useful because it drags the b1 knight to d2, where it blocks the bishop on c1 and leaves the d4 pawn short of support. } 10. Nbd2 Ne7 { Develop, and ...Nb6 or ...Nc6 follow. Avoid taking on d4 with the knight too early; the pawn is poisoned only when everything else is ready. } 11. a3 Ba5 12. O-O { Overall: White has more space, but you have a healthy position and targets on d4; ...Nc6 and ...f6 are your breaks. This is a closed Advance structure with White's pawn chain on d4 and e5 and yours on c5, d5 and e6. White has more space, but d4 is your target. Your plan: finish development with ...Nc6 and ...Ne7, castle short, then keep hitting d4 with ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 and prepare the break ...f6 to open the position. White wants to hold d4 with c3 and Nbd2 and to keep the centre closed while his space tells. You meet that by trading pieces and attacking the base of his chain. The risk is playing ...cxd4 too early, which frees his game and gives him the c-file. If White plays Bb5 or Qe2, just castle and keep your knights coordinated. }) 7... cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nc6 9. f4 Nxd4 10. Bxd4 Ne7 { White keeps the centre with f4 and you trade off a pair of knights. } 11. Nd2 { The position is level (about +0.1). Your plan is ...Nc6 or ...Qb6, hitting the bishop on d4 and the pawn on b2. }) (4. Ne2 { 4.Ne2 develops flexibly, keeping the option of either Ng3 or Nf4 depending on Black's setup. } 4... e6 5. Ng3 Bg6 { ...and Black equalizes comfortably with normal development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a quiet, sound try that sidesteps all the sharper fourth-move theory.] } 6. c3 $10 { Quiet development: c3 supports the d4 pawn so that ...c5 can be met calmly. } (6. h4 h5 7. c4 c5 8. Bg5 Be7 9. cxd5 exd5 10. Bb5+ Nc6 { h4 threatens h5 to chase the bishop, so you stop it with ...h5 (about -0.1). }) 6... c5 { You strike at the centre immediately, before White can consolidate with Bd3. } 7. Bb5+ Nc6 8. O-O Rc8 { Your rook comes to the half-open c-file against the d4 and c3 pawns. } 9. Be3 a6 10. Bxc6+ Rxc6 { White has traded off a bishop for a knight, so you hold the bishop pair and a solid structure: equal (about 0.0). }) (4. c3 { 4.c3 supports the center quietly before committing the king's knight anywhere, keeping options for both Nf3 and f4 later. } 4... e6 5. Be3 (5. Bd3 { White develops the bishop to challenge your light-squared bishop on f5. If you trade, the advanced pawn on e5 loses its protector, but your structure remains sound. It is a very common move (played in about 776 thousand games). } 5... Ne7 $10 { You develop the knight to e7 so that, after the bishops are exchanged, the knight can reach f5 and pressure the d4 pawn. The plan is ...Bxd3, then ...h6 to deny the knight on g5, ...Nf5 and finally ...c5 to hit the d4 pawn. Keep the knight flexible and castle short when ready. Avoid retreating with ...Bg6 without a plan, since White can then play h4 with attacking ideas. The position is level. } (5... Bxd3 { Trading at once is also fine. After Qxd3 you aim for ...c5 and ...Nc6 with a free game, then ...h6 and ...a6 as useful waiting moves. } 6. Qxd3 c5 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. O-O h6 9. h3 a6 10. a3) 6. Nf3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 h6 { Stops any Ng5 or Bg5 tricks and prepares ...Nf5. } 8. O-O Nf5 9. c4 dxc4 10. Qxc4 { Overall: your knight sits happily on f5, you hold the e4 and d5 squares and your structure is sound, so the game is equal with plenty of play for you. This is a closed French-style position where White has more space with pawns on e5 and d4, but your knight on f5 attacks d4 and you hold the e4 and d5 squares. Your plan: bring the other knight to d7 to hit e5, play ...Be7 and castle short, then challenge d4 with ...c5. White wants Nc3 and Ne4 to reach d6, or Qb3 to hit your pawn on b7. If Qb3 comes, defend with ...Qb6 and keep your pieces coordinated. The mistake is leaving b7 hanging while you develop. If White plays Bd2, simply continue ...Nd7. }) 5... Qb6 6. Qb3 { ...reaching a level, flexible position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a sound, calm fourth-move try alongside the chapter's sharper main lines.] } 6... Nd7 $10 { The knight protects your queen on b6, so a queen trade does not damage your pawn structure. } (6... Be7 7. Be2 h5 8. Nf3 Nd7 9. O-O Nh6 10. h3 a5 11. c4 { Another safe setup with the same balance (about 0.0). }) 7. Nd2 h5 { The h-pawn stops g2-g4 from harassing the bishop on f5. } 8. Be2 h4 { Now Ng3 is no longer possible either, so the bishop keeps its diagonal. } 9. Ngf3 Ne7 10. O-O Ng6 11. Ne1 { The h4 pawn has fixed White's kingside and your knight reaches g6, pressuring e5. The position is level (about 0.0), and ...Rc8 with ...c5 is your way to challenge d4. }) (4. Be3 { 4.Be3 develops toward c5/d4 immediately, preparing a later Nd2-Nf3 setup without blocking the c-pawn. } 4... Qb6 5. Qc1 e6 6. c4 { ...and the position stays balanced. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), a sound fourth-move alternative.] } 6... Nd7 $10 { You add a defender for the ...f6 break: the pawn on e5 is the base of White's space, so attack it and the advantage becomes a liability. } (6... dxc4 7. Nd2 Qd8 8. Nxc4 b5 9. Nd2 Ne7 10. Ne2 Nd5 11. Ng3 { Grabbing the pawn is possible (about +0.2) but costs time and White's pieces gain ground. }) 7. Nf3 f6 8. exf6 Ngxf6 9. c5 Qd8 { The queen must leave b6, but your pieces develop smoothly and White's c5 pawn has given up the d5 pressure. } 10. Be2 Be7 11. Nc3 { Black has a comfortable game with the d5 pawn intact (about 0.0). }) 4... e6 $14 { Black has locked in the position with a very solid pawn chain, is comfortable defensively and positionally, and can look for activity in the middlegame. } { [%csl Ge6,Rd3,Rc4,Ra3,Re2,Rc3][%cal Ge7e6,Rf1d3,Rc2c4,Ra2a3,Rf1e2,Rc2c3] } 5. Bd3 $10 { White swaps of LSB right away resulting in a less complication position. Black gets to fight back towards the center right away with the move c5 and then castle kingside. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } (5. c4 $10 { This was the move Magnus Carlsen played against Alireza Firouzja at the 2021 World Rapid and Blitz Championship; Firouzja went on to lose after errors in the middlegame. It hits Black's centre immediately, but the position is defensible and less worrying than other move orders. Caro-Kann players should be ready for such surprises. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 5... Bb4+ $10 { ...Bb4 is recommended, since it lets Black castle quickly. } { [%csl Bb4,Bd2,Bc3][%cal Bf8b4,Bc1d2,Bb1c3] } 6. Bd2 { Bd2 leads to roughly equal (+0.0), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } (6. Nc3 $10 { Another line for white. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 6... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 7. Bd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } 7... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 8. a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba2a3] } 8... Bxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb4c3] } 9. Bxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bd2c3] } 9... O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. Nh4 $10 { White has the bishop pair and wants to remove your light-squared bishop, so Nh4 attacks the bishop on f5. } (10. cxd5 Nxd5 11. Bd3 Bxd3 12. Qxd3 h6 13. O-O c5 14. Rac1 Rc8 { A quieter way: your knight lands on d5 and the ...c5 break comes with ease (about -0.3). }) 10... c5 { You strike at the centre while the white knight is offside on h4. } 11. dxc5 Nxc5 12. Nxf5 Nxf5 13. cxd5 exd5 14. Rc1 Rc8 { White keeps the bishop pair, but your knights are active, your rook takes the c-file and the position is level (about -0.2). }) 6... Bxd2+ { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb4d2] } 7. Nbxd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 7... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 8. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 8... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 9. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 9... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. Re1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 10... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6,Bf5h7] } 11. Qb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd1b3] } 11... Qb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 12. c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc4c5] } 12... Qc7 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] [%csl Bc7][%cal Bb6c7] } 13. Qc3 $10 { White prepares b4 and a3 to build a queenside clamp with the c5 pawn, and the queen eyes a5. } (13. a3 b6 14. Qc3 bxc5 15. dxc5 a5 16. b4 Rfb8 17. Bf1 Ng6 { If White is slow with Qc3, ...b6 comes at once and hits c5 (about -0.1). }) 13... a5 { You answer with the standard undermining move: ...a5 and ...b6 attack the c5 chain from both sides. } 14. a3 b6 15. b4 Rfb8 { The rook goes behind the b-pawn lever. } 16. Bd3 Bxd3 17. Qxd3 axb4 { You open the a-file for the rook and the position is balanced (about +0.1). }) (5. a3 $10 { Perhaps this move is a bit unorthodox but white probably wants to play c4. } { [%csl Ba3,Bc4][%cal Ba2a3,Bc2c4] } 5... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 6. c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 6... dxc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd5c4] } 7. Bxc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 7... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 8. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... Nd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be7d5] } 9. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 9... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 10. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bc4d3] } 10... Bxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf5d3] } 11. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd1d3] } 11... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 12. a4 $14 { White stops ...b5 and prepares a rook lift with Re1-e4-g4 aimed at g7. } (12. Qe4 Nxc3 13. bxc3 c5 14. d5 exd5 15. Qxd5 Qc8 16. Bg5 Nb6 { Qe4 hits h7 and eyes the centre, but ...Nxc3 and the ...c5 break equalise (about +0.3). }) 12... a5 { You fix the pawn on a4 and keep b4 free for your pieces. } 13. Re1 h6 { A luft move: the king is no longer short of air and Re4 will not come with tempo. } 14. Re4 Nxc3 15. Qxc3 Kh8 16. Rg4 Rg8 { You step off the g-file, and the rook on g8 covers g7. White keeps a small edge (about +0.3) but there is no direct threat. }) (5. Be2 $14 { White does have to option to play Be2 to keep the tension on the center and make the position less complicated since an exchange would help simplify the position. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 5... c5 $14 { From here ...c5 is needed to challenge the centre. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc7c5] } 6. O-O { O-O leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } (6. Be3 $10 { Be3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 6... Qb6 7. Nc3 $10 { This is independent, fresh engine analysis (depth 28) of one of the most heavily-debated modern tabiyas in the whole Advance Variation - the sharp 5.Be2 c5 6.Be3 Qb6 line, where Black grabs the b2 pawn and White gets real piece activity in return. This is a different branch than this course's main recommendation (which meets 4.Nf3 e6 with the calmer 5.Bd3, trading light-squared bishops immediately) - it's included here purely as supplementary reference, so you understand the objective status of the sharper try too, in case you ever want it or face it from the other side of the board. At depth 28, White's most testing try is the calm developing move shown here rather than grabbing immediate central space - engaging Black's queenside pawn grab head-on with active piece play rather than rushing. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } (7. O-O $15 { Also possible: O-O (+7), a close alternative the engine also approves of at real depth. } { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 7... Qxb2 { [%csl Bb2][%cal Bb6b2] } 8. c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 8... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 9. Nbd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 9... Nbc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 10. Nb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd2b3] } 10... dxc4 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the c-file.] [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd5c4] } 11. Bxc4 $10 { White wins the pawn back and opens the position against your king, while your queen on b2 is away from the action. You should castle long and centralise the knight on d5, which stops White from using c3 and e3. This is the same structure as in the 7.c4 line. (about 0.0) } (11. Nxc5 b5 12. Nh4 Bc2 13. Qc1 c3 14. Bxb5 Qxb5 15. Qxc2 Nd5 $17 { Grabbing the pawn on c5 gives you a strong initiative (about -1.2). }) 11... O-O-O 12. Nxc5 Nd5 13. Bg5 Bxc5 14. Bxd8 Rxd8 15. dxc5 Ne3 $10 { The knight fork on the queen and rook balances the line. }) (7. c4 $10 { Also possible: c4 (+1), a close alternative the engine also approves of at real depth. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 7... Qxb2 { [%csl Bb2][%cal Bb6b2] } 8. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 9. Nbd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 9... Nbc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 10. Nb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd2b3] } 10... dxc4 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the c-file.] [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd5c4] } 11. Bxc4 $10 { White regains the pawn and opens lines against your king while the queen is out of play on b2. You castle long, and the knight lands on d5 to cover c3 and e3. The sequence is sharp but level. (about 0.0) } (11. Bc1 Qc2 12. Qxc2 Bxc2 13. Bxc4 Bxb3 14. axb3 cxd4 15. Bb2 a6 $17 { Trying to trap the queen backfires and leaves you clearly better (about -1.1). }) 11... O-O-O 12. Nxc5 Nd5 13. Bg5 Bxc5 14. Bxd8 Rxd8 15. dxc5 Ne3 $10 { The final knight fork on the queen and rook keeps things level. }) (7. Nbd2 { Nbd2 - routes the knight to a flexible square instead of the main lines with c4 or castling, keeping the option of Nb3 or Nb1-c3 open depending on how Black continues. } 7... Nc6 { ...Nc6 (Solid & Optimal) - simple, natural development that keeps pressure on d4 without taking any risks; the safest route to a normal, comfortable middlegame. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), the easiest of the two to play well.] } (7... Qxb2 { ...Qxb2 (Complicated & Imbalanced) - grabs the offered pawn at once, accepting a sharper position where White gets some initiative for the material but nothing immediately decisive. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), objectively fine but requires more careful, concrete calculation than simply developing.] } 8. c4 $10 { White opens the centre while your queen is far from home. The knight will go to b3 to hit c5, and ...Qxb2 gets hunted by Rb1. Your solution is to develop quickly with ...Ne7, ...Nbc6 and ...O-O-O, so the queen has support. (about 0.0) } (8. Rb1 Qxc2 9. Qxc2 Bxc2 10. Rb2 Ba4 11. dxc5 Nd7 12. Rxb7 Bxc5 $15 { Chasing the queen allows you to trade queens and stay a little better (about -0.3). }) 8... Ne7 9. O-O Nbc6 10. Nb3 O-O-O 11. Nxc5 dxc4 12. Bxc4 Nd5 $10 { Both sides have targets and the position is level, but it needs careful play. }) 8. c3 $10 { White lets you grab at b2, with the idea of dxc5 and Rc1 so that your queen runs out of safe squares near a3. In return your pieces on the kingside are undeveloped. You can take two pawns, but you must give one back. (about +0.1) } (8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. Bxc5 Qxc5 10. c3 Nge7 11. O-O Qb6 12. Qb3 Qc7 $15 { This simple line gives you a small edge (about -0.4). }) 8... Qxb2 9. dxc5 Qxc3 10. Rc1 Qa3 11. Nb3 Nge7 12. Nfd4 a6 $10 { Your queen is tied up, yet White has no immediate win. }) 7... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 8. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... Qxb2 { [%csl Bb2][%cal Bb6b2] } 9. Qe1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bd1e1] } 9... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 10. Bxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be3d4] } 10... Nxd4 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc6d4] } 11. Nxd4 $14 { Recapturing is forced. Now your queen on b2 is the target: White threatens Rb1 and a knight attack. The cunning point is ...Bb4, pinning the knight against the queen on e1, so that after ...Bxc3 the queens come off and you keep the bishop pair. (about +0.6) } (11. Qb1 Nxe2+ $19 { Any attempt to ignore the knight on d4 loses the bishop on e2 with check (about -5.2). }) 11... Bb4 12. Rb1 Bxc3 13. Rxb2 Bxe1 14. Rxe1 b6 15. Bb5+ Kf8 $14 { You have the bishop pair but a displaced king, and White gets a slight edge from the lead in development. }) 6... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 7. Be3 { Be3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } (7. dxc5 $14 { White takes the pawn, this isn't really a pawn sacrifice since white usually do not keep the pawn anyway. Any move orders that tries to saves the pawn would end badly for white. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd4c5] } 7... Nec6 { [%csl Bc6,Be5,Bc5][%cal Be7c6,Bc6e5,Bf8c5] } 8. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 8... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7,Be5,Bc5][%cal Bb8d7,Bd7e5,Bc6e5,Bf8c5] } 9. c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 9... dxc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd5c4] } 10. Nbd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 10... c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc4c3] } 11. bxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb2c3] } 11... Bxc5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 12. Nd4 $14 { This is the same idea as in the 7.dxc5 move order: White hits c6 so that ...O-O Nxc6 bxc6 wrecks your structure. Bxc5 and Nc4 then pick on the c-pawns, while ...Qd5 and ...Bd3 are your counterplay against the bishop on e2. (about +0.3) } (12. Nc4 O-O 13. Nd4 Be4 14. Nd6 Nxd4 15. Bxd4 Bc6 16. Qd3 Bxd4 $10 { Putting the knight on d6 leads to a quick equalisation (about +0.2). }) 12... 