hat after ...O-O Nxc6 bxc6 your pawns are broken. Then Bxc5 and Nc4 attack the weak c-pawns. You answer with ...Qd5 and ...Bd3, using your active bishop to hit the e2 bishop. (about +0.3) } (12. Bxc5 Nxc5 13. Nc4 Qxd1 14. Rfxd1 Be4 15. Nd4 Ke7 16. f3 Bd5 $10 { If White trades dark bishops at once, you can steer into a level endgame (about 0.0). }) 12... O-O 13. Nxc6 bxc6 14. Bxc5 Nxc5 15. Nc4 Qd5 16. Qd4 Bd3 $14 { White keeps a slight edge. }) (5. c3 $14 { Other players of this line may play c3 to avoid theory, and White may well succeed in that. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 5... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 6. Bd3 $10 { Other move orders, such as Be2, are met by the recommended set-up: ...Nd7, ...Ne7, then ...c5. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 6... Bxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf5d3] } 7. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd1d3] } 7... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 8. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 9. dxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd4c5] } 9... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6,Be5,Bc5][%cal Be7c6,Bc6e5,Bd7e5,Bf8c5] } 10. b4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb2b4] } 10... a5 { [%csl Ba5,Ba3][%cal Ba7a5,Bb1a3,Bc1a3] } 11. Qb5 $14 { A threat to seize the b pawn. } { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bb5b7] } (11. Na3 $17 { Na3 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.8): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns.    (the engine edge for Black coexists with White's extra material here because this is a known pawn-grab-for-compensation pattern in this line, not a tactical oversight - Black has concrete compensation: faster development, open lines, or attacking chances that outweigh the missing pawn(s)) } { [%csl Ba3][%cal Bb1a3] } 11... axb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Ba5b4] } 12. cxb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bc3b4] } 12... Nxb4 $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.7).] [%csl Bb4][%cal Bc6b4] } 13. Qd4 $17 { The knight on b4 hits both Qd3 and a2, so the queen has to move. Qd4 attacks the knight in return, because your bishop on f8 cannot protect b4 while the pawn on c5 blocks the diagonal. } (13. Qb3 Bxc5 14. Bd2 Nc6 15. Qxb7 Ncxe5 16. Nxe5 Nxe5 17. Qb5+ Nd7 { Qb3 attacks b4 too, but ...Bxc5 protects the knight and wins the c5 pawn (about -2.1). }) 13... Nc6 { The knight escapes with a gain of time, since it now hits the queen on d4. } 14. Qg4 Nxc5 { The knight from d7 takes the pawn on c5, and White cannot keep it. } 15. Nb5 h5 16. Qh3 Be7 17. Be3 Ra4 { You gain space with h5, the rook swings out, and White's pieces are loose; you are clearly better (about -1.5). }) (11. Ba3 $15 { White tries to save one of the pawns but would fail eventually. } { [%csl Ba3][%cal Bc1a3] } 11... Ndxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd7e5] } 12. Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 12... Nxe5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the c-file.] [%csl Be5][%cal Bc6e5] } 13. Qg3 $15 { The queen must leave the knight's attack on d3, and from g3 it hits both e5 and g7. } (13. Qe2 Nc4 14. Bc1 axb4 15. Nd2 Nxd2 16. Bxd2 Be7 17. cxb4 Bf6 { Qe2 lets the knight jump to c4 and you pick up a pawn with ...axb4 (about -0.7). }) 13... Ng6 { The knight guards your kingside and heads for f4. } 14. Rd1 axb4 15. Bxb4 Be7 { You trade the bishop. } 16. Nd2 O-O 17. Nc4 Ra6 { Your pawns on d5 and e6 are passed, because White has no d- or e-pawns, and White's doubled c-pawns are weak. That is why you are slightly better (about -0.5). }) 11... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 12. a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba2a3] } 12... axb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Ba5b4] } 13. cxb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bc3b4] } 13... b6 $14 { Effectively breaking up white's over-extended pawns. } { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 14. cxb6 $14 { White has to take, because ...b6 attacks the c5 pawn. If White does nothing, for example with Bf4, ...bxc5 wrecks the pawn chain and you are better (about -0.6). } (14. Bf4 bxc5 15. Nc3 Qc8 16. Rfe1 cxb4 17. axb4 Rb8 18. Qe2 Bxb4 { The loose pawns on c5 and b4 fall and White's structure collapses. }) 14... Qxb6 15. Qxb6 Nxb6 { The queens come off and your knight is active on b6 against White's loosened queenside. } 16. Be3 Nc4 17. b5 N6a5 18. a4 Bb4 { Your knights are well placed and the bishop has targets, but White's pieces are fine too (about +0.5). }) (5. Nc3 { White develops the knight to pressure d5 and prepares Bd3 or Be2 without losing control of e5. It is very common in practice. White does not mind trading bishops on d3 if the pawn recapture is useful. For you the question is where to put your knights while your light-squared bishop is already outside the chain. } 5... Nd7 $10 { Prepare ...c5 and support e5. Plan: ...Ne7 and ...Nf5, then ...c5 to hit d4. Keep the center pawns on e6 and d5 safe, and do not hurry to castle before you see what White does with the dark-squared bishop. If White plays Bf4 you answer with ...Ne7 and ...Nf5 anyway. } (5... Ne7 { Also fine, and it keeps ...Nf5 in mind. } 6. Be2 Bg4 7. O-O Nf5) 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. cxd3 Ne7 { Trade the bishop; White's pawn on d3 is a slightly worse structure for White. } 8. Ne2 Nf5 9. O-O h5 { This is a useful lever: you will play ...h4 or ...Be7 and aim for ...c5. Avoid playing ...g5 too early without a knight on f5. Overall: equal and comfortable. This is a closed French-style position where your light-squared bishop has been traded for White's d3 bishop, so White has doubled pawns on d3 and d4, and you have good squares on f5 and g6. Your plan: complete development with ...Be7, then hit d4 with ...c5 and, with the knight on f5, use ...h4 to cramp the kingside. White wants Ng3 to challenge your knight, then Nxf5 and exf5, or Qd2 with Bg5. Meet Ng3 calmly and recapture with the e-pawn. The mistake is ...g5 early, because it weakens the light squares without support. If White plays Ng3, retreat only if needed. }) 5... Bxd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf5d3] } 6. Qxd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Rb7d5,Rf7d5,Gd1d3] } 6... Nd7 $14 { ...Nd7 is useful in case White attacks on the kingside: from d7 the knight can go to f8 and add another defender of h7, forming a fortress. } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 7. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 7... Ne7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gg8e7] } 8. Nc3 { Nc3 leads to roughly equal (-0.0): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Gc3,Rf5,Ra6][%cal Gb1c3,Re7f5,Ra7a6] } (8. c4 $14 { Engine-suggested line (Stockfish 16, depth 20), not in the original chapter. This is White's strongest try here (about +0.34); the chapter's own Nc3 scores about +0.03. Review before relying on it. } 8... dxc4 9. Qxc4 Nb6 10. Qc2 Nf5 11. Rd1 Nh4 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] } 12. Nxh4 $14 { White removes the knight that was eyeing f3 and g2, and after ...Qxh4 your queen is exposed to Nc3-e4 and g3 tempo moves. Your rook goes to d8 to hit the d4 pawn, and the knight on d5 later blocks the centre. (about +0.4) } (12. Qe4 Nxf3+ 13. Qxf3 f5 14. exf6 Qxf6 15. Qh5+ Qf7 16. Qe2 O-O-O $10 { If White keeps the queens on, the ...f5 break undermines e5 and you are fine (about +0.2). }) 12... Qxh4 13. Nc3 Rd8 14. Ne4 Be7 15. Qe2 Nd5 16. h3 h6 $14 { You have a comfortable position with a good knight on d5, but White keeps the extra space. }) 8... Nf5 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores +0.53, while c5 scores -0.01, so it costs about 0.5. It is playable, but it is a concession] [%csl Gf5][%cal Ge7f5] } (8... a6 $14 { Another plan is to focus on the queenside rather than the kingside and find counterplay there. Note that ...c5 is not possible because of the Nb5 threat, after which a knight outpost on d6 would cause great problems for Black. } { [%csl Bb5,Bc3,Ba6][%cal Ba6b5,Bc3b5,Ba7a6] } 9. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 9... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc7c5] } 10. dxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd4c5] } 10... Nc6 $14 { Black pressures the c-pawn, and White cannot hold on to it much longer. } { [%csl Bc6,Be5,Bc5][%cal Be7c6,Bc6e5,Bd7c5] } 11. Na4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Bc3a4] } 11... Qa5 $14 { Black adds another attacker on the c-pawn, which will soon fall. } { [%csl Bc5,Ba5][%cal Bd7c5,Bf8c5,Ba5c5,Bd8a5] } 12. Qb3 { [%csl Bb3,Bb7][%cal Bd3b3,Bb3b7] } 12... Rd8 { [%csl Bd8][%cal Ba8d8] } 13. c3 { c3 leads to a small White edge (+0.4): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } (13. Qxb7?? $19 { Blunders the knight on the rim. } { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bb3b7] } 13... Qxa4 $19 { [Engine verdict: winning for Black (-3.0). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the c-file.] [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba5a4] } 14. Qb3 $19 { White is a knight down after ...Qxa4, and the queen on b7 can be harassed by ...Rb8. Qb3 brings it back and challenges yours. } (14. c4 dxc4 15. b4 Nxb4 16. Rac1 Nc6 17. Nd2 Ndxe5 18. Nxc4 Nxc4 { Opening lines with c4 and b4 only helps your pieces (about -3.6). }) 14... Qb5 { You offer the queens yourself: simplification is your friend with an extra piece. } 15. Qxb5 axb5 16. b4 Nxb4 { Taking the pawn is safe, since the knight returns to c6 after Rab1. } 17. Rab1 Nc6 18. Rxb5 Be7 { You are a knight up for two pawns, and the position is simple to handle (about -3.4). }) 13... Ndxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd7e5] } 14. Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 14... Nxe5 $14 { Black is doing fine here. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bc6e5] } 15. Qd1 $14 { The queen leaves b3 because your Ne5 was about to jump to c4 or d3 with tempo, and Qd1 still protects the knight on a4. } 15... Qb5 { Your queen hits the knight on a4, the c5 pawn and b2. } 16. Bd4 Nc6 17. Re1 Nxd4 18. cxd4 Be7 { Bd4 protects c5 and hits the knight, so you trade it off, leaving White with c5 and d4 pawns that need constant protection. } 19. a3 Bf6 { Your bishop pressures d4, and White's advanced c5 pawn is passive. White is slightly better (about +0.7) but you are doing fine. }) 9. Bf4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gc1f4] } 9... Be7 { [%csl Ge7,Rg4,Ra4,Re1][%cal Gf8e7,Rg2g4,Ra2a4,Rf1e1] } 10. g4 $10 { White is playing with fire: the kingside is at risk, but White can play Kh1 and stack rooks on the g-file to launch an attack. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gg2g4] } (10. a4 $10 { If White does not play g4, Black can castle, play ...c5 and then storm the queenside with ...a7-a5. } { [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba7a4,Bd8b6,Ba8c8,Bc6c5,Be8g8,Ba2a4] } 10... g5 $10 { ...g5 is an option for those who prefer a more dangerous line; it leads to the same thing after Rad1, the line covered in this course. } { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bg7g5] } (10... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5).] } 11. Ne2 $14 { White prepares g4 or Ng3 to challenge the knight on f5 and clears c3 for the c-pawn. } (11. Bc1 f6 12. g4 fxe5 13. gxf5 e4 14. Nxe4 dxe4 15. Qxe4 exf5 { If White retreats the bishop and plays g4, the game turns into a tactical melee that is roughly equal (about +0.2). }) 11... c5 { You start your queenside play and hit the d4 pawn, as you planned. } 12. c3 Rc8 13. Bd2 Nb6 14. g4 Nh4 15. Nxh4 Bxh4 { White's g4 kicks your knight, but you trade it off and keep a healthy position (about +0.4). The bishop on h4 takes aim at the king's rook. }) 11. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bf4e3] } 11... h5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 12. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bc3e2] } 12... g4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bg5g4] } 13. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bf3d2] } 13... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 14. c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 14... dxc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd5c4] } 15. Nxc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd2c4] } 15... O-O-O { [%csl Bc8,Bb4,Bd1][%cal Be8c8,Bb2b4,Bf1d1] } 16. b4 { b4 leads to roughly equal (-0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb2b4] } (16. Rfd1 $15 { Rfd1 leads to a small Black edge (-0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bf1d1] } 16... h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh5h4] } 17. b4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb2b4] } 17... h3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh4h3] } 18. g3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 18... Bxb4 $10 { Although position is quite messy and a bit dangerous, black has pieces to reinforce a fortress on black's kingside. } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Be7b4] } 19. Bg5 $15 { White hits your rook on d8 and wants to trade the active bishop on b4. } (19. Rab1 Nxe3 20. fxe3 a5 21. Nf4 Kb8 22. Qe2 Nb6 23. Nxb6 Qxb6 { Rab1 allows ...Nxe3, and fxe3 weakens White's king (about -0.6). }) 19... Rdg8 { The rook attacks the bishop on g5, which must retreat, and keeps the g-file under watch. } 20. Bd2 Bxd2 21. Rxd2 Rd8 { Back to the d-file to pressure d4, with the king still safe on c8. } 22. Rb1 Nb6 23. Ne3 Nxe3 { Your chain on g4 and h3 cramps White's king, and every trade makes your fortress easier to hold (about -0.4). }) 16... Nxe5 $10 { An interesting trap. } { [%csl Bd3,Be5][%cal Bd7e5,Bd8d3,Bc4e5] } 17. Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bc4e5] } 17... Qxe5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.2).] [%csl Be5,Bd3][%cal Bc7e5,Bd8d3] } 18. Rac1 { White puts the rook on the c-file against your king on c8, while the knight on e2 covers d4. } (18. dxe5 Rxd3 { Taking the queen looks free, but the d-pawn is pinned to the queen on d3 by your rook on d8, so ...Rxd3 comes first and the queens just trade. }) 18... Qd5 { Your queen steps out of the d-pawn's attack and offers a queen trade. } 19. b5 c5 { You hit d4 and stop White's pawn storm from opening the file. } 20. Qc2 Kb8 21. dxc5 Nxe3 22. fxe3 Qe5 { The balance is level, with White's pawns on e3 split (about -0.1). }) (10. Rfe1 $10 { Other quiet moves are possible, such as ...g5, when Black attacks the white kingside with a pawn storm. } { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 10... g5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bg7g5] } 11. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bf4e3] } 11... g4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bg5g4] } 12. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bf3d2] } 12... h5 $10 { Black has enough counterplay on the kingside and is aiming for mate, though the attack will not be immediate. The queen must go to c7 first; castling queenside is perhaps dangerous, so ...c5 or ...f6 should come first, and once the position is less complicated Black can castle long and continue the kingside attack. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 13. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bc3e2] } 13... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 14. c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 14... dxc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd5c4] } 15. Nxc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd2c4] } 15... O-O-O $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.4).] [%csl Bc8][%cal Be8c8] } 16. Rac1 $15 { White brings the rook to the c-file against the c6 pawn and your king, while Nc4 eyes d6 for a check on c8. } (16. Qe4 Nb6 17. Rec1 Kb8 18. Rab1 Qd7 19. Nxb6 axb6 20. a4 Qd5 { Qe4 hits g4 and c6, but ...Nb6 trades the knight and your queen finds d5 (about -0.6). }) 16... Kb8 { The king steps off the c-file and out of the Nd6+ check. } 17. Red1 Nb6 18. Ng3 Nxg3 19. fxg3 Nd5 { You trade a pair of knights and the knight on d5 is a monster in the centre. } 20. Bf2 Rd7 { You have a small edge (about -0.4): your pieces are active and the king is safe on b8. }) 10... Nh4 { [%csl Gh4,Re1][%cal Gf5h4,Rf3h4,Rf3e1] } 11. Nxh4 { Nxh4 leads to roughly equal (-0.0): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s); White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gf3h4] } (11. Ne1 $17 { If White retreats the knight, ...h5 lets Black attack the king at once. } { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf3e1] } 11... h5 $17 { This is simply just crushing white now. Black attacks white's kingside and pretty much it's nevertheless over. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 12. f3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf2f3] } 12... hxg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bh5g4] } 13. fxg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bf3g4] } 13... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 14. Bg3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bf4g3] } 14... cxd4 $17 { White cannot take due to the Bc5 pin. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 15. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bc3e2] } 15... Qb6 $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.7). Notable features: Black is a pawn up; Black has doubled pawns on the d-file.] [%csl Bb6,Bg1][%cal Bd8b6,Bb6g1] } 16. Qb3 $17 { White offers a queen trade and covers b2, hoping to take the sting out of your extra pawn. Do not worry: the d4 pawn cramps White and the knight on h4 is annoying, so you simply retreat ...Bd8, which still guards h4 along the diagonal, and bring a rook to c8. Then ...Rc4 hits the weak g4 pawn while keeping d4 alive. (about -1.9) } (16. b3 Nc5 17. Qxd4 Ne4 18. Qxb6 axb6 $17 { Hitting the d4 pawn at once only leads to an ending where you are still clearly better (about -2.0). }) 16... Bd8 17. Nd3 Rc8 18. Nef4 Rc4 19. Rf2 O-O 20. Raf1 Qc6 $17 { White has piled up on the f-file but you are safe and a pawn up. }) 11... Bxh4 { [%csl Gh4][%cal Ge7h4] } 12. Ne2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gc3e2] } 12... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 13. Kh1 { [%csl Gh1][%cal Gg1h1] } 13... f6 { [%csl Gf6,Rd1][%cal Gf7f6,Re5f6,Ra1d1] } 14. exf6 { exf6 leads to roughly equal (+0.0): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge5f6] } (14. Rad1 $15 { The f-file will be open for Black's rook whether or not White takes the f-pawn. Black is better because White's kingside is weak with little counterplay, and White lacks pieces to switch to the queenside. With the open f-file and a bishop on h4, Black gets a very dynamic attack on the king. } { [%csl Bd1][%cal Ba1d1] } 14... fxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf6e5] } 15. dxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd4e5] } 15... Qe7 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.4).] [%csl Be7][%cal Bd8e7] } 16. Qe3 $15 { White wants to bring the bishop to g3 and double on the f-file, while the queen eyes the loose a7 pawn. You answer with ...Rf7 to guard the seventh rank and prepare ...Raf8. When the dark bishops come off on g3, a lone knight on d7 is enough to keep e5 under pressure. (about -0.4) } (16. Kg2 Rf7 17. Bg3 Qg5 18. Bxh4 Qxh4 19. Qg3 Qxg3+ 20. Kxg3 Nxe5 $15 { If White spends a tempo on the king, the e5 pawn falls once the queens leave the board. }) 16... Rf7 17. Bg3 Bxg3 18. hxg3 Qb4 19. f4 Qxb2 20. Nd4 Re8 $15 { You take the pawn only because the knight on d4 gives White very little in return. }) 14... Rxf6 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.2).] [%csl Gf6][%cal Gf8f6] } 15. Qe3 $10 { White wants to use the queen on the e-file and the bishop on f4 to pile up on e5 and the d-file, since your knight on d7 is the key defender. You reply with ...Qf8, which defends the f-file and keeps an eye on f2, then bring the rook to e8 and f7. The position is level, so keep your pieces coordinated around the loose bishop on h4. } (15. Rae1 Rf7 16. Bg3 Qg5 17. f3 h5 $10 { A sharper try: after 18.gxh5 Qxh5 you get active play against the king. }) 15... Qf8 16. Kg2 Re8 17. Be5 Rf7 18. f4 h6 $10 { Be5 aims to trade your good knight, and f4 gains space. Both ideas are met by ...h6 and the central break ...Nxe5. } *


