1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Van der Wiel Attack is White's most aggressive try in the Advance complex, an early kingside pawn storm (f4-g4-h4) aimed straight at Black's king before Black finishes developing. The resulting structure (half-open c-, g- and h-files for Black) shows this is a race, not a slow maneuvering game: Black's practical plan is rapid, concrete development (get the king safe or accept temporary discomfort while counter-attacking in the center with ...c5/...cxd4), not the patient bishop-maneuvering that works in the calmer Advance lines. Memorize the concrete defensive sequence in the PLAN rather than trying to improvise against White's initiative, this is the sharpest chapter in the Advance complex for a reason.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives -0.4 to +0.1, roughly equal. Black is already comfortable here if White follows the chapter's line. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Bxc2? on moves 4 to 6 loses material (about 3.6); ...Bxf2+? at move 12 loses material (about 2.5); 14...Kd7? (about 2.2; prefer ...Kf8); 12...Qb8? (about 1.1; prefer ...Be7); 8...Nc6? (about 0.9; prefer ...Bh7); 8...Qb6? (about 0.8; prefer ...Bh7).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 14.Qxf7? d4 (costs White about 2.5); 12.Nxg7+? Kf8 (costs White about 1.7); 19.Rfe1? Rxh6 (costs White about 1.6).
Chapter #17 continues the Advance Variation. Other Advance Variation chapters: #15, #18, #19, #20, #21. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #7. Model game: #8. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 $14 { The Advance Variation is one of the most challenging tries against the Caro-Kann. White gains space and has kingside attacking chances, much as in the French Defence. The principled, classical way to meet it is recommended here: Black makes full use of the bishop on f5 and gets strong queenside counterplay. In the French that same bishop is often the problem piece, and Black may even have to double the b-pawns just to trade it for a White bishop or knight; in the Caro-Kann Black avoids that fate because the bishop is developed before ...e6. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 3... Bf5 $14 { Thus, this is the classical way to play this, the ECK will be met with the principled and classical Bf5! In that case black solves all his problem on the LSB. } { [%csl Gf5,Rg4,Rh4,Rc4,Rc3][%cal Gc8f5,Rg2g4,Rh2h4,Rc2c4,Rb1c3] } 4. Nc3 $14 { A strong Advance Variation sideline: Nc3!? first, with c3 not strictly needed since White can hold the centre anyway. Delaying c3 keeps the square free for the knight, which can cover e4 in lines where Black tries to place a bishop there (typical of the Bayonet Attack with g4, here delayed until Nc3 has been played). After ...e6 g4! White gets dangerous play. Despite the age of the idea, it remains effective. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } (4. c4 $14 { The early c4 move is rather a bit inaccurate since black's fortified center is unbreakable. The early c4 move would be met with e6. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 4... e6 { [%csl Be6,Ba3,Bc3][%cal Be7e6,Ba2a3,Bb1c3] } 5. Nc3 { Nc3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (5. a3 $10 { A well-prepared opponent may play a3; Plan B is to take the pawn and then play ...c5. } 5... dxc4 6. Bxc4 c5 7. Qb3 cxd4 8. Qxb7 Nd7 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.2).] WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.4 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nd2 Ne7 Bb5+ Kd8 a4 Qb6 } 9. Bb5 $10 { White has grabbed b7 and now attacks the rook on a8 while pinning the knight on d7. Your reply is ...Rc8, moving the rook out of danger and unpinning the knight indirectly, and then ...Bc5 and ...Bd3 harass the white pieces that wandered out. (about -0.1) } (9. b4 Rb8 10. Qxa7 Nxe5 11. Nd2 Nd3+ 12. Bxd3 Bxd3 13. Ngf3 Be7 $10 { Taking a second pawn is risky for White and gives you play (about -0.2). }) 9... Rc8 10. Nd2 Bc5 11. Nc4 Bd3 12. b4 Bxb4+ 13. axb4 Bxc4 $10 { Everything is balanced. }) 5... Bb4 6. Qb3 { Qb3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (6. Bd2 $15 { White does not want to ruin the pawn structure. } 6... Ne7 7. c5 b6 8. cxb6 axb6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3).] } 9. h4 $15 { White wants to play g4 and trap the bishop on f5. You answer with ...h6 so the bishop can retreat to h7. The a3 move then forces you to decide about the bishop on b4. After the trade on c3 the pawn break ...c5 gives you fine play. (about -0.2) } (9. Nge2 c5 10. Ng3 O-O 11. Bb5 Bg6 12. a3 cxd4 13. axb4 dxc3 $17 { Quiet development lets you strike in the centre (about -0.7). }) 9... h6 10. g4 Bh7 11. a3 Bxc3 12. bxc3 c5 13. Bb5+ Nbc6 $15 { You keep a slight edge. }) 6... Qa5 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Nd7 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2). Notable features: White has the bishop pair; White has doubled pawns on the c-file.] } 9. cxd5 $14 { White opens the position to use the bishop pair, and queens come off on d5. Then h4 and Nh3 aim to shut your bishop out with g-pawn play. You neutralise this with ...h5 and ...f6. (about +0.4) } (9. Qb4 Qc7 10. cxd5 cxd5 11. a4 Rc8 12. Bd2 Nb8 13. a5 Nc6 $14 { Offering the queen trade is another try (about +0.1). }) 9... Qxd5 10. Qxd5 cxd5 11. h4 Rc8 12. Bd2 h5 13. Nh3 f6 $14 { White has a small edge with the bishops, but your position is solid. }) (4. g4 $14 { This move order of the Bayonet Attack is objectively not good, since it exposes White's kingside at once, but it contains a few traps, so be prepared. } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bg2g4] } 4... Be4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf5e4] } 5. f3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf2f3] } 5... Bg6 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 fxe4 Qxd4 Nc3 e6 } { [%csl Bg6][%cal Be4g6] } 6. h4 h5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 7. Nh3 { Nh3 leads to roughly equal (+0.0), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bg1h3] } (7. e6 { 7.e6 sacrifices a pawn to rip open Black's kingside before the bishop on g6 can retreat safely. } 7... Qd6 8. exf7+ Bxf7 { ...and Black is already better after accepting the pawn accurately. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.4), an aggressive try worth recognizing but objectively unsound if met correctly.] } 9. Be3 $15 { The e6 gambit is over and material is level, but you own the bishop pair and White's pawns on f3, g4 and h4 are loose. White develops the bishop to e3 and brings the queen to d2, while you answer with ...Nd7 and ...e5 to open the centre. After ...hxg4 the h-file is yours too. (about -0.3) } (9. Qe2 Qg3+ 10. Qf2 Qxf2+ 11. Kxf2 c5 12. Bb5+ Nc6 13. c3 hxg4 $15 { Offering the queen trade does not solve White's problems (about -0.7). }) 9... Nd7 10. Nc3 e5 11. Qd2 hxg4 12. fxg4 Ngf6 13. Bh3 exd4 $15 { Now your central pawn mass is strong. }) 7... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 8. Nf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bh3f4] } 8... Bh7 { [%csl Bh7][%cal Bg6h7] } 9. Nxh5 $10 { With the open h-file and the bishop pair, Black has enough compensation for the pawn. The pawn is a sacrifice for the initiative, so keep the light-squared bishop to maintain both the initiative and the bishop pair. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bf4h5] } 9... Bg6 10. Nc3 Bxh5 11. gxh5 Be7 12. f4 Bxh4+ $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] } 13. Kd2 $10 { You took h4 with check. The tempting Rxh4 fails to Qxh4+ (about -4.5), so the king has to move. Kd2 is the best square because Ke2 lets you take on h5 with the rook. White then develops with Bd3 and Ne2 to harass your bishop on h4, while you play ...Nh6 and ...c5. (about 0.0) } (13. Ke2 Rxh5 14. Be3 g6 15. Bh3 Na6 16. Bg4 Rh8 17. Bf2 Rh6 $15 { The king step to e2 allows ...Rxh5 and leaves you slightly better (about -0.8). }) 13... Nh6 14. Bd3 Bf2 15. Ne2 c5 16. Qf1 Bxd4 17. Nxd4 cxd4 $10 { Everything is balanced. }) (4. h4 $14 { White gains space on the kingside. Unlike in other variations of the advanced Caro-Kann, black cannot play e6 immediately as g4 would trap the Bishop. } { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 4... h5 { [%csl Bh5,Bg5,Bd3][%cal Bh7h5,Bc1g5,Bf1d3] } 5. Bg5 { Bg5 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.6), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bg5,Bd8][%cal Bc1g5,Bg5d8] } (5. Bd3 $10 { Bd3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 5... Bxd3 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.8 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 Bxf5 e6 Bd3 Qxd4 } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf5d3] } 6. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd1d3] } 6... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7. Bg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 7... Qb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 8. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 8... Qa6 $14 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores +0.68; c5 scores +0.25, so c5 was stronger] [%csl Ba6][%cal Bb6a6] } 9. c4 $14 { Your queen on a6 faces the queen on d3, so c4 blocks that diagonal and hits d5. After ...Bb4 pins the knight on d2, b3 supports the pawn, and you counter with ...c5 and ...Nc6. (about +0.6) } (9. Qxa6 Nxa6 10. c3 c5 11. a3 Nb8 12. Ne2 Nc6 13. Nf3 cxd4 $15 { If the queens come off you hit d4 and equalise (about -0.1). }) 9... Bb4 10. b3 c5 11. Nf3 Nc6 12. O-O cxd4 13. Nxd4 Nge7 $14 { White keeps a small edge. }) (5. Nf3 { White finally develops and prepares c4 to hit your centre, while the pawn on h4 stays as support for an attack on the kingside. It is a common move after this pawn push. For you it means White wants to open the centre while you have not castled. } 5... e6 $10 { You support the d5 pawn and open a retreat for your bishop on f5. Your plan is ...Bg4 or ...Be7, ...Nh6 and ...Nf5 to put a piece on f5, and ...c5 to attack d4. Avoid ...Qb6 too early, which loses time. } (5... Bg4 6. Bg5 f6 7. Bf4 e6 8. Bd3 Ne7 9. Nc3 Nd7 10. Qd2 { This is also playable but slightly less accurate. }) 6. c4 Bg4 7. Nc3 Be7 8. Bg5 dxc4 9. Bxc4 Nh6 10. Qb3 { After the queen comes to b3 you play ...Qc8 or ...Nc6 to cover b7 and keep your king in the middle for one more move. White's other approach is 6.Bd3 instead of c4, and then you take the bishop with ...Bxd3 and settle into a solid structure. Overall: you are fine and roughly equal, but be careful about the b7 pawn. This is a semi-open position after the h-pawn push: your pawns on c6, e6 and h5 face White's centre, and your bishops are active. Your king may stay in the centre for a move or two. Your plan: protect b7 with ...Qc8, bring the knight with ...Nf5 or ...Nd7, trade bishops on g5 with ...Bxg5 when useful, and then castle short. White wants to attack b7 and e6 with Qb3 and Bc4 and use the h4 pawn as a hook. Meet that with ...Qc8 or ...Nc6. The risk is the b7 pawn and a sacrifice on e6. If White plays Bd3 instead of c4, take with ...Bxd3. }) 5... Qb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 6. Bd3 { Bd3 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.6), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } (6. Nd2 $15 { Nd2 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.8), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 6... Qxd4 7. Ngf3 Qb6 8. Bd3 Bxd3 { WATCH OUT: Qxb2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bxf5 e6 Rb1 Qa3 Bh3 Nd7 } 9. cxd3 e6 { WATCH OUT: Qxb2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -2.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Rb1 Qa3 e6 f6 Bf4 Qa6 } 10. O-O Be7 11. Be3 Qd8 $17 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.9). Notable features: Black is a pawn up.] [%cal Bg8h6,Bh6f5,Bb8d7,Bc6c5] } 12. Rc1 $15 { White has traded a pawn for activity. The rook on c1 supports the central break d4, which would open the c-file against your queenside. You respond with ...a5 to stop b4, develop the knight on d7 and regroup the bishop to f8. You are a pawn up, so a calm position is enough. (about -0.7) } 12... a5 13. d4 Nd7 14. g3 Qb6 15. Kg2 Bf8 16. Ne1 Ne7 $15 { You keep an extra pawn and a safe position. }) 6... Bxd3 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.6 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 Bxf5 e6 Bd3 Qxd4 WATCH OUT: Qb4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: c3 Qxb2 Bxf5 e6 Bd3 Qxa1 } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf5d3] } 7. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd1d3] } 7... e6 $10 { ...e6 reinforces the central pawn. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 8. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2,Ba6][%cal Bb1d2,Bb6a6] } 8... Qa6 $14 { This move order avoids heavy theory: c4 is the main line but runs into well-known theory after ...c5. Both are covered here, but this set-up is the one preferred.  [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores +0.68; c5 scores +0.25, so c5 was stronger] [%csl Ba6][%cal Bb6a6] } (8... c5 $10 { c5 leads to roughly equal (+0.0), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bc5,Bc3,Bc4][%cal Bc6c5,Bc2c3,Bc2c4] } 9. c3 { c3 leads to roughly equal (+0.0), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } (9. c4 $10 { The Tal Variation has been expanded with a new idea: after Nd2 the main recommendation is ...c5. White typically expects to play c3; the sharper c5 is highly theoretical, so extra variations have been added to cover it. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 9... Qxb2 { [%csl Bb2,Bb1,Bb3][%cal Bb6b2,Ba1b1,Bd2b3] } 10. Rb1 $15 { Despite of top engine's choices. Rb1 falls under Qxd4! Although endgame isn't forced simply Qe2 then b6! Would have already made black's position quite playable. } { [%csl Bb1][%cal Ba1b1] } (10. Nb3 $10 { Nb3 is another variation, a highly theoretical line in the Tal Variation. The pressure on the centre from both sides makes it very complicated, so Black must take care to avoid errors in the opening. } { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd2b3] } 10... Nc6 $10 { ...Nc6! Black's plan is ...Nb4, forking the king and the rook. } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 11. cxd5 $10 { Black cannot play Nb4 because of Qb5+! A horrible position for white in this particular position.   WATCH OUT: Nxc5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.4 from White's own side of the board), after: Qxa1+ Qd1 Qxd4 Nxb7 Bb4+ Bd2 } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc4d5] } 11... exd5 { WATCH OUT: cxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: dxc6 Bb4+ Bd2 Bxd2+ Qxd2 Qxd2+ } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be6d5] } 12. Rb1 { WATCH OUT: Nxc5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.5 from White's own side of the board), after: Qxa1+ Qd1 Qxd1+ Kxd1 Nxd4 Nxb7 } { [%csl Bb1][%cal Ba1b1] } 12... Qxa2 $10 { As the position unfolds, white has four pieces +1 if added the queen in contrast against black's only develop piece is the knight +1 if added the queen. But eventually white is hopeless on stopping black from further developing his pieces.   WATCH OUT: Nxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -8.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: Rxb2 Be7 Nxd4 cxd4 Rxb7 Bxg5 WATCH OUT: cxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.8 from Black's own side of the board), after: Rxb2 Ba3 Rb1 Bb4+ Kf1 Nge7 } { [%csl Ba2][%cal Bb2a2] } 13. dxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd4c5] } 13... Bxc5! $10 { Bxc5 is made possible because Qa5+! with a fork on the king and knight would give his pieces back. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 14. Nxc5 { WATCH OUT: Qxd5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -7.8 from White's own side of the board), after: Bb4+ Kf1 Qxb1+ Nc1 Rd8 Bxd8 } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bb3c5] } 14... Qa5+ { WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.8 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qb5+ Kf8 Nh3 Qc4 Qxc4 Nxc4 } { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba2a5] } 15. Ke2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Be1e2] } 15... Qxc5 $14 { White needs to cover up the d4 pawn since the knight would later land in there. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Ba5c5] } 16. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bc6d4,Bg1f3] } 16... Nb4 { WATCH OUT: Nd4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxd4 Qc4 Rhc1 Qxd3+ Kxd3 f6 } 17. Qb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd3b3] } 17... a5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: Black is 2 points of material up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side.] WATCH OUT: Qc4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.6 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qxc4 dxc4 Rxb4 f6 Be3 Nh6 } { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 18. Rhc1 $10 { White hits the queen at once and wants to use the open c-file, since you are two pawns up but your pieces are tangled in the centre. You give the check with ...Qb5+ and then bring the knight into play with ...Nh6, aiming to trade queens. (about -0.2) } (18. e6 f6 19. Be3 Qb5+ 20. Kd2 Ne7 21. Rhc1 O-O 22. Bc5 Nf5 $17 { The e6 push does not work (about -1.6). }) 18... Qb5+ 19. Kd2 Nh6 20. Nd4 Qb6 21. Qa4+ Kf8 22. Be3 Kg8 $10 { It is an equal, double-edged position. }) 10... Qxd4 { WATCH OUT: cxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -8.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Rxb2 Ne7 Rxb7 Nd7 cxd5 Nxd5 } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bb2d4] } 11. Qe2 { WATCH OUT: Rxb7 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -7.6 from White's own side of the board), after: Qxd3 Rb3 Qc2 Ne2 dxc4 Rc3 } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd3e2] } 11... b6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 12. Ngf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 12... Qg4 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6). Notable features: Black is 2 points of material up.] WATCH OUT: dxc4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -7.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxd4 c3 Qf3 cxd2+ Bxd2 cxd4 } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bd4g4] } 13. Qd3 $15 { White is two pawns down and calls on the queen to hold the position together. You push ...d4 to take space from the knight on f3, then trade White's dangerous bishop with ...Bxg5 so your king can walk to e7. (about -0.4) } (13. O-O Be7 14. cxd5 exd5 15. Rfe1 Nc6 16. e6 f6 $15 { The e6 break is White's only way to open lines, but you stay ahead (about -0.9). }) 13... d4 14. O-O Be7 15. Rfe1 Bxg5 16. Nxg5 Nh6 17. Nde4 Ke7 $15 { You hold the extra pawns. }) 9... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 10. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 10... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 11. Rb1 { [%csl Bb1][%cal Ba1b1] } 11... Bxg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Be7g5] } 12. hxg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bh4g5] } 12... Nge7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 13. a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba2a3] } 13... a5 $10 { This discourages White from playing b4. } { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 14. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd2f3] } 14... g6 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the g-file.] [%csl Bg6][%cal Bg7g6] } 15. a4 $10 { White stops ...a4 and fixes your a5 pawn. The idea is to trade knights and take the d4 square. You castle and take the c-file with ...Rfc8, so queen and rooks come into play. (about +0.1) } (15. dxc5 Qxc5 16. Qd2 Qc4 17. Rh4 Qa6 18. Ned4 Nxd4 19. Nxd4 Nc6 $10 { Taking on c5 is also level (about 0.0). }) 15... cxd4 16. Nexd4 O-O 17. Qe3 Rfc8 18. Kf1 Nxd4 19. Nxd4 Nc6 $10 { You hold comfortably. }) 9. c4 $14 { A queen trade would only allow Nxa6, which would favour both sides; still, an endgame is also acceptable for Caro-Kann players. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 9... Bb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bf8b4] } 10. b3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bb2b3] } 10... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 11. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 11... Nf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Be7f5] } 12. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 12... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.6).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 13. a3 $14 { White asks the bishop on b4 to decide. After ...Bxd2 Nxd2 White has traded your active bishop and plans c5 to gain space. Your plan is ...Nd7 and ...Rac8, followed by ...Qb6 to hit the c5 pawn. (about +0.5) } (13. Qc2 Nd7 14. Rfd1 c5 15. dxc5 Bxc5 16. b4 Bxb4 17. cxd5 Rac8 $14 { The ...c5 break is your main resource (about +0.5). }) 13... Bxd2 14. Nxd2 Nd7 15. Rfe1 Rac8 16. Qf3 Qb6 17. c5 Qa6 $14 { White has more space but no clear attack. }) (4. Bd3 { 4.Bd3 offers the immediate trade of light-squared bishops before developing anything else, a distinct system from the Nf3-first approach the chapter otherwise builds around. } 4... Bxd3 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 Bxf5 e6 Bd3 Qxd4 } 5. Qxd3 e6 6. Nf3 { ...reaching a simplified, very level position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), a genuine independent fourth-move system alongside Nf3, Nc3, Nd2, h4 and g4.] } 6... c5 $10 { You have already traded your light bishop, so ...c5 hits d4 at once. White wants to support e5 with Be3 and f4 and keep a space edge. You trade on d4 and develop with ...Nc6 and ...Ne7. (about +0.1) } (6... Qa5+ 7. Nbd2 Qa6 8. c4 Bb4 9. O-O Ne7 10. Qc2 Bxd2 11. Nxd2 $14 { The check is a bit slower and gives White a small edge (about +0.3). }) 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nc6 9.