p the queen for a pawn leads to a forced loss. }) 21... Qxe2 22. Qf6 Rd2 23. Rf1 Qxg4+ 24. Kh2 Be3 25. Nh5 Qxh5 $19 { You get an extra piece and keep the attack. }) (15. Rg1 $15 { Rg1 leads to roughly equal (-0.1): White has a material edge of about 2 point(s); Black retains the bishop pair. } { [%csl Bg1][%cal Bh1g1] } 15... Be4 { [%csl Be4,Bc3,Be2][%cal Bh7e4,Bc3e4,Bf3e2] } 16. Qe2 { Qe2 leads to roughly equal (-0.1): White has a material edge of about 2 point(s); Black retains the bishop pair. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf3e2] } (16. Nxe4? $17 { Nxe4 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2): White has a material edge of about 5 point(s); Black's king has reached safety while White's has not yet. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 16... dxe4 $17 { Black's pressure is so strong that white would overpress and lose in the near future.   WATCH OUT: Bb4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nc3 d4 Qxc6+ bxc6 a3 Ba5 } { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bd8d1] } 17. Qe2 $17 { White is a lot of material up but his knight on g7 is nearly trapped and the king is stuck in the centre. The queen on e2 guards the king and the e4 pawn, while Nh5 later tries to rescue the knight. You keep the initiative with ...e3 and ...Rhg8. (about -0.9) } (17. Qf4?! Bb4+ 18. c3 Bxc3+ 19. bxc3 Qxc3+ 20. Kf1 Rd1+ 21. Kg2 Rxg1+ $19 { This natural queen move just loses to the check on b4 (about -2.5). }) 17... e3 18. Bxe3 Bxe3 19. fxe3 Rhg8 20. Nh5 Nxg4 21. Rd1 Nxe5 $17 { Your king is safer and the pieces are more active, so you stay a little better. }) 16... Bb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bc5b4] } 17. Bd2 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: White is 2 points of material up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side; Black has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } 17... Bxc3 $10 { Your plan is to remove the knight on c3 so you can play ...d4 and open the long diagonal against White's king, since you are down material. The checks on h1 and e4 keep you in the game. (about 0.0) } (17... Bh7 18. Qb5 Bxc3 19. Qxc6+ bxc6 20. bxc3 Be4 21. Ke2 Rdg8 22. g5 $16 { Retreating the bishop passively makes White's extra material count, which gives White an edge (about +1.9). }) 18. bxc3 d4 19. Rg3 Bxc2 20. cxd4 Qh1+ 21. Qf1 Qe4+ 22. Qe2 $10 { The queen checks keep the position level. }) 15... Be4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gh7e4] } 16. Nxe4?! { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 16... dxe4 { WATCH OUT: Bb4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nd2 Qxc2 Rd1 Rdg8 Nh5 Qxb2 } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 17. Qg2 { [%csl Gg2][%cal Gf3g2] } 17... Bb4+ { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gc5b4] } 18. Kf1 { [%csl Gf1][%cal Ge1f1] } 18... Qc4+ { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc6c4] } 19. Kg1 { [%csl Gg1][%cal Gf1g1] } 19... e3 { WATCH OUT: Qxc2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: Rc1 Qxc1+ Bxc1 Bd2 Bxd2 Rxd2 } { [%csl Ge3][%cal Ge4e3] } 20. Bxe3 { Bxe3 leads to a clear Black edge (-2.4): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s).   (the engine edge for Black coexists with White's extra material here because this is a known pawn-grab-for-compensation pattern in this line, not a tactical oversight - Black has concrete compensation: faster development, open lines, or attacking chances that outweigh the missing pawn(s)) } { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gg5e3] } (20. fxe3 $19 { Engine-suggested line (Stockfish 16, depth 20), not in the original chapter. This is White's strongest try here (about -1.99); the chapter's own Bxe3 scores about -2.77. Review before relying on it. } 20... Rd2 21. Qf3 Nxg4 22. Rf1 Nxe5 { WATCH OUT: Qxa2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qxg4 Qxb2 Rh2 Rxh2 Kxh2 Qxe5+ } 23. Qf4 Qd5 $19 { [Engine verdict: winning for Black (-2.9). Notable features: White is a pawn up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side.] WATCH OUT: Qxa2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qxb4 Rxc2 Rh2 Rc4 Qd2 Qb3 } 24. Rh2 $19 { White wants to trade a pair of rooks to relieve the pressure on the second rank; your rook on d2 hits c2 and f2, so it is the most dangerous attacker. After the rooks go off, the queen trade on f1 leaves you with the better pieces and an ending White cannot save. (about -4.2) } (24. e4? Bc5+ 25. Rf2 Rxf2 26. Qxf2 Bxf2+ $19 { Kicking the queen with the pawn opens the g1-a7 diagonal for the check and White loses material (about -7.7). }) 24... Rxh2 25. Qxh2 Nf3+ 26. Rxf3 Qxf3 27. Qg2 Qd1+ 28. Qf1 Qxf1+ $19 { You win the exchange of queens and keep a big plus. }) 20... Nxg4 $19 { Black's pressure is so strong that White can hardly hold: Black dominates the d-file, White's king is stuck and the kingside is weak.   WATCH OUT: Qxc2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.0 from Black's own side of the board), after: Rc1 Qxc1+ Bxc1 Rd4 Bxh6 Rxh6 } { [%csl Gg4,Gg2][%cal Gh6g4,Gh8g8,Gg8g2] } 21. h5 $19 { White wants to open the h-file for the rook on h1 against your rook on h8, and the knight on g7 supports that attack. But you take the bishop on e3 and bring the rook into the seventh rank with ...Rd2. White's pawns on e3 and c2 become targets. (about -3.0) } (21. Bxa7 Rdg8 22. b3 Qe2 23. f4 Qa6 24. Qxg4 Bf8 25. Rh3 Qxa7+ $19 { Taking the a7 pawn only wins a pawn for a moment, since the bishop falls (about -3.9). }) 21... Nxe3 22. fxe3 Rd2 23. Qf3 Bc5 24. Re1 Qxc2 25. Rh3 Kb8 $19 { Your king is safe on b8 and all your pieces are working. }) 11... Bg6 12. Bg5 Be7 { WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxg7+ Kf8 Nxe6+ fxe6 Bxd8 Rxd8 } 13. f4 Qb6 14. Nxg7+ Kf8 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1). Notable features: White is 2 points of material up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side.] } 15. Nxe6+ $10 { The knight on g7 is nearly trapped, so White cashes it in for the e6 pawn with check. The plan is to fix your structure with fxe6 and then use the queen on d2 to trade bishops. You have the knight on b4 to hit c2. The material is gone and the position is balanced. (about -0.5) } (15. Bxe7+ Ngxe7 16. f5 Qxb2 17. Rh3 exf5 18. Rb1 Qa3 19. gxf5 Nxf5 $17 { A kingside pawn storm is tempting but it leaves White worse (about -1.5). }) 15... fxe6 16. Qd2 Bxg5 17. fxg5 Nb4 18. Na4 Qb5 19. Nc3 Qc4 $15 { You keep the slightly better pawn structure. } *


