1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Botvinnik-Carlsbad Defence inside the Advance Variation reaches a structure much like the Exchange Variation's Carlsbad (Structure One) but with the center still locked by e5 rather than traded off, plus White's doubled c-pawns as a long-term target. Black's king often stays on c8/queenside here too, similar to Chapter #11. The practical plan combines both ideas: press the half-open c-file against White's doubled pawns while keeping the king safe enough to avoid a counter-race. This is a hybrid chapter, treat White's structural weakness as the long-term goal, but don't neglect king safety while you work toward it.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives -0.1 to +0.5, roughly equal. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Nxe5? at move 5 loses material (about 4.3); 7...O-O-O? (about 1.7; prefer ...e6); 11...Nc6? (about 0.9; prefer ...Bxf3); 8...Qd8? (about 0.8; prefer ...Qc7); 8...Rc8? (about 0.7; prefer ...Qc7).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 10.Bf4? Qa5+ (costs White about 4.4); 17.Ke3? Ndxc5 (costs White about 2.1); 15.Kd2? Ng3 (costs White about 1.6).
Chapter #18 continues the Advance Variation. Other Advance Variation chapters: #15, #16, #19, #20, #21. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #3. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 $14 { The Advance Variation is one of the most challenging tries against the Caro-Kann. White gains space and has kingside attacking chances, much as in the French Defence. The principled, classical way to meet it is recommended here: Black makes full use of the bishop on f5 and gets strong queenside counterplay. In the French that same bishop is often the problem piece, and Black may even have to double the b-pawns just to trade it for a White bishop or knight; in the Caro-Kann Black avoids that fate because the bishop is developed before ...e6. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 3... c5!? $14 { An interesting move that gives the position a French flavour. Black strikes at the centre at once and starts undermining White's Advance set-up. White must either keep the centre together or release it by taking the pawn, in which case Black spends several moves chasing the c- or e-pawn. } { [%csl Rc5,Rc3][%cal Gc6c5,Rd4c5,Rc2c3] } 4. dxc5 $14 { Perhaps the most challenging amongst them all. White gives up the center and temporarily is up a piece. But in exchange black would then chase both the e pawn and the c pawn. White has to sacrifice one of them or otherwise overpress. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gd4c5] } (4. c3 $14 { White supports the centre with c3, but Black simply takes on d4 and follows with ...Nc6 and ...Bg4. A real fight lies ahead. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 4... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } (4... Nc6 { CHOICE GUIDE - 4.c3 Nc6 (SAFE to BALANCED). Choose this IF you want a French-style Advance structure with a good bishop on f5: ...Bf5, ...e6, ...Bg6, then break with ...cxd4 at the right moment. The main line 5.Be2 and the sidelines 5.Bb5, 5.f4, 5.a3 and 5.Be3 are shown. 4.c3 Nc6 invites White to build a classical Advance centre. White's usual choice is 5.Be2, but 5.Bb5, 5.f4, 5.a3 and 5.Be3 all appear. } 5. Be2 { 5.Be2 is White's most flexible move. Black develops the bishop with ...Bf5 and plays ...e6 and ...Bg6. [Engine verdict: about +0.2.] } (5. Bb5 { 5.Bb5 pins the knight and tries to win a tempo. 5...Qa5 6.Ba4 cxd4 keeps the queen active and Black is fine. [Engine verdict: about +0.2 at the start, equal later.] } 5... Qa5 6. Ba4 cxd4 7. b4 Qc7 8. Nf3 (8. cxd4 Bd7 9. Bf4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.07).] }) 8... a6 9. cxd4 (9. O-O { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.14).] }) 9... Bg4 10. Be3 b5 11. Bb3 e6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.6).] }) (5. f4 { 5.f4 supports e5 but weakens the king. Black takes on d4 and develops with ...Bf5 and ...e6. [Engine verdict: roughly equal.] } 5... cxd4 6. cxd4 Bf5 7. Nc3 e6 8. Nf3 h5 9. Be2 Nh6 10. O-O Be7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.10).] }) (5. a3 { 5.a3 prepares b4 but costs a tempo. ...Bf5 and ...e6 give Black an easy game. [Engine verdict: about equal.] } 5... Bf5 6. Be3 e6 7. dxc5 f6 8. exf6 Nxf6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.24).] }) (5. Be3 { 5.Be3 is an odd move order. After ...Nh6 and ...Nf5 Black wins the bishop pair. [Engine verdict: about -0.1 at the start; the sample line ends with a clear Black edge.] } 5... Nh6 6. Nf3 (6. Bxh6 gxh6 7. Nf3 Qb6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.2).] }) 6... Nf5 7. a3 Nxe3 8. fxe3 g6 9. Bd3 Bh6 10. Qe2 Qb6 11. b4 c4 12. Bc2 a5 { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -1.4).] }) (5. Nf3 { White adds development and eyes d4 and e5. The motive is plain: complete development with Be2 or Bb5, O-O and stay flexible instead of pushing the e-pawn at once. It is very common in practice. It also lets White support d4 and prepare dxc5 if you open the position. } 5... cxd4 $10 { Take on d4 and open lines for your pieces. Plan: ...Bg4, ...e6, then ...Bb4+ to pin the knight on c3 and ...f6 to attack the e5 pawn. Your king goes short. } (5... Bg4?! { A tempting developing move, but White simply takes on c5 and breaks your pawn structure. } 6. dxc5 e6 7. b4 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nxe5) 6. cxd4 Bg4 { Develop this bishop before locking it in with ...e6. } 7. Be3 e6 8. Bd3 Bb4+ { Check and pin. } 9. Nc3 f6 { This is the key lever: attack the pawn on e5. If White takes with exf6 you recapture with the knight and get active pieces. } 10. h3 { Now the bishop on g4 retreats or takes on f3 and you keep the central tension. Overall: equal and rich in possibilities for you. This is an open, tense position where White has the pawns on d4 and e5, and you have attacked with ...f6. Your bishops are active: the one on b4 pins the knight on c3 and the one on g4 eyes f3. Your plan: take on f3 with ...Bxf3 and recapture on f3 with the queen, then play ...fxe5 to open the f-file, develop with ...Nge7 and castle short. White wants exf6 to open the centre, or Qd2 to unpin. If exf6, recapture with the knight and keep developing. The mistake is delaying ...fxe5 and letting White take on f6 with a tempo. }) 5... Bf5 6. Nf3 e6 7. O-O Bg6 8. Be3 cxd4 { After 8.Be3 Black resolves the centre with ...cxd4 and ...Nge7, heading for f5. Both sides have a clear plan and the position is balanced. [Engine verdict: about +0.3.] } (8... Nh6 9. dxc5 Nf5 10. Bd4 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.6).] }) 9. cxd4 (9. Nxd4 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 9... Nge7 10. Nc3 (10. Nh4 Nf5 11. Nxf5 Bxf5 12. g4 (12. Nc3 Be7 13. f4 Bg6 14. g4 f5 15. exf6 Bxf6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.10).] }) 12... Bg6 13. f4 f5 14. Bd3 fxg4 15. f5 Bxf5 16. Bxf5 exf5 17. Rxf5 g6 18. Rf1 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.00).] }) 10... Nf5 11. Rc1 (11. Bd3 Be7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.07).] }) 11... Be7 12. Na4 O-O 13. Nc5 Rc8 14. Nxb7 Qb6 15. Nc5 Ncxd4 { A sharp sample line: the knight excursion to b7 costs White time, and Black's central pieces become active. [Engine verdict: about -1.3, good for Black.] [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -1.3).] }) 5. cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc3d4] } 5... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 6. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 6... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 7. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } (7. Be2 { White accepts that the pin must be answered and retreats the bishop to e2 so that O-O, Nbd2 and h3 follow without worry. It avoids your Bxf3 ideas and keeps the position closed. It is common in practice (played in about 384 thousand games). For you it means the centre stays closed and you should play on the queenside. } 7... e6 $10 { You protect d5 and make way for the kingside knight to e7. The plan is ...Nge7, ...Qb6 hitting b2 and d4, ...Rc8 and ...Bh5, with the ...f6 break to undermine the pawn on e5. } 8. O-O Nge7 9. Nbd2 Qb6 { The queen turns the pressure up on d4 and b2. Do not play ...Bxf3 automatically; keep the pin on and take when you want to remove a defender of d4. } 10. h3 Bh5 11. a3 Rc8 { Your rook arrives with a tempo on the c-file and your pieces target d4 and the c-file squares. You avoid castling too early; the king is safe in the centre until the moment you choose. } 12. Qa4 { Overall: White has more space but you have no weaknesses, and by playing ...f6 or ...Nf5 you get an equal game. This is a closed Advance structure with White's pawns on d4 and e5, you with c6 and d5, and your light bishop already out on g4. White has more space but your pieces are active. Kings will probably stay in the centre for a while before you castle. Your plan: keep the pin with ...Bg4, develop the rook to c8 with tempo, then aim at d4 with ...Nc6 and ...Qd7 and prepare ...f6 or ...Nf5 to undermine e5. White wants to hold d4 and break with c4 or push on the kingside. You meet it by trading pieces. The risk is castling too early or releasing the tension with ...cxd4 before your pieces are ready. If White deviates, stay calm and keep the c-file in mind. }) 7... Qb6 $14 { the b3 pawn is currently hanging, but black cannot take the b2 pawn since white would gain a lot of counterplay on the queenside. Especially with the a and c pawn opened up. } { [%csl Bb2,Bb6][%cal Bb6b2,Bd8b6] } 8. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 8... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 9. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 9... Nge7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 10. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 10... Nf5 $14 { Black puts pressure on d4, so after the bishop takes the knight Black would have been a piece up. } { [%csl Bf5,Bh3][%cal Bf5d4,Bc6d4,Be7f5,Bd3f5,Bh2h3] } 11. Bxf5 { Bxf5 leads to roughly equal (-0.2): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s); White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bd3f5] } (11. h3 $10 { h3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 11... Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 12. Qxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd1f3] } 12... Ncxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc6d4] } 13. Qf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bf3f4] } 13... g5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bg7g5] } 14. Qxg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bf4g5] } 14... Bh6 $10 { Rapid development for Black. } { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bf8h6] } 15. Qf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg5f6] } 15... Bg7 { [%csl Bg7][%cal Bh6g7] } 16. Qg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bf6g5] } 16... Bh6 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bh6][%cal Bg7h6] } 17. Qf6 $10 { White's queen is on an active post but exposed: your bishop on h6 attacks it, while Qf6 hits the rook on h8, so the game heads for a repetition. } (17. Qh5 Nxe3 18. fxe3 Bxe3+ 19. Kh1 O-O-O 20. Rae1 Nc6 21. Nb5 Kb8 { This avoids the repetition without dropping material and is roughly level (about -0.2). }) (17. Bxd4?! Qxd4 18. Bb5+ Kf8 19. Qg4 Qxe5 20. Bd3 Rg8 21. Qb4+ Kg7 { The check and Qg4 look dangerous, but your king reaches g7 safely and you win the e5 pawn (about -1.2). }) 17... Bg7 { The bishop must return to block the diagonal and save the rook. } 18. Qg5 Bh6 $10 { The same position arises again, so either side can claim a draw by repetition. }) 11... Bxf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bg4f5] } 12. Nh4 { [%csl Bh4,Bf5][%cal Bf3h4,Bh4f5] } 12... Bg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf5g6] } 13. Nxg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bh4g6] } 13... hxg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bh7g6] } 14. Rb1 { [%csl Bb1][%cal Ba1b1] } 14... Bb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bf8b4] } 15. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bc3e2] } 15... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bb4e7] } 16. b4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb2b4] } 16... Qd8 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the g-file.] [%csl Bd8][%cal Bb6d8] } 17. Qd2 $14 { The queen adds a second guard to b4, which your bishop on e7 attacks, and White's plan is b4-b5 to kick your knight and gain queenside space while your doubled g-pawns give you little to do on the kingside. } (17. Qd3 Bxb4 { Here only the rook guards the pawn, so you may take it. } 18. a3 Ba5 19. Rxb7 Bb6 20. Rc1 Qc8 21. Rxb6 axb6 { The rook on b7 is nearly trapped and has to be given up at a cost; the evaluation stays level (about 0.0). }) 17... a5 { Challenge b4 before b5 clamps the position. } 18. b5 Nb8 { The knight is kicked but reroutes via d7 to b6, where it covers d5 and c4. } 19. Qe1 Bb4 { A free tempo: the bishop attacks the queen along the e1-a5 diagonal. } 20. Qd1 Nd7 21. g3 Nb6 $14 { White keeps a small space edge (about +0.2) but you have a solid setup and no weaknesses to fear. }) (4. Nc3 { 5.Nc3 (instead of the usual 4.c3 or 4.Nf3 plans) - develops immediately without first deciding how to meet the tension on c5/d4. } 4... Nc6 { 5...Nc6 is simplest: develop and let White decide how to resolve the center. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] } 5. dxc5 { 5.dxc5 resolves the center immediately rather than the sharper Bb5 pin, heading for a simpler structure. } (5. Bb5 { 5.Bb5 pins the newly-developed knight immediately rather than resolving the center with dxc5 first. } 5... cxd4 6. Qxd4 e6 7. Nf3 { ...and Black holds comfortably with accurate play. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.