cal Be7h4] } 15. Kd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Be1d2] } 15... Ng3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bf5g3] } 16. Rg1 { [%csl Bg1][%cal Bh1g1] } 16... Ne4+ { [%csl Be4,Bd5][%cal Bg3e4,Bd5e4] } 17. Ke3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bd2e3] } 17... Ndxc5 $19 { Online game, 2021, 0-1. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd7c5] } 18. Rg2 $19 { The rook leaves g1 because with your bishop on h4 any careless move allows ...Bf2+ forking king and rook. White's king on e3 is stuck, your pieces are far more active, and ...f6 will blow the e-file open. } (18. Bg2? Bf2+ 19. Ke2 Bxg1 20. Bxg1 h5 { You win the exchange and more (over 3 pawns down). }) 18... f6 19. exf6 Bxf6 20. g5 Bxd4+ 21. cxd4 Rc8 22. dxc5 Qe7 { Every black piece points at the king and the c5 pawn, and Black stays clearly ahead (about -2.2). }) 7... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 8. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 8... d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd5d4] } 9. Bf2 { [%csl Bf2][%cal Be3f2] } 9... Bxc5 $14 { Black wins the pawn back and is fine. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 10. h3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 10... Bf5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.6).] [%csl Bf5][%cal Bg4f5] } 11. g4 $14 { White hits the bishop on f5 and wants to remove your good bishop with a gain of time. } (11. O-O Bxd3 12. Qxd3 Nf5 13. Nbd2 O-O 14. Ne4 Bb6 15. g4 Ne3 { Castling first is equally good (about +0.6). }) 11... Bxd3 12. Qxd3 f6 { You challenge the e5 pawn that cramps you. } 13. Qc4 Qd5 { Offer a queen trade. } 14. Qxd5 exd5 { White keeps a small plus (about +0.6): the pawn on e5 is hit, so exf6 will follow. }) (5. Bb5 $14 { Bb5 leads to a modest White edge (+0.8): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bf1b5] } 5... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 6. Bxc6+ { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb5c6] } 6... bxc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb7c6] } 7. Qd4 $10 { If White insists on keeping the pawn, the consequences are a wasted move with the queen and the initiative passing to Black, who keeps attacking the e- and c-pawns while White has to defend. } { [%csl B