r king steps off the e-file and the h-pawn lever opens the g-file (about -2.1). }) 14... e4 { The pawn hits the queen and opens lines. } 15. Nxe4 dxe4 16. Qxe4+ Ne7 17. Nf4 Bh6 18. O-O Bxf4 { White gave up a knight for two pawns to break your centre but remains clearly worse (about -1.9). }) (5. Be3 $14 { Be3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } 5... Nh6 6. Bxh6 $14 { Independent depth-28 verification of another well-known critical try in the 3...c5 complex - 4.dxc5 Nc6 5.Be3 Nh6!?, hitting the bishop before White can consolidate. This course's main recommendation for the 3...c5 approach goes a different route (5.Nf3 Bg4 6.Bb5 Qa5+), so this is supplementary reference material for a related, also-critical branch. Even the engine's top choice here (simply taking the knight) only holds a modest, very manageable edge - confirming this whole complex remains fully sound and well-explored territory for Black either way. } { [%csl Bh6][%cal Be3h6] } (6. Nf3 $14 { Also possible: Nf3 (+39), a close alternative the engine also approves of at real depth. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 6... Nf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bh6f5] } 7. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be3f4] } 7... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 8. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 8... Bxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 9. Nbd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 9... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. O-O $14 { White's bishop on f4 eyes c7 and the one on d3 eyes your knight on f5, while the e5 pawn cramps you. White castles and brings the queen to e2. Your plan is ...h6 for air, ...a5-a4 to gain queenside space and stop b3/b4, and ...Qc7 to trade bishops. } (10. h4 f6 { h4 hopes to dislodge the knight, but the f6 lever hits the clamp first. } 11. Bxf5 exf5 12. O-O fxe5 13. Nxe5 Nxe5 14. Bxe5 f4 { About +0.2. }) 10... h6 11. c3 a5 12. Qe2 a4 13. Rae1 Qc7 14. b4 axb3 { The en passant capture opens the a-file for your rook. }) (6. Qd2 $14 { Also possible: Qd2 (+28), a close alternative the engine also approves of at real depth. } { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bd1d2] } 6... Nf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bh6f5] } 7. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 7... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 8. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 8... Bd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bc8d7] } 9. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 9... Nxe3 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair; White has doubled pawns on the c-file.] [%csl Be3][%cal Bf5e3] } 10. Qxe3 $14 { The queen recapture is the only good one, and the c5 pawn is now the weak point: White has doubled c-pawns and you have the bishop pair. You undermine it at once with ...b6. } (10. fxe3? Bxc5 { The pawn recapture opens the f-file but costs you c5 and then e5 is next. } 11. Qe2 Qc7 12. Nbd2 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 Qxe5 14. e4 Bc6 { You are clearly better (about -1.8). }) (10. Rg1? Ng4 { The knight jumps out with a threat and the h-pawn and g-pawn trap the queen. } 11. Qf4 h5 12. h3 g5 13. Qg3 Bxc5 14. hxg4 h4 { Decisive (about -5.0). }) 10... b6 11. cxb6 Qxb6 { Your queen faces the queen on e3 and eyes b2. } 12. Qe2 Be7 13. b4 f6 { Break the e5 pawn. } 14. O-O O-O { White's edge is small and you have a sound game. }) 6... gxh6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bg7h6] } 7. Bb5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bf1b5] } 7... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 8. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 8... Bxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 9. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 9... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.6). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair; Black has doubled pawns on the h-file.] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. Nc3 $14 { White brings the last minor piece in and aims for a4 to harass your bishop on c5. Your trumps are the bishop pair and the open g-file for your rook. } (10. Bxc6 bxc6 11. c4 Kh8 12. Qc1 Be7 13. Qxh6 Ba6 14. b3 Rg8 { Grabbing the doubled h-pawn is possible but costs time: your bishops swing toward the white king (about +0.3). }) 10... f6 { Hit e5 at once; without that pawn the Bb5 pin loses its bite. } 11. Na4 Be7 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. Qd4 Qc7 14. b4 fxe5 $14 { Your pawn recaptures with a tempo on the queen, and White's edge shrinks to about +0.5. }) 5... Bg4 $14 { This is the typical reply in the Caro-Kann. White has two choices, c3 or Bb5, and both are reasonable. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gc8g4] } 6. Bb5 $14 { White wants to eliminate one of black's finest attacker of his central pawns. Once the knight falls the e5 pawn could be kept defended. } { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gf1b5] } (6. c3 $14 { Another alternative move which falls unto this idea of either b4 to protect the c5 pawn or to simply make way for the queen Q-b3. } { [%csl Bc3,Ba6,Be5,Bf3][%cal Bc2c3,Ba7a6,Bc6e5,Bg4f3] } 6... Bxf3 $14 { Other approaches prefer ideas such as ...a6 or even ...e6, but simplicity works here: take the knight, recapture on the pawn, and the pawn is regained. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } (6... a6 $14 { a6 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Ba6,Bb3,Bb5,Bb4][%cal Ba7a6,Bd1b3,Bb2b4] } 7. Qb3 { Qb3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd1b3] } (7. b4? $14 { b4, either white is out of his mind or prefer to keep the c pawn than the e pawn. Bxf3 followed by knight takes pawn only wins the pawn back. } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb2b4] } 7... Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bc6e5] } 8. Nbd2 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the c-file.] [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 8... g6 $14 { Do not rush to take on f3: the knight on e5 and bishop on g4 already tie down the f3 knight, because it cannot leave without hanging the queen. The fianchetto prepares ...Bg7 against c3 and the long diagonal. } (8... Nxf3+ 9. Nxf3 Nf6 10. Be2 e6 11. h3 Bxf3 12. Bxf3 a5 13. O-O { Capturing at once hands White the bishop pair (about +0.7). }) 9. Be2 Nxf3+ 10. Nxf3 Bg7 11. Bb2 Nf6 12. h3 Bf5 13. O-O { Your bishop is safe on f5. White's doubled c-pawns are a weakness you target with ...O-O, ...Qc7 and the e5 break; the evaluation is about +0.4. }) 7... Qd7 { [%csl Bd7,Bb7][%cal Bd8d7,Bd7b7] } 8. Nd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bf3d4] } 8... e6 $14 { Both the e- and c-pawns hang. Black takes the c-pawn, followed by ...Bh5 and ...Bg6, and castles kingside. } { [%csl Bc5,Be5,Be6][%cal Bg4h5,Bh5g6,Bf8c5,Bc6e5,Be7e6] } 9. Qa4 $14 { White pins your knight against the queen and prepares Nb5, with Nd6+ in mind. } (9. h3 Bh5 10. Nxc6 Qxc6 11. Be3 Ne7 12. g4 Bg6 13. Bg2 Be4 { The bishop is chased, but ...Be4 trades off White's light bishop (about +0.7). }) 9... Nge7 10. Nb5 Ng6 { Hits e5. } 11. Nd6+ Bxd6 12. exd6 e5 { The d6 pawn is a thorn, but you block it and castle. } 13. Na3 O-O { White has a small edge (about +0.7). }) (6... Nxe5?? $18 { This is a blunder: after 7.Nxe5 Bxd1 8.Bb5+ White comes out ahead. } 7. Nxe5 Bxd1 8. Bb5+ $18 { It may look as though a piece is being won, but more is lost: with Black's king stuck on e8, Black has to give up the queen and the light-squared bishop. } 8... Qd7 { The only move. The king cannot step aside because the knight on e5 covers d7 and the squares d8 and f8 are occupied, so the queen has to block, and White simply takes it. } 9. Bxd7+ Kd8 10. Kxd1 $18 { The bishop on d1 falls too, so the greedy capture on d1 cost your queen and a piece. } 10... f6 11. Be6 fxe5 12. Bxd5 Kc7 13. Bxg8 { White ends up far ahead in material (about +6.4). }) 7. Qxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd1f3] } 7... Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bc6e5] } 8. Bb5+ { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bf1b5] } 8... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Be5c6] } 9. c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc3c4] } 9... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 10. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 10... Qf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd8f6] } 11. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 11... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 12. O-O-O { [%csl Bc1][%cal Be1c1] } 12... O-O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.8). Notable features: White has the bishop pair; White has doubled pawns on the c-file.] [%csl Bc8][%cal Be8c8] } 13. cxd5 $14 { White opens the centre and hits your knight on c6 with pawn and bishop. } (13. Qxf6 gxf6 14. f4 h5 15. Nf3 Bh6 16. Rhe1 Nf5 17. g3 dxc4 { Trading queens doubles your pawns, but you get activity (about +0.1). }) 13... Qxf3 14. Nxf3 Rxd5 15. Ng5 Nf5 { Hitting the bishop. } 16. Rxd5 exd5 17. Bd3 g6 { White has the bishop pair and your d5 pawn is isolated (about +0.8). }) (6. Be2 { White simply develops and prepares castling, keeping a solid structure. The bishop on e2 supports d3 and protects against the pin on f3. The pawn on c5 is not under direct attack, so White is in no hurry, but he intends c4 to break up your centre. } 6... e6 { You support your d5 pawn and open the f8 bishop so it can retake on c5. Your plan: ...Bxc5 when possible, ...Nge7 and castle short. The pawn on c5 is the target, because it cannot be held for long. } (6... Rc8?! { This is tempting because it defends c5 in a way, but it does not help, and White gets a comfortable edge. } 7. c4 dxc4 8. Qxd8+ Rxd8 9. Be3 Nh6 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Bxf3 { White has the better structure and the bishop pair. }) 7. c4 Bxc5 { Take it back at once; the bishop on c5 is active and aims at f2. } 8. cxd5 Qxd5 9. Qxd5 exd5 { You accept an isolated pawn on d5, but your pieces are active and White has no clear target. } 10. O-O Nge7 11. Bf4 { Overall: you are close to equal with a small pull for Black. Keep the knights flexible and plan ...O-O and ...Rfd8. This is an open position with no pawn on d4 for White: you have an isolated pawn on d5, while White's pawn on e5 is a target supported by the bishop on f4. Both sides can castle and the game is balanced. Your plan: castle short with ...O-O, put a rook behind your isolated pawn with ...Rfd8, then hit e5 with ...Nf5 and ...Bxf3 when useful. White wants Nbd2 and Nb3 to hit your bishop on c5, and Nc3 to pile on d5. Keep d5 guarded after Nc3. The risk is the weak isolated pawn; if White trades on f3, recapture and keep the pieces active. }) 6... Qa5+ $14 { Forcing white to play Nc3 or otherwise lose a bishop. Dislodging white from ever playing Nd2 to defend the f3 knight since the f3 knight is quite valuable since it holds unto the e pawn. } { [%csl Ga5,Gb5,Ge1][%cal Gd8a5,Ga5b5,Ga5e1] } 7. Nc3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 7... e6 { [%csl Ge6,Gd5,Gf7][%cal Ge7e6,Gf7d5] } 8. Bd2 { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gc1d2] } 8... Qc7 { [%csl Gc7][%cal Ga5c7] } 9. Na4 { Na4 leads to roughly equal (+0.0): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Ga4][%cal Gc3a4] } (9. b4 $14 { Engine-suggested line (Stockfish 16, depth 20), not in the original chapter. This is White's strongest try here (about +0.32); the chapter's own Na4 scores about -0.03. Review before relying on it. } 9... Bxf3 10. Qxf3 Qxe5+ 11. Qe2 Qxe2+ $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2). Notable features: White has the bishop pair; White has doubled pawns on the c-file.] } 12. Nxe2 $14 { The knight recaptures and the bishop stays on b5, where it pins your knight on c6 to the king, so the b4 pawn is immune for now. White keeps the bishop pair and a pawn chain that shuts in your bishop on f8. } (12. Bxe2? Nxb4 { The bishop was doing a job on b5: moving it releases the pin and wins you a pawn. } 13. Rb1 Bxc5 14. O-O b6 15. Bb5+ Ke7 16. Rxb4 Bxb4 { You are a clean pawn up. }) 12... a6 { Kicks the bishop and prepares to unpin with ...Ne7. } 13. Ba4 Ne7 14. h4 h5 15. Nd4 g6 16. Nxc6 Nxc6 { White still has a small edge (about +0.3) thanks to the bishops, but your position has no weaknesses. }) 9... O-O-O $10 { An odd-looking move, since Black usually avoids castling queenside, but here it is useful: the rook reaches the d-file with counter-chances in the middlegame or endgame, and the d-pawn gets extra protection, making ...d5-d4 possible. } { [%csl Gc8,Rg1,Rc6,Rf4][%cal Ge8c8,Re1g1,Rd2f4] } 10. O-O { O-O leads to a modest Black edge (-0.5): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns.   (the engine edge for Black coexists with White's extra material here because this is a known pawn-grab-for-compensation pattern in this line, not a tactical oversight - Black has concrete compensation: faster development, open lines, or attacking chances that outweigh the missing pawn(s)) } { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } (10. Bf4?? $19 { White has to let go of the pawn or otherwise suffer the inevitable. Qa5! And either the light square bishop or the knight falls. } { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bd2f4] } 10... Qa5+ { [%csl Ba5,Be1,Bc3,Bd5,Bd4][%cal Bc7a5,Ba5e1,Ba4c3,Bd5d4] } 11. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Ba4c3] } 11... d4! $19 { The c3 knight is pinned, white is forced to lose a knight and suffer the inevitable. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd5d4] } 12. Qe2 $19 { Your pawn on d4 attacks the knight on c3, and that knight is pinned to the king by your queen on a5, so it cannot move. White loses a piece whatever he does, which is the whole point of 11...d4. } (12. Bxc6 dxc3 13. Qb1 cxb2+ 14. Bd2 Qc7 15. Qxb2 Qxc6 16. O-O-O Bxf3 { Taking a piece back does not rescue the knight; you stay a piece up (about -4.9). }) 12... Bxf3 { Remove the defender first so that gxf3 wrecks the white pawns. } 13. gxf3 dxc3 14. b4 Nxb4 15. c6 Ne7 16. cxb7+ Kb8 { White picks up a pawn with check, but you are a piece ahead (about -4.9). }) 10... Nxe5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gc6e5] } 11. Be2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gb5e2] } 11... Bxf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg4f3] } 12. Bxf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Ge2f3] } 12... Nxf3+ { [%csl Gf3][%cal Ge5f3] } 13. Qxf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gd1f3] } 13... Qc6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 14. b3 { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gb2b3] } 14... Nf6 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the c-file.] [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 15. Rad1 $14 { White aims a rook at your isolated pawn on d5 and prepares c4. The pawn on c5 is attacked by your bishop, but the knight on a4 guards it, so you trade it off. } (15. Bc3 d4 16. Qxc6+ bxc6 17. Ba5 Rd5 18. b4 Be7 19. Rae1 d3 { The bishop move tries to hit f6, but ...d4 kicks it first and the queen trade leaves the position equal (about 0.0). }) 15... Bxc5 16. Nxc5 Qxc5 17. Bg5 Kb8 { Your king steps off the c-file out of the way of Rc1 ideas. } 18. Bxf6 gxf6 { Doubled f-pawns, but you get the g-file and a solid centre. } 19. c4 f5 { White has only a small edge: the isolated d5 pawn is your weak point, so keep it protected. } *


