1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. A sharper sideline of the Botvinnik-Carlsbad complex where material gets unbalanced quickly (note Black's d4-pawn and the open c-file at the target) rather than settling into the calm structural fight of Chapter #17. Because the position is concrete and tactical this early, there is little generalizable 'theory' beyond the specific move order, the practical skill is accurate calculation and double-checking every capture against the engine line in the PLAN, not pattern recognition from the other Advance chapters.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives -0.7 to +0.6, roughly equal. Black is already comfortable here if White follows the chapter's line. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: 6...Nd7? (about 1.6; prefer ...Qxd5); 6...Qa5+? (about 1.3; prefer ...Qxd5); 5...a6? (about 1.2; prefer ...e6); 7...Qc5? (about 1.1; prefer ...Qd7).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 9.Bf4? e6 (costs White about 2.2; engine-found, not in the chapter tree); 9.Bg5? Bh5 (costs White about 2.1; engine-found, not in the chapter tree).
Chapter #19 continues the Advance Variation. Other Advance Variation chapters: #15, #16, #17, #20, #21. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #3. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 $14 { The Advance Variation is one of the most challenging tries against the Caro-Kann. White gains space and has kingside attacking chances, much as in the French Defence. The principled, classical way to meet it is recommended here: Black makes full use of the bishop on f5 and gets strong queenside counterplay. In the French that same bishop is often the problem piece, and Black may even have to double the b-pawns just to trade it for a White bishop or knight; in the Caro-Kann Black avoids that fate because the bishop is developed before ...e6. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 3... c5!? $14 { An interesting move that gives the position a French flavour. Black strikes at the centre at once and starts undermining White's Advance set-up. White must either keep the centre together or release it by taking the pawn, in which case Black spends several moves chasing the c- or e-pawn. } { [%csl Gc5,Rc3,Rf3][%cal Gc6c5,Rc2c3,Rg1f3] } 4. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } (4. c3 $14 { White supports the centre with c3, but Black simply takes on d4 and follows with ...Nc6 and ...Bg4. A real fight lies ahead. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 4... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 5. cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc3d4] } 5... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6,Bd4][%cal Bb8c6,Bc6d4] } 6. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3,Bd4][%cal Bf3d4,Bg1f3] } 6... Bg4 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bc8g4,Bg4d1] } 7. Be3 { [%csl Be3,Bd4][%cal Bc1e3] } 7... Qb6 $14 { the b3 pawn is currently hanging, but black cannot take the b2 pawn since white would gain a lot of counterplay on the queenside. Especially with the a and c pawn opened up. } { [%csl Bb2,Bb6][%cal Bb6b2,Bd8b6] } 8. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 8... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 9. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 9... Nge7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 10. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 10... Nf5 $14 { Black puts pressure on d4, so after the bishop takes the knight Black would be a piece up. } { [%csl Bh3,Bf5][%cal Bf5d4,Bc6d4,Be7f5,Bh2h3,Bd3f5] } 11. Bxf5 { Bxf5 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.5): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s); White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet.   (the engine edge for Black coexists with White's extra material here because this is a known pawn-grab-for-compensation pattern in this line, not a tactical oversight - Black has concrete compensation: faster development, open lines, or attacking chances that outweigh the missing pawn(s)) } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bd3f5] } (11. h3!? $10 { 11.h3 attacks the bishop and looks sharp, but it's actually a normal, roughly equal try (+0.1), not a mistake. The misconception runs the other way: after h3, grabbing the center with 11...Ncxd4? looks natural (hitting Be3 and opening lines) but it's a real trap - 12.Qa4+! forks the king and the loose knight, and after 12...Qc6 13.Nxd4 Qxa4 14.Nxa4 White has simply won a clean piece (verified: +4.8 after this sequence). The safe and correct reply is 11...Bxf3, trading the bishop for the knight into a level position; White's 12.Qxf3 recapture leaves things close to equal. } { [%csl Bh3,Bf3][%cal Bh2h3,Bg4f3] } 11... Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 12. Qxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd1f3] } 12... Ncxd4 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc6d4] } 13. Qf4 $10 { The queen attacks both of your knights, d4 and f5, so you have to react. } (13. Bxd4? Nxd4 14. Qd1 a6 15. Kh1 g6 16. Rc1 Nc6 17. Na4 Qa7 { Trading off a bishop gives your knight a monster post on d4 and White loses the bishop pair (about -1.8). }) 13... g5 { You hit the queen with a pawn at once. } 14. Bxd4 Nxd4 { Your second knight recaptures and lands on d4 with a threat on c2. } 15. Qxg5 h6 16. Qf4 Nc6 17. Bb5 Rg8 { White has won a pawn but your rook hits g2 and the white king is not entirely safe, so the game is level (about 0.0). }) 11... Bxf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bg4f5] } 12. Nh4 { [%csl Bh4,Bf5][%cal Bf3h4,Bh4f5] } 12... Bg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf5g6] } 13. Nxg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bh4g6] } 13... hxg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bh7g6] } 14. Rb1 { [%csl Bb1][%cal Ba1b1] } 14... Bb4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bf8b4] } 15. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bc3e2] } 15... Be7 16. b4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb2b4] } 16... Qd8 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the g-file.] [%csl Bd8][%cal Bb6d8] } 17. Qd2 $14 { This queen move adds a second guard to b4, which your bishop on e7 attacks, and White's plan is b4-b5 to kick your knight and gain queenside space while your doubled g-pawns give you little to do on the kingside. } (17. Qd3 Bxb4 { Here only the rook guards the pawn, so you may take it. } 18. a3 Ba5 19. Rxb7 Bb6 20. Rc1 Qc8 21. Rxb6 axb6 { The rook on b7 is nearly trapped and has to be given up at a cost; the evaluation stays level (about 0.0). }) 17... a5 { Challenge b4 before b5 clamps the position. } 18. b5 Nb8 { The knight is kicked but reroutes via d7 to b6, where it covers d5 and c4. } 19. Qe1 Bb4 { A free tempo: the bishop attacks the queen along the e1-a5 diagonal. } 20. Qd1 Nd7 21. g3 Nb6 $14 { White keeps a small space edge (about +0.2) but you have a solid setup and no weaknesses to fear. }) (4. Nc3 { 5.Nc3 (instead of the usual 4.c3 or 4.Nf3 plans) - develops immediately without first deciding how to meet the tension on c5/d4. } 4... Nc6 { 5...Nc6 is simplest: develop and let White decide how to resolve the center. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] } 5. dxc5 { 5.dxc5 resolves the center immediately rather than the sharper Bb5 pin, heading for a simpler structure. } (5. Bb5 { 5.Bb5 pins the newly-developed knight immediately rather than resolving the center with dxc5 first. } 5... cxd4 6. Qxd4 e6 7. Nf3 { ...and Black holds comfortably with accurate play. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.15), a real try but a touch less reliable for White than the simplifying dxc5.] } 7... Qc7 $10 { Note the pin: Bb5 holds your knight on c6 against the king, so ...Nxd4 is impossible and White hopes to use the time for Qg4 or Bxc6+. The queen adds a second attacker to e5 and prepares ...a6, which breaks the pin on your terms. } (7... Bd7 { Unpinning with the bishop is also level (about 0.0), but it allows the queen to head for g4. } 8. Qg4 Nge7 9. O-O h5 { The pawn kick answers the queen at once. } 10. Qh3 Ng6 11. Re1 Bc5 12. Bd3) 8. O-O a6 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 { Doubled pawns, but you keep the bishop pair and the half-open b-file. } 10. b4 { White stops ...c5, which would open the centre in your favour. } 10... Rb8 11. a3 c5 { You break anyway; after bxc5 the recapture ...Bxc5 hits the queen with tempo, and the evaluation stays at about 0.0. } 12. bxc5) 5... e6 6. Nf3 Bxc5 7. Bd3 { ...and White keeps a small, lasting pull. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.15), a sound simplifying alternative to Bb5 in this Advance 4.Nc3 sideline.] } 7... a6 $10 { A flexible waiting move: it takes b5 away from White's pieces and keeps your knight on c6 and bishop on c5 aimed at e5 and d4. White keeps only a slight space edge (about 0.0), so there is no need to force matters. } (7... f6 { The immediate lever against e5 opens the f-file and removes White's clamp. } 8. Bf4 fxe5 9. Bxe5 Nf6 10. Bg3 O-O 11. O-O Kh8 12. Qe2 { Also sound (about +0.1), but White's pieces are all active, so the quiet plan is easier to play. }) 8. O-O Nge7 { Heading for g6 to hit e5 a second time. } 9. Ne2 Ng6 10. Bxg6 { White gives up the bishop because it is the only way to relieve the pressure on e5. } 10... fxg6 { You get a half-open f-file against f2 in return for the doubled g-pawns. } 11. Ned4 Nxd4 12. Nxd4 $10 { The knight on d4 is strong and eyes e6, so keep your bishop on c5 and watch that pawn; White has nothing concrete (about 0.0). }) (4. Bb5+ { 5.Bb5+ - an immediate check, trying to disrupt Black's development before dealing with the pawn tension on c5/d4. } 4... Nc6 { 5...Nc6 blocks the check with a developing move rather than an awkward pawn push, and Black is simply comfortable. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4).] } 5. dxc5 $14 { White grabs the pawn and hopes to hold it with Be3 and c3, while the bishop on b5 pins your knight on c6 to the king (about +0.4). You are not worried: White has loose pawns on c5 and e5, and your pieces get time. } (5. Ne2 Qa5+ 6. Nbc3 e6 7. O-O Bd7 8. Bxc6 bxc6 9. dxc5 Qxc5 { White can also keep things compact with Ne2; after the trade on c6 your doubled pawns are compensated by active play (about +0.3). }) 5... e6 { Supports d5 and opens the bishop's route to c5. } 6. Be3 Ne7 7. c3 Nf5 8. Qd2 Nh4 { The knight eyes g2 and f3 and makes it uncomfortable for White to castle. } 9. f4 Bd7 { You unpin and offer a bishop trade; White's c5 pawn is isolated, so you keep playing against it. }) (4. c4 { 4.c4 strikes immediately on the queenside instead of developing the knight first, trying to open the position before Black can consolidate the extra space. } 4... cxd4 5. Qxd4 e6 6. cxd5 { ...reaching a sharp but balanced Isolated-Queen-Pawn-style structure. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), a genuine independent fourth-move system, not just a sideline inside the Nf3 tree.] } 6... Nc6 $10 { Your knight hits the queen on d4 and the pawn on e5 at once. White answers Bb5, which pins the knight to your king, so ...Nxd4 is not possible, and the way to take the sting out is to win the pawn back on d5 with a queen swap. } (6... a6? { Stopping Bb5 is tempting, but it loses time. } 7. d6 f6 8. f4 Nc6 9. Qc5 Bd7 10. Nc3 Rc8 11. Qe3 { The d6 pawn cramps you for good (about +1.3). }) 7. Bb5 Qxd5 { Your queen hits both the queen on d4 and the loose bishop on b5, so White has to trade. } 8. Qxd5 exd5 { An isolated pawn on d5, but it controls c4 and e4. } 9. Nc3 Ne7 { Unpins and heads for f5 or g6; e5 is White's main trump, so you aim at it with ...Be6 and ...Nf5. } 10. f4 a6 11. Ba4 $10 { Level (about 0.0): White has a space edge but you have an easy plan. }) 4... Bg4 { (Solid & Optimal: keeps the tension on d4 rather than resolving it immediately.) } { [%csl Gg4,Rc5,Rc3,Rc4][%cal Gc8g4,Rd4c5,Rc2c3,Rc2c4] } (4... cxd4 { 4...cxd4 (Complicated & Imbalanced) - resolves the tension at once instead of pinning first, heading for sharper isolated-pawn-style structures where both sides' pieces have more open lines to work with. This is a complete alternative main system, not just a sideline, so a reader who prefers this character can adopt it as their primary answer to 4.Nf3 instead of 4...Bg4. } 5. Nxd4 { 5.Nxd4 is White's most natural recapture (5.Qxd4 is the other main try, transposing to similar structures after 5...Nc6). } (5. Qxd4 { 5.Qxd4 - recaptures with the queen, the other of the two main tries alongside 5.Nxd4; the queen is immediately hit by ...Nc6 so White has to pick a square for it right away. } 5... Nc6 { 5...Nc6 gains a tempo on the queen at once. White's two main 6th moves here are 6.Bb5 (pinning the attacker) and 6.Qf4 (sidestepping while eyeing g3/c7); both are covered below. } 6. Bb5 { 6.Bb5 - pins the knight to the king along the long diagonal; the knight cannot simply grab the queen because it is absolutely pinned. } (6. Qf4 { 6.Qf4 - sidesteps the attack on the queen while keeping an eye on c7 and preparing Nc3; this is the source course's own main recommendation at this exact fork. CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE 6...g6 IF you want a stable fianchetto set-up where White's queen struggles to find a good post (solid, positional, medium theory). CHOOSE 6...f6 IF you are happy to loosen your structure for fast piece play and a direct attack on e5 (sharp, higher risk). After 5.Qxd4 Nc6 6.Qf4 White's queen eyes c7 and f7. Black has two serious answers, ...g6 and ...f6, and they lead to very different games. } 6... g6 { 6...g6 fianchettoes and prepares ...Bg7, meeting White's central pressure with a solid, flexible setup; both ...Bh6 ideas and normal development are available depending on White's next move. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.6), the sharper of the two 6th-move tries here.] } (6... f6 { 6...f6 hits e5 immediately and is the more aggressive option. It does weaken the e6-square and the king's diagonal, so it suits players who like open fights. [Engine verdict: about +0.55 for White, still playable.] } 7. Bb5 { 7.Bb5 g5! wins a tempo on the queen: after 8.Qg3 Nh6 Black's pieces coordinate well and ...Nf5 follows. Black is fine, even slightly better after 9.Nd4 Qb6. } (7. Nc3 a6 { 7.Nc3 a6 prepares ...fxe5 while keeping ...d4 in the air. White's best is the quiet 8.Bd3 and the position is balanced (about +0.5). } 8. Bd3 fxe5 (8... d4 9. exf6 Nxf6 10. Ne4 e5 11. Qh4 { [Engine check: a clear edge for White (about +0.8).] }) 9. Nxe5 Nf6 10. O-O e6 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Qh4 Bd6 13. Bg6+ Ke7 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.4).] }) 7... g5 8. Qg3 Nh6 9. Nd4 (9. O-O Nf5 10. Qg4 h5 11. Qa4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.08).] }) 9... Qb6 10. c3 (10. Qc3 Kf7 11. Nxc6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.04).] }) 10... Nf5 11. Qd3 (11. Bxc6+ { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.5).] }) 11... Nxd4 12. cxd4 fxe5 13. dxe5 Bf5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.13).] }) 7. Nc3 { 7.Nc3 develops naturally, adding pressure to d5 and preparing long castling. } (7. Bd3 { 7.Bd3 is a mistake: 7...Bh6 hits the queen and Black takes over. [Engine verdict: about -2.0 for White.] } 7... Bh6 8. Ng5 f6 { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -2.1).] }) 7... Bg7 (7... d4 { The thematic push: the knight is attacked and ...Bh6 will hit the queen next. After 8.Nb5 Black keeps comfortable counterplay. White's initiative is real but modest (about +0.4 to +0.6). } 8. Nb5 Bh6 9. Qh4 (9. Qg3 a6 10. Bxh6 Nxh6 11. Na3 Qa5+ { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -1.5).] }) 9... Bxc1 10. Rxc1 a6 11. Nbxd4 (11. Na3 Nxe5 { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -0.7).] }) 11... Nxe5 { The liquidation: Black trades the dark-squared bishops, wins back material and reaches a roughly equal position (about +0.3). } 12. Nxe5 (12. Be2 Nxf3+ 13. Nxf3 Qa5+ 14. b4 (14. c3 Nf6 15. Qh6 Rg8 16. h4 Bf5 17. O-O O-O-O { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.5).] }) 14... Qxa2 15. O-O Qe6 16. Rfe1 Qg4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.04).] }) 12... Qa5+ 13. c3 Qxe5+ 14. Be2 g5 15. Qh5 Nf6 16. Qf3 Qf4 17. Qxf4 gxf4 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.4).] }) 8. Be3 Nh6 9. O-O-O { ...and White's development lead translates into a real, lasting initiative. [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+1.4), the sharpest and most dangerous try in the entire Qxd4 system, worth knowing precisely since it's easy for Black to misplay.] } 9... O-O $16 { White has a lead in development and the king is safe on c1. Your d5 pawn is attacked twice (Nc3 and Rd1) but only defended by the queen, so castling lets White take it. You hope to use the bishop on g7 and open lines for compensation. } (9... e6 10. Ne4 Nf5 11. g4 Nxe3 12. fxe3 Rf8 13. Bb5 f6 14. Nxf6+ { The knight on e4 dominates f6 and d6, and ...f6 simply loses a pawn (about +1.8). }) 10. Nxd5 Qa5 { Counterattack on the knight and the a2 pawn. } 11. a4 { Saves the pawn. } 11... Nf5 12. Bb5 Bh6 13. Qe4 Bxe3+ 14. fxe3 { You traded the dangerous bishop and White's e-pawns are doubled, but the extra pawn stays (about +1.3). }) 6... e6 { 6...e6 is the only fully sound reply (Bd7, Qa5+, a6 and Rb8 all run into tactics along that same diagonal or on a7/d5). Black unpins next move with ...Bd7 or ...a6 once the structure is clarified. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), the position's main tabiya after this recapture.] } 7. O-O $10 { White castles and keeps the pin, so ...Nxd4 is still impossible. The idea is Bxc6+ to double your c-pawns, then c4 to hit d5. } (7. Qg4 Bd7 8. Nc3 Qc7 9. O-O a6 10. Bxc6 Qxc6 11. Re1 Ne7 { Qg4 eyes g7 and discourages ...Ng6 ideas, but you unpin with ...Bd7 and trade the pinning bishop; level (about 0.0). }) 7... a6 { You unpin and ask the bishop what it wants. } 8. Bxc6+ bxc6 { You get the bishop pair and the b-file as compensation for the doubled pawns. } 9. c4 Ne7 10. Nc3 Nf5 { Gains time on the queen. } 11. Qf4 h6 { The game is level (about +0.2): White has more space, you have the bishops and the plan of ...Be7 and castling. }) 5... Nc6 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE THIS IF you want solid piece play with ...e6 and ...Nc6 and no early queen adventures: the lines here are positional and fairly forgiving. It covers 6.c4, 6.Nxc6, 6.f4 and 6.Bb5, the tries White uses to avoid the Qxd4 structures. Risk level: LOW to MEDIUM. After 5.Nxd4 Nc6 White chooses between hitting the centre with c4, simplifying with Nxc6 or testing Black with f4/Bb5. } 6. c4 { 6.c4 is White's most ambitious try, grabbing extra central space; Black continues with normal development (...Nxd4, ...Qxd4-style exchanges, or ...e6 and ...Bc5 depending on move order) and the position stays balanced. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.4-0.5 across both main recapture tries), the same objective assessment as 4...Bg4, just a sharper, more committal structure.] } (6. Bb5 { 6.Bb5 - pins the knight as in the 5.Qxd4 line, but here there is no queen on d4 to win back material, so the position stays calmer. } 6... Bd7 { 6...Bd7 unpins directly and prepares ...a6 or ...Nxd4 next; this is the natural, fully sound reply here (the long-diagonal tactic that refutes Bd7 in the 5.Qxd4 line doesn't exist in this version). [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.3), a second sound try alongside the existing 6.c4 line in this chapter.] } 7. Nxc6 $10 { Your bishop has unpinned the knight, so White cashes in the knight for the one on c6. After ...Bxc6 White aims at quick development with Bd3 and Qe2. } (7. Bxc6 bxc6 8. O-O e6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.16).] }) (7. e6 fxe6 8. Bxc6 bxc6 9. Qe2 Nf6 10. O-O { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.11).] }) 7... Bxc6 8. Bd3 (8. Bxc6+ bxc6 9. O-O e6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.03).] }) 8... d4 { You gain space and cramp the white knight. } 9. Qe2 Bxg2 { The pawn grab hits the rook on h1. } (9... e6 10. O-O Qd5 11. f3 Bc5 12. Kh1 Ne7 13. c4 Qd7 14. Nd2 a5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.12).] }) 10. f3 { The rook is not lost: if you take it, you walk into a check. } 10... Bh3 (10... Bxh1? 11. Bb5+ Qd7 12. Bxd7+ Kxd7 13. Qb5+ Ke6 { The bishop on h1 is trapped and your king is exposed, so this is losing. }) 11. Nd2 g6 { This blocks the bishop's diagonal to h7. You are a pawn up but your bishop is out of play and White is ahead in development (about +0.2). }) (6. Nxc6 { 6.Nxc6 trades off immediately rather than retreating or developing, damaging Black's queenside pawn structure in exchange for giving up a tempo and the bishop pair. } 6... bxc6 { 6...bxc6 is forced; the resulting doubled c-pawns are a long-term structural concession, but Black gets the half-open b-file and quick development to compensate. } 7. Bd3 { 7.Bd3 develops naturally, eyeing the e4-h7 diagonal and preparing to meet ...Qb6 or ...Ba6 ideas. } 7... e6 { 7...e6 solidifies the center and prepares ...Ne7/...Bd6 development. } 8. O-O { 8.O-O gets the king safe before deciding on c4 or Nd2 plans. } (8. Nd2 { 9.Nd2 reroutes the knight toward f3 or c4 one move earlier than the main 9.O-O line. } 8... Ne7 9. Qg4 Qb6 10. O-O { ...and Black develops comfortably. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), transposing into similar structures to the main line a tempo earlier.] } 10... Ba6 $10 { Your bishop attacks Bd3, so White has to decide whether to allow the trade of the light-squared bishops that would relieve you on the kingside. } (10... c5?! 11. b3 Nf5 12. Re1 g6 13. c3 Be7 14. Nf3 h5 15. Qh3 { Too slow: ...c5 weakens d5 and the g6/h5 pawns loosen your king, while the queen swings to h3 to hit h5 (about +0.7). }) 11. c4 Ng6 { Your knight hits e5 and makes the Bxg6 trade natural. } 12. Bxg6 hxg6 { The doubled g-pawns are a price you pay for the open h-file against the king on g1. } 13. b3 Qb4 { The queen hits d2 and prepares ...dxc4. } 14. Nf3 dxc4 15. Ng5 { The knight eyes e6 and f7, but the position is level (about 0.0) and your h-file gives you counterplay. }) 8... Ne7 { 8...Ne7 heads for g6 or f5, a flexible square that also eyes the long diagonal via ...Ba6. } 9. Nd2 { 9.Nd2 reroutes the knight toward f3 or e4, keeping c4 in reserve. } (9. Re1 { 11.Re1 centralizes the rook immediately, preparing e5-e6 ideas or supporting a knight on e5 later. } 9... a5 10. c3 Ng6 11. Nd2 { ...and White keeps a genuine, lasting initiative. [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+0.6), the most ambitious try at this node and one Black needs to meet precisely.] } 11... Bc5 $10 { You develop actively: the bishop aims at f2 and you prepare ...O-O and the ...f6 lever against e5. } (11... Be7 12. Qg4 h5 13. Qg3 Bh4 14. Qe3 Qg5 15. Qxg5 Bxg5 16. Bxg6 { Here the queen on g4 eyes g6 and your h-pawn, and you end up slightly worse (about +0.7). }) 12. Nf3 O-O 13. Bc2 Qb6 { Your queen joins the attack on f2 and b2. } 14. Nd4 f6 15. exf6 Rxf6 16. Bg5 { White's bishop hits your rook, but the position stays balanced with a small White edge (about +0.4). }) (9. Be3 { 11.Be3 develops toward c5/a7 immediately, supporting a later Qe2 and f4 plan. } 9... a5 (9... Ng6 10. f4 Be7 11. Nd2 (11. c4 O-O 12. Qc2 d4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.24).] }) 11... c5 12. c3 (12. c4 d4 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.5).] }) 12... O-O 13. Nf3 a5 14. Qe2 Qb6 15. Rad1 Ba6 16. Bxa6 Qxa6 17. Qxa6 Rxa6 18. f5 exf5 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.5).] }) 10. Qe2 Ng6 11. f4 { ...and White keeps a comfortable, lasting edge. [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+0.5), a sound and ambitious alternative to Nd2 here.] } 11... Be7 $10 { White's f4 prepares f5, which would kick your knight on g6 and open the kingside, so you develop and aim to castle. } (11... c5?! 12. Bb5+ Bd7 13. Nc3 Rb8 14. Rad1 d4 15. Bc1 Be7 16. a4 { The immediate break lets White play Bb5+ with a gain of time and then pile up on d4, so White keeps a pleasant game. }) 12. Nd2 c5 { The break hits d4 and cuts down the scope of Bd3. } 13. Rad1 O-O 14. Rf3 Qe8 15. Rh3 h6 16. Qh5 { White lifts the rook to h3 and brings the queen to h5 to attack your king, but your pieces cover the kingside and the edge is small (about +0.3). }) (9. f4 Nf5 { 9.f4 runs into ...Nf5! and ...Qb6+ with a clear plus for Black. [Engine verdict: about -0.5.] } 10. Bxf5 Qb6+ 11. Kh1 exf5 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.6).] }) 9... a5 { 9...a5 starts queenside expansion and prepares ...Ba6 to trade off White's good bishop. [Engine verdict: roughly equal throughout (+0.35 to +0.45), a genuine third try for White alongside the existing c4 and Bb5 lines in this chapter, and the one most frequently recommended across the source material at this exact tabiya.] } 10. Nf3 { 13.Nf3 heads back toward d4/e5 rather than staying on d2, adding pressure to the center. } (10. Qe2 { 13.Qe2 supports a later e5-e6 break or f4 push rather than routing the knight to f3 immediately. } 10... Ng6 11. g3 Be7 12. h4 { ...and the position remains balanced with chances for both sides. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5), a sound and slightly more ambitious alternative to Nf3 here.] } 12... O-O $10 { Castle before the h-pawn gets going. White's plan is h5, which kicks your knight off g6, and then Nf3-e5 for a kingside squeeze. } (12... f5 13. Nf3 O-O 14. c4 d4 15. h5 Nh8 16. c5 Bxc5 17. Bg5 { Starting the break at once is too loose: Bg5 hits your queen and the knight on h8 is stranded (about +0.5). }) 13. h5 Nh8 { The knight has few squares, but it heads for f7 once you break with ...f5. } 14. Nf3 f5 15. exf6 Bxf6 16. Bf4 N