5,Bd1,Bf3][%cal Bb8c6,Bc6e5,Bg4d1] } 6. c3 $14 { White has two ideas here: try to keep the c-pawn with b4?!, or simply make room for the queen with Qb3. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 6... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 7... a6! $14 { Prevents white from playing this pesky move Bb5! An attempt to try and eliminate the knight. } { [%csl Ba6][%cal Ba7a6] } 8. Nbd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 8... Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bc6e5] } 9. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 9... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Be5c6] } 10. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 10... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 11. Rc1 { [%csl Bc1][%cal Ba1c1] } 11... Be7 { [%csl Be7,Bc5][%cal Bf8e7] } 12. b4 { [%csl Bb4,Bc5][%cal Bb2b4,Bb4c5] } 12... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 13. h3 { [%csl Bh4,Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 13... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5,Bb1][%cal Bg4f5,Bf5b1] } 14. Nd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bf3d4] } 14... Bg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf5g6] } 15. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be3f4] } 15... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bf6d7] } 16. N2f3 $14 { Biggest weakness of white's position is the b4 pawn. Without the b pawn white's queenside pawn structure would have been demolished. Black should target white's queenside and provoke white's pawns. } { [%csl Bf3,Bc3,Bb4,Bc5,Ba2][%cal Bd2f3] } 16... Re8 $14 { Your rook takes the e-file, where White's pieces crowd around e5, and you accept that the knight on c6 will be traded. } (16... Ndb8?! 17. a4 Bf6 18. Bg3 Re8 19. Re1 e5 20. Nc2 Bxc2 21. Rxc2 { Regrouping the knight loses time and a4 hits b5: you are worse (about +0.9). }) 17. Nxc6 bxc6 { Your pawns are doubled, but you get the b-file against the pawn on b4, which has no pawn neighbours to support it. } 18. Nd4 Qc8 19. c4 Bf6 20. Bd6 Ne5 21. cxd5 { White breaks with c4 and Bd6 takes the dark squares, so you are slightly worse (about +0.6) but your pieces are active. }) (5. c3 $10 { White defends the pawn to keep the centre together, though the centre could still be given up later. } 5... e6 { [%csl Bc5,Be3][%cal Bd4c5,Bc1e3] } 6. dxc5 $15 { White gives up the center, black has to eliminate white's knight or otherwise the e pawn would have a defender. } { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd4c5] } (6. Be3 $10 { Others prefer to keep things simple and would have decided to just stick to his opening fundamental and principle. } { [%csl Be3,Bd4][%cal Bc1e3] } 6... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 7. Nbd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 7... Nf5 { [%csl Bf5,Be3][%cal Be7f5,Bf5e3] } 8. Qa4+ { [%csl Ba4][%cal Bd1a4] } 8... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 9. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be3f4] } 9... Bh5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bg4h5] } 10. h3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 10... Bg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bh5g6] } 11. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 11... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 12. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 12... O-O $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.2).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 13. Rfc1 $15 { White's queen is a little offside on a4 after the earlier check, and the bishop on f4 supports e5. White puts a rook on the c-file, but you have the easier game (about -0.2). } (13. g4 Nh4 14. Nxh4 Bxh4 15. Be3 Be7 16. f4 f6 17. Nf3 c4 { Kicking the knight with g4 is the tempting try, but it weakens the kingside, and ...c4 gains space for you (about -0.2). }) 13... a6 { Takes b5 away and prepares ...Qb6. } 14. Qd1 f6 { The lever against e5. } 15. Bd3 Qb6 16. exf6 Bxf6 17. Bxf5 Bxf5 { The trades leave a balanced structure where you are slightly better. }) (6. Be2 { White develops the bishop to support the knight on f3 and prepare short castling, avoiding any tricks with the knight pinned. It is a common choice in practice. It means White wants to hold the center solidly and answer ...Bxf3 with the bishop. } 6... Bxf3 $15 { You trade the bishop for the knight, which removes a defender of the d4 pawn and of the e5 pawn. Your plan is to play ...Nc6, then take on d4 and use the queen on b6 to put pressure on the b2 pawn. Your structure is healthy, and White has nothing special. Avoid delaying the trade, because then White can play Ne5 or d4 with gain of time. } (6... Nc6 7. Nbd2 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bxe2 9. Nxc6 Bxd1 10. Nxd8 Rxd8 11. Kxd1 { This leads to a simple endgame that is equal, but it gives you less to play for. }) 7. Bxf3 Nc6 8. O-O cxd4 9. cxd4 Qb6 { Your queen attacks the d4 and b2 pawns at once. White has to decide how to defend. } 10. Be3 Qxb2 11. Nd2 { You have won a pawn but White has activity on the queenside, so be ready to return it or bring the queen back. Your plan is ...Nxd4 and castling, with your king safe. Overall: this line is slightly better for you. This is a position where you have grabbed the b2 pawn and White has more active pieces, so your queen on b2 is a target. Your plan: retreat the queen at once with ...Qb6 so it cannot be trapped by Rb1, then develop with ...Nge7 and ...Nf5, and castle short. White wants Rb1 or Nb3, hoping to win your queen or take compensation for the pawn. If White plays Rb1, drop the queen back to b6 or b4 and be ready to give the pawn back. The risk is greed: do not play ...Nxd4 when d4 is protected by the bishop on e3, because the tactics favour White. }) 6... Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 7. Qxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd1f3] } 7... Bxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 8. Qg3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bf3g3] } 8... Ne7 $10 { Black has to sacrifice the g pawn or otherwise Kf8 or g6 would have been a horrible move. } { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 9. Qxg7 $10 { Black temporarily gives up the pawn for rapid development; the e5-pawn is the key target. } { [%csl Bg7][%cal Bg3g7] } 9... Ng6 { [%csl Bg6,Bg7][%cal Be7g6] } 10. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 10... Bf8 { [%csl Bf8][%cal Bc5f8] } 11. Qf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg7f6] } 11... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 12. Qf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf6f3] } 12... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7,Be5][%cal Bd8c7,Bc7e5,Bg6e5] } 13. Bb5+ { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bd3b5] } 13... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6,Be5][%cal Bb8c6,Bc6e5,Bc7e5,Bg6e5] } 14. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 14... Nxe5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Be5][%cal Bg6e5] } 15. Qh5 $14 { Your knight on e5 hit the queen on f3, so it moves with a threat of its own: Qh5 attacks the knight and eyes h7. } (15. Bxc6+ bxc6 16. Qh5 Nd3 17. Be3 Qe5 18. Qe2 Bd6 19. g3 Qe4 { Giving up the bishop doubles your pawns but gives you active pieces; level (about -0.1). }) 15... Ng6 { Your knight retreats to g6, where the h- and f-pawns protect it. } 16. Be3 Qe5 { The queen centralises and hits b2. } 17. Qe2 Bg5 18. Nd2 Bxe3 19. fxe3 O-O { You gave up the bishop pair, but White's doubled e-pawns are a long-term weakness and your king is safe; White keeps a small edge (about +0.3). }) (5. Be2 { White simply develops and wants to answer ...Bxf3 with a bishop recapture, then play c4 to undermine your d5 pawn. It is very common in practice (played in about 279 thousand games) and avoids the sharper moves. For you it means you can pick your own set-up calmly. } 5... Nc6 $10 { You add pressure to d4 and e5, develop a piece and keep the option of ...e6. Your plan is to trade on d4, take on e2 when it helps, and use the light squares left by the exchanged bishops. Be careful with the d5 pawn: if White takes on d5 you want to recapture with the queen or the pawn without losing time. Avoid ...c4 here, which gives away your pressure on d4. } (5... e6 { This is also fine and keeps everything protected; White again plays c4 and you recapture with the queen. } 6. c4 Nc6 7. cxd5 Qxd5 8. Nc3 Qd7) 6. c4 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Bxe2 { You swap off White's bishop so that his king has to recapture. } 8. Kxe2 e6 { Now the king is awkward and you have a solid centre. } 9. Qa4 Qd7 { This defends the pinned knight and keeps the c6 piece safe. } 10. cxd5 { Overall: the position is balanced, your king will castle long or short, and White's king on e2 gives you targets. This is a semi-open position where your pawns are on c5, d5 and e6 against White's pawns on d4 and e5, with White's king on e2 and no castling for him. You have the more comfortable game because his king is awkward. Plan: recapture on d5 with ...exd5, trade on d4 with ...Nxd4 only when it works, then develop with ...Nf6, ...Bc5 and castle short, aiming at his king along the e-file. White wants to hide the king and play Nc3 or Rd1 with pressure on d5. The risk is the pin on c6: if the knight moves carelessly, Qxd7 wins material. If White plays Nc3 first, recapture ...exd5 anyway. }) (5. h3 { White asks your bishop a direct question: trade on f3 or retreat. He is happy to give up the knight, hoping to recapture with the queen and then use the open lines in the centre. } 5... Bxf3 $15 { Trading is the right answer. After Qxf3 White's queen is out early and you can take on d4. Your plan is ...cxd4, ...Qd7 to stop Qb3 from hitting b7, then ...Nc6 and ...e6, followed by short castling. Avoid retreating with the bishop, which loses time and a good minor piece. } (5... Bd7?! 6. dxc5 e6 7. b4 a5 8. b5 Bxc5 9. Bd3 Ne7 10. a4 { The retreat is clearly worse because White takes space and you have given up the better bishop. }) 6. Qxf3 cxd4 7. Qb3 Qd7 { You defend b7 and stay compact. } 8. Qg3 Nc6 9. Nd2 e6 { White's queen wanders around, but your pieces are fine and you have the sounder pawn structure. You develop the bishop to e7 and castle short. } 10. Nf3 { If White plays 8.Nc3 or 8.Nd2 instead of the queen move, you reply with ...e6 and ...Nc6 and take the pawn on d4 once it is safe. The pawn on d4 will often fall because White has no easy way to defend it. Overall: you hold a slight edge, with no weaknesses. This is a semi-open position in which you hold a small but real edge, with pawns on d5, e6 and the tension in the centre; you will probably castle short once g7 is safe. Your plan: protect the d4 pawn with ...Nc6, develop ...Nge7 and ...Nf5 to hit the queen, then ...Bc5 and ...Qd7. White wants to take on d4, play Bd3 and use his queen on g3 against g7 and h7. The risk is leaving g7 loose: do not let Qxg7 come with tempo, and do not let a knight take on d4 for free. If White uses Nd2 instead, still play ...e6 and ...Nc6. }) 5... cxd4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc5d4] } 6. cxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc4d5] } 6... Qxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd8d5] } 7. Nc3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 7... Bxf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg4f3] } 8. Nxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc3d5] } 8... Bxd1 { [%csl Gd1][%cal Gf3d1] } 9. Nc7+ { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gd5c7] } 9... Kd7 $10 { White's knight is trapped. Black gets two pieces for a rook and a pawn, which is a good exchange for Black. } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Ge8d7] } 10. Nxa8 { [%csl Ga8][%cal Gc7a8] } 10... Bc2 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4). Notable features: White is a pawn up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side.] [%csl Gc2][%cal Gd1c2] } 11. Bd2 $14 { The knight on a8 is stuck in the corner, so White first develops and then brings the rook to c1, which lines up against your bishop on c2. } (11. e6+?! fxe6 12. Bb5+ Nc6 13. Bf4 Nh6 14. Rc1 d3 15. Nc7 e5 { Throwing in e6+ opens lines for your pieces and is no better than equal for White. }) 11... d3 { The pawn protects the bishop on c2 and cramps White. } 12. Rc1 e6 13. Nc7 { The knight gets out via c7, because taking it loses your bishop. } 13... f6 (13... Kxc7? 14. Bxd3 { The rook on c1 pins your bishop on c2 to the king on c7, so it is lost. }) 14. Nb5 Nc6 15. h4 fxe5 { The knight has escaped and the material is balanced, so the game is dynamic with a small White edge (about +0.6). } *


