1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The So/Carlsen sidelines (4.Nd2 and 4.c4) both aim to develop more flexibly than the committal main lines, so the resulting structure (still the Advance Variation's locked center, but with White's knight or c-pawn placed differently) asks Black to react rather than follow a fixed plan. The half-open c-file for Black is the constant across both sidelines, build pressure against White's queenside there regardless of which exact move order White chooses, and treat the specific knight/pawn placement as a detail to adapt to rather than a reason to change the overall strategy.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.3 to +0.6, a small White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Bxc2? on moves 4 to 6 loses material (about 4.1); ...Qxd4? on moves 7 to 8 loses material (about 6.3); 11...Nd7? (about 1.6; prefer ...Bxc5); 11...Qc7? (about 1.6; prefer ...Bxc5); 13...Qd8? (about 1.0; prefer ...Qxc5); 12...Ke7? (about 0.8; prefer ...Nc6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 13.g4? Bxd4 (costs White about 1.4; engine-found, not in the chapter tree); 15.O-O? Nxe5 (costs White about 1.0; engine-found, not in the chapter tree).
Chapter #20 continues the Advance Variation. Other Advance Variation chapters: #15, #16, #17, #18, #21. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #6 and #8. Model game: #7. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 3... Bf5 $14 { The standard Short Variation setup covered in the main Advance Variation chapters. This chapter covers two respectable independent tries White has instead of the main 4.Nf3: 4.Nd2 and the immediate 4.c4. BLACK'S PLAN against both: these sidelines avoid committing the king's knight to f3 immediately, but they don't change Black's fundamentals - develop the e7-knight (toward f5 or g6), challenge the center with ...c5 (or accept a favorable structure if White trades on d5/c4 first), and use the extra tempo White spends on these knight/pawn maneuvers to finish development slightly ahead of schedule. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 4. Nd2 $14 { An idea popularized at the top level by Wesley So. Rather than the natural-looking 4.Nf3, White routes the knight to b3 instead, eyeing c5 and d4 while keeping f3 open for the other knight or a future f4 push. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } (4. c4 $14 { A pure, immediate challenge to the center, associated at the top level with Magnus Carlsen. White doesn't bother developing the king's knight first at all. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 4... dxc4 $14 { Simplest and most reliable response - just take the pawn. White has to spend real time winning it back, and Black gets a free, comfortable structure in return. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd5c4] } (4... e6 $14 { Keeping the central tension instead, with a later ...c5 break in mind, is equally playable - a matter of style rather than necessity. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 5. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 5... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 6. a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba2a3] } 6... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 7. cxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc4d5] } 7... Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be7d5] } 8. Nxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc3d5] } 8... cxd5 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.0 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nc3 c5 Nf3 cxd4 Nxd4 O-O-O } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc6d5] } 9. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 9... Bxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf5d3] } 10. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd1d3] } 10... Qa5+ { [%csl Ba5][%cal Bd8a5] } 11. Bd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } 11... Qb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Ba5b6] } 12. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 12... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 13. Qg3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bd3g3] } 13... Qxb2 $10 { Simple, solid development pays off here: Black trades down comfortably and even grabs a loose pawn on b2 once the position opens up - a good demonstration that this sideline carries no real venom once Black knows the basic setup. } { [%csl Bb2][%cal Bb6b2] } 14. Bc3 $10 { The bishop hits your queen on b2, and the point is to win the g7 pawn back in return, with your queen running around (about 0.2). } (14. Qc3 Qxc3 15. Nxc3 Nb8 16. h4 Nc6 17. Ne2 h5 18. Rb1 b6 { Hitting the queen looks natural, but after the queen trade White has nothing for the missing pawn and you are clearly better (about -2.0). }) 14... Qc2 { Your queen steps aside and keeps pressure on c3. } 15. Qxg7 Rf8 { The rook on h8 was attacked, and f8 is a safe square. } 16. Qg4 Qg6 { Offer a queen trade to ease the pressure from the open position. } 17. Qh3 Rg8 18. O-O Qe4 { The queen is centralised and the material is back to balance, so you can play on freely. }) 5. Bxc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 5... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 6. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 6... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 7. Bb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc4b3] } 7... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 8. Nbc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 8... Nb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd7b6] } 9. Ng3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Be2g3] } 9... Bg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf5g6] } 10. h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 10... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 11. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 11... Bh7 { [%csl Bh7][%cal Bg6h7] } 12. h5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh4h5] } 12... a5 { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 13. a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba2a3] } 13... Ned5 $14 { A perfectly normal, comfortable structure. White's small lead in development is balanced out by Black's extra pawn and completely sound position - the knight lands on the excellent d5 outpost right on schedule. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be7d5] } 14. Ba2 $14 { The bishop steps back because ...a4 was about to harass it on b3. From a2 it still eyes e6 and f7. White's small edge (about 0.8) comes from the h5 pawn, the knight on g3 and the d4 centre, so play accurately. } (14. Re1 Be7 15. Qg4 g5 16. hxg6 Bxg6 17. Nh5 a4 18. Ng7+ Kd7 { Re1 and Qg4 hunt the king that has not castled yet. You survive, but it is a more uncomfortable version (about 0.7). }) 14... g5 { Clamp down on the h5 pawn and take f4 away from the knight on g3. } 15. Bb1 Bxb1 { White offers the trade of your best bishop, and you accept. } 16. Rxb1 Bg7 17. f4 Nxf4 18. Bxf4 gxf4 { White opens the position with f4 while your king is still in the centre, and the resulting tactics keep White somewhat ahead (about 0.8). Avoid extra pawn grabs and finish your development. }) (4. Bd3 { 4.Bd3 offers the immediate trade of light-squared bishops before developing anything else, a distinct system from the Nf3-first approach the chapter otherwise builds around. } 4... Bxd3 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 Bxf5 e6 Bd3 Qxd4 } 5. Qxd3 e6 6. Nf3 { ...reaching a simplified, very level position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), a genuine independent fourth-move system alongside Nf3, Nc3, Nd2, h4 and g4.] } 6... c5 $10 { Challenge d4 now, before White completes development with c3 and Nbd2. The pawn break is the thematic way to free your position (about 0.1). } (6... Qa5+ 7. Nbd2 Qa6 8. c4 Bb4 9. O-O Ne7 10. Qc2 Bxd2 11. Nxd2 { Another sound way to equalise (about 0.3). }) 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nc6 { Attack the knight that is cementing the centre. } 9. f4 Nxd4 { White supports e5 with f4, so trade the knight before it settles. } 10. Bxd4 Ne7 { The bishop aims at g7, but ...Ne7 covers it and prepares ...Nf5 to hit the bishop. } 11. Nd2 { White has a space edge on e5, but you have easy development and the c-file, so the position is level. }) (4. Ne2 { 4.Ne2 develops flexibly, keeping the option of either Ng3 or Nf4 depending on Black's setup. } 4... e6 5. Ng3 Bg6 { ...and Black equalizes comfortably with normal development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a quiet, sound try that sidesteps all the sharper fourth-move theory.] } 6. c3 $10 { White builds a quiet centre: c3 protects d4 and gets ready for Bd3 or Bb5+. The knight on g3 also eyes h5 and f5 (about 0.0). } (6. h4 h5 7. c4 c5 8. Bg5 Be7 9. cxd5 exd5 10. Bb5+ Nc6 { The sharper try is h4-h5 to trap your bishop. Answer with ...h5, and you stand fine (about -0.1). }) 6... c5 { Strike at d4 before White has castled. } 7. Bb5+ Nc6 8. O-O Rc8 9. Be3 a6 10. Bxc6+ Rxc6 { White parts with the bishop and your rook lands actively on c6. Your pieces are better placed and there is nothing to fear. }) (4. c3 { 4.c3 supports the center quietly before committing the king's knight anywhere, keeping options for both Nf3 and f4 later. } 4... e6 5. Be3 Qb6 6. Qb3 { ...reaching a level, flexible position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a sound, calm fourth-move try alongside the chapter's sharper main lines.] } 6... Nd7 $10 { Qb3 hits b7 and offers a queen swap that would leave you passive. Keep the queens on: the knight supports ...c5 and ...h5, and your queen stays active on b6 (about 0.0). } (6... h5 7. Be2 Nd7 8. Nd2 h4 9. Ngf3 Ne7 10. O-O h3 11. g3 { Playing ...h5 first is just as good and cramps the kingside immediately (about 0.0). }) 7. Nd2 h5 { The plan is ...h5-h4, which stops White from expanding on the kingside and makes your bishop safe on f5. } 8. Be2 h4 9. Ngf3 Ne7 10. O-O Ng6 { The knight on g6 pressures e5 and also guards the h4 pawn. } 11. Ne1 { White shuffles back to prepare f4. You have a sound structure and no weaknesses to worry about. }) (4. Be3 { 4.Be3 develops toward c5/d4 immediately, preparing a later Nd2-Nf3 setup without blocking the c-pawn. } 4... Qb6 5. Qc1 e6 6. c4 { ...and the position stays balanced. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), a sound fourth-move alternative.] } 6... Nd7 $10 { Be3 and Qc1 exist only to cover b2 against your queen, so White has already spent time before c4 hits d5. Developing the knight supports d5 and prepares the usual ...f6 lever against e5 (about 0.0). } (6... dxc4 7. Nd2 Qd8 8. Nxc4 b5 9. Nd2 Ne7 10. Ne2 Nd5 11. Ng3 { Grabbing c4 also works and the knight lands on d5, but White gets time against your queenside pawns, so it is a shade less clean (about 0.2). }) 7. Nf3 f6 { Strike at the head of the pawn chain right away. } 8. exf6 Ngxf6 9. c5 Qd8 { c5 gains a tempo on your queen, but it also gives up control of d4 and b4, so the retreat costs you little. } 10. Be2 Be7 11. Nc3 { You are fully developed and can castle, while c5 can later be undermined with ...b6. }) 4... e6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 5. Nb3 $14 { The point of the maneuver - the knight eyes c5 and d4 and makes Black's usual ...c5 break slightly harder to execute for free, since ...c5 can sometimes be met by Nxc5 tricks depending on the position. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gd2b3] } 5... Qb6 $14 { Hit straight back at the undefended b2 pawn and develop with tempo - the most natural and testing response. } { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gd8b6] } 6. Be2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gf1e2] } 6... Ne7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gg8e7] } 7. c3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 7... h6 { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gh7h6] } 8. a4 { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga2a4] } 8... a5 { [%csl Ga5][%cal Ga7a5] } 9. Nc5 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gb3c5] } 9... Nc8 { [%csl Gc8][%cal Ge7c8] } 10. Nd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gc5d3] } 10... c5 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc6c5] } 11. Nxc5 { [%csl Rc5][%cal Rd3c5] } 11... Bxc5 { [%csl Rc5][%cal Rf8c5] } 12. Bb5+ { [%csl Gb5][%cal Ge2b5] } 12... Nc6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 13. dxc5 { [%csl Rc5][%cal Rd4c5] } 13... Qxc5 { [%csl Rc5][%cal Rb6c5] } 14. Ne2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gg1e2] } 14... O-O $14 { A realistic, fully engine-verified continuation showing the whole point of Black's setup: even though White's knight maneuver (Nb3-c5-d3) looks annoying, Black simply castles into safety and keeps the position balanced with no real weaknesses - exactly what the 4.Nd2 sideline is trying, and failing, to avoid. } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 15. Be3 $10 { White develops with tempo on your queen. The bishop prepares f4 to hold e5, then Ng3 to chase your bishop off f5 (about 0.3). } (15. Ng3 d4 16. Nxf5 exf5 17. O-O dxc3 18. bxc3 Rd8 19. Qf3 Nxe5 { Hitting the bishop at once is tempting, but ...d4 opens the centre and you win the e5 pawn back (about -0.2). }) 15... Qe7 { Step out of the bishop's attack and keep an eye on e5. } 16. f4 f6 { Your thematic break: hit the e5 chain before White consolidates with Ng3 and castling. } 17. exf6 Rxf6 18. Ng3 Bg6 19. O-O Nd6 { Your knight finally reaches d6 with a threat against the b5 bishop. Your king is safe and your pieces cover each other, so White has found no target. } *


