1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Tal Variation (4.h4) and Bayonet Attack (4.g4) both try to win the bishop-vs-pawn-storm race before Black can consolidate, similar in spirit to Chapter #16's Van der Wiel idea but reached one move earlier. Black's queen often swings to a6 or a5 to create counterplay on the queenside while the kingside pawn storm develops, and the half-open c-file remains the main lever. As with the other sharp Advance sidelines, precise move-order knowledge matters more than general structural understanding, White is trying to win a tempo race, so Black's countermeasures have to be equally concrete.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.2 to +0.3, a small White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd2+? on moves 7 to 8 loses material (about 5.9); ...Nxd4? on moves 10 to 12 loses material (about 5.3); 7...Qb5? (about 1.4; prefer ...e6); 13...Be7? (about 1.0; prefer ...Bxc5); 7...Rh6? (about 1.0; prefer ...e6); 14...Qb7? (about 0.9; prefer ...Nc6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: no White mistake costing 0.5 or more was found along this line.
Chapter #21 continues the Advance Variation. Other Advance Variation chapters: #15, #16, #17, #18, #19. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #4 and #7. Model games: #10, #12. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 3... Bf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 4. h4 $14 { The Tal Variation - named for Mikhail Tal, who tried this exact pawn thrust against Botvinnik in their 1961 World Championship match. This is a distinct system from the Bayonet Attack covered later in this chapter: here White pushes the h-pawn FIRST while the bishop is still allowed to sit on f5, rather than hitting the bishop immediately with g4. A serious, popular try in its own right, not just a minor sideline. } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gh2h4] } (4. g4 $14 { The Bayonet Attack - a completely different try from the Tal Variation above. Instead of preparing the pawn storm with h4 first, White goes straight for the point: a pawn on g4 attacks the Bf5 bishop directly (gxf5 is a real threat next move), forcing an immediate decision. Black has three reasonable retreats - 4...Be4, 4...Bg6, and 4...Bd7 - each covered below. BLACK'S GENERAL PLAN across this whole branch: none of the three retreats loses material or the game by force; the practical difference between them is how much central/kingside space Black concedes versus how active the bishop stays, and whether Black is willing to meet White's typical e5-e6!? pawn sacrifice by capturing (...fxe6, opening the f-file for a rook) or declining it (...Qd6, keeping the structure intact at the cost of a tempo). } { [%csl Rf5][%cal Rg2g4] } 4... Be4 $14 { 4...Be4 keeps the bishop active and centralized rather than retreating passively. The point is that after 5.f3, the bishop is kicked again, but Black has already provoked White into weakening the e3-square and slightly loosening the kingside.  WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 gxf5 Qxd4 Nf3 Qe4+ } (4... Bg6 $10 { 4...Bg6 is the most natural retreat - the bishop stays on the same diagonal and simply steps back one square, without inviting the further f3 tempo-gain that 4...Be4 allows.  WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qa4 gxf5 Qxd4 Nf3 Qe4+ } 5. e6 $10 { The same thematic sacrifice as in the 4...Be4 line, offered a tempo earlier since Black never played ...Be4-g6 in two moves. } { [%csl Ye6][%cal Ye5e6] } (5. h4 { White wants to harass your bishop on g6 and open the h-file or push h5 to trap it. It is a sharp, very common idea (played in about 815 thousand games), so you must know the answer. } 5... h5 { This is the key reply. It stops h5 for good, so the bishop on g6 is not threatened, and it invites White to open the position where your pieces are ready. If White takes on h5 you recapture with the bishop or the knight, and if g4 goes forward you take it. Your plan afterwards is ...Nh6 to reach f5 or g4, ...Nd7 and ...e6, and keep your king flexible. The position is balanced. } (5... h6?! { A natural but inferior move. After 6.h5 the bishop is driven to e4 and the rook on h1 becomes active, giving White the better game. Avoid pushing the pawn only one square. } 6. h5 Be4 7. Rh3 e6 8. Nd2 Bh7 9. Nb3 Nd7 10. Be3) 6. e6 { White opens lines at the cost of the e-pawn. Do not be afraid; just take what is offered. } 6... hxg4 7. Qxg4 Nh6 { The knight attacks the queen with gain of time and heads for the f5 square. } 8. exf7+ Bxf7 9. Qh3 Nd7 { Your pieces are all developed: the bishop on f7 guards, and you can bring the knight to f5 and play ...e6 or ...Nb6. Beware of the queen alone going after pawns: stay calm and complete development. } 10. Nf3 { Overall: you have given a pawn back but your structure is healthy and your pieces are active, so the position is balanced. Avoid the tempting 5...Qa5+, which allows 6.Nc3 and gives White a clear edge. This is an open, gambit-style position where material is level, White's king is still on e1 and his queen on h3 is exposed. Your pieces are active with Nd7, Nh6 and the bishop on f7. Your plan: play ...Nf5 to hit the queen and d4, then ...e6, ...Be7 and ...Qc7, and castle long when safe. White wants Bd3 and Ne5 to trade your active pieces, and to open the h-file with h5. The mistake is grabbing pawns with your queen and neglecting development. If White plays Bd3, meet it with ...e6 and keep trading the most dangerous attackers. }) (5. f4 { White supports the e5 pawn and prepares f5 to trap your bishop. It is very common in practice (played in about 204 thousand games). For you it means the bishop on g6 can be harassed by f5, so you must react precisely. } 5... e6 $10 { This supports d5 and prepares ...Be4 or ...h5. Your plan is ...Be4 to avoid being kicked, then ...h5 to undermine g4. Keep your bishop active and castle long or short later. Avoid ...h5 immediately, because f5 would then trap the bishop on h7 and leave you short of squares. If White ever delays f5, you can play ...c5 and attack d4 at the right moment, trading White's centre for activity. Your knights go to d7 or c6 and f5 or h6 at the right time. } (5... h5?! 6. f5 Bh7 7. h3 e6 8. Nf3 { This is too slow, since after f5 your bishop is stuck on h7 and White is the one with the initiative. }) 6. h4 Be4 { The bishop escapes. } 7. Nf3 h5 { This breaks open the g-file. } 8. Nbd2 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 hxg4 { Overall: the position is dynamic, with equal chances, and your task is to keep the bishop active and strike at the centre with ...c5 once the king side opens. } 10. Qxg4 { This is an open, tense position after the bayonet g4 line: you have an open h-file with a rook on h8, and White's king is stretched. Your plan: develop with ...Ne7 and ...Nd7, then strike with ...c5 against d4, and put pressure on the h4 pawn with ...Rh8. Your king can go long or stay for now. White wants to play f5 to open the centre and bring his queen to g4 and g7. Meet f5 with ...exf5 and the queen. The risk is leaving g7 and the h-file loose and grabbing h4 hastily. If White plays f5 first, simply recapture and pile up on d4. }) 5... fxe6 $14 { Accepting again - the same kind of lasting compensation (extra pawn, open f-file) as before. } (5... Qd6 $14 { Declining again, for the same reasons as in the 4...Be4 branch. } 6. exf7+ { [%cal Ge6f7] } 6... Bxf7 7. Nc3 $14 { A calm, balanced position - Black's king can go either side depending on how the game develops, and the extra tempo compared with the 4...Be4 move order means Black is, if anything, slightly more comfortable here. } 7... e5 $10 { The central push takes space and prepares ...e4 to cramp the knight. Your king can still go either way (about 0.3). } (7... Nf6 8. Qe2 Nbd7 9. f4 g6 10. Bh3 Bg7 11. Nf3 Ne4 12. O-O { Developing the knight is also fine (about 0.3). }) 8. Nf3 e4 9. Ne5 Be6 { You develop and cover your pieces. The knight is strong on e5, and Bf4 adds an x-ray against your queen. } 10. Bf4 Qd8 { Step off the pinned diagonal before White gains a tempo. } 11. g5 Bd6 12. Bh3 { White has the bishop pair, but you have a spacious position and no weaknesses. The knight on e5 can be challenged by ...Nd7. }) 6. h4 { [%cal Gh2h4] } 6... h5 { [%cal Gh7h5] } 7. Bd3 { [%cal Gf1d3] } 7... Bxd3 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.0 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Bxd3 Bxa5 Ba6 g5 b6 } 8. Qxd3 { WATCH OUT: gxh5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.9 from White's own side of the board), after: Bf5 Nf3 Nf6 Ne5 c5 dxc5 } 8... hxg4 $14 { Here Black deviates from the quiet recapture pattern and grabs the loose g4-pawn instead of trading on d3 passively - a sharper try that holds up to engine scrutiny, though it invites some temporary queen activity from White. } 9. Qg6+ $14 { A forcing check that looks dangerous but leads nowhere concrete - Black's king is perfectly safe on d7 for the few moves it takes to consolidate, and White's queen ends up doing all the work alone. } { [%csl Yd7][%cal Ye8d7] } 9... Kd7 10. Qxg4 Nf6 11. Qh3 $10 { Black has weathered the checks and stands at least equal, with an extra pawn and a perfectly safe king - a good demonstration that the Bayonet Attack's tactics need to be calculated precisely, not feared on reputation alone. Engine-verified throughout. } 11... c5 $10 { The right idea. You open the centre while your king is on d7, and the pawn break gives the knight on c6 a square and your king room (about 0.2). } (11... g5 12. Bxg5 Qb6 13. Nd2 Qxb2 14. Rb1 Qxc2 15. Rxb7+ Kc8 16. Rb3 { The pawn grab opens lines and White's bishop gets in with tempo. This leaves White clearly better (about 1.1). }) 12. Nf3 Nc6 13. dxc5 Qa5+ { Check, which forces White to block and wins a tempo. } 14. Nbd2 g6 { This takes f5 and h5 away from White's knights. } 15. Ng5 Nd4 { The knight on d4 shields e6, hits c2 and holds the position together. } 16. Qd3 { You have a very solid position and White has nothing concrete. }) (4... Bd7 $14 { 4...Bd7 is the most modest retreat - the bishop gives up its active diagonal entirely and tucks in next to the king, but in exchange Black avoids all the immediate tactics of the other two retreats and keeps the position simple. } 5. Nc3 { [%cal Gb1c3] } (5. f4 { White backs up the e5 pawn with a second pawn and plans Nf3, Be3 and a knight or bishop to a good square, keeping a big grip on space. The cost is that the king side is stretched: the f4 and g4 pawns can become targets and d4 is guarded only by pieces. For you this means one thing: attack the base of the chain at once. The move is common in practice (played in about 106 thousand games). } 5... c5 $15 { This is the thematic break: you hit d4 before White finishes development. After 6.dxc5 you answer ...e6, rebuilding a solid centre and preparing ...Bxc5 to win the pawn back with a good bishop. Your plan is bishop to c5, knight to c6 or e7, and ...h5 to question the g4 pawn. If White declines with 6.Nf3, just keep adding pressure with ...Nc6. } (5... e6 6. Nf3 h5 7. g5 Ne7 { This is playable but slower: White gets Nf3 and g5 in without being disturbed and you are the one who has to defend a cramped position instead of striking at d4. }) 6. dxc5 e6 7. Be3 h5 { White covers d4 but you do not mind, because ...h5 attacks g4. If White ignores it, ...hxg4 opens the h-file for your rook. } 8. g5 d4 { Now your pawn kicks the bishop and the c5 pawn is doomed. After 9.Bf2 Bxc5 your bishop eyes f2, and you follow with ...Ne7 and ...Nc6. Avoid dropping back with ...e5 ideas; the point is to keep White's pawns overextended. Overall: White has more space but a loose pawn chain, and you are slightly better. } 9. Bf2 Bxc5 10. Nf3 { This is an open, sharp position with a loose white pawn chain on e5, f4 and g5, against your active bishops on c5 and d7 and a pawn on d4. You are a little better. Your plan: defend d4 with ...Nc6, then play ...Nge7 and ...Qb6, and castle long once the queen has moved. White wants to win d4 with Nxd4 and Bxd4, and use his space on the kingside with h4. The risk is that d4 is attacked twice and defended once, so add a defender at once before anything else. If White plays Bxd4, recapture with the bishop and keep the c5 bishop active. }) 5... e6 { [%cal Ge7e6] } 6. Be3 $14 { White develops naturally rather than committing to an immediate pawn sacrifice - since the bishop is no longer on an exposed diagonal, there is less to gain from ripping the position open early. } { [%cal Gc1e3] } 6... h5 $14 { BLACK'S PLAN in this branch: challenge the overextended g4-pawn directly with ...h5 before White can consolidate it with h3/Nf3. } (6... c5 $14 { The other main try: strike in the center immediately rather than committing to ...h5 first. Both approaches are fully playable. } 7. dxc5 { [%cal Gd4c5] } 7... Qc7 $14 { Regaining the pawn immediately rather than letting White consolidate the extra material. } { [%cal Gd8c7] } 8. Nb5 Bxb5 { WATCH OUT: Bxc5 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -9.6 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxc7+ Kd8 Nxa8 Bxe3 fxe3 Nc6 } 9. Bxb5+ Nc6 10. c3 $14 { Black regains the pawn next move with ...Qxc5 and stands at least equal, with a healthy structure and no weaknesses - a clean, low-theory way to meet the Bayonet Attack for players who would rather not memorize the sharper 4...Be4/4...Bg6 tactics. Engine-verified. } 10... Qxe5 $10 { White's c3 prepares to protect the bishop on b5, but you simply win back the e5 pawn that cramps you and take the queen to the centre (about 0.6). The pawn on c5 is also under fire from ...Bxc5. } (10... a6 11. Qa4 Ne7 12. f4 g5 13. Nf3 h5 14. fxg5 hxg4 15. Bxc6+ { Asking the bishop a question at once is slower: White gets f4 in and the lines open while you are underdeveloped. You are slightly worse here (about 0.8). }) 11. Nf3 Qc7 12. Qa4 a6 { Qa4 piles up on the pinned knight, so break the pin at once. } 13. Nd4 Ne7 14. O-O-O O-O-O 15. Be2 { Both kings are safe and the position is balanced. Your knight on e7 guards c6 and prepares ...Nd5. }) 7. gxh5 { [%cal Gg4h5] } 7... c5 $14 { Striking in the center immediately, while White's kingside pawns are stretched out and the rook on h5 needs a moment to get back into the game. } { [%cal Gc6c5] } 8. dxc5 Nc6 9. Nf3 Rxh5 10. h4 $14 { A sharp, roughly balanced position where Black's activity (the h5-rook, the half-open c-file pressure) compensates fully for the slightly awkward-looking rook placement. Engine-verified. } 10... Nxe5 $10 { White's pawn on e5 is the only thing holding the position together, so take it. The knight exchange keeps your rook active and the exchanges favour you (about 0.4). } (10... Be7 11. Bd3 Qa5 12. Nd4 Rxe5 13. Nb3 Qd8 14. Qg4 Nf6 15. Qxg7 { Passive development lets White take the g7 pawn (about 0.7). }) 11. Nxe5 Rxe5 12. Qd4 { Qd4 attacks the rook on e5. } 12... Rxe3+ { Give the exchange: the rook is hit anyway, and recapturing on e3 wrecks White's pawn structure. } 13. fxe3 Qc7 14. O-O-O Qxc5 15. Qd2 { You win the c5 pawn back, and White's doubled e-pawns are weak. The position is balanced (about 0.4), with your king safer. }) 5. f3 { [%cal Gf2f3] } 5... Bg6 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.3 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Bxc2 Qxc2 Qb6 e6 Qxd4 } { [%cal Ge4g6] } 6. e6 $10 { The thematic pawn sacrifice of this whole system: White offers the e5-pawn to rip open the center and the f-file while Black's king is still uncastled. Black has two ways to answer it, both fully playable. } { [%csl Ye6][%cal Ye5e6] } 6... fxe6 $14 { Accepting the sacrifice. Black's structure is a little loosened (the e6-pawn is now a target), but the extra pawn and the newly-opened f-file for Black's own rook are real, lasting compensation - this is not a case of Black simply being worse for grabbing material. } (6... Qd6 $10 { Declining the sacrifice with a developing move that also defends f7 indirectly and keeps the pawn structure symmetric. This is the calmer of the two replies. } 7. exf7+ { [%cal Ge6f7] } 7... Bxf7 8. Nc3 Nd7 9. f4 $10 { Both sides have simple, natural plans from here - White expands with f4-f5 ideas later, Black finishes development with ...Ngf6/...Be7/...O-O. Roughly balanced, engine-verified. } 9... Ngf6 $10 { Develop the knight and challenge White's central grip: the g4 pawn is attacked at once (about 0.2). } (9... h5 10. g5 g6 11. Be3 e6 12. Bh3 Ne7 13. Nf3 Bg7 14. Qd2 { Pushing the h-pawn lets g5 clamp your kingside, and White is a bit better (about 0.3). }) 10. Bh3 Qe6+ { The key resource. The g4 pawn blocks the bishop's diagonal, so you check and force the queens off. } 11. Qe2 Qxe2+ 12. Ncxe2 e6 { Closing the diagonal for the bishop on h3 and securing your king's side. } 13. Nf3 Bd6 { Hit the f4 pawn and develop the bishop. } 14. O-O { With the queens off, f4-f5 is no longer a real threat and you are fully developed. }) 7. h4 $14 { White follows up in typical fashion, preparing to meet ...h5 or just gaining more space. } { [%cal Gh2h4] } 7... h5 { [%cal Gh7h5] } 8. Bd3 $14 { Offering the trade of light-squared bishops, just as in the Tal Variation above. } { [%cal Gf1d3] } 8... Bxd3 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 Bxg6+ Kd7 Nc3 e5 } 9. Qxd3 Qd6 $10 { A fully sound, balanced tabiya: Black has an extra central pawn structure and open lines to work with once development finishes (...Ne7, ...O-O-O or ...O-O depending on king safety needs). Engine-verified as close to equal. } 10. Ne2 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.2). Notable features: Black is a pawn up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side; Black has doubled pawns on the e-file.] } 10... Nd7 $10 { Black is the pawn up. White wants gxh5 with Bf4 and Qg6+ ideas and open lines against your king. Development comes first (about 0.0). } (10... hxg4 11. Bf4 Qd7 12. Nd2 Nh6 13. O-O-O g3 14. Qg6+ Kd8 15. Bxb8 { Grabbing the g4 pawn opens the h-file, and Qg6+ forces your king around. After Bxb8 you lose a piece (about 1.9). }) 11. Bf4 e5 { Bf4 hits your queen, so counter in the centre. } 12. dxe5 Nxe5 13. Qc3 d4 { The pawn hits the queen and gains space. } 14. Qb3 O-O-O 15. Nd2 { You have a strong pawn on d4, your king is safe and the position is balanced. }) (4. Bd3 { 4.Bd3 offers the immediate trade of light-squared bishops before developing anything else, a distinct system from the Nf3-first approach the chapter otherwise builds around. } 4... Bxd3 5. Qxd3 e6 6. Nf3 { ...reaching a simplified, very level position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), a genuine independent fourth-move system alongside Nf3, Nc3, Nd2, h4 and g4.] } 6... c5 $10 { The break at d4 is the standard way to free your game and fits the simplified structure after the bishop trade (about 0.1). } (6... Nd7 7. O-O Ne7 8. c4 dxc4 9. Qxc4 Nd5 10. Qd3 Be7 11. Qe4 { A sound alternative, though White is a touch more comfortable with the extra space in the centre. }) 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nc6 9. f4 Nxd4 10. Bxd4 Ne7 { White's bishop on d4 aims at g7 and f4 holds e5, but ...Ne7 covers g7 and heads to f5. } 11. Nd2 { A balanced position. You have the half-open c-file, and White has a little extra space. }) (4. Ne2 { 4.Ne2 develops flexibly, keeping the option of either Ng3 or Nf4 depending on Black's setup. } 4... e6 5. Ng3 Bg6 { ...and Black equalizes comfortably with normal development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a quiet, sound try that sidesteps all the sharper fourth-move theory.] } 6. h4 $10 { A thematic thrust: h5 next would chase your bishop off g6 and let White gain space with tempo (about -0.1). } (6. c4 c5 7. cxd5 Qxd5 8. Nc3 Qxd4 9. Qb3 Qb4 10. Qxb4 cxb4 { c4 tries to open lines, but your queen takes d4 and the queen trade leaves you comfortable (about -0.1). }) 6... h5 { Stop the pawn dead. Now h4 is fixed and the knight on g3 has no way to harass your bishop. } 7. c4 c5 8. Bg5 Be7 9. cxd5 exd5 10. Bb5+ Nc6 { The structure is symmetrical with an isolated d5 pawn, but all your pieces are active and the pin is harmless. Equality is easy to hold. }) (4. c3 { 4.c3 supports the center quietly before committing the king's knight anywhere, keeping options for both Nf3 and f4 later. } 4... e6 5. Be3 Qb6 6. Qb3 { ...reaching a level, flexible position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a sound, calm fourth-move try alongside the chapter's sharper main lines.] } 6... h5 $10 { Fix the kingside before you develop. This stops White expanding and keeps ...Nd7 and ...h4 in reserve (about 0.0). } (6... Ne7 7. Nd2 Nd7 8. Be2 h5 9. f4 Ng6 10. Ngf3 a5 11. a4 { Solid too, but White gets f4 in for free (about 0.1). }) 7. Be2 Nd7 8. Nd2 h4 9. Ngf3 Ne7 10. O-O h3 { Pushing the pawn to h3 cramps White's kingside and forces a weakening reply. } 11. g3 { White has to accept the weak pawn structure on the kingside. The position is level and your pieces have easy squares. }) (4. Be3 { 4.Be3 develops toward c5/d4 immediately, preparing a later Nd2-Nf3 setup without blocking the c-pawn. } 4... Qb6 5. Qc1 e6 6. c4 { ...and the position stays balanced. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), a sound fourth-move alternative.] } 6... f6 $10 { The immediate lever. Be3 and Qc1 only protect b2, so you attack the head of the chain at once before White has a developed centre (about 0.0). } (6... Nd7 7. Nf3 f6 8. exf6 Ngxf6 9. c5 Qd8 10. Be2 Be7 11. Nc3 { The same position by transposition (about 0.0). }) 7. exf6 Nxf6 8. Nf3 Nbd7 9. c5 Qd8 { c5 gains time on your queen, but it also gives up control of d4 and b4, so the retreat costs you very little. } 10. Be2 Be7 11. Nc3 { You are fully developed with a safe king after castling, and the c5 pawn can later be undermined with ...b6. White has no lasting edge. }) 4... h5 $14 { BLACK'S PLAN IN THIS WHOLE SYSTEM: the h4/h5 pawns are now fixed, which means the g4-square is permanently weak for White and the h4-pawn itself is a long-term target once rooks and knights get active. Black's recipe is almost always the same regardless of which fifth move White chooses: trade off the light-squared bishop or reroute it to a safe square (h7 or g6), meet the center with the freeing ...c5 break at the right moment, and look to land a knight on f5 or use the half-open h-file/g4 hole in the middlegame. None of this requires memorizing forcing lines - it is a structural plan, and the two main branches below (5.Bd3 and 5.c4) both illustrate it in real, verified grandmaster games rather than abstract theory. } { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gh7h5] } 5. Bd3 $10 { The calmer, more positional approach - White simply offers the trade of light-squared bishops rather than letting Black keep the good bishop. Objectively very safe for White, but also gives up any hope of a quick attack. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } (5. c4 $14 { The sharper, more aggressive try - open the center immediately while Black's king is still uncastled, rather than trading pieces off. This exact fifth move, move for move, was played in Beerdsen - Donchenko, Tata Steel Masters 2023 (0-1); this specific game is not one of this course's Instructive Games and has not been independently re-verified in this round, so treat the reference as a pointer to further study, not a confirmed source. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 5... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 6. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 6... dxc4 { dxc4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): Black has a material edge of about 1 point(s); Black has doubled pawns.   (the engine edge for White coexists with Black's extra material here because this is a known pawn-grab-for-compensation pattern in this line, not a tactical oversight - White has concrete compensation: faster development, open lines, or attacking chances that outweigh the missing pawn(s)) } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd5c4] } (6... Ne7 $14 { Keeping the central tension instead of taking on c4 is also perfectly playable - a simpler, less theory-dependent choice if you would rather avoid the sharper structures above. } { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 7. Nge2 $10 { The knight heads to g3 to hit both your bishop and the h5 pawn at once (about 0.2). } (7. Bg5 Qb6 8. Qd2 Nd7 9. Nf3 f6 10. Be3 O-O-O 11. c5 Qa5 { Bg5 aims to disturb your knight, but ...f6 solves it and the game is level (about 0.1). }) 7... Nd7 8. Ng3 Bg6 { Guard h5 and keep the bishop. } 9. Be3 Qb6 { Pressure on b2, which forces White to keep spending tempi. } 10. Qd2 dxc4 { Now the tension is resolved on your terms. } 11. Bxc4 O-O-O { You castle into safety and your pieces have good squares. The position is balanced. }) 7. Bxc4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 7... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 8. Nge2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 8... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 9. g3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 9... Nh6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bg8h6] } 10. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bc1f4] } 10... Qb6 $14 { Black's plan here is concrete: pressure b2 and d4 with the queen while the knight heads for f5, exactly the ideas outlined at the start of this chapter. } { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 11. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 11... Rd8 { [%csl Bd8][%cal Ba8d8] } 12. Qc1 { [%csl Bc1][%cal Bd1c1] } 12... a6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2).] [%csl Ba6][%cal Ba7a6] } 13. Bb3 $14 { The bishop retreats from the threat of ...b5. White is slightly better (about 0.5) thanks to the space advantage and the stable bishop on b3. } (13. Bxh6 Rxh6 14. Qe3 Qc7 15. Nf4 g5 16. hxg5 Bxg5 17. Rad1 Rg6 { Trading the knight for the bishop is also possible, and it leaves the position close to level. }) 13... Ng4 { The knight dodges the Bxh6 trade and attacks e5. } 14. Bg5 f6 { The bishop on g5 hits e7, so hit back at e5 and break the bind. } 15. exf6 gxf6 16. Be3 c5 { Open the position with the central pawn lever. } 17. Re1 Kf7 { Your king is safe on f7 and your pieces are active, so you can hold a small deficit. }) (5. Bg5 $10 { A real try from established theory, not just a blunder. The bishop develops actively and rules out ...Qb6 for a move, but the critical test below shows Black equalises comfortably with precise play. } 5... Qb6! { Striking at b2 and d4 at once, exactly as in the simpler 5.Bd3 and 5.c4 lines. } 6. Bd3 { White's only real try. Defending b2 with 6.b3?! is well met by 6...e6 followed by ...c5, which is very bad for White. } 6... Bxd3 { Simplest. Grabbing the pawn with 6...Qxd4?! allows 7.Nf3 Qg4, and after the trades 8.Bxf5 Qxf5 White castles with 9.O-O, which produces the sort of tangle Black's opponents would gladly steer clear of; Black may well be fine there too, but there is no need to go looking for complications when the position is already comfortable. } 7. Qxd3 Qa6 $14 { The point: the queen chases White's queen off d3 and offers a trade, a thoroughly Caro-Kannesque resource. (7...Qxb2?! is dangerous here because of 8.e6!, hitting f7 with real force; stick with 7...Qa6.) [Engine verdict: roughly equal across White's main tries below (-0.1 to +0.2), a far cry from White's normal +0.6 to +0.8 edge elsewhere in this chapter.] } 8. Qb3 $10 { The engine's actual top choice here (+0.21), avoiding the forced queen trades below and keeping an eye on the queenside. } (8. e6 $10 { Forces matters into a queenless middlegame at once rather than retreating. } 8... Qxd3 9. exf7+ Kxf7 10. cxd3 Nd7 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1).] Black continues with ...e6 and ...Bd6 next. } 11. Nd2 $10 { White wants to untangle with Ndf3 and bring the pieces out around the doubled pawns (about 0.1). } (11. Nf3 e6 12. Nc3 Bd6 13. Ne2 Re8 14. Kf1 Nh6 15. Bf4 e5 { A more active plan, but ...e5 equalises comfortably (about 0.0). }) 11... Nh6 { Heading for f5 to hit the h4 pawn and the Bg5. } 12. Ndf3 Nf5 13. Bd2 Re8 14. Bc3 e6 15. g3 Bd6 { Your king on f7 is not in danger with the queens off, and your bishop and knight dominate. The doubled pawns are White's only structural problem. }) (8. Qxa6 $10 { The most forcing try, trading queens immediately rather than letting Black choose the moment. } 8... Nxa6 9. e6 { Otherwise Black simply plays ...e6 with a comfortable game; if White is ever forced to take on e6, Black stands clearly better. } 9... f6 10. Bf4 O-O-O { Playing around the pawn rather than winning it back immediately. } 11. Nd2 Nh6 12. Nh3 Nf5 13. Nf3 { Following Nisipeanu-Rozum, Skopje 2019. } 13... Nc7 { The improvement over that game: rather than continuing the knight manoeuvre, Black offers the trade of knight for bishop at once, heading for an endgame where White's bishop pair never materialises. } 14. Bxc7 Kxc7 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.57) and growing; White's kingside knight manoeuvre has achieved nothing concrete, while Black's king is perfectly safe and the structure is sound.] } 15. Nf4 $17 { White hits h5 and aims for g6, trying to eliminate the h5 pawn with the king stuck on e1 (about -0.5). } (15. Ke2 g6 16. Kd3 Bh6 17. Rae1 Kd6 18. Rhg1 Rdg8 19. g3 b6 { The king walk to d3 looks like it defends e6, but your bishop hits the pawn and the king is exposed in the centre. Black is clearly better (about -1.1). }) 15... g6 { Guard h5 and force the knight to decide. } 16. Nxg6 Rg8 17. Nxf8 Rdxf8 18. g3 Kd6 19. c3 Kxe6 { You win the e6 pawn, and your king is safe in the centre with the queens already off. }) 8... e6 9. Nd2 Nd7 10. Ne2 f6 { A typical central break. } 11. Bf4 Nh6 { [Engine verdict: dynamic equality. The knight heads for f5, with ...Be7 to follow.] } 12. c4 $10 { White hits the centre with c4. The queen on a6 stares at the knight on e2, but the pawn blocks the diagonal (about 0.2). } (12. exf6 gxf6 13. O-O Qxe2 14. Qxb7 Rd8 15. Rfe1 Qg4 16. Bc7 Rg8 { Dangerous looking: White offers the knight on e2, and when you take it Qxb7 and Bc7 give real counterplay. Even so it stays level (about 0.0). }) 12... Nf5 { The knight attacks h4 and pressures the centre. } 13. O-O Be7 14. exf6 Bxf6 15. cxd5 cxd5 16. Nf3 O-O { You are fully developed with your king safe, and the pawn structure is symmetrical. The position is dynamically level. }) 5... Bxd3 { [%csl Rd3][%cal Rf5d3] } 6. Qxd3 { [%csl Rd3][%cal Rd1d3] } 6... Qa5+ $10 { The critical, most-tested continuation - and the one both real games below actually chose over the quieter 6...e6. The check gains a tempo and keeps options open for where the queen and king ultimately want to be. } { [%csl Ga5][%cal Gd8a5] } (6... e6 $14 { The quieter alternative to 6...Qa5+ - simply completing development. A very safe, thoroughly tested structure. Both choices are fine; 6...Qa5+ is included in more depth above because it is what both real games below actually played, giving a fuller picture of the resulting middlegame. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7. Nf3 $10 { A natural developing move that supports d4 and prepares c3 or Bg5. White keeps a slight lead in development (about 0.3). } (7. Bg5 Qb6 8. Nd2 c5 9. c4 Qxb2 10. Rd1 Nc6 11. Ne2 Nb4 { Grabbing b2 looks risky, but Nb4 hits the queen and keeps everything under control. The position is about level (about 0.1). }) 7... c5 { The thematic break. Strike at d4 now, while White's king is still in the centre. } 8. dxc5 Qc7 { The queen attacks e5 and c5 together. } 9. c4 dxc4 10. Qe2 Bxc5 11. O-O Ne7 { You win back the pawn and your pieces are well placed. }) 7. Nd2 $14 { The calmer reply. White simply blocks the check with the knight and prepares normal development (Ngf3, O-O). This exact position, move for move, was reached in Hjartarson - Pultinevicius, Reykjavik 2024 (0-1) - the continuation below is that real game's actual score, useful because it shows the full middlegame plan in action, not just the first few moves. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } (7. b4 $14 { The sharper try. White grabs queenside space and, as this real game shows, is even willing to sacrifice the e5-pawn's shelter with a quick e6 to disrupt Black's coordination before Black can consolidate. This exact sequence is claimed to have occurred in Bersamina - Bluebaum, 45th Chess Olympiad 2024 (0-1), but that game has not been independently verified and is not one of this course's Instructive Games; treat the reference as unconfirmed. } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb2b4] } 7... Qa6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Ba5a6] } 8. e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be5e6] } 8... Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Ba6d3] } 9. cxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bc2d3] } 9... fxe6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Bf7e6] } 10. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 10... Nh6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bg8h6] } 11. Ndf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd2f3] } 11... Nf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bh6f5] } 12. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 12... g6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bg7g6] } 13. Nf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be2f4] } 13... a5 { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 14. Nxe6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Bf4e6] } 14... Kd7 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the d-file.] WATCH OUT: axb4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nc7+ Kd7 Nxa8 Bg7 Nb6+ Kc7 } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Be8d7] } 15. Nxf8+ $10 { The knight on e6 attacks f8 and g7, so White cashes it in before it is chased away. Material and structure are balanced (about -0.1). } (15. Nc5+ Kc8 16. bxa5 e5 17. Nb3 Nxd4 18. Nfxd4 exd4 19. O-O Bb4 { Nc5+ drives your king to c8, but the central ...e5 break gives you a little edge (about -0.3). }) 15... Rxf8 16. bxa5 Rxa5 { Recapture with the rook, which is now active along the a-file. } 17. a4 b5 { Challenge the a4 pawn. } 18. O-O bxa4 19. Bd2 Ra8 { You have a very safe king on d7 with the queens off and your bishop on g7 stays powerful. }) 7... e6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 8. Nf3 { Nf3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } (8. Ne2 { 14.Ne2 - a flexible developing move instead of the main 14.Nf3, keeping the knight ready to jump to f4 or g3 depending on how the position opens up. } 8... c5 { 14...c5 (Complicated & Imbalanced) - strikes at the center at once, inviting a sharper fight where both sides' pieces have more open lines to work with. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), but a livelier, less forcing position than the quiet main line.] } (8... Ne7 { 14...Ne7 (Solid & Optimal) - simplest piece development, aiming the knight toward d5/f5 and keeping the position closed and easy to play without memorizing anything sharp. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.4), the calmer and objectively safest of the two.] } 9. b4 $10 { White hits your queen to gain space and force it to a6 (about 0.1). } (9. c3 Qa6 10. Qxa6 Nxa6 11. b4 b6 12. a4 c5 13. b5 Nc7 { The quiet c3 also leads to an equal endgame with ...c5 as the plan. }) 9... Qa6 10. Qb3 Nf5 { The knight attacks the h4 pawn and controls d4. } 11. Nf3 Qc4 { Offer the queen swap. } 12. Bd2 Nd7 13. Qxc4 dxc4 { The pawn on c4 is a bit loose but it takes away squares from White's knights and the position is level. }) 9. c4 $10 { White opens the centre: the pawn hits d5, and your queen on a5 keeps the d2 knight pinned for the moment (about 0.0). } (9. Qb3 Qb6 10. c4 Ne7 11. O-O Nbc6 12. dxc5 Qxb3 13. axb3 Nxe5 { Hitting b7 and offering a queen trade is natural, but you take on e5 and stand fine (about -0.2). }) 9... dxc4 10. Qxc4 Nc6 11. O-O cxd4 12. Nf3 Qc5 { Trade queens to simplify. } 13. Qb3 Qb6 { You have traded off the d4 pawn and your queen is comfortable on b6, so the position is level. }) 8... Nh6 { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gg8h6] } 9. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 9... Nf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gh6f5] } 10. Nb3 { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gd2b3] } 10... Qa6 { [%csl Ga6][%cal Ga5a6] } 11. Qd1 { [%csl Gd1][%cal Gd3d1] } 11... b6 { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gb7b6] } 12. Bg5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gc1g5] } 12... c5 $14 { Here is the freeing break in action, right on schedule: Black has finished developing (knight to f5, queen re-routed, queenside secured with ...b6) before challenging the center. This is the textbook execution of the plan described above. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc6c5] } 13. dxc5 { [%csl Rc5][%cal Rd4c5] } 13... Bxc5 { [%csl Rc5][%cal Rf8c5] } 14. c3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 14... Nc6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 15. Re1 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gf1e1] } 15... Rc8 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2).] [%csl Gc8][%cal Ga8c8] } 16. Nc1 $14 { Your bishop on c5 was hit by the knight on b3, so White reroutes the knight toward e2 where it supports d4 and challenges your active pieces (about 0.4). } (16. Nxc5 bxc5 17. b3 Rc7 18. Nd2 Qa5 19. Qf3 Rd7 20. Rac1 Qc7 { Taking the bishop leaves you with hanging pawns on c5 and d5, but they control the centre and you are fine (about 0.2). }) 16... Be7 17. Bxe7 Ncxe7 18. Ne2 Rc4 { The rook is active, and White's knight tries to reach d4. } 19. Ned4 Nxd4 20. Nxd4 Nc6 { You trade the dangerous knights and keep a solid position, so you are close to equal. } *


