1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Shirov (4.Nc3) and 4.g4!? sidelines transpose into slightly different flavors of the same locked Advance center, with Black's structure (half-open c- and h-files) showing the position has opened somewhat on the flanks while the center stays fixed. This is another 'know the moves' chapter rather than a 'understand the structure' chapter, since both White tries are relatively rare and designed to dodge Black's main-line preparation rather than to reach a textbook structure, react with sound development and the concrete lines in the PLAN.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives -0.6 to -0.1, a small Black edge. Black is already comfortable here if White follows the chapter's line. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Bxc2? on moves 4 to 6 loses material (about 3.6); 11...Qd7? (about 1.2; prefer ...Ne7); 10...Ne7? (about 1.2; prefer ...Nc6); 11...Qc7? (about 1.1; prefer ...Ne7); 7...f6?! (about 0.7; prefer ...cxd4).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 10.Na4? Nc6 (costs White about 2.2; engine-found, not in the chapter tree); 10.Nb1? Qc7 (costs White about 1.5; engine-found, not in the chapter tree).
Other Advance Variation chapters: #15, #16, #17, #18, #19, #20. Model game: #11. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 3... Bf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 4. Nc3 $14 { The Shirov Variation - a flexible try that develops a piece and keeps the option of a later g4 pawn storm in reserve. BLACK'S PLAN: whichever way White times the g4 advance (immediately, as in the 4.g4!? branch below, or after Nc3 first), the bishop simply steps back to g6 and then h7 - it is never actually trapped, just relocated to a long, safe diagonal. Once that is settled, Black's normal freeing break ...c5 followed by ...Nc6/...Ne7 and, if White grabs the h5-pawn, a well-timed ...cxd4 tactical point (explained below) give Black completely comfortable, often better, positions in practice. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } (4. g4 $14 { The immediate, more committal try - grab the bishop's retreat square before Black can complete development at all. } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bg2g4] } 4... Bg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf5g6] } 5. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 5... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 6. Nge2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 6... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 7. h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 7... h5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 8. Nf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be2f4] } 8... Bh7 { [%csl Bh7][%cal Bg6h7] } 9. Nxh5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bf4h5] } 9... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 10. Qxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd1d4] } 10... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 11. Bb5 $15 { This simply transposes back into the exact same structure and tactical point covered above (Nxh5 met by ...cxd4) - whichever move order White chooses to reach this pawn storm, Black's response is identical. } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bf1b5] } 11... Ne7 $15 { Bb5 pins your knight on c6 against the king, so you add a defender and prepare ...a6 (about -0.5). } (11... Bxc2 12. Bg5 Qd7 13. Qd2 Bg6 14. Nf4 Bh7 15. Rh3 a6 16. Bxc6 { Taking c2 costs time, since Bg5 hits your queen with tempo and White finishes development (about 0.3). }) 12. Bg5 a6 13. Bxc6+ Nxc6 14. Qf4 Qa5 15. O-O Qb4 16. Qxb4 Nxb4 17. Rad1 Rc8 { The queens come off and White's knight on h5 is offside. You keep a small, pleasant edge. }) (4. Bd3 { 4.Bd3 offers the immediate trade of light-squared bishops before developing anything else, a distinct system from the Nf3-first approach the chapter otherwise builds around. } 4... Bxd3 5. Qxd3 e6 6. Nf3 { ...reaching a simplified, very level position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), a genuine independent fourth-move system alongside Nf3, Nc3, Nd2, h4 and g4.] } 6... Qa5+ $10 { An in-between check that makes White decide how to block it. The knight on d2 ends up pinned along the diagonal, which is why Qa6 is playable next (about 0.3). } (6... c5 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nc6 9. f4 Nxd4 10. Bxd4 Ne7 11. Nd2 { The main plan of attacking d4 is just as good (about 0.1). }) 7. Nbd2 Qa6 { Hit the c4 square and offer a queen trade. } 8. c4 Bb4 { Pin the knight again. } 9. O-O Ne7 10. Qc2 Bxd2 11. Nxd2 { You swap off the knight, and the position is simplified and level. }) (4. Ne2 { 4.Ne2 develops flexibly, keeping the option of either Ng3 or Nf4 depending on Black's setup. } 4... e6 5. Ng3 Bg6 { ...and Black equalizes comfortably with normal development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a quiet, sound try that sidesteps all the sharper fourth-move theory.] } 6. Bd3 $10 { Bd3 aims at your bishop. If White takes on g6 you recapture hxg6 and gain the half-open h-file (about -0.2). } (6. c3 c5 7. Bb5+ Nc6 8. O-O Rc8 9. Be3 a6 10. Bxc6+ Rxc6 { A quieter route that also levels out (about 0.0). }) 6... c5 7. c3 Nc6 8. O-O Qb6 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. b4 Be7 { White gains space on the queenside, but your pieces are active and the b4 pawn is a target. You are fine. }) (4. c3 { 4.c3 supports the center quietly before committing the king's knight anywhere, keeping options for both Nf3 and f4 later. } 4... e6 5. Be3 Qb6 6. Qb3 { ...reaching a level, flexible position. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a sound, calm fourth-move try alongside the chapter's sharper main lines.] } 6... Be7 $10 { Quiet development. The bishop eyes g5 and prepares castling, since White's queen on b3 has no real targets (about 0.0). } (6... Nd7 7. Nd2 h5 8. Be2 h4 9. Ngf3 Ne7 10. O-O Ng6 11. Ne1 { Equally fine and keeps the queens on (about 0.0). }) 7. Be2 h5 { Fix the kingside before White can expand. } 8. Nf3 Nd7 9. O-O Nh6 { The knight heads to f5 to hit the bishop on e3. } 10. h3 a5 11. c4 { Your pieces are flexible and your king is safe. The position is level. }) (4. Be3 { 4.Be3 develops toward c5/d4 immediately, preparing a later Nd2-Nf3 setup without blocking the c-pawn. } 4... Qb6 5. Qc1 e6 6. c4 { ...and the position stays balanced. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), a sound fourth-move alternative.] } 6... Nd7 $10 { White's c4 questions your d5 pawn while Qc1 keeps b2 safe. The knight supports d5 and keeps the queen free (about 0.0). } (6... Qd8 7. Nc3 Ne7 8. Nge2 Nd7 9. Ng3 Bg6 10. h4 h5 11. Qd2 { Retreating the queen immediately also holds, but White gains time with h4 against your bishop (about 0.2). }) 7. Nf3 f6 8. exf6 Ngxf6 { Recapture with the knight so your pieces stay compact. } 9. c5 Qd8 10. Be2 Be7 11. Nc3 { After castling you have an easy position. The c5 pawn can later be undermined with ...b6. }) 4... e6 $10 { Simple and solid - Black keeps developing normally rather than reacting to a threat that hasn't been played yet. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 5. g4 $10 { Now White follows up with the pawn storm, gaining space on the kingside at the cost of real structural commitments (the g- and h-files will be easier for Black to use later, and e5/f4 can become weak). } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gg2g4] } 5... Bg6 { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gf5g6] } 6. Nge2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gg1e2] } 6... c5 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc6c5] } 7. h4 { h4 leads to a small White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gh2h4] } (7. Be3 { 7.Be3 develops before committing to h4, supporting d4 and preparing queenside castling. } 7... h5 8. dxc5 hxg4 9. Nd4 { ...and Black holds the balance. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.2), a sound alternative to the sharper h4-h5 plans in this Shirov/Van der Wiel structure.] } 9... Nh6 $15 { The knight guards the g4 pawn and heads for f5 where it hits the knight on d4 and the bishop on e3 (about -0.5). } (9... Nd7 10. Qxg4 Qh4 11. Qxh4 Rxh4 12. Rg1 Bxc5 13. Nxe6 fxe6 14. Bxc5 { A tricky line where White returns material with Nxe6 tactics and you lose some of your advantage (about -0.3). }) 10. Bd3 Bxd3 11. Qxd3 Nc6 { Challenge the knight on d4 and prepare ...Bxc5. } 12. O-O-O Bxc5 13. h3 g3 14. fxg3 { You recover the c5 pawn and White's pawn structure on the kingside is wrecked. Black is slightly better. }) 7... h5 { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gh7h5] } 8. Nf4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Ge2f4] } 8... Bh7 { [%csl Gh7][%cal Gg6h7] } 9. Nxh5 $15 { THE KEY TACTICAL POINT of this whole sideline: White grabs the h5-pawn, but the bishop is completely safe on h7 and Black has two move orders here that both work well, illustrated by a real tournament game below (Aabling Thomsen - Schandorff, Denmark 2010, with a genuine grandmaster, Lars Schandorff, on the Black side; see also Instructive Game #11; a second claimed game, Nielsen - Pedersen, Denmark 2005, could not be verified and was removed from this course). The common idea in both: meet Nxh5 with an immediate strike on d4, either right away with ...cxd4 or after first developing the knight to c6. } { [%csl Rh5][%cal Rf4h5] } (9. g5 { 9.g5 pushes through immediately rather than trading on g6 first, trying to lock the bishop out of the game entirely. } 9... cxd4 10. Nce2 { WATCH OUT: Nxh5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.7 from White's own side of the board), after: dxc3 Bb5+ Nc6 Bxc6+ bxc6 bxc3 } 10... Be4 11. Rh3 { ...and Black's central counterplay with ...cxd4 proves faster. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.1), a thematic try worth recognizing but one that doesn't hold up to accurate defense.] } 11... Ne7 $17 { The knight develops and supports ...Nbc6 and ...Nf5, so White's rook lift on h3 comes too late (about -1.0). } (11... Qc7 12. Nxd4 Nc6 13. c3 Nge7 14. Nxh5 O-O-O 15. Ng3 Nxe5 16. Be3 { A greedy try that is less accurate, since White gets counterplay (about -0.5). }) 12. Nxd4 Nbc6 13. Rb3 Qc7 { Rb3 hits b7, so cover it and keep the pressure on e5. } 14. Be3 Nf5 15. Nxf5 Bxf5 16. Bd3 { Your pieces are better placed and White's rook lift on h3 has not led to anything. You are clearly better. }) 9... cxd4 { cxd4 leads to roughly equal (+0.0): Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc5d4] } (9... Nc6 $10 { Nc6 leads to roughly equal (-0.2): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s). } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 10. Bb5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bf1b5] } 10... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 11. Qxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd1d4] } 11... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 12. Bg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 12... a6 $15 { The alternative move order - developing the knight first and only then resolving the center - reaches an equally comfortable position; a matter of taste rather than necessity. } { [%csl Ba6][%cal Ba7a6] } 13. Bxc6+ $15 { Your a-pawn threatens the b5 bishop, so White trades it off with check. This gives you a slight edge (about -0.5). } (13. Bxe7 axb5 14. Bxd8 Nxd4 15. Bb6 Nxc2+ 16. Ke2 Nxa1 17. Rxa1 Kd7 { Grabbing your queen looks tempting, but your knight takes the white queen and you end up far ahead (about -2.8). }) 13... Nxc6 14. Qf4 Qa5 { Hit e5 and the c3 square. } 15. O-O Qb4 16. Qxb4 Nxb4 17. Rad1 Rc8 { The queen trade leaves your pieces active and White's knight on h5 offside. You are a bit better. }) 10. Qxd4 { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rd1d4] } 10... Nc6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 11. Bb5 { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gf1b5] } 11... Ne7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gg8e7] } 12. Bh6 { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gc1h6] } 12... Rg8 $15 { Black already has an initiative on the kingside (the rook eyes g4/g2 down the g-file) for nothing more than the pawn White grabbed a few moves ago - a completely fair, often better, trade of assets.   WATCH OUT: gxh6 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it's not just worse, it's losing outright for Black: after Nf6#, White forces mate. } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Gh8g8] } 13. Qf4 $15 { The queen defends the bishop on h6 and eyes the kingside squares, but you keep the initiative (about -0.5). } (13. O-O-O a6 14. Bxc6+ Nxc6 15. Qf4 Qb6 16. Bg5 Nb4 17. a3 Nxc2 { Castling long runs into ...Nb4 and ...Nxc2, which costs a pawn (about -0.9). }) 13... a6 14. Ba4 Qc7 15. O-O-O b5 { Chase the bishop, gaining space and tempo. } 16. Bb3 Na5 17. Rhe1 Rc8 { You have strong pressure on the c-file and kingside, and White's pieces are scattered. } *


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Chapter #22: Two Knights Attack, Tartakower Variation"]
