[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/1T4jX1rn"]

1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Two Knights Attack's Tartakower treatment reaches a structure that looks like the Classical Variation's Tartakower idea (Chapter #10): doubled Black f-pawns, open e-file, bishop pair as compensation. The difference is move order, White has delayed d4 in favor of developing both knights first, so Black should recognize the resulting position as the same type of favorable trade (structure for piece activity) rather than treating the Two Knights Attack as unfamiliar territory. The half-open d-file is White's only real asset; keep it under control with a well-placed piece rather than letting a rook land there uncontested.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives -0.3 to +0.2, roughly equal. Note: along the chapter's line the engine value falls from +0.2 (move 6) to -0.3 at the target, because White does not play the engine's first choice at every step. Against best play, expect the higher figure. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Bxc4? on moves 11 to 14 loses material (about 5.1); ...Bxh2+? on moves 8 to 10 loses material (about 5.1); 4...Nd7?! (about 0.4; prefer ...Nf6); 4...Qd5?! (about 0.4; prefer ...Nf6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 12.c5?! Bc7 (costs White about 0.7).
Chapter #23 continues the Two Knights Attack. Other Two Knights Attack chapters: #24, #25, #26, #27. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #11. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nc3 $14 { The Two Knights Variation is a positional and solid opening against the Caro Kann. White develops two pieces ahead of black and is looking forward to an easy play. Since white has developed two pieces ahead of black, black should catch up in development right away. Black has to take the pawn so white's knight would be forced to waste a tempo followed by Nbd7 to Nf6. Following the Caro Kann Karpov Variation. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 3... dxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE 4...Nf6 (as in Chapters #22-#25) IF you want a repertoire that works in every Two Knights line. 4...Bf5 here is shown mainly as a WARNING: with White's knight on f3 the standard Bf5-g6-h6 plan runs into Ne5 and Qh5 tactics, which is why this repertoire avoids it against 2.Nf3. The 5.Ng3 h5 gamble is shown as well. Risk level: HIGH. After 4.Nxe4 Black normally develops with 4...Nf6. The alternatives below show why. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 4... Nf6 $14 { A further option is ...Nf6, which usually ends up in a Classical Caro-Kann after the knight trade and the recapture, followed by d4. That central push is the natural continuation. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } (4... Bf5 { 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 Bg6 6.h4 h6 7.Ne5 Bh7 8.Qh5 g6 9.Bc4 leaves Black in trouble. [Engine verdict: about +1.5 for White.] Avoid this move order against 2.Nf3. } 5. Ng3 (5. Nc3 $10 { White retreats the knight from e4 to c3 to keep the position solid and avoid exchanges; the knights on c3 and f3 help control d5 and e4. It is a very common choice, played in about 304 thousand games, and the position is level. } 5... Nf6 { A natural developing move. Your plan is ...Nbd7 and ...e6 or ...e5, followed by ...Bc5 and kingside castling. If the knight from f3 jumps to h4, the bishop goes to g6. Avoid shutting the bishop in with ...e6 before it has a safe square. } 6. h3 Nbd7 7. Nh4 { White prevents ...Bg4 and eyes your bishop on f5 with the knight, planning to trade it. } 7... Bg6 { The bishop retreats to g6 where it stays active, and White may take it with Nxg6 if he wishes, after which you recapture with the h-pawn and gain an open h-file. Do not be afraid of the doubled pawns. } 8. g3 e5 { Strike in the centre. The pawn on e5 gains space and opens the f8 bishop. } 9. d3 Bc5 { Develop and aim at f2. Overall: you have a comfortable game with all your pieces out and a flexible centre; keep castling short and avoid pushing pawns carelessly. } 10. Nxg6 hxg6 { This is a semi-open position with doubled g-pawns, so you have an open h-file and a flexible centre with pawns on c6 and e5. White has a solid structure and a g3 fianchetto. Your plan: bring the queen to e7 and knight to b6, keep the bishop active on c5 aiming at f2, put a rook on h8 against the h3 pawn, and castle long, since the h-file belongs to you. White wants Bg2, then f4 or d4 to undermine e5. Meet d4 by ...exd4 and ...Bb6, and meet f4 with ...exf4. The risk is a hasty ...Rxh3 or a rushed kingside castle; the doubled pawns are fine, but do not leave f2 and h3 stuff loose. }) (5. d3 { White opens the bishop on c1 and prepares g3 and Bg2, a quiet set-up in which he keeps his knight on e4 supported by the d-pawn. It is very common in practice (played in about 234 thousand games). For you it means White is not trying to refute you, so you can aim for ...e5 and a healthy centre. } 5... Nd7 $10 { You develop the knight and prepare ...e5 and ...Ngf6 in a way that avoids doubled pawns. The e4 knight is White's only active piece, so your plan is to trade it off and play ...e5 and ...Bd6. Do not allow Nd6+ ideas by neglecting the e7 pawn. } (5... e6 6. Qe2 Nf6 7. g3 Be7 8. Bg2 O-O { This is also playable. }) 6. g3 e5 { You take the centre and give your bishop f5 a retreat. } 7. Bg2 Ngf6 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { Your queen is active and supports e5 and f5. } 9. O-O Bd6 { Your bishop takes a good diagonal and your king will castle short. } 10. a4 { Overall: you have a solid and active position with a space advantage in the centre and a plan of ...O-O and ...Rae8. This is an open, active position where White's knight on d2 has been moved and you have a space advantage in the centre with pawns on c6 and e5, and both kings castle short. Your plan: castle short, then play ...Rae8 and keep your bishop on f5 and queen on f6 coordinated. When ready, push ...e4 to gain space, or play ...Nf6 to bring the last knight in. White wants to play a4-a5, trade your bishop with Nh4 or Nd2-c4 and pressure e5. Meet Nh4 by ...Bg6 and Nxg6, hxg6. The risk is a loose e5 pawn, so keep it protected and never push ...e4 without checking that d3 is covered. }) (5. Bd3 { White develops and attacks your f5 bishop twice over, since the bishop and the e4 knight both look at it. He hopes to win the bishop pair or force concessions. It is very common in practice (played in about 214 thousand games). For you it means the bishop must stay protected. } 5... e6 { This protects the bishop with a pawn and opens the f8 bishop. Your plan is ...Nf6, ...Nbd7 and ...Be7 or ...Bxd6. If White plays Nd6+ you can recapture with the bishop. Avoid ...Nd7 too early because White gets Nd6+ ideas that damage your structure. White keeps only a small edge here. } (5... Bg6 6. O-O e6 7. Re1 Nd7 8. a4 Ngf6 9. Nxf6+ Nxf6 10. Bxg6 hxg6 { This gives White a free trade of his bishop but is playable. }) 6. O-O Nf6 { Challenge the e4 knight. } 7. Re1 Nbd7 { Develop and cover the d6 square. } 8. Nd6+ Bxd6 { Take the knight, a good trade for you because you get the bishop pair. } 9. Bxf5 O-O { Do not recapture hastily; castle first. } 10. Bh3 { Overall: you keep a healthy structure and White has only a slight initiative. This is a semi-open position in which White has two bishops against your one after the exchanges, but your pawn structure is healthy and you are solid. Both kings will castle short. Your plan: recapture sensibly and play ...e5 or ...c5 to challenge the d4 pawn, then ...Qe7, ...Rfe8, and bring the knight to f8 and g6. White wants to open the game for the bishop pair and use the weak light squares such as e6 and f5. The risk is those light squares and the bishop on h3 attacking e6: do not let a bishop pin your knight on d7, and avoid weakening g6. If White deviates with Ng3 earlier, play ...Bg6. }) 5... Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Ne5 Bh7 8. Qh5 g6 9. Bc4 { [Engine check: a large advantage for White (about +1.5).] }) 5. Nxf6+ { Nxf6+ leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s). } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge4f6] } (5. Qe2 { 5.Qe2 - sidesteps the main 5.Nxf6+ trade entirely, pinning the e4-knight's retreat square and preparing to meet ...Nxe4 with a recapture that keeps the queen centralized. } 5... Na6 { 5...Na6!? is the cleanest reply: the knight heads for c7 or b4 rather than committing to d7, and sidesteps any awkward pin along the e-file once White recaptures on e4. CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE THIS IF you want a surprise weapon against 5.Qe2: it sets a trap at once (the e7-pawn is pinned, so ...Nbd7?? loses to Nd6#) and often drags the game into quiet structures where Black is perfectly fine. Risk level: MEDIUM. If you prefer a completely safe structure, answer 5.Nxf6+ exf6 as in Chapter #22 and answer 5.Qe2 with 5...Nxe4 6.Qxe4 Qa5. After 4...Nf6 5.Qe2 White threatens Nd6 mate, because the e7-pawn is pinned against the king. 5...Na6 solves that problem and prepares ...Nc7 and ...Nxe4. } 6. d3 { d3 leads to a small White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (6. c3 { 6.c3 - supports a later d4 push and keeps options flexible instead of resolving the center with 6.d3 right away. } 6... Qd5 { 6...Qd5 centralizes the queen, attacking e4 and g2 at once and forcing White to untangle before Black finishes development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.3), a second sound try alongside the existing 6.d3 line.] } 7. Nxf6+ $10 { White removes the knight that your queen was attacking, and takes the sting out of your play, with a slight pull for White (about 0.3). } (7. Nfg5 Nxe4 8. Qxe4 Qxg5 9. Bxa6 Bf5 10. Qf3 Bg4 11. Qe4 Bf5 { This is a trick. White trades knights and wins your knight on a6, but you took the g5 knight too and Bf5 hits the queen, so the line ends in a repetition (about 0.0). }) 7... gxf6 8. g3 h5 { The pawn heads to h4 and your rook uses the g-file. } (8... Bg4 { 6.c3 Qd5 7.Nxf6+ gxf6 gives White a pleasant position but Black's pieces come out quickly and the queen on d5 is active. [Engine verdict: about +0.25.] } 9. Bg2 O-O-O 10. Nh4 (10. h3 Bh5 11. O-O e5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.24).] }) (10. O-O Qd3 11. Qxd3 Rxd3 12. Nh4 e5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.21).] }) 10... Bxe2 11. Bxd5 Bd3 12. Bxf7 Bh6 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.2).] }) 9. Bg2 Qe6 10. d4 Qxe2+ 11. Kxe2 Bg4 { The queens come off and your pieces are active, with the bishop pinning the knight on f3. }) (6. Nxf6+ { 6.Nxf6+ - trades off the centralized knight immediately rather than retreating it with d3 or c3 first. } 6... gxf6 { 6...gxf6 recaptures toward the center, giving Black the bishop pair and an open g-file for the rook in return for a slightly disrupted kingside structure, a standard and comfortable trade-off in this family of structures. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.3), a third sound try at this fork.] } 7. c3 $10 { White solidifies d4 and prepares to meet ...Qd5 with g3 (about 0.3). } (7. d4 h5 8. c3 h4 { After 6.Nxf6+ gxf6 Black accepts doubled f-pawns for an open g-file and plays ...h5-h4 to harass the king's side. A messy but balanced game. [Engine verdict: about +0.2 to +0.5.] } 9. h3 (9. Qe4 Qd5 10. Qxd5 cxd5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.12).] }) 9... Nc7 (9... Qd5 10. Qc2 Nc7 (10... Qh5 11. Bd3 Rg8 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.03).] }) 11. c4 Bf5 12. Bd3 Bxd3 13. Qxd3 Qh5 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.3).] }) (9... Bf5 10. Bg5 fxg5 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.5).] }) 10. Qd1 Be6 11. Bd3 Qd7 { Pushing the h-pawn to cramp the kingside is also possible (about 0.2). }) 7... Qd5 { The queen centralises and attacks g2 and the knight on f3. } 8. g3 Bh6 { Your bishop attacks c1 and the open g-file is yours. } 9. Bg2 Qe6 10. Nd4 Qxe2+ 11. Kxe2 Bg4+ { You get your pieces out with check and your bishop pair is strong. White's king is stuck in the centre. }) (6. d4 { 6.d4 is the principled reply. Black trades knights and the queens often come off. [Engine verdict: about +0.4 for White.] } 6... Nxe4 7. Qxe4 Qd5 8. Qxd5 (8. Qe2 Bg4 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 8... cxd5 9. Bxa6 (9. c3 Nb8 10. Bd3 Nc6 11. O-O f6 12. Bf4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.20).] }) 9... bxa6 10. O-O (10. b3 Bf5 11. c4 dxc4 12. bxc4 e6 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.2).] }) 10... Bf5 11. c3 f6 { An endgame with doubled a-pawns that is perfectly playable: Black's bishops are active and the structure is sound. [Engine verdict: about -0.5, Black comfortable.] [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.5).] }) (6. a3 { 6.a3 prevents ...Nb4 ideas and keeps a bishop pair option. Black trades on e4 and plays ...Nc7 and ...Qd5. [Engine verdict: about +0.3.] } 6... Nxe4 (6... Nc7 7. Nxf6+ gxf6 8. d4 Bg4 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.3).] }) 7. Qxe4 Nc7 (7... Qd5 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.3).] }) 8. d4 (8. Bc4 Ne6 9. O-O g6 { [Engine check: a clear edge for White (about +1.0).] }) 8... Qd5 9. Qxd5 Nxd5 10. c4 Nc7 11. Bf4 Ne6 12. Be3 g6 13. O-O-O Bg7 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.4).] }) 6... Nxe4 { 6...Nxe4 simply trades off the strong centralized knight before it becomes a long-term asset for White. } 7. dxe4 e5 { 7...e5 stakes out the center immediately and equalizes comfortably; Black's minor pieces have clear, natural squares (Be7/Bc5, Nc7-e6 or b4) and there is nothing to memorize beyond normal development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.4), in the normal range for this chapter.] } 8. Qd2 $10 { White offers the queen trade and aims to win the e5 pawn after Nxe5 (about 0.2). } (8. Qe3 f6 9. Bc4 b5 10. Bb3 Nc5 11. O-O Be7 12. Nh4 Qd4 { Qe3 is a more cautious alternative where ...f6 keeps your centre solid. }) 8... Qxd2+ 9. Bxd2 Nc5 10. Nxe5 Nxe4 { The knight regains the pawn and attacks the bishop on d2 and the f2 square at once. } 11. Bc4 Bc5 12. f3 Nxd2 { White wins the e5 pawn with Nxe5, but your knight regains it with tempo. }) (5. Ng3 { 5.Ng3 retreats the knight to safety rather than trading on f6 immediately, keeping more pieces on the board. } 5... c5 (5... h5 { The h-pawn gamble: 5.Ng3 h5 6.Bc4 h4 7.Ne2 b5 is double-edged and Black must be exact. [Engine verdict: about -0.4, but the position is very sharp.] RISKY. } 6. Bc4 h4 7. Ne2 b5 8. Bb3 c5 9. d3 h3 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 6. Bc4 Nc6 7. O-O { ...and the position stays level with a normal, balanced middlegame ahead. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a sound fifth-move alternative to the main Nxf6+ trade in this Two Knights move order.] } 7... g6 { You develop with a fianchetto. The plan is ...Bg7 and later ...b6 and ...Bb7, so that your pieces cover the long diagonal and the d5 square before White gets organised. } 8. Ng5 e6 { Ng5 eyes e6 and f7. Playing ...e6 blocks the Bc4 and keeps f7 safe in one move, so there is nothing to fear. } 9. d3 Bg7 10. N5e4 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 b6 { You have traded a pair of knights, which eases your game, and the queenside bishop is ready for b7. } 12. Bg5 $10 { White gets a pin on f6 to play with, but the position is level (about 0.0) and your structure is healthy, so continue with ...Bb7 and ...O-O. }) (5. Nc3 { 5.Nc3 retreats the other knight back, offering to repeat moves or head into a quieter structure. } 5... Bf5 6. d4 e6 { ...and the position is dead level. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a calm, fully sound alternative to the main trade.] } 7. Bd3 $10 { White offers to trade the light-squared bishops. His motive is a calm Queen's Gambit type of position in which Ne4 and Bf4 later press on f6 and c5, so there is nothing to fear: trade, develop and keep your structure. } (7. Be2 h6 8. Bd3 Bxd3 9. Qxd3 Qc7 10. Bd2 Nbd7 11. O-O-O Be7 $10 { Be2 only transposes into the same kind of position. }) 7... Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Be7 9. O-O O-O 10. Ne4 { The knight eyes f6 and c5 and aims to remove your best defender; just keep developing. } 10... Nbd7 11. Bf4 Qb6 $10 { The queen hits b2 and d4 and prepares ...c5 or ...Nd5. Neither side has a target and the position is level (about +0.1), so you are free to choose your plan. }) (5. d3 { 5.d3 supports the knight on e4 quietly rather than committing it to a trade or a retreat. } 5... Na6 6. Be3 Bf5 7. Ng3 { ...and White keeps a small, pleasant edge. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3), a sound, solid fifth-move try.] } 7... Bg6 $14 { Retreat the bishop to keep it safe and protect the h5 square. It stays on the b1-h7 diagonal, and White's edge is small (about 0.3). } (7... Be6 8. Be2 g6 9. O-O Bg7 10. d4 Nc7 11. Re1 O-O 12. h3 { Placing the bishop on e6 blocks your central play and lets White develop freely, which gives a small White edge (about 0.8). }) 8. c3 e6 9. d4 Nb8 { Re-route the awkward knight toward d7. } 10. Qb3 Qb6 11. Ne5 Nd5 12. Bd2 { You have a solid position and the knight on d5 is great. White keeps a tiny pull but there are no immediate threats. }) 5... exf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge7f6] } 6. d4 { d4 leads to a small White edge (+0.3): Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } (6. Bc4 { 6.Bc4 develops actively toward f7 immediately after the trade, rather than the calmer d4/Nf3 setups. } 6... Bd6 7. Qe2+ (7. O-O O-O 8. d4 Re8 { The solid main line: Black has a safe structure and ...Nd7-b6 gives play on the queenside. [Engine verdict: about -0.3 to 0.0, equal.] } 9. c3 (9. h3 Nd7 10. c3 Nf8 11. Be3 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.10).] }) 9... Nd7 10. Re1 Rxe1+ 11. Qxe1 Nb6 12. Bd3 Bg4 13. Qe4 f5 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 7... Qe7 8. Qxe7+ { ...and the position remains balanced, with White's slightly easier development offset by Black's extra central pawn presence. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.25), a sound alternative in this Two Knights move order.] } 8... Kxe7 { With the queens off, your king is safe in the centre and you keep both bishops available. Your plan is quick development: ...Rd8, ...Nd7 and ...Ne5 to hit the Bc4. } (8... Bxe7 9. d3 Nd7 10. O-O b5 11. Bb3 a5 12. a4 Nc5 13. Nd4 $14 { Also playable (about +0.3), but the king stays on e8 and White gets a free hand with Nd4. }) 9. d3 Rd8 10. a4 Nd7 11. O-O Ne5 12. Re1 Be6 13. Nxe5 { White trades before you can hit the bishop on c4. Recapture with the bishop or the f-pawn and you are fine; the position stays level to a small White edge (about +0.2). }) 6... Bd6 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf8d6] } 7. Bd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 7... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 8. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 8... Be6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Gc8e6] } 9. Be3 { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gc1e3] } 9... Nd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 10. Qd2 { Qd2 leads to a small Black edge (-0.3): Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gd1d2] } (10. c4 $14 { Engine-suggested line (Stockfish 16, depth 20), not in the original chapter. This is White's strongest try here (about +0.23); the chapter's own Qd2 scores about -0.18. Review before relying on it. } 10... Re8 11. Qc2 Nf8 12. Nd2 Qc7 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 13. h3 { White takes the g4 square away from your bishop and prepares to bring the knight back to f3. His motive is to keep the c4/d4 pair and slowly make something of your doubled f-pawns. } (13. g3?! Bh3 14. Rfe1 Qd7 15. a3 Rad8 16. Ne4 Bc7 17. Rad1 Bg4 $15 { Weakening the kingside this way lets your bishop jump to h3 with a tempo on the f1 rook and stay there, which is why h3 comes first (about -0.3 for White). }) 13... Bf4 { Hitting the Be3 forces a decision about the dark-squared bishops. } 14. Nf3 Bd7 15. Bxf4 Qxf4 16. Qc3 Bf5 17. Bxf5 Qxf5 $14 { You trade off most minor pieces. White keeps a small edge (about +0.2), so aim for ...Ne6 and ...Rad8 and keep your pawns together on the queenside. }) 10... Re8 { [%csl Ge8][%cal Gf8e8] } 11. c4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc2c4] } 11... Nf8 { [%csl Gf8,Rc5,Re1][%cal Gd7f8,Rc4c5,Rf1e1] } 12. Rfe1 { Rfe1 leads to roughly equal (+0.0): Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gf1e1] } (12. c5?! $17 { Well, white do have the option to advance the pawn forward but white is going to suffer from the queenside since the d4 pawn is isolated and therefore weak. Besides that the c5 pawn can easily be broken apart once the d4 pawn is pressured. Also the light square from d5-h1 has been weakened.  [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores -1.00; Qc2 scores +0.00, so Qc2 was stronger] [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc4c5] } 12... Bc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd6c7] } 13. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be3f4] } 13... Ng6 { [%csl Bf4,Bg6][%cal Bf8g6,Bg6f4,Bc7f4] } 14. Bg3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bf4g3] } 14... Bd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be6d5] } 15. Bc2 { [%csl Bc2][%cal Bd3c2] } 15... Qd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bd8d7] } 16. Rfe1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 16... Rad8 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.4). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] [%csl Bd8][%cal Ba8d8] } 17. Rxe8+ { White trades a pair of rooks because your Bd5 and Bc7 are aimed at his kingside and the c5 pawn is fixed, so fewer pieces means less pressure. } (17. Qc3 h5 { The pawn push threatens ...h4 and the Bg3 has no good square. } 18. a3 Bxg3 19. hxg3 Bxf3 20. Rxe8+ Rxe8 21. gxf3 f5 $15 { Your pieces are better placed and the damaged f3 pawn is a weakness (about -0.6). }) 17... Rxe8 18. Re1 Rxe1+ 19. Nxe1 Nf8 20. Bxc7 Qxc7 21. Nd3 Ne6 $15 { You keep a small edge (about -0.5). The knight on e6 and the bishop on d5 dominate the board and White has no active plan to speak of. }) 12... Qd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gd8d7] } 13. Rad1 { [%csl Gd1][%cal Ga1d1] } 13... Rad8 $10 { The opening phase is over and Black stands very well: the rooks are active on the d- and e-files, the bishop pair is in place, and Black has an extra knight available for defence. } { [%csl Gd8,Gd1,Ge1][%cal Ga8d8,Gd8d1,Ge8e1] } 14. Be2 $15 { Engine line (Stockfish 16, depth 18), not in the original chapter: White's strongest continuation with Black's best replies. Review before relying on it. } (14. b3 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 18, White's point of view): this is the chapter's original move; it scores -0.68, while Be2 scores -0.10, so it costs White about 0.6. The chapter's original line continues here as a variation.] [%csl Gb3][%cal Gb2b3] } 14... Bg4 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] [%csl Gg4,Gd1,Gd4][%cal Ge6g4,Gg4d1] } 15. Be2 $15 { This is White's best try, since Bg4 threatens to take on f3. His motive is to add a defender to f3 so that ...Bxf3 can always be answered with Bxf3, and to get the bishop off the d3-h7 diagonal. } (15. c5?! Bc7 16. Be2 Ng6 17. a3 Ne7 18. Bf4 Bxf4 19. Qxf4 Nd5 $17 { Pushing the pawn looks active but it hands you the d5 square and the pressure on d4 is gone (about -1.0). }) 15... Ng6 16. Kh1 h5 { Your h-pawn gains space and White's pieces are awkward. } 17. Ng1 Bc7 18. Qb4 Bb8 19. Qc3 Ne7 $15 { You keep the edge (about -0.6): your knight is heading for f5 and White still has no plan. }) (14. Qc2 $10 { Engine-suggested line (Stockfish 16, depth 20), not in the original chapter. This is White's strongest try here (about -0.16); the chapter's own b3 scores about -0.66. Review before relying on it. } 14... Bc7 15. Nh4 g6 16. Qc3 b5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 17. cxb5 { White opens the c-file and the centre. His motive is the d5 break, which would attack your Be6 and try to pin the b5 pawn and the Bc7 together. } (17. d5?! Bg4 18. Rd2 f5 19. Qf6 cxd5 20. cxd5 Qe7 $15 { The queen invasion looks scary but you simply trade queens and the d5 pawn is a weakness (about -0.6). }) 17... cxb5 18. d5 Bxd5 19. Bxb5 Qxb5 20. Qxc7 Qxb2 21. Qxa7 Ne6 $15 { Both sides grab pawns and queens run through the other camp. Material stays even and your bishop on d5 and the knight on e6 keep a small edge (about -0.2). }) 14... Ng6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 15. Qc3 { White lines up the queen behind d4 and plans d5 to gain space. } (15. d5 cxd5 16. cxd5 Bg4 17. Nd4 Bxe2 18. Nxe2 Bb8 19. Ng3 f5 $15 { Playing d5 at once simply opens the position for you (about -0.4). }) 15... Bg4 { The pin on f3 means d5 will cost White time. } 16. d5 c5 { You lock the centre. The d6 bishop is a little passive, but your knights get the f5 and e7 squares. } 17. Kh1 h5 18. Ng1 Ne7 19. h3 Nf5 $15 { You keep a small edge (about -0.3), with the knight on f5 well placed and ...h4 still in reserve. } *


