1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Karpov treatment of the Two Knights Attack features a fianchettoed bishop on b7 aiming at White's center along the long diagonal, plus a half-open d-file for Black. This is a flexible, strategically sound setup: Black's plan is queenside expansion (...b6/...c5, already underway at the target) combined with pressure on d4 from the fianchetto bishop, while White's half-open e-file provides the only real counterplay. Keep the dark-squared bishop active on the long diagonal rather than trading it off, it's doing more work than any other piece in this structure.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.2 to +0.3, a small White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd2+? at move 4 loses material (about 7.7); 10...Re8?! (about 0.6; prefer ...c5); 11...Qc8?! (about 0.6; prefer ...c5); 8...Qc7?! (about 0.6; prefer ...c5); 8...h6?! (about 0.5; prefer ...c5).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 7.Ng3? Be7 (costs White about 1.0); 12.Be4?! Nxe4 (costs White about 0.6; engine-found, not in the chapter tree).
Chapter #24 continues the Two Knights Attack. Other Two Knights Attack chapters: #22, #25, #26, #27. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nc3 $14 { The Two Knights Variation is a positional and solid opening against the Caro Kann. White develops two pieces ahead of black and is looking forward to an easy play. Since white has developed two pieces ahead of black, black should catch up in development right away. Black has to take the pawn so white's knight would be forced to waste a tempo followed by Nbd7 to Nf6. Following the Caro Kann Karpov Variation. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 3... dxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 4... Nd7 { [%csl Gd7,Rd4,Ge2,Gg3,Rc4][%cal Gb8d7,Rd2d4,Rf1e2,Rd1e2,Rg2g3,Rf1c4] } 5. d4 { d4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.7), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } (5. Qe2 $14 { White is aiming for the opening trap of Nd6 checkmate. Either ways this isn't white's one any only plan. White also wants to dominate the e file once white castles kingside or queenside followed by either rook to e1. White is going to develop is pieces with ease. Black should also do the same, just beware of checkmating ideas. } { [%csl Be2,Bf6][%cal Bd1e2,Be4f6] } 5... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 6. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 6... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 7. Nxf6+ { Nxf6+ leads to a modest White edge (+0.7): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s). } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be4f6] } (7. Ng3 $10 { White may even try to keep the tension on the center and avoid exchanging minor pieces to further complicate the game. } 7... Be7 8. Bd2 O-O 9. O-O-O b5 10. h4 a5 11. h5 h6 $15 { Eventually, both opposing sides would attack each other's king. Black has to expand his control on the queenside to find counterplay at white's king. White may try to launch a devastating pawn storm around black's barricaded kingside structure. } 12. Ne5 { White's knight jumps in to hit the d7 knight and the f7 pawn; his motive is to keep the h-pawn storm alive with Ng6 ideas before your queenside play arrives. } 12... Bb7 13. Ng6 { A trade offer that attacks e7 and f8; answer calmly. } 13... Re8 14. Nxe7+ Qxe7 15. Ne4 b4 { You gain space on the queenside, which is your counter to the h-pawn. } 16. Nxf6+ Nxf6 $15 { You keep a small edge (about -0.3) and have ...a4 and ...b3 coming, aimed at the white king on c1. }) 7... Nxf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd7f6] } 8. Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bc1f4] } 8... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 9. Bxd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf4d6] } 9... Qxd6 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: c3 b6 Nd2 Bb7 Nc4 Qd5 } { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bd8d6] } 10. O-O-O { [%csl Bc1][%cal Be1c1] } 10... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 11. h4 $14 { Once White launches a pawn storm on the kingside, Black should also attack on the queenside with a pawn storm of his own; it is a race, and both sides must watch out for mating threats. } 11... b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 12. h5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh4h5] } 12... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 13. Qe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Be2e5] } 13... Qd5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd6d5] } 14. b3 { Your queen on d5 hits a2, so White closes the diagonal and keeps his king safe, while Qe5 and the h5 pawn keep your kingside cramped. } (14. Qxd5 exd5 15. Ne5 Ne4 16. Nd3 Re8 17. Re1 Bf5 18. Be2 a5 $10 { The calm alternative gives a tiny White edge (about +0.2) and your bishop on f5 is active. }) 14... a5 { You start your own queenside play. } 15. Qxd5 exd5 16. Ne5 Ne4 17. Nxc6 Nxf2 { A knight fork: ...Nxf2 attacks both rooks. } 18. Ne7+ Kh7 { White must see that the knight on e7 comes with check before you take a rook; the position is sharp but balanced, only about +0.3 for White. }) (5. Be2 $10 { Be2 another alternative for white. Although this move may appear passive. White is aiming for a positional and solid gameplay rather than a dynamic game. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 5... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 6. d3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd2d3] } 6... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 7. dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } 7... e5 { [%csl Be5,Bg1,Bc5][%cal Be7e5,Be1g1,Bf8c5] } 8. O-O { O-O leads to a small White edge (+0.3): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } (8. Bc4 $14 { White is aiming for the f7 pawn. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Be2c4] } 8... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 9. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 9... b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 10. Bb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc4b3] } 10... a5 { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 11. a4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Ba2a4] } 11... bxa4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Bb5a4] } 12. Bxa4 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Bb3a4] } 12... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7,Be5][%cal Bd8c7,Bc7e5,Bd7e5] } 13. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 13... O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 14. Qd2 { White unpins and prepares c3 and b4 to harass the c5 knight. His bishop on a4 is a little loose, which is the thing to remember. } (14. Bb3 Ba6 15. Re1 Nc5 16. Qc1 Nxb3 17. cxb3 Bb4 18. Bd2 Rfb8 $10) 14... Bb4 { Forcing c3 makes d3 a hole. } 15. c3 Be7 16. Qc2 Nc5 17. b4 axb4 18. cxb4 Nxa4 $10 { The a4 bishop is traded off and the position is level (about +0.1). }) 8... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7,Be5][%cal Bd8c7,Bc7e5,Bd7e5] } 9. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 9... Bd6 $14 { Black has created a battery of Bishop and Queen. This can unleash a potential attack after black opens up the b8-h2 diagonal. } { [%csl Bc7,Bd6][%cal Bd6h2,Bf8d6] } 10. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bf3d2] } 10... O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 11. Bg4 { A forcing way to trade pieces: Bg4 hits the d7 knight, so you have to answer actively. } 11... Bc5 12. Bxd7 Bxe3 13. Bxc8 Bxd2 14. Bxb7 Qxb7 15. Qxd2 Qxb2 $10 { The flurry of captures leaves material even and the position equal (about +0.1). Your queen picks up b2 and attacks the a1 rook, which gives you the final tempo. }) (5. g3 $10 { g3 is another option for white, a positional and solid way to meet the Caro Kann. } { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 5... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 6. Nxf6+ { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be4f6] } 6... Nxf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd7f6] } 7. Bg2 { [%csl Bg2][%cal Bf1g2] } 7... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 8. h3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 8... Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 9. Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg2f3] } 9... e5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Be7e5] } 10. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 10... Bc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 11. d3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd2d3] } 11... O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 12. Kg2 { Kg2 prepares Bd2 and Qe2, keeps the h3 pawn safe and lets White use the bishop pair slowly with a4 and Rae1. } 12... Qd6 13. a4 a5 { You stop the queenside expansion. } 14. Bd2 Rfe8 15. Qe2 h6 16. Rfe1 Re7 $10 { Your centre is solid and you can play ...Bb4 to trade the dark bishop. The balance is level to a tiny White edge (about +0.2). }) (5. Bc4 $14 { Here White tries another way to keep to the e4-e5 principle. This line is less challenging than others, causing Black only a temporary problem with f7. Black plays ...e6, ...Be7 and ...0-0 to maintain equality. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 5... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 6. Neg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Be4g5] } 6... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7. Qe2 $14 { White wants to take on f7 with the knight, because after ...Qe6+ Black gets mated. } { [%csl Be2,Bd6,Bd5][%cal Bd1e2,Bf8d6,Bf6d5] } 7... Nd5 $14 { Black should play ...Nd5 to cover against the Nxf7 idea. ...Nb6 is an alternative, but the knight does little on the queenside; ...Nd5 followed by ...Bf7 is the better order. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } (7... Bd6?? $18 { Black has fallen for the Nxf7 trap and is effectively lost. } { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 8. Nxf7 { [%csl Bf7][%cal Bg5f7] } 8... Kxf7 { [%csl Bf7][%cal Be8f7] } 9. Qxe6+ { [%csl Be6][%cal Be2e6] } 9... Kg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf7g6] } 10. Qf7+ { [%csl Bf7][%cal Be6f7] } 10... Kh6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bg6h6] } 11. d3+ { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd2d3] } 11... Bf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bd6f4] } 12. Bxf4+ { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bc1f4] } 12... g5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bg7g5] } 13. Bxg5# { [Engine verdict: checkmate.] [%csl Bg5][%cal Bf4g5] }) 8. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 9. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 9... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. Qd3! $14 { White threatens mate in one. The only good defence is ...N7f6; ...N5f6? would be a serious mistake since Black's position would be cramped. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Be2d3] } 10... N7f6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd7f6] } 11. Ne5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 11... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 12. Ngf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg5f3] } 12... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 13. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 13... b6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 14. Re1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf1e1] } 14... Bb7 { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bc8b7] } 15. Bd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } 15... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 16. Nxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bf3d4] } 16... Bd6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7).] [%csl Bd6][%cal Be7d6] } 17. Qh3 { White's queen eyes e6 and h6, and with Rae1 and g4-g5 to follow it is a direct kingside attack. } (17. Rad1 Qb8 18. Qg3 Nh5 19. Qh4 Bxe5 20. Qxh5 Bf4 21. Bxd5 Bxd5 $10 { A slower move lets you trade the dangerous pieces (about +0.1). }) 17... Bxe5 { Trade the attacking knight. } 18. Rxe5 Qc7 19. Bxd5 Bxd5 20. Rae1 Kh7 21. g4 Rad8 $14 { White keeps the initiative (about +0.8), so you must defend carefully; the king goes to h7 and the rook to d8. }) 5... Ngf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 6. Ng3 $14 { The game returns to a Classical Caro-Kann. } { [%csl Gg3][%cal Ge4g3] } 6... e6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 7. Bd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 7... Be7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gf8e7] } 8. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 8... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 9. Bf4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gc1f4] } 9... b6 { [%csl Gb6,Gb7][%cal Gb7b6,Gc8b7] } 10. Re1 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gf1e1] } 10... Bb7 { [%csl Gb7,Gh1][%cal Gc8b7,Gb7h1] } 11. Qe2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gd1e2] } 11... c5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc6c5] } 12. Rad1 { White aims for d4-d5 and a central initiative, with the Bf4 and the knights helping. } (12. Be5?! cxd4 13. Bxd4 Nc5 14. Bxc5 Bxc5 15. Ne4 Bxe4 16. Bxe4 Rc8 $15 { Taking the d4 pawn and trading bishops leaves you better placed on the c-file (about -0.2). }) 12... Qc8 13. Nf5 { The point is a sacrifice: if you take with 13...exf5 then 14.Qxe7 wins the bishop back, and your structure is damaged. } 13... exf5 14. Qxe7 Re8 15. Qd6 Ne4 { Your knight hits the queen and the position stays near equal (about +0.2). } 16. Bxe4 Bxe4 $10 *


