1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Classical treatment of the Two Knights Attack reaches a fairly standard, balanced structure (half-open d-file for Black, e-file for White) with Black fianchettoed on g7 instead of using the more common ...Bd6/...Be7 setups seen elsewhere in the repertoire. The practical plan centers on the long diagonal and a timely ...c5 or ...e5 break once development is complete; because the structure is sound and symmetrical in spirit, patience and piece activity matter more than memorized tactics here.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.1 to +0.2, roughly equal. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd4? on moves 7 to 11 loses material (about 6.7); ...Qxa5? at move 12 loses material (about 6.5); 5...g6? (about 1.3; prefer ...Nf6); 4...Bc8? (about 0.9; prefer ...Bxf3); 4...Bh5?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Bxf3); 5...d4?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Nf6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 11.Nd6+? Bxd6 (costs White about 1.2).
Chapter #25 continues the Two Knights Attack. Other Two Knights Attack chapters: #22, #23, #26, #27. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #1. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. Nc3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } (3. exd5 { White trades pawns at once to open the game. The idea is to use the knight already on c3 for quick development, then play d4 and Nf3 and hope you misplace a piece. It is very common in practice, so be ready for it. Your only question is which recapture keeps your structure healthy. } 3... cxd5 $10 { Recapture with the c-pawn. You keep a solid pawn on d5 and a half-open c-file for a rook later. Because the knight stands on c3, White cannot support d4 with c3, so the center is a little less stable. Plan: ...Nc6, then ...a6 to stop Nb5 jumps, then ...e6 and ...Bd6 or ...Be7, and castle short. Your breaks are ...e5 and ...f6 against a pawn on e5. } (3... Nf6?! { The natural developing move is the tempting reply, but White takes on c6 and gets a lead in the center and in development. } 4. dxc6 Nxc6 5. Nf3 e5 6. Bc4) 4. d4 Nc6 5. Nf3 a6 6. Ne5 Nxe5 { When White puts a knight on e5, trade it. It is White's most active piece and you do not want it jumping on to other squares. } 7. dxe5 e6 8. Bd3 { Now the e5 pawn is cramped and becomes a target. Hit it with ...Nd7, ...Qc7 and the lever ...f6, and bring your bishop out to d7 or pin the knight with ...Bb4. Overall: your structure is healthy and the plan against e5 is easy. This is a Caro-Kann Exchange structure with White's pawn on e5 locked against your pawns on d5 and e6, and the bishops of both sides still on the board. White has a small space edge, but the pawn on e5 can become a target. Your plan: develop the knight with ...Nd7, bring the queen to c7 to hit e5, and play ...f6 to undermine it, with ...Bb4 or ...Bd7 for your bishop. White wants Bg5, Qg4 or Qh5 for a kingside attack, and h7 is the target. The mistake is playing ...f6 before you are developed, since Qh5+ becomes annoying. If White plays Qh5, answer calmly and keep your king flexible. }) (3. e5 { White pushes the pawn to gain space and cramp your pieces, planning d3 and f4 and a quick Nf3 and d4. It is very common in practice and White is hoping for a closed game where you have no space. For you the fight is for the center. } 3... d4 $10 { Kick the knight and gain space at once. Plan: ...c5, ...Nc6, ...Bd7 and ...e6, then castle short. Your pawn chain on c5-d4 gives you a space advantage, and your pieces can use e5 and f5. Aim for the break ...f6 or ...Qb6 to hit b2. Avoid leaving the pawn on d4 without support, and avoid trading all your pieces, because the space you gain is your main trump. If White plays d3 early you can strike with ...c5 and ...Nc6 at once, and if White castles you can safely do the same. White's usual alternative is to play f4 and Nf3, and then you hold d4 with ...c5 and ...Nc6. } (3... Bf5 { Also playable and keeps the d5 pawn. } 4. d4 e6 5. Nf3 Nd7) 4. Nce2 c5 5. f4 Nc6 6. Nf3 Bd7 7. Ng3 e6 8. Be2 { Your center is solid and your pieces go to e7, f5 and g6 as needed. Overall: balanced with a clear plan. This is a closed centre where your pawn chain c5-d4 gives you space on the queenside, and White has the pawn chain e5-f4. Both kings are likely to castle short. Your plan: play ...Nge7, then ...Qb6 to hit b2, and ...Nf5 or ...Ng6 to hold your centre. White wants c3 to undermine d4, Bb5 and h4 to attack your king, so you hit back with ...Qb6 and ...f6. The mistake is leaving d4 unprotected; after c3 take with ...dxc3. If White deviates with Bb5, keep the knight on c6 protected with ...Be7. }) 3... Bg4 $14 { This is the Classical System of the Caro Kann Defence against the standard "Two Knights Attack". Black's suppressive light square bishop is trying to loosen white's grasp on the center. } { [%csl Gg4,Re2,Rh3][%cal Gc8g4,Rf1e2,Rh2h3] } 4. h3 { h3 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gh3][%cal Gh2h3] } (4. Be2 $14 { Another interesting sideline: White does not want Black to restrict White's piece activity, and Black will find it hard to counter White's centre. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 4... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6,Be5,Bd3][%cal Bg8f6,Be4e5,Bd2d3] } 5. e5 { e5 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } (5. d3 $10 { d3, white wants to keep the pressure on the center. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd2d3] } 5... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 6. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 6... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bg4f5] } 7. Nxf6+ { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be4f6] } 7... exf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Be7f6] } 8. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 9. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd3d4] } 9... O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. c4 { White builds a Queen's Gambit structure and targets your doubled f-pawns. } (10. Bd3 Bg6 11. Re1 Re8 12. Rxe8+ Qxe8 13. Be3 Nd7 14. c4 c5 $10) 10... Re8 11. Be3 a5 12. Bd3 Bg6 13. Nh4 { The knight attacks your bishop. } 13... Bxd3 14. Qxd3 g6 $10 { ...g6 stops Nf5 and your position is solid (about +0.2). }) 5... Nfd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bf6d7] } 6. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 6... e6 $14 { A French-type structure has arisen. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 7... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 8. Bg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 8... Qc8 { [%csl Bc8][%cal Bd8c8] } 9. Nb5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bc3b5] } 9... Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 10. Bxf3 { WATCH OUT: Nd6+ looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -3.6 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd6 Bxf3 Bc7 c4 Nc6 cxd5 } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Be2f3] } 10... cxd4 { [%csl Bd4,Bd6][%cal Bc5d4,Bb5d4,Bb5d6] } 11. Nxd4 { Nxd4 leads to roughly equal (-0.1): White retains the bishop pair; White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bb5d4] } (11. Nd6+? $17 { A horrible decision which fall short after Nc6 } { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bb5d6] } 11... Bxd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 12. exd6 { WATCH OUT: Qxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -5.2 from White's own side of the board), after: Nc6 Qxd5 exd5 exd6 Nce5 c3 } { [%csl Bd6][%cal Be5d6] } 12... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6,Bd4][%cal Bb8c6,Bc6d4] } 13. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 13... dxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bd4c3] } 14. bxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb2c3] } 14... O-O $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.3). Notable features: Black is a pawn up; White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 15. c4 { White tries to open lines to compensate for the pawn; the d6 pawn is his spearhead. } (15. Re1 Nb6 16. Bf4 Qd8 17. Rb1 Qf6 18. Bg3 Rad8 19. a4 Qxc3 $19 { The c3 pawn drops (about -1.6). }) 15... dxc4 16. Re1 Nc5 17. Rb1 Rb8 18. Be4 f6 { Kick the Bg5 out; ...Rf7 follows and the d6 pawn will fall. } 19. Bf4 Rf7 $17 { Black is clearly better (about -1.3). }) 11... Nc6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 12. c4 { White's idea is to undermine d5 and use the bishop pair. } (12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. c4 h6 14. Bf4 Qb7 15. Rc1 Be7 16. Qe2 O-O $14 { Another try, about +0.2. }) 12... Nxd4 13. Qxd4 Bc5 14. Qc3 d4 { Gaining space with tempo on the queen. } 15. Qb3 Qc7 $14 { White can snap the b7 pawn, but the open lines give you compensation and the engine still gives only about +0.3. }) (4. d4 { 4.d4 (instead of the quieter 4.Be2 or 4.h3) - grabs the center immediately while the pin on f3 keeps White from castling right away. } 4... e6 { 4...e6 is simplest: finish development and meet White's center with a solid structure; the pin means White still needs an extra move (Be2 or h3) before castling. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.2).] } 5. Be2 { White unpins and prepares to castle; the pin means he needs this extra move first. } (5. a3 dxe4 6. Nxe4 Bxf3 7. Qxf3 Qxd4 8. Bd3 Nd7 9. Be3 Qd5 $10 { Grabbing the d4 pawn is safe here, White's only compensation is development (about +0.1). }) 5... dxe4 6. Nxe4 Nd7 7. O-O Ngf6 8. Nxf6+ Nxf6 9. h3 Bf5 $14 { White has a little extra space (about +0.4), but you have no weaknesses. Play ...Be7, ...O-O and ...c5 and the position is fine. }) (4. exd5 { White opens the position and takes on d5, hoping your bishop on g4 is loose. White often follows with h3 to challenge it. It is very common in practice. For you the plan is to recapture with the pawn and keep trading on f3. } 4... cxd5 $10 { Recapture with the pawn. Plan: ...Bxf3 when White plays h3, then ...Nf6 and ...Nc6, ...e6, and castle short. Your break is ...e5 later. Avoid ...Nf6 now, because White gets the center with d4 and a free game. } (4... Nf6?! { Tempting but loses a pawn structure battle: White takes on c6 and gets a big lead. } 5. dxc6 Nxc6 6. h3 Bh5 7. g4 Bg6) 5. h3 Bxf3 6. Qxf3 Nf6 7. d4 Nc6 { Your knights hit d4 and pressure the position. } 8. Be3 e6 9. Bd3 { You have a solid setup, with all pieces developed and castling short next. If White tries to avoid trades with Bg5 or Qe2 you still play ...Be7 and ...O-O, and keep ...Rc8 for the open c-file. The long-term plan is to pressure the pawn on d4 with ...Nc6 and ...Rc8, while White tries to hold the isolated-looking center with Rd1. Overall: equal. This is a Caro-Kann Exchange position with symmetrical pawns on d4 and d5 and a half-open c-file for you. White has a slight space advantage, but you can challenge it. Your plan: play ...Nb4 to hit the bishop on d3 and the pawn on c2, then ...Be7 or ...Bd6, castle short, and ...Qd7 and ...Rc8. White wants a3 to kick the knight and then Bb5 or a minority attack with b4. If a3 comes, take ...Nxd3+ and recapture. The mistake is playing ...Nb4 without a follow-up, so know your next move. If White plays Bb5+, block with ...Nc6. }) 4... Bxf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg4f3] } 5. Qxf3 { WATCH OUT: exd5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.7 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 dxc6 Nxc6 Nxd1 e6 Bb5 } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gd1f3] } 5... e6 $14 { A very solid position, black's solid position would have been a very difficult task to crack. White sure do have the advantage due to his piece development but black do have some attacking chances on the queenside. } { [%csl Ge6,Gd5,Gc6,Gb7,Gf7][%cal Ge7e6,Gf7d5,Gb7d5] } 6. d4 $14 { Also possible: d4 (+29), a close alternative the engine also approves of at real depth. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } (6. Be2 $14 { This exact tabiya is reached in this course's own Chapter #24 (Classical Variation of the Two Knights) - and independent fresh engine analysis at depth 28 confirms the course's own choice (d4) is essentially tied for best, only 2 centipawns behind the engine's nominal top pick and well within normal engine noise at this depth. Another direct validation: your existing repertoire choice here is objectively correct, not merely reasonable. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 6... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 7. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 7... Bc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 8. Rd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bf1d1] } 8... Bd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 9. Qf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bf3f4] } 9... Bxc3 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bc3][%cal Bd4c3] } 10. bxc3 { White accepts the structural damage in exchange for the bishop pair and open b-file. } (10. exd5? Ba5 11. dxe6 fxe6 12. Qe5 Qd5 13. d4 O-O 14. c4 Qxe5 $19 { Ignoring the recapture loses material (about -2.7). }) 10... Nxe4 11. Ba3 { The bishop on a3 prevents castling because it covers f8. } 11... Nd7 12. Rab1 b6 13. c4 Qf6 14. Qe3 Qg5 $14 { White gets an edge (about +0.4), but you can still play ...Bb7. }) (6. g3 $14 { Also possible: g3 (+24), a close alternative the engine also approves of at real depth. } { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 6... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 7. Qe3 { Qe3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1): White retains the bishop pair. } { [%csl Be3][%cal Bf3e3] } (7. Bg2 { 8.Bg2 - fianchettoes immediately instead of the main 8.Qe3, aiming the bishop at the long diagonal before Black can block it with ...c5 or ...Bc5. } 7... Bc5 { 8...Bc5 (Solid & Optimal) - develops to an active, flexible square and prepares to castle without any immediate commitments; straightforward and easy to play. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), the calmer of the two.] } (7... d4 { 8...d4 (Complicated & Imbalanced) - grabs space in the center right away, closing the long diagonal and steering for a sharper, more committal structure rather than quiet development. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4), riskier but with real winning chances for Black if White misplays the resulting structure.] } 8. Ne2 { White attacks d4 at once. His motive is to remove the pawn that cramps him before you can support it, and the Qf3 also eyes the b7 pawn. } (8. O-O? dxc3 9. bxc3 Bd6 10. Rb1 Rb8 11. Qg4 h5 12. Qe2 Ne7 $19 { Castling leaves the c3 knight en prise to the d4 pawn, and the rook on b1 does not repay a whole piece (about -2.1). }) 8... Qb6 { Defends d4 and hits b2 in one move. } 9. O-O Ne5 { The knight hits the queen and takes a central square for good. } 10. Qh5 Bd6 { Bd6 supports e5 so you are not forced to give up the knight. } 11. d3 h6 12. Nf4 g5 $10 { The knight on f4 is kicked away and you keep the d4 pawn that gives you space. White is about level (about +0.1) and you have the easier game, the only worry being the weaknesses ...g5 leaves behind. }) 8. O-O { White finishes development before you can strike with ...d4, which is less attractive now that the Bg2 looks down the long diagonal. } (8. Na4 Be7 9. Qe2 Qa5 10. b3 Bf6 11. c3 Ne7 12. O-O dxe4 $10 { Na4 chases the bishop but you retreat with tempo and nothing has changed (about +0.3). }) 8... Ngf6 9. Qe2 O-O { You castle at once and the king is safe. } 10. d3 dxe4 11. dxe4 e5 { You take the centre and block the Bg2 diagonal. } 12. Nb1 Qc7 $10 { White wanted Qe2 and d3 to support e4 and now has to reroute the knight from b1. The position is balanced with a small White edge (about +0.4), so complete development with ...Rad8 and ...Rfe8 and watch the c5 bishop. }) 7... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 8. Bg2 { [%csl Bg2][%cal Bf1g2] } 8... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 9. Qxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Be3e4] } 9... Nf5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bf5][%cal Be7f5] } 10. O-O { White castles and keeps the bishop pair. His motive is to open the long diagonal with d4 and c3 and to use the extra space, while the Qe4 hits the b7 pawn. } (10. Qe2 Be7 11. d3 O-O 12. O-O Qb6 13. Ne4 Nf6 14. Kh2 a5 $10 { A slower alternative that reaches the same structure (about +0.3). }) 10... Be7 11. Ne2 O-O 12. d4 Nf6 { The knight hits the queen with tempo. } 13. Qd3 Qb6 { You aim at b2 and d4, and the queen is well placed for ...c5. } 14. c3 Rad8 $10 { White has the bishop pair for a small edge (about +0.3), but your pieces are active and ...c5 is a natural break; do not rush it, first complete development with ...Rfe8. }) 6... dxe4 { dxe4 leads to roughly equal (+0.1): Black has a material edge of about 1 point(s); Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } (6... Nf6 $14 { Nf6 leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White retains the bishop pair. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 7. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 7... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 8. Qe3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bf3e3] } 8... Nbd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 9. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 9... Nxe4 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 10. Qxe4 { Forced: the knight on e4 is hanging and White must take it back, otherwise he has simply lost a piece. } (10. g4? Nd6 11. c4 h5 12. c5 Nc8 13. Qb3 Qc7 14. Qf3 hxg4 $19 { Not taking back leaves White a knight down (about -4.3). }) 10... Nf6 11. Qd3 Be7 12. O-O O-O 13. a4 Qc7 14. a5 c5 $14 { White has the bishop pair for a small edge (about +0.4), but ...c5 gives you counterplay. }) 7. Qxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf3e4] } 7... Nf6 { [%cal Gg8f6] } 8. Qe3 { [%csl Gf6,Ge3][%cal Ge4e3] } 8... g6 { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gg7g6] } 9. Bd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 9... Bg7 { [%csl Gg7][%cal Gf8g7] } 10. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 10... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 11. a4 { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga2a4] } 11... Nbd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 12. a5 { [%csl Ga5][%cal Ga4a5] } 12... a6 $14 { Black's position is very solid and Black can even play for a win if necessary. } { [%csl Ga6][%cal Ga7a6] } 13. Rd1 { White develops the rook and prepares Qe4 or Qf3 hitting Black's centre. } (13. Re1 Nd5 14. Nxd5 exd5 15. g3 Nf6 16. Qf3 Re8 17. Bd2 Ne4 $10) 13... Nd5 14. Qe4 Re8 15. Qf3 Qe7 16. Bf1 Rad8 17. Na4 e5 $10 { Your solid structure holds (about +0.2) and ...e5 is the break. } *


