1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Dano-Russian (Bronstein-Larsen) treatment of the Two Knights Attack is the sharpest chapter in this complex and, per the course's own benchmark, the one line in the whole repertoire where best play gives White a clear, not marginal, edge (roughly +1.1 to +1.9). The structure (doubled Black f-pawns, isolated h-pawn, half-open d- and g-files) mirrors Chapter #11's aggressive Bronstein-Larsen idea, but here the compensation doesn't fully balance the ledger against accurate defense. Treat this chapter as a practical, surprise-value weapon rather than a theoretically equal main line, and see the Opening Boxes summary for the standing author's-decision flag on whether to keep it in the repertoire at all.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +1.1 to +1.5, a clear White edge. Against best play this line is objectively worse for Black and is only defensible as a practical gamble; consider whether it belongs in the repertoire. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +1 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd2+? at move 4 loses material (about 7.7); ...Qxd4? at move 6 loses material (about 6.3); 14...Qc8? (about 1.3; prefer ...Qxf4); 13...c5?! (about 0.7; prefer ...Qc7); 11...Qe7?! (about 0.7; prefer ...Qb6); 11...Nd7?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Qb6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 15.d5? Bxf4 (costs White about 1.8; engine-found, not in the chapter tree); 15.Be3? f4 (costs White about 1.5; engine-found, not in the chapter tree).
Chapter #26 continues the Two Knights Attack. Other Two Knights Attack chapters: #22, #23, #24, #27. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nc3 $14 { The Two Knights Variation is a positional and solid opening against the Caro Kann. White develops two pieces ahead of black and is looking forward to an easy play. Since white has developed two pieces ahead of black, black should catch up in development right away. Black has to take the pawn so white's knight would be forced to waste a tempo followed by Nbd7 to Nf6. Following the Caro Kann Karpov Variation. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 3... dxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 4... Nf6 $14 { A further option is ...Nf6, which usually ends up in a Classical Caro-Kann after the knight trade and the recapture, followed by d4. That central push is the natural continuation. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 5. Nxf6+ { Nxf6+ leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White has a material edge of about 3 point(s). } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge4f6] } (5. Ng3 { 5.Ng3 retreats the knight to safety rather than trading on f6 immediately, keeping more pieces on the board. } 5... c5 6. Bc4 Nc6 7. O-O { ...and the position stays level with a normal, balanced middlegame ahead. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a sound fifth-move alternative to the main Nxf6+ trade in this Two Knights move order.] } 7... Bg4 { Pinning the f3 knight and getting ready to trade it, which would double White's pawns. } (7... a6 8. Re1 Bg4 9. d3 e6 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Qxf3 Be7 12. c3 $10 { Another solid way to keep the balance (about +0.1). }) 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 e6 { Now the Bc4 and the queen hit f7, which is why ...e6 comes. } 10. Bb5 Rc8 { The rook takes the open c-file. } 11. b3 Be7 12. Bb2 $10 { Balanced (about +0.1): you have a good structure and the plan ...a6, ...O-O and ...d5. }) (5. Nc3 { 5.Nc3 retreats the other knight back, offering to repeat moves or head into a quieter structure. } 5... Bf5 6. d4 e6 { ...and the position is dead level. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (0.0), a calm, fully sound alternative to the main trade.] } 7. Be2 { A modest developing move: White keeps the option of Bd3 and meets ...h6 with a calm retreat. His motive is to keep the structure intact and to castle queenside. } 7... h6 { A useful waiting move that stops Bg5. } 8. Bd3 Bxd3 { Trading White's good bishop. } 9. Qxd3 Qc7 { Qc7 keeps an eye on the g3 and h2 diagonal. } 10. Bd2 Nbd7 11. O-O-O Be7 $10 { White has castled long and the kings are on opposite wings. The position is level (about +0.0). Your plan is ...O-O, ...c5 or ...Nd5 and then pushing in the centre while White starts a pawn storm. }) (5. d3 { 5.d3 supports the knight on e4 quietly rather than committing it to a trade or a retreat. } 5... Na6 6. Be3 Bf5 7. Ng3 { ...and White keeps a small, pleasant edge. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3), a sound, solid fifth-move try.] } 7... Bg6 { Ng3 attacks your Bf5, so the bishop has to move or be traded; ...Bg6 keeps it on the diagonal. } (7... Be6?! 8. Be2 g6 9. O-O Bg7 10. d4 Nc7 11. Re1 O-O 12. h3 $14 { The bishop is passive on e6 and White has a free hand in the centre (about +0.8). }) 8. c3 e6 { Solid, opens the bishop on f8. } 9. d4 Nb8 { The knight on a6 had no real destination, so bring it back and develop it to d7 later. } 10. Qb3 Qb6 { Challenging the queen so that b7 is safe. } 11. Ne5 Nd5 12. Bd2 $14 { White keeps a small edge (about +0.3), but your position is solid and your Nd5 is a fine blockader. Be patient and aim at ...Nd7, ...Bd6 and ...O-O. }) 5... gxf6 $16 { The Bronstein-Larsen Variation of the Two Knights Attack, also called the Dano-Russian Variation. Black accepts doubled pawns and a weakened kingside in return for the open g-file and a flexible position, with play aimed at White's kingside.  [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores +0.94, while exf6 scores +0.18, so it costs about 0.8. It is playable, but it is a concession] [%csl Gf6,Rc4,Rd4][%cal Gg7f6,Rf1c4,Rd2d4] } 6. d4 { d4 leads to a clear White edge (+1.0): Black has doubled pawns; Black has an isolated pawn. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } (6. Bc4 $14 { Bc4 an alternative to d4. With Bc4 white can easily castle kingside and with intentions of Nc3 d5 Be3 and so on. White would usually play g3 to counter white's attack on the kingside!  [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 18, White's point of view): this is the chapter's original move; it scores +0.32, while d4 scores +1.00, so it costs White about 0.7. The chapter's original line continues here as a variation.] [%csl Gc4][%cal Gf1c4] } 6... Rg8 { [%csl Gg8][%cal Gh8g8] } 7. g3 { [%csl Gg3][%cal Gg2g3] } 7... Bh3 $14 { A sign of domination: Black finds a temporary outpost on h3 that stops White castling. White must bring the bishop back to f1, losing a tempo to get rid of the intruder. } { [%csl Gh3][%cal Gc8h3] } 8. d4 { [%csl Gh3,Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 8... Nd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 9. c3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 9... Nb6 { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gd7b6] } 10. Bf1 { [%csl Gf1][%cal Gc4f1] } 10... Qd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gd8d7] } 11. Bxh3 { [%csl Gh3][%cal Gf1h3] } 11... Qxh3 { [%csl Gh3][%cal Gd7h3] } 12. Qe2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gd1e2] } 12... e6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 13. Bd2 { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gc1d2] } 13... O-O-O $14 { A pleasant feature of the Dano-Russian Variation: White has to defend against a quick kingside assault, and Black's play is comfortable and flexible, with queenside castling available. } { [%csl Gc8,Gd1][%cal Ge8c8,Gd8d1] } 14. a4 { White gains queenside space with a4-a5 to kick the Nb6, and with a6 to follow he hopes to attack the long-castled king. } 14... a6 { Stops a5-a6 ideas and secures the b5 square. } 15. a5 Nd5 16. O-O-O { White castles long too. } 16... h5 { Your attack: the h-pawn hurries forward. } 17. Rhe1 Qf5 18. Nh4 Qh7 $10 { The position is balanced (about +0.2) and the kings are on the same wing, so everything depends on who is faster; your h-pawn plus the g-file is a fast attack. }) 6... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 7. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 7... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 8. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... Rg8 { [%csl Bg8][%cal Bh8g8] } 9. c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc2c4] } 9... f5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bf6f5] } 10. g3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 10... h5 $16 { Black has a strong kingside attack. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 11. Qb3 $16 { Engine continuation (Stockfish 16, depth 18), not in the original chapter: best play for both sides, added so that the line reaches the target position. Review before relying on it. } 11... Qb6 12. Qc2 Be7 13. Rd1 Qc7 14. Bf4 Bd6 $16 { [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+1.6). Notable features: Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] } 15. Bxd6 { Your king is stuck in the centre since ...Rg8 gave up castling, so White removes your best defender and opens the position. } (15. Be5?! Nd7 16. Bxd6 Qxd6 17. d5 O-O-O $14 { This gives you time to castle long and White's edge shrinks (about +0.5). }) 15... Qxd6 16. d5 { A break to blast open the centre against the uncastled king. } 16... Nd7 17. Nd4 cxd5 18. cxd5 Qxd5 19. Rac1 Bxe2 $18 { White is clearly better (about +1.6): the rooks come to the c-file while your king is stuck in the centre. If you can, avoid this setup and castle before ...Rg8. } *


