1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. This ...a6 treatment keeps a sound, symmetrical-feeling structure (half-open d-file for Black, e-file for White) with the king staying in the center briefly before castling. Nothing here is forced or especially sharp; the practical plan is ordinary good development, pressure on d4 via the half-open d-file, and a pawn break (...c5 or ...e5, depending on White's setup) once pieces are coordinated. This chapter is a good one to internalize as a 'template' for the quieter Two Knights lines generally.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.6 to +0.8, a modest White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +1 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: 6...g6? (about 1.5; prefer ...e6); 5...Be6? (about 1.0; prefer ...Bxf3); 5...Bc8? (about 0.7; prefer ...Bxf3); 9...Bd6?! (about 0.7; prefer ...Nbd7).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 4.h3?! dxe4 (costs White about 0.7); 4.exd5?! cxd5 (costs White about 0.6).
Chapter #27 continues the Two Knights Attack. Other Two Knights Attack chapters: #22, #23, #24, #25. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nc3 $14 { The Two Knights Variation is a positional and solid opening against the Caro Kann. White develops two pieces ahead of black and is looking forward to an easy play. Since white has developed two pieces ahead of black, black should catch up in development right away. Black has to take the pawn so white's knight would be forced to waste a tempo followed by Nbd7 to Nf6. Following the Caro Kann Karpov Variation. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 3... a6!? $14 { THE ...a6 SYSTEM. This chapter covers a flexible ...a6 idea for sidestepping memorised theory against the Two Knights Attack. First and foremost, a6!? is a waiting move: rather than committing immediately, Black keeps an eye on the b5 square, denying White's pieces that outpost, and waits to see whether White occupies the centre with d4 or resolves the tension with an early exchange on d5. If White plays d4, the game usually transposes into normal Two Knights structures a tempo down for White's would-be b5 ideas; if White exchanges on d5 instead, Black recaptures freely and reaches a comfortable, no-theory position. The whole point of the line is practical: it is easy to remember, hard for White to punish, and it steers the game away from whatever sharp theory White may have prepared.  [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores +0.62, while Bg4 scores +0.25, so it costs about 0.4. It is playable, but it is a concession] [%csl Ga6,Rd4,Rd5,Rh3][%cal Ga7a6,Rd2d4,Re4d5,Rh2h3] } 4. d4 { d4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.8), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } (4. exd5?! $10 { Pawn takes pawn only leads to total equality for both opposing sides. White lets go of his pressure on the center pawn which would lead to a simple game. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be4d5] } 4... cxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc6d5] } 5. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 5... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 6. h3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 6... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 7. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 7... Bxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf5d3] } 8. Qxd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd1d3] } 8... Nf6 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 9. Ne2 { White prepares c3 and Bf4 and avoids ...Nb4 hitting the queen. } 9... e6 10. O-O Bd6 { Solid and natural. } 11. Bf4 O-O 12. c3 b5 13. Rfe1 Qc7 $10 { The position is equal (about 0.0) and quite symmetrical. Play ...b5 and ...Rfc8 or ...Rac8, keep the d5 pawn protected, and try for a queenside play. }) (4. h3 $10 { There's frankly not much to be addressed. Bg4 e6 Bd6 0-0 as usual if h3 isn't played. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 4... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 5. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 5... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 6. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 6... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 7. Ned2 { Ned2 leads to a small White edge (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd2][%cal Be4d2] } (7. Bd3 $14 { Rather than retreating with Ned2, White keeps more pieces on and more central control by playing Bd3 directly, meeting a future ...Nxe4 with a simple recapture. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 7... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 8. Bxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } 8... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd7f6] } 9. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Be4d3] } 9... Bf5 $14 { A small, normal opening pull for White - fully within the range of what's expected from this system, and worth having on record as the more testing option. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 10. Bxf5 { White removes your active bishop at once. His motive is to follow with Qb3 and hit b7, since your queen cannot recapture on f5 yet. } (10. O-O Bxd3 11. Qxd3 e6 12. c4 Bd6 13. Qb3 Qc7 14. Re1 O-O $14 { Declining the trade is slightly less annoying for you (about +0.4). }) 10... Qa5+ { A check that wins the piece back with tempo, because the c3 square is empty and White has to block. } 11. c3 Qxf5 12. O-O e6 { Opens the bishop on f8 and shuts the b1-h7 diagonal against your queen. } 13. Qb3 Qb5 { Offers a queen trade that removes the b7 pressure. } 14. Qc2 Be7 $14 { White keeps a small pull (about +0.4), but you are solid and can castle next. }) 7... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 8. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 8... c5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 9. O-O b6 { You get ready to play ...Bb7 and put pressure on e4. } 10. c3 Bb7 11. Qe2 Be7 12. Ne4 cxd4 13. Nxf6+ Nxf6 $10 { Equal (about +0.1). White has traded one pair of knights, you have the c-file and an easy plan with ...O-O, ...Rc8. }) 4... Bg4! $14 { With intentions of playing e6 then either sliding back the bishop to h5 - g6 or simply exchange bishop for knight. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gc8g4] } 5. h3 { [%csl Gh3][%cal Gh2h3] } 5... Bxf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg4f3] } 6. Qxf3 { WATCH OUT: exd5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -7.0 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Nxd1 cxd5 c3 Nc6 Bf4 } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gd1f3] } 6... e6 $14 { This is a Slav-like position, but without the light-squared bishop, which is actually beneficial since Black has no bad bishop. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 7. Bd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 7... Nf6 $14 { White now has two choices: advance with e5, or simply develop or castle. } { [%csl Gf6,Rg1,Re5][%cal Gg8f6,Re1g1,Re4e5] } 8. O-O { O-O leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): White retains the bishop pair; White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } (8. e5 $14 { e5, transposes into an improvised advance variation except the light square bishop is out. Black's goal is to try and cut off white's central pawns. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } 8... Nfd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bf6d7] } 9. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bc3e2] } 9... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 10. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 10... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 11. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 11... Qb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 12. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 12... Ndxe5!! $15 { After the pawn capture, ...Nxe5 forks the queen and the light-squared bishop, and there is no defence. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd7e5] } 13. dxe5 { WATCH OUT: dxc5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.7 from White's own side of the board), after: Nxf3+ gxf3 Bxc5 Bxc5 Qxc5 Nf4 } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd4e5] } 13... Nxe5 { WATCH OUT: Qxb2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.9 from Black's own side of the board), after: Rfb1 Qa3 Rxb7 Nxe5 Qh5 Bd6 } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bc6e5] } 14. Qg3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bf3g3] } 14... Nxd3 { WATCH OUT: Qxb2 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.8 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qxe5 c4 Rfb1 Qxa1 Rxa1 cxd3 } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Be5d3] } 15. Rad1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Ba1d1] } 15... Qxb2 { [%csl Bb2][%cal Bb6b2] } 16. Rd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bd1d2] } 16... Qb5 $14 { Black won a pawn and his piece back. Just keep an eye to that knight on d3. } { [%csl Bd3,Bb5][%cal Bb5d3,Bb2b5] } 17. a4 { White hits the queen so that he regains the knight on d3. } (17. Nf4 Nxf4 18. Bxf4 O-O-O 19. a4 Qxa4 20. Rb1 b5 $19 { Ignoring the knight on d3 costs a lot (more than 2.5 pawns), so the knight must be dealt with first. }) 17... Qxa4 18. Rxd3 O-O-O { Castle at once; the king is safe. } 19. Rd2 Qe4 20. Nc1 Bd6 21. f4 g5 $14 { White is a little better (about +0.6) with active rooks, but you have counterplay and the bishop on d6. }) 8... dxe4 $14 { Simply exchange pawns to avoid complications, its often for the best. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 9. Nxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4] } 9... Nxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf6e4] } 10. Bxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd3e4] } 10... Nd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 11. c3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 11... Bd6 $14 { Black can now castle kingside comfortably. } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf8d6] } 12. Bc2 { [%csl Gc2][%cal Ge4c2] } 12... Qf6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.6). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Gf6][%cal Gd8f6] } 13. Qe4 { Queen and bishop line up toward h7, and White avoids a queen trade. His motive is a kingside attack, backed by the bishop pair. } 13... Nb6 { The knight eyes c4 and d5. } 14. h4 h6 { Stops the bishop attack on h7 and g5. } 15. h5 O-O-O 16. g3 Rdg8 17. Kg2 g6 $14 { White has the bishop pair and space (about +0.7). Black should close the h-file with ...g6 and aim for a blocked position where the bishops are of little use. } *


