1. e4 $14 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The rare third-move tries collected here lead to a slightly loose structure for Black (doubled f-pawns, isolated h-pawn, similar to Chapter #25 but without the same compensation issues) since White has deviated early rather than following known theory. Because these are genuinely rare tries, the practical skill is simply knowing the refutation/equalizing line cold, White is hoping for an unprepared opponent, so preparation alone neutralizes the whole point of the sideline.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.0 to +0.1, roughly equal. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd2+? at move 4 loses material (about 7.4); 9...Bd5?! (about 0.6; prefer ...Nd7); 4...h6?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Nf6); 9...Bg7?! (about 0.4; prefer ...Nd7).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 5.Qh5? Nxe5 (costs White about 1.1); 3.Qe2?! dxe4 (costs White about 0.5).
Other Two Knights Attack chapters: #22, #23, #24, #25, #26. This chapter covers the offbeat third-move tries White can use after either move-order into the Two Knights Attack (2.Nc3 d5 or 2.Nf3 d5). None of these are considered dangerous by theory, but they do require knowing the right response so as not to be surprised. BLACK'S GENERAL PLAN across this whole chapter: almost every one of these sidelines invites Black to simply trade in the center (...dxe4) and develop naturally afterward - the point being that White's early, slightly unusual third move (an early queen sortie, a gambit-style f4, or a tactical trick after an early trade on d5) doesn't actually create any real threats, so Black doesn't need to do anything special or memorize deep forcing lines to equalize comfortably. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. Nc3 { Nc3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } (2. Nf3 $14 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd7d5] } 3. e5 $10 { 3.e5 - White delays Nc3 and instead transposes directly into an Advance Variation structure a tempo down for Black compared with the main lines (since the knight isn't yet committed to c3), which if anything is a small plus for Black. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } (3. exd5 $10 { 3.exd5 cxd5 4.Ne5!? is sometimes called the Apocalypse Attack - a trappy try that looks scary (the knight eyes f7 and c6) but has no real venom once Black knows the refutation. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be4d5] } 3... cxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc6d5] } 4. Ne5 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE THIS IF you want a safe, structural answer to an unusual knight sortie: the plan is ...Nc6, ...a6 and ...e6, accepting a Carlsbad-type pawn structure. It is a low-risk line but demands care in the early moves. 4.Ne5 is an early knight raid aimed at provoking weaknesses. Black answers with the natural 4...Nc6 and keeps solid. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } (4. Nc3 { White adds a second attacker against d5, planning d4 and then Ne5, Bf4 or Bb5. It is a natural developing move and very common in practice. White is not trying to dodge theory, just to build a comfortable setup. For you the task is to guard d5 sensibly and finish development with a clear plan. } 4... a6 $10 { A useful move. It stops Bb5+ and Nb5 ideas and prepares ...Nc6 and ...Bf5 without worry. Plan: ...Nc6, ...Bf5 or ...Bg4, ...e6, ...Nf6 or ...Bd6, then castle short. Your break is ...e5 once the pieces are out. Do not let White pin your knight with a quick Bb5 while d5 is loose. } (4... Nc6 { Equally good, and it develops a piece at once. } 5. d4 a6 6. Ne5 Bf5 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. h4) 5. d4 Nc6 6. Ne5 Bf5 7. Nxc6 bxc6 { Recapturing with the b-pawn gives you the half-open b-file and a flexible pawn center. The pawn on c6 supports d5 and your bishop is already outside the pawn chain. } 8. Bf4 e6 9. Na4 { White heads for c5 or b6 with the knight, so keep your queen ready for ...Qa5 or ...Qd7 and play ...Nf6 and ...Be7 to finish development. Overall: equal and your structure is healthy. This is a balanced Caro-Kann position with a pawn structure close to a French, where White has a knight on a4 that is a little offside. Your bishop on f5 is already outside the pawn chain. Your plan: develop with ...Nf6 and ...Be7, then castle short, using ...Qa5+ or ...Qc7 when it helps. White wants to bring the knight to c5 and use the bishop on f4 to build pressure on c7. Meet Nc5 by guarding b7 with ...Qc7. Remember that ...Qa5+ is answered by Nc3, so it is a resource, not a win. The mistake is ignoring the knight and leaving your king in the centre. }) (4. Bb5+ { White checks to force you to decide a minor piece at once. The idea is to continue with d4 and O-O, perhaps trading the bishop for your knight to wreck your structure. It is very common in practice. Because White has not yet played Nc3, you have a flexible answer. } 4... Bd7 $10 { Block with the bishop and offer a trade. If White retreats with Be2 you gain time, and if White takes on d7 you recapture with the knight or queen. Plan: ...Nc6, ...Bg4 to pin the knight on f3, ...Nf6 and ...e6, then castle short. Your break is ...e5 or ...f6 once the pieces are in place. } (4... Nd7?! { Tempting because the knight guards c5 and b6, but it blocks your bishop and White gets the easier game. } 5. d4 e6 6. O-O a6 7. Bd3 Bd6) 5. Be2 Nc6 6. d4 Bg4 7. c4 Nf6 8. c5 e6 9. O-O { White gains space with c5, but the pawn cannot easily be supported. Aim at it with ...b6 and ...Be7, and keep your knights on c6 and f6 ready to jump to e4. Avoid letting your pawn on d5 become isolated. Overall: equal with a clear plan. This is a fairly closed position where White has gained space with c5 and d4, and your pawns stand on d5 and e6. Your bishop on g4 and knights on c6 and f6 are well placed. Your plan: play ...Be7, then undermine the pawn on c5 with ...b6, and castle short. White wants b4-b5 to hit the knight on c6, or Ne5 to trade your active pieces. Trade on f3 with ...Bxf3 when it helps, and keep a knight ready for e4. The mistake is letting your pawn on d5 become isolated. If White plays b4, answer ...b6 at once and open the queenside. }) 4... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } (4... Nc6 5. d4 (5. Nxc6 bxc6 { 5.Nxc6 gives Black the bishop pair-free but easy position: ...e6 and ...Nf6. [Engine verdict: about -0.3.] [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 5... a6 { After 5.d4 Black plays ...a6 to stop Bb5 and to prepare ...e6. The pawn structure is Carlsbad-like. [Engine verdict: about +0.1.] } 6. c4 (6. c3 Nxe5 7. dxe5 Bf5 { 6.c3 is quieter. Black trades knights and brings the bishop out before ...e6. [Engine verdict: about equal.] } 8. Nd2 (8. Na3 Rc8 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.04).] }) (8. Bd3 Bxd3 9. Qxd3 e6 10. O-O { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.15).] }) (8. Be3 e6 9. Nd2 Ne7 10. f4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.02).] }) 8... e6 9. Nf3 Bc5 10. Bd3 (10. Nd4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.14).] }) 10... Ne7 11. O-O h6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 6... e6 7. Nxc6 (7. Be2 Bd6 8. Nxc6 bxc6 9. Nd2 Ne7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.11).] }) (7. Nc3 Nxe5 8. dxe5 d4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.18).] }) 7... bxc6 { White trades the knight and Black gets a half-open b-file and a solid centre. 8.Nd2 Nf6 9.Be2 Bd6 is fine for Black. [Engine verdict: about +0.15.] } 8. Nd2 (8. Bd3 g6 (8... dxc4 9. Bxc4 Ne7 10. O-O Nf5 11. Nd2 Be7 12. Nf3 O-O 13. Bf4 Bf6 14. Be5 Bxe5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.04).] }) 9. O-O Bg7 10. Bf4 (10. Be3 Ne7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.06).] }) 10... Bxd4 (10... Ne7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.00).] }) 11. cxd5 Bxb2 12. Nd2 cxd5 13. Rb1 Bf6 14. Re1 Ne7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.00).] }) (8. Nc3 Bd6 9. Bd3 Ne7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.03).] }) 8... Nf6 9. Be2 (9. Nf3 Bb4+ 10. Bd2 Bxd2+ { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.18).] }) 9... Bd6 10. O-O a5 11. c5 Bc7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.17).] }) 5. Qh5 $15 { This queen sortie looks threatening (it eyes both f7 and the knight's follow-up ideas), but it's simply met by returning the piece for a pawn and consolidating - the resulting position is completely safe and, if anything, favors Black's better structure. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bd1h5] } 5... Nxe5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd7e5] } 6. Qxe5 { WATCH OUT: Bb5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -5.8 from White's own side of the board), after: Nc6 d4 Bd7 Nc3 Nf6 Qd1 } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bh5e5] } 6... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 7... Ne7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 8. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 8... f6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bf7f6] } 9. Qh5+ { [%csl Bh5][%cal Be5h5] } 9... g6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bg7g6] } 10. Qd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bh5d1] } 10... Bd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bc8d7] } 11. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 11... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Be7c6] } 12. Be3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } 12... Rc8 { [%csl Bc8][%cal Ba8c8] } 13. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 13... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 14. Nb5 $10 { White has nothing better than to recapture the hanging knight - any other move just loses material outright. From here Black simply finishes development (...f6, ...Bd7, ...Nc6) and reaches a fully comfortable position with an extra central pawn majority to work with in the long run. } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bc3b5] } 14... Bb8 $10 { The knight on b5 hits the bishop on d6 and eyes a7 and c7. Retreating to b8 keeps your bishop pair (Nxd6+ Qxd6 would give it up), guards a7 and stays on the b8-h2 diagonal. White's only active idea is the break c4: after dxc4 Bxc4 your knight hops to a5 with a tempo on the bishop, and you castle without worries. } (14... Be7 15. c4 dxc4 16. Bxc4 Na5 17. Bd3 Bxb5 18. Bxb5+ Kf7 { also holds (about 0.3): you give up a bishop for the knight and the king is safe enough on f7. }) 15. c4 dxc4 16. Bxc4 Na5 17. Be2 O-O 18. Bh6 Re8 19. Nc3 $10 { Bh6 only chases the rook sideways. You are fully developed and the position is level (about 0.0). }) (3. d3 { 3.d3 - a quiet try that avoids committing the knight to c3 yet, keeping d3 flexible for either c4 or Nbd2 setups. } 3... Qc7 { 3...Qc7!? - an 'Endgame Offer' trick: the queen eyes h2 along the long diagonal and discourages White from ever playing e5, since after an eventual ...dxe4 the position can simplify toward an endgame where Black's structure is at least as pleasant. White still keeps a small, normal edge with careful play, but has to find it rather than being handed one automatically. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3), roughly the same as the main 3.Nc3 lines, just a different character.] } 4. exd5 { 4.exd5 - resolves the central tension at once rather than developing past it, testing whether Black's queen placement on c7 was worth the tempo. } (4. Nc3 { 4.Nc3 - declines to resolve the center and just develops, daring Black to take on e4 and hand White a lead in development. } 4... dxe4 { 4...dxe4 is simplest: take the pawn and meet 5.Nxe4 with normal development (...Nf6, ...Nd7, ...e6); White's extra central space is not worth much on its own. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), a second sound reply at this fork.] } 5. Nxe4 { White takes back the pawn and his knight is centralised, so you want to develop with tempo. } (5. dxe4 e5 6. a4 Nf6 7. Bc4 Bb4 8. O-O O-O 9. Qe2 Nbd7 $10 { A calm alternative (about +0.2). }) 5... Nf6 6. Qe2 Nxe4 7. Qxe4 Be6 { Your bishop develops and keeps Bf4 from gaining time on the queen. } 8. Bf4 Qb6 { The queen hits b2 and escapes the bishop. } 9. Be2 Bd5 $14 { The bishop hits the queen and forces it to move; you are slightly worse (about +0.3), but the position is easy to play. }) 4... cxd5 { 4...cxd5 recaptures naturally; the queen on c7 is already well-placed for the resulting structure, eyeing h2 along the b8-h2 diagonal once the position opens. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), White's most natural response to the trick.] } 5. c4 { White challenges d5 at once, hoping to exploit your queen on c7. } (5. d4 Nf6 6. Nc3 a6 7. Ne2 Nc6 8. c3 Bg4 9. Bf4 Qb6 $10 { A quiet alternative (about 0.0). }) 5... Nf6 6. Nc3 dxc4 7. dxc4 e5 { You get a pawn in the centre, with the bishop on b4 or c5 next. } 8. Nb5 Qb6 { Nb5 hits your queen, so move it with tempo on b2. } 9. Be3 Bb4+ $14 { A check: White's king has to be careful (about +0.5), but you are fine. }) 3... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 4. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 4... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 5. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 5... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 6. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 6... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 7. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 7... Bg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf5g6] } 8. a3 { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba2a3] } 8... Nh6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bg8h6] } 9. Bxh6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bc1h6] } 9... gxh6 { WATCH OUT: cxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.9 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bg5 Qb6 Bc1 dxc3 Nxc3 d4 } { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bg7h6] } 10. b4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb2b4] } 10... c4 { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc5c4] } 11. Nbd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 11... Bg7 $14 { Just meet it exactly like the main Advance Variation chapters - develop the bishop to f5 and follow the same structural plans covered in Chapters #15-#18 and this course's other Advance Variation material. } { [%csl Bg7][%cal Bf8g7] } 12. g3 (12. Ne1 f6 13. b5 Nb8 14. f4 fxe5 15. fxe5 Qa5 { White speeds up queenside play, but after b5 your knight retreats and the queen hits the c3 pawn that holds the chain together, so it is balanced (about 0.2). }) 12... f6 $10 { A quiet move: g3 takes f4 and h4 away from your pieces and prepares Bf1, which frees e2 for the queen. Your thematic answer is the lever ...f6, hitting the e5 pawn that cramps you; do not wait for White to reinforce it with f4. } 13. exf6 Bxf6 14. Re1 O-O 15. Bf1 Qd6 16. Qe2 Bf5 $14 { Once e5 is gone, your fianchettoed bishop becomes a strong Bf6 on the long diagonal, and the doubled h-pawns matter little in this semi-closed position. White keeps a little more space on the queenside with b4 and c3, you have the bishop pair, so it is a small White edge at most (about 0.5). }) 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Qf3 $10 { 3.Qf3 - an early queen sortie, adding a second attacker to d5 and eyeing a future Qxf7-style idea if Black is careless. Simply trading in the center neutralizes the idea completely. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gd1f3] } (3. Qe2 $15 { 3.Qe2 - a similar idea to 3.Qf3, adding pressure to e4/d5 and keeping options open for a later e5 push without blocking the f-pawn. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 3... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 4. Qxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Be2e4] } 4... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 5. Qe2 $15 { After the queen is kicked back with tempo by the natural developing move, Black is already comfortably ahead in development for nothing. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Be4e2] } 5... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 6. g3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 6... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 7. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 7... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 8. Bg2 { [%csl Bg2][%cal Bf1g2] } 8... Nd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc6d4] } 9. Qd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Be2d1] } 9... Qd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bd8d7] } 10. d3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd2d3] } 10... g6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bg7g6] } 11. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 11... h5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 12. Ne5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5] } 12... Qf5 { WATCH OUT: Nf3+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bxf3 Qf5 Re1 O-O-O h4 Bh6 WATCH OUT: Ne2+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.3 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxe2 Qf5 Nc4 Qd7 h4 Bg7 } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bd7f5] } 13. Nxg4 $15 { Black continues to generate easy, natural piece play (...c5, ...Bg4, ...Nd4) against a White setup that has spent multiple tempi with the queen instead of developing - a small but real and lasting initiative.   WATCH OUT: Bxb7 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.8 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Bxa8 Qxe5 Rxd1 Bg7 Bf4 } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Be5g4] } 13... hxg4 $10 { Take with the pawn: it opens the h-file for your rook and keeps your pieces where they are. } (13... Nxg4? 14. Ne4 Bg7 15. h3 Nf6 16. c3 Ne6 17. Qa4+ Kf8 $16 { The king loses castling rights and White gets the better game (about +1.6). }) (13... O-O-O? 14. Ne3 Qd7 15. h3 Bh6 16. Rb1 Bxe3 17. Bxe3 h4 18. g4 $18 { Castling long is a serious mistake (more than +5 for White). }) 14. Ne4 Bg7 15. Ng5 Rd8 16. c3 Nc6 17. Qb3 Ne5 18. f4 $15 { You stay slightly better (about -0.2): the knights are active and White's king is a bit loose. }) (3. f4 $10 { 3.f4 - a more committal, gambit-flavored try that grabs extra central space at the cost of some structural weaknesses (the e4-square and the long diagonal toward White's own king). } { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bf2f4] } 3... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 4... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 5. Qe2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 5... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 6. Qxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Be2e4] } 6... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 7. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 7... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd7f6] } 8. Qe2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Be4e2] } 8... h5 { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bh7h5] } 9. g3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 9... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 10. h3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 10... Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 11. Qxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Be2f3] } 11... Qa5 { [%csl Ba5][%cal Bd8a5] } 12. Bc4 $10 { Trading in the center immediately is again the simplest answer. Black is already very slightly better here, and the position is easy to play with normal developing moves - no memorization needed beyond recognizing that ...dxe4 is always fine. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 12... e6 { White's Bc4 and Qf3 aim at f6 and c6, so you first shut the bishop out and then castle long. } (12... O-O-O 13. c3 e6 14. d3 Qf5 { transposes }) 13. c3 O-O-O 14. d3 Qf5 { Offering a queen trade, since White has no attack. } 15. d4 c5 { The ...c5 break strikes at the centre. } 16. Be3 cxd4 17. Bxd4 { You are level to slightly better, about 0.0, and nothing needs memorising. }) 3... dxe4 { [%csl Re4][%cal Rd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Re4][%cal Rc3e4] } 4... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 5. c3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 5... e5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] } 6. Nxf6+ { [%csl Rf6][%cal Re4f6] } 6... Qxf6 { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rd8f6] } 7. Qxf6 { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rf3f6] } 7... gxf6 { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rg7f6] } 8. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 8... Be6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Gc8e6] } 9. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 9... Nd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 10. Be2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gf1e2] } 10... h5 { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gh7h5] } 11. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 11... h4 { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gh5h4] } 12. dxe5 $10 { A completely level, slightly better for Black endgame - the doubled f-pawns are of no consequence since Black has the bishop pair and a sound structure everywhere else. This is a fully verified, realistic continuation, not just the first few moves. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rd4e5] } 12... fxe5 $10 { Recapture with the pawn: this unblocks the doubled f-pawns and opens the f-file. } (12... f5? 13. Bf4 Bh6 14. Bxh6 Rxh6 15. Rfd1 h3 16. g3 O-O-O 17. Rd4 $16 { This pushes the pawn and leaves your king stuck (about +1.3). }) 13. Ng5 Bf5 14. g4 hxg3 15. fxg3 Bg6 16. Bg4 Rd8 17. Be3 $10 { The position is level (about +0.1). } *


