1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The Fantasy Variation's Tartakower treatment (I) reaches a Classical-style structure (half-open d-file) but a full move behind the normal Classical Variation, since White spent an extra tempo on an early f4/e4 central commitment. Black's practical plan: treat it like the calmer Classical chapters (press d4 down the half-open file, look for a timely central break) while remembering White's structure is objectively more ambitious but also slightly riskier (the half-open f-file for White is a two-way street).
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.1 to +0.6, a small White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxd4? at move 4 loses material (about 6.6); 8...Qc7? (about 1.0; prefer ...Nbd7); 8...b5? (about 0.9; prefer ...Nbd7); 10...a6? (about 0.7; prefer ...Bd6); 7...Qe7? (about 0.7; prefer ...Bh5).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 7.Bxf7+? Kxf7 (costs White about 2.5); 11.d5? cxd5 (costs White about 1.2); 10.Qe2? Nxe4 (costs White about 1.2).
Chapter #29 continues the Fantasy Variation. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #5. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. f3 $10 { This is the Fantasy Variation of the Caro Kann. The Fantasy Variation of the Caro Kann is an aggressive opening choice against the Caro Kann. White actually wants a fantasy land where he has an open f file for his rook to take advantage of. Second to that white wants to get a fortified center following a principled development on the center. So far white just wants an easy play against black but at the same time get the initiative and advantage. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gf2f3] } 3... dxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 4. fxe4 { fxe4 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf3e4] } (4. Nc3 { 4.Nc3!? - a Milner-Barry-style gambit idea, developing immediately rather than recapturing the pawn on e4 right away. } 4... Nf6 { 4...Nf6 is simplest: hold onto the extra pawn and develop normally. White's pawn on e4 is simply extra material, not enough compensation for the lost tempo. [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3).] } 5. Nxe4 { Strictly, your extra pawn lasts only until White recaptures on e4, and the knight is well placed there eyeing d6 and f6. What you keep is a healthy structure and an easy game. } (5. fxe4 e5 6. dxe5 (6. Nf3 { White develops the kingside knight and adds a second attacker against d4, so that dxe5 or d5 both stay available next move. There is no trap here; it is simply the most natural move and it is very common in practice (played in about 71 thousand games). For you it means White is happy to wait, and you must decide how to settle the tension between your e5 pawn and the d4 pawn. } 6... exd4 $15 { You take on d4 and accept the queen trade that follows. After Qxd4 Qxd4 Nxd4 you have no weaknesses, and your dark-squared bishop gets to hit the knight on d4 with tempo. } (6... Bg4?! { Pinning the knight looks natural and many players choose it, but White just plays dxe5, trades queens with Kxd1 and has the easier game: the pin is not a real problem for White, and your bishop is misplaced. This leaves White a slight edge, so avoid it. } 7. dxe5 Qxd1+ 8. Kxd1 Nh5 9. a4 Nd7 10. Be2 O-O-O 11. Bd2) 7. Qxd4 Qxd4 8. Nxd4 Bc5 { Hit the knight at once. } 9. Nb3 Bb4 { Your plan is simple: keep pressure on the knight on c3 with the bishop, castle short, and bring the b8 knight to d7 or a6. You can aim for ...c5 or ...Nxe4 later when the c3 knight is overloaded. Do not rush the knight forward before your king is safe. } 10. Bd3 O-O 11. Bf4 { Overall: after the early queen trade you have a healthy structure and easy development, and you keep a small edge. You have reached a queenless middlegame with a healthy structure and an easy game, and you are a little more comfortable. The bishop on f4 attacks your knight on b8, so your first step is ...Nbd7 or ...Na6. Then put a rook on e8 to pressure the e4 pawn, and keep the bishop on b4 pinning the c3 knight. Your third step is to look for ...Bxc3 followed by ...Nxe4 when the pawn becomes loose. White wants to castle long, double on the d-file and push e5. The risk is rushing ...Nxe4 before the pin is built; if White plays e5, hit it with ...Nd7 and ...Nxe5. }) 6... Qxd1+ 7. Nxd1 Nxe4 8. Bc4 Nd7 9. Nf3 Nb6 { the other recapture lets you strike with ...e5, trade queens and win the e4 pawn, and the bishop gets hit by ...Nb6 (about -0.3). }) (5. Bg5 { White pins the knight on f6 to the queen and wants to rid you of your best defender, intending to recapture on e4 with a good structure, or to follow with Qd2 and queenside castling. It is very common in practice (played in about 51 thousand games). For you it means the pin on f6 must be handled calmly, and you should not drop the pawn back without gaining something. } 5... exf3 $15 { Capture the f3 pawn before White can recapture on e4. After Nxf3 you play ...Bg4, pinning the knight in return, and ...Nbd7 to bring the second knight out. Your plans are ...e6 or ...e5 once the bishop on g5 is traded or when it leaves, castling short, and ...Qb6 pressure. This leaves you slightly better. } 6. Nxf3 Bg4 7. Qd2 Nbd7 8. d5 h6 { Ask the bishop a question at once. Avoid ...Nxe4 here, because the pin on f6 and the queen on d2 leave you tactically exposed. } 9. Bxf6 Nxf6 10. Ne5 { Overall: after the bishop trade you keep a solid structure, the bishop pair is not a worry, and you aim for ...Bf5 or ...e6 and ...Be7 next. This is an open position after the bishop trade, with White's pawn on d5 attacking your c6 pawn and your bishop on g4 attacked by the knight on e5, and you are slightly better. Your plan is, first, to move the bishop with ...Bf5, keeping it protected. Second, challenge d5 with ...cxd5 or ...e6. Third, complete development with ...Qc7, ...e6 and ...Be7, castling on the side that is safe. White wants to use the knight on e5 and the d-pawn to cramp you, and checks Nxf7 tricks. The risk is dropping the bishop or leaving f7 loose. If White plays Nxg4, recapture with the knight. }) 5... Nbd7 6. Nxf6+ Nxf6 7. c3 (7. Be2 { This is the slow move: White develops the bishop but leaves the centre unprotected. The Fantasy position after the knight trades is tricky because White's e-pawn is gone and your pieces are free. } 7... e5 { You strike at the centre at once, taking advantage of the missing white pawn on e4. } 8. dxe5 Qa5+ 9. Qd2 Qxe5 10. Qe3 { This is an open position where you have a lead in the centre and good piece play. Your plan: bring the bishop to d6 or e7, castle short, and add ...Bg4 or ...Be6 to speed up development. White wants f4 or Nf3 to kick your queen and win time. Watch for tricks on the e-file when your king is in the centre. If White plays c3 and Bd3 or f4 early, trade queens only if it helps your structure, and keep the extra central pawn. The risk is grabbing too many pawns and leaving the king in the centre; castle first. If White deviates with Be3 or Bd3, take the d4 pawn only after checking that the queen still has an escape square. }) 7... Bf5 8. Ne2 e6 9. g4 Bg6 $15 { White's g4 lunge wants g5 and h4-h5 to harass the bishop, but the bishop is safe on g6; if h4-h5 arrives you drop back to h7, where it still eyes the long diagonal. You are a little more comfortable (about -0.2). }) 4... e5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] } 5. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } (5. c3 { White shores up d4 and prepares to meet ...exd4 with cxd4, hoping to open the centre while the king is still on e1. The trouble is that it ignores the weakened e1-h4 diagonal, since the f2 pawn is gone. } 5... Qh4+ $17 { This is the whole point: the check forces the king to move. White cannot block with g3 because ...Qxe4+ wins material, so he plays Kd2 and the king is stuck in the middle. Your plan is to bring pieces with tempo, ...Nf6, ...Qf2+ or ...Nxe4+, and open the position with ...exd4. Keep checking only while it gains time; avoid pushing the queen alone far into the enemy camp. This is clearly better for you. } (5... Nf6?! { Developing the knight is natural but misses the check; after Nf3 exd4 Bc4 White gets a comfortable game and the position is about equal. Do not play it. } 6. Nf3 exd4 7. Bc4 Be7 8. e5 Nd5 9. cxd4 Be6 10. O-O) 6. Kd2 Nf6 7. Nf3 Qf2+ 8. Be2 Nxe4+ 9. Kc2 exd4 { Open the e-file and the centre while the king is exposed. Your knights and bishops are all better coordinated than White's. } 10. Nxd4 { Overall: White's king on c2 is the target, and you keep the initiative. This is an open position where White's king stands on c2 and your pieces are more active, so you keep the initiative. Your queen on f2 and knight on e4 are annoying, and it is your move. Your plan is, first, to bring the dark bishop out with ...Bc5 or ...Bb4, hitting the knight on d4. Second, develop ...Nc6 to chase it away. Third, keep checking and opening files toward the king rather than grabbing pawns. White wants to trade queens or to consolidate with Rd1 and Be3. The typical mistake is to play the queen alone to g2 and get trapped. If White trades pieces, keep the bishop pair active. }) 5... Bg4 { [%csl Gg4,Rc4,Rc3][%cal Gc8g4,Rf1c4,Rc2c3] } 6. c3 { c3 leads to a small White edge (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame.   WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.1 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Kxd1 Qxd4+ Nd3 Na6 Nc3 } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } (6. Bc4 $14 { Bc4 white will castle then in the near future would play Ng5 once white's queen is out of the pin. Seemingly white is alright with a pawn sacrifice in exchange for quick development. White also has a strong threat of Bxf7! Kxf7 Nxe5+ Ke8 Qxg4. White is up a piece and at the same time black's position is just horrible due to the fact that black can no longer castle and the position is also cramped. } { [%csl Bf7,Bg4,Bc4][%cal Bc4f7,Be1h1,Bf3g5,Bg5f7,Bf1f7,Bf1c4] } 6... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7,Bg1,Bf7][%cal Bb8d7,Be1g1,Bc4f7] } 7. O-O { O-O leads to roughly equal (+0.1): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } (7. Bxf7+?? $19 { Sacrifices like this would not work since black can defend and patch the position after Nf6. White cannot also play Ng5 since the queen is guarding the g5 square. } 7... Kxf7 8. O-O Ngf6 $19 { [Engine verdict: winning for Black (-2.4). Notable features: Black is 2 points of material up; Black has the bishop pair.] } 9. c3 (9. dxe5 Bxf3 10. e6+ Kg8 11. Qxf3 Ne5 12. Qb3 Bc5+ 13. Kh1 Qb6 { the intermezzo e6+ does not help: your pieces swarm the white king. }) 9... Bxf3 10. gxf3 Qb6 11. a4 c5 12. a5 Qc6 13. dxe5 Nxe5 $17 { White's Bxf7+ was a sacrifice that dragged your king out, but White has no follow-up: no more checks, and the open f-file is not enough. Taking on f3 first wrecks White's kingside pawns, and ...Qb6 hits b2 and d4 at once. You are clearly ahead in material and only have to keep the king covered (about -2.4). }) 7... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6,Bf7][%cal Bg8f6,Bf1f7,Bc4f7] } 8. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 8... Bd6 { WATCH OUT: Nxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.4 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bxf7+ Ke7 Bb3 Ke8 Nbd2 Nxd2 } { [%csl Bd6,Be3,Bg5][%cal Bf8d6,Bc1e3,Bc1g5] } 9. Be3 { Be3 leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet.   WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -3.6 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Nxf7 Qc7 e5 Nxe5 dxe5 } { [%csl Be3][%cal Bc1e3] } (9. Bg5 $10 { With plans of opening up the f file s } { [%csl Bf7][%cal Bf1f7,Bc4f7] } 9... Qc7 { WATCH OUT: Nxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.8 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bxd8 Kxd8 Qe1 Bxf3 Rxf3 Nef6 } { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 10. Qe1 { WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -5.4 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Nxd7 Bxh2+ Kh1 Qxd7 Kxh2 } { [%csl Be1,Bh4][%cal Bd1e1,Be1h4] } 10... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 11. Qh4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Be1h4] } 11... Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg4f3] } 12. gxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg2f3] } 12... Qb6 { [%csl Bb6,Bg1][%cal Bc7b6,Bb6g1] } 13. Qf2 { Qf2 leads to roughly equal (+0.1): White has an isolated pawn; White retains the bishop pair. } { [%csl Bf2][%cal Bh4f2] } (13. Bb3 $14 { Rather than the immediate Qf2, repositioning the bishop to b3 first is a cleaner try, keeping it safely out of tactics on the long diagonal before continuing the attack. } { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc4b3] } 13... exd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be5d4] } 14. Kh1 { [%csl Bh1][%cal Bg1h1] } 14... dxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bd4c3] } 15. Nxc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 15... Be5 $14 { A real, moderate improvement for White - not as sharp as the Main Line #1 note above, but still meaningfully stronger than the main line's original try in this sub-line. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd6e5] } 16. Rg1 (16. Rad1 Rfe8 17. Rg1 c5 18. Bh6 g6 19. Rg2 Qa6 20. Bc2 Bd4 { the same set-up with another move order lets you equalise (about 0.1). }) 16... Kh8 17. Rad1 c5 18. Qh3 Bd4 19. Rg2 Rae8 20. Ba4 Qe6 $14 { White is a pawn down, but has the g-file, the bishop on b3 aimed at f7 and the queen on the kingside: Rg1 and Bg5 threaten to remove your knight on f6 and then play against g7. Kh8 steps off the b3-g8 diagonal, and ...c5 and ...Bd4 gain space and activity. The queen trade on e6 takes the sting out of White's play, leaving a modest White edge (about 0.5). }) 13... Be7 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Be7][%cal Bd6e7] } 14. Be3 (14. Na3? Nxe4 15. fxe4 Bxg5 16. Bxf7+ Kh8 17. Nc4 Qc7 18. dxe5 Nxe5 { the knight tour fails to a tactic: ...Nxe4 takes a pawn and the pin on g5 is broken by taking the bishop (about -0.4). }) 14... Qc7 15. a4 Nh5 16. Kh1 Kh8 $14 { White retreats the bishop to avoid ...Bxg5 and keep the bishop pair, advances the a-pawn for queenside space and wants the f4 break (f3-f4) to open the f-file. You aim your knight at f4 and tuck the king onto h8, away from the b3-g8 diagonal. If White plays f4 anyway, ...Nxf4 Bxf4 exf4 wins a pawn but White regains it with Qxf4. A modest White edge (about 0.6). }) 9... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. Nbd2 { Nbd2 leads to a small White edge (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame.   WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -5.7 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Rxd1 Qb8 Nxf7 Bxh2+ Kh1 } { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } (10. Qe2? $17 { Qe2 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 10... Nxe4! $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.3).] [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 11. Nbd2 (11. Qc2 exd4 12. cxd4 Nef6 13. Nc3 Bh5 14. Bg5 Qc7 15. Bb3 Bg6 { even the direct Qc2 hitting e4 is met by the same retreat, and you stay clearly better (about -1.7). }) 11... Nef6 12. Qe1 b5 13. Bb3 exd4 14. Nxd4 Re8 15. h3 Bh5 $17 { White's Qe2 allowed you to win a pawn on e4. Nbd2 attacks the knight, so you simply retreat it to f6 and keep the extra pawn. Then ...b5 gains a tempo on the bishop, and ...exd4 and ...Re8 pile pressure on the e-file. You are a clean pawn up and only need to finish development (about -1.3). }) 10... exd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be5d4] } 11. cxd4 { WATCH OUT: Nxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.0 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Raxd1 Ng4 Bf4 Bxf4 Rxf4 } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc3d4] } 11... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 12. dxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd4c5] } 12... Bxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd6c5] } 13. Bxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Be3c5] } 13... Nxc5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd7c5] } 14. e5 (14. a4? Bxf3 15. Nxf3 Ncxe4 16. Qxd8 Raxd8 17. a5 a6 18. Rad1 Rc8 { shows the tactical point: once Bxf3 deflects the knight from d2, the pawn on e4 hangs (about -0.7). }) 14... Nfd7 15. h3 Bh5 16. Qe2 Qb6 17. Qe3 Rad8 18. Rae1 Ne6 $10 { The e4-e5 push gains space and kicks the knight, but your pieces find good squares: the knight lands on e6 heading for d4 or g5, the bishop is safe on h5, and the rooks take the open files. The position stays level (about 0.0). }) (6. dxe5 { Theory note: Black should develop as actively as possible here. } 6... Qxd1+ 7. Kxd1 Nd7 8. Be2 O-O-O 9. Nbd2 f5 { This move looks aggressive and gambit-like, but it is not: the e5 pawn will soon fall because Black's pieces are active and White is cramped, so White must eventually give it up to develop. Lines where White tries to hold e5 need more detailed study. } 10. exf6 (10. Ke1 Re8 11. Rf1 Bxf3 12. Nxf3 fxe4 13. Ng5 Nxe5 14. Nf7 Nxf7 15. Rxf7 Nf6 16. Bf4 Re7 17. Rxe7 Bxe7 18. Rd1 Rd8 19. Be5 Rxd1+ 20. Kxd1 Kd7 21. Bd4 a5 22. Bxf6 Bxf6 23. Bg4+ Kd6 $15 { White's compensation for the pawn is the bishop pair: Be5 hits the knight on f6 and the rooks come off. You march the king to the centre, which is safe now that the queens are gone, and after the trade on f6 the bishops are of opposite colours. That is why the extra pawn is hard to convert and the position is only slightly better for you (about -0.3). }) 10... Ngxf6 11. h3 (11. c3 Bd6 12. Kc2 Rhe8 13. Re1 Nc5 14. Nd4 Bxe2 15. Rxe2 Bxh2 16. N2f3 { The bishop on h2 won a pawn but is in danger of being trapped by g3, so White hits it with the knight at once and it has to run. } (16. g3? Bxg3 17. Nf5 Bc7 18. Nxg7 Re5 19. Nc4 Rxe4 20. Rxe4 Ncxe4 { the attempt to trap the bishop fails, because it simply takes another pawn and escapes (about -1.0). }) 16... Be5 17. Nf5 g6 18. Nh6 Rd7 19. Be3 Ncxe4 20. Nxe5 Rxe5 $15 { White's knights hunt for f5 and h6 to harass the g7 pawn and your rook; you answer with ...g6 and ...Rd7, then take the pawn on e4. You stay a pawn up with a small edge (about -0.3), because White's pieces are active and you must keep your king safe. }) 11... Bh5 12. Re1 Bd6 13. c3 Bg3 14. Rf1 Nc5 15. Kc2 (15. a4? Bg6 16. Nd4 Rhe8 17. Kc2 Ncxe4 18. Nxe4 Bxe4+ 19. Kb3 Bxg2 { pushing the a-pawn loses a tempo and you pick up pawns while the king is exposed (about -2.2). }) 15... Rhe8 16. Nd4 Bg6 17. b4 Ncxe4 18. Nxe4 Be5 19. Bd3 Bxd4 20. cxd4 Bxe4 21. Bxe4 Rxe4 $17 { You are a pawn down on paper, but White's king is stuck in the middle and the pawn on e4 is weak. You pile on it with ...Rhe8 and ...Bg6, and when b4 tries to kick your knight, ...Ncxe4 only gives up a piece for a moment: after Nxe4 your bishop jumps to e5, the knight on e4 is attacked twice and d4 is hit as well, so the material comes straight back. You end with the more active rook and a clear edge (about -1.4). }) 6... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 7. Bc4 $14 { A knight, a bishop and, once White has castled, a rook can all bear down on f7. Watch what White is planning, as hidden dangers lurk in the opening.   WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -5.2 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Nxf7 Kxf7 Bc4+ Ke8 Kxd1 } { [%csl Yf3,Yf7,Gc4][%cal Rc4f7,Rf3g5,Rg5f7,Re1g1,Yh1f1,Yf1f7,Gf1c4] } (7. Be2 { White keeps the bishop on e2 instead of putting it on c4. The idea is quiet development: castle, then play h3 to deal with your bishop on g4. If you take on f3, White recaptures with the bishop and keeps a healthy structure with the bishop pair. This is a calmer way to play than 7.Bc4, and the pin on the knight is less annoying here. } 7... Nbd7 { Bring the last knight out and keep the centre solid. You do not need to hurry. Your pieces head for d6 and c7, and your king goes short. } (7... exd4 8. Nxd4 Bxe2 9. Qxe2 Bc5 10. e5 { This trade opens the centre while White is a move ahead in development. You can survive, but you will have to defend the e-file, so prefer ...Nbd7. }) 8. O-O Bd6 { The bishop eyes the kingside and supports the e5 pawn, so White's d4 break loses force. } 9. Bg5 Qc7 10. Nbd2 { This is a balanced centre where both sides have a pawn on e4 or e5 and the e5 pawn is yours to guard. Your plan: castle short, put a rook on e8 or d8, and play ...h6 to ask the bishop on g5 what it wants. Aim for ...Bxf3 only when it wins the bishop pair or wrecks White's pawn on f3 (it forces Nxf3). White wants d4 or Nc4, hitting the e5 pawn, and a later Qd3 or Qe3 with pressure on your king. Meet d4 with ...exd4 and ...Bxf3 so that the white queen is dragged out. The risk is greed: taking on e4 with ...Nxe4 looks free but after O-O and Nxe5 your knights lose their grip on the centre. If White plays Bc4 or Bd3 instead, keep the same set-up. }) (7. Bd3 { White puts the bishop on d3, eyeing your kingside and the h7 pawn. The drawback is that the bishop blocks the queen's protection of the d4 pawn. Many club players play this because it looks natural. } 7... exd4 { Take on d4 straight away. White cannot simply recapture because the pawn on d4 has lost the queen's support. } (7... Be7 8. Be3 Nbd7 9. O-O O-O 10. Nbd2 { This quiet route also works, but taking on d4 is more ambitious. }) 8. e5 Nfd7 { White pushes to gain a tempo and open the long diagonal for the bishop. Your knight retreats to d7 and is ready for ...Nxe5 if the pawn is loose. } 9. cxd4 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 { This is an open position where White has the bishop pair and the open f-file, but your position is solid and the e5 pawn is a target. Your plan: play ...e6 to guard d5 and f5, then ...Nb6 or ...Qb6 to hit the pawns on d4 and b2. Castle short and put a rook on c8 or d8. White wants Bxh7, e6 or Qf3-f4, so keep your king safe and watch the h7 pawn and the f7 square. Meet e6 with ...fxe6 or ...Nb6 depending on what is hanging. The risk is castling too early into the attack on h7. If White avoids e5, play ...Be7 and ...O-O and sit comfortably. }) 7... Bh5 $14 { Bh5 adding another defender on the f7 pawn. Problem with other moves such as lets say Nbd7. White has this horrendous threat of Qb3! Attacking the b7 pawn and the f7 pawn. Black would be left with no choice but to defend the f7 pawn but the b7 pawn will hang thus white would have a terrific control of the 7th Rank. } { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gg4h5] } 8. O-O { O-O leads to a small White edge (+0.2): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } (8. Qb3? $10 { Qb3 doesn't work because of b5 thus eliminating white's threat of Qxb7 and at the same time lose one tempo. } { [%csl Bb3,Bf7,Bb7][%cal Bd1b3,Bc4f7,Bb3f7] } 8... b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 9. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bc4e2] } 9... Nbd7 { Nbd7 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (9... exd4 $14 { exd4 leads to a small White edge (+0.4): Black has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has an isolated pawn. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be5d4] } 10. cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc3d4] } 10... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 11. Qe3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bb3e3] } 11... Bb4+ { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.0 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qd8 Qxe4+ Be7 Bb4 Bg6 } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bf8b4] } 12. Bd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } 12... Qe7 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4). Notable features: Black is a pawn up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side.] [%csl Be7,Bg8][%cal Bd8e7,Be8g8] } 13. Bxb4 (13. O-O Nxd2 14. Qxe7+ Kxe7 15. Nbxd2 Re8 { castling is too slow: you remain a clean pawn up with the e-file in your hands (about -0.8). }) 13... Qxb4+ 14. Nbd2 Bxf3 15. Bxf3 Qxd2+ 16. Qxd2 Nxd2 17. Kxd2 Ke7 $10 { This forced liquidation is White's best try: your queen is facing White's on the e-file with only your knight in between, and the bishop on d2 is attacked by both bishop and knight, so White trades everything. You keep the extra pawn, but White's rooks reach the open files before your knight and king are developed, so the position is balanced (about 0.1). Bring the king to safety and the knight into play. }) 10. O-O $10 { White has a tactic of Nxb5! Black is obligated to take back with Nxe5 otherwise Qxf7#. Problem is black's bishop on h5 will hang. } { [%csl Bb3,Be2,Be5,Bf1,Bf3,Bf7,Bh5][%cal Bf3e5,Be2h5,Bb3f7,Bf1f7] } 10... Bg6 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] WATCH OUT: Nxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -2.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxe5 Nxe5 Bxh5 g6 Be2 Qb6 } 11. a4 (11. Bg5 Be7 12. a4 bxa4 13. Qxa4 Nxe4 14. Bxe7 Qxe7 15. Qxc6 O-O { here you answer the pin by trading bishops, then take on e4 while White takes on c6, and the game is level (about -0.1). }) 11... a6 { You must hold b5: a4 attacks it directly, and ...a6 supports it so that axb5 axb5 does not open the a-file with tempo on your rook. } 12. Nbd2 Bd6 13. Nxe5 { White tries Nxe5 Nxe5 dxe5 to open the d-file and the diagonals towards your king, but your bishop on d6 is waiting to take back on e5. } 13... Nxe5 14. dxe5 Bxe5 15. Nf3 Qb6+ $10 { The check gains the tempo you need to save the bishop, and material is level; you will castle and the position is balanced (about 0.0). }) 8... Nbd7 { Nbd7 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } (8... Nxe4?? $16 { The e-file is open and Black, still uncastled, is on the verge of losing. Poisoned pawns should not be taken: with so little development, Black lacks the pieces to hold against White's strong winning combination starting with Qe1. } 9. Qe1 { [%csl Be1,Bh4][%cal Bd1e1,Be1h4] } 9... Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bh5f3] } 10. Bxf7+ { [%csl Bf7][%cal Bc4f7] } 10... Kxf7 { [%csl Bf7][%cal Be8f7] } 11. Qxe4 { WATCH OUT: dxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -3.7 from White's own side of the board), after: Bc5+ Be3 Re8 Bxc5 Nxc5 Nd2 WATCH OUT: gxf3 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -3.0 from White's own side of the board), after: Nd6 dxe5 Nf5 Nd2 Qd5 Ne4 } { [%csl Be4][%cal Be1e4] } 11... Qd5 { WATCH OUT: exd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.3 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qxf3+ Qf6 Qh5+ g6 Rxf6+ Kxf6 } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd8d5] } 12. Rxf3+ { WATCH OUT: dxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.1 from White's own side of the board), after: Qxe4 Rxf3+ Kg8 Be3 Qxe5 Nd2 } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf1f3] } 12... Kg8 { [%csl Bg8][%cal Bf7g8] } 13. Qg4 { Qg4 leads to a clear White edge (+0.9): Black has an isolated pawn.   WATCH OUT: dxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.2 from White's own side of the board), after: Qxe4 Be3 Nd7 Nd2 Qxe5 Nc4 } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Be4g4] } (13. Qf5 $16 { Instead of Qg4 in the main line, the more testing try is Qf5, keeping the queen more centrally placed and creating immediate threats against Black's slightly exposed king. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Be4f5] } 13... Nd7 { WATCH OUT: exd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it's not just worse, it's losing outright for Black: after Qxf8#, White forces mate. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 14. c4 { WATCH OUT: dxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.2 from White's own side of the board), after: Bc5+ Re3 Nxe5 b4 Bxe3+ Bxe3 } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bc3c4] } 14... Qxc4 { WATCH OUT: exd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it's not just worse, it's losing outright for Black: after cxd5 h5 Qf7+ Kh7 Qxh5+ Kg8 Qf7+ Kh7 Rh3#, White forces mate. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bd5c4] } 15. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 15... Re8 $18 { White keeps a genuine, lasting initiative here that the main line's Qg4 understates. This is the critical test of this whole sharp line.   WATCH OUT: Qxd4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: Be3 Qxe3+ Rxe3 Bc5 Qe6+ Kf8 } { [%csl Be8][%cal Ba8e8] } 16. Qxd7 { This is the point of White's initiative: when your queen went to c4 it stopped guarding the knight on d7, so that knight simply falls. The rest of the line is damage control. } (16. d5 Nf6 17. Bg5 e4 18. Rff1 Qd4+ 19. Kh1 h6 20. Bxf6 Qxf6 { the alternative d5 lets you win time with ...e4 and is less testing (about 0.2). }) 16... Qxd4+ 17. Qxd4 exd4 18. Nb1 Re1+ 19. Rf1 Rxf1+ 20. Kxf1 Bd6 $16 { White has an extra knight for two pawns (about 1.7), but the knight on b1 and bishop on c1 are still undeveloped, so you get a few tempi to push the d-pawn and bring the king and bishop into play. White must untangle first with Nd2 and Bf4. }) 13... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 14. Bh6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bc1h6] } 14... g6 { WATCH OUT: exd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.3 from Black's own side of the board), after: Rxf8+ Kxf8 Qxg7+ Ke8 Qxh8+ Ke7 } { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bg7g6] } 15. Be3 $14 { This is just a horrendous position for black. } { [%csl Be3][%cal Bh6e3] } 15... Bg7 $14 { The bishop supports the e5 pawn that White's queen and bishop are eyeing, and prepares ...h5 later. The numbers say White is only modestly better (about 0.6), so the plan is to get your pieces out and look for a queen trade. } (15... e4?! 16. Rf1 Re8 17. Nd2 Qe6 18. Qxe6+ Rxe6 19. Rae1 h6 20. Bf4 { pushing the pawn only creates a target: rooks and knights pile onto e4, and White's pieces are better placed (about 1.9). }) 16. c4 Qd6 17. Qg3 h5 { A useful resource: ...h5-h4 would kick the queen off g3, and it takes the g4 square away from White's pieces. } 18. h4 Qe6 19. d5 Qg4 20. Nc3 $14 { You offer a queen trade on g4 and reach a solid defensive set-up (about 0.6). }) 9. Bg5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gc1g5] } 9... h6 $14 { The move h6 does not only kick the DSB but at the same time gives black's LSB a bit of a breathing space in case white gets a chance to play g4.   WATCH OUT: Nxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bxd8 Kxd8 Qe1 Bxf3 Rxf3 Nd6 } { [%csl Gh5,Gh6][%cal Gh5g6,Gg6h7,Gh7h6] } 10. Bh4 { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gg5h4] } 10... Bd6 { WATCH OUT: Nxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.0 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bxd8 Kxd8 Qc1 Bxf3 gxf3 Ng5 } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf8d6] } 11. Nbd2 { Nbd2 leads to a small White edge (+0.3): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet.   WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -3.0 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Nxf7 Qc7 e5 Nxe5 dxe5 } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } (11. d5 $15 { d5 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.7): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd4d5] } 11... cxd5 { WATCH OUT: Nxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.4 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bxd8 Rxd8 Qe1 cxd5 Bxd5 Nef6 } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc6d5] } 12. exd5 { exd5 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.8): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } (12. Bxd5 $15 { Bxd5 is also possible for White here. } 12... O-O 13. Bxf7+ Rxf7 14. Qxd6 Nxe4 15. Bxd8 Nxd6 $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.4).] WATCH OUT: Rxd8 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.4 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qd5 Bxf3 gxf3 Ng5 Kh1 e4 } 16. Bc7 (16. Rd1? Nf5 17. Ba5 e4 18. Rxd7 Rxd7 19. Nd4 e3 20. Na3 Nxd4 { Rd1 looks natural, but after ...Nf5 and ...e4 everything goes your way: White's rook has to take on d7 and ends up a piece down (about -5.2). }) 16... Ne8 17. Ba5 e4 { Here is the point: White's last bishop is nearly trapped, and every retreat costs a tempo. Your pawn push attacks the knight on f3 and opens the diagonal for the bishop on h5. } 18. Nd4 Rxf1+ 19. Kxf1 Ne5 20. Nd2 Nf6 $17 { You are clearly better (about -1.4): the pawn on e4 cramps White, your knights are centralised and the bishop on a5 is far from the action. }) 12... e4 { [%csl Bd1,Bf3,Bh5][%cal Bh5d1,Be4f3,Be1e8] } 13. Qe1 O-O 14. Nd4 Qc7 $17 { White has too many hanging pieces - the bishop, the d5-pawn and h2 - and is on the verge of collapse. } { [%csl Bc4,Bc7,Bd5,Bd6,Bf6,Bh2][%cal Bf6d5,Bd6h2,Bc7c4] } 15. Nd2 (15. Be2 Bxe2 16. Qxe2 Bxh2+ 17. Kh1 Bf4 18. Na3 Rae8 19. Rad1 e3 { trading the bishop does not stop the bleeding: the h2 pawn is gone and the e-pawn rolls on (about -2.3). }) 15... Bxh2+ { Nd2 attacks the pawn on e4 and opens the f-file against your knight, but it comes too late: the bishop takes with check, and the queen on c7 covers h2, so the king cannot take it. } 16. Kh1 Rae8 17. Ne6 { White's last tactical try: a knight sacrifice to get a pawn to e6 hitting your knight on d7. } 17... fxe6 18. dxe6 Kh8 19. exd7 Nxd7 $17 { The sacrifice runs out of steam: you keep an extra pawn and the white king is still exposed (about -1.8). }) 11... O-O $14 { The Fantasy Variation. } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 12. Bb3 $14 { Engine continuation (Stockfish 16, depth 18), not in the original chapter: best play for both sides, added so that the line reaches the target position. Review before relying on it.  WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.2 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Nxf7 Rxf7 Raxd1 Qe8 Bxf6 } 12... b5 13. a4 { WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.4 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Nxf7 Qb6 Raxd1 Rxf7 Nf3 } 13... Qb6 14. Kh1 { WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.7 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxd1 Nxd7 Nxd7 Raxd1 bxa4 Bxf7+ } 14... Rae8 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] } 15. axb5 (15. Bc2 a6 16. Bf2 Qc7 17. h3 Bg6 18. Nh4 Bh7 19. Bg1 exd4 { the quieter plan, with the bishop retreating to f2 and the knight to h4, leaves you fully equal (about 0.0). }) 15... cxb5 16. Bc2 Ng4 17. Qe2 exd4 18. Nxd4 Be5 19. Rf5 Ngf6 $14 { White opens the a-file with axb5 and drops the bishop back to c2 to support e4. Your counter is ...Ng4, aiming at h2 and e3, followed by ...exd4 and ...Be5 to put your bishop on a strong central square. Rf5 harasses the bishop on h5, but ...Ngf6 holds everything together. White keeps a small, annoying edge (about 0.3). } *


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Chapter #29: Fantasy Variation, Tartakower Treatment (II)"]
