1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The second Tartakower treatment of the Fantasy Variation features White with an isolated e-pawn and open/half-open files on both sides (d-file open, e-file half-open for Black, f-file half-open for White), a genuinely unbalanced structure where piece activity against the isolated pawn is Black's main long-term asset. As with other isolani-facing chapters (compare Chapter #4), the technical plan is to trade down toward an ending where the isolated pawn becomes a real liability rather than rushing a attack that lets White's extra central space compensate.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives +0.2 to +0.4, a small White edge. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Qxc3? at move 13 loses material (about 6.5); ...Qxh2? at move 14 loses material (about 7.8); 12...Qc7? (about 1.9; prefer ...Qf6); 10...Kf8? (about 1.6; prefer ...Bf6); 10...h5? (about 1.2; prefer ...Bf6); 14...b5? (about 1.0; prefer ...Ne7).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: no White mistake costing 0.5 or more was found along this line.
Other Fantasy Variation chapters: #28. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #5. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. f3 $10 { This is the Fantasy Variation of the Caro Kann. The Fantasy Variation of the Caro Kann is an aggressive opening choice against the Caro Kann. White actually wants a fantasy land where he has an open f file for his rook to take advantage of. Second to that white wants to get a fortified center following a principled development on the center. So far white just wants an easy play against black but at the same time get the initiative and advantage. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gf2f3] } 3... e6 $10 { e6, this variation has a Semi Slav taste against the Fantasy Variation. First of all you create a very solid and fortified center. This is another alternative choice against the Fantasy Variation if you like positional and solid gameplay. You are going to try and set up a Solid Semi Slav like structure. This can even transpose into a French Defence like position if white were to advance his central pawn forward. } { [%csl Ge6,Gd5,Gc6,Gb7,Gf7][%cal Ge7e6,Gf7d5,Gb7d5] } (3... Qb6 { 3...Qb6!? (Aggressive Alternative) - instead of meeting 3.f3 with the structural ...e6 or ...dxe4, Black immediately presses on b2 and d4, forcing White to react right away rather than Black settling into a slow structure. CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE 3...Qb6 IF you are happy to play a more active, slightly unusual line that puts the queen on the b6-g1 diagonal and meets f3 with immediate counter-pressure on d4 and b2 (medium risk). For a calmer game stick with 3...dxe4 (Chapter #28) or 3...e6 (the main line of this chapter). 3...Qb6 puts pressure on d4 and b2. White's main tries are 4.Nc3, 4.a3, 4.a4 and 4.c3. } 4. a4 { 4.a4 is White's most testing try, gaining a tempo on a future ...a5/...Qb4 ideas while defending b2 indirectly; after 4...e5 the position opens into a sharper fight than the main 3...e6 lines. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), a livelier option for a reader who wants to sidestep the quieter main lines of this chapter entirely.] } (4. c3 { 4.c3 - the quieter alternative to 4.a4, simply supporting the center and ignoring the pressure on b2 for now. } 4... e5 { 4...e5! strikes back in the center immediately, exploiting the tempo White spent on move 2 (f3) rather than development; Black already stands slightly better here. [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.1), a second sound reply alongside the existing 4.a4 line in this chapter.] } 5. a4 (5. exd5 cxd5 6. Ne2 Nc6 7. Qb3 Qxb3 8. axb3 exd4 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 { a safe route into a queenless position where you stay comfortable. }) (5. Ne2 Bd6 6. exd5 Ne7 (6... cxd5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.05).] }) 7. dxc6 Nbxc6 8. d5 Bc5 9. Ng3 (9. dxc6 { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -2.7).] }) 9... Bf2+ 10. Ke2 Nxd5 11. Qxd5 Be6 12. Qe4 O-O { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 5... exd4 6. a5 Qd8 7. cxd4 Bb4+ 8. Bd2 Bxd2+ 9. Nxd2 dxe4 $10 { The a4-a5 jab chases your queen back, but White has spent time on it while you take the centre pawns. You win a pawn on e4, which White recaptures quickly, and the position is level (about 0.0). }) (4. Nc3 { 4.Nc3 - simply develops instead of committing to a3/c3 right away, daring Black to take on b2 for real. } 4... e6 { 4...e6 is the calmest and best-scoring reply: finish development and let White prove the pawn grab on b2 is worth anything (it generally isn't, since Qxb2 walks into Nb5 or Rb1 tricks). [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.3), a third sound try alongside the existing 4.a4 and 4.c3 lines in this chapter.] } (4... dxe4 5. Nxe4 (5. fxe4 { White recaptures with the pawn and keeps a big centre on d4 and e4 with an open f-file. The idea is to build with Nf3, Bd3 and Qe2, and to attack with e5 and Bc4 later. It is common in practice (played in about 49 thousand games in the online database). It means White has a big centre, so you must hit it before it becomes a wall. } 5... e5 $10 { Strike the centre at once. After dxe5 your queen is not hit, and after Nf3 exd4 you reach an easy ending. Your plan is simple: exchange queens, play ...Bd6 and ...Ne7 with your king on its home square, and enjoy the healthy pawn structure. Avoid delaying, since a pawn on e4 backed by d4 gives White a free hand. } (5... Nf6?! { The natural developing move is a mistake here. White plays 6.e5 and the knight is kicked, so you lose the centre and fall behind in development. } 6. e5 Nd5 7. Nxd5 cxd5 8. Bd3) 6. Nf3 exd4 7. Qxd4 Qxd4 8. Nxd4 Bd6 { Your bishop comes to d6 and then e5 to hit the knight on c3 and the pawn on b2. } 9. g3 Be5 { The bishop is strong in the centre. Next you play ...Ne7, ...Nd7 and ...Nf6 or ...Bg4 and you can castle long, with a level ending. } 10. Nf3 { Overall: after the queens go off you are equal, with ...Ne7 and ...Be5 to come and no weaknesses to worry about. This is an open, queenless position with no centre pawns for you and a lone e4 pawn for White, so activity matters more than structure. Your bishop on e5 is attacked by the knight on f3, so settle it first: trade with ...Bxc3+ bxc3 to wreck White's queenside pawns, or retreat to c7 or f6. Then play ...Ne7, ...Nd7 and ...Nf6 or ...Bg4, and castle long or short. White wants Bc4, Be3 and Rd1 to use the open d-file; put a rook on d8 to answer. The mistake is leaving the bishop to be hit with tempo. If White plays Bf4, simply trade bishops. }) 5... Bf5 6. Ng3 (6. c3 Nd7 7. a4 (7. Bd3 e5 8. Ne2 Bg6 9. O-O Ngf6 10. Re1 Nxe4 11. Bxe4 Bd6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 7... Ngf6 8. Bd3 Bg6 9. a5 Qc7 10. a6 b6 11. Ne2 e5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.08).] }) (6. Ne2 Nd7 7. N2g3 Bg6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.03).] }) 6... Bg6 7. f4 e6 8. Nf3 Bd6 9. Ne5 Ne7 { The bishop pair is safe and Black's king can castle comfortably. Roughly equal. [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 5. a3 (5. Qd2 Nd7 6. b3 dxe4 7. fxe4 e5 8. dxe5 Nxe5 9. Bb2 Bg4 { the quieter Qd2 and b3 plan lets you develop with ...Bg4 and equalise (about 0.1). }) 5... Be7 6. Be3 Nd7 7. Be2 e5 8. exd5 Nh6 9. Na4 Qc7 $10 { White's a3 takes b4 from your bishop and queen and prepares Na4 to harass the queen. Your answer is to blast open the centre with ...e5 before White is ready: the pawn on d5 is given back for play, because f3 weakened the e1-h4 diagonal (...Bh4+ ideas) and your knight heads to f5 to hit d4. After Na4 the queen steps out of the attack. You have comfortable play for the pawn (about 0.4). }) (4. a3 { 4.a3 stops ...Bb4 but wastes time. 4...e5! gives Black a strong initiative. [Engine verdict: about -0.4 and worse for White after exd5.] } 4... e5 5. exd5 cxd5 6. Ne2 Nc6 7. dxe5 Bc5 { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -1.5).] }) 4... e5 $10 { The lever hits d4 at once: the pawn has lost support because f3 blocks the g1 knight, so White has to resolve the centre right away. After exd5 you take on d4 with the queen, offer a queen trade, and the pawn count is soon level again. } (4... e6 5. Ne2 Nf6 6. a5 Qd8 7. e5 Nfd7 8. c3 c5 9. dxc5 { the calmer route costs you a tempo with the queen and leaves White a little more comfortable (about 0.3). }) 5. exd5 (5. dxe5 a5 (5... Bc5 6. a5 { [Engine check: a clear edge for White (about +1.2).] }) 6. exd5 Bc5 7. Nh3 Bxh3 8. gxh3 Bf2+ 9. Ke2 cxd5 10. Ra3 Bc5 11. Rb3 Qe6 12. f4 Nc6 13. Ke1 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.7).] }) 5... Qxd4 6. Qxd4 exd4 7. Na3 Nf6 8. dxc6 Nxc6 9. Bd3 $10 { Your pawn on d4 cramps the white pieces and the knight on a3 is trying to find a good square, so you are fine (about -0.2). }) 4. Nc3 { Nc3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } (4. Be3 { 6.Be3 - develops the bishop to a quiet square before deciding how to recapture on e4, rather than committing with the main 6.Nc3 right away. } 4... dxe4 { 6...dxe4 (Solid & Optimal) - simply takes the pawn and meets the recapture with normal development; the simplest route to a comfortable, slightly favorable structure for Black. [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.2), the safest and most accurate reply.] } (4... Qb6 { 6...Qb6 (Complicated & Imbalanced) - keeps the central tension and immediately eyes b2, provoking White into an early decision rather than resolving the pawn structure yet. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), livelier but less precise than simply taking on e4.] } 5. b3 (5. Nd2 Qxb2 6. a4 Qb6 7. c3 c5 8. Bb5+ Nc6 9. exd5 exd5 { the pawn grab works because the queen returns to b6, though White gets some development (about -0.2). }) 5... Nd7 6. Qd2 dxe4 7. fxe4 Ngf6 8. e5 Ng4 9. Bf4 f6 $10 { The queen on b6 attacks d4 and b2; b3 simply protects the pawn and Qd2 shores up d4. You take e4, hit the bishop with ...Ng4 and undermine the e5 pawn with ...f6, so your pieces come to life. The position is level (about 0.0). }) 5. fxe4 (5. Nd2 exf3 6. Ngxf3 Nf6 7. Bd3 c5 8. dxc5 Nbd7 9. Nb3 Qc7 { you keep the pawn and hit the centre with ...c5 (about -0.3). }) 5... Qh4+ 6. g3 Qxe4 7. Qe2 Nf6 8. Bg2 Qf5 9. Nf3 Qa5+ $10 { White recaptures on e4, but your check with the queen lets you take that pawn as well. White's compensation is development: Qe2 defends the bishop on e3 and challenges your queen, and Bg2 hits it again. You answer by keeping the queen safe on f5 and checking on a5, ending with an extra pawn and a level game (about 0.1). }) (4. Bd3 { 6.Bd3 - develops actively toward the kingside instead of 6.Nc3, planning to meet ...dxe4 by recapturing with the bishop. } 4... dxe4 { 6...dxe4 (Solid & Optimal) - takes the pawn at once; after 7.Bxe4 Black develops normally and reaches a comfortable, well-tested structure with no memorization required. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2), the simplest and safest choice.] } (4... c5 { 6...c5 (Complicated & Imbalanced) - strikes at the center immediately instead of resolving the e4-tension, aiming for a sharper, more unbalanced middlegame with chances for both sides. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), a livelier but less forcing alternative.] } 5. c3 (5. exd5 exd5 6. Qe2+ Be6 7. Nh3 Qh4+ 8. Kd1 Qxd4 9. Nf4 c4 { the white king is in the middle and your queen takes a pawn with tempo (about -0.1). }) 5... Nc6 6. Ne2 dxe4 7. fxe4 Be7 8. Be3 cxd4 9. cxd4 Bh4+ $10 { c3 supports d4 against your c5 lever. You pile on with ...Nc6, take on e4 and play ...cxd4, and ...Bh4+ comes with check, forcing a concession in front of the king (g3 weakens the light squares, Kd2 is worse). You get comfortable play (about 0.1). }) 5. fxe4 (5. Bc4?! b5 6. Be2 Nf6 7. fxe4 Nxe4 8. Nf3 Be7 9. Bd3 Nf6 { the bishop is hit and you stay a pawn up (about -1.0). }) 5... Qxd4 6. Nf3 Qd8 7. O-O e5 8. Kh1 Bg4 9. Qe1 Nd7 $10 { Taking on d4 is safe because the bishop on d3 blocks White's queen from defending it. The knight hit costs White time, so you retreat the queen and set up the e5 and Bg4 centre. White has a lead in development for the pawn (about 0.1) and you must finish developing quickly. }) (4. e5 { 4.e5 grabs space immediately rather than developing with Nc3 or Be3 first, trying to punish Black's slower 3...e6 setup before it's consolidated. } 4... c5 5. c3 Nc6 6. Bb5 { ...and Black is already comfortably better once the center is challenged. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-0.8), the most aggressive try at this exact fork but objectively the weakest of White's options here, worth knowing purely as a trap-avoidance line.] } 6... Qa5 (6... Bd7 7. Bxc6 bxc6 8. Ne2 cxd4 9. cxd4 c5 10. O-O Ne7 11. Be3 { another good route (about -0.7). }) 7. Qa4 Qb6 8. Be3 Bd7 9. Na3 cxd4 10. Bxd4 Bc5 11. Bxc5 Qxc5 $17 { Bb5 pins the knight and White hopes for Bxc6+ and dxc5. You hit the loose bishop with the queen; once it is supported by Qa4 you tuck the queen back, unpin with ...Bd7 and win time on d4. You are clearly better and the strike at d4 is the key to the position. }) (4. c4 { White grabs space on the queenside and attacks d5 at once, hoping you will push or exchange and that his lead in the centre will grow before the f3 pawn becomes a liability. It is very common in practice (played in about 70 thousand games). Note that the knight on b1 has not moved, so White is a little loose and you can use that. } 4... dxe4 $15 { Take the pawn. White cannot simply recapture and keep an easy game, because his pieces are not ready. After Nc3 you play ...Bb4, pinning the knight, then ...c5 to undermine d4. Do not feel you must hold the pawn on e4; giving it back in exchange for a clean structure and active pieces is fine. } (4... Bb4+?! { This check looks active, but White answers Bd2 and after ...Be7 the bishop has lost time and White finishes his development with tempo. The game becomes near equal, so you throw away your best chance. } 5. Bd2 Be7 6. Be3 dxe4 7. fxe4 e5 8. dxe5 Nd7 9. Nf3) 5. Nc3 Bb4 6. Qb3 c5 { The queen eyes b4 and b7, but ...c5 defends the bishop and hits d4 at once, so you answer the threat with a counter-threat. Then recapture on c5 and develop the knight to c6. } 7. dxc5 Nc6 8. a3 Bxc5 9. Nxe4 { Overall: you are fully developed, the bishop pair is active on c5, and you have a slight edge. Aim for ...Nf6 and short castling next. This is an open game with the centre cleared and both sides developed, and you are a little more comfortable. Your c5 bishop is attacked by the knight on e4, so your first step is to move it with ...Be7 or ...Bb6. Second, trade White's best piece with ...Nf6, recapturing on f6 with the queen or pawn as needed. Third, castle short and bring the rooks to the centre. White wants to use the queen on b3 against b7 and e6, so keep both pawns covered. The risk is leaving b7 or the bishop hanging in one move. If White plays Bg5 or Nf3, simply continue your development. }) (4. c3 { White supports d4 so that he can meet ...dxe4 with fxe4, and prepares Nd2 or Bd3 with a flexible set-up. It is a quiet, solid choice that is common in practice (played in about 48 thousand games), and it avoids the sharp lines with Nc3. For you it means a fight for a small positional edge, not a tactical melee. } 4... dxe4 $15 { Take on e4 and then ...exf3, stripping White of his centre pawn. After Nd2 exf3 Ngxf3 you have the better structure, since e4 and f3 have gone. Your plan is ...Nf6, ...c5 to undermine d4, bishop to e7 or d6 and castle short. Avoid leaving the pawn on e4 under attack from too many pieces. The edge is small. } (4... c5 { A fine alternative, but White gets an easy position with Bd3 and Ne2, and the advantage drops to about equal. } 5. Bd3 Nc6 6. Ne2 dxe4 7. fxe4 Qh4+ 8. g3 Qd8 9. Bc2) 5. Nd2 exf3 6. Ngxf3 Nf6 7. Bd3 c5 { Strike at the centre now, before White castles. } 8. Qe2 cxd4 9. Nxd4 { Overall: your pieces come out smoothly, you have a good structure, and you can aim for ...Bd6 and ...O-O with a comfortable game. This is an open, healthy position with the f- and e-pawns exchanged, so you have the better structure and a smooth, comfortable game. Your plan is, first, to complete development with ...Nc6 hitting the knight on d4. Second, play ...Bd6 and castle short, aiming at the h2 pawn. Third, use the half-open c-file and the knight on f6 to pressure White's centre. White wants Nf5 or Nxe6 with Bxh7 ideas against your king. The risk is leaving e6 loose or letting Bxh7 work when your f6 knight leaves. If White plays Nb3, answer calmly with ...Bd6 or ...Be7, but do not lose time. }) 4... Nd7 { Nd7 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } (4... Bb4 $14 { Bb4 leads to roughly equal (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bf8b4] } 5. Bd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } 5... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 6. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 6... Bxd2+ { WATCH OUT: Qxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.9 from Black's own side of the board), after: c3 Qd8 cxb4 Ne7 Bc4 O-O } { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb4d2] } 7. Qxd2 $14 { Independent depth-28 verification of the critical tabiya in the 3...e6 4.Nc3 Bb4 branch of the Fantasy Variation. This particular branch (4...Bb4, pinning the knight before deciding on a central break) is NOT the line this course actually recommends - the course's own main choice against 4.Nc3 is the simpler 4...Nd7, covered in Chapter #29. This is included purely as supplementary reference for a related, well-known try, along with a genuine practical warning worth knowing: if you ever reach this position and follow up carelessly with an immediate ...Nd7 before resolving the pin on b4, White wins material outright with Bxb4! - there is no way to recapture. The correct, sound continuation trades the bishop off first with ...Bxd2+, exactly as shown here, before continuing development. } { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bd1d2] } 7... b6 $16 { The engine's top choice here (b6, +93) - a small, normal edge for White typical of this whole system, nothing alarming. } { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } (7... Nf6 $16 { Also fine: Nf6 (+101), a very similar alternative at essentially the same small edge for White. } { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 8. O-O-O { [%csl Bc1][%cal Be1c1] } 8... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 9. h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 9... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 10. fxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf3e4] } 10... e5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Be6e5] } 11. dxe5 $16 { [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+1.1). Notable features: White has doubled pawns on the e-file.] [%csl Be5][%cal Bd4e5] } 11... Qxd2+ (11... Qc7 12. Qd6 Qxd6 13. exd6 Bg4 14. Nf3 Nd7 15. Bc4 Rfe8 16. Rhf1 { avoiding the trade lets White keep the pawn on d6 as a thorn (about 1.0). }) 12. Rxd2 Nd7 13. e6 Nc5 14. exf7+ Kxf7 15. Nf3 Ke7 16. b4 $16 { The queens come off first, because on the open d-file White's queen would otherwise take yours. White's doubled e-pawns are not weak yet, since e6 gains time; you meet it with the king on e7. White's initiative with b4 keeps a clear edge. }) (7... Ne7 $16 { Also fine: Ne7 (+111), a very similar alternative at essentially the same small edge for White. } { [%csl Be7][%cal Bg8e7] } 8. O-O-O { [%csl Bc1][%cal Be1c1] } 8... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 9. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 9... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 10. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 10... b6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 11. Kb1 $16 { [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+1.1).] [%csl Bb1][%cal Bc1b1] } 11... Nd7 (11... Bb7 12. Rhe1 Na6 13. Qc3 O-O 14. Qa3 b5 15. g4 Nd5 16. Rg1 { another setup, but White's pieces are active here too (about 1.2). }) 12. c4 Bb7 13. c5 bxc5 14. dxc5 O-O 15. g4 Ne5 16. Nd6 $16 { Kb1 prepares c4 and a grip on the d-file; your knight comes to d7 to cover f6 and e5, but the c4-c5 break opens lines and lets the knight jump to d6, an outpost you cannot easily remove. The g4 pawn storm comes with it. You are under pressure but your pieces are solid (about 1.1). }) 8. O-O-O { [%csl Bc1][%cal Be1c1] } 8... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 9. h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 9... Ba6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Bc8a6] } 10. Nh3 { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bg1h3] } 10... Bxf1 { [%csl Bf1][%cal Ba6f1] } 11. Rhxf1 $14 { A small, stable White edge from the extra space and bishop pair, consistent with the overall known assessment of the Fantasy Variation - fully playable and well-trodden for Black. } { [%csl Bf1][%cal Bh1f1] } 11... Qe7 $14 { The queen develops and eyes e4, and it stays flexible for ...Nbd7. White's plan is a kingside pawn storm with g4 and g5; your counter is ...Nbd7, then ...Nxe4 and ...e5 so that your central play arrives first. } (11... Nxe4 12. fxe4 O-O 13. g4 Nd7 14. h5 Qh4 15. Rh1 Qxg4 16. Nf2 { grabbing pawns with the queen costs time and the queen soon runs short of squares (about 1.2). }) 12. g4 Nbd7 13. g5 Nxe4 14. fxe4 e5 15. d5 cxd5 16. exd5 $14 { The structure is now dynamic: White has a passed d-pawn but also doubled pawns, and you must watch the long diagonal. You are slightly worse (about 0.9). }) 5. Be3 { Be3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gc1e3] } (5. Nge2 { 5.Nge2 - develops the second knight flexibly instead of committing the bishop yet, keeping Nf4 or g3 available later. } 5... Ngf6 { 5...Ngf6 meets this with simple, solid development; nothing here needs memorizing beyond normal piece placement. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4), within the normal range for this chapter.] } 6. e5 (6. Bf4 Qb6 7. e5 Ng8 8. Qc1 Ne7 9. h4 h5 10. Nd1 c5 { with the bishop out you answer in the same way (about -0.1). }) 6... Ng8 7. f4 Ne7 8. Be3 Qb6 9. Rb1 Nf5 10. Bf2 c5 $14 { This is an Advance French structure with f4 included: White gains space with e5 and f4, you kick back and reroute your knight to f5. Your queen hits d4 and b2, which forces Rb1, and the knight on f5 adds to the pressure on d4. The key plan is ...c5: keep hitting d4 until White has to resolve the tension. You are only a little worse. }) 5... a6 $14 { THE ...a6 IDEA, AGAIN. The same ...a6 idea from the Two Knights Attack chapters shows up here too: a6 keeps an eye on the b5 square, ruling out any White piece landing there and preparing a future ...b5 expansion of Black's own queenside space. In this particular structure it also simply gains a tempo for development, since White's pieces are not yet ready to punish the small loss of time. } { [%csl Ga6][%cal Ga7a6] } 6. Bd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 6... Be7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gf8e7] } 7. Nge2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gg1e2] } 7... dxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4,Rf3e4,Rc3e4] } 8. fxe4 $10 { Black strikes at the centre with ...e5 and does not let White take over the game. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf3e4] } (8. Nxe4 $14 { Another option for white. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 8... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 9. c4 { [%csl Bc4,Bd4][%cal Bc2c4,Bd2d4] } 9... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 10. Bxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } 10... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6,Be4][%cal Bd7f6,Bf6e4] } 11. Bc2 { [%csl Bc2][%cal Be4c2] } 11... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 12. Qd3 (12. g4 b5 13. g5 Ne8 14. Qd3 g6 15. c5 Bxg5 16. Bxg5 Qxg5 { the pawn storm gives up the g5 pawn, though White still has some play for it (about 0.1). }) 12... b5 13. c5 a5 14. Bg5 g6 15. h4 e5 16. dxe5 Nd5 $14 { The bishop on c2 and queen on d3 aim at h7, and the g5 pin on your knight adds to it; h4 followed by h5 is White's kingside plan. You block the h7 diagonal with ...g6 and hit the centre with ...e5, and the knight lands on d5 with an attack on e3, so White's extra pawn on e5 will not be easy to keep (about 0.4). }) 8... e5 $14 { e5 needs to be played in order to develop the knight with ease on Ngf6. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge6e5,Gd8c7,Gc7e5] } 9. Ng3 { [%csl Gg3,Ge4][%cal Ge2g3,Gg3e4,Gc3e4,Gd3e4] } 9... exd4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge5d4] } 10. Bxd4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge3d4] } 10... Bf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge7f6] } 11. Be2 $14 { The quieter Be2 (rather than Bc4 in the main line) is actually the more accurate try, keeping the bishop safely out of any future ...Nb6 or ...b5 ideas while still eyeing the kingside. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd3e2] } (11. Bc4 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 18, White's point of view): this is the chapter's original move; it scores -0.52, while Be2 scores +0.32, so it costs White about 0.8. The chapter's original line continues here as a variation.] [%csl Gc4][%cal Gd3c4] } 11... Ne5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gd7e5] } 12. Bb3 { Bb3 leads to a small Black edge (-0.4): White has an isolated pawn. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gc4b3] } (12. Be2 $15 { Be2 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.6): White has an isolated pawn. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Bc4e2] } 12... Nf3+ { [%csl Bf3][%cal Be5f3] } 13. Bxf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Be2f3] } 13... Bxd4 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.7). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bd4][%cal Bf6d4] } 14. Qd3 (14. Nge2 Be5 15. Qxd8+ Kxd8 16. O-O-O+ Kc7 17. Na4 Nh6 18. Nc5 Bd6 { the knight move allows a queen trade, but you keep your bishops and the better structure (about -0.9). }) 14... Be6 15. O-O-O Qg5+ 16. Kb1 O-O-O $15 { The queen on d3 attacks your strong bishop on d4, so you develop with tempo: ...Be6 brings the last minor piece out, ...Qg5+ gains time with a check, and castling long adds a rook to the defence of d4 and takes the d-file. You keep the bishop pair and the better development (about -0.6). }) 12... Bg4 { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gc8g4] } 13. Qd2 { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gd1d2] } 13... Nc4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Ge5c4] } 14. Bxc4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gb3c4] } 14... Qxd4 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair.] [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd8d4] } 15. Qxd4 (15. Nf5 Qxd2+ 16. Kxd2 O-O-O+ 17. Kc1 Kb8 18. h3 Bh5 19. Rf1 Bg6 { the knight jump hits your queen, but you trade queens with check and keep a lead in development (about -1.4). }) 15... Bxd4 16. h3 Be6 17. Bxe6 fxe6 18. O-O-O O-O-O 19. Nge2 Bb6 $15 { With the queens off you keep the bishop pair and a powerful bishop on d4. The h3 move kicks your bishop on g4, so you trade it for White's good bishop, which opens the f-file for you. Both sides castle long and your bishops give you the better of it (about -0.4). }) 11... Bxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bf6d4] } 12. Qxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd1d4] } 12... Qf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd8f6] } 13. Qd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bd4d2] } 13... Qh6 $14 { A small, normal edge for White after the further simplification shown - manageable for Black with careful play, and a minor but genuine improvement on the main line. } { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bf6h6] } 14. Qd3 $14 { Engine continuation (Stockfish 16, depth 18), not in the original chapter: best play for both sides, added so that the line reaches the target position. Review before relying on it. } 14... Ne7 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.2).] } 15. Nf5 (15. Qd4 Qf6 16. O-O-O O-O 17. Qxf6 Nxf6 18. Na4 Ng6 19. Rd4 Re8 { an easy line for you: queens come off and you take the open e-file (about 0.1). }) 15... Nxf5 16. exf5 O-O 17. g4 Qh4+ 18. Qg3 Qxg3+ 19. hxg3 Re8 $14 { The knight jumps to f5 to hit your queen and the g7 pawn; you take it, accept White's f5 pawn and castle. When White pushes g4 you check, trade queens and take the e-file with the rook, so White's pawns end up bent but the pieces stay active (about 0.4). } *


