1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The 2.d3 Breyer treatment reaches an unusual structure for a Caro-Kann repertoire: White's own a-pawn is isolated and half-open files appear on the queenside (a-file for Black, b-file for White) rather than the center. Because 2.d3 avoids the main theoretical battlegrounds entirely, Black's practical plan is simply sound, flexible development (...Nf6, ...g6 or ...e5 depending on White's setup) and patience, White has voluntarily given up the chance for a central advantage, so there is rarely a need for Black to force anything.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives -0.7 to -0.3, a small Black edge. Black is already comfortable here if White follows the chapter's line. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: 4...f6? (about 0.8; prefer ...Bd6); 6...d4?! (about 0.6; prefer ...Nf6); 7...Qe7?! (about 0.5; prefer ...O-O); 6...a5?! (about 0.5; prefer ...Nf6).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 11.g4? Qxd4 (costs White about 1.3); 2.Bc4?! d5 (costs White about 0.9); 3.Qe2?! d4 (costs White about 0.5).
Chapter #31 continues the 2.d3 systems. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #11. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6,Rd3,Rc3,Rc4][%cal Gc7c6,Rd2d3,Rb1c3,Rf1c4] } 2. d3 $10 { d3 is a positional way of handling the Caro Kann, eventually white is not looking for a quick or aggressive attack but rather would play a positional and quiet game. This however may not be the strongest option for white but it is a quiet and positional response when it comes to defensive play. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd2d3] } (2. Nc3 $14 { Nc3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bd5,Bf3,Be2][%cal Bd7d5,Bd1f3,Bd1e2] } 3. Qf3 { Qf3 leads to a small Black edge (-0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bd1f3] } (3. Qe2 $15 { Qe2 is an unorthodox reply but a logical one, as White wants to launch an aggressive kingside attack. } { [%csl Bd4,Be4,Be2][%cal Bd5d4,Bd5e4,Bd1e2] } 3... d4 { d4 leads to a modest Black edge (-0.6), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd5d4] } (3... dxe4 $14 { A much easier approach is the exchange of pawns followed by the move Bf5, end of Opening Preparation. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 4... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 5. d3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd2d3] } 5... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 6. g4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bg2g4] } 6... Bxe4 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.9 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Bxe4 Bxa5 Bxh1 f3 h5 } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf5e4] } 7. dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } 7... Qb6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5). Notable features: White has the bishop pair.] [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 8. Nf3 (8. a4 Bd6 9. Nf3 Nd7 10. a5 Qc7 11. a6 bxa6 12. g5 Ne7 { a4-a5-a6 opens the a-file, but your structure is fine and the game is balanced (about 0.4). }) 8... Nd7 9. g5 Bd6 10. h4 h6 11. g6 fxg6 12. Bh3 Ngf6 $14 { White plays g4-g5 and h4 for a kingside pawn storm, with the bishop pair as a bonus. You meet it with ...Bd6 and ...h6, break open g6 on your terms and develop the knight to f6 (about 0.5). }) 4. Nd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bc3d1] } 4... Qc7 { [%csl Bc7,Bd3,Bc3][%cal Bd8c7,Bd2d3,Bc2c3] } 5. d3 { d3 leads to a small Black edge (-0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd2d3] } (5. c3 $15 { Another breakthrough idea is c3, which is neither complicated nor hard to understand. } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 5... e5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Be7e5] } 6. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 6... c5 { [%csl Bc5,Bd4,Be5][%cal Bc6c5,Bc5d4,Be5d4] } 7. b4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb2b4] } 7... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 8. Bb2 { [%csl Bb2][%cal Bc1b2] } 8... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 9. bxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bb4c5] } 9... Bxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bf8c5] } 10. cxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc3d4] } 10... Nxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc6d4] } 11. Nxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bf3d4] } 11... Bxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4] } 12. Bxd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bb2d4] } 12... exd4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be5d4] } 13. f3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf2f3] } 13... O-O $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.4).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 14. Qb5 (14. Qd3 Qd6 15. a4 Be6 16. Qa3 Qe5 17. Nf2 Rac8 18. Qb2 Nd7 { the same plan with a different queen square (about -0.4). }) 14... Be6 15. Be2 Rac8 16. Nf2 Nd7 17. O-O Rfd8 18. Rfb1 b6 $15 { Your advanced pawn on d4 cramps White, but it also needs defending, so you shore it up with ...Rfd8 and develop around it. White's plan is pressure on the b-file with Qb5 and Rfb1, so you answer with ...b6 and keep the c-file. You keep a small edge. }) 5... e5 { [%csl Be5][%cal Be7e5] } 6. g3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bg2g3] } 6... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 7. Bg2 { [%csl Bg2][%cal Bf1g2] } 7... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 8. f4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bf2f4] } 8... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 9. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 9... Nbd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 10. Bd2 { WATCH OUT: Nxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -5.8 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxe2 Nxd7 Nxd7 Kxe2 O-O Ne3 } { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bc1d2] } 10... O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.5).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 11. Nf2 (11. f5 c5 12. c4 dxc3 13. Nxc3 Nb8 14. h3 Bxf3 15. Qxf3 b5 { gaining space on the kingside is met by your queenside counter (about 0.2). }) 11... Bxf3 12. Bxf3 a5 13. O-O a4 14. Kh1 h6 15. Rac1 Rfe8 $14 { The knight reroutes to f2 and the bishop pair is the long-term asset. Your plan is to expand with ...a5-a4 and keep the e5 pawn guarded; White wants f4-f5 or the exchange on e5 to open the f-file. A small White edge (about 0.6). }) 3... dxe4 $10 { Taking the pawn is simpler than d4, which brings complications for both sides; it returns to the main line of the Classical Caro-Kann. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 4... Nd7 $10 { The Karpov Variation is a good way to play the Classical Caro-Kann, but here ...Nd7 is better, avoiding doubled pawns; the queen on f3 can create tactical ideas if ...Nf6 is played instead. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 5. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 5... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 6. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 6... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 7. Qxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf3e4] } 7... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bd7f6] } 8. Qf4 { [%csl Bf4][%cal Be4f4] } 8... Bg4 { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 9. f3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf2f3] } 9... Bh5 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.8 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 fxg4 Nd5 Qe5 Qxb2 } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bg4h5] } 10. Qh4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bf4h4] } 10... e6 { [%csl Be6,Bh3,Bg4][%cal Be7e6,Bg1h3,Bg2g4] } 11. Nh3 { Nh3 leads to roughly equal (+0.0), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bg1h3] } (11. g4?! $15 { This allows ...Qxd4. } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bg2g4] } 11... Qxd4 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5).] WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -2.9 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Bb4 c3 Bxg4 cxb4 Qd5 } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd8d4] } 12. Ne2 (12. Qf2? Qxf2+ 13. Kxf2 Bg6 14. Bxg6 hxg6 15. Kg2 Nd5 16. Nh3 Bc5 { the queen trade leaves you a pawn up and much better (about -2.0). }) 12... Qb4+ 13. Nc3 Bg6 14. a3 Qd4 15. Bxg6 fxg6 16. Bd2 Qe5+ $17 { White's tempo moves (Ne2, a3) chase your queen, but you stay a pawn up, the bishop trades itself off on g6 and the checks keep coming. The white king is stuck in the middle (about -1.2). }) 11... Be7 { [%csl Be7,Bh4][%cal Bf8e7,Be7h4] } 12. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 12... O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 13. Bxh7+ (13. Bg5 Bg6 14. Bxg6 hxg6 15. Nf2 Re8 16. O-O-O Qa5 17. Kb1 e5 { the quiet pin is met by ...Bg6 (about -0.2). }) 13... Kxh7 14. Bg5 Kg8 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Qxh5 Rc8 17. O-O-O c5 $10 { Bxh7+ is a deflection: it drags the king to h7 so that Bg5 can pin the knight on f6 against the bishop on h5, and White regains the piece. You have to know this line so that you do not fall for it. White ends a pawn up, but the pawn on d4 is isolated and your bishop on f6 is active, so ...c5 equalises (about 0.0). }) (2. Bc4 $15 { Another variation in this particular opening is the move Bc4 the Hillbilly Attack. This move is complete nonsense against the Caro Kann but it is indeed playable. Its nonsense because after the move d4 the bishop on c4 fails to stop the d5 breakthrough and at the same time once the d5 pawn has been played. The bishop has to reroute back to either e2 or b3. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Bf1c4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Bb3,Bd5][%cal Bd7d5,Be4d5,Bc4b3] } 3. exd5 { exd5 leads to a small Black edge (-0.4): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be4d5] } (3. Bb3 $17 { Another interesting idea is the Hillbilly Gambit, in which White sacrifices a pawn and aims for a quick attack or mate. } { [%csl Bb3,Bh5,Bf7][%cal Bc4b3,Bd1h5,Bh5f7] } 3... dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 4. Qh5 { Qh5 leads to a clear Black edge (-1.0): Black has a material edge of about 1 point(s); Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bd1h5] } (4. Nc3 { 4.Nc3 goes directly for the recapture on e4 rather than delaying with f3 first, since the pawn can't be defended long-term. } 4... Nf6 5. f3 exf3 6. Nxf3 { ...and Black is already clearly, comfortably better. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.1), the sharpest try against this Hillbilly Attack sideline but objectively the weakest for White.] } 6... Bf5 7. d4 e6 8. O-O Bd6 9. Ne5 Bg6 10. Qe1 Nbd7 11. Bf4 $17 { You are a pawn up, and White's compensation is development plus the Bb3 bishop aimed at f7 and the knight on e5. Close the diagonal with ...e6, develop with ...Bd6 and offer trades with ...Bg6 and ...Nbd7: every piece that comes off removes an attacker. Do not grab anything else and do not leave your king in the middle too long. You remain clearly better (about -1.0). }) 4... e6 $15 { ...e6 is needed to secure Black's kingside. ...g6 is another option, but White can launch a quick and unpleasant kingside attack. } { [%csl Be6,Bb3][%cal Be7e6,Bb3f7,Bh5f7] } 5. Nc3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb1c3] } 5... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 6. Qh4 { [%csl Bh4,Be4,Bc3][%cal Bh5h4,Bh4e4,Bc3e4] } 6... Na6 $10 { Black needs to give back the pawn but in exchange black's position is better and development is faster. } { [%csl Ba6][%cal Bb8a6] } 7. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bc3e4] } 7... Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4] } 8. Qxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bg4e4] } 8... Nc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Ba6c5] } 9. Qe3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Be4e3] } 9... Nxb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc5b3] } 10. axb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Ba2b3] } 10... b6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bb7b6] } 11. d3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bd2d3] } 11... Bb7 { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bc8b7] } 12. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 12... c5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6). Notable features: Black has the bishop pair; White has doubled pawns on the b-file.] [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 13. Qe5 (13. O-O Bd6 14. Bd2 O-O 15. Rfe1 e5 16. Ng5 h6 17. Ne4 Bc7 { castling first lets you meet Ng5 with ...h6 and the knight on e4 is no problem after ...Bc7 (about -0.6) }) 13... Qd7 14. O-O f6 15. Qh5+ g6 16. Qg4 e5 17. Qxd7+ Kxd7 $15 { White's queen is hunting the c5 and g7 pawns, but each harassing move is answered by a useful pawn push: ...f6 gives the queen a kick and ...e5 builds a centre. In the queenless ending you have the bishop pair and the doubled b-pawns of White are a lasting weakness (about -0.5). Your king on d7 is perfectly safe with the queens gone. }) 3... cxd5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc6d5] } 4. Bb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bc4b3] } (4. Bb5+ { Your d5 pawn attacked the bishop on c4, so White saves it with check and a tempo. White hopes to follow with a4, d4 and Nf3 and reach a comfortable game with no weaknesses. It is very common in practice, so you should have a clear answer. } 4... Bd7 $15 { Block with the bishop and offer a trade. If White retreats with Be2 you have gained time, and if White takes on d7 you recapture with the knight or queen and are comfortable. Plan: ...e6, then ...a6 to challenge the bishop, ...Bd6 and ...Ne7, and castle short. Your d5 pawn is well supported and the structure is sound. Avoid trading your light-squared bishop for no reason, because that leaves d5 weaker. } (4... Nd7 { A playable alternative, aiming for an early ...e5, but the knight blocks your own bishop on c8 and is a little less good. } 5. d4 a6 6. Bd3 e5 7. dxe5 Nxe5) 5. a4 e6 6. Nf3 a6 7. Be2 Bd6 8. d4 Ne7 9. b3 { White has spent several moves on the bishop and you have a compact setup. Castle, bring a knight to c6 and push ...f5 or ...b6 only when needed. Your later breaks are ...Nbc6 followed by ...e5 or ...f6. Overall: you are slightly better thanks to the lost time. This is a closed, compact position where White has spent several moves on the bishop and you hold a solid centre with pawns on d5 and e6. Your plan: castle short, bring the other knight out with ...Nbc6, then free your game with ...e5 or ...f6 once your pieces are placed. White wants Ba3 to trade your dark-squared bishop, or c4 to challenge d5. You are happy to trade bishops on a3, and you meet c4 with ...dxc4 bxc4. The typical mistake is drifting passively because the position looks quiet, so keep making useful moves. If White delays castling, you have time for ...Nbc6. }) 4... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 5. d4 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } (5. Nf3 { White develops the knight and keeps the bishop on b3 pointing at your d5 pawn and at f7. The idea is to follow with d4 and reach a comfortable isolated-pawn type of position. This is a very common choice (played in about 861 thousand games), so expect it. For you, d5 is the target, so keep it defended while you develop with tempo. } 5... Nc6 $15 { Nc6 adds a second defender to d5 and prepares ...e5, the central break this whole line is built on. Your plan is to play ...e5 at once, answer d4 with ...e4 to gain space and chase the knight, then bring the bishop to d6 and the other bishop to e6 and castle short. Avoid slow set-ups with ...e6, which lock in your c8 bishop and leave d5 weak and static. The numbers give you a small edge. } (5... Bf5 { A solid alternative. Put the bishop outside your pawn chain first, then play ...e6, ...Nc6 and ...Be7. It is a quieter game: you give up the quick ...e5 break but keep a healthy structure against 6.d4. } 6. d4 Nc6 7. O-O h6 8. Bf4 e6 9. Re1 Be7 10. Nbd2) 6. h3 { This stops ...Bg4, so you cannot pin the knight. It is slow, and that is why you can strike in the centre immediately. } 6... e5 7. d4 e4 { The pawn on e4 attacks the knight and is protected by d5. White must decide where the knight goes. } 8. Ne5 Bd6 { You challenge the knight at once; if White supports it with f4 you keep a firm grip on the light squares. Develop Be6, castle, and look for ...Qe7 or ...Nxe5 to remove the strong piece when it suits you. } 9. Nc3 Be6 10. Bf4 { Overall: you hold more space in the centre and have easy development, while White has to prove the knight on e5 is not just overextended. This is a space-gaining position where you have e4 and d5 supported by pieces, and White's knight on e5 is strong but not unassailable. You have more space in the centre and easy development. Your plan: castle short, then ...Qd7 or ...Qe7, and challenge the knight with ...Nxe5 when it suits you, using ...Ng4 or ...Nd7 to bring pieces over. White wants Nxc6, then Bxd6, to ruin your structure, or f3 to break up the e4 pawn. The mistake is allowing Nxc6 bxc6 with a damaged structure; if White plays f3, answer with ...exf3 and keep control of d5. }) (5. d3 $15 { White avoids sharp theory and plans a slow build-up with Nf3, c3 or Nbd2 while the bishop on b3 keeps eyeing your d5 pawn. It is very common, played in about 497 thousand games, but d3 is a modest move that spends a tempo, so you get a free hand in the centre. You are slightly better here. } 5... Nc6 { Develop with a purpose. The knight hits d4 and prepares ...e5, the break you want: with pawns on d5 and e5 you own the centre and White's pieces have little room. Your plan is ...e5, then the light-squared bishop to e6 or g4, ...Bd6 or ...Be7, and castle kingside. Avoid the passive ...e6 set-up that locks in your c8 bishop; you do not need it. } 6. h3 (6. Nf3 e5 { If White develops first, you play ...e5 anyway and answer Bg5 with ...Be7. }) 6... h6 { White stops ...Bg4, so you spend a move on ...h6 to take the sting out of Bg5 pins. Do not drift with ...Bf5 and ...e6; keep the e5 break in mind. } 7. Nf3 e5 8. d4 e4 { White challenges the centre, and you gain space with ...e4, which shuts out his knight from e4 and cramps his position. } 9. Ne5 Be6 { The knight on e5 looks strong, but you can challenge it with ...Nxe5 or ...Nd7 and ...Bd6, and ...Be6 develops while keeping d5 protected. Avoid ...exd4, which opens lines for White. Overall: you hold a big centre and develop easily while White's pieces tie themselves to d4 and e5. } 10. Bf4 { This is an open centre where you hold the e4 and d5 pawns and have space, so you are slightly better. Your knight on e5 is attacking, but White's own knight hits your c6 knight. Your plan is, first, to trade the strong knight with ...Nxe5. Second, play ...Bd6 to trade the dark bishops and castle short. Third, support the centre with ...Qd7 and ...Rd8. White wants c4 to undermine d5 and a quick attack on f7. The risk is playing ...Bd6 first, which lets White play Nxc6 with tricks. If White plays c4, answer with ...dxc4 and keep your centre solid. }) 5... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 6. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 6... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 7. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 7... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 8. Bg5 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 8... O-O $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3).] [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 9. c3 (9. Nbd2 h6 10. Bh4 Nc6 11. c3 g5 12. Bg3 Nh5 13. Ne1 Nxg3 { the immediate Nbd2 lets you hit the bishop with ...g5 (about -0.3) }) 9... Nc6 10. Bc2 Bxc2 11. Qxc2 b5 12. a3 h6 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 $15 { White offers a bishop trade to remove your good bishop and aims the queen along the c-file. You accept, then gain queenside space with ...b5 and ask the dark-squared bishop what it wants with ...h6. After the bishops swap, your bishop on f6 puts pressure on d4 together with Nc6 (about -0.2). Your plan is ...Rc8 and ...Qd6 with a slightly better, safe game. }) 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nd2 $10 { White is eventually aiming for a King's Indian-type structure with Ngf3, g3, Be2 and 0-0. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } (3. f4 { 3.f4!? - a more ambitious try than the main 3.Nd2, grabbing extra central space at the cost of some kingside structure. Nothing to fear: just develop. } 3... Nf6 { Simple development is enough; if White pushes 4.e5 the knight just retreats to g8 and comes back via f6 or h6 later, while Black's structure stays sound and White's kingside is slightly loosened. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4), well inside the normal range for this repertoire.] } 4. e5 (4. exd5 cxd5 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Ne5 h5 7. c3 Bf5 8. Nd2 e6 { White's more modest capture gives you an easy game (about -0.5) }) 4... Ng8 5. Be3 Bf5 6. g4 Bd7 7. Bg2 h5 8. g5 Bg4 $14 { White's motive after e5 is space: f4 backs up the e5 pawn and g4-g5 will cramp your pieces and kick your bishop. You answer with ...Bd7 and the counter-thrust ...h5, which undermines g4 and fixes the g5 pawn, and then ...Bg4 hits the queen and keeps the light squares under your control. You can follow up with ...e6, ...c5 and ...Ne7 to undermine e5. White keeps a small edge (about +0.5) but his kingside is very loosened. }) (3. Qe2 { 3.Qe2!? - an unusual developing move, preparing to meet ...dxe4 with a recapture that keeps the queen centralized. } 3... dxe4 4. dxe4 e5 { Taking back in the center and immediately answering with ...e5 is the cleanest equalizer: Black grabs an equal share of the center at once rather than letting White keep it. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] } 5. Nf3 { 5.Nf3 develops naturally instead of the sharper f4 or Nc3 tries against this early ...e5. } 5... Nf6 6. Nbd2 Qc7 7. Nc4 { ...and Black's extra space and development lead give a real, lasting edge. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-0.7), a real try worth recognizing but clearly the weaker choice at this fork.] }