1. e4 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. The King's Indian Attack / Leela setup is White's quietest try covered in the repertoire, and the resulting structure (fully symmetrical pawns, open d- and e-files) reflects that: nobody has any structural advantage at all. The whole chapter is about piece placement rather than pawns, Black's knight on d5 and b6 are already well-posted at the target position, and the practical plan is to keep contesting the open central files with active pieces, since there's no pawn-break race to win or lose. Treat this chapter as 'equalize comfortably and outplay from a level position' rather than looking for a forcing sequence.
Against White's best play, Stockfish 16 (White's point of view) gives -0.4 to -0.3, a small Black edge. Black is already comfortable here if White follows the chapter's line. Treat anything noticeably worse than about +0 as a sign an earlier move slipped, not as how this line is supposed to go.
A few specific tries are worth flagging before you even start: ...Bxg3? at move 12 loses material (about 4.2); 10...h6? (about 1.3; prefer ...Rxe1+); 4...f6? (about 0.8; prefer ...Bd6); 10...Nb8? (about 0.8; prefer ...Rxe1+); 11...Ne5?! (about 0.6; prefer ...Nxd5).
If White slips instead, it usually looks like this: 12.a3?! N7f6 (costs White about 0.7; engine-found, not in the chapter tree); 13.h4?! Qe8 (costs White about 0.7; engine-found, not in the chapter tree).
Other 2.d3 systems chapters: #30. Practise the middlegames in Middlegame Plan #11. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d3 $10 { White delays d4 entirely and aims for a King's Indian Attack style setup with Nd2, g3, Bg2 and O-O, keeping the center flexible rather than committing to a specific pawn structure right away. BLACK'S PLAN: this system lives or dies on Black's central response. Grabbing full central space immediately with ...e5 (rather than waiting) is the single most important practical lesson here - it denies White any chance of a favorable King's Indian Attack-style kingside buildup and leaves Black with at least equal, often slightly better, chances with completely natural development. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd2d3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nd2 $10 { The main King's Indian Attack move order - White prepares Ngf3 and a kingside fianchetto without touching the center pawns yet. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } (3. Nf3 $10 { The alternative move order, sometimes nicknamed the 'Leela' setup after the engine that helped popularize delaying Nd2 in favor of an immediate Nf3. } { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 3... dxe4 $10 { By far the simplest practical answer - trade in the center immediately, before White gets a chance to recapture with a piece and keep some initiative. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 4. dxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd3e4] } (4. Ng5 { This is an unorthodox gambit in which White trades a pawn for fast development. It is not a cause for concern: Black should simply develop normally and try to disrupt White's set-up. } 4... exd3 5. Bxd3 Qc7 { This prevents Bf4. Black should keep disrupting White's ideal development, otherwise Black ends up cramped and difficult to play. } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gc7f4] } 6. O-O Nf6 7. Nc3 Bg4 { White is forced to move his queen to e1. If f3 then his knight cannot go back to f3 anymore. } 8. Qe1 h6 9. Nge4 Nbd7 10. Nxf6+ Nxf6 11. h3 Bd7 12. Be3 e6 13. Rd1 Be7 { Comfortable position for black. Black should consolidate his central control and develop comfortably. } 14. Bd4 (14. f4 c5 15. Bc4 O-O-O 16. f5 Bd6 17. fxe6 fxe6 18. Qe2 a6 { White's f4-f5 lever opens the f-file, but your king on c8 is far from the action and you have the extra space in the centre (about -0.5) }) 14... Qa5 15. Be5 O-O 16. f4 Be8 17. a3 Nd7 18. Bd4 Bf6 $15 { Bd4 aims at your f6 knight and the g7 pawn. ...Qa5 hits the c3 knight and keeps the queen active, and after Be5 you castle and challenge the bishop with ...Nd7 and ...Bf6. White's f4 only weakens his own king. You keep a small, comfortable edge (about -0.1). }) 4... Qxd1+ { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bd8d1] } 5. Kxd1 $14 { Believe it or not, this simplification is completely fine for Black. Yes, White has to recapture with the king, but the position is so simple and symmetrical that this costs nothing concrete - no real attack is possible on an open board with almost no pieces left to attack with. } { [%csl Bd1][%cal Be1d1] } 5... Bg4 $14 { Pin the knight right away. Black already has a very comfortable, easy position with essentially no theory to memorize. } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bc8g4] } 6. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 6... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 7. Ne1 { [%csl Be1][%cal Bf3e1] } 7... Bxe2+ { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg4e2] } 8. Kxe2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bd1e2] } 8... g6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bg7g6] } 9. Nd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Be1d3] } 9... Bh6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bf8h6] } 10. Nd2 { [%csl Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 10... Ngf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Bg8f6] } 11. f3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bf2f3] } 11... Nf8 { [%csl Bf8][%cal Bd7f8] } 12. b4 { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb2b4] } 12... Ne6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Bf8e6] } 13. Nb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd2b3] } 13... Bxc1 { WATCH OUT: Nf4+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.3 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxf4 Bxf4 Bxf4 Nh5 Be3 g5 } { [%csl Bc1][%cal Bh6c1] } 14. Rhxc1 { [%csl Bc1][%cal Bh1c1] } 14... Nd7 $14 { A fully verified sample line all the way into a simple, balanced middlegame: Black trades the light-squared bishop for a knight, then the dark-squared bishop as well, reaching a position with no weaknesses and no real winning try for White - exactly the kind of safe, low-maintenance equality this line is meant to offer against an opponent trying to avoid main-line theory. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bf6d7] } 15. a4 f6 16. f4 Kf7 17. c4 c5 18. b5 b6 19. a5 h5 $14 { White's plan is to gain space on the queenside with a4, c4 and b5 and to fix your pawns there, hoping that the knights end up passive. You hold the queenside shut with ...c5 and ...b6, give the king room with ...f6 and ...Kf7 and then counter on the kingside with ...h5. Your knights have good squares on e6 and d7 and White has no breakthrough, so the position stays a small, safe plus for White (about +0.4). }) (3. f4 { 3.f4!? - a more ambitious try than the main 3.Nd2, grabbing extra central space at the cost of some kingside structure. Nothing to fear: just develop. } 3... Nf6 { Simple development is enough; if White pushes 4.e5 the knight just retreats to g8 and comes back via f6 or h6 later, while Black's structure stays sound and White's kingside is slightly loosened. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4), well inside the normal range for this repertoire.] } 4. e5 (4. exd5 cxd5 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Ne5 h5 7. c3 Bf5 8. Nd2 e6 { White's more modest capture gives you an easy game (about -0.5) }) 4... Ng8 5. Be3 Bf5 6. g4 Bd7 7. Bg2 h5 8. g5 Bg4 $14 { After e5 White wants space: f4 backs up the e5 pawn and g4-g5 will cramp your pieces and kick your bishop. You answer with ...Bd7 and the counter-thrust ...h5, which undermines g4 and fixes the g5 pawn, and then ...Bg4 hits the queen and keeps the light squares under your control. You can follow up with ...e6, ...c5 and ...Ne7 to undermine e5. White keeps a small edge (about +0.5) but his kingside is very loosened. }) (3. Qe2 { 3.Qe2!? - an unusual developing move, preparing to meet ...dxe4 with a recapture that keeps the queen centralized. } 3... dxe4 4. dxe4 e5 { Taking back in the center and immediately answering with ...e5 is the cleanest equalizer: Black grabs an equal share of the center at once rather than letting White keep it. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0).] } 5. Nf3 { 5.Nf3 develops naturally instead of the sharper f4 or Nc3 tries against this early ...e5. } 5... Nf6 6. Nbd2 Qc7 7. Nc4 { ...and Black's extra space and development lead give a real, lasting edge. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-0.7), a real try worth recognizing but clearly the weaker choice at this fork.] } 7... Nbd7 (7... Be6? 8. Ncxe5 Na6 9. Bf4 Nc5 10. Ng6 Qa5+ 11. Bd2 Qb6 12. Nxh8 { your e5 pawn is attacked twice (Nc4 and Nf3) and defended once, so blocking with ...Be6 lets White take it and even pick up the h8 rook (about +0.3, nearly a full point worse than ...Nbd7) }) 8. Bd2 b5 9. Ba5 Qb8 10. Ncd2 Bc5 11. b4 Bb6 12. Bxb6 $15 { Here Nc4 attacks e5 a second time, so ...Nbd7 adds the second defender and prepares ...b5 with tempo on the knight. White's Ba5 hits your queen, but Qb8 simply keeps everything protected. After the trade on b6 you still hold the centre and have more space (about -0.4). }) (3. Nc3 { 3.Nc3 - transposes toward a Two Knights-style setup a tempo behind the usual move order, since White already spent a move on d3. } 3... dxe4 { 3...dxe4 is simplest: take the pawn and meet the recapture with normal development; the extra tempo White spent on d3 barely matters. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] } 4. dxe4 (4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nf3 (5. Ng3 { White retreats the knight to a safe square instead of exchanging it, so he keeps an extra piece for the middlegame and eyes f5 and h5 for later. The move is simple development with no direct threat and it is very common (played in about 101 thousand games). For you it means the centre is yours to fight for: you have a free hand to play the usual pawn breaks. } 5... c5 { Your plan is to take space in the centre and the queen side with ...c5, then ...Nc6, ...e6 and ...Be7, castling short. The pawn on c5 stops White from ever playing d4 comfortably and gives your pieces natural squares. Avoid rushing ...e5 and ...Bd6 without preparation, because White's knight on g3 then looks at f5 and e4 and can become annoying. } 6. Nf3 Nc6 { Develop with tempo-free simplicity: the knight on c6 pressures d4 and supports ...e5 or ...d5 ideas later. } 7. Be2 e6 { Now the pawn on e6 opens the bishop's diagonal and defends d5 and f5. If White tries 8.Ng5 hitting f7 and h7, you simply answer ...h6 or ...Be7 and the knight has nowhere good to go. } 8. O-O Be7 9. c3 O-O 10. Qa4 { White wants to pin and annoy your knight on c6. You can answer with ...Qc7 and ...b5 ideas when needed. Overall: you have a comfortable, flexible game and are slightly better here. This is a flexible semi-open position: your pawns stand on c5 and e6, White has d3 and c3, both kings have castled short and neither side has a space edge. Your plan: first meet the queen on a4 with ...Qc7 or ...Bd7 so the knight on c6 is comfortable; then bring a rook to d8 and c8; then consider ...e5 or ...Nd4 to claim the centre. White wants to regroup with Nbd2-c2 or Re1 and play d4 at the right moment, so keep the c5 pawn solid. The risk is ...e5 played too early, when Nf5 and Ng3 pieces become annoying. If White deviates with d4, recapture calmly. }) 5... Nxe4 6. dxe4 Qxd1+ 7. Kxd1 Nd7 8. Be3 e6 { another way to trade queens that costs White castling (about +0.2) }) 4... Qxd1+ 5. Nxd1 Nf6 6. f3 a5 7. a4 e5 8. Ne3 Nfd7 $14 { Recapturing with the knight keeps White's castling rights, so do not expect a completely safe endgame. f3 props up e4 and stops ...Ng4, and a4 stops your a-pawn. You still have a solid centre with ...e5, and ...Nfd7 heads for c5 to hit e4. White has a slight edge (about +0.3). }) (3. Be2 { 3.Be2 - a quiet developing move, keeping options flexible rather than committing to the main 3.Nd2 King's Indian Attack plan. } 3... dxe4 { 3...dxe4 again the simplest equalizer: take the pawn and develop normally against the recapture. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1).] } 4. dxe4 (4. Nc3 exd3 5. Bxd3 Nd7 6. Qe2 Ngf6 7. Bf4 Nc5 8. O-O-O Bg4 { grabbing space with tempo does not give White enough for the pawn: you keep a clear lead in development and the extra pawn (about -0.7) }) 4... Qxd1+ 5. Kxd1 e5 6. a4 Bc5 7. Ke1 Nf6 8. Nd2 a5 $10 { Trading queens costs White the right to castle, so the king has to walk back to e1. a4 stops ...b5 and grabs queenside space, but ...Bc5, ...Nf6 and ...a5 give you easy development and the d4 square. You have nothing to worry about here (about +0.3). }) 3... e5 $10 { Grab the center immediately - this is the single most important practical lesson against a King's Indian Attack setup as White: if Black is slow, White gets to choose the structure; if Black grabs the center first, White's whole setup loses most of its point. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] } 4. Ngf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 4... Nd7 { [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): this move scores +0.43, while Bd6 scores -0.05, so it costs about 0.5. It is playable, but it is a concession] [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } (4... Bd6 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE THIS IF you want a classical, symmetrical set-up against the King's Indian Attack: ...e5, ...Bd6, ...Nf6 and ...O-O, with ...Re8 and ...a5-a4 for queenside space (low risk, easy to learn). Against 5.d4 you can also play the open structures shown below. White's main set-ups are 5.g3 (King's Indian Attack) and 5.d4 (transposing to an open game). } 5. g3 (5. d4 exd4 6. exd5 cxd5 { 5.d4 exd4 6.exd5 cxd5 leads to an open position with a symmetrical pawn structure. Black has no problems. [Engine verdict: about -0.1 to -0.3.] } 7. Nxd4 (7. Nb3 Nf6 8. Nbxd4 Nc6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.23).] }) (7. Bb5+ Nc6 8. O-O a6 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. Nxd4 Ne7 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 7... Nc6 8. Bb5 Bd7 (8... Ne7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.07).] }) 9. N2b3 (9. N2f3 Nf6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 9... Nf6 10. O-O O-O 11. Bg5 h6 12. Bh4 Re8 13. Bd3 g5 14. Bg3 Nxd4 15. Nxd4 Bc5 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 5... Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Re8 { Black develops normally and waits for White to commit. [Engine verdict: about -0.2, equal.] } 8. b3 (8. Nh4 Bg4 { 8.Nh4 Bg4 hits the queen and the bishop pair is not important here. [Engine verdict: about -0.7.] } 9. Qe1 (9. Bf3 Be6 { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -0.8).] }) (9. f3 Be6 10. f4 exf4 11. gxf4 Ng4 { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -2.8).] }) 9... a5 10. h3 (10. f4 exf4 11. gxf4 Nh5 { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -1.0).] }) 10... Be6 11. a4 Na6 { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -0.8).] }) (8. Re1 Nbd7 { 8.Re1 Nbd7 and ...a5 and ...Nc5 give Black a standard, safe plan. [Engine verdict: about -0.3.] } 9. c3 a5 10. a4 (10. d4 exd4 11. Nxd4 Ne5 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 10... Nc5 11. Qc2 dxe4 (11... h6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 12. dxe4 b5 13. axb5 cxb5 14. b4 Na4 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 8... a5 { 8.b3 a5 prepares ...Na6 and ...Nc5. White's queenside play is slow and Black gets a comfortable position. [Engine verdict: about -0.5, a small Black edge.] } 9. a3 (9. Bb2 a4 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.7).] }) (9. a4 Na6 10. Bb2 Nb4 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 9... Na6 10. Bb2 dxe4 11. dxe4 (11. Nxe4 Nxe4 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 11... b5 12. Qe2 Nc5 13. Nh4 Bg4 14. Bf3 Be6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 5. g3 { g3 leads to roughly equal (-0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Gg3][%cal Gg2g3] } (5. d4 $14 { Engine-suggested line (Stockfish 16, depth 20), not in the original chapter. This is White's strongest try here (about +0.50); the chapter's own g3 scores about -0.14. Review before relying on it. } 5... exd4 6. exd5 cxd5 7. Nxd4 Bd6 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.6).] } 8. Nf5 (8. Bd3 Ne7 9. O-O Nc5 10. N2b3 Nxd3 11. Qxd3 h6 12. Bd2 O-O { the quiet move is less testing (about +0.1) }) 8... Be5 9. Nf3 Ne7 10. Bd3 O-O 11. Nxe7+ Qxe7 12. O-O Bc7 $14 { Nf5 hits the bishop on d6 with tempo. You step back to e5 and, after Nf3, offer a knight trade with ...Ne7 so that White's active knight disappears. You then have an isolated d5 pawn but no real weaknesses, and ...Bc7, ...Nf6, ...Bg4 give easy piece play. White keeps a small edge (about +0.5). }) 5... Ngf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 6. Bg2 { [%csl Gg2][%cal Gf1g2] } 6... Bd6 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf8d6] } 7. O-O { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 7... O-O { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 8. Re1 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gf1e1] } 8... Re8 $15 { A natural, comfortable position where Black is already doing at least fine, arguably slightly better, thanks to the bigger central footprint. } { [%csl Ge8][%cal Gf8e8] } 9. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd3d4] } 9... exd4 { [%csl Rd4][%cal Re5d4] } 10. exd5 { [%csl Rd5][%cal Re4d5] } 10... Rxe1+ { [%csl Re1][%cal Re8e1] } 11. Nxe1 { [%csl Re1][%cal Rf3e1] } 11... Nxd5 { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rf6d5] } 12. Ne4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd2e4] } 12... Bf8 { [%csl Gf8][%cal Gd6f8] } 13. Qxd4 { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rd1d4] } 13... Qb6 { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gd8b6] } 14. Qxb6 { [%csl Rb6][%cal Rd4b6] } 14... N7xb6 { [%csl Rb6][%cal Rd7b6] } 15. b3 { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gb2b3] } 15... Bf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 16. Nd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Ge1d3] } 16... Re8 $15 { A fully verified, realistic continuation showing the point in practice: once the center clarifies and queens come off, Black's pieces (bishop pair, active rook, well-placed knights) are at least as good as White's, if not better - proof that grabbing the center early pays off well into the middlegame and endgame. } { [%csl Ge8][%cal Ga8e8] } 17. f3 (17. Ndc5 Nb4 18. Bf4 Nxc2 19. Rf1 Nd5 20. Bd6 Bxe4 21. Nxe4 Rd8 { the double-knight jump looks active but ...Nb4 hits c2 and takes over: you are clearly better (about -1.6) }) 17... Bxe4 18. fxe4 Nc3 19. e5 Ne2+ 20. Kf1 Nxc1 21. Rxc1 g6 $15 { White's motive for f3 is to drive away your strong bishop on f5 and win the e4 square back. You accept: ...Bxe4 fxe4 and the knight hops into c3 with tempo, forcing e5 and then ...Ne2+ wins the bishop pair. After the trades you are slightly more comfortable with the more active pieces (about -0.2); the plan is ...g6 and ...Kg7 with a safe, equal ending. } *


