1. e4 $10 { WHERE THIS CHAPTER IS HEADED. This chapter is the single master index of every White second move against 1...c6 covered anywhere in this course, plus a handful of genuinely new tries that weren't covered anywhere else before now. It is organised in three parts. PART ONE just points you to the chapter that already covers each of White's four main systems (2.d4, 2.Nf3, 2.c4, 2.d3) - no new moves here, pure navigation. PART TWO recovers the real sideline tries that already exist elsewhere in this course (the Scaffolding Guide's quick-reference lines, and Chapter #30's Hillbilly Attack / odd-queen-sortie material) so you have them all in one place, each tagged with exactly where the full treatment lives. PART THREE is the genuinely new material: ten White second moves (2.g4!?, 2.h4, 2.b4!?, 2.f3, 2.a3, 2.h3, 2.Na3, 2.Nh3, 2.Qh5?!, 2.Qg4?!) that do not appear anywhere else in the 64 chapters of this course, checked and verified for this chapter specifically. None of this is dangerous with accurate play - the whole point of this chapter is that there is nothing left for White to surprise you with on move two. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE THIS SECTION when White avoids the main systems: each branch below gives a SAFE and a SHARP option. Against 2.Bc4 (Hillbilly Attack) and 2.Nc3 d5 3.Qf3 the sharp refutations are important; the other sidelines are harmless if you simply develop and fight for the centre. Risk level: LOW unless you misplay the early moves. The rare second moves all give Black an easy game if the usual principle is applied: challenge the centre with ...d5 and develop. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { PART ONE - MAIN SYSTEMS (navigation only, no new content). 2.d4 is White's main try and the backbone of this entire course. Depending on White's third move this transposes into: 3.exd5 cxd5 (Exchange Variation, Chapters #1-#3), 3.Nc3/3.Nd2 dxe4 (Classical Variation, Chapters #9-#14), 3.e5 (Advance Variation, Chapters #15-#21), or 3.f3 (Fantasy Variation, Chapters #28-#29). See the Course Map and Scaffolding Guide chapters for the full breakdown of which third move leads where. } (2. Nf3 { 2.Nf3 is the Two Knights Attack. See Chapters #22-#27 for the full treatment (Tartakower, Karpov, Classical, Dano-Russian and ...a6 Variations, and rare third-move tries). No new content here. } 2... d5 3. Nc3 { ...transposing into the main Two Knights tabiya covered in full starting at Chapter #22. [Engine verdict: roughly equal, pure navigation, see Chapter #22.] } (3. d3 Qc7 { CHOICE GUIDE - CHOOSE THIS IF you want to answer a quiet, non-committal 3.d3 with ...Qc7 and simple development (low risk, little theory): the plan is ...dxe4 and ...Nd7, then ...e5 or ...e6 and ...Ngf6. Against 3.d3 the alternative is 3...dxe4 4.dxe4 Qxd1+ which leads to a drawish endgame. Risk level: LOW. After 3.d3 Qc7 Black keeps the queen active and avoids the symmetrical endgame. White's main moves are 4.Nc3, 4.Nbd2, 4.Qe2, 4.e5 and 4.exd5. } 4. Nc3 (4. Nbd2 Bg4 { 4.Nbd2 Bg4 pins the knight and prepares ...e6. After 5.h3 Bh5 6.g4 Bg6 Black is fine. [Engine verdict: about +0.2.] } (4... e5 5. d4 exd4 6. Bd3 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.3).] }) 5. h3 (5. g3 Nf6 6. Bg2 e6 7. O-O Be7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.13).] }) (5. Be2 e6 6. O-O Nf6 7. Nh4 Bxe2 8. Qxe2 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.10).] }) 5... Bh5 6. g4 (6. Qe2 Nd7 7. g4 Bg6 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.3).] }) 6... Bg6 7. Bg2 e6 8. O-O Nf6 9. Re1 Be7 10. b3 dxe4 11. dxe4 Nbd7 12. Bb2 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.17).] }) (4. Qe2 { 4.Qe2 dxe4 5.dxe4 e5 gives Black a comfortable position with a pawn on e5. [Engine verdict: about -0.1.] } 4... dxe4 5. dxe4 e5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.12).] }) (4. e5 { 4.e5 Bg4 hits the knight and prepares ...e6, ...Nd7 and ...c5. [Engine verdict: about 0.0.] } 4... Bg4 5. Nbd2 e6 6. Be2 Nd7 7. d4 c5 8. c4 (8. c3 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.14).] }) 8... Ne7 9. O-O cxd4 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Nxf3 dxc4 12. Qxd4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.10).] }) (4. exd5 cxd5 5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 e6 { Transposing into a Queen's Gambit-like structure; Black plays ...Bb4 and ...O-O comfortably. [Engine verdict: about +0.2.] } 7. Be2 Bb4 8. Qb3 (8. O-O Bxc3 9. bxc3 O-O { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.10).] }) 8... Nc6 9. O-O O-O 10. Nxd5 (10. cxd5 Nxd5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.24).] }) 10... exd5 11. cxd5 Nxd5 12. Qxd5 Be6 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.22).] }) 4... dxe4 5. dxe4 Nd7 { 4.Nc3 dxe4 5.dxe4 Nd7 is the main line: Black develops with ...e5 or ...e6 and ...Ngf6. [Engine verdict: about +0.05, equal.] } 6. Bc4 b5 { The sharp 6.Bc4 b5! gains space and keeps the bishop under pressure. After 7.Bb3 e6 8.O-O Ngf6 Black has no problems. [Engine verdict: roughly equal.] } 7. Bb3 (7. Bd3 Ngf6 8. O-O e5 9. a4 b4 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.12).] }) 7... e6 8. O-O Ngf6 9. Qe2 (9. Re1 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.22).] }) 9... Bd6 10. a4 (10. Rd1 Ne5 11. Nd4 Ng6 12. g3 a6 13. f4 e5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.08).] }) 10... b4 11. Nb1 c5 12. Rd1 (12. Nbd2 O-O 13. Nc4 Ba6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.5).] }) 12... Ng4 13. h3 Nge5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (+0.00).] })) (2. c4 { 2.c4 is the Accelerated Panov Attack. See Chapters #7-#8 for the full treatment. (The main, non-accelerated Panov-Botvinnik Attack, Chapters #4-#6, arises instead after 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5, with White then playing 4.c4.) } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 { ...transposing into the Accelerated Panov tabiya covered in full starting at Chapter #7. [Engine verdict: roughly equal, pure navigation, see Chapter #7.] }) (2. d3 { 2.d3 Systems. See Chapters #30-#31 for the full treatment (Breyer Variation and the King's Indian Attack & Leela Setup). That same Chapter #30 also contains White's odd 3.Qf3/3.Qe2 queen sorties after 2.Nc3 and the Hillbilly Attack/Gambit after 2.Bc4, both recovered in condensed form below. } 2... d5 3. Nd2 e5 { Transposing into the main 2.d3 Breyer/King's Indian Attack tabiya; full treatment at Chapters #30-#31, recovered here in condensed form rather than as a bare pointer. } (3... dxe4 { The simplifying alternative: just take the pawn at once rather than building the same King's Indian Attack structure Black gets anyway after 3...e5. } 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ exf6 $10 { A clean, simple position, similar in spirit to an Exchange-style structure but a tempo different since White never got round to d4. [Engine verdict: roughly equal, a safe practical alternative to the main 3...e5 recommendation.] }) 4. Ngf3 { White's most natural follow-up, developing before committing the dark-squared bishop. } (4. g3 { Also natural, fianchettoing immediately; transposes to the same structures after a normal developing sequence. } 4... Nf6 5. Bg2 Bd6 6. Ngf3 O-O 7. O-O $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal.] }) 4... Bd6 { The standard developing scheme: the bishop takes its most natural diagonal before Black castles. } 5. g3 { Preparing the long fianchetto, the point of this whole King's Indian Attack set-up. } 5... Ne7 { Keeping f6 free for the other knight and preparing a timely ...f5 or ...Nf5 reroute later. } 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O $10 { A normal, balanced King's Indian Attack structure. [Engine verdict: roughly equal, within the normal range for this kind of structure.] Black's simplest plan from here is ...Bg4 or ...Re8 and ...Nd7-f6 regrouping; there is nothing sharp or dangerous for Black to worry about. }) (2. Nc3 $14 { PART TWO - RECOVERED SIDELINES (already covered elsewhere; reproduced here condensed, each citing its home chapter). 2.Nc3 d5 mostly just transposes (3.d4 = main systems above, 3.Nf3 = Two Knights Attack above), but White also has two odd early queen sorties here, both already fully annotated in Chapter #30, recovered condensed below. [RECOVERED FROM: Chapter #30.] } 2... d5 3. Qf3 { 3.Qf3, full treatment in Chapter #30. } (3. Qe2 $15 { 3.Qe2!?, an unorthodox but logical try aiming to meet ...dxe4 while keeping the queen centralized. Full treatment in Chapter #30. } 3... d4 (3... dxe4 $14 { 3...dxe4 is the simpler reply to 3.Qe2: just take the pawn and meet the recapture with normal development. } 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. d3 e6 6. g4 Bxe4 7. dxe4 Qb6 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge, see Chapter #30 for the full line and its WATCH OUT trap after 6...Bxe4.] }) 4. Nd1 Qc7 5. d3 e5 6. g3 Nf6 7. Bg2 Be7 8. f4 Bg4 9. Nf3 Nbd7 10. Bd2 O-O { [Engine verdict: a small White edge, see Chapter #30 for the full line including the 5.c3 alternative.] }) 3... dxe4 (3... e5 { 2.Nc3 d5 3.Qf3 e5! is the right reply, hitting the centre at once. [Engine verdict: about 0.0 after 4.exd5.] } 4. exd5 (4. Qg3 f6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 4... Nf6 5. Bc4 e4 6. Qe2 (6. Nxe4 Qe7 7. d3 cxd5 { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -1.7).] }) (6. Qg3 cxd5 7. Bb5+ Nc6 { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -1.1).] }) 6... cxd5 7. Bxd5 (7. Bb3 Nc6 { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -1.8).] }) 7... Nxd5 8. Qb5+ Nc6 9. Qxd5 Qxd5 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.21).] }) 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. d4 Ngf6 6. Bd3 Nxe4 7. Qxe4 Nf6 8. Qf4 Bg4 9. f3 Bh5 10. Qh4 e6 11. Nh3 Be7 12. c3 O-O { [Engine verdict: roughly equal, see Chapter #30 for the full line including White's inaccurate 11.g4?! trick.] }) (2. Bc4 $15 { 2.Bc4, the "Hillbilly Attack". Active-looking but the bishop just gets kicked by ...d5 with tempo. Full treatment (including the "Hillbilly Gambit" 3.Bb3!?) in Chapter #30. [RECOVERED FROM: Chapter #30.] } 2... d5 3. exd5 { 3.exd5 is the main line. } (3. Bb3 $17 { 3.Bb3!?, the "Hillbilly Gambit": White sacrifices a pawn hoping for a quick kingside kill. Full treatment in Chapter #30, deepened here with the critical trap and the 4.Nc3 alternative. } 3... dxe4 4. Qh5 { The point of the gambit: the queen heads straight toward f7 before Black can consolidate. } (4. Nc3 { 4.Nc3, the sharper alternative mentioned above: instead of chasing the pawn back immediately with the queen, White tries to regain it through faster development. } 4... Nf6 { Simplest: just develop and let White prove the piece play is worth a pawn. } 5. f3 { The critical try, trying to blast the centre open before Black consolidates. } 5... exf3 6. Nxf3 Bf5 $16 { Black keeps the extra pawn and has no weaknesses to show for White's development lead. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.1), confirming 4.Nc3 is a touch trickier in practice but no sounder than 4.Qh5.] }) 4... g6 $15 { The most accurate response, hitting the queen and blunting its route to f7 in one move. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.76), the critical test of the gambit.] } (4... e6 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Qh4 Na6 7. Nxe4 Nxe4 8. Qxe4 Nc5 9. Qe3 Nxb3 10. axb3 b6 { Also fine, and the course's original recommendation: just a little less precise than 4...g6. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge, see Chapter #30 for the full line.] }) (4... Nf6?? $18 { WATCH OUT: this natural developing move walks straight into the point of the whole gambit. } 5. Qxf7+! $18 { White wins back the pawn with interest and leaves Black's king stuck in the center for the rest of the game. [Engine verdict: winning for White (+2.0).] }) 5. Qe2 { White's best try, pinning the e4-pawn to nothing in particular but at least keeping the queen active. } (5. Qh4 { The Hillbilly Attack: 2.Bc4 d5 3.Bb3 dxe4 4.Qh5 g6 5.Qh4 a5 leaves White a pawn down with a loose queen. [Engine verdict: about -1.5 for White.] } 5... a5 6. Qxe4 a4 7. Bxa4 (7. Bc4 Bf5 8. Qe2 b5 9. Bd3 { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -2.3).] }) 7... Nf6 8. Qf4 g5 9. Qg3 Nh5 10. Qxg5 Ra5 11. Qh4 { [Engine check: a large advantage for Black (about -1.9).] }) 5... Nf6 $15 { Black simply continues development, up a clean pawn with no weaknesses to show White for it. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.67), confirming the gambit has failed.] }) 3... cxd5 4. Bb3 Nf6 5. d4 Bf5 6. Nf3 e6 7. O-O Be7 8. Bg5 O-O { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge, see Chapter #30 for the full line.] }) (2. b3 { 2.b3, a quiet Nimzowitsch-style fianchetto try. Harmless: just develop normally. Full treatment in the Scaffolding Guide. [RECOVERED FROM: Scaffolding Guide.] } 2... d5 3. Bb2 Nd7 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4), see the Scaffolding Guide for the full line, including the 3.exd5 alternative.] } (3... dxe4 { 2.b3 d5 3.Bb2 dxe4 wins a pawn but White gets compensation along the diagonal; careful. [Engine verdict: about -1.0.] } 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Nge2 g6 6. Nxe4 Nxe4 7. Bxh8 f6 8. Ng3 Qd4 9. Nxe4 Qxe4+ { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -1.0).] })) (2. f4 { 2.f4, the "La Bourdonnais Attack". An old, aggressive-looking try that overextends more than it threatens. Full treatment in the Scaffolding Guide. [RECOVERED FROM: Scaffolding Guide.] } 2... d5 3. e5 Bf5 (3... c5 4. d4 { 2.f4 d5 3.e5 c5 4.d4 cxd4 5.Nf3 Bg4 gives Black active pieces. [Engine verdict: about equal.] } 4... cxd4 5. Nf3 Bg4 6. Nbd2 (6. Qxd4 Bxf3 7. gxf3 Nc6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.7).] }) 6... e6 7. h3 Bh5 8. Bd3 Nc6 9. O-O Nge7 { [Engine check: roughly equal (-0.17).] }) 4. Nf3 e6 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge, see the Scaffolding Guide for the full line, including the 3.d3 and 4.d4 transposing alternatives.] }) (2. g3 { 2.g3, a fianchetto try that develops quietly rather than fighting for the center with d4. Full treatment in the Scaffolding Guide. [RECOVERED FROM: Scaffolding Guide.] } 2... d5 3. Bg2 dxe4 4. Bxe4 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge, see the Scaffolding Guide for the full line.] }) (2. Ne2 { 2.Ne2, keeps options open (the knight can go to g3 or later support a d4 push). Full treatment in the Scaffolding Guide. [RECOVERED FROM: Scaffolding Guide.] } 2... d5 3. e5 Bf5 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal, a normal Advance Variation with an extra tempo spent on Ne2, see the Scaffolding Guide.] } (3... c5 { 2.Ne2 d5 3.e5 c5 gives Black a comfortable French-style position where ...Nc6 and ...Bf5 come easily. [Engine verdict: roughly equal.] } 4. d4 (4. b4 cxb4 5. d4 e6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) (4. c3 d4 5. Nf4 (5. b4 d3 (5... Nc6 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.3).] }) 6. Nf4 c4 7. Na3 g5 8. e6 Nf6 9. Qa4+ Nbd7 { [Engine check: a small edge for Black (about -0.4).] }) 5... e6 6. Bb5+ (6. Na3 Nc6 7. Nc4 Qc7 { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -1.2).] }) 6... Bd7 7. Na3 Bxb5 8. Nxb5 a6 9. Na3 Nc6 { [Engine check: a clear edge for Black (about -1.0).] }) 4... cxd4 5. Nxd4 { [Engine check: a small edge for White (about +0.4).] })) (2. c3 { 2.c3, a kind of reversed Alapin idea, preparing d4 with extra support. Full treatment in the Scaffolding Guide. [RECOVERED FROM: Scaffolding Guide.] } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge, transposing into an Exchange Variation structure a tempo down for White, see the Scaffolding Guide.] }) (2. e5 { 2.e5?!, premature; the pawn gets no real support and Black simply challenges it at once. Full treatment in the Scaffolding Guide. [RECOVERED FROM: Scaffolding Guide.] } 2... d6 3. exd6 exd6 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal, completely symmetrical, see the Scaffolding Guide.] }) (2. Qf3 { 2.Qf3?!, an eccentric try, developing the queen far too early for no real gain. Full treatment in the Scaffolding Guide. [RECOVERED FROM: Scaffolding Guide.] } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge, see the Scaffolding Guide for the full line, including the 3.Nc3 alternative.] }) (2. Be2 { 2.Be2, a quiet developing move that keeps options open. Full treatment in the Scaffolding Guide. [RECOVERED FROM: Scaffolding Guide.] } 2... d5 3. e5 Bf5 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge, a normal Advance Variation with the bishop already committed to a modest square, see the Scaffolding Guide.] }) (2. g4 $15 { PART THREE - GENUINELY NEW GAP-FILL SIDELINES. These ten moves (2.g4, 2.h4, 2.b4, 2.f3, 2.a3, 2.h3, 2.Na3, 2.Nh3, 2.Qh5, 2.Qg4) do not appear anywhere else in this 64-chapter course - this is the first time they have been checked and written up. 2.g4!?, a flank lunge that weakens the kingside for no real gain. Black just takes the center. } 2... d5 3. exd5 (3. e5 $15 { 3.e5 keeps the tension and transposes into an Advance-style structure, except White has spent a move on g4 instead of a useful developing move. } 3... Bf5 4. h4 { ...and the position is close to equal; the g4-h4 pawns give White some practical kingside space but Black's development is completely normal. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1), a sound alternative try against this rare 2.g4 sideline.] } 4... Bd7 $10 { The calmest reply, simply keeping the bishop safe rather than inviting g4-g5 or h4-h5 ideas against it on f5. 4...Be6 and 4...Bc8 give White a small but real plus instead. } 5. d4 $10 { [Engine verdict: close to dead equal (-0.1), confirming 3.e5 is at least a sound practical try for White here, even though it is nothing special objectively.] }) 3... cxd5 $15 { Simplest and strongest: open the center at once while White's kingside pawn is already loosened. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.9), the clearest way to punish 2.g4.] } 4. d4 $17 { The critical test: White finally plays a real central move, though a full tempo behind a normal Exchange Variation because of the wasted g4. 4.c3 and 4.h3 score almost identically. } 4... Nc6 $17 { Simplest, just developing. 4...h5 first, hitting the overextended pawn immediately, is about as good. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.8), confirming the chapter's existing verdict even against White's best try.] } 5. Nc3 h5 6. g5 Bg4 7. Be2 e5 $15 { Black attacks the overextended g-pawn at once with ...h5. After g5 the pawn is cut off from support, the f5 square is firm for Black, and the break ...e5 opens the centre while White is still undeveloped (about -0.8). }) (2. h4 $15 { 2.h4, a flank expansion with no real point yet, since there's nothing on the kingside to attack. } 2... d5 3. exd5 (3. e5 $14 { 3.e5 transposes into an Advance Variation structure, and 4.h5 next actually gives this sideline a bit more bite than most of the others on this list by hitting the bishop's retreat square early. } 3... Bf5 4. h5 $14 { ...and White gets a small, real edge, the one try on this list worth remembering by name rather than just dismissing. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3), the critical test of 2.h4.] } 4... d4 $10 { The computer's own preference, grabbing extra space at once while the h5 pawn has already committed White's kingside. Simple development (4...Nd7, 4...e6) is just as sound. } (4... c5!? { The engine's choice at greater depth. The idea is to hit d4 and, after dxc5, to take on e5 where the pawn is under-protected. } 5. d4 cxd4 6. Ne2 Qc7 7. Nxd4 Qxe5+ 8. Be3 $10 { Black wins the e5 pawn with check and White's h5 push has not yet bought anything concrete (about +0.1). Slightly more accurate than 4...d4. }) 5. Nf3 $10 { 4.Bc4 scores almost the same. [Engine verdict: a small lingering White edge (+0.2), the one try on this whole list worth remembering by name, see the note at move 3 above.] }) 3... cxd5 $15 { Simplest: open the center and meet h4 with normal development. [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3).] } 4. d4 $10 { The most natural try, simply developing. 4.Bb5+ is the other real option here, scoring almost identically. } (4. Bb5+ $15 { The other genuine try, trading the awkward tempo for a cheap check instead of quiet development. } 4... Bd7 $15 { Simplest, blocking and preparing to trade off the bishop. [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3), essentially interchangeable with 4.d4 above.] }) 4... Nf6 $10 { [Engine verdict: nearly equal (-0.2), the small Black edge from 3...cxd5 alone shrinks further once White develops accurately.] }) (2. b4 $15 { 2.b4!?, a wing-gambit-style try, grabbing queenside space instead of fighting for the center. } 2... d5 { The simplest and strongest reply: ignore the flank entirely and just take the centre. } (2... a5!? { A more direct challenge, attacking the gambit pawn head-on instead of ignoring it. } 3. b5 { Keeping the chain rolling forward rather than trading; 3.bxa5 just hands Black an open b-file and an easy initiative for nothing. } 3... d5 4. exd5 cxd5 $10 { A roughly balanced position; White's queenside space is matched by Black's extra central pawn and freer development. [Engine verdict: roughly equal, a reasonable practical alternative to the main 2...d5.] } 5. Nf3 $15 { Continuing to develop normally. } 5... Nf6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.4), the same assessment as the main 2...d5 line, 2...a5 first does not change the long-term verdict either way.] }) 3. exd5 (3. e5 $10 { 3.e5 transposes into an Advance Variation structure with an extra queenside pawn push thrown in instead of a useful developing move. } 3... Bf5 { ...and Black is at least equal, arguably already a touch better thanks to White's wasted tempo. [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1).] } 4. d4 $10 { The critical test, directly challenging the bishop's square and completing development. } 4... a5 $10 { The thematic queenside counter-strike against the gambit pawn, exactly as in the main 2...a5 line above. } (4... e6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3), solid and simple.] }) 5. b5 $10 { [Engine verdict: still roughly equal (-0.2), the extra space from e5 and the extra weakness from b4 cancel each other out either way.] }) 3... cxd5 $15 { Simplest: take the center, White's b4 pawn does nothing useful here and can become a long-term weakness. [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5).] } 4. Nf3 $15 { Simple development, eyeing e5 next. 4...Bg4, pinning the knight at once, scores just as well as the main try below. } 4... Nf6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.4), basically unchanged from 3...cxd5 alone, the b4 pawn remains a long-term target with no compensation behind it.] }) (2. f3 $15 { 2.f3, supports a later e4/d4 setup but blocks White's own king's knight from its natural square and loses time. } 2... d5 3. exd5 (3. e5 $15 { 3.e5 transposes into an Advance Variation structure, but f3 (instead of a useful developing move) is a real loss of time here. } 3... Bf5 { ...and Black is comfortably at least equal. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6).] } 4. d4 $15 { Completing the center, though f3 still costs a real tempo compared to a normal Advance Variation. } 4... c5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.4), also perfectly good.] } (4... e6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6), f3 continues to cost White real time.] })) 3... cxd5 $15 { Simplest: open the center immediately while White's knight is still stuck behind its own f-pawn. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.7).] } 4. d4 $15 { The critical try, finally getting a real central pawn out while admitting f3 was a loss of time. } 4... Nc6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6), confirmed even against White's best try.] }) (2. a3 $10 { 2.a3, a pure waiting move with no point at all against the Caro-Kann. Just develop normally. } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (-0.1), nothing to remember beyond simple development.] } 4. Nf3 $10 { Simple development, there is nothing else to try. } 4... Nc6 $10 { [Engine verdict: still roughly equal (-0.2), a3 does nothing either way, exactly as expected.] }) (2. h3 $10 { 2.h3, another pure waiting move, slightly more useful than 2.a3 since it at least prevents a future ...Bg4 pin, but still no real try. } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.1), nothing to remember beyond simple development.] } 4. Nf3 $10 { Simple development. Amusingly, the engine's own top choice here is 4.a3, yet another pure waiting move, underlining just how little h3 achieved on its own. } 4... Bf5 $10 { [Engine verdict: still roughly equal (-0.1), developing normally is more than enough.] }) (2. Na3 $15 { 2.Na3, an odd rim development with no clear follow-up, the knight will likely need to re-route via c4 or c2 later. } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3), nothing to remember beyond simple development.] } 4. d4 $15 { Finally getting a real pawn into the center. 4...Bf5 first is about as good. } 4... Nc6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3), unchanged, the knight on a3 still has nowhere useful to go next.] }) (2. Nh3 $15 { 2.Nh3, develops to the rim instead of the natural f3/e2 squares, blocking the kingside pawns and achieving little. } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6), nothing to remember beyond simple development.] } 4. Ng1 $15 { Remarkably, the engine's own top choice is simply retreating the knight straight back home. 4.Bb5+ and 4.Ng5 both score a little worse, since the knight genuinely has nowhere useful to go from h3. } 4... Nc6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5), essentially the same assessment as without the retreat, confirming 2.Nh3 is just a wasted move and nothing more. This exact position, reached here by a knight going to h3 and straight back to g1, is also reached below by both 2.Qh5 and 2.Qg4 retreating their own queen all the way back to d1, three different "premature piece sortie" sidelines all transposing into the identical structure: a normal Exchange Variation with White a full tempo behind.] }) (2. Qh5 $15 { 2.Qh5?!, a very premature queen sortie with no target, Black's center play comes with tempo on the misplaced queen. } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.4), White's queen will likely need another move just to get out of the way.] } 4. d4 $15 { The normal developing try. } (4. Qd1 $15 { The engine's other near-equal try: simply retreating the queen all the way back home, giving up the whole excursion. See the note at 2.Nh3/4.Ng1 above, this transposes into the exact same position as that knight retreat, and into 2.Qg4's own queen retreat below. } 4... Nc6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5), confirming the queen trip achieved nothing, whichever square it started the trip from.] }) 4... Nc6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5), basically unchanged from the -0.4 after 3...cxd5 alone.] }) (2. Qg4 $15 { 2.Qg4?!, another premature queen sortie, this time eyeing the kingside with nothing to actually attack yet. } 2... d5 3. exd5?? { A natural-looking try, but it's a genuine blunder: capturing on d5 clears Black's own d-pawn off the c8-g4 diagonal, leaving the queen on g4 completely undefended on that same diagonal. } 3... Bxg4! $19 { Simplest and strongest: the bishop just wins the queen outright, since nothing stands between c8 and g4 anymore. [Engine verdict: winning for Black (-6.6), there is no way back for White here.] } (3... cxd5 $15 { Also fine if you miss the tactic - Black keeps a safe, normal structural edge - but this gives back almost all of the much bigger advantage 3...Bxg4! would have won outright. [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5), the same idea as 2.Qh5, just on a different square - though 3...Bxg4! is far stronger.] } 4. Qd1 $15 { Retreating at once, the exact same idea, and the exact same resulting position, as 2.Qh5's own queen retreat above (and as 2.Nh3's knight retreat before that), see those notes for the full comment. } (4. Bb5+ $15 { The more natural try in practice, most players would rather throw in an annoying check than quietly admit the queen trip failed. } 4... Nc6 $15 { Blocking with the knight is actually stronger than the more natural-looking 4...Bd7. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.7 to -0.8), the check gains nothing real.] }) 4... Nc6 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.5).] }) 4. h3 $19 { Desperately trying to dislodge the bishop and salvage something, but there is no saving this position, White is down a full queen for a single pawn with no compensation anywhere on the board. } 4... Bf5 $19 { Simplest, the bishop just steps aside with the extra queen safely tucked away. [Engine verdict: completely winning for Black (-6.7), confirming 3...Bxg4! simply ends the game on the spot.] }) 2... d5 $14 { This is the Exchange Variation main tabiya, see Chapter #1 for the full explanation. Everything in this chapter has been a detour around this single, reliable backbone move: whatever White tries on move two, ...d5 (or the matching central break for that specific try) gets Black to a comfortable, well-understood structure. } 3. Nc3 { PART THREE (continued) - GAP-FILL GAMBITS INSIDE THE CLASSICAL VARIATION. The normal replies here, 3.exd5 (Exchange, Chapter #1) and 3.Nc3/3.Nd2 dxe4 (Classical Variation, Chapters #9-#14), are both covered in full elsewhere. Nested a little deeper inside that same Classical tree, though, are three genuinely named gambits/sidelines that do not appear anywhere in those chapters or in the rest of this 64-chapter course. Recovered here for completeness. } (3. Nd2 { The quieter move order; the Alien Gambit below is normally reached this way rather than via 3.Nc3. } 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Ng5?! { The "Alien Gambit": an eccentric, mostly-for-fun knight sacrifice on f7, occasionally seen in blitz and rapid play. } (5. Nxf6+ { The normal move, and the reason 5.Ng5 is only a sideline. White trades the knight that has just recaptured on e4 and Black accepts a slightly doubled f-pawn, getting the bishop pair and a very comfortable game in return. White loses nothing here (about +0.2). The Alien move does the opposite: it spends a tempo on an attack that Black can switch off with one pawn move. } 5... exf6 6. Bc4 Bd6 7. Qe2+ Be7 8. Nf3 Nd7 $10) 5... h6 (5... Bf5 { Also perfectly good: Black simply develops. The knight on g5 attacks nothing that matters (f7 is guarded by the king and the e4 pawn is already gone), so White has to spend another move bringing it back. } 6. Bd3 (6. N1f3?! { White wants to retreat the knight from g5 only after bringing the other knight out, and hopes to gain time with Nh4 against your bishop on f5. It is very common in practice, but the knight on g5 is stuck with no good square, which makes the move a little dubious. } 6... h6 $15 { You kick the knight at once, and it has only h3 as a decent retreat. After Nh3 you play ...c5 and ...e6, hitting the centre while the White knights sit on poor squares on h3 and f4. Your plan is ...Nbd7, ...Be7, castle short and ...a6. Avoid blocking your own light bishop and avoid ...e5 early, because the d4 pawn is not yet weak. The small edge is yours. } (6... Nbd7?! 7. Nh4 h6 8. Nxf5 hxg5 9. c3 e6 10. Ne3 e5 11. d5 { This is the tempting reply many players choose, but White trades your bishop and gets a stable centre, so you only equalise. }) 7. Nh3 c5 8. Nf4 e6 9. Bb5+ Nbd7 10. O-O a6 11. Bd3 { Do not be afraid of Bb5+: you answer with ...Nbd7 and then ...a6 pushes the bishop back, since your king is safe in the centre until you castle. Overall: you have more active pieces, a healthy centre and an easy plan of ...Be7 and castling. This is an open-ish position where you hold a healthy centre and White's knights on f3 and f4 stand a little awkwardly, though your king is still in the middle. Your plan: complete development with ...Be7, castle short, and then play ...Qc7 and ...Rac8 so that ...cxd4 can come at the right moment. White wants to trade light-squared bishops with Bxf5 and open the centre with dxc5; recapture ...exf5 and keep the structure solid, or take back on c5 with a knight. The risk is the Nxe6 shot if your bishop leaves f5 carelessly. If White plays dxc5 at once, take back with ...Nxc5 and trade bishops. }) 6... Bxd3 7. Qxd3 h6 8. N5f3 $10 { White has lost two knight moves for nothing and the game is level (about -0.1). }) 6. Nxf7!? { WATCH OUT if you're on the Black side and haven't seen this before: it looks alarming, but it just isn't sound. } (6. N5f3 { The honest retreat and White's best by a wide margin. The gambit idea is dropped and the knight goes back to a normal square. The net result is that White has spent two knight moves (Ng5 and back) and Black has spent one pawn move (...h6) that is mildly useful, so the position is level. Everything that follows with 6.Nxf7 is White trying to avoid admitting this. } 6... c5 { Black hits the centre at once. Because White's king's bishop is still at home and the queens will come off, Black has no problems. } 7. Be3 Qa5+ 8. Qd2 Qxd2+ 9. Nxd2 cxd4 10. Bxd4 Nc6 11. Ngf3 Nxd4 12. Nxd4 Bd7 $15 { Black has the more comfortable endgame (about -0.3), with the bishop pair and no weaknesses. }) (6. Bc4? { A classic trap in reverse: White threatens Nxf7, but it is too slow. } 6... hxg5 7. Bxg5 Bf5 8. Nf3 e6 9. Qe2 Be7 $19 { Black is simply a whole knight up for a pawn (-3.7). The knight on g5 had no retreat once Black spent a tempo on ...h6, which is exactly why 6.Nxf7 or 6.N5f3 are the only real choices. }) 6... Kxf7 $17 { Simplest and best: take the knight back and get on with the game. The sacrifice really doesn't work objectively, but this exposed king creates real, concrete traps over the next few moves - worth knowing by name, not just by verdict, since several natural-looking replies below throw back most or all of the advantage, and one even loses outright. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.8 to -1.9) with accurate play, confirming the sacrifice doesn't work - but accuracy matters here more than the headline number suggests.] } (6... Qd5? { Declining the sacrifice looks active (the queen hits g2 and c4 is covered) but it loses material by force. } 7. Nxh8 Na6 8. Bxa6 bxa6 9. Nf3 Qe4+ 10. Qe2 Qxe2+ 11. Kxe2 $18 { White keeps the sacrificed piece AND wins a whole rook (+5.8). The lesson for Black: when the sacrifice is unsound, simply take the knight. All the traps below come only after 6...Kxf7. }) 7. Nf3 $17 { White's most natural try, developing before deciding what to do about the missing piece. } (7. Bc4+ $17 { The immediate alternative, checking at once rather than developing the knight first. } 7... Be6 $17 { Simplest: block the check by developing the other bishop, offering a trade on White's terms. } (7... e6 { Also sound: the pawn blocks the diagonal and keeps the king on f7. } 8. Qe2 c5 9. Be3 cxd4 10. O-O-O Bc5 11. Nf3 Nc6 $17 { Black has an extra piece for a pawn and White has not developed much real pressure (about -1.9). 7...Be6 is simpler, but this is perfectly good. }) 8. Bxe6+ { White trades down rather than retreat, since the bishop has no better square anyway. } 8... Kxe6 $17 { Correct and only real choice: recapture with the king. It looks bold marching the king to e6, but there are no immediate checks available and Black's king is perfectly safe there with queens still on. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-2.0), a full piece with no compensation in sight.] } (8... Ke8?? $18 { WATCH OUT: retreating instead of recapturing gives back almost the entire advantage for nothing. [Engine verdict: a winning White edge (+5.0) - simply hand back the extra piece while leaving the king worse placed than before.] }) (8... Kg6?? $19 { WATCH OUT: this one doesn't just give back the advantage, it walks straight into a forced mate. } 9. Qd3+ Kh5 10. Qh3+ Kg6 11. Qf5# { Checkmate. }) 9. Nf3 Kf7 10. Qd3 c5 11. Be3 e6 12. Qb3 Qb6 $17 { The king goes back to f7 as soon as the checks have run out, and Black trades queens into an ending where the extra piece is simply decisive (about -1.7). }) (7. Bd3 { A quieter way to build the attack: develop the bishop to a square where it eyes the kingside, keep Nf3 and Ne5+ ideas in hand, and avoid giving a check Black can answer comfortably. } 7... Bf5! { The key reply. Black offers to trade off White's only attacking piece. Without the light-squared bishop White has almost nothing left against the king. } 8. Nf3 e6 9. Ne5+ Kg8 10. O-O Bxd3 11. Qxd3 c5 $17 { White has spent its whole attack and Black has all the extra material (about -2.1). }) 7... e6 $17 { The practical recommendation: this blocks White's Bc4(+) idea along the long diagonal for good. It does NOT stop every check, though - White's other knight can still jump into e5 with check, and that one needs care (see below). A touch less than the engine's absolute top choice (7...c5), but still the safer overall choice since it avoids 7...c5's own Bc4+ minefield. } (7... c5 $17 { The engine's own top choice, fractionally more precise than 7...e6 - but White's reply forces Black onto one exact square, and several natural-looking alternatives there throw the advantage away or worse. } 8. Bc4+ { The point: this check is still available even here, and it comes with real sting. } (8. Ne5+ { Same idea as in the main line: the knight jumps in with check before Black can coordinate. } 8... Kg8 9. Be3 cxd4 10. Bc4+ e6 11. Bxd4 Nc6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 $17 { Black's king is safe on g8 and White has nothing left to attack with (about -1.9). }) 8... e6 $17 { The only move that keeps the full advantage - block with the pawn and nothing else. } (8... Ke8? $10 { WATCH OUT: the natural-looking king retreat gives back almost all of Black's advantage. [Engine verdict: essentially level (+0.1) - a whole piece's worth of advantage gone in one careless move.] }) (8... Qd5?? $18 { WATCH OUT: this looks natural (blocking the check while centralizing the queen) but it just hangs the queen outright. } 9. Bxd5+ $18 { [Engine verdict: winning for White (+6.2) - the whole point of the piece sacrifice, several moves later.] }) 9. Ne5+ Kg8 10. Be3 cxd4 11. Bxd4 Nc6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. O-O c5 14. Bc3 Qxd1 15. Raxd1 Be7 $17 { White has traded off the attacking knights and the queens come off. Black is a piece for a pawn up in a very simple ending (about -2.3) and just needs to bring the king and bishop pair into play. }) (7... Nbd7 { Also playable, but it gives up about half a pawn compared with 7...c5 because White can now expand with tempo. } 8. Bd3 e6 9. Qe2 Bb4+ 10. c3 Bd6 11. Bc2 Re8 $17 { Black keeps the extra piece and can castle by hand with ...Kg8, but there is less to show for it than in the main lines (about -1.2). }) (7... Bg4? { Pinning the knight is the most natural developing move in the world, and it is a blunder here. } 8. Ne5+! { The check comes first, with tempo. } 8... Ke8 9. Nxg4 $18 { The knight escapes the pin with check, and the bishop that was pinning it is left hanging (+3.6). The pattern to remember: with the king on f7 any pin on the f3-knight can be answered by Ne5+. }) (7... Be6 { A sound alternative to 7...e6 and 7...c5. The bishop blocks the c4 diagonal for good and heads for d5. } 8. Bd3 c5 9. dxc5 Nc6 10. O-O Bd5 $17 { Black has developed everything with tempo and the king is safe behind the pawns (about -1.6). It is still a clear edge, just slightly less than the main lines, so it is a reasonable choice if you prefer a calm game. }) 8. Bd3 $17 { The most popular try in practice: simple development, no immediate tactics. } (8. Ne5+ $17 { The point missed above: e6 stops the bishop check but not this one. Very popular in practice, and for good reason - the only safe square is narrow and two natural-looking tries fail badly. } 8... Kg8 $17 { The only move that keeps the full advantage. Simple and safe - the king has no further checks to worry about from here. } (8... Ke8? $14 { WATCH OUT: this natural-looking retreat gives back almost all of the advantage and then some. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.75) - a two-and-a-half point swing from one move.] }) (8... Ke7?? $18 { WATCH OUT: this one loses material outright, not just the advantage - the king walks into a fork. } 9. Ng6+ $18 { The point: this check forks the king and the rook on h8 - there's no way to save both. } 9... Ke8 10. Nxh8 $18 { [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+3.65) - the rook is simply gone.] }) 9. Ng6 (9. Be3 { Develop first and keep the knight jump Ng6 in reserve. } 9... Nbd7 10. Ng6 Qe8 11. Nxh8 Kxh8 12. Qe2 Nd5 13. O-O-O $17 { Material is now roughly level on paper (a rook and pawn for two knights), but Black's pieces are far more active and White's king is still in the centre, so Black keeps a clear edge (about -1.7). }) (9. Bc4 { Hitting the e6 pawn and trying to pile pressure on the king's diagonal. } 9... c5 10. Be3 cxd4 11. Bxd4 Nc6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. O-O $17 { White has castled and traded a pair of knights, but Black is still a piece for a pawn up (about -2.0) and the c6 and e6 pawns hold everything together. }) 9... c5! { The best way to meet unsound gambits is to play actively: ...c5 invites a queen exchange and challenges White's structure immediately. } { [%csl Gd1][%cal Gd7d1,Gd8d1] } (9... Qe8 { Also fine: the queen attacks the knight on g6 and protects the rook on h8 from behind the king. } 10. Nxh8 Kxh8 11. Be3 Nd5 12. Qd2 Nd7 $17 { White has won the exchange back and material is roughly level, but the black knights are well placed and White's king is stuck in the centre (about -1.9). }) 10. Be3 (10. dxc5 { White trades queens to relieve the pressure. } 10... Qxd1+ 11. Kxd1 Bxc5 12. Bd3 Nc6 13. Nxh8 Kxh8 14. f3 $17 { The white king has lost castling and Black has the bishop pair and the more active pieces (about -2.3). }) (10. Bc4 { Piling on the e6 pawn. } 10... Nc6 11. O-O Qe8 12. Nxf8 Qxf8 13. Be3 cxd4 14. Bxd4 $17 { After the dust settles Black has an extra piece for a pawn and a safe king (about -2.4). }) 10... Qa5+ 11. c3 Nc6 { Black forces White to capture and gets ahead in development. } { [%csl Ge1,Gc5][%cal Ga5e1,Gf8c5] } 12. dxc5 Bxc5 13. b4 Nxb4 14. cxb4 Bxb4+ 15. Bd2 Ne4) (8. Bc4 $17 { No longer check (the e6 pawn blocks the diagonal), just another developing try. } 8... c5 $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-1.9), clean and trap-free.] } 9. Qe2 cxd4 10. Ne5+ Kg8 11. Ng6 Qe8 12. Nxh8 Kxh8 13. O-O) (8. Be3 { A developing move that heads for long castling and keeps the Ne5+ trick up White's sleeve. } 8... Nbd7 9. Bd3 Bd6 10. Qe2 Re8 11. O-O-O b5 $17 { Black has finished development and the king can tuck into g8 whenever needed. White has pieces out but nothing to hit (about -1.3). The extra piece decides. }) 8... c5 $17 { [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-2.0), clean and trap-free - just continue developing normally.] } 9. Ne5+ { White tries the knight jump anyway, now that the bishop on d3 supports it. } 9... Kg8 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. Qe2 Nc6 12. Bf4 $17 { White's pieces look active, but Black's bishop on c5 aims at f2 and ...Qa5+ will come with tempo, so Black remains clearly better (about -2.3). }) 3... dxe4 (3... e5 4. Bd3 (4. dxe5 d4 5. Nce2 c5) 4... Nd7 5. dxe5 Nxe5 6. Bf4 Bd6) 4. f3!? { The Rasa-Studier Gambit (ECO B15): instead of recapturing the pawn immediately, White tries to blast the center open while still a pawn down. } (4. Bc4!? { The Von Hennig Gambit (ECO B15): the same pawn sacrifice, but aiming the bishop at f7/e6 instead of trying to open the center with f3. } 4... Nf6 { Simplest and best: just finish development and meet whatever comes next. } (4... Nd7?? { WATCH OUT: this natural-looking developing move walks straight into a forced mate. } 5. Qh5! { The point: nothing currently covers f7, so this threatens mate in one. } 5... g6 $14 { Forced, and just about sufficient - the queen gets chased off and Black keeps the extra pawn. [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.1), essentially level again once the trap is avoided.] } (5... Ngf6?? $18 { The losing mistake: this natural-looking developing move still does not cover f7. } 6. Qxf7# { Checkmate. })) (4... b5!? { Kicking the bishop at once is a good alternative to 4...Nf6. } 5. Bb3 Nf6 6. f3 a5 7. a3 exf3 8. Nxf3 e6 9. O-O Be7 $15 { Black keeps the extra pawn and the queenside pawns have gained space (about -0.5). }) (4... Bf5?! { Natural-looking, but it runs into 5.g4! } 5. g4 Bg6 6. Nge2 Nf6 7. h4 Nxg4 8. h5 Bf5 $10 { White gets real play for the pawn and the evaluation drops from about -0.6 to level (0.0). Playable, but worse than 4...Nf6. }) 5. Nge2 $15 { White's most natural try, developing the last minor piece before deciding what to do about the missing pawn. } (5. f3 $17 { By far White's most common try here (over ten times as popular as 5.Nge2 below) - and this is the real main line of the whole gambit complex, not 5.Nge2. } 5... exf3 $17 { Simplest and best - take the second pawn too. } 6. Nxf3 $17 { Transposing straight back into the Rasa-Studier Gambit's own main tabiya (reached there via 4.f3 exf3 5.Nxf3 Nf6 6.Bc4) - see that branch above (under 3.Nc3) for the full, fully engine-verified tree, including the 6...Bf5! recommendation and the 6...Bg4? trap. There is no need to learn this twice; it is the identical position either way. } 6... Bf5 $15 { The same improving move as in the Rasa-Studier line. Black develops the bishop and prepares ...e6, keeping the extra pawn. White's sharpest try is 7.Ne5 e6 8.g4, to which Black must reply 8...Nfd7!, while 7.Nh4 Bg4 is harmless. }) 5... Bf5 $15 { Black simply continues developing, a clean pawn up with no real weaknesses. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6), confirming White doesn't have enough for the pawn.] } (5... b5 { Kicking the bishop gains space, and it leaves the knight on e2 awkwardly placed. } 6. Bb3 b4 7. Na4 Bf5 8. c3 e6 9. O-O Nbd7 $15 { The knight on a4 is offside and Black keeps the pawn comfortably (about -0.4). })) 4... exf3 { Simplest and best: take the second pawn too, planning to give one back later for a clean structural edge rather than trying to hold onto both. } (4... Nf6 { A safe way to avoid every gambit trick: Black returns the pawn at once. } 5. Nxe4 Nbd7 6. Nxf6+ Nxf6 7. c3 Bf5 $10 { A quiet position with only a tiny Black edge (about -0.2). It works, but you give up the extra pawn for nothing, and 4...exf3 scores clearly better (about -0.6). }) 5. Nxf3 (5. Qxf3? { White retakes with the queen to develop quickly and hit the f-file, but this loses the d4 pawn. Nxf3 was the correct move, so you should be ready to punish this common mistake. } 5... Qxd4 $17 { You take the pawn and your queen lands in the centre, attacking the knight and the b2 area. Your plan is ...Nf6 and ...Bg4 to develop with tempo, and ...e6 to castle. Avoid taking pawns further and leaving your queen offside. } (5... Nf6 6. Qf2 Bg4 7. Bd2 Nbd7 8. h3 Bh5 9. Nge2 e6 10. g4 { This is a calmer path, but you are still clearly better. }) 6. Qg3 Qg4 7. Qf2 Qb4 8. Nf3 Nf6 9. Bd3 Qb6 10. Qh4 { White may instead try 6.Nb5, hoping to catch the queen out of position, but your queen simply retreats with ...Qb6 or ...Qe5 while still being a pawn up. Do not rush and keep your king safe by castling soon. After the queen trade you only need to keep developing. Overall: you are a clean pawn up and White has little compensation. This is an open game where you are a clean pawn up and White has only some activity, with your queen in the centre and both kings short of safety. Your plan: develop with ...e6, ...Be7 and ...Nbd7, challenge the queen on h4 with ...Be7 at a good moment, and castle short. White wants to bring the pieces out and attack with Ng5, Bxh7 and Nb5 against your queen. Meet Nb5 by retreating ...Qa5 or ...Qd8 quickly. The risk is being greedy: do not grab b2 or d4 with the queen, because White gets tempi against it. If White plays Nb5 first, retreat and keep your extra pawn. }) 5... Nf6 6. Bd3 (6. Bc4 $17 { By far the most heavily-played try here in practice, well ahead of 6.Bd3 above - and the exact same tabiya reached from the Von Hennig Gambit via 4.Bc4 Nf6 5.f3 exf3 6.Nxf3 (see Part Three's Von Hennig branch below, which transposes straight back here). } 6... Bf5! $17 { Black's best and most natural try, developing actively before White can castle. [Engine verdict: a clear Black edge (-0.6 to -0.7), confirming White still has nothing for the pawn even in this, White's most popular try.] } (6... e6 $15 { Solid but noticeably less ambitious than 6...Bf5 - safe, but gives back a good chunk of Black's edge for no real reason. [Engine verdict: only a small Black edge (-0.2), well below 6...Bf5's -0.6 to -0.7.] }) (6... Bg4? $16 { WATCH OUT: this looks like the same natural developing move as in the 6.Bd3 line above, but here it runs into a real tactic - a very common practical mistake. } 7. Bxf7+! { The point: this sacrifices the bishop back, but opens the king up for a second strike. } 7... Kxf7 { Forced - there's nothing better. } 8. Ne5+! $16 { The only move that actually cashes in the sacrifice - White's other tries here (8.h3 or 8.Bd2, say) just hand the whole point straight back and leave White clearly worse instead. [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+1.7 to +1.9) with 8.Ne5+ found - this is the real reason 6...Bg4 is a mistake, not the sacrifice by itself.] }) 7. Ne5 { The most dangerous try: the knight hits f7 and the bishop on f5 loses its retreat square. } (7. Nh4 { A less dangerous alternative that also hits the bishop immediately. } 7... Bg4 8. Qd3 Bh5 9. O-O e6 10. h3 Be7 $15 { The bishop is safe on h5 and the extra pawn is intact; White has not found a good way to use the lead in development (about -0.7). }) 7... e6 8. g4 { White now attacks the bishop with the g-pawn. } 8... Nfd7! { The only good reply: Black challenges the knight and keeps the bishop. } (8... b5? { Hitting the bishop is natural, but it walks into trouble. } 9. gxf5 bxc4 10. fxe6 fxe6 11. Bd2 $16 { Black has won a bishop back but the king is exposed on the open f-file and White's pieces flood forward (about +1.2). }) 9. gxf5 Nxe5 10. Be2 Qh4+ 11. Kf1 Qh3+ 12. Ke1 Be7 $15 { White has won a bishop for the pawn but the white king is stuck in the middle and Black keeps the initiative (about -0.8). }) (6. Bf4 { A developing move that aims at c7 and prepares Qd2 and long castling. } 6... Bg4 7. Bd3 e6 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Qxd4 10. O-O-O $15 { Black can take a second pawn, but White's lead in development means it must be done accurately (about -0.7). The safe approach is simply to keep the structure intact with ...Nbd7 and ...Be7. }) 6... Bg4 $15 { Black is just a clean pawn up, with a safe and comfortable position and no weaknesses to show for it. [Engine verdict: a modest Black edge (-0.6), confirming the Rasa-Studier Gambit doesn't generate real compensation either.] } 7. Be3 e6 8. h3 Bh5 9. Qe2 Bb4 10. a3 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 $15 { Black keeps the extra pawn and has traded off White's best attacking piece. The plan from here is simple: ...Nbd7, ...Qc7 and castle (about -0.7). } *


