1. e4 c6 $14 { With the Caro-Kann, Black foregoes central control in favour of more manageable growth. The queen's bishop may still move freely, unlike the French position, but the knight can no longer occupy the c6 square. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 2. Nc3 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 2... d5 $14 { This could either transpose to the Exchange Variation, but White usually plays Nf3 here, leading to the Two Knights Attack. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nf3 $14 { These are the moves that characterise the Two Knights Attack. See the Two Knights Attack chapters (Chapters #22-#27) for the full theory tree this game grows from. } 3... Bg4 { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gc8g4] } 4. Be2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gf1e2] } 4... Bxf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg4f3] } 5. Bxf3 { WATCH OUT: exd5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -5.4 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxg2 Rg1 Bxd5 Nxd5 cxd5 c4 } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Ge2f3] } 5... e6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 6. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 6... Ne7 $14 { White is pressing hard on d5, so Black plays Ne7 before c5. Black's plan is Nd7 and then Nf6, Qb6. } { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gg8e7] } 7. Bg5 $14 { Black's original idea of c5 followed by Nc6 is interrupted by the pin via Bg5, removing a potential defender of d5. } { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gc3d5,Ge7d5,Gf3d5,Gg5e7,Gc1g5,Gd7d5] } 7... h6 $14 { Black wants to trade off White's dark-squared bishop, but White keeps pinning the e7-knight instead. } { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gh7h6] } 8. Bh4 { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gg5h4] } 8... Qb6 $14 { This threatens ...Qxb3 and supports the c5 break. } { [%csl Rb1,Gb6,Rb3][%cal Gd8b6,Rb2b3,Ra1b1] } 9. b3 $17 { White defends b3 rather than accept structural damage after ...Qxb3. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gb2b3] } 9... Ng6 $17 { The key resource: Ng6 creates a relative fork on White's dark-squared bishop and, via ...Bb4, on the pinned Nc3. } { [%csl Gg6][%cal Ge7g6,Gf8b4,Gg6h4] } 10. Bg3 { [%csl Gg3][%cal Gh4g3] } 10... Bb4 $17 { Black pins the knight to the White king, and now White must find a precise way to untangle - this is the exact moment where the real recorded game (tedgecco 1191 - AndyPridmore 1224, Live Chess, Chess.com, 2022.09.22) continued 21.O-O??, walking straight into 21...Bxc3, a 3.7-pawn swing that hands Black a piece for nothing and effectively decides the game in one move. That is not a useful "Do" to copy, so from here this chapter switches to Stockfish's own top defensive try instead, to show what actually happens when White defends as accurately as possible. } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gf8b4,Gb4c3] } 11. Qd3 $17 { 11.Qd3! is the engine's top choice and the correct practical try - unlike 11.O-O??, it does not hang anything on the spot. The point is subtle: the queen adds a second defender along the d-file/e4 diagonal and prepares Kd2, so that if Black ever plays ...Bxc3, White can recapture with the queen rather than being forced into a losing pin-exploitation. Even so, watch the evaluation: Black is already close to two pawns better here with only correct play left for White - the damage from the earlier pin (10...Bb4) is already done, and no single 11th move fully repairs it.   WATCH OUT: exd5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -5.9 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxc3+ Kf1 cxd5 Rc1 Nc6 Bh5 } { [%csl Rc3,Rd5][%cal Bd1d3] } 11... Qa5 $19 { Black keeps the queen active on the a5-e1 diagonal, maintaining pressure on the pinned knight and eyeing the long-term weakness of White's king, which can no longer safely castle short without walking into similar tactics. } { [%cal Ga5e1,Ga5a2] } 12. Kd2 $19 { 12.Kd2 unpins the knight by hand since castling is no longer safe (12.O-O still runs into 12...Bxc3 13.Qxc3 Qxc3 followed by Rb1 lines with an extra pawn for Black). This is the critical weakness this whole line stems from: White's king has no completely safe home for the rest of the game.   WATCH OUT: exd5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.4 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxc3+ Ke2 cxd5 b4 Bxb4 c3 } { [%csl Yd1,Ye1][%cal Bd2d1] } 12... dxe4 { [%cal Gd5e4] } 13. Bxe4 { WATCH OUT: Qxe4 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -6.3 from White's own side of the board), after: Bxc3+ Kd3 Bxa1 Rxa1 O-O Qe1 } { [%cal Rf3f5] } 13... f5 $19 { Black opens the position further while the White king sits awkwardly on d2, and prepares to castle into safety - note that this is now completely sound for Black, since the kingside pawn structure (g7, h6) has no real weaknesses that White's remaining bishop can exploit. } 14. Bf3 { [%cal Bf3h5] } 14... O-O $19 { Black tucks the king away first. Compare this with the real game's 21.O-O??, which was fatal for White in the exact same structural sense that this move is completely safe for Black: king safety only matters once the position has actually been made safe for it, and here it has. } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 15. a3 { [%cal Ya3b4] } 15... Bxc3+ $19 { Cashing in the long-standing pin from 10...Bb4 for the bishop pair and a permanently damaged White queenside structure (doubled a- and c-pawns after the coming exchanges) - this is the actual point of Black's whole plan starting from move 6, realized cleanly rather than via a one-move gift.   WATCH OUT: Bxa3 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd6 Qxc3+ Qxc3 Bxd6 Rhe1 Rf6 } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb4c3] } 16. Qxc3 { [%cal Bc3b4] } 16... Qxc3+ { [%cal Bb4c3] } 17. Kxc3 $19 { With queens off, White's king is stuck in the center on a permanently exposed square - this is the critical weakness Black's whole plan (pin, trade the pin off favorably, keep the White king from ever finding safety) was designed to create. } { [%csl Yd3,Yc3][%cal Yd3c3] } 17... f4 { [%cal Rf5f4] } 18. Bh5 { [%cal Bf3h5] } 18... Ne7 { [%cal Ge7d5,Ge7f5] } 19. Bg4 { [%cal Bh5g4] } 19... fxg3 $19 { Trading the last minor piece off wins back the structurally weak dark-squared bishop and leaves White with permanently isolated, doubled g-pawns after the recapture. } { [%csl Rg3][%cal Rf4g3] } 20. Bxe6+ $19 { White grabs a pawn back with check rather than simply being a piece down, since the earlier trades were roughly balanced - but this does not solve the king safety problem. } { [%cal Rg4e6] } 20... Kh8 { [%cal Gg8h8] } 21. fxg3 { [%cal Gg2g3] } 21... Nd5+ $19 { A further check, forcing the king to stay in the center. } { [%csl Rd3][%cal Rf6d5] } 22. Kd3 { [%cal Rd3d3] } 22... Nf6 $19 { Compare the resulting position with the real game's actual 21.O-O??: instead of a single blunder deciding everything on the spot, Black has earned a clean, lasting, and fully explainable advantage through correct technique alone - an extra pawn, a much safer king, White's remaining bishop offside on e6, and White's king still stuck in the center facing further harassment (...Rf6-d5+/...Rf8 ideas next). The conversion from here (trade down, activate the rooks, target the exposed king and the weak queenside pawns) is standard technique and is the same plan taught throughout this course's Two Knights Attack chapters. } { [%csl Rd3,Re6][%cal Bf6d5] } 23. Rhe1 Na6 24. Re5 Rad8 25. Rae1 Nd7 26. Bxd7 Rxd7 27. Re7 Rfd8 $17 { You are a knight up for two pawns, so your plan is to bring the last knight into play with ...Na6 and ...Rad8 and then to trade pieces. White's rooks pile up on the e-file and the bishop on e6 hits your knight, so you offer ...Nd7 to swap it and keep control of d7 with the rook. Once the rooks come off, the extra piece will tell (about -2.2). } *


