ck a club player would consider), but it's not just worse, it's losing outright for Black: after Qxh7#, White forces mate. WATCH OUT: Nxf4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it's not just worse, it's losing outright for Black: after Qxh7#, White forces mate. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gd7f6] [%clk 0:26:41] } 18. g3 { [%csl Gg3][%cal Gg2g3] [%clk 0:11:08] } 18... Nb4 { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gd5b4] [%clk 0:26:42] } 19. Qb3 { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gd3b3] [%clk 0:10:50] } 19... Rfd8 $15 { At the time: h4, h6 | Nf3, Ne4 is awkward | Rd1, Nf2 Bb4, b6 | Bxe6 | fxe6, Nxe6 wins; Bb4, h6 | Nf3, Ne4 | Re2 holds everything together. } { [%csl Gc4,Gd8][%cal Gf8d8] [%clk 0:26:11] } 20. Bc4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Ge2c4] [%clk 0:07:39] } 20... h6 { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gh7h6] [%clk 0:25:24] } 21. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg5f3] [%clk 0:07:31] } 21... b5 $14 { Retreating the bishop here would run into a crushing attack. } { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gb7b5] [%clk 0:24:14] } 22. cxb6 { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gc5b6] [%clk 0:07:11] } 22... Qxb6 { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gc7b6] [%clk 0:24:13] } 23. a3 { [%csl Ga3][%cal Ga2a3] [%clk 0:06:56] } 23... Nbd5 $14 { A lot of captures to calculate here: Bxd5, Nxd5 | Qxb6, Nxb6 | Rc2 goes after the weak queenside pawns; Qxb6, Nxb6 | Ba2 looks like a worse version of that line, as Black's knights support each other. The computer rates both as slightly better for the side to move. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gb4d5] [%clk 0:23:24] } 24. Bxd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc4d5] [%clk 0:05:50] } 24... Nxd5 { WATCH OUT: Rxd5 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.4 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qxb6 Rdd8 Rc2 Rac8 Re1 Nd5 WATCH OUT: exd5 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qxb6 R