3... Nd5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gf6d5] [%clk 0:24:11] } 44. g5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gg4g5] [%clk 0:01:19] } 44... f6+ { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gf7f6] [%clk 0:23:24] } 45. gxf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg5f6] [%clk 0:01:31] } 45... gxf6+ { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg7f6] [%clk 0:23:44] } 46. Ke4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge5e4] [%clk 0:01:45] } 46... Kd6 $15 { Engine honesty check: ...Kd6 here lets White's advantage nearly evaporate for a moment (this is deep in a long endgame, move 46, well past anything opening-related) - Black still goes on to win, but the objectively cleaner try was to keep the king further back. Included as-is because the opening and middlegame plan earlier in this game are exactly what this chapter is here to teach. } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Ge7d6] [%clk 0:23:47] } 47. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gd4f3] [%clk 0:01:24] } 47... f5+ { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf6f5] [%clk 0:23:57] } 48. Kd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Ge4d3] [%clk 0:01:34] } 48... Nxf4+ { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gd5f4] [%clk 0:24:15] } 49. Ke3 { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gd3e3] [%clk 0:01:53] } 49... Nxh3 $19 { Online classical game, 0-1 } { [%csl Gh3][%cal Gf4h3] } 50. Nd2 e5 51. Nc4+ Kd5 52. Nb6+ Ke6 53. Kf3 Nf4 54. Kg3 Ng6 $19 { White has only a knight against your knight and three pawns, so this is a matter of technique. The knight checks on c4 and b6 only chase your king around, so you answer ...e5 and bring the king back to support the pawns, then reroute the knight to g6 to cover the e5 and f4 squares. You win by pushing the central pawns with the king and knight in front of them; just watch out for forks (about -6.3). } 0-1


