1. e4 $14 { BRIDGE: this plan grows from the Advance Variation, Tal Variation (4.h4 h5). See Chapter #20 (Advance Variation, Tal Variation) for the full opening theory behind it. } 1... c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. h4 $14 { The exact Tal Variation tabiya covered in Chapter #20 (Advance Variation, Tal Variation): White commits to h4-h5 before Black can play the move first. } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gh2h4] } 4... h5 5. Bd3 Bxd3 { WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.5 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 Bxf5 e6 Bd3 Qxd4 } 6. Qxd3 Qa5+ 7. b4 Qa6 $14 { White has already sacrificed material for activity and central play - engines rate this as objectively sound but demanding for both sides. Note the structure: once queens come off, Black's job is to prove the extra pawn matters more than White's temporary activity, exactly the theme this course's Advance Variation chapters stress. } 8. e6 Qxd3 { WATCH OUT: fxe6 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -3.0 from Black's own side of the board), after: Qg6+ Kd8 Nf3 Nf6 Ne5 Kc8 } 9. cxd3 fxe6 10. Nd2 Nh6 11. Ndf3 Nf5 12. Ne2 g6 13. Nf4 a5 14. Nxe6 $10 { Black's typical trump in this whole system: even after heavy simplification, the knight (or bishop, depending on the exact move order) finds a great outpost on f5, and White's structure is the one with long-term problems. Compare this with the general Advance Variation structural themes covered in the Key Structures Master Guide. } { [%csl Gf5] } 14... Kd7 15. Nxf8+ Rxf8 16. bxa5 Rxa5 17. O-O Ra4 18. Ne5+ Kc8 19. Nxg6 Rg8 { WATCH OUT: Rxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxf8 Nxh4 f3 Na6 Bg5 Nc5 } 20. Nf4 Nxd4 21. f3 Nb3 22. axb3 Rxa1 23. Nxh5 Na6 24. d4 Nb4 25. Nf4 Nc2 26. Ne2 Rb1 27. h5 Rxb3 28. Kf2 c5 29. Nf4 Rh8 30. dxc5 e5 31. Nxd5 Rxh5 32. Nf6 Rh8 33. g4 Nd4 34. Kg2 Rc3 35. Ne4 Rc2+ 36. Bd2 Nb3 37. Rf2 Nxc5 38. Bc3 Rxf2+ 39. Nxf2 Re8 40. Kg3 Kd7 41. g5 Ke6 42. f4 exf4+ 43. Kg4 Rc8 44. Bd4 f3 45. Kxf3 Kf5 46. Be3 b5 47. Bd2 Rd8 48. Bc3 Ne6 49. g6 Kxg6 50. Ke4 Nc5+ 51. Ke3 Kf5 52. Bb4 Ne6 53. Nd3 Rg8 54. Bd6 Rh8 55. Bb4 Rh3+ 56. Kd2 Rf3 57. Ne1 Rh3 58. Nd3 Nf4 59. Nc5 Rh2+ $19 { A long grind, but instructive precisely because of that: Black never needed a single tactical shot to win - just steady technique converting a small, stable structural edge into a full point in a pure minor-piece endgame. This is the realistic, patient side of playing the Caro-Kann for a full point, not just memorized forcing lines. } 60. Kd1 Nd5 61. Bd2 Ke5 62. Kc2 Nb6 63. Kc3 Kd5 64. Nb3 Rh4 $17 { You have a rook and a knight against two minor pieces, so use the king. After Kd1 you bring the knight to the centre with ...Nd5 and the king up with ...Ke5 and ...Kd5, then reroute with ...Nb6 and ...Rh4 to attack the bishop on d2. Avoid cheap checks that drive your king back. Your passed b-pawn and the extra material mean this is a technical win (about -4.2). } 0-1


