TURNING POINTS (engine, depth 15 to 17; take or develop, and what to avoid): 24...Nf4?! by Black: +1.3 before, +2.1 after; the capture axb5 was stronger (+1.1). }
1. e4 $14 { BRIDGE: this plan grows from the Advance Variation, Bayonet Attack - reached here via the 4.h4 move order (4.h4 h6 5.g4, rather than the immediate 4.g4) - viewed from White's side of the board. See Chapter #20 (Advance Variation, Bayonet Attack) for the full opening theory behind it. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 $14 { Leko is not averse to repeating the main lines of the Caro-Kann. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 $14 { By contrast, Kramnik must seek sharper paths. This move, favoured by Nimzowitsch, Tal and Short, is ideal for a must-win situation, since it locks pawn formations and avoids premature exchanges. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 3... Bf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 4. h4 $14 { Nimzowitsch liked this 3 e5 variation and would play here 4 Bd3. His most famous game with this line is sadly a loss, a magnificent manoeuvring game against Capablanca from New York 1927. The most common alternative these days is 4 Nc3, as played for example by Kasparov in a drastic win over Karpov at Linares 2001, which continued 4...e6 5 g4 Bg6 6 Nge2 Ne7 7 Nf4 c5 8 dxc5 Nd7 9 h4 Nxe5 10 Bg2 h5 11 Qe2 N7c6 12 Nxg6 Nxg6 13 Bg5 Be7 14 gxh5 Nf8 15 Nb5 Nd7 16 h6. The latest try for Black is 4 Nc3 a5!? as in Sebag-Chiburdanidze, FIDE WCh Women KO, Elista 2004. Black won in 33 moves after 5 Be3 a4 6 a3 Qb6 7 Rb1 Qa5 8 g4 Bd7 9 Bg2 e6 10 Nge2 c5 11 0�0 Nc6 12 f4 h5. This 4 h4 line was tested repeatedly in the Tal-Botvinnik WCC match, Moscow 1961, with generally favourable results for Black. The paths they followed are retraced below. } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gh2h4] } 4... h6 $14 { 4...h5 may be preferable, after which Tal tried 5 Ne2 e6 6 Ng3 g6 7 Nxf5 gxf5 8 c4 c5 9 cxd5 Qxd5 but only drew their 14th game from 1961. This is exactly right, and it is why the Tal Variation (Chapter #20) teaches 4...h5 as the main line there. This game is included deliberately as a 'don't': watch how the older 4...h6 lets White transpose straight into the Bayonet Attack with a further tempo (5.g4), gaining a freer hand for the coming kingside expansion. } { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gh7h6] } 5. g4 $14 { Instead 5 Ne2 is possible and in their 20 th game 5 e6 6 Ng3 Ne7 7 Nc3 Nd7 8 Be3 Bh7 9 Bd3 Bxd3 10 cxd3 h5 led to a marathon struggle and another draw. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gg2g4] } 5... Bd7 $14 { It looks more natural to retreat the bishop along the b1-h7 diagonal, but, in that case, Black has to reckon with the dangerous pawn sac e5-e6.   WATCH OUT: Qa5+ looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -5.2 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd2 Qb6 gxf5 Qxd4 Nf3 Qg4 } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gf5d7] } 6. Nd2 $14 { This looks like a novelty. In the 10th game of the 1961 Tal-Botvinnik match, Black won in 42 moves after 6.h5 c5 7.c3 Nc6 8.Bh3 e6 9.Be3 Qb6 10.Qb3 cxd4 11.Qxb6 axb6 12.cxd4 Na5. In the 18th game 6.c3 c5 7.Bg2 e6 8.Ne2 Bb5 9.Na3 Bxe2 10.Qxe2 cxd4 11.cxd4 Bxa3 12.bxa3 Nc6 also ended in a Black win. Tal kept faith with the line and finally scored in Tal-Pachman, Bled 1961, with an attack beginning 7...e6 8.f4 Qb6 9.Nf3 Nc6 10.Na3 cxd4 11.cxd4 O-O-O 12.Nc2 Kb8 13.Bd3 Nge7 14.Rb1 Na5 15.Bd2 Rc8 16.b4. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } 6... c5 $14 { Black has one way to free himself before White's pawns begin to exert a fatal grip. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc6c5] } 7. dxc5 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gd4c5] } 7... e6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 8. Nb3 { [%csl Gc5,Gb3][%cal Gd2b3,Gb3c5] } 8... Bxc5 $14 { A small combination that regains his pawn, but at the cost of trading his dark squared bishop. 9...Qc7 would doubtless transpose, but this is more forcing. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gf8c5] } 9. Nxc5 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gb3c5] } 9... Qa5+ { [%csl Ga5][%cal Gd8a5] } 10. c3 { [%csl Gc2,Gc3] } 10... Qxc5 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Ga5c5] } 11. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 11... Ne7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gg8e7] } 12. Bd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 12... Nbc6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 13. Be3 { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gc1e3] } 13... Qa5 { [%csl Ga5][%cal Gc5a5] } 14. Qd2 $14 { The tone of play for the middlegame is set -- Black's position is resilient, but White has a promising future on the central dark squares. Fritz now wanted to try the line-opening gambit 14...d4!?, but no human player would yet regard such drastic measures as necessary. (It's a mark of how far computer programs have advanced that Fritz makes this dynamic choice.). } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gd1d2] } 14... Ng6 { [%csl Gg6][%cal Ge7g6] } 15. Bd4 $14 { Accepting some exchanges, but White's dark square grip persists. In contrast, 15 Bxg6 gives black too much counterplay along the half-open f-file. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge3d4] } 15... Nxd4 { WATCH OUT: Ngxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.1 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxe5 Nxe5 Bxe5 f6 Bg6+ Ke7 WATCH OUT: Ncxe5 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.4 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxe5 Nxe5 Bxe5 f6 Bg6+ Ke7 } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc6d4] } 16. cxd4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc3d4] } 16... Qxd2+ { [%csl Gd2][%cal Ga5d2] } 17. Kxd2 { [%csl Gd2][%cal Ge1d2] } 17... Nf4 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gg6f4] } 18. Rac1 $14 { From here White's play is direct and ruthless. Bf1 followed by Ke3 would have been more restrained, but Kramnik goes straight for the kill. } { [%csl Gc1][%cal Ga1c1] } 18... h5 $16 { An alternative here was to leave Black's kingside pawns alone and trade on d3. After 18...Nxd3 19.Kxd3 Ke7 20.Rc7 Rab8 and ...Rhc8, Black can probably hold, though White's knight will always be better than Black's bishop. } { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gh6h5] } 19. Rhg1 $14 { 19 g5 is possible instead, but Kramnik is all for open lines. } { [%csl Gg1][%cal Gh1g1] } 19... Bc6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gd7c6] } 20. gxh5 { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gg4h5] } 20... Nxh5 { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gf4h5] } 21. b4 { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gb2b4] } 21... a6 { [%csl Ga6][%cal Ga7a6] } 22. a4 $14 { An energetic move, but after 22...Bxa4 23.Rc7 Bb5 24.Rxb7 O-O Black reduces the tension and gains counterplay; taking the pawn could even be risky for White after 25.Bxb5 axb5 26.Rxb5 Ra2+. For that reason 22.Ke3, restricting Black's knight, deserves consideration: if 22...O-O then 23.Rg5 g6 24.Rcg1 Ng7 25.h5!, or if 24...Kh8 25.Bxg6! fxg6 26.Rxg6 Rf7 27.Ng5 and White wins. } { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga2a4] } 22... Kd8 $16 { ? Leko exhibits an alarming tendency towards a bunker mentality. After this passive move, Black is strangled in the style of his hero Petrosian or of Nimzowitsch, the arch-blockader and early prophet of 3 e5 against the Caro-Kann. } { [%csl Gd8][%cal Ge8d8] } 23. Ng5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gf3g5] } 23... Be8 { WATCH OUT: Bxa4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.3 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxf7+ Kd7 Nxh8 Rxh8 Ke3 Kd8 } { [%csl Ge8][%cal Gc6e8] } 24. b5 { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gb4b5] } 24... Nf4 $18 { Clearing the a-file merely offers White a further avenue of attack after 24...axb5 25 axb5 Ra3 26 Ra1! } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gh5f4] } 25. b6 $18 { Creating a terrible outpost on c7 for his rook. Black must prevent such an invasion at all costs. } { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gb5b6] } 25... Nxd3 { WATCH OUT: Bxa4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.6 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxf7+ Ke8 Nxh8 Nxd3 Kxd3 Kf8 } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf4d3] } 26. Kxd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd2d3] } 26... Rc8 { WATCH OUT: Bxa4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -6.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: Nxf7+ Ke7 Rc7+ Bd7 Nxh8 Rg8 } { [%csl Gc8][%cal Ga8c8] } 27. Rxc8+ { [%csl Gc8][%cal Gc1c8] } 27... Kxc8 { [%csl Gc8][%cal Gd8c8] } 28. Rc1+ { [%csl Gc1][%cal Gg1c1] } 28... Bc6 $18 { Black has plugged one path for White's forces, but more soon open: Black is badly weakened on the central dark squares. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Ge8c6] } 29. Nxf7 { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gg5f7] } 29... Rxh4 { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gh8h4] } 30. Nd6+ { [%csl Gd6,Gd5][%cal Gf7d6] } 30... Kd8 { [%csl Gd8][%cal Gc8d8] } 31. Rg1 { [%csl Gg1][%cal Gc1g1] } 31... Rh3+ $18 { Passive defence is hopeless, so Black tries to clear off as many white pawns as he can. } { [%csl Gh3][%cal Gh4h3] } 32. Ke2 { [%csl Ge2][%cal Gd3e2] } 32... Ra3 { [%csl Ga3][%cal Gh3a3] } 33. Rxg7 { [%csl Gg7][%cal Gg1g7] } 33... Rxa4 { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga3a4] } 34. f4 $18 { !! The decisive coup. White's small but highly efficient army is ready to concentrate its powers and deal the death blow. Had Black tried 34...Rxd4, White would continue 35.f5 exf5 and then 36.e6 Re4+ 37.Nxe4 fxe4 38.Rc7 with the threat of Rxc6; and after 37...Bb5+ comes 38.Ke3, when Black's b-pawn is doomed and White's king penetrates. } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gf2f4] } 34... Ra2+ { [%csl Ga2][%cal Ga4a2] } 35. Kf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Ge2f3] } 35... Ra3+ { [%csl Ga3] } 36. Kg4 { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gf3g4] } 36... Rd3 { [%csl Gd4,Gd3][%cal Ga3d3] } 37. f5 $18 { The same theme. White's king storming the Black fortress with just rook and knight against rook and bishop recalls the king march in game 5 of the 1963 Petrosian-Botvinnik match in Moscow. } { [%csl Gf5,Be8][%cal Be5e8,Gf4f5] } 37... Rxd4+ { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd3d4] } 38. Kg5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gg4g5] } 38... exf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Ge6f5] } 39. Kf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg5f6] } 39... Rg4 { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gd4g4] } 40. Rc7 { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gg7c7] } 40... Rh4 { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gg4h4] } 41. Nf7+ { 1-0, Vladimir Kramnik - Peter Leko, Kramnik - Leko World Championship Match, Brissago SUI (14), 2004.10.18 } { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gd6f7] } 1-0


