1. e4 $14 { BRIDGE: this plan grows from the Advance Variation, Short Variation main line. See Chapter #15 and Chapter #19 (4.Nd2 (So)/4.c4 (Carlsen) Sidelines) for the full opening theory behind it. } 1... c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 $14 { The exact main-line tabiya from Chapter #15: 4.Nf3 e6 5.Be2, the course's recommended Short Variation move order. } { [%cal Ge2d3] } 4... e6 5. Be2 Nd7 6. O-O a5 $14 { The early ...a5-a4 idea gains space on the queenside and gives Black's knight a route to b4 or c5 later - a useful practical resource on top of the pure theory in Chapter #15. } { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga7a5] } 7. Nc3 a4 8. Bd2 a3 9. b3 h6 10. Rc1 Ne7 11. h3 Bh7 12. Bd3 Bxd3 13. cxd3 Qb6 14. Be3 Ng6 15. Nh2 Be7 16. f4 Nh4 17. Nf3 Nxf3+ 18. Qxf3 g6 19. Rc2 h5 20. g3 Nb8 21. g4 Na6 22. f5 hxg4 $17 { Once the position opens up on the kingside, Black's rook lift down the h-file becomes a real attacking asset - a nice reminder that the Advance Variation's structure isn't purely about slow maneuvering. } { [%csl Gh3] } 23. hxg4 Kd7 24. f6 Bb4 25. Na4 Qb5 26. Bc1 Qa5 27. Kg2 Rh7 28. Be3 Rah8 29. Bg1 Qd8 30. Nc5+ Bxc5 31. dxc5 Nb4 32. Re2 Qf8 33. d4 Qh6 34. Qc3 Qg5 35. Kf2 Rh3 $19 { Vincent Keymer, one of the strongest young grandmasters in the world, converts cleanly here - a genuinely elite-level demonstration of the exact system taught in Chapter #15, not just a club-level illustration of the idea. } 36. Re3 Rxe3 37. Qxe3 Qxg4 38. Qf3 Qxd4+ 39. Kg2 Qxe5 40. Qf4 Qxf4 $19 { The other candidate moves all run into a forced mate, so White gives up the exchange of rooks and hopes to hold the draw. But the king on f2 has no cover: ...Qxg4 and ...Qxd4+ win the pawns in front of it and the e5 pawn falls as well. Black is winning (about -5.2). } 0-1


