1. e4 $14 { BRIDGE: this plan grows from the Advance Variation, Short Variation main line, viewed from White's side of the board. See Chapter #15 and Chapter #19 (4.Nd2 (So)/4.c4 (Carlsen) Sidelines) for the full opening theory behind it. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 3... Bf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 4. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 4... e6 { [%csl Ge6,Ge7] } 5. c3 $14 { Two ideas here: prevent the bishop check on b4 at once with c3, or wait for the check and then play c4, gaining a tempo. A Caro-Kann aim for Black is ...c5, so White should develop pieces so as to prevent it. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc2c3] } 5... h6 { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gh7h6] } 6. Nbd2 $10 { This is a developing move, but it has a drawback: it prevents Bd3 supported by the queen. White would like to trade for the bishop on f5, which is an annoying piece. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } 6... Be7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gf8e7] } 7. Nb3 $14 { This accomplishes two things, it allows us to play Be3, and it guards c5. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gd2b3] } 7... f6? $18 { It is a fundamental not to play ...f6; here it creates a hole on g6 that White can exploit.
In the French Defence ...f6 can undermine White's central control, but the Caro-Kann differs: Black lacks the tempi and resources, because the light-squared bishop is already on f5. The result is multiple weaknesses and holes on the e7-h5 diagonal, a lasting headache for Black. } { [%csl Gf6,Gh5,Gg6,Gf7,Ge8][%cal Gf7f6,Ge8h5] } 8. Bd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 8... Bxd3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf5d3] } 9. Qxd3 $16 { White trades off Black's annoying bishop and puts pressure on g6. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd1d3] } 9... f5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf6f5] } 10. g4 $18 { Black's pawn blocked the view of g6, so White tries to deflect it. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gg2g4] } 10... g6 { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gg7g6] } 11. Be3 { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gc1e3] } 11... Nd7 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 12. Rg1 { [%csl Gg1][%cal Gh1g1] } 12... Nb6 { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gd7b6] } 13. Nbd2 $16 { The knight needs to be directed to guard c4. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb3d2] } 13... Qd7 $18 { Black is realizing that he has nothing on the Kingside so he is preparing to Castle Queenside. } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gd8d7] } 14. a4 { [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga2a4] } 14... a5 { [%csl Ga5][%cal Ga7a5] } 15. h4 { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gh2h4] } 15... g5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gg6g5] } 16. gxf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gg4f5] } 16... exf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Ge6f5] } 17. hxg5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gh4g5] } 17... hxg5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gh6g5] } 18. Nxg5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gf3g5] } 18... Bxg5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Ge7g5] } 19. Bxg5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Ge3g5] } 19... Nc4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gb6c4] } 20. Nxc4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gd2c4] } 20... dxc4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gd5c4] } 21. Qxc4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gd3c4] } 21... Ne7 $18 { A high-level trade opportunity: Black's knight is heading to d5, where it will be very powerful, while White's bishop is restricted by its own pawns, so trading the bad bishop for that knight is sensible. } { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gg8e7] } 22. Bxe7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gg5e7] } 22... Qxe7 $18 { Another opportunity to line up a rook with the queen and king. } { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gd7e7] } 23. O-O-O { [%csl Gc1][%cal Ge1c1] } 23... Rh2 { [%csl Gh2][%cal Gh8h2] } 24. Rg8+ $18 { 1-0, online game, Chess.com, 2022.06.21 } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Gg1g8] } 1-0


