1. e4 $14 { BRIDGE: this plan grows from the Classical Variation, Gurgenidze (Modern) Variation, viewed from White's side of the board. See Chapter #14 for the full opening theory behind it. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. Nd2 $14 { B12 Caro-Kann Defense: Modern Variation } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } 3... Nf6?! $16 { Inaccuracy: ...dxe4 was best. Black normally transposes into the main line here, and leaving the pawn on e4 gains nothing; the masters' database agrees, with 4.e5 the only move played. This is the point of this 'don't' example: the repertoire always meets 3.Nc3/3.Nd2 with 3...dxe4, transposing to the Classical Variation lines in Chapters #9-#14. The 3...Nf6 shown here abandons that plan and hands White a space advantage for free. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } (3... dxe4 $14 { dxe4 leads to a small White edge (+0.3): Black has a material edge of about 1 point(s); Black has doubled pawns. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd5e4] } 4. Nxe4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Bd2e4] } 4... Bf5 { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 5. Ng3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Be4g3] } 5... Bg6 { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bf5g6] } 6. h4 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 6... h6 { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bh7h6] } 7. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3][%cal Bg1f3] } 7... Nd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 8. Bd3 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3).] [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 8... Bxd3 { White's h4 and Bd3 work together: the point is Bxg6, after which ...fxg6 or ...hxg6 damages your pawns, so you take the bishop first. } (8... e6?! 9. Bxg6 fxg6 10. Qd3 Kf7 { lets White take on g6 with tempo, wrecking your pawns and dragging your king out (about +2.0). }) 9. Qxd3 e6 10. Bf4 { Bf4 eyes c7 and d6, so you cannot easily develop a bishop to d6. } 10... Qa5+ { A free tempo that also takes the queen out of the Bf4 line. } 11. Bd2 Qc7 12. O-O-O Ngf6 13. Ne4 $10 { You have a sound structure and White's edge is tiny (about +0.2). Next you complete development and castle on whichever side White's attack is not aimed at. }) 4. e5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 4... Nfd7 $16 { Here in the master's database there is only the move Bd3 and Black won. } { [%csl Gd7,Rf4,Rd3][%cal Rf2f4,Rf1d3,Gf6d7] } 5. f4?! $14 { Inaccuracy. Bd3 was best. The move is thematic: the centre is heading toward closure and the pawn chains point in opposite directions, so White's chain runs c3-e5 with the break f5. Instead of developing the knight and then having to move it, 5.f4 sets up the battle lines immediately. It is not flexible, but nothing is inherently wrong with it. } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gf2f4] } (5. Bd3 $16 { Bd3 leads to a clear White edge (+1.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bf1d3] } 5... e6 { [%csl Be6][%cal Be7e6] } 6. c3 { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bc2c3] } 6... c5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bc6c5] } 7. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg1e2] } 7... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 8. O-O { [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 8... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 9. Nf3 { [%csl Bf3,Bd4][%cal Bc3d4,Bd2f3,Bf3d4,Bf2d4] } 9... a5 $16 { [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+1.3).] [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 10. Nf4 { White's plan is a kingside space grab: the knight heads for h5 while h4 follows, and the e5/d4 chain keeps your pieces cramped. } 10... h6 11. Be3 a4 12. h4 Bxh4 { Taking the pawn is playable, but White gets time for Nh5 and Nf4 to harass the kingside. } 13. Nh5 g6 14. Nf4 Nf8 $16 { White stays clearly better (about +1.3). Your counterplay is ...a4 for queenside space, ...c4 to blunt the Bd3, and ...f6 to undermine e5, not pawn grabbing. }) 5... e6 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 6. Ngf3 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 6... c5 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc6c5] } 7. c3 { [%csl Gc3] } 7... b6?! $16 { Inaccuracy. Nc6 was best. Black probably wanted to trade light-squared bishops, marginally improving his own pieces while lowering the quality of White's. } { [%csl Ra4,Rh4,Ga6,Gf1][%cal Rd1a4,Rh2h4,Gc8a6] } (7... Nc6 $14 { Nc6 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 8. Be2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bf1e2] } 8... Be7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7] } 9. dxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd4c5] } 9... Bxc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Be7c5] } 10. Nb3 { [%csl Bb3][%cal Bd2b3] } 10... Bb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bc5b6] } 11. Bd3 { [%csl Bd3][%cal Be2d3] } 11... Nc5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bd7c5] } 12. Nxc5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.7).] [%csl Bc5][%cal Bb3c5] } 12... Bxc5 { Take the knight at once: any other move leaves you a piece down (+4.6 or worse). Then Qe2 and h4 start the kingside play. } 13. Qe2 Bd7 14. h4 h5 { ...h5 closes the h-file and stops White from using Ng5 and Bxh7. } 15. Be3 Qb6 16. Bxc5 Qxc5 17. Ng5 $14 { A modest White edge (about +0.8). Your next moves are ...O-O-O or ...O-O and ...f6 to challenge e5. }) 8. Qa4?! $14 { Inaccuracy. h4 was best. This move is kind of a multi-purpose move. The Queen controls the a6-square and as a coincidental benefit, causes a little bit of annoyance along the a4-e8 diagonal. } { [%csl Gd7,Ga4,Ga6][%cal Ga4e8,Ga4a6,Gd1a4] } (8. h4 $16 { h4 leads to a clear White edge (+1.0), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Bh4][%cal Bh2h4] } 8... Be7 $16 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+1.0).] [%csl Be7][%cal Be8e7] } 9. h5 { White wants to prise open the kingside with h5-h6, fix your g7 square and use the f4 pawn. } (9. Bb5 a6 10. Bd3 Nc6 11. Nf1 b5 12. Ng3 f5 13. Ng5 Nb6 { is the alternative, where you close the kingside with ...f5. }) 9... O-O 10. Bb5 a6 11. Bd3 a5 12. Nf1 Ba6 13. Bxa6 Nxa6 $14 { You trade White's light-squared bishop and get a playable game; the modest edge for White (about +1.1) is mostly space. Your plan is ...a5, ...Ba6 and ...f6. }) 8... Be7 { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gf8e7] } 9. Bd3 $14 { This structure resembles the symmetrical pawn preponderance in the centre known from classical middlegame theory, except that here there are pawns on d4 and d5. White's superior kingside space and control of f6 offer attacking chances, and a Bb1/Qc2 battery can build real strength. } { [%csl Ge6,Ge5,Gd4,Gd3][%cal Gf1d3,Gd3b1,Ga4c2] } 9... Bb7?! $16 { Inaccuracy. a5 was best. } { [%csl Gb7][%cal Gc8b7] } (9... a5 $14 { a5 leads to a modest White edge (+0.5), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } { [%csl Ba5][%cal Ba7a5] } 10. Nf1 { [%csl Bf1][%cal Bd2f1] } 10... b5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb6b5] } 11. Bxb5 { WATCH OUT: Qxb5 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -4.8 from White's own side of the board), after: Ba6 Qa4 Bxd3 Ng3 O-O Qd1 } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bd3b5] } 11... Qb6 { [%csl Bb6][%cal Bd8b6] } 12. Ng3 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bf1g3] } 12... Ba6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Bc8a6] } 13. Bxa6 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Bb5a6] } 13... Qxa6 { WATCH OUT: cxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for Black here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves Black clearly worse (roughly -4.7 from Black's own side of the board), after: Bd3 dxc3 bxc3 Qc7 Ne2 Nc6 } { [%csl Ba6][%cal Bb6a6] } 14. Ne2 { [%csl Be2][%cal Bg3e2] } 14... O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 15. Qd1 { [%csl Bd1][%cal Ba4d1] } 15... Nc6 { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bb8c6] } 16. O-O $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.4). Notable features: White is a pawn up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side.] [%csl Bg1][%cal Be1g1] } 16... a4 { You are a pawn down, but the compensation is real: White's e5/d4 chain leaves the queenside thin and your Qa6 already hits the knight on e2. The plan is ...a4, ...Rfb8 and ...axb3 to open the b-file against b2. } (16... cxd4 17. cxd4 a4 18. Bd2 Rfb8 19. Rb1 Rb7 { reaches the same type of position with the b-file as your target. }) 17. Be3 Rfb8 18. Rb1 g6 19. b3 axb3 20. axb3 Qb5 21. Bf2 $14 { White keeps a slight edge (about +0.4) but has to keep defending b3 and c3. }) 10. O-O $16 { Only after 10.0-0 does the point of ...Bb7 become clear; ...Bc6 to evict the queen had been expected. } { [%csl Rg6,Gg1,Rh6][%cal Rh7h6,Ge1g1,Rg7g6] } 10... h6?! $16 { Inaccuracy. g6 was best. Yes, this does control g5, which does seem like quite an aggressive outpost for White's knight. } { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gh7h6] } (10... g6 $14 { g6 leads to a clear White edge (+0.9): White's king has reached safety while Black's has not yet. } { [%csl Bg6][%cal Bg7g6] } 11. Rf2 $16 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.9).] [%csl Bf2][%cal Bf1f2] } 11... Nc6 { Your Nd7 is pinned along a4-e8, so the first job is to bring a piece to c6, pressure d4 and block the queen. } (11... a6 12. Ng5 Nc6 13. Ndf3 Nf8 { is slower, since the knight on g5 jumps at e6 and h7 and White keeps more pressure. }) 12. Qd1 Qc7 13. Nf1 f5 { ...f5 locks the kingside so e5 cannot be attacked and your e6 pawn is safe. } 14. Ne3 a6 15. a4 c4 { ...c4 hits the bishop on d3 and shuts it out of play. } 16. Bc2 $14 { White still has a little more (about +0.9): his king is safe and yours is not yet, so castle only when the kingside is closed. }) 11. f5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf4f5] } 11... exf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Ge6f5] } 12. Bxf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gd3f5] } 12... O-O { [%csl Gb7,Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 13. Bb1?! $16 { Inaccuracy. Rf2 was best. The bishop retreat looked good because the light squares around the Black king are weak: the knight cannot defend from f6 and the light-squared bishop is out of play on b7. } { [%csl Gb7,Gb1][%cal Gf5b1,Ga4c2] } (13. Rf2 $18 { [Engine verdict: a clear White edge (+1.8).] [%csl Bf2][%cal Bf1f2] } 13... Re8 { The first problem is the Nd7, attacked by Bf5 and Qa4 and pinned. The rook to e8 prepares ...Nf8 so the knight gets out of the pin and guards g6, h7 and e6. } (13... cxd4?! 14. cxd4 Qe8 15. Qc2 { is the greediest-looking reply and gives White a free hand with Nf1-g3 (about +2.4). }) 14. Nf1 Nf8 15. Ng3 Bc8 { Offering to trade off the dangerous bishop on f5. } 16. Be3 cxd4 17. cxd4 Bxf5 18. Nxf5 $16 { White keeps a clear edge (about +2.2) because the h6 pawn and your king remain targets, but your pieces are untangled and you can play ...Qd7 or ...Ne6 to challenge the knight on f5. }) 13... cxd4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc5d4] } 14. cxd4 { cxd4 leads to a clear White edge (+0.8): Black has an isolated pawn.   WATCH OUT: Qxd4 looks like a natural, tempting try for White here (it's a capture/check a club player would consider), but it leaves White clearly worse (roughly -5.0 from White's own side of the board), after: Bc5 Nb3 Bxd4+ Nbxd4 Nc6 Bf4 } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc3d4] } (14. Qc2 $16 { After 14.cxd4 this seemed the superior move, because taking the pawn first allows 14...Qc8!, which the opponent did not play. } 14... d3! { White's whole idea is the Bb1/Qc2 battery aimed at h7, where Qh7 would be mate. The pawn on d3 blocks the diagonal with tempo, attacking the queen. } (14... g6? 15. e6 Nc5 16. exf7+ Rxf7 17. Qxg6+ Rg7 18. Qh5 { shreds your king (about +2.2). }) (14... f5? 15. Nxd4 Nxe5 16. Ne6 { forks the queen and rook (about +2.0), and 14...Nf6?? loses to 15.exf6. }) 15. Qxd3 f5 { A second block: the f5 pawn shuts the diagonal again. } 16. Nd4 Nxe5 17. Qg3 f4 18. Rxf4 Rxf4 19. Qxf4 $14 { You come out with only a modest disadvantage (about +0.7). }) 14... Re8!? $16 { Black seems to want either an escape square or a knight to help the defence, but stopping the battery altogether with 14...Qc8 is simply superior. } { [%csl Ge8][%cal Gf8e8] } (14... Qc8?! $18 { White's attack seems dead in the water: the Qc2 battery is no longer possible, so White must return to positional play with manoeuvring and the collection of small advantages.  [Engine check (Stockfish 16, depth 20, White's point of view): after this move White is still clearly better (+2.2), so the attack is not over; Re8 (+1.3) was the stronger defence] [%csl Bc2][%cal Bc8c2] } 15. Re1 { White's attack is not dea