1. e4 $14 { COVERAGE NOTE: this reference chapter now demonstrates technique from the Exchange Variation (Chapters #1-#3), the Panov-Botvinnik Attack in both its Fianchetto (Chapter #4) and Standard (Chapter #5) forms plus the Accelerated Panov (Chapters #7-#8), the Two Knights Attack (Chapter #22), the Fantasy Variation (Chapters #28-#29), and 2.d3 systems (Chapter #31 (2.d3 Systems) and Chapter #30's other sidelines). See the Course Map chapter for the full Middlegame Guide coverage map tying every plan to its source chapters. } 1... c6 $14 { This chapter folds together four originally-separate 'Modern Practice Example' chapters into one combined Do's and Don'ts reference, covering the Exchange, Panov-Botvinnik (Fianchetto), Two Knights, and Fantasy Variation systems side by side. Each branch below starts from White's second move and follows an engine-verified, realistic continuation straight out of that system's main-line theory elsewhere in this course - use this chapter as a single place to compare how Black's technique looks across several different structures. Follows Chapter #29's second main line: 3.f3 e6 4.Nc3 Nd7, the quieter Fantasy Variation treatment. } 2. d4 { d4 leads to a small White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (2. Nf3 $14 { Nf3 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 2... d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 $14 { Matches Chapter #22's main line exactly: 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 Nf6, trading down into the structurally simple Tartakower treatment. } { [%cal Be4f6] } 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. d4 Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. O-O Be6 9. Be3 Nd7 10. Qd2 Re8 11. c4 Nf8 12. Rfe1 Qd7 13. h3 Bxh3 $10 { Here is the key defensive/attacking resource of this structure: even when Black has nothing better on the board, ...Bxh3 followed by ...Qxh3 and a queen check on g4/h3 gives Black at least a forced draw by perpetual check. This is exactly the kind of practical resource worth memorizing from Chapter #22's structure - in practice, many opponents mishandle the king safety here and Black gets real winning chances instead of just the draw. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bg4h3] } 14. gxh3 Qxh3 15. Qd1 Qg4+ 16. Kh1 Qh3+ 17. Kg1 Qg4+ 18. Kf1 Qh3+ 19. Kg1 Qg4+ $10 { [Engine verdict: roughly equal (+0.0). Notable features: White has the bishop pair; Black has doubled pawns on the f-file.] }) (2. c4 $10 { c4 leads to a modest White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. Nxd5 Qxd5 7. Qf3 Be6 $15 { BRIDGE: the Accelerated Panov Attack (2.c4!?, before Nc3/d4) - see Chapters #7-#8. Everything trades off through the center a tempo faster than the normal Panov, reaching a simplified, roughly balanced structure where Black's technique (trade actively, don't fear the open position) is the whole point. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc4d5,Bd5c6] } 8. Qxd5 { White's best is to trade queens. } (8. Qc3?! Nc6 9. Nf3 Rd8 10. b3 Bg4 11. Bc4 Qe4+ 12. Qe3 Bxf3 { avoids the queen trade but you gain tempi (about -0.8). }) 8... Bxd5 9. Ne2 Nc6 10. Nc3 O-O-O 11. Nxd5 Rxd5 12. d3 e6 $15 { You have a small edge (about -0.5): your rooks are active, your pieces are better developed, and your plan is ...e6, ...Bd6 and play on the d-file. }) (2. d3 $10 { d3 leads to roughly equal (+0.0), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 2... d5 3. Nd2 e5 4. Ngf3 Nd7 { Nd7 leads to a small White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (4... Bd6 $10 { Bd6 leads to roughly equal (-0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 5. Be2 Bc7 6. O-O Nf6 $15 { BRIDGE: a quieter 2.d3 sideline without White fianchettoing (the same family as Chapter #30's Breyer Variation material, including the Breyer-style setup). Black develops the bishop to d6/c7 and completes development normally; there is no independent theoretical problem to solve here beyond sound development. } { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd6h2,Bc7b6] } 7. b4 { White takes queenside space. } (7. c4 d4 8. b4 h6 9. a4 b6 10. b5 O-O 11. Qc2 Re8 { closes the centre, and you can play on the kingside. }) 7... a5 8. Bb2 O-O 9. a3 Re8 10. Re1 Nbd7 11. Bf1 h6 $10 { You challenge it with ...a5. The position is level (about -0.2), and your plan is ...Nf8, ...Ng6, ...Bd7 and a later ...e4 or ...dxe4. }) 5. g3 Ngf6 6. Bg2 Bd6 7. O-O O-O $15 { BRIDGE: White's 2.d3 King's Indian Attack / Leela setup - see Chapter #31 (2.d3 Systems). Black mirrors the fianchetto-vs-fianchetto structure and meets the slow buildup with the standard ...e5/...Re8/...exd4 central break covered there. } { [%csl Bg2][%cal Bd6h2,Bf6e4] } 8. exd5 { White opens the e-file. } (8. Re1 Re8 9. d4 exd4 10. exd5 Rxe1+ 11. Qxe1 c5 { shows the same ...exd4 break. }) 8... cxd5 9. c4 d4 10. Ng5 Be7 11. Re1 a5 12. Ngf3 Bd6 $10 { You answer with ...d4 to gain space. The position is level (about 0.0). Your plan is ...a5, ...Nc5 and ...Re8. }) 2... d5 3. exd5 { exd5 leads to roughly equal (+0.1): White has a material edge of about 1 point(s); White has doubled pawns. } (3. f3 $10 { f3 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 3... e6 4. Nc3 Nd7 5. Be3 a6 6. Bd3 Be7 7. Nge2 dxe4 8. fxe4 e5 9. Ng3 exd4 10. Bxd4 Bf6 11. Bc4 Ne5 12. Bb3 Bg4 13. Qd2 Nc4 14. Bxc4 Qxd4 $15 { The point of Chapter #29's whole approach: Black's central breaks (...dxe4 and ...exd4) have dissolved White's pawn duo completely and left Black with the more comfortable structure and the safer king. } { [%csl Bd4] } 15. Qxd4 Bxd4 16. h3 Be6 17. Bxe6 fxe6 18. Nge2 Be5 19. O-O-O Nf6 20. Nd4 Ke7 21. Na4 Rad8 22. c3 Nd7 $15 { From here Black's technique is standard: trade pieces to reach an endgame where the structural edge decides, avoid unnecessary complications, and centralize the king. This engine-verified continuation shows a realistic, modern way that edge gets converted - the same plan taught throughout Chapter #29, carried into the middlegame and beyond. } 23. Kc2 Bxd4 24. cxd4 Rhf8 25. Rd2 b5 26. Nc3 c5 27. Rhd1 b4 28. Na4 c4 $15 { [Engine verdict: a small Black edge (-0.3).] } 29. Kb1 { White has to stop ...c3 breaking through and avoid being squeezed by the b4 and c4 pawns. } (29. Rc1 Rb8 30. Kb1 c3 31. Rdd1 e5 32. bxc3 bxc3+ 33. Ka1 Rb4 { This is worse for White, about -0.3, because the passed c-pawn cramps him }) 29... Rf4 { Your rook attacks e4 and keeps the initiative. } 30. Rc1 Rc8 31. Rdc2 e5 32. Rxc4 Rxc4 33. Rxc4 Rxe4 { You win the pawn back and stay a shade better, about -0.2. }) 3... cxd5 $10 { Follows Chapter #1's main-line move order exactly through the opening: 3.exd5 cxd5, the Rubinstein Exchange structure. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc6d5] } 4. Bd3 { Bd3 leads to roughly equal (+0.2), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (4. c4 $10 { c4 leads to roughly equal (+0.1), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 4... Nf6 { Nf6 leads to a small White edge (+0.3), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } (4... e6 $14 { e6 leads to a small White edge (+0.4), with no major structural imbalance yet; the position is just a normal, level middlegame. } 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Nf3 Bd6 7. Be2 Nc6 8. O-O O-O $14 { BRIDGE: the Panov-Botvinnik Attack, Standard (Classical/IQP) Defence - see Chapter #5. Black develops naturally (...e6, ...Bd6, ...Nc6) rather than fianchettoing, meeting White's IQP with normal piece pressure instead of the Chapter #4 Fianchetto setup shown in the branch above. } { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bd6h2,Bc8g4] } 9. c5 { White grabs space and keeps your bishop out of d6, giving a kingside initiative. } (9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Bd3 Bd7 11. Re1 Rc8 12. Nxd5 exd5 13. h3 Nb4 { is the quieter alternative, where you hit the d3 bishop. }) 9... Bc7 10. Bg5 b6 11. Bb5 Ne7 12. b4 h6 13. Bxf6 gxf6 $14 { Your pawn structure is damaged but you have the bishop pair (about +0.5). Break with ...b6xc5 or ...a5 to hit b4, and use the bishop pair while keeping your king safe behind the damaged pawns. }) 5. Nc3 g6 6. Qb3 Bg7 $14 { The Fianchetto Defense to the Panov Attack, matching Chapter #4's main line move for move. } { [%csl Bg7][%cal Bf8g7] } 7. cxd5 O-O 8. Be2 Nbd7 9. Bf3 Nb6 10. Bf4 Bg4 $14 { Pinning the knight to challenge the center before White can consolidate the IQP - the standard plan from Chapter #4. } { [%cal Bc8g4] } 11. Bxg4 Nxg4 12. Nf3 Nf6 $14 { Black has reached the well-known slightly-worse-but-very-solid IQP-facing structure that Chapter #4 identifies as the main practical battleground of this line. This practice continuation shows the typical maneuvering war both sides face: White tries to make the isolated d-pawn count with piece activity, Black blockades and looks to trade into a favorable endgame. Engine-verified throughout, not taken from any specific recorded game. } 13. d6 exd6 14. O-O Qd7 15. h3 Rac8 16. Rad1 Rfe8 17. Nb5 Nfd5 18. Bg3 Bf8 19. Nxa7 Ra8 20. Nb5 Ra5 21. Nc3 Nxc3 22. bxc3 Rb5 $14 { [Engine verdict: a small White edge (+0.3). Notable features: White is a pawn up, but the engine sees enough compensation for the other side.] } 23. Qc2 { Your Rb5 is hitting the queen on b3, so the queen moves. White is a pawn up and wants to simplify. } (23. Ne5? dxe5 24. dxe5 Rxb3 25. Rxd7 Nxd7 26. axb3 Nc5 { loses a piece (about -4.1). }) 23... Ra5 24. Rb1 Nc4 25. Qb3 b5 26. a4 Rxa4 27. Qxb5 Qxb5 28. Rxb5 $14 { White keeps a tiny edge (about +0.3). }) 4... Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Bf4 g6 7. Nf3 Bg7 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. O-O Bf5 $14 { Trading off the light-squared bishop before it gets shut in, exactly as recommended in Chapter #1. } { [%cal Gc8f5] } 10. Bxf5 gxf5 11. Ne5 Nxe5 12. Bxe5 e6 $14 { The position after the standard minority-attack-style maneuvering is a small, stable White edge thanks to the isolated d5-pawn - fully in line with known Exchange Variation theory. The plan for Black from here (as covered in Chapter #1) is patient piece activity and timely central breaks rather than passive defense; this practice line shows a realistic modern continuation of exactly that plan, engine-verified at every step rather than taken from a specific game. } 13. Bf4 Ne4 14. Nb3 Re8 15. Kh1 f6 16. f3 Ng5 17. Bxg5 fxg5 18. Nc5 Bf8 19. Nd3 Bd6 20. f4 Qf6 21. Qh5 gxf4 22. Nxf4 Re7 $14 { [Engine verdict: a modest White edge (+0.8).]
