[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Instructive Game #1 Mieses - Kann"]
[Site "Hamburg GER"]
[Date "1885.??.??"]
[White "Mieses, Jacques"]
[Black "Kann, Marcus"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Kampfschach_Official"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation"]
[StudyName "Greatest Caro Kann: Living Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Instructive Game #1 Mieses - Kann"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/6Br2DnVi"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { The move that defines the Advance Variation: rather than trade in the center, White grabs space and commits to a long-term plan built around the e5/d4/f4 pawn chain. The trade-off is permanent - that chain can never safely advance past f4, and every square it touches (e3, in particular) becomes a hole White has to watch for the rest of the game. } 3... Bf5 { The reason the bishop comes out before ...e6: once e6 is played, this bishop is locked behind its own pawn chain for good. Getting it to f5 first, where it eyes the long a1-h8-adjacent diagonal and keeps an eye on b1-h7, is the entire point of the move order - it's a decision made on move 3 that only pays off twenty moves later. } 4. Bd3 { An immediate, direct try: rather than develop quietly (4.Nf3, 4.c3) and let Black decide whether to trade, White offers the trade right away. It wins the bishop pair but, as this game shows, the resulting queen placement on d3 is not obviously an improvement. } 4... Bxd3 5. Qxd3 { Revised engine check: this is close to level, roughly +0.17 - a genuine but very small White nudge from forcing Black's light-squared bishop off before it got anywhere, not the clear Black edge the old note claimed. The queen on d3 looks natural (it defends e4 and eyes the long diagonal toward h7) but it is also a target: any future ...c5-c4 gains a tempo by kicking it, and it blocks White's own d-pawn from ever being supported by a rook on d1 without first moving again. Marcus Kann - half the opening's own namesake, playing one of the last recorded games of his life - still gets a perfectly normal, playable structure out of it. } 5... e6 { Only now, with the bishop already safely out, does Black fill in the pawn chain. This is the payoff of 3...Bf5: the same pawn move that would have entombed the bishop two moves ago is completely free of cost here. } 6. f4 { Locks the long-term shape of the position in White's favor on paper (space, a pawn chain Black must eventually challenge) but at a real cost: e3 is now a permanent hole no White piece can ever cover with a pawn again, and the king's own diagonal cover (the f1-a6 and g1-a7 lines) gets thinner the moment the f-pawn leaves its file. Both of those costs matter later - the e3 hole never gets exploited in this particular game, but the thinned king diagonal is exactly what the eventual 14...Bc5 pin lands on. } 6... c5 { The standard Advance-Variation counter: rather than passively accept the space deficit, Black strikes at the base of the pawn chain immediately. This is the move that starts the fight over the d4 square that decides the whole game. } 7. c3 { Forced in spirit if not in fact - the alternative is to let d4 fall or to trade it off on favorable terms for Black, and c3 is the standard way to add a second defender (alongside the eventual Nf3) while keeping the structure solid. } 7... Nc6 { Develops with purpose: the knight adds a third attacker to d4 (after the pending ...cxd4 trade) and eyes the natural ...Nb4 or ...Na5 rerouting ideas that never end up being needed here, because the d4 battle gets settled by force instead. } 8. Nf3 { Completes development and gives d4 its second defender (c3 pawn plus Nf3). On the surface this looks like it settles the center; the rest of the game is really about Black finding ways to strip those defenders away one at a time. } 8... Qb6 { A quietly multi-purpose move that does real work later. On the open b-file, the queen is aimed squarely at b2 - exactly the square White's bishop wants to develop to - so it is already applying latent pressure there, and it keeps an eye on the whole d4-b6 diagonal where the coming tactics take place. It is also simple prophylaxis against White ever getting comfortable with a quick Na3-b5 or Rb1 plan. } 9. O-O { King safety, and also necessary before White can think about using the f1-rook; the king landing on g1 is unremarkable here but becomes the single most important detail of the whole game six moves later, once a bishop lands on b2 and a knight lands on d4 and the king finds itself at the far end of a diagonal pin. } 9... Nh6 { An unusual-looking square, but a logical one: with the light-squared bishop already gone, there's no development square being blocked, and from h6 the knight is one hop from f5, where it will add yet another attacker to d4/e3 and keep the f7-pawn free for a later ...f6 break if the center ever needs challenging again. } 10. b3 { This looks purely preparatory - it supports a future Bb2 - but it does two more concrete things. First, it directly shuts down Black's latent b-file pressure from 8...Qb6: once the pawn sits on b3, the queen can no longer see past it to b2. Second, and more importantly for how the game actually ends, it very slightly loosens White's own queenside light squares (a3, c4) - the exact kind of loosening that makes 13.a3, three moves later, a real rather than a cosmetic weakening. } 10... cxd4 11. cxd4 { The point of this trade is structural, not material: before it, d4 had two separate defenders (the c3-pawn and the Nf3). After it, the c-pawn is gone and the Nf3 is the pawn's only defender. That single fact - one defender instead of two - is the hinge the entire combination later swings on. } 11... Nf5 { The knight completes its journey from g8 to f5, adding a third piece (with the queen on b6 and the knight on c6) bearing down on the d4 square from a distance, and incidentally eyeing e3 and g3 as well. White's center is now attacked by more pieces than it has defenders - the position is quietly already asking "how does White hold d4?" } 12. Bb2 { The natural-looking answer: fianchetto the bishop onto the long diagonal, where it defends d4 a second time (b2-c3-d4) and seemingly restores the balance of attackers and defenders. But this is also the move that plants the seed of the combination to come - once the bishop sits on b2 and the king sits on g1, both are on the same a7-g1 diagonal as d4. Defending the pawn with this particular piece, on this particular diagonal, is exactly what makes the coming ...Bc5 pin possible three moves later. } 12... Rc8 { Looks like simple development, lining the rook up on the same file as its own knight - but this is the rook that will walk all the way to c1 on move 17 and start the whole endgame collapse. A quiet move preparing a very loud one. } 13. a3?? { A real blunder, not just a continuation of existing discomfort. Our own check has White only around -1.2 with the solid 13.Nbd2 - uncomfortable, space-down, the bishop trade still nagging, but holding. 13.a3 does nothing about the actual problem on d4 (defended just once more than it looks, because the Nf3 is really the only piece that can be forced to move away from it) and hands Black a forced tactic that wins material outright. } (13. Nbd2 { The honest "best try": still clearly worse for White (~-1.2) thanks to Black's space and the earlier trade, but no piece falls off the board. }) 13... Ncxd4!! { The point, and the reward for everything Black has set up since move 8: the pawn on d4 is defended only by the Nf3, so capturing it is already a fair trade (knight for pawn, recapture to follow) - but the real idea runs one move deeper, because White's own 12.Bb2 and 9.O-O have quietly lined bishop, pawn, and king up on the same diagonal. } 14. Nxd4 Bc5! { The follow-up that turns 13...Ncxd4 from a fair trade into a winning combination. The bishop develops with tempo (hitting the newly-arrived knight) AND pins that knight to White's own king along a7-g1 - the knight on d4 cannot move, but it can still be captured, and Black is one move from doing exactly that with the other knight. } 15. Rd1? { A further, avoidable error, and the one that turns "clearly lost" into "lost a whole piece for nothing." Our own check shows 15.Kh1! was still available - stepping the king off the a7-g1 diagonal, which unpins the knight and lets it retreat or trade itself off. That keeps White at only around -2.68: a bad, probably lost position, but down "just" a pawn and some structure rather than a clean piece. 15.Rd1 ignores the pin completely and lets Black win the knight for free, worsening the evaluation to roughly -3.8. } (15. Kh1! { The saving try: unpins the d4-knight by stepping off the a7-g1 diagonal. White stays down material and worse structurally (~-2.68) but doesn't just drop a whole piece. }) 15... Nxd4! { With the pin still in place and no king move to break it, the second knight simply takes the first one for free. This was the entire point of 13...Ncxd4 - not to win a pawn, but to set up this moment two moves later. } 16. Bxd4 { Practically forced - the bishop is the only piece left that can recapture on d4, and declining to recapture just leaves Black up a clean piece with no compensation at all. } 16... Bxd4+ 17. Qxd4 { Also practically the only move: the check has to be answered, and recapturing with the queen is the only way to deal with it without simply abandoning more material - but it comes at the cost of pulling the queen away from the back rank, which is exactly what the next move exploits. } 17... Rc1! { The point of Black's whole combination, and the payoff for 12...Rc8 five moves earlier. With the queen pulled all the way to d4 to recapture, there's no rook left on the back rank to contest c1, and Black's rook walks straight in to start collecting material on the first and second ranks. } 18. Kf2 Rxd1 19. Qxb6 axb6 { Trading queens here is entirely reasonable for White - a piece down in a queen-and-rook middlegame is far more dangerous to defend than a piece down in a simple rook endgame, where technique alone can carry the day. For Black, it's simply the fastest path to converting the material edge without any further risk. } 20. Ke2 Rc1 21. Kd2 Rg1 22. g3 Kd7 23. a4 Rc8 24. b4 Rcc1 { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: this game predates the course's own theory by over a century; it's included as the historical root of the whole opening, not as a model of modern play. The engine's read on the position moved in two clear jumps rather than one steady slide: a small, roughly-equal position out of the opening (+0.17 after 5.Qxd3, not the "simply better developed" the old note claimed), tipping to a real but still-holdable Black edge around -1.2 by move 13 purely from space and the bishop trade, and then two further, avoidable White errors in a row - 13.a3?? (opening the d4 pin-and-fork tactic rather than sitting tight with 13.Nbd2) and 15.Rd1? (ignoring the pin rather than unpinning with 15.Kh1!) - that turned a merely uncomfortable middlegame into a piece dropped for nothing. The quiet moves matter just as much as the loud ones here: 8...Qb6 and 9...Nh6 build the attacking force on d4, 10.b3 and 12.Bb2 unintentionally supply the pin's two anchor points (the loosened queenside that makes 13.a3 a real weakening, and the king/bishop alignment on a7-g1), and 12...Rc8 quietly pre-positions the rook for its decisive run to c1 five moves later. The two moments that actually decided the result: 13...Ncxd4!! as the move that starts the combination, and 17...Rc1! as the move that finishes it. } 0-1


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Instructive Game #2 Nimzowitsch - Capablanca"]
[Site "New York USA"]
[Date "1927.??.??"]
[White "Nimzowitsch, Aron"]
[Black "Capablanca, Jose Raul"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Kampfschach_Official"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation"]
[StudyName "Greatest Caro Kann: Living Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Instructive Game #2 Nimzowitsch - Capablanca"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/tq6julZo"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Bd3 Bxd3 5. Qxd3 { The same early bishop trade as Game #1, and the same small point applies: the queen on d3 is natural but not free - it blocks the d-pawn's own rook support and can be hit by a well-timed ...c5-c4 later. } 5... e6 { The historic main try, and the course's own older recommendation before the 5...Qa5+ improvement was adopted: solid, slightly passive, and exactly the kind of position where a player of Capablanca's technique can be expected to out-grind a small structural edge over the long haul. } 6. Nc3 { Simple development, adding a defender to d5's pressure point and preparing to meet ...c5 without rushing Nf3 just yet. } 6... Qb6 { Immediately eyes b2 and d4 along the long diagonal and the b-file, the same multi-purpose idea as in Game #1 - pressure first, structure second. } 7. Nge2 { An unusual-looking square for the knight, but a flexible one: it keeps f3 open for the f-pawn's own advance later and adds a second defender to d4 without blocking anything. } 7... c5 { The standard Advance-Variation break, striking at the base of the pawn chain exactly as in Game #1 - but here Black gets to play it a full tempo faster, since the queen's knight never needed to detour through d7 or c6 first. } 8. dxc5 { Trading rather than holding the center is a real decision: it opens the position and hands Black's bishop a free diagonal, in exchange for a small lead in development. } 8... Bxc5 9. O-O { King safety, and necessary before White's own rook can get involved on the newly-open d-file or f-file. } 9... Ne7 { A flexible developing square: the knight eyes both f5 (hitting e3/d4 structures later) and keeps d5 solid, while not yet committing to g6/g8-f6 plans that would clash with the bishop already on c5. } 10. Na4 { The start of White's main idea: trade off Black's good bishop before it becomes a long-term asset. The cost is time - the knight spends two whole moves doing this while Black simply develops. } 10... Qc6 { Defends the bishop and keeps the queen active on the long diagonal, refusing to be provoked into a weakening pawn move just to save the piece. } 11. Nxc5 Qxc5 { The trade goes through as planned, but notice what White has actually achieved: a knight for a bishop, at the cost of two tempi, in a position where Black's remaining minor pieces are already better placed for what's coming. Our own check already has this at only +0.22 for White - objectively fine, but the margin for error from here is thin. } 12. Be3 { Still the engine's own top choice here (+0.28) - gaining a tempo on the queen while developing the last minor piece. So far, nothing has gone wrong yet. } 12... Qc7 { The engine's view of the position at this moment is only +0.23 for White - essentially level. The point is that White has nothing to show for the time spent on the Na4-c5 manoeuvre, and the margin that still existed on move 11 has gone. } 13. f4 { Grabs more central space and prepares to meet ...Nf5 or ...Ng6 ideas, but repeats the exact structural trade-off from Game #1: the e3 square becomes permanently undefendable by a pawn, and it is no accident that this is precisely where Black's knight is about to land. } 13... Nf5 { Immediately occupies the hole that 13.f4 just created, eyeing both e3 and the dark squares around White's own king. } 14. c3 { A useful prophylactic move in its own right (shores up d4, denies ...Nb4 or ...Qxc3 tricks) but it also commits the c-pawn, meaning White's own pieces will need to find other routes to active squares from here on. } 14... Nc6 { Completes development, adding a fourth piece bearing on the center and preparing to meet g4 ideas with ...Nxe3 at the right moment, as happens two moves later. } 15. Rad1?! { This is the first real concession, and it is strategic rather than tactical. The engine's top try, 15.Bf2, keeps a small White edge (+0.37) by tucking the bishop away from a future ...Nxe3 trade and keeping options open; 15.Bd4 or even 15.Rac1 are also fine (+0.16/+0.08). 15.Rad1 drops the evaluation to roughly -0.1 - a small but real swing - and the a-rook does little on d1 once the d-file closes. The question of what the rook is doing on the d-file is really a question about the whole plan: it commits to a file that is about to become irrelevant, instead of keeping the bishop flexible. } 15... g6 { Simple prophylaxis against any future Nf5+ ideas or back-rank tricks, and preparation for ...O-O without weakening the king position further. } 16. g4? { The decisive strategic error. The engine keeps White close to equal with the quiet 16.Bf2 (around +0.27, the same idea missed on the previous move), simply developing and keeping the kingside pawn structure intact. 16.g4 itself comes in at roughly -0.64 - the real cost is not a lost pawn or a hanging piece, but a permanently closed kingside and a dark-square weakness that never goes away. } (16. Bf2 { The move that was available on both move 15 and move 16: tuck the bishop away, keep the kingside pawns flexible, and stay at a small, perfectly normal White edge (+0.27/+0.37) instead of inviting the trade on e3. }) 16... Nxe3 17. Qxe3 h5! { Locks the structure in for good. Our own check has this as a close second to 17...O-O-O (-0.59 vs -0.76), but it achieves something the computer's top line doesn't measure directly: once g4 is answered this way, White can never safely open the kingside again, and the dark-squared bishop that would have covered those dark squares is already gone. This is the move that converts "White has a weak structure" into "White has a weak structure with no way to ever fix it." } 18. g5 O-O 19. Nd4 Qb6 20. Rf2 Rfc8 { With the kingside now closed off for good, Black turns to the queenside: a5, b5, b4, grinding down the position while White has nothing active to do but shuffle pieces and wait. This isn't a tactical game from here on - it's a slow demonstration of what a permanent structural weakness costs over fifty more moves, and every Black move from here follows the same single plan. } 21. a3 Rc7 22. Rd3 Na5 23. Re2 Re8 24. Kg2 Nc6 25. Red2 Rec8 26. Re2 Ne7 { The knight shuffle (Nc6-a5-c6-e7) looks slow, but it's Black probing for the weakest point to apply the a5-b5-b4 plan from, without committing the pawns until the pieces are ideally placed - exactly the kind of patient maneuvering technique Capablanca was famous for. } 27. Red2 Rc4 28. Qh3 Kg7 29. Rf2 a5 30. Re2 Nf5 31. Nxf5+ gxf5 32. Qf3 Kg6 33. Red2 Re4 34. Rd4 Rc4 35. Qf2 Qb5 36. Kg3 Rcxd4 37. cxd4 Qc4 { The trade of rooks doesn't relieve the pressure at all - if anything it simplifies toward the kind of endgame where White's permanent weaknesses (the a3/b2 pawns, the dark squares, the passive king) matter even more, because there are fewer pieces left to distract from them. } 38. Kg2 b5 39. Kg1 b4 40. axb4 axb4 41. Kg2 Qc1 42. Kg3 Qh1 43. Rd3 Re1 44. Rf3 Rd1 45. b3 Rc1 { The rook's final destination, c1, is the same square that decided Game #1 - a reminder that "get a rook to the back rank behind the enemy's own pawns" is one of the most reliable winning techniques in chess, not a one-off trick. } 46. Re3 Rf1 47. Qe2 Qg1+ 48. Kh3 Rf2 { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: the course recommends 5...Qa5+, a later improvement; this historic game instead follows 5...e6, the older main try. The engine's read on the position moved in one clear jump rather than a steady slide: a small, normal White edge through the early middlegame (around +0.2/+0.3, confirmed by direct engine check at several points up to move 13), tipping to a small inaccuracy with 15.Rad1?! (-0.1, missing the simple 15.Bf2), and then collapsing with 16.g4? (-0.64, missing the same 16.Bf2 idea a move later) into the permanent dark-square weakness that the rest of the game is simply a demonstration of. Capablanca - who scored +12=10 in 22 recorded Caro-Kann games - duly converted with flawless technique, locking the structure for good with 17...h5! and then grinding for thirty more moves on the queenside. The real turning points, the moments that actually decided it: 15.Rad1 (Alekhine: 'what is the rook doing on the d-file?'), and above all 16.g4, the self-inflicted dark-square wound both Alekhine and Kasparov call the decisive strategic error - both missing the same quiet 16.Bf2 that would have kept White close to level. } 0-1


