1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 { The Two Knights Attack: rather than commit to the center with d4 right away, White develops both knights first and keeps options open about the pawn structure - a quieter, more flexible try than the main lines. } 3... Bg4 { Immediate pressure on f3, provoking White into either trading the knight's defender away or accepting a slightly awkward h3/structural concession. } 4. h3 Bxf3 5. Qxf3 { A third game in this course now where the queen recaptures a minor piece on a central square early - and again, the question is always the same: is the activity worth the time and the slightly exposed queen? } 5... Nf6 { The course's own recommendation, 5...e6, is the newer try; this game follows the older 5...Nf6, developing immediately and trusting the position rather than refining the move order first. } 6. d3 { A modest, flexible choice - rather than stake out the center with d4 immediately, White keeps the structure soft and waits to see how Black commits first. } 6... e6 7. g3 { Prepares Bg2, fighting for the long diagonal rather than developing the bishop to a more natural square like e2 - consistent with the whole Two Knights approach of flexibility over immediate central commitment. } 7... Bb4 { Develops with a pin-like idea on c3, provoking the trade that follows and softening White's queenside structure before Black has even finished developing. } 8. Bd2 d4 { The move that defines the whole game. Rather than let the centre resolve itself, Black pushes the pawn forward, driving the c3-knight to the rim and planting a wedge that cramps White's pieces for the next twenty moves. } 9. Nb1 { The knight has nowhere better to go: c3 is attacked, and every other square is worse. This is the direct cost of 8...d4 - White's own developed piece is shoved into the most passive square on the board. } 9... Bxd2+ 10. Nxd2 { The recapturing knight is no better off than the one it replaces: d2 is a square with very little to do until the center opens up, and for now it just blocks the queen's own bishop from easily reaching the long diagonal too. } 10... e5 { Completes the pawn wedge: with pawns on d4 and e5, Black has a firm grip on the center that neither White knight can challenge, and the position already has the feel of a good version of a King's Indian for Black. } 11. Bg2 c5 { Supports the whole structure and prepares ...Nc6, adding yet another piece to the pile bearing down on the queenside dark squares and the backward d3 pawn. } 12. O-O Nc6 13. Qe2 { Our own check confirms the position is still close to balanced here (+0.16) - the structural problems for White are real but not yet decisive; this is the last moment where accurate play could have kept things fully level. } 13... g5 { The start of a full-board plan: rather than just sit on the space advantage, Black begins a kingside pawn storm too, using the extra space to attack on both wings at once - the hallmark of a truly cramping pawn structure. } 14. Nf3 h6 15. h4 Rg8 { Meets the challenge to the kingside pawns head-on rather than backing down, keeping the rook ready to support the g-file once it opens. } 16. a3 Qe7 17. hxg5 hxg5 18. Qd2 Nd7 { Reroutes the knight toward e5 or b6, adding yet another piece to the queenside while the kingside pawns (now on g5 and g4-to-come) do their own work for free. } 19. c3 O-O-O { Our own check already has Black slightly for choice here (-0.76) - not from any single tactic, but from the accumulated cost of the cramped Nb1-d2-f3 knight tour, the permanently backward d3 pawn, and a king that will always need defending on the queenside now that Black has castled directly into the open file. } 20. cxd4 exd4 { Trading the c-pawn relieves a little of the tension but hands Black a protected, advanced d4 pawn in return - from a structural standpoint, White has traded a problem for an even more permanent one. } 21. b4 Kb8 { Simple prophylaxis, sidestepping any future check or discovered tactic on the c-file before the real fighting on the queenside begins. } 22. Rfc1 Nce5 { The knight lands on its first dream square of the game, hitting d3 and f3 at once - though as the next move shows, it is traded off immediately rather than staying there, so its real value here is forcing White into a slightly awkward recapture rather than anything permanent. } 23. Nxe5 Qxe5 { The recapture keeps the queen beautifully centralized on e5, eyeing both the long diagonal toward White's king and the weak d3/b2 pawns - a far better square for it than it had on e7. } 24. Rc4 Rc8 25. Rac1 g4 { The kingside pawn finally advances, and it does real work: it takes away f3 from White's bishop and queen for good, one more small cramp added to a position already full of them. } 26. Qb2 Rgd8 27. a4 Qe7 { A quiet regrouping move that turns out to be the calm before the storm - by simply improving the queen's safety and flexibility one more time, Black makes sure every piece is ready before the position explodes on the next move. } 28. Rb1?? { The critical moment of the game, where a quiet-looking move and a losing move turn out to be the same thing. The engine finds that the only move holding the balance was the surprising 28.bxc5!, returning the exchange on d4 to remove Black's main asset, the advanced pawn, in return for material (dead equal, 0.00). Every other try, including the actual 28.Rb1, is already around -1.2 to -1.7 for White; Rb1 itself checks out at -1.67. By this stage normal moves are no longer enough - only the counter-intuitive exchange sacrifice keeps the position playable. } (28. bxc5 { The one defense: hand back the exchange to remove the d4-pawn, the single piece of the position Black's whole plan depends on. Objectively equal (0.00) - and exactly the kind of move that's hard to find, since it means voluntarily giving back material that was just won. } 28... Ne5 { The same knight jump Black gets in the actual game, but here it no longer wins anything - the d4-pawn it would otherwise help support is about to be recaptured instead. } 29. Rxd4 { The point of the sacrifice: with the rook recapturing on d4 instead of the pawn being allowed to sit there forever, White's position is simply equal rather than permanently worse. }) 28... Ne5! { The move 28.bxc5 was meant to prevent. With the rook still on c4 rather than having traded itself off on d4, the knight lands on its true dream square with no good answer this time - it attacks the rook on c4, covers d3, and can't be challenged by anything White has left. } 29. Rxc5 Rxc5 30. bxc5 Nxd3 { Picks up the first pawn of the material harvest, and the knight on d3 is now untouchable - supported by the advanced d4-pawn and eyeing both b2 and f2. } 31. Qd2 Nxc5 { A second pawn falls, and Black now has an extra pawn, the bishop pair's worth of central control, and an attack - there is no defense left to coordinate. } 32. Qf4+ Qc7 33. Qxg4 Nxa4 { From here it's pure technique: an extra pawn and a monster passed d-pawn, Petrosian taking his time to convert without giving White a single chance for the rest of the game. } 34. e5 Nc5 35. Qf3 d3 { The passed pawn begins its march - the same structural trump that was sacrificed back on move 28's missed defense is now simply walking down the board uncontested. } 36. Qe3 d2 { One square from promotion, and tying down White's queen and bishop to stopping it is worth more than any amount of material counterplay elsewhere on the board. } 37. Bf3 Na4 38. Qe4 Nc5 39. Qe2 { The knight shuffle (Na4-c5-a4-c5) isn't aimless - it's keeping the d2-pawn covered while probing for the moment to add a rook or the king itself to the attack on White's back rank. } 39... a6 40. Kg2 Ka7 { Simple king safety before the final conversion, taking the king off any back-rank or long-diagonal tricks while there's no rush. } 41. Qe3 Rd3 42. Qf4 Qd7 43. Qc4 b6 44. Rd1 a5 45. Qf4 Rd4 46. Qh6 b5 47. Qe3 Kb6 48. Qh6+ Ne6 49. Qe3 Ka6 50. Be2 a4 51. Qc3 Kb6 52. Qe3 Nc5 53. Bf3 b4 { The queenside pawns roll forward exactly as the plan starting back on move 20 always intended - White's pieces can shuffle and check, but there's no way to actually stop two separate passed-pawn majorities with one queen and one bishop. } 54. Qh6+ Ne6 55. Qh8 Qd8 56. Qh7 Qd7 57. Qh8 b3 { The second passed pawn, on the other wing, starts its own run - White's queen has spent the last several moves giving checks and threatening nothing, while Black simply advances on both sides of the board in turn. } 58. Qb8+ Ka5 59. Qa8+ Kb5 60. Qb8+ Kc4 61. Qg8 Kc3 62. Bh5 Nd8 63. Bf3 a3 64. Qf8 Kb2 65. Qh8 Ne6 66. Qa8 a2 67. Qa5 Qa4 68. Rxd2+ Ka3 { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: this course recommends 5...e6 rather than the older 5...Nf6, so the game diverges almost immediately after the opening, but remains the classical demonstration of Black's resources in this whole structure. The engine's read on the position moved in clear, identifiable stages rather than one single swing: close to level through the opening (+0.16 at move 13), drifting to a real positional edge for Black by move 20 (-0.76) purely from the cramped Nb1-d2 knight tour and the permanently backward d3 pawn, and then turning decisively on one missed defense - 28.Rb1?? (-1.67), where only the startling exchange sacrifice 28.bxc5! would have held the balance (0.00). From there it's a clean two-pawn, two-passed-pawn conversion with no further chances for White. The real turning points, the moments that actually decided it: 8...d4 (the structural decision the whole game is really about), and 28.Rb1?? / 28...Ne5! (missing 28.bxc5! and allowing the knight to land on its real dream square, not the one it briefly visited and traded off back on move 22). } 0-1


