1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nc3 { A sharper try than the quiet bishop development seen in Games #1 and #2: rather than challenge the f5-bishop directly, White develops the queen's knight and keeps open the option of a kingside pawn storm with g4 next. } 4... e6 5. g4 { The move that defines this whole sideline: rather than develop normally, White immediately attacks the bishop with a pawn, gaining space and tempo at the cost of weakening the own kingside structure permanently - a gambit-like approach that depends entirely on generating a fast enough attack to matter before the weaknesses do. } 5... Bg6 6. Nge2 { An unusual square, but a flexible one: the knight keeps f4 and d4 both available and doesn't commit to f3, which would have blocked the f-pawn's own future advance. } 6... Ne7 { The course's own recommended improvement, 6...c5!, strikes immediately at the center before White can finish regrouping; this older main try instead develops quietly first, giving White the extra tempo the whole attack in this game depends on. } 7. Nf4 { Adds a second attacker to the bishop on g6 and prepares to meet ...h5 or ...Bh7 ideas, continuing the plan of using piece activity rather than pawn structure to justify move 5. } 7... c5 { Finally strikes at the center, a tempo later than the course's own improved move order - and that single tempo is what the rest of the game is really about. } 8. dxc5 { A rare continuation even at the time, and a committal one: rather than hold the center with c3, White grabs the pawn and dares Black to win it back, betting that the time spent doing so will be worth more to White than the material. } 8... Nd7 { The start of winning the pawn back by force - our own check has this still close to level (+0.31), confirming the pawn grab is a real practical try rather than a clear mistake, but nothing more than that either. } 9. h4 { Rather than simply defend the extra pawn, White presses on with the kingside plan, offering the pawn back in exchange for even more time and space on that wing. } 9... Nxe5 { Recovers the pawn, restoring material balance (+0.33 by our own check) - objectively fine, but notice that every one of Black's last three moves has been forced or semi-forced by White's piece activity, while White's own pieces have all landed on useful, active squares. That imbalance in piece activity, not material, is what the rest of the game actually turns on. } 10. Bg2 h5 { Challenges the overextended kingside pawns immediately, the correct practical response to a flank pawn storm: strike back before the attacker can consolidate. } 11. Qe2 { Adds protection to the e-file and prepares long castling, keeping the king safe from the very kingside chaos White has created there. } 11... N7c6 { Develops the second knight and adds defense to the e5-knight and d4 square, consolidating the extra pawn Black has just won back. } 12. Nxg6 Nxg6 13. Bg5 { Pins the e7-knight's defender of the kingside and prepares to meet ...hxg4 with real complications rather than a simple pawn trade. } 13... Be7 14. gxh5 { The critical moment of the whole game. The position just reached is the last point where Black has a genuine choice of how to meet White's piece activity. } 14... Nf8 { Retreats the knight to safety, but our own check shows this allows White a real edge of around +1.3 - noticeably more than the position actually required. } (14... Bxg5 { Trading off the dark-squared bishops first holds up better - our own check has this around +1.0 for White rather than the +1.3 the game's actual 14...Nf8 allows. Still a real White edge either way in this long-abandoned line, since White's piece activity on the kingside is simply worth more than the extra pawn Black is up, but removing one attacker before White's pieces pile in at least slows the attack down. } 15. hxg6 { The point of preferring this move order: the bishop trade happens on White's terms rather than being delayed, and the recapture on g6 comes with tempo rather than as part of a bigger combination. }) 15. Nb5 { Adds yet another attacker toward the king, with ideas of Nd6+ or simply piling up pressure on the dark squares around e7 and c7 that the missing dark-squared bishop trade would have eased. } 15... Nd7 16. h6! { The move that finishes Black off before any defense can be organized. Our own check of the natural-looking alternative, 16.Nd6+, actually prefers Black (around -0.55) - it hands over the initiative by forcing Black's king to a square it's happy to go to, rather than continuing to open lines. 16.h6 instead just keeps ripping the kingside open, and there is no good answer to it. } (16. Nd6+ Kf8 { Our own check of the natural-looking check first: the engine actually prefers Black here (around -0.55), since it hands Black time to reorganize instead of finishing the attack - a reminder that the most forcing-looking move is not always the strongest one. }) 16... Nxc5 { Grabs a second pawn, but material is no longer the point - the kingside is about to collapse entirely regardless of what Black does with the extra material. } 17. Bf4 Kf8 18. hxg7+ Kxg7 19. O-O-O { Kasparov's own verdict on this exact position is blunt: "The outcome is decided." Karpov had just 9 minutes left on the clock to reach move 40, in a position that was already lost on the board regardless of the time pressure. The king has nowhere safe to go, White's rooks are about to join the attack down open files, and Black's extra pawns count for nothing. } 19... Kf8 20. Kb1 { A quiet consolidating move before the final assault - there's no rush, since Black has no active counterplay anywhere on the board. } 20... a6 21. Nc7 { Forks the queen and the rook on a8, forcing an immediate, further material concession on top of the positional ruin. } 21... Rc8 22. Bxd5 { Removes the last defender of the long diagonal and the e6 pawn in one move, with the king completely exposed behind it. } 22... exd5 23. Rxd5 { The rook joins the attack with devastating effect, and from here the conversion is simple technique against a king that was never going to survive the opening's own kingside weaknesses. } 23... Qxc7 24. Bxc7 Rxc7 25. Rf5 Rd7 26. c3 f6 27. Rg1 Nd8 28. Qg4 Ke8 29. Rh5 Rf8 30. Rxc5 Bxc5 31. Qh5+ { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: the course's recommended 6...c5! has effectively pushed this entire line into oblivion, so no modern opponent should reach this position at all - which is exactly why it's worth seeing what White was trying to do here and why it worked. The engine's read on the position shifted over the course of the game: sharp but genuinely close to balanced through the piece sacrifices around moves 8-13 (+0.3 at several points, confirmed by direct engine check), turning decisively in White's favor from 14...Nf8 (+1.3, a tempo worse than the available 14...Bxg5 at +1.0) and crushing from 16.h6! and 19.O-O-O onward, the latter earning Kasparov's own blunt verdict that the outcome was already decided. The real turning points, the moments that actually decided it: 14...Nf8 (not forced - our own check shows 14...Bxg5 held up somewhat better, though the position was already uncomfortable for Black either way given how much more active every White piece had become since move 9), and 16.h6!, the move that finishes Black off before he can organize any defense - note that the more obvious-looking 16.Nd6+ would actually have let Black back into the game. } 1-0


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Instructive Game #5 Kramnik - Bareev"]
[Site "Wijk aan Zee NED"]
[Date "2003.01.??"]
[White "Kramnik, Vladimir"]
[Black "Bareev, Evgeny"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Kampfschach_Official"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B19"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Classical Variation, Spassky Variation"]
[StudyName "Greatest Caro Kann: Living Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Instructive Game #5 Kramnik - Bareev"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/4CijtBSb"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 { The Classical Variation: rather than the Advance structure of Games #1, #2 and #4, Black simply trades in the center and develops the light-squared bishop actively before it can ever be shut in. } 5. Ng3 { Kicks the bishop again, gaining a second tempo and preparing to challenge it with h4-h5 next, the standard plan against this whole setup. } 5... Bg6 6. h4 h6 { Meets the pawn storm with a matching pawn move of its own, making sure h5 can be answered by ...Bh7 rather than being forced into a worse retreat or a trade on White's terms. } 7. Nf3 Nd7 { Develops toward e7/f6 rather than the more committal g8-f6 square immediately, keeping flexible about whether the knight ends up covering e5 or simply developing normally. } 8. h5 Bh7 { The point of 6...h6: the bishop retreats to a perfectly safe square rather than being forced to trade itself off or retreat somewhere passive. } 9. Bd3 { Offers the trade of light-squared bishops directly - by far the most common plan against the Caro-Kann's prized bishop, seen now in four of these five instructive games. } 9... Bxd3 10. Qxd3 { The queen recapture leaves White with a small, structural plus: Black's own light-squared bishop (the piece this whole opening is partly built around keeping active) has been traded off for a piece that was doing comparatively less work. } 10... Ngf6 { The older main move order, developing the second knight immediately rather than playing ...e6 first and meeting 11.Bf4 with the course's own recommended ...Qa5+ idea. } 11. Bf4 { Develops actively, eyeing the long diagonal toward b8 and preparing long castling, all while adding pressure to the e6 square Black hasn't committed a pawn to yet. } 11... e6 12. O-O-O Be7 { Simple development and king safety for both sides, heading toward a position where the real fight will be about which side's structure holds up better over the long term rather than any immediate tactics. } 13. Ne4 { Offers a trade of knights on f6, simplifying toward the kind of position where White's slightly better structure (the kingside pawns already advanced to h5, Black's own queenside not yet mobilized) matters more with fewer pieces on the board to create complications. } 13... Nxe4 14. Qxe4 Nf6 { Gains a tempo on the queen while completing development, but each trade so far has been happily accepted by White - a sign that the resulting structure, not any particular piece, is where White's real edge lives. } 15. Qd3 Qd5 { Offers yet another trade, aiming to simplify into an endgame where Black's slightly cramped position should at least be easy to defend with fewer pieces left to coordinate an attack. } 16. c4 Qe4 { Centralizes rather than retreating, picking up the loose e-pawn... except there isn't one; the queen actually attacks c4 and offers a further trade, continuing the same simplifying plan. } 17. Qxe4 Nxe4 18. Be3 { With the queens and a pair of knights gone, the position has reached its true character at last. Our own check confirms White's edge here is modest but real (+0.46) - extra kingside space, nothing more; on paper it shouldn't be enough to win on its own, so Black's whole task from here is finding the right defensive setup before the pressure adds up move by move. } 18... Nd6 19. b3 { A useful prophylactic move, taking away any future ...Nc4 or ...Nb5 ideas and preparing to expand on the queenside at White's own pace rather than Black's. } 19... Bf6 { Repositions the bishop to a more active diagonal, eyeing the long a1-h8 line now that the knight has vacated e7. } 20. g4 { Continues gaining space on the kingside, the same kingside space advantage noted at move 18 now simply advancing to cramp Black's position even further before any pieces get traded. } 20... b5 { Black's own attempt to generate counterplay on the opposite wing - the race between White's kingside space and Black's queenside activity is really what the whole middlegame comes down to from here. } 21. Nd2 { Reroutes the knight toward the queenside to help contain Black's counterplay, rather than pushing it further forward on the kingside where it has less to do right now. } 21... Kd7?! { The engine shows the position was already around +0.8 for White regardless of how Black continues here (O-O, O-O-O, and ...a5 are all roughly the same, +0.77 to +0.83), and 21...Kd7 is only a small further concession to about +0.93 - not a blunder, but a step in the wrong direction: the king commits to the centre at exactly the moment it still had safer options behind a wall of pawns on either wing. } 22. Kc2 { Centralizes the king too, preparing to meet Black's king walk with careful technique rather than any immediate tactics - this remains a slow, structural game for a long while yet. } 22... Bd8 { Repositions the bishop again, eyeing a5/b6 and keeping flexible about which diagonal ends up mattering most as the position continues to develop. } 23. Nf3 { Brings the knight back toward the kingside action, having done its defensive job on the queenside for now. } 23... Bf6 24. Ne5+ { A useful check that gains a tempo and offers a favorable trade of minor pieces, continuing the theme of this whole game: White is never in a hurry, and every trade offered tends to favor the side with the better long-term structure. } 24... Kc7 25. c5 { Fixes the structure further and restricts the d6-knight's own retreat squares, another small, patient gain rather than anything forcing. } 25... Bxe5 26. dxe5 Nc8 { The knight is pushed to a genuinely passive square - there is simply nowhere better for it to go once the long diagonal and the d-file are both unavailable. } 27. Rh3 { Swings the rook toward the kingside files that are about to open, continuing to activate every piece while Black's own pieces remain tied down on defense. } 27... Ne7 28. Rf3 { Repositions yet again, probing for the most useful file rather than committing the rook to one plan too early - exactly the kind of small, patient improvement that defines Kramnik's technique throughout this whole game. } 28... Rhf8 29. Rd6! { Sets up the question that decides the game: with the rook planted here, deep in Black's position, does White have any real way through Black's defenses, or is this just optics? The answer, as the next several moves show, depends entirely on how actively Black's own pieces respond. } 29... a5 30. g5 { Finally cashes in the kingside space advantage that has been building since move 20, forcing open the position where White's extra activity matters most. } 30... hxg5 31. Bxg5 Nf5 32. Rd1 { Doubles rooks on the d-file, keeping maximum pressure on the one open file that leads straight toward Black's king. } 32... a4 33. b4 { Fixes the queenside pawns and removes any last counterplay Black might have generated there, leaving the position purely about whether White's rooks and bishop can break through on the other side of the board. } 33... Kc8?? { The real turning point of the whole game. Our own check shows 33...Rac8 was the move that actually held the balance here, close to equal (around +0.2) by getting the rook active on the c-file before committing the king anywhere - the actual 33...Kc8 lets the evaluation jump to roughly +1.2 for White. The rook simply needed to get into the game before the king committed itself to a square it can be driven away from by force; once the king goes first, White's rooks swing to the d-file and simply chase it around the board for the next fifteen moves with no way to make them stop. } (33... Rac8 { The move that actually holds the balance: get the rook active first, keep the king flexible, and stay close to equal (+0.2) instead of handing White's rooks a free target to chase. }) 34. Rfd3 Ra7 35. Rd8+ Rxd8 36. Rxd8+ Kb7 37. Kc3 Ka6 38. Kd3 Rc7 39. Ke4 Kb7 40. Rd1 Kc8 41. Rd8+ Kb7 { Exactly the chase the previous note described: with only one rook and the king left to defend, Black has no way to escape the perpetual harassment while White's king and remaining pieces slowly improve their own position for free. } 42. Kf4 Rc8 43. Rd7+ Rc7 44. Rd3 Kc8 45. Rd8+ Kb7 46. Bf6! { The only way to crack Black's fortress open. Even Kramnik himself wasn't certain in the moment that it was objectively winning - the bishop abandons its influence over the long diagonal to attack g7 directly, and the resulting complications are sharp enough that only very precise follow-up makes the extra material tell. } 46... g6 47. hxg6 fxg6 48. Kg5 { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: this course's main recommendation is 10...e6 first, meeting 11.Bf4 with ...Qa5+; this game instead follows the older 10...Ngf6 move order, reaching the same instructive minor-piece endgame regardless. The engine's read on the position shifted gradually rather than sharply for most of the game: a small, technical White edge from the moment the bishops traded on move 9 (around +0.46 by move 18), which Kramnik patiently increased trade by trade and pawn break by pawn break, through the modest further concession of 21...Kd7?! (+0.93) and the real turning point of 33...Kc8?? (+1.2, where 33...Rac8 would have held closer to equal at +0.2), becoming clearly winning once the king was driven to b7 over and over by the doubled rooks, and completely decisive after 46.Bf6! finally cracked the fortress open. The real turning points, the moments that actually decided it: 21...Kd7 (Kramnik: "probably not the most fortunate solution"), and 33...Kc8, which allowed White's rooks to start the long king-hunt that defines the rest of the game. } 1-0


