1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 { A quieter move order than 3.Nc3 in Game #5: the knight heads to e4 by a different route, but the resulting structure after the central trade ends up identical either way. } 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Ngf6 11. Bf4 e6 12. O-O-O Be7 { The same sequence of trades and developing moves as Game #5, move for move - the two games are, in effect, two different attempts at the same endgame, which makes comparing them directly instructive. } 13. Kb1! { Kasparov's own improvement over the Kramnik-Bareev game above, played just two months earlier: it gets White a better version of the same endgame by tucking the king away safely before any further trades happen, rather than leaving it on c1 where it could later be bothered by a check or a tempo-gaining tactic once the position opens up. A small move, but exactly the kind of prophylaxis that turns a nagging edge into a slightly larger nagging edge. } 13... O-O { Unlike Bareev in Game #5, Black castles immediately here rather than walking the king out to d7 - already a sign this game's defensive technique starts from a sounder footing. } 14. Ne4 { The same simplifying idea as Game #5: trade knights, then trade again, heading toward the structure where White's small, lasting pluses matter most. } 14... Nxe4 15. Qxe4 Nf6 { Gains a tempo on the queen while completing development, exactly mirroring the earlier game's move 14. } 16. Qe2 { A small improvement in move order over the earlier game's immediate Qd3 - here the queen keeps d3 open for a future piece rather than committing to the square right away. } 16... Qd5 17. Ne5 { Offers yet another trade while centralizing the knight on its best square, continuing the same pattern of simplification that favors the side with the better long-term structure. } 17... Qe4 18. Qxe4 Nxe4 19. Rhe1 { Immediately contests the e-file rather than letting the knight sit comfortably on e4 for long, a small but real improvement in activity over simply developing another piece passively. } 19... Nf6 20. g4 { Begins expanding on the kingside exactly as in Game #5 - the same structural plan, the same small space-gaining idea, now with the extra tempo 13.Kb1 bought along the way. } 20... Rfd8 21. Be3 { Our own check confirms a modest, genuine White edge here (+0.37) - not large, but real, and it is exactly this kind of small, nagging plus that defines the whole rest of the game: nothing forcing, nothing tactical, just a slightly better structure that needs patient technique to convert. } 21... Bd6 { Repositions the bishop toward a more useful diagonal, keeping flexible about the coming queenside play. } 22. f3 { A useful consolidating move, supporting the g4-pawn and keeping the structure solid before any further expansion. } 22... Rac8 23. c4 { Begins the same kind of queenside space-gaining that defines so much of this endgame's character, fixing the structure in White's favor one small step at a time. } 23... a5 24. a4 { Answers in kind, fixing the queenside pawns on both sides and setting up the long maneuvering phase that follows - neither side can easily make further progress on this wing without the pieces doing real work first. } 24... Nd7 { Reroutes the knight to challenge the e5 outpost directly, the natural response to White's centralized piece. } 25. Bd2 { Retreats to keep options open rather than trading the bishop off for the knight immediately - patience, not forcing simplification, remains the theme. } 25... Bc7 26. Bc3 { Returns to a useful diagonal, adding pressure to e5 and g7 both, while continuing to avoid any committal trade before it's clearly favorable. } 26... Nxe5 27. dxe5 { The trade finally happens, and on White's terms: the resulting pawn on e5 cramps Black's position further and gives White's remaining bishop a genuinely strong diagonal. } 27... c5 { A rather curious moment, and the first real decision of the technical phase. Our own check shows both this and the slightly more solid 27...g5 are close to equal here (around +0.2 to +0.35) - Anand keeps the position "natural" rather than fully fortifying the kingside first, but at this exact point the difference between the two tries is genuinely small. The real damage in this game happens much later, not here. } (27... g5 { The slightly more solid alternative: shore up the kingside structure immediately. Our own check has this at essentially the same evaluation as the game continuation (+0.2 to +0.35) - a real option, but not a missed save, since the position was never actually close to lost at this point either way. }) 28. Kc2 Rxd1 29. Kxd1 Rd8+ 30. Ke2 Rd7 31. f4 Bd8 32. f5 Bg5 33. f6 b6 34. Ra1 Rd8 35. Ra3 { Both sides continue maneuvering patiently for another dozen moves, each small improvement adding a fraction to White's edge without ever producing a single decisive tactic - a textbook demonstration that a small structural advantage, nursed correctly for long enough, eventually starts to matter on its own. } 35... gxf6 36. Rb3 Bf4 37. Rxb6 Bxe5 38. Bxe5 fxe5 39. Rb5 Rd4 40. Rxc5 Rxg4 41. b3 Rg3 42. Rxe5 Rxb3 43. Rxa5 { Both sides have by now decimated the opponent's pawn mass; the position has calmed into a sharp rook endgame where Kasparov simply operates more precisely over the coming moves, converting the last traces of the long middlegame edge into concrete, countable progress. } 43... f5 44. Ra8+ Kg7 45. c5 Rc3 46. Rc8 Ra3 47. c6 Rxa4 48. Re8 Rc4 49. Rxe6 f4 50. Rg6+ Kh7 51. Kd3 Rc5 52. Kd4 Rc1 53. Ke4 Rc4+?? { The real losing move of the whole game - not because it hangs anything directly, but because the check simply escorts White's king to the one square it actually wants to reach. Our own check confirms the swing precisely: the quiet 53...Rc5 holds the draw outright (0.06, essentially dead level, just staying put rather than giving a check that achieves nothing useful), while the game's actual 53...Rc4+ lets the evaluation jump all the way to a clearly winning +2.0 for White. The difference between the two moves is purely about where White's king ends up next - and a check that hands the opponent exactly the square they wanted is often worse than no check at all. } (53... Rc5 { The move that actually holds the draw: stay put, keep the rook where it already restrains the king and the pawn both, and don't volunteer a tempo that only helps White. Essentially dead level (0.06). }) 54. Kd5 Rc3 55. Kd4 Rc1 56. Ke4 Rc4+ 57. Kf3 Kh8 58. Rxh6+ Kg7 59. Rd6 Kh7 60. Kg4 Kg7 61. Rd7+ Kf6 62. c7 { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: like the Kramnik-Bareev game above, this follows the older 10...Ngf6 move order rather than the course's preferred 10...e6 first; the resulting endgame is one of the Classical Variation's most instructive precisely because it shows the same structure handled slightly better by both sides. The engine's read on the position shifted gradually through the whole middlegame - a modest, nagging White edge from as early as move 21 (+0.37, confirmed by direct engine check) that Kasparov never let go of through two dozen more moves of patient maneuvering and simplification, remaining only a small, technical plus even as late as move 27 (+0.2/+0.35 either way Black chose to continue) - before tipping decisively into a clearly won rook endgame on a single move near the very end. The real turning point, the moment that actually decided it, was not 27...c5 (which the engine shows was never really a mistake at all) but 53...Rc4+??, which let a drawn rook endgame slip away for good by escorting White's own king to exactly the square it needed. } 1-0


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Instructive Game #7 Hulshof - l'Ami"]
