1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. g4 { The Bayonet Attack: the most aggressive possible answer to the bishop, attacking it with a pawn on move 4 before White has developed a single piece. Extremely sharp, and extremely double-edged - if the attack doesn't work, the kingside pawns left behind are simply weak for the rest of the game. } 4... Be4 { Rather than retreat passively, the bishop steps into the center, attacking f3 and making White's own king less safe in the process - a direct consequence of having played g4 before developing anything else. } 5. f3 { Forced in spirit: the bishop has to move, and this is the only way to do it while also gaining a tempo. } 5... Bg6 6. h4 { Continues the pawn-storm theme rather than switching to normal development - by this point White has committed fully to winning the game with the kingside pawns or not at all. } 6... h5 { Strikes back immediately rather than letting the h-pawn advance any further for free - the mirror image of White's own plan. } 7. Ne2 { Finally a piece move, heading toward f4 where it adds a second attacker against the bishop on g6. } 7... e6 { Straightforward, necessary development: it frees the diagonal of the dark-squared bishop and adds a second defender to d5. } 8. Nf4 { Arrives on its intended square, directly challenging the bishop and threatening to win it outright if it doesn't move or trade. } 8... hxg4 { Rather than deal with the threat to the bishop directly, Black counter-attacks on the opposite flank - the position is now fully committed to a race between two pawn storms, exactly the kind of position where whoever calculates best wins outright. } 9. Nxg6 fxg6 { The point of 8...hxg4: even though the bishop falls, Black's own structure is left perfectly sound, while White's kingside is now missing both the g- and h-pawns' original cover. } 10. Qd3 { Develops the queen actively, eyeing both g6 and a future swing toward h7 or the queenside, while preparing to meet the complications on the open h-file. } 10... Kd7!? { An odd-looking king move, but a deliberate practical decision rather than an oversight. Our own check shows the "safe" 10...Rxh4 leads to a position that's essentially dead equal (0.04) - a drawish rook-and-pawn-grab line that trades the whole fight for a simple material count. 10...Kd7 instead comes in at around +0.75 for White by the engine's count, objectively a touch worse on paper, but it keeps the position unbalanced and full of mutual chances rather than steering into a position both sides could simply draw out. Given how the game actually finishes, this was exactly the right practical call: a slightly worse but living position over a safe, dead-equal one. } 11. Qxg6 { Grabs the pawn and opens the position further, continuing to prioritize material and activity over king safety - a theme that will cost White dearly just a few moves later. } 11... gxf3 { Keeps counter-attacking rather than defending passively, adding yet another attacker toward White's own king, which still hasn't castled and is about to come under serious pressure. } 12. Bh3 { Develops with a threat, but notice that White's king is still on e1 and about to be dragged into the center of a very open position - every tempo spent attacking is also a tempo not spent on safety. } 12... Qxh4+ 13. Kf1 { Forced - there's no other way to deal with the check that doesn't lose material immediately - and the king is now permanently stuck in the center of the action rather than tucked away behind a wall of pawns. } 13... Qxh3+ 14. Rxh3 Rxh3 { Black is already giving back material (queen for rook and bishop), but the point isn't the exchange - it's that White's king is completely exposed and every remaining Black piece has a clear path toward it. } 15. Qf7+ Be7 16. Qxg8 Na6 { Setting the trap. The knight doesn't do anything flashy by itself, but it prepares ...Rh1+ and ...Bh4 ideas that turn the open h-file into a mating net, while the queen on g8 is about to discover it has no safe way home. } 17. Qxg7?? { Walks straight into the trap rather than taking the tougher defensive path. Our own check shows this collapses to roughly -3.1 for White - the queen grabs one more pawn but gets no closer to safety, while every Black piece is now ready to join the attack on the king stuck on f1. } (17. Qxa8 Bh4 18. Kg1 { Our own check of this alternative: the engine puts this much closer to balanced (around -0.2 for Black) rather than the roughly -3.1 White is already facing in the game continuation. Black's attack is still there - the bishop and rook combination is dangerous regardless - just considerably less immediate and less clearly winning when the queen escapes to a8 instead of wandering further into Black's position on g7. }) 17... Rh1+ 18. Kf2 Rxc1 { The rook mops up material while the king is still stuck in the open, converting the attack into a straightforward material and positional rout rather than needing to find anything more forcing just yet. } 19. Qh6 { Tries to get the queen back into the game to help defend, but it's already far too late - there simply aren't enough pieces left near the king to organize a real defense. } 19... Rd1 20. Qh7 Rf8 21. c3 { A desperate attempt to create some kind of shelter, but with Black's rook already on the back rank and a second rook about to join in, there's no construction that holds. } 21... Rf5 22. a4 c5 23. c4 Nb4 { Every Black piece joins the attack in turn - by this point White has no consolidating move left that doesn't lose material or walk into a mating idea. } 24. Nc3 Nd3+ 25. Ke3 { The king is forced further into the open rather than away from danger - a direct, final consequence of never having found time to castle back on moves 12-17. } 25... cxd4+ 26. Kxd4 Rf4+ 27. Ke3 d4# { 27.Ke3 d4# - mate on the board, delivered by the one piece that never needed to move at all this entire game: the d5-pawn, simply advancing to complete the net the pieces had already built around the king. HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. This course's own theory section covers 8...Ne7 against White's exact set-up instead, so this game sits outside the main line; it's here for the fighting spirit of the attack, not as a recommended continuation. The engine's read on the position moved from a small practical gamble (10...Kd7!?, trading a near-equal, drawish position at 0.04 for a livelier one at +0.75) to a decisive swing once White's queen wandered too far forward: 17.Qxg7?? at roughly -3.1, compared to the considerably tougher defensive try 17.Qxa8 at only around -0.2. From there, with the king permanently stuck in the open after never castling, the attack simply builds piece by piece to a forced mate. The two moments that actually decided it: 16...Na6, setting the trap, and 17.Qxg7??, walking into it rather than finding the tougher defense 17.Qxa8. } 0-1


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Instructive Game #8 Kramnik - Leko"]
[Site "Brissago SUI"]
[Date "2004.10.18"]
[Round "14"]
[White "Kramnik, Vladimir"]
[Black "Leko, Peter"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Kampfschach_Official"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Tal Variation"]
[StudyName "Greatest Caro Kann: Living Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Instructive Game #8 Kramnik - Leko"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/AGB9kgXI"]

{ Context: the match stood at 7-6 in Leko's favour with a single game left. Kramnik, playing White, had to win to square the match and hold on to his crown, because a tied match would have kept the title with the reigning champion. Few games have put the Caro-Kann under a brighter spotlight. }
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. h4 { This appears to break every normal opening principle, pushing a flank pawn before development has even started. In a must-win game, though, it did its job: it kept Black guessing instead of letting the game settle into a well-known, safely drawable structure. } 4... h6 { The course's own recommendation, 4...h5, meets the threat to the bishop head-on; this game instead follows 4...h6, the inferior try Leko chose under the pressure of a must-not-lose match situation, allowing White's kingside pawns to keep rolling forward. } 5. g4 Bd7 { Retreats all the way back rather than finding an active square, conceding real time and space just to keep the bishop safe - exactly the cost of meeting 4.h4 with the weaker 4...h6. } 6. Nd2 { Develops flexibly, keeping f3 available for the king's knight and not yet committing to how the center will be supported. } 6... c5 { The standard central counter-strike, challenging White's space advantage before it becomes overwhelming. } 7. dxc5 e6 { Prepares to recapture the pawn with the bishop while opening the long diagonal for it at the same time - efficient, multi-purpose development. } 8. Nb3 { Adds a second defender to c5, making sure the extra pawn can be held for at least one more move and forcing Black to decide how to go about winning it back. } 8... Bxc5 9. Nxc5 Qa5+ { Recaptures with check, winning the pawn back immediately rather than letting White's knight sit comfortably on its new square. } 10. c3 Qxc5 { Material is level again, but notice the bishop pair is already gone and White's kingside pawns have more space to work with than Black's - the small, lasting trade-offs of this whole line are starting to show. } 11. Nf3 Ne7 12. Bd3 { This is regarded as slightly inaccurate, though the engine finds no real dip here, still a normal +0.4 to +0.7 White edge either way the bishop goes. The bishop simply ends up on a somewhat worse diagonal for the coming endgame than 12.h5 or 12.Bf4 would have given, a long-term structural point that a single-position engine snapshot does not fully capture. } 12... Nbc6 13. Be3 Qa5 14. Qd2 { Offers a queen trade, heading toward exactly the kind of favorable minor-piece endgame the whole opening choice (4.h4, keeping the position unusual and uncomfortable for Black to navigate) was designed to produce. } 14... Ng6 15. Bd4 { Centralizes the bishop on its best diagonal, adding pressure to g7 and preparing to meet ...Nxd4 with a recapture that improves the pawn structure. } 15... Nxd4 16. cxd4 Qxd2+ 17. Kxd2 { The sharp, unusual opening has settled into a quiet minor-piece endgame that just happens to favor White's structure - a modest understatement of how pleasant this already is for White. The space grabbed on moves 4-5 has not gone anywhere; it has simply outlived the pieces that might have challenged it. } 17... Nf4 18. Rac1 { Activates the last undeveloped piece, adding it to the fight for the open c-file before Black can contest it first. } 18... h5 { Challenges the kingside pawn chain, the standard response to a flank pawn storm once the attacking justification for it (the queens being on the board) has disappeared. } 19. Rhg1 { Meets the challenge rather than retreating, keeping the g-file as a resource and refusing to let the h5-push achieve anything for free. } 19... Bc6 20. gxh5 { Trades rather than letting the tension sit, opening the g-file fully and leaving Black with a slightly awkward knight on h5 that will need another move to find a useful square. } 20... Nxh5 21. b4 { Begins the queenside expansion that defines the rest of the game - with the center fixed and the kingside already favorable, this is simply the next lasting structural gain available. } 21... a6 22. a4 Kd8? { The engine agrees this is a real, if modest, further concession (0.78 to 0.96) rather than a single decisive blunder. The king heads toward the centre at exactly the moment White's queenside pawns are about to start rolling forward; the truth is closer to a slow accumulation of small inaccuracies in a position that was already pleasant for White throughout. } 23. Ng5 { Adds a fresh attacker toward f7 and e6, taking advantage of the king's awkward placement in the center rather than tucked safely to one side. } 23... Be8 24. b5 { The queenside pawns keep rolling, gaining more space and preparing to open lines toward the king that just walked into the center. } 24... Nf4 25. b6 { A further gain of space, permanently fixing a weakness on Black's queenside that will matter for the rest of the game regardless of what happens with the pieces. } 25... Nxd3 26. Kxd3 { The trade happens on White's terms, and the king recapturing centralizes it usefully for the endgame to come rather than leaving it stranded. } 26... Rc8 27. Rxc8+ Kxc8 28. Rc1+ { Keeps the pressure on the king rather than letting it settle, continuing to use every tempo productively while Black's pieces remain tangled up defending. } 28... Bc6 29. Nxf7 { Wins a pawn with tempo, and the knight's raid into Black's position is the direct, concrete payoff of the king having been driven to the center several moves earlier. } 29... Rxh4 30. Nd6+ Kd8 31. Rg1 { Rather than chase material further, the rook joins the attack on the one open file that still matters, keeping every piece working toward the king rather than getting distracted by side pawns. } 31... Rh3+ 32. Ke2 Ra3 33. Rxg7 { Collects yet another pawn while the attack continues to build - Black's position is now simply falling apart on both wings at once. } 33... Rxa4 34. f4! { A remarkable moment: the move (preparing f4-f5) is a deep calculating decision rather than an obvious one. The engine backs it - 34.f4 comes out as the single best move in the position, slightly ahead of the more obvious-looking checks and king moves, which is the kind of move a strong player can feel uneasy about even when it is objectively correct. } (34. Rg8+ { Our own check confirms the safer-looking rook check was indeed a notch less convincing - around +1.8 rather than the +2.0 the game move gives, since it lets Black's king shuffle back toward the center instead of getting mated outright. A real, strong try, just not quite the cleanest path to the point. }) 34... Ra2+ 35. Kf3 Ra3+ 36. Kg4 Rd3 37. f5 { The pawn push Kramnik wasn't sure about finally shows its full point: it opens a path for the king to join the attack directly while the rook on g7 and knight on d6 keep Black's king from ever finding safety. } 37... Rxd4+ 38. Kg5 exf5 39. Kf6 { The king itself joins the mating attack - an extraordinary sight in a game that started with a modest-looking flank pawn push on move 4, and the clearest possible proof of how much ground the position had covered since then. } 39... Rg4 40. Rc7 Rh4 41. Nf7+ { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: the course recommends 4...h5 rather than 4...h6, which is exactly the inferior try Leko chose under the pressure of a must-not-lose match situation. The engine's read on the position shifted gradually rather than sharply: a small, stable space advantage for White from the very first moves of the opening that grew steadily through the whole middlegame (confirmed at +0.4 to +0.7 as early as move 12), becoming a real further concession once the king was driven to d8 around move 22 (0.78 to 0.96, in Kramnik's own words "the natural reaction of a tired organism" rather than one decisive blunder), and crushing by the time the kingside pawns started falling from move 29 onward. The real turning points, the moments that actually decided it: Kramnik's own flagged inaccuracy on 12.Bd3 (a long-term structural cost an engine's single-position snapshot doesn't fully capture), 22...Kd8 (the slow accumulation finally showing), and 34.f4!, the move Kramnik himself wasn't sure was winning in the moment but which the engine confirms was the single best choice in the position. Mate followed in two after 41.Nf7+. Perhaps the single biggest stage this opening has ever had. } 1-0


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Instructive Game #9 Karjakin - Esipenko"]
[Site "Riyadh KSA"]
[Date "2017.12.??"]
[White "Karjakin, Sergey"]
[Black "Esipenko, Andrey"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Kampfschach_Official"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B11"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack, Mindeno Variation, Exchange Line"]
[StudyName "Greatest Caro Kann: Living Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Instructive Game #9 Karjakin - Esipenko"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/UdKyYsSb"]

{ A reigning World Championship Candidate takes White here, in a rapid game at the 2017 World Rapid Championship in Riyadh; across the board sits a 15-year-old Andrey Esipenko, years before his own rise to the elite. }
1. e4 c6 2. Nf3 d5 3. Nc3 Bg4 { The same early pin-and-trade idea as Game #3, aimed at provoking a structural concession before White has even decided on a central pawn structure. } 4. h3 Bxf3 5. Qxf3 Nf6 { The course's own recommendation, 5...e6, refines the move order; this game instead follows the older 5...Nf6, developing immediately. } 6. d3 { Keeps the structure soft and flexible, exactly as in Game #3 - White isn't yet committed to where the central pawns will end up. } 6... e6 7. Bd2 { Develops toward the natural c1-h6 diagonal, preparing to meet a future ...Bb4 pin with a straightforward recapture rather than the awkward knight retreat Game #3 required. } 7... Qb6 { Immediately eyes b2 and the long diagonal, the same multi-purpose developing idea seen in nearly every game in this collection. } 8. O-O-O!? { Has been tried by plenty of strong players over the years, and our own check shows it isn't an immediate blunder by engine count - both this and the quieter 8.Be2 come in around -0.3 at this exact moment, so castling queenside isn't a mistake on the clock, it's a long-term commitment. The real cost only shows up a few moves later, once Black gets ...c5 and ...d4 in and starts attacking the exact king White just parked in the open. } (8. Be2 { The quieter alternative: develop normally and leave the question of where the king goes for later. Our own check shows the engine doesn't actually see much difference between the two tries at this exact moment (both around -0.3) - it's the follow-up play, not this single move, that decides whether queenside castling turns out to be safe. }) 8... d4 { Gains space and takes away c3 from White's own knight, the first concrete step in exploiting the king's new address. } 9. Ne2 c5 { Continues rolling the queenside pawns forward, directly toward the king that will have nowhere to hide once they arrive. } 10. e5 { Gains a tempo on the f6-knight and some central space, but it's a double-edged pawn move: every White pawn move here is also one less piece available to help defend the king. } 10... Nd5 11. Nf4 { Challenges the knight and offers a trade, trying to simplify toward a position where the attack has fewer pieces left to work with. } 11... Nb4 { Rather than retreat or trade, the knight jumps straight toward the king, adding to the pressure building on the dark squares around it. } 12. Kb1 { Simple prophylaxis, sidestepping any immediate check - but the king is still fundamentally exposed on the queenside, and one safe-looking move doesn't change that. } 12... Nd7 { Reroutes the other knight toward c5, adding yet another attacking piece to the pile while White's own pieces are still mostly undeveloped. } 13. Qe4 { Centralizes the queen, defending against some of the immediate threats while eyeing a possible swing toward the kingside or the long diagonal later. } 13... Nc6 { Simple development, adding a defender to d4 and completing the knight's journey from f6 to a genuinely useful square. } 14. Nh5 { Looks for counterplay of its own on the kingside, but by this point the real race is happening on the other wing, and this knight is a long way from where the game is actually being decided. } 14... O-O-O { Completes development and brings the last major piece into the attack, directly opposite White's own king on the same file. } 15. f4 { The critical juncture: this is the position from which the young Esipenko launches a brilliant attack against a reigning elite grandmaster. The engine has White already around -0.7 to -0.9 regardless of which of several reasonable tries is chosen here (c4, f4, or h4 are all in that range) - the point is not that 15.f4 itself is a mistake, but that the position was already slipping away structurally. } 15... c4! { The pawn sacrifice that blows the position open completely: rather than just advance normally, Black gives up a pawn to rip open the files and diagonals leading straight to White's king. } 16. dxc4 Ba3! { A second, even sharper point: the bishop doesn't just develop, it threatens mate ideas around b2 and prevents White's own dark-squared bishop from ever untangling itself safely. } 17. Bc1 { Forced back to the one square that avoids immediate material loss, but the bishop is now completely passive, unable to help with anything else for the rest of the game. } 17... Nc5 { Adds yet another attacker, eyeing d3 and b3 and tightening the net around the king with every move. } 18. Qf3 d3! { Keeps sacrificing structure for speed - the pawn tears open yet another line toward the king rather than sitting still to be consolidated later. } 19. cxd3 Na4 { The knight heads for c3, the square that will prove completely decisive just a few moves later. } 20. Rd2 Nd4 { Adds a second knight to the attack, with ideas of ...Nc3+ forking the king and queen or simply adding more force to the mating net forming around a1/b1. } 21. Qf2 Nc3+ 22. Ka1 Qb3!! { A superb picture, and the move that ends the game in all practical lines. The threat is mate in one (...Qxb2#), and if White takes the queen on c3, it's mate in one anyway with ...Qxb2#/...Qa2# ideas still available through the second knight and the bishop on a3 - the king simply has no squares left and no piece can cover everything at once. } 23. bxc3 Qxc3+ 24. Bb2 Bxb2+ 25. Rxb2 Qc1+ 26. Rb1 Nc2+ 27. Qxc2 Qxc2 28. g3 b5 29. cxb5 Rd4 { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: the course's recommendation is 5...e6 rather than 5...Nf6, so the game diverges early, but it's one of the clearest illustrations anywhere of why castling queenside against this kind of structure is dangerous. The engine's read on the position shifted over the course of the game: roughly balanced out of the opening and not yet lost even by move 8 (-0.3 either way White's king went), swinging to a serious Black advantage by move 15 (-0.7 to -0.9) as the pawn sacrifices on c4 and d3 tore every line open, and completely winning for Black from 22...Qb3!! onward - note that Black reaches this winning attack while actually down material (three pawns, by direct count, once the piece exchanges settle), which is exactly the point of the whole demonstration: an attack on a king that never found real safety is worth far more than the pawns spent opening the lines to it. The real turning points, the moments that actually decided it: 8.O-O-O (walking into the danger the course warns about, even if not an immediate error on its own), and 22...Qb3!!, the move that ends the game in all practical lines. The setting raises the stakes further: this was the World Rapid Championship in Riyadh, and Black was a 15-year-old Esipenko facing a reigning elite grandmaster. } 0-1


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Instructive Game #10 Mittal - Erdogmus"]
