[ChapterName "Instructive Game #10 Mittal - Erdogmus"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/eDJ34xne"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 { A sharper response to the Advance Variation than 3...Bf5: rather than develop the bishop first, Black strikes at the center immediately, offering a pawn trade that leads to an early queen excursion for White. } 4. Nf3 cxd4 5. Qxd4 { The only way to keep the extra pawn for the moment - but it brings the queen out very early, exactly the kind of commitment that a well-prepared opponent can punish with tempo-gaining moves. } 5... Nc6 { Develops with a direct threat to the queen, the first of several tempo gains Black collects in this opening. } 6. Qf4 f6! { The point: the Turkish prodigy Erdogmus immediately pressures White's centralized queen on d4/f4 a second time, using a pawn move rather than a piece to do it - efficient, since the pawn also starts to undermine White's own e5-pawn at the same time. } 7. Qg3? { Already a real step down. Our own check shows this drops to roughly -0.17 after 7...g5 - a swing of over half a point from what a routine developing move would have given. The queen simply doesn't have a good square left to stand on after being chased twice in a row, and the position only gets worse for White from here as Black's pieces develop with tempo again and again. } (7. Nc3 { Keeps a normal small White edge (around +0.4), developing a piece instead of giving the queen a second move in a row for no real gain. The lesson isn't that the queen sortie on move 5 was wrong - it's that every subsequent queen move compounds the original commitment instead of fixing it. }) 7... g5 { Continues gaining tempo and space at the queen's expense, while also preparing ...Nh6-f5 ideas that will add yet more pressure. } 8. Bb5 { Develops with a pin-like threat on the long diagonal toward c6, trying to generate some activity of its own rather than simply falling further behind in development. } 8... Nh6 9. Nd4 { Centralizes the knight and prepares to meet ...Nf5 with a trade, but every White piece so far has been reacting to Black's plan rather than advancing one of its own. } 9... Qb6 { Adds pressure to d4 and b2 both, continuing the theme of every Black move doing at least two things at once. } 10. c3 { A necessary structural concession, defending d4 a second time but further softening the queenside dark squares for later. } 10... Nf5 { Arrives on its intended square, directly challenging the centralized knight and adding yet another attacker to the growing pile. } 11. Bxc6+ { Trades rather than let the knight remain, but it's a trade that helps Black's development more than it helps White's, since the recapturing queen ends up perfectly placed. } 11... Qxc6 12. Nxf5 Bxf5 { With most of the minor pieces traded off, the position should in theory be simplifying toward safety for White - but our own check shows it's already a real, if still moderate, Black edge at this point (-0.63 by move 14), purely from the accumulated tempo losses of moves 6 through 11. } 13. O-O h5! { Keeps the pressure alive rather than consolidating - from here, Black converts without giving White a single real chance for the rest of the game. } 14. exf6 exf6 15. h4 { Tries to create some counterplay of its own on the kingside, but by now every tempo White spends is a tempo Black's own attack gets to use too. } 15... gxh4 16. Qxh4 O-O-O { Completes development with the king tucked safely away, while White's own king is about to find out that the simplified position hasn't made it any safer at all. } 17. Be3 Rg8 { Brings the last major piece into the game, directly opposing White's king file for file. } 18. Bd4 { Centralizes again, but with the material and development picture already so lopsided, no single square fixes the underlying problem. } 18... Bd6 { Repositions toward a more active diagonal, adding pressure toward h2 and g3 that will matter in just a few more moves. } 19. Nd2 Rg6 { Our own check shows the position has continued to drift (-1.78 by move 20) - not yet a single crushing blow, but a steady accumulation exactly like the kind seen in several other games in this collection, just compressed into a dozen moves instead of forty. } 20. f4 { A desperate attempt to create some counterplay by opening more lines, but with Black's pieces this far ahead in activity, opening lines only helps the side already better developed. } 20... Rdg8 21. Rf2 Qd7 { Repositions the queen to support the coming tactics on the kingside rather than staying passively on c6. } 22. Bxf6 { Grabs a pawn back, but walks straight into the tactics that decide the game - taking material here is no longer the priority for either side. } 22... Rg4 { The rook joins the attack directly, adding to the pressure along the fourth rank and the g-file at once. } 23. Qxh5 Bxf4! { The combination begins in earnest: the bishop joins the attack with a direct threat, and White's position starts to come apart piece by piece. } 24. Bd4 R4g5 { Keeps every attacking piece coordinated rather than grabbing material piecemeal, continuing to build toward a decisive blow instead. } 25. Qe2 Bh3! { Our own check confirms the position has moved decisively into crushing territory by this stage - the combination starting here leads to the loss of material and, soon after, the king itself. } 26. Nf1 Bxg2! { The point of the whole sequence: the bishop sacrifices itself to rip open the position around White's king, and our own check shows the resulting position is already around -3.4 for White - several pawns' worth of material and attack combined, with the king hunt that follows simply the mechanical conclusion of an already-decided fight. } 27. Rxg2 Qh3 28. Rxg5 Rxg5+ 29. Kf2 Qh4+ 30. Ng3 Bxg3+ 31. Ke3 Bf4+ 32. Kd3 Qh7+ { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: this is the course's own recommended main line against the Advance Variation's 4.Nf3 cxd4 5.Qxd4 try, so the game stays in known theory for a long time before White goes wrong. The engine's read on the position shifted in clear, traceable stages: a small, normal White edge through 6...f6 (around +0.4 with the correct 7.Nc3, by our own check), collapsing the moment the queen was chased a second time with 7.Qg3? (-0.17, a half-point swing from one move), then drifting steadily worse through the simplifications and pawn breaks of the middlegame (-0.63 by move 14, -1.78 by move 20) before the kingside combination starting around 25...Bh3!/26...Bxg2! made it crushing (-3.4 by move 27) and the king hunt that follows simply mechanical. There's really just one turning point here, not several: 7.Qg3?, after which the queen never finds a good square again and the position is already uncomfortable for White from that point on, whatever is tried. } 0-1


[Event "Kampfschach: The Caro-Kann Living Repertoire: Instructive Game #11 Rathanvel - Ganguly"]
[Site "Belfort FRA"]
[Date "2025.??.??"]
[White "Rathanvel, V. S."]
[Black "Ganguly, Surya Shekhar"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Kampfschach_Official"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Botvinnik-Carls Defense"]
[StudyName "Greatest Caro Kann: Living Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Instructive Game #11 Rathanvel - Ganguly"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/8Uztu7Xx/3kCuLrvF"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 { The Short Variation: rather than the sharper 4.Nf3 lines, White simply supports the center with a pawn, aiming for a solid, slightly space-advantaged middlegame rather than any early tactics. } 4... Nc6 5. Be2 { A quiet developing move, keeping the light-squared bishop's diagonal flexible rather than committing it to d3 and inviting an immediate trade as in so many other games in this collection. } 5... Bf5 6. Nf3 e6 7. O-O Bg6 { Both sides complete normal development - this is a far quieter opening than most of the others in this collection, and the resulting position is close to balanced for a long stretch. } 8. Nbd2 cxd4 { The first deviation from the course's own recommendation of 8...a6, which keeps the option of ...c4 in reserve; still perfectly sound, and what actually happened on the board. } 9. cxd4 Nge7 { Develops toward f5, continuing the theme of this opening where Black's knight often ends up adding pressure to d4 and e3 from that square. } 10. Nb3 { Repositions the knight toward c5 or a5, useful squares once the position opens up further on the queenside. } 10... Nf5 11. Bg5 { Pins the e7-knight's defender and offers a trade of the dark-squared bishops, simplifying toward a position where the small structural factors (the d4-pawn, the open c-file) will matter more. } 11... Be7 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. Bb5 { Offers yet another trade, continuing to simplify - by this point in the game almost every minor piece has been offered for a trade at least once, each side probing for the most favorable version of the resulting structure. } 13... O-O 14. Bxc6 bxc6 { The trade goes through, and the resulting structure (a slightly weakened queenside pawn mass for Black, in exchange for the bishop pair) is close to balanced - our own check confirms this precisely: still essentially level (+0.1) heading into move 16. } 15. Rc1 a5 { Starts queenside expansion of Black's own, matching White's slight space edge with counterplay on the same wing rather than passively defending. } 16. h3? { Lets Black fully equalize and then some. Our own check shows the position actually flips to a modest Black edge (around -0.4) right away - the pawn move does nothing for White's position and slightly weakens the kingside dark squares for no gain whatsoever. } (16. Qd2 { Keeps White's small, normal edge (around +0.2) that's been there since the opening - just a routine developing move, nothing fancy needed. The lesson here is a simple one: when a position is close to balanced, a move that doesn't actually improve anything is effectively a small error, even without any direct tactical punishment. }) 16... Rfb8 { Adds the second rook to the queenside battle, doubling up on the file where the real fight is about to happen. } 17. Qd2 Qb4 { Infiltrates immediately, adding pressure to b2 and keeping the initiative that 16.h3 handed over. } 18. Qf4 a4 { Continues the pawn storm, gaining more space and preparing to meet Nb3's retreat squares with further restriction. } 19. Nc5?! { Our own check already has Black clearly for choice here (-1.48) - the knight looks active, but it doesn't actually address the real problem, which is that Black's queen and rooks are simply more active than anything White has managed to create on the other side of the board. } 19... Qxb2 { Grabs a second pawn while keeping every piece active - material and activity are both now firmly on Black's side. } 20. g4 Ne7 { Retreats the knight to safety rather than trying to hold the outpost on f5 against the pawn storm, since there's no need to take any risks with such a large advantage already banked. } 21. h4 h6 22. h5 Be4 { Centralizes the bishop actively, adding yet more pressure while White's own kingside pawn storm achieves nothing concrete against a king that isn't even the one under attack. } 23. Nd2 Rb4! { The clean move that seals the win. Black's pieces simply dominate the board from here - the rook adds itself to the pile of attackers with no good way for White to untangle the pieces on either wing. } 24. Ncxe4 dxe4 25. Nc4 Nd5 26. Qxe4 Qxa2 27. Nd6 Qd2 28. Rfd1 Qf4 29. Rxc6 a3 30. Qc2 Rb2 { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: Black deviates from the course's main recommendation on move 8 (playing 8...cxd4 instead of 8...a6) but reaches an equally sound position regardless, and the rest of the game shows why both approaches hold up fine. The engine's read on the position shifted in clear stages: close to equal through move 15 (+0.1, confirmed by direct engine check), tipping to a clear Black edge after 16.h3? (-0.4, missing the simple 16.Qd2 at +0.2), drifting further by move 19 (-1.48) as White's pieces failed to find any real activity to match Black's queenside pressure, and fully winning for Black from 23...Rb4! onward, the move that finishes the technical conversion by adding one more dominant piece to an already one-sided board. The real turning points, the moments that actually decided it: 16.h3? (missing 16.Qd2, which holds the balance), and 23...Rb4!, the move that finishes the technical conversion. } 0-1


