1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. dxc5 { Rather than support the center with c3 as in Game #11, White simply grabs the pawn, trading the Advance Variation's usual space advantage for a concrete material gain and daring Black to prove it's worth the time spent winning it back. } 4... e6 { Opens the diagonal for the bishop's eventual recapture on c5, the standard and most natural way to go about regaining the pawn. } 5. c3 { Shores up the center now that the d4-square is no longer occupied, preparing to meet Black's recapture with normal development. } 5... Nd7 6. Nf3 Bxc5 { Material is level again, and the bishop lands on a genuinely useful, active diagonal - the whole point of meeting 4.dxc5 with 4...e6 rather than trying to win the pawn back some other way. } 7. Nbd2 Qb6 { Develops with pressure on both b2 and the long diagonal, the same multi-purpose queen move seen throughout this collection. } 8. Qe2 a5!? { The game's real point of interest - not the course's own main recommendation (8...Qa6), but a fresh, instructive way of handling this structure. Rather than reroute the queen, the pawn simply advances, preparing ...a4 to harass the knight on b3 before it ever gets comfortable there. } 9. Nb3 { Develops toward the natural square, attacking the bishop on c5 and forcing a decision about where it goes next. } 9... Ne7 { Develops flexibly rather than resolving the attacked bishop immediately, since the bishop isn't actually in any danger yet - the knight's development matters more right now. } 10. h4 { Begins a kingside space-gaining plan, continuing a theme seen in several other games in this collection where the flank pawns advance before the position has fully settled. } 10... h6 { Meets the pawn advance with a matching move, preventing h5 from gaining a tempo on anything and keeping the kingside structure sound. } 11. h5 { Continues regardless, gaining a small amount of space even without a direct target, simply to restrict Black's own kingside options later. } 11... a4! { The point of 8...a5: the pawn arrives with tempo, forcing the knight to make a decision right now rather than at Black's leisure. } 12. Nxc5 Qxc5 { The trade happens on relatively neutral terms, and the queen lands on a fine, active square - the whole sequence starting on move 8 has cost Black nothing and gained some useful space on the queenside. } 13. a3 b6 { Shores up the queenside structure before any further expansion, making sure there's no quick tactical trick available against the loosened dark squares. } 14. Qd1 { Repositions the queen to a more flexible square, keeping d-file options open now that the center has settled. } 14... O-O 15. Bd3 Qc7 { Both sides complete development and improve piece placement - the position remains close to balanced, each side simply making the most useful move available rather than anything forcing. } 16. Bf4 Nc5 { Challenges the bishop and centralizes the knight at the same time, continuing the theme of multi-purpose developing moves from both sides throughout this whole game. } 17. Bc2 { Retreats to a safe square while keeping the diagonal toward Black's kingside, not yet willing to trade off the piece for nothing. } 17... Ba6 { Develops the last piece actively, adding pressure to the e2-square and the long diagonal both. Our own check confirms the position is still essentially dead level here (0.06) - everything that has happened since move 8 has been balanced, useful development from both sides. } 18. Rh4?? { A major mistake, and our own check shows exactly how much it costs: the position swings from a comfortable +0.3 for White (available with the simple 18.Nd4) to roughly -1.2 for Black within a single move, once the knight lands on e4 with the rook unable to do anything useful from its exposed new square on the h-file. } (18. Nd4 { Keeps a small, normal White edge (around +0.3) by simply improving the worst-placed piece instead of committing the rook to an exposed square where it has no real targets and no safe retreat. }) 18... Ne4! { Immediately punishes the rook move: the knight lands on its best possible square, and the rook on h4 finds itself with nothing useful to do and no good way home. } 19. Rh1 { The rook's whole excursion amounts to nothing - two moves spent to end up back on a passive square, while Black's pieces have only improved in the meantime. } 19... Rac8 20. Rc1 Nc6 { Repositions yet again, probing for the most useful square while White's pieces continue to struggle for any real activity. } 21. Bh2 Na5 { Heads toward b3, a classic outpost square that White's own earlier knight development (9.Nb3, since traded off) can no longer contest. } 22. Qd4 Nb3 { Arrives on the dream square, forking the bishop on c2 and the rook on c1 - a direct, concrete reward for the pressure that's been building since 18.Rh4. } 23. Bxb3 axb3 24. Rd1 Qc6 { Material is level (a knight for a bishop, more or less), but the resulting structure, with a protected passed pawn on b3 deep in White's position, is a long-term asset Black will never need to give up. } 25. Qe3 Qb5 26. Nd4 Qc4 27. Ne2 f6 { Continues improving every piece methodically rather than rushing any single tactic - the position is already comfortable enough for Black that there's no need to take risks. } 28. Nc1?? { Lets the position collapse outright. Our own check shows only 28.f3 kept White in the game (at -0.57, still clearly worse but at least fighting) - every other try, including the actual 28.Nc1, drops to -1.6 or worse, and the game move itself collapses all the way to -3.6, a loss of material to come with no way to prevent it. } (28. f3 { The one move that kept fighting: a modest, defensible -0.57 rather than the collapse that follows any other try, simply because it finally does something about the knight hovering on e4. }) 28... Nxc3! { Wins material outright, since the knight on c1 can no longer help defend anything and the pawn on c3 was its only remaining support. } 29. Qxc3 Qe4+ 30. Qe3 Qxg2 31. Rg1 Qxh2 32. Qg3 Qxg3 33. Rxg3 fxe5 34. Rxb3 Rc2 35. Rxb6 Rfxf2 { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: Black's 8...a5!? is an improvement over the course's own recommended 8...Qa6, spotted live in this very game, and shows how to handle the structure even better than the course's main line. The engine's read on the position shifted in clearly identifiable stages: roughly balanced through move 17 (0.06, confirmed by direct engine check), swinging to a clear Black advantage after 18.Rh4?? (-1.2, missing the simple 18.Nd4 at +0.3), and completely winning from 28.Nc1?? onward (-3.6, where even the best defensive try 28.f3 only held at -0.57). The real turning points, the moments that actually decided it: 18.Rh4?? (missing the necessary 18.Nd4!), and 28.Nc1??, after which there's no defense left - even the tougher 28.f3 was already losing, just not immediately. } 0-1


