1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ exf6 { A different recapture from Games #5 and #6: rather than keep the knight and trade the bishop off on d3 later, Black trades knights immediately and accepts a slightly unusual pawn structure (a doubled-looking f-pawn complex) in exchange for an open position and a free hand with the bishop pair. } 6. c3 { A solid, flexible structural move, supporting a future d4-d5 push and keeping options open about where the light-squared bishop develops. } 6... Bd6 { Develops actively and prepares to meet any future e5 break with ...f5 or similar if ever needed, while also eyeing h2 along the long diagonal. } 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Qc2 { Adds a second defender to d3/h7 ideas and prepares long castling, the same general plan seen against the Classical Variation in Games #5 and #6. } 8... Re8+ { A useful check that gains a small tempo and gets the rook actively placed on the e-file before White's king finds safety. } 9. Ne2 { Blocks the check while developing the last knight, heading toward g3 or staying flexible on e2 for now. } 9... h5 { Starts a kingside pawn advance of Black's own, continuing to generate activity rather than settling for a quiet position. } 10. Be3 Nd7 { Develops the last minor piece, completing the setup before committing to any particular plan on either wing. } 11. O-O-O b5!? { The course's own recommended idea against White's queenside castling plan, played here to full effect: rather than develop quietly, Black immediately attacks the king's new home with a pawn storm of its own, turning the position into a race between two wings. } 12. d5 { Strikes back in the center rather than deal with the pawn storm directly, gaining space and opening lines - but every tempo spent here is also a tempo not spent on king safety. } 12... c5 13. Bxb5 Rb8 { Adds pressure along the b-file immediately, continuing to build the attack rather than worrying about the pawn just given up. } 14. c4 a6 15. Ba4 { Retreats to a relatively safe square while keeping the diagonal, but the bishop is now a long way from the kingside action and from defending the queenside structure too. } 15... Re5 { A flexible, multi-purpose rook lift: it can swing to e3, b5, or stay put to support a later ...Nb6 and ...Bf5 setup, all while adding pressure along the open e-file. } 16. Nc3 { Finally develops the last piece, though by this point Black's attack has a significant head start in tempo. } 16... Nb6 17. Bb3 Bf5 { Develops actively, adding pressure to the long diagonal and preparing to double up on the attack with ...Qc7 and ...Rbe8 next. } 18. Qe2 Bg4 19. f3 { Forced in spirit - the bishop has to be dealt with somehow, and this at least gains a tempo while doing it, though it commits the f-pawn and slightly loosens the king's own shelter. } 19... Qc7 20. Rd2 Rbe8 { Both sides complete their setups, and our own check confirms the position is still essentially balanced here (+0.19) - a genuinely sharp, roughly even battle between two wings of attacking pawns and pieces. } 21. fxg4?! { Walks into trouble. Our own check shows the position swinging to roughly -1.15 for Black in a single move, since the bishop on e3 falls to ...Rxe3 the moment the f-pawn stops guarding it - a direct, concrete tactical cost that the simple alternative avoids entirely. } (21. Nd1 { Retreating the knight keeps a real White edge (around +0.5), since it defends e3 and keeps the structure intact rather than opening the f-file for Black's rook that's already sitting on e5 eyeing exactly that square. }) 21... Rxe3! { The direct point of 21.fxg4?!: the bishop simply falls, since nothing else defends it once the f-pawn moves away. } 22. Qd1 hxg4 23. Qxg4 Be5 { Centralizes the bishop on a commanding diagonal, adding to the pressure while Black is already up material and has the more active pieces on top of it. } 24. Na4 Bf4 25. Kb1 R3e4 { Keeps every piece coordinated and active rather than simplifying prematurely - there's no rush, since the position is already comfortable. } 26. Rd3? { A real further step in the wrong direction. Our own check shows the position was still only around -0.2 with the tougher 26.Nc3! available, but 26.Rd3 lets it slide to roughly -1.2 - not an instant game-ending blunder on its own, but one more concession in a position that no longer has much room for them, and the dark-square weaknesses it leaves behind prove permanent for the rest of the game. } (26. Nc3 { The tougher defensive try: keeps the position to a smaller, more manageable deficit (around -0.2) by not loosening the position any further than it already was. }) 26... Bd6 { Repositions the bishop to a square where it can't be challenged, keeping all the pressure that's been building without giving White any tactical chances in return. } 27. Qd1 Nxc4! { Wins a further pawn cleanly, since the knight on c4 can't be taken without losing even more material to the pin along the fourth rank. } 28. Bxc4 Rxc4 29. Re1 Rxe1 30. Qxe1 Qd7 31. Nc3 Qf5 32. Kc2 Be5 33. Kd2 Bf4+ 34. Re3 Rd4+ { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: this game follows the course's own recommended 11...b5!? move for move, making it a near-perfect illustration of the plan rather than a divergence from it. The engine's read on the position shifted in clear stages rather than one dramatic swing: roughly balanced through move 20 (+0.19, confirmed by direct engine check), swinging decisively to Black after 21.fxg4?! (-1.15, missing the simple 21.Nd1 at +0.5), and comfortably winning from 26.Rd3? onward (a further slide from -0.2 with the tougher 26.Nc3! to -1.2) thanks to White's permanent dark-square weaknesses from that point on. The real turning points, the moments that actually decided it: 21.fxg4?! (missing 21.Nd1, which would have kept a real White edge), and 26.Rd3?, missing the tougher defensive try 26.Nc3!. } 0-1


