1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Qe2 Na6!? { The course's own recommendation, and this game shows exactly why it works: White spent a long time on move 6, a tell that he wasn't ready for it. The knight's move to the rim looks strange, but it sidesteps a pin and keeps open the option of recapturing on d5 with a piece rather than committing the structure too early. } 6. d4 Qd5 { Centralizes immediately, pressuring both the knight on e4 and the pawn on g2, forcing White into a series of trades rather than a quiet continuation. } 7. Nxf6+ gxf6 { Accepts a slightly unusual pawn structure (the doubled f-pawns) in exchange for open lines and the bishop pair - a direct, deliberate trade-off rather than an accident. } 8. Be3 Bg4 { Develops actively with a pin on the long diagonal, adding pressure before White has finished organizing the position. } 9. h3 Bxf3 { Forced in spirit - retreating loses the bishop pair's activity for nothing, so trading it off on the best available square makes more sense. } 10. Qxf3 Qxf3 11. gxf3 { With the queens gone, the position has reached its true character: a dead-level material count but a structure that will reward whichever side maneuvers more patiently from here, since there's no more tactical fireworks left to decide things quickly. } 11... Nb4 { Repositions the oddly-placed knight toward a more useful square, eyeing d3 and c2 and adding some irritation to White's position while the king still hasn't found a permanent home. } 12. Kd2 O-O-O { Completes development with the king tucked safely away, continuing to make the most useful move available in a position where nothing is forced. } 13. c3 Nd5 14. Bd3 e6 15. Rhg1 h5 { Both sides improve methodically rather than committing to anything sharp - this remains a purely structural battle for a long stretch, exactly the kind of position where small, patient improvements matter more than any single tactic. } 16. Rg2 h4 17. a3 Bh6 18. Rag1 f5 { Black's bishop finds an active diagonal and the pawn structure continues to firm up - every move so far has been useful without being forcing. } 19. Ke2 Kd7 { A patient, purely positional squeeze from here: Black improves one small thing at a time - the king toward the center, then the queenside pawns - while White has no active plan to counter it with at all. } 20. Bc4 Ne7 21. a4 Rde8 { Adds the rook to a more useful file, continuing the slow accumulation of small improvements rather than rushing anything. } 22. Bxh6?? { The bluff that backfires. Our own check shows the position was dead equal here (0.00, with the simple 22.f4 keeping things perfectly balanced) - the trade looks like it wins a tempo on the rook, but it actually drops the position to around -0.5 for Black once the recapture happens, since the trade removes White's only genuinely active piece for nothing concrete in return. } (22. f4 { Just pushing the pawn keeps things dead equal (0.00) here - there was no need to go into the bishop trade at all; the position simply didn't call for it. }) 22... Rxh6 { Recaptures and immediately improves: the rook is now more active on h6 than the bishop ever was on h6 for White, and Black's structure has lost nothing in the exchange. } 23. f4 Rg6 24. Rg5 Rh8 25. Ke3 Kd6 26. Kf3 a5 27. R1g2 Rb8 28. Rg1 b5 { The queenside pawns begin their advance exactly as the patient maneuvering since move 19 was always building toward - White's own pieces, by contrast, have spent the same stretch of moves simply shuffling back and forth with no real plan. } 29. axb5 cxb5 30. Be2 a4 { The passed a-pawn begins its run, and by this point White's position is already clearly worse (our own check puts the best defensive tries around -1.5 to -1.75 even several moves before this point) with no real way to generate counterplay anywhere on the board. } 31. Ra1 Rxg5 32. fxg5 Nd5 33. Bd1 Ke7 34. Rc1 Kf8 35. b3?? { The final straw. Our own check shows the position was already difficult even with the best available tries (around -1.5 to -1.75), but 35.b3 lets it slide further still, to roughly -2.4 - one more structural concession in a position that had no room left for any. White's position simply falls apart from here, with the a-pawn and the active knight proving completely decisive. } 35... a3 36. c4 bxc4 37. bxc4 a2 38. Ra1 Nc3 39. Bc2 Rb2 40. Bd3 Rd2 { HOW THIS GAME ACTUALLY WENT. Where the book ends: this game is played move for move in the course's own recommended line, and shows just how easy Black's technical task can be even from a position engines call dead equal (0.00) straight out of the opening. The engine's read on the position shifted gradually rather than sharply: genuinely close to level through the early middlegame despite the engine's 0.00 verdict at move 22, tipping to a real if modest Black edge the moment White traded away the only active piece with 22.Bxh6?? (-0.5, when the simple 22.f4 would have kept things dead equal), and growing steadily worse as Black's patient queenside play (the king march, then the a5-b5-a4 pawn advance) outpaced a White position with no plan of its own, becoming clearly winning well before move 35 and finally collapsing outright after 35.b3?? (-2.4). The real turning points, the moments that actually decided it: 22.Bxh6??, a self-inflicted trade that gave up White's only active piece for nothing, and 35.b3??, after which White's position has no more resources left at all. } 0-1


