1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 { The Exchange Variation: the simplest possible response to the Caro-Kann, trading the center pawns immediately and relying on a tiny structural edge (the extra half-tempo of development) rather than any space advantage. } 4. Bd3 Nf6 { Develops naturally in the perfectly symmetrical pawn structure that results from the exchange - there's very little to fight over yet except piece activity. } 5. c3 Bg4 { Develops actively, pinning the knight's future square and adding pressure before White has finished organizing the position. } 6. Qb3 { A double-purpose move: it defends b2 and eyes b7, forcing Black to spend a tempo on queenside safety rather than just continuing to develop for free. } 6... Qc7 7. h3 Bd7 { Retreats the bishop to a flexible square rather than trading it off, keeping the option of ...Bc6 later on a more useful diagonal. } 8. Nf3 e6 9. O-O Bd6 { Both sides complete normal, healthy development in the symmetrical structure - nothing has gone wrong for either side yet, and the position remains close to balanced. } 10. Re1 Bc6 { The bishop arrives on its intended, more active diagonal, eyeing the long a8-h1 line and adding pressure toward g2 and the kingside. } 11. Qd1 Nbd7 12. Qe2 O-O 13. Nbd2 Nh5 { Continues developing actively, probing for a square (f4 or g3) where the knight can add to the kingside pressure - but this also slightly loosens the king's own defensive cover, which the next move exploits immediately. } 14. Bxh7+! { Starts a genuinely messy, double-edged sequence of sacrifices on both sides - this is where the position gets sharp and hard to calculate for both players. Our own check confirms this is actually the engine's own top choice here (+0.79), ahead of any quiet alternative: the sacrifice isn't a speculative gamble, it's objectively the best try, trading material for a king hunt that's worth more than the piece given up. } 14... Kxh7 15. Ng5+ Kg6 16. Ndf3 Nf4 17. Qc2+ Kf6 18. g3 Ng6 19. Nxf7 { The knight joins the sacrifice, continuing to rip open the king's position rather than consolidating - the whole point of 14.Bxh7+ was always this kind of relentless follow-up, not a one-off trick. } 19... Kxf7 20. Ng5+ Kg8 21. Qxg6 { Collects material back while keeping the king hunt going, and the position remains sharp and roughly balanced in the engine's own count throughout this entire forcing sequence. } 21... Nf6 22. Nxe6 Qd7 23. Bh6 Ne4! { The precise defensive resource: our own check confirms this only restores material equality (0.00) rather than winning outright for Black - 24.Rxe4 is simply forced, and the position stays dead level a move later too. The point isn't that Black wins material here, it's that the king hunt finally runs out of fuel and the position settles back to balanced. } 24. Rxe4 dxe4 25. Ng5 Rf7 26. Qh7+ { Where the course's own theory stops: it covers this exact position up to this move; everything from here is the two players working it out live at the board, which is exactly what makes the rest of the game worth studying in its own right. } 26... Kf8 27. Nxf7 Qxf7 28. d5 { Tries to keep generating threats with the extra material rather than letting the position settle, since any quiet continuation here would simply leave White down material for the earlier sacrifices. } 28... Qxd5 29. Qxg7+ Ke8 30. Qg6+ Kd7 31. Qg7+ Ke8 32. Qg6+ Kd7 33. Qg7+ Be7 { The checks keep coming, but each one that doesn't actually trap the king is really just marking time - the question from here is whether White has one more genuine try left or whether the king simply walks to safety. } 34. c4? { Our own check of this position says White still had a perpetual-check-style resource with 34.Qg4+, which keeps the position only mildly worse (around -0.3) rather than letting Black's king settle for good. Instead 34.c4 drops straight to around -1.7 - a real, engine-confirmed swing of over a full pawn, not just a cosmetic difference - because it does nothing to stop the king from reaching safety on the very next move. } (34. Qg4+ Kc7 { The king has to walk further and White keeps generating checks; our own check still has Black only around -0.3 here, nowhere near the full point the king eventually escapes with in the actual game. }) 34... Qf5 { Consolidates and offers to trade down into a position where the material deficit finally starts to tell, now that the king has real shelter to walk toward. } 35. b4 Ke6 36. Rd1 Bf6 37. Rd6+ Kxd6 38. Qf7 Bd7 39. Bf4+ Kc6 40. b5+ Kc5 { After the smoke clears, Black's king finds safety and the game concludes in Black's favor. The engine's read on the position swung wildly through the forced sequence of sacrifices starting at move 14 (objectively sound for White throughout, at +0.79 when it began) before settling back to dead equal once the precise 23...Ne4! restored material balance. The real turning point is 34.c4?: our own check shows the king hunt was still salvageable with 34.Qg4+ (keeping the deficit to a modest -0.3), and it's only once Black's king reaches genuine safety on the queenside that the position tips decisively in Black's favor. } 0-1


