1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 { Rather than the quiet Exchange Variation of Game #15, White immediately challenges the d5-pawn a second time, transposing into Panov-Botvinnik Attack structures by a different move order - a sharper, more committal choice than simple development. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Nf3 a6!? { The course's own recommended idea: rather than develop normally, the pawn move prepares ...Be6 and ...g6 without worrying about a future Bb5 pin, and keeps the structure flexible about whether Black ends up with an isolated queen's pawn or not. } 7. Bg5 Be6 8. Be2 g6 { Prepares to meet the pin with ...Bg7 and fianchetto the bishop, while also preparing to recapture favorably if White ever trades on f6. } 9. Bxf6 { Resolves the pin by trading rather than retreating, accepting that Black's structure (doubled-looking pawns on f6/f7 after the recapture) will be slightly unusual in exchange for the bishop pair. } 9... exf6 10. O-O Bg7 { Completes development, and our own check confirms the position is close to balanced here (+0.15 for White with the best available quiet moves) - a perfectly normal, roughly even position out of the opening. } 11. Qb3?! { The first wrong turn: the apparent threats on d5/b7 are an illusion, since Black simply answers with ...Na5 in both cases. Our own check shows this drops the position to around -0.31 - a real, if modest, step down from the dead-equal positions available with a quieter move. } 11... O-O 12. cxd5 Na5! { The point: rather than let the queen's threats land, the knight simply counter-attacks it directly, exploiting exactly the tactical flaw 11.Qb3 overlooked. } 13. Qa4 Bxd5 14. Nxd5 Qxd5 { Material is level, but notice Black's queen has landed on a wonderfully active central square while White's own queen is stranded on the rim at a4 - the practical difference in piece activity, not the material count, is what the position is really about from here. } 15. Rac1 Rfc8 16. Rfd1 Bh6 { Both sides complete development and improve piece placement - the position remains close to balanced on the surface, even though Black's pieces are noticeably more comfortable. } 17. Rc3? { Invites the trade Black wants most. Our own check still has the position only around -0.6 right after the forced ...Rxc3 - this isn't yet a losing blunder on its own, it just hands Black the long-term structural edge (doubled c-pawns to target, an open file to dominate) that eventually decides the game many moves later. } (17. Rc5 { Holds the balance at roughly -0.1 - essentially dead equal, a world away from what the game continuation allows. Simply avoiding the trade Black wants keeps the position genuinely level. }) 17... Rxc3 18. bxc3 Rc8 { Takes immediate possession of the one open file, exactly the kind of long-term structural asset 17.Rc3 handed over for nothing. } 19. Qa3 Bf8 { Repositions the bishop to a more flexible diagonal now that its work on h6 (provoking the rook trade) is done. } 20. Qb2 b5 { Continues expanding on the queenside, both gaining space and preparing to meet any future Nd2-b3 ideas with a solid pawn chain already in place. } 21. Nd2 f5 { Grabs some central space of its own while the knight is temporarily on its way to a different square, making sure White's own pieces never get fully comfortable. } 22. Nb3 Nc4 { Centralizes the knight on a dominant square, adding pressure to the c3-pawn and the b2-queen at once. } 23. Qc1 Nb6 { Repositions again, probing for the most useful square while keeping all the structural pressure that's built up since move 17. } 24. Bf3 Qc4 { Infiltrates further, adding pressure along the fourth rank and eyeing the weak c3-pawn directly. } 25. Bb7 Rc7 26. Bxa6?? { Grabs a pawn but drops the bishop next move. Our own check has the damage more modest than it looks, though: around -2.4 after Bxa6 versus roughly -1.6 for White's best try, 26.Nc5 - this is really just the last nail, not the turning point itself, since the position was already clearly lost for White regardless of which bishop move was chosen here. } (26. Nc5 { The relatively best try available, though still clearly losing (-1.6) - by this point in the game, no single move salvages the position; the damage from 17.Rc3? and the accumulated structural weaknesses has already been done. }) 26... Nd5 { Simply traps the misplaced bishop, converting the last move's material grab into a clean material win for Black. } 27. Re1 Qc6 28. Re5 Nxc3 { Wins the long-targeted pawn too, adding to an already decisive material and positional advantage. } 29. Nc5 Ne4 30. Qf1 Nxc5 31. Bxb5 Qf6 { Where the course's own theory ends: the game follows the recommended 6...a6!? line exactly, making it a clean demonstration of the plan rather than a deviation from it. The engine's read on the position moved gradually rather than in one dramatic swing: close to equal through move 16 despite Black's easier practical task (confirmed at +0.15 as early as move 11, before 11.Qb3?! gave that up for -0.31), tipping to a real but containable edge for Black after 17.Rc3? (around -0.6, not yet decisive, where the simple 17.Rc5 would have held dead equal at -0.1), and only becoming clearly lost for White around move 25-26 once the structural weaknesses from that trade finally told (-1.6 even with White's best try, 26.Nc5, and -2.4 after the actual 26.Bxa6??). The real lesson here is less about a single blunder and more about how a small concession - 17.Rc3? instead of the equalizing 17.Rc5! - can compound quietly into a lost position several moves later, with every subsequent White move simply managing an already-worsening situation rather than fixing it. } 0-1


