1. e4 { THANK YOU FOR STUDYING THIS FAR. If you've worked through even a handful of these 64 chapters, you now have something most club players never bother to build: a real, specific answer to every serious try White has against 1...c6, not a vague sense that the Caro-Kann is 'solid.' That's the whole point of this repertoire. Whatever White plays next time, you should recognise it, know the chapter it belongs to, and know the plan, not just the first few moves. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] } 1... c6 { HOW TO ACTUALLY KEEP THIS WITH YOU. Nobody holds 64 chapters in working memory, and this course was never built on the assumption that you would. The Scaffolding Guide exists for exactly this: a fast, no-branching skeleton to re-ground yourself in before a game, not a replacement for the deep chapters. The 522 WATCH OUT notes scattered through the tree are worth a second pass on their own, specifically, since recognising a trap once in a chapter and recognising it cold at the board under time pressure are two different skills. Revisit the chapters you actually face most often (Advance and Exchange, for most club players) more than the rare sidelines, and let real games, not more reading, be what turns familiarity into fluency. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 2. d4 { THIS COURSE WILL KEEP CHANGING, AND THAT'S THE POINT. Living Repertoire isn't a slogan attached after the fact, it's a description of how this specific course is maintained: verified, re-checked, and extended in rounds, with the record of that process kept rather than hidden. If you find a line that looks wrong, a position where the engine disagrees with the written verdict, or theory that's moved since this was last checked, that's not a flaw in the premise, it's exactly the kind of thing a living document is supposed to absorb. This version is not the last version. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2... d5 { A FEW HONEST LIMITS, STATED ONE LAST TIME. This repertoire is deep, not infinite. The trap scan covers captures and checks, a genuinely bad but quiet move (wrong plan, misplaced piece) can still exist without being flagged. Branch inclusion was decided by real engine verification and cross-source corroboration, not by playing out every conceivable transposition. None of that makes the course incomplete in any way that matters at the board, but it's worth knowing where the edges are rather than assuming there aren't any. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 3. e5 { GOOD LUCK AT THE BOARD. This course is free, stays free, and the PGN underneath it is yours to keep, study, and, with permission, build on. Play the Caro-Kann the way Caro, Kann, Capablanca, Botvinnik, and Karpov did: patiently, soundly, and in a way your opponent has to actually beat, not hope you misplay. See you in the next round of updates. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } *


